nd Act', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T01:03:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Ravitch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ravitch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a60763b-af8d-517f-9a40-809b64c35458', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a60763b-af8d-517f-9a40-809b64c35458'}, {'abstract': 'The Yankee pitchers formed a bond last September, when Ivan Nova starting asking A. J. Burnett questions about his career while they shagged fly balls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/sports/baseball/burnett-and-nova-are-inseparable-teammates-and-as-opposite-as-can-be.html', 'snippet': 'The Yankee pitchers formed a bond last September, when Ivan Nova starting asking A. J. Burnett questions about his career while they shagged fly balls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OAKLAND, Calif. — A few months ago, A. J. Burnett added another member to his family. It was all unexpected. The new addition is 24 years old, stands 6 feet 4 inches and hails from the Dominican Republic, and Burnett is still searching for how best to characterize their relationship: Is Ivan Nova more like his third son or his little brother?', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/01/sports/YYANKEES/YYANKEES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 984, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/01/sports/YYANKEES/YYANKEES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/01/sports/YYANKEES/YYANKEES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inseparable Teammates, as Opposite as Can Be', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inseparable Teammates, As Opposite as Can Be', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burnett, A J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nova, Ivan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T01:15:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f2af6b3-9e7b-55b6-b3fc-5da4824ca01c', 'word_count': 1245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f2af6b3-9e7b-55b6-b3fc-5da4824ca01c'}, {'abstract': 'Alex Rodriguez, Joba Chamberlain and Derek Jeter swept the top three spots in Sports Illustrated’s survey of the Most Overrated Player.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/05/31/overrated-rodriguez-and-chamberlain-can-laugh-about-it/', 'snippet': 'Alex Rodriguez, Joba Chamberlain and Derek Jeter swept the top three spots in Sports Illustrated’s survey of the Most Overrated Player.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OAKLAND, Calif. — A common practice of sporting publications is undertaking surveys that rate players, hoping to provoke a response. The latest version appears in this week’s Sports Illustrated, which asked 185 major leaguers to vote in the category of Most Overrated Player.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Overrated? Rodriguez and Chamberlain Can Laugh About It', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chamberlain, Joba', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeter, Derek', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodriguez, Alex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T01:16:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc9c5796-4230-59be-9f14-4d0e55b77552', 'word_count': 312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc9c5796-4230-59be-9f14-4d0e55b77552'}, {'abstract': 'The Black Sea region of Abkhazia, which considers itself independent but is claimed by Georgia, won recognition Tuesday from a second South Pacific island, the Republic of Vanuatu.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/world/europe/01briefs-Abkhazia.html', 'snippet': 'The Black Sea region of Abkhazia, which considers itself independent but is claimed by Georgia, won recognition Tuesday from a second South Pacific island, the Republic of Vanuatu.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Black Sea region of Abkhazia, which considers itself independent but is claimed by Georgia and viewed as Georgian territory by most of the international community, won recognition Tuesday from a second South Pacific island, the Republic of Vanuatu. Earlier, Abkhazia was recognized by Nauru, which is the world’s smallest country and like Vanuatu is situated in the Pacific Ocean east of Australia. Abkhazia is also recognized by Russia — which fought a war with Georgia over it and another breakaway region — as well as Nicaragua and Venezuela. Tiny South Pacific island states have been a particular prize for Abkhazia, as they have been for decades for new nations seeking to bulk up on the number of countries offering recognition. The government of Vanuatu, which is in an economic slump, said in a statement that recognition was intended to “foster the development of friendly relations” and explore joint economic projects. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Caucasus: New Allies for Breakaway Region', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Caucasus: New Allies for Breakaway Region', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia (Georgian Republic)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vanuatu', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ABKHAZIA (GEORGIAN REPUBLIC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nauru', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T01:23:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cecad2f0-335b-5116-8f34-991c08955f13', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cecad2f0-335b-5116-8f34-991c08955f13'}, {'abstract': 'Very few cases related to the financial crisis have been brought against executives at large companies, and the ones that have been involved are almost entirely civil fraud, rather than criminal allegations. Here are the prominent cases so far. In many of them, the government negotiated a settlement with the defendants before it even filed a complaint, then announced both the case and settlement at the same time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/business/financial-crisis-cases.html', 'snippet': 'Very few cases related to the financial crisis have been brought against executives at large companies, and the ones that have been involved are almost entirely civil fraud, rather than criminal allegations. Here are the prominent cases so far. In...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Very few cases related to the financial crisis have been brought against executives at large companies, and the ones that have been involved are almost entirely civil fraud, rather than criminal allegations. Here are the prominent cases so far. In many of them, the government negotiated a settlement with the defendants before it even filed a complaint, then announced both the case and settlement at the same time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tracking Financial Crisis Cases', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T01:26:00+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/03e961a2-7a0a-53b2-af39-4ca814a33bd0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/03e961a2-7a0a-53b2-af39-4ca814a33bd0'}, {'abstract': 'Osama really did a number on all of us. Just look at these holes we’re in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/opinion/01friedman.html', 'snippet': 'Osama really did a number on all of us. Just look at these holes we’re in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Visiting the Middle East last week, and then coming back to Washington, I am left with one overriding impression: Bin Laden really did a number on all of us. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Bin Laden Decade', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_