line •\\ˈa-kwə-ˌlin, -lən\\• adjective', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Word of the Day | aquiline', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T04:01:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fadc155-b175-57d6-89c5-23ef6c5fb11a', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fadc155-b175-57d6-89c5-23ef6c5fb11a'}, {'abstract': 'A high-fat, low-carbohydrate, Atkins-style diet can lead to rapid weight loss without harming heart health, provided you get some exercise.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/eating-fat-staying-lean/', 'snippet': 'A high-fat, low-carbohydrate, Atkins-style diet can lead to rapid weight loss without harming heart health, provided you get some exercise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the memory of Memorial Day cheeseburgers and bratwursts still lingering, many of us may be relieved to hear that a new study suggests that a meaty, high-fat, Atkins-style diet can do more than contribute to rapid weight loss. It may also be less unhealthy for the heart than many scientists had feared — provided you chase the sausage with a brisk walk. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eating Fat, Staying Lean', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Heart', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 5, 'major'