 '_id': 'nyt://article/2fffdbd1-0e16-562c-b710-226cb9d7f213', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fffdbd1-0e16-562c-b710-226cb9d7f213'}, {'abstract': 'Michel Barnier, the European commissioner for the internal market and services, calls for trans-Atlantic unity on financial regulation ahead of a meeting with Treasury Secretary Timothy F. Geithner.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/e-u-financial-regulator-criticizes-u-s-on-banker-bonuses/', 'snippet': 'Michel Barnier, the European commissioner for the internal market and services, calls for trans-Atlantic unity on financial regulation ahead of a meeting with Treasury Secretary Timothy F. Geithner.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:43 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'European Regulator Criticizes U.S. on Banker Bonuses', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bonuses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barnier, Michel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Geithner, Timothy F', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Basel Committee on Banking Supervision', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T14:42:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a17197e-2584-5532-bf21-8f991a0e91fb', 'word_count': 426, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a17197e-2584-5532-bf21-8f991a0e91fb'}, {'abstract': 'This year’s Biennale has started to shape up as a knock-down-drag-out contest among immersive, environmental installation artworks.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/venice-biennale-an-installation-art-contest/', 'snippet': 'This year’s Biennale has started to shape up as a knock-down-drag-out contest among immersive, environmental installation artworks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VENICE — After the first day spent looking at most, but not all, of the national pavilions in the Giardini, this year’s Biennale has started to shape up as a knock-down, drag-out contest among immersive, environmental installation artworks. The battle is especially pitched on the little hillock dominated by the pavilions of Britain (Mike Nelson), Germany (Christoph Schlingensief) and France (Christian Boltanski), although the fray has also been joined by Switzerland (Thomas Hirshhorn) and the Czech Republic. At that pavilion, a little-known artist named Dominik Lang, barely 30 years old, has mounted a strangely affecting time-capsule-like installation featuring a great deal of generic postwar figurative sculpture by his father, Jiri Lang (1927-1996), who stopped making art years before his son was born. It serves as a sobering reminder of the obscurity that awaits most of the art produced at any given point in time, as well as the ability of art objects, being objects, to wait out different phases of neglect.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Venice Biennale: An Installation Art Contest', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venice Biennale', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boltanski, Christian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nelson, Mike', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tintoretto', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Czech Republic', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Denmark', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Switzerland', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Venice (Italy)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T14:44:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b794886-94db-596d-a855-b75df9b171e3', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b794886-94db-596d-a855-b75df9b171e3'}, {'abstract': 'For travelers who look beyond big-brand hotel chains, some boutique and independent hotels have joined together to offer loyalty programs with perks that reflect personalized services.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/travel/small-hotels-offer-new-loyalty-programs-practical-traveler.html', 'snippet': 'For travelers who look beyond big-brand hotel chains, some boutique and independent hotels have joined together to offer loyalty programs with perks that reflect personalized services.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TRAVELERS who prefer boutique hotels to big cookie-cutter brands have long faced a trade-off. While often more charming and memorable than your typical chain hotel, most independent hotels don’t offer loyalty programs. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/travel/05prac/05prac-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 527, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/travel/05prac/05prac-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/travel/05prac/05prac-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Expanding Hotel Loyalty Rewards', 'kicker': 'Practical Traveler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Expanding Hotel Loyalty Rewards', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T14:49:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Higgins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Higgins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a513a968-e848-56c3-8d42-f680a64f78a4', 'word_count': 1174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a513a968-e848-56c3-8d42-f680a64f78a4'}, {'abstract': 'While readers may think the plaintiff has a case of sour grapes, the Madoff victim seeking to renegotiate his divorce settlement may be in the right.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/why-madoff-victim-may-have-a-case-in-divorce-do-over/', 'snippet': 'While readers may think the plaintiff has a case of sour grapes, the Madoff victim seeking to renegotiate his divorce settlement may be in the right.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is all really fair in love and war?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Madoff Victim May Have a Case for a Divorce Do-Over', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madoff, Bernard L', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T14:55:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Ross Sorkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Ross', 'lastname': 'Sorkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95b2e255-bb53-59f0-933e-84c86e3257c4', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95b2e255-bb53-59f0-933e-84c86e3257c4'}, {'abstract': 'Allegra, who is best known for her supersoft cotton, has turned some of her focus to leather.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/01/in-her-skin-raquel-allegra/', 'snippet': 'Allegra, who is best known for her supersoft cotton, has turned some of her focus to leather.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Summer is all about showing skin. But for those who aren’t into baring it all, one option is dipping into early fall offerings. Pared-down basics by the Los Angeles-based designer Raquel Allegra have long been favored by stars such as Halle Berry, Hanneli Mustaparta and Agyness Deyn. Although Allegra is best known for her supersoft cotton shredding technique, for her latest collection, which hits stores in July, she began thinking about leather. “I asked myself, What do I want to wear as a second skin?” The results include a dappled goatskin bouclé cardigan that is as pleasing to the touch as it is to the eye — and that still leaves enough to the imagination. There are also slimming thigh-high leather leggings that mold to one’s body, supple camel leather shirts and buttery black body-con dresses that look effortless and sensual, not overripe or overtly sexy. The collection, available July 31, ranges from $250 to $1,100 and can be purchased at barneys.com or netaporter.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Her Skin | Raquel Allegra', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T15:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kavitha Surana', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kavitha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Surana', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/958c8d27-daca-53cc-986a-fc40413df1c2', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/958c8d27-daca-53cc-986a-fc40413df1c2'}, {'abstract': 'Ford, G.M. and Chrysler reclaimed industry leadership for the first time in five years as Toyota and Honda were hurt by production disruptions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/business/02auto.html', 'snippet': 'Ford, G.M. and Chrysler reclaimed industry leadership for the first time in five years as Toyota and Honda were hurt by production disruptions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — The steady recovery in auto sales stalled in May, as more consumers stayed away from new-car showrooms because of higher prices, shortages of some Japanese models, and concerns over the economy.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/business/Auto/Auto-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/02/business/Auto/Auto-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'j