: 'nyt://article/0dfe3db1-8233-5ae8-a16a-44565a7129d7'}, {'abstract': 'In Ann Patchett’s new novel, a research scientist goes outside her comfort zone, to the Amazon jungle, to help solve the mystery of a colleague’s death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/books/state-of-wonder-by-ann-patchett-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Ann Patchett’s new novel, a research scientist goes outside her comfort zone, to the Amazon jungle, to help solve the mystery of a colleague’s death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dr. Marina Singh, the 42-year-old research scientist who is the heroine of “State of Wonder,” Ann Patchett’s most far-flung yet somehow least exotic book, is in her office at a large pharmaceutical company in Minnesota when the bad news arrives. Marina does unremarkable research on cholesterol. She is having an unremarkable affair with Mr. Fox, the company’s C.E.O. The bland, far-from-fantastic Mr. Fox arrives to tell her that her research partner, Dr. Anders Eckman, has died of a fever in a remote part of Brazil. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/jp-book/jp-book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/jp-book/jp-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/jp-book/jp-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Perilous Trek to Amazon Reveal Heart of Darkness?', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Will Perilous Trek To Amazon Reveal Heart of Darkness?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMAZON JUNGLE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patchett, Ann', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T22:07:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/08bbbb6d-39f0-5eef-9590-ba10c46e6bdb', 'word_count': 956, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08bbbb6d-39f0-5eef-9590-ba10c46e6bdb'}, {'abstract': 'Inexpensive apps and third-party peripherals make the iPad an excellent device for photographers, artists, writers and bloggers to create original content.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/technology/personaltech/02basics.html', 'snippet': 'Inexpensive apps and third-party peripherals make the iPad an excellent device for photographers, artists, writers and bloggers to create original content.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The iPad is great for activities like watching movies, surfing the Web, playing video games or reading digital magazines and newspapers. The rap against the iPad is that it is not as useful as a computer for creating. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/business/BASICS-1/BASICS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/02/business/BASICS-1/BASICS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/business/BASICS-1/BASICS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'h