selections in 1997 were children’s books by Bill Cosby, which should in itself sustain the question of her value as a national educator.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/OPRAH/OPRAH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/OPRAH/OPRAH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/OPRAH/OPRAH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 549, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/OPRAH/OPRAH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/OPRAH/OPRAH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Oprah’s Network Goes to the Movies', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oprah’s Network Goes to the Movies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oprah Winfrey Network', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winfrey, Oprah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-01T22:23:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bd8700f-eb9f-5220-973f-3501131a8abb', 'word_count': 982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bd8700f-eb9f-5220-973f-3501131a8abb'}, {'abstract': 'Echoes of a 19th-century classic in Balanchine’s “Donizetti Variations.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/arts/dance/new-york-city-ballet-in-donizetti-variations-review.html', 'snippet': 'Echoes of a 19th-century classic in Balanchine’s “Donizetti Variations.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Although you don’t need to know the ballets from one century to appreciate those from another, the connections are in some cases so profound that recognizing them deepens the whole experience. It was exciting on Tuesday night, amid American Ballet Theater’s week of performances of “Giselle” ( 1841) across the Lincoln Center plaza at the Metropolitan Opera House, to watch New York City Ballet dance George Balanchine’s “Donizetti Variations” (1960) at the David H. Koch Theater. Although “Donizetti” has no scenery and no narrative, it looks at first like an outtake from the older ballet’s celebration of the festival of the vine, with the jollification of decorous peasants.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/NYCB1/NYCB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/NYCB1/NYCB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/NYCB1/NYCB1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/NYCB1/NYCB1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thum