': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turner, Justin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Terry L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Pirates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T03:54:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Caldwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caldwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/724b3c51-677c-50a7-a6eb-5da3366e2747', 'word_count': 973, 'uri': 'nyt://article/724b3c51-677c-50a7-a6eb-5da3366e2747'}, {'abstract': 'Prosecutors in Rome ordered the release on Wednesday of 23 million Euros, or almost $33 million, seized from the Vatican Bank last year in a continuing money-laundering investigation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/world/europe/02briefs-Italy.html', 'snippet': 'Prosecutors in Rome ordered the release on Wednesday of 23 million Euros, or almost $33 million, seized from the Vatican Bank last year in a continuing money-laundering investigation.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Prosecutors in Rome ordered the release on Wednesday of 23 million Euros, or almost $33 million, seized from the Vatican Bank last year in a continuing money-laundering investigation. They said they released the money after the Vatican instituted new mechanisms to help it adhere to international anti-money laundering and antiterrorist financing standards. The changes included creating a financial watchdog to coordinate with international organizations — a new leaf for an organization known for its opaque finances. The Vatican on Wednesday said it “appreciated” the release of the funds. Prosecutors had preventively seized the funds last September and placed the bank’s top two financial officers under investigation on the grounds that they transferred the money without properly disclosing the sender or motive. The two officials remain under investigation but have not been charged with a crime. The Vatican has said the investigation is the result of a misunderstanding. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Italy: Money Seized From Vatican Bank Is Released', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Italy: Money Seized From Vatican Bank Is Released', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Money Laundering', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T03:55:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Donadio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Donadio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/19fac846-e877-5686-a2c1-f73c63aab5a0', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19fac846-e877-5686-a2c1-f73c63aab5a0'}, {'abstract': 'A developer financed the network, designed to be available outdoors between the Brooklyn and Manhattan Bridges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/nyregion/free-wi-fi-network-starting-for-dumbo-neighborhood.html', 'snippet': 'A developer financed the network, designed to be available outdoors between the Brooklyn and Manhattan Bridges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg frequently proclaims New York as an ideal city to start a technology company, but until now not a single neighborhood offered free wireless access to the Internet.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Using Internet Outside? In Part of Brooklyn, Free Wireless Access Arrives', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Using Internet Outside? In Part of Brooklyn, Free Wireless Access Arrives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dumbo (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Towerstream Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Two Trees Management', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T04:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b62f2ac7-eef3-584a-a4f7-72198ef07b79', 'word_count': 350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b62f2ac7-eef3-584a-a4f7-72198ef07b79'}, {'abstract': 'Contrary to conventional wisdom, patient outcomes had little to do with the time of the operation or the fatigue of the surgeon. Rather, they seemed to hinge on the reliability of the surgical team.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/surgeons-who-burn-the-midnight-oil/', 'snippet': 'Contrary to conventional wisdom, patient outcomes had little to do with the time of the operation or the fatigue of the surgeon. Rather, they seemed to hinge on the reliability of the surgical team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few years ago during a visit home, my mother interrupted me as I was recounting the details of a transplant operation I’d done a week earlier. For as long as I’ve been a doctor, my mother, who owns a small business, has queried me about my long work hours, usually punctuating her questions with sympathetic clucks and gentle exhortations to eat more of the Taiwanese food she has prepared.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Surgeons Who Burn the Midnight Oil', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fatigue', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surgery and Surgeons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transplants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T04:01:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pauline W. Chen, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pauline', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02e27e4b-c8d6-5222-8fd8-dc24c8b05bae', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02e27e4b-c8d6-5222-8fd8-dc24c8b05bae'}, {'abstract': 'This word has appeared in 19 New York Times articles in the past year.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/word-of-the-day-submissive/', 'snippet': 'This word has appeared in 19 New York Times articles in the past year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'submissive •\\ˈsəb-ˈmi-siv\\• adjective', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Word of the Day | submissive', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T04:02:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0fecd199-1aa0-5759-a28c-96654fa1c7cb', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0fecd199-1aa0-5759-a28c-96654fa1c7cb'}, {'abstract': 'A war crimes tribunal official at The Hague said Wednesday that Ratko Mladic appeared to have no cognitive difficulty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/world/europe/02webbrief-Hague.html', 'snippet': 'A war crimes tribunal official at The Hague said Wednesday that Ratko Mladic appeared to have no cognitive difficulty.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A war crimes tribunal official at The Hague said Wednesday that Ratko Mladic, who arrived there Tuesday, had been cooperative and appeared to have no cognitive difficulty. Mr. Mladic, the former Bosnian Serb military commander, had earlier claimed he was mentally and physically unfit for trial. John Hocking, the court official, also said that Mr. Mladic was not placed on suicide watch. Mr. Mladic is due to make his first court appearance in a brief hearing on Friday, when he will be asked to enter his plea on charges including violations of the laws of war, crimes against humanity and genocide, court officials said. Mr. Mladic, 69, was arrested last week in Serbia after more than 15 years on the run. He spent his first night at The Hague in a separate section of the prison, without mingling with other tribunal prisoners, who include former friends and enemies from the Balkan wars. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hague: Mladic Said to Be Cooperative', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Hague: Mladic Said to Be Cooperative', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mladic, Ratko', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Serbia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T04:07:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marlise Simons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marlise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/85aa0c64-82c8-5930-a711-fd38a96107ba', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85aa0c64-82c8-5930-a711-fd38a96107ba'}, {'abstract': 'Explosives hidden in a plastic bag were detonated at the wedding of a policeman about 40 miles south of Baghdad on Wednesday, killing 7 people and wounding 15.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/world/middleeast/02webbrief-iraq.html', 'snippet': 'Explosives hidden in a plastic bag were detonated at the wedding of a policeman about 40 miles south of Baghdad on Wednesday, killing 7 people and wounding 15.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Explosives hidden in a plastic bag were detonated at the wedding of a policeman about 40 miles south of Baghdad on Wednesday, killing 7 people and wounding 15, according to a local security official. The policeman’s brother was among the dead, but few other details were immediately available. The blast occurred around 9:30 p.m. in the predominately Sunni village of Musayyib. The blast went off before a significant number of people had arrived at the wedding, according to the security official. It was unclear who was behind the attack. Policemen and soldiers are often the targets of insurgents. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iraq: Blast at Policeman’s Wedding Kills 7', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | MIDDLE EAST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iraq: Blast at Policeman’s Wedding Kills 7', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T04:08:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael S. Schmidt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e55a8b8-22d4-54f8-aca3-bbc5e37a89c7', 'word_count': 98, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e55a8b8-22d4-54f8-aca3-bbc5e37a89c7'}, {'abstract': 'Programs would lose their ability to dispense federal student aid if, over the next four years, their graduates fail to meet new benchmarks for loan repayment and ratio of debt to income.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/education/02gainful.html', 'snippet': 'Programs would lose their ability to dispense federal student aid if, over the next four years, their graduates fail to meet new benchmarks for loan repayment and ratio of debt to income.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Department of Education on Wednesday tightened its regulation of for-profit colleges and other vocational programs that get billions of dollars in federal aid but leave many students with crushing debt and credentials worth little on the job market. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Education Department Increases Its Regulation of For-Profit Colleges', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Education Department Increases Its Regulation of For-Profit Colleges', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'For-Profit Schools', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T04:10:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamar Lewin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b490563e-7718-521d-b93c-7f42707db089', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b490563e-7718-521d-b93c-7f42707db089'}, {'abstract': 'State governments are strained by the end of federal stimulus money, which helped many stay afloat, and rising Medicaid costs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/us/02states.html', 'snippet': 'State governments are strained by the end of federal stimulus money, which helped many stay afloat, and rising Medicaid costs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Half the states plan to cut spending on higher education, and nearly a third plan cuts to elementary and high schools. Public assistance and transportation face cuts. Eighteen states have proposed slashing aid to struggling cities and local governments. Some states will raise taxes or fees. Others plan to lay off workers, or cut their salaries or benefits.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Improved Tax Collections Can’t Keep Pace With States’ Fiscal Needs, Survey Finds', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Improved Tax Collections Can’t Keep Pace With States’ Fiscal Needs, Survey Finds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T04:14:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e53c129e-2de4-5fd8-9818-a0d4a320192e', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e53c129e-2de4-5fd8-9818-a0d4a320192e'}, {'abstract': 'House Republicans say spending cuts can be combined with an increase in the federal debt limit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/us/politics/02obama.html', 'snippet': 'House Republicans say spending cuts can be combined with an increase in the federal debt limit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Speaker John A. Boehner said Wednesday that Congress and the White House should try to reach an agreement within the next month that would combine an increase in the federal debt limit with deep spending cuts, and he called on President Obama to play a larger role in negotiations.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/us/alt-obama/alt-obama-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/02/us/alt-obama/alt-