tions', 'value': 'CareFusion Corp', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T16:58:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Egner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66d50b5d-1feb-5219-8d7d-a43ff42e2c96', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66d50b5d-1feb-5219-8d7d-a43ff42e2c96'}, {'abstract': 'The cool, wet spring in the West may make for an exceptional white-water rafting season.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/a-banner-season-for-whitewater-rafting/', 'snippet': 'The cool, wet spring in the West may make for an exceptional white-water rafting season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rafting outfitters in the West expect higher water and a longer river-running season because of above-average snowpack and a cool, wet spring.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Banner Season for Whitewater Rafting', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rafts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Western States (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T16:58:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/678910d8-c663-584a-ac52-48efef9f5c3f', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/678910d8-c663-584a-ac52-48efef9f5c3f'}, {'abstract': 'I believe there is such a thing as too much theater.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/theater-talkback-anything-but-theater-at-least-for-a-night-or-two/', 'snippet': 'I believe there is such a thing as too much theater.', 'lead_paragraph': ' During the summer, ArtsBeat will invite guest contributors to the Theater Talkback column. Howard Sherman is the executive director of the American Theater Wing, which along with the Broadway League, puts on the Tony Awards every year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Theater Talkback: Anything But Theater! (At Least for a Night or Two)', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T17:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Sherman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sherman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/045504f3-27ae-5a8e-9296-6bd2867f4af5', 'word_count': 789, 'uri': 'nyt://article/045504f3-27ae-5a8e-9296-6bd2867f4af5'}, {'abstract': 'An interactive look at the history of Cirque du Soleil.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/06/05/theater/20110605-cirque.html', 'snippet': 'An interactive look at the history of Cirque du Soleil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An interactive look at the history of Cirque du Soleil.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Under the Tent', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cirque du Soleil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Circuses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T17:16:08+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Theater', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/aa3feb82-05fb-522b-a8ba-3651322804be', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/aa3feb82-05fb-522b-a8ba-3651322804be'}, {'abstract': 'One Police Plaza: More questions about the stopping and searching of people by police officers; and a former New York police official is re-elected to his position in an international bomb technician group.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/the-young-boss-for-a-day-has-his-say/', 'snippet': 'One Police Plaza: More questions about the stopping and searching of people by police officers; and a former New York police official is re-elected to his position in an international bomb technician group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was that time of year in the Police Department when a bright high school student is chosen as police commissioner for a day. So, on Wednesday, after a formal ceremony at Police Headquarters, Police Commissioner Raymond W. Kelly stepped before the klieg lights and news reporters accompanied by this year’s recipient, Gabriel Gomez, as well as the teenager’s mother, sister and an aunt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Young Boss for a Day Has His Say', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search and Seizure', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gomez, Gabriel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Raymond W', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Civil Liberties Union', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T17:21:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d7db7fe-33b0-501e-87cb-afcc44c19e2b', 'word_count': 1055, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d7db7fe-33b0-501e-87cb-afcc44c19e2b'}, {'abstract': 'Jim Tressel’s resignation further points out how a multimillion-dollar sports enterprise like the N.C.A.A. is fueled by student-athletes who get in trouble for selling trinkets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/sports/ncaafootball/jim-tressel-ohio-state.html', 'snippet': 'Jim Tressel’s resignation further points out how a multimillion-dollar sports enterprise like the N.C.A.A. is fueled by student-athletes who get in trouble for selling trinkets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the Final Four in Houston last April, Gene Smith, the Ohio State athletic director, talked about the pressures on a megaprogram like his to maintain a winning edge. It can be a beastly challenge, he said: paying more for coaches, spending more on the recruiting budget to land top-flight players. The difference between David and Goliath is money. Ohio State’s annual athletic budget is more than $100 million. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/sports/rhoden/rhoden-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 777, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/