': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Blackstone Group, The', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T17:43:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01230592-bc69-54f2-b5c3-d98d43e86dd7', 'word_count': 541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01230592-bc69-54f2-b5c3-d98d43e86dd7'}, {'abstract': 'The biggest surprise of the International Contemporary Furniture Fair? An amazingly polished collection of classroom furniture that was designed by a group of eighth graders.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/bright-young-things-tools-at-schools/', 'snippet': 'The biggest surprise of the International Contemporary Furniture Fair? An amazingly polished collection of classroom furniture that was designed by a group of eighth graders.', 'lead_paragraph': ' NYTD.fullScreenSlideShowFactory.createSlideshow("#763", 592, 400, true, chameleonData);', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bright Young Things | Tools at Schools', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T17:46:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/770392a1-8a7f-5022-8273-1555ecdbcb9a', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/770392a1-8a7f-5022-8273-1555ecdbcb9a'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/the-werewolf-within.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I thoroughly enjoyed Alex Pappademas’s article (dare I say “tribute”?) in the magazine this week. Although “Teen Wolf” did well at the box office all those years ago, this current and rather astonishing flurry of attention and examination would certainly have been welcome on opening day. I believe that with any film, it is up to the filmmaker to put forth his or her story, and it is then up to the audience to ascribe motivations and meaning. Having said that, I thought you might be interested in a few of my intentions while directing the film. To me, this was never a film about a werewolf. It was about a normal kid fighting his way through puberty, girls, peer pressure, school, etc., who has a bomb dropped on him at the end of Act I. For me, the key scene in the film occurs when Scott, Michael J. Fox’s character, reveals the wolf for the first time in public, during a basketball game. In about 20 seconds, the reactions of the actors go from horror, fear and amazement to curiosity and acceptance.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Werewolf Within', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE WEREWOLF WITHIN', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wolves', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T17:48:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a75120a-d8fb-5e32-b29e-59026f9e81a8', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a75120a-d8fb-5e32-b29e-59026f9e81a8'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/does-twitter-make-you-stupid.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a high-school junior, I am deeply saddened by the attitude of despondency toward my generation expressed by Bill Keller in “The Twitter Trap.” There are some ill side effects of our prolonged exposure to the Internet, notably a shortened attention span and an unappeasable desire to be instantly gratified. But those are only the cons of being in constant communication with one another. Never before could a high-school student so brazenly reach out to a Harvard graduate student and ask for mentorship on his research paper. Never again will we think it odd for someone from the farthest corner of the globe to be exchanging witticisms on Twitter with a well-known celebrity.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Twitter Make You Stupid?', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'DOES TWITTER MAKE YOU STUPID?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T17:50:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/c118d566-f1b1-5ef0-afb0-d8b8593461b3', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c118d566-f1b1-5ef0-afb0-d8b8593461b3'}, {'abstract': 'NBC said Thursday that Vivian Schiller, who resigned from NPR in March in the midst of controversy, would lead an expansion of the NBC News division’s ventures in digital media.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/nbc-names-schiller-as-chief-digital-officer/', 'snippet': 'NBC said Thursday that Vivian Schiller, who resigned from NPR in March in the midst of controversy, would lead an expansion of the NBC News division’s ventures in digital media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vivian Schiller, who resigned as NPR’s chief executive in March in the midst of controversy, was named Thursday to the newly created position of chief digital officer at NBC News.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'NBC Names Schiller as Chief Digital Officer', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schiller, Vivian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NBC News', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T17:51:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91930c29-0bf7-56e7-bb0d-ddd884ba4ea1', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91930c29-0bf7-56e7-bb0d-ddd884ba4ea1'}, {'abstract': 'This latest installment of the “X-Men” series reaches back to the early 1960s for an origin story of mutants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/movies/x-men-first-class-review.html', 'snippet': 'This latest installment of the “X-Men” series reaches back to the early 1960s for an origin story of mutants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a close call with franchise death (diagnosis: anemia), the X-Men film series has bounced back to life with its fifth installment, rescued with a straight injection of pop. Directed by Matthew Vaughn, “X-Men: First Class” reaches back to the early 1960s for an origin story of mutants, mad men and mods that takes some of its cues from James Bond and more than a few costumes from Austin Powers. Like “Mad Men,” this new “X-Men” indulges in period nostalgia as it gazes into the future, using the backdrop of the cold war (and its turtlenecks) to explore how the past informs the present (while also blowing stuff up).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/JP-XMEN/JP-XMEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 257, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/JP-XMEN/JP-XMEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 257}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/JP-XMEN/JP-XMEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/JP-XMEN/JP-XMEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/JP-XMEN/JP-XMEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Born That Way, and Proud of It', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'X-Men: First Class'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Born That Way, and Proud Of It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vaughn, Matthew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'X-Men: First Class (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T17:54:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': 