se/', 'snippet': 'A Republican committee chairman says the administration appeared more concerned about protecting its own media profile and avoiding responsibility for recovery efforts than protecting gulf jobs and livelihoods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Representative Darrell Issa, the California Republican who is chairman of the House Oversight and Government Reform Committee, charged Thursday that the Obama administration bungled the response to the Deep Water Horizon explosion and oil spill last year. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'House Panel Faults Administration Spill Response', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barbour, Haley', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bromwich, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Issa, Darrell E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BP Plc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gulf Coast Claims Facility', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House Oversight and Government Reform Committee', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Interior Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Minerals Management Service', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Coast Guard', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gulf of Mexico', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Louisiana', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T18:22:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48984f46-59c2-54b0-b5b0-11fce1e7f99a', 'word_count': 688, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48984f46-59c2-54b0-b5b0-11fce1e7f99a'}, {'abstract': 'A private equity firm helps Duane Sorenson’s company add cafes and roasting facilities.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/stumptown-expands-with-the-help-of-a-powerful-investor/', 'snippet': 'A private equity firm helps Duane Sorenson’s company add cafes and roasting facilities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It looks like a big year for Stumptown Coffee Roasters. The Portland, Ore., company, known for getting some of the finest coffee in the world and serving it with rock ‘n’ roll flair, plans to open two coffee bars in Brooklyn, add a bottling facility to its roaster in Red Hook for its cold-brewed coffee and, Duane Sorenson, Stumptown’s founder, says the company will try to open roasters in Chicago and San Francisco.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stumptown Expands With the Help of a Powerful Investor', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coffee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stumptown Coffee Roasters', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Portland (Ore)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T18:22:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Oliver Strand', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Oliver', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strand', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d686863-00c1-570e-8198-ed91c30ecbd0', 'word_count': 600, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d686863-00c1-570e-8198-ed91c30ecbd0'}, {'abstract': '“So You Think You Can Dance” draws 8.4 million viewers.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/fox-dances-to-no-1-again/', 'snippet': '“So You Think You Can Dance” draws 8.4 million viewers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fox topped the ratings on Wednesday night as 8.4 million viewers tuned in for another two-hour installment of “So You Think You Can Dance,” according to Nielsen’s estimates. Although down a bit compared to last week’s strong season-premiere ratings on Thursday (9.5 million), the Fox reality show still easily outperformed its repeat competition on CBS and ABC. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fox Dances to No. 1 Again', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stanley Cup', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen Media Research', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T18:34:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73bac6d4-1065-5585-b5b5-c472117c8fc2', 'word_count': 132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73bac6d4-1065-5585-b5b5-c472117c8fc2'}, {'abstract': 'During the long days of summer, Stockholm moves outdoors, an easy feat since water and parkland make up nearly two-thirds of the city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/travel/36-hours-in-stockholm.html', 'snippet': 'During the long days of summer, Stockholm moves outdoors, an easy feat since water and parkland make up nearly two-thirds of the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STOCKHOLMERS revel in the long days of summer when the sun rises as early as 3:30 a.m. and doesn’t set until after 10 p.m. Many locals escape to their country houses, but those who remain in the city spend as much time as possible outdoors — an easy feat since water and parkland make up almost two-thirds of Stockholm. Situated on the Baltic at the mouth of Lake Malaren, Stockholm is built on 14 islands connected by bridges. On sunny days, waterside bars and cafes are packed with people wolfing down summer favorites like herring (served in a variety of ways) and toast Skagen (shrimp in mayonnaise with lemon and dill served with rye bread). Everywhere, visitors will find establishments — new and venerable — celebrating Nordic design, cuisine and culture.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/travel/05hours-span/05-HOURS-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/travel/05hours-span/05-HOURS-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 