or': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T19:56:00+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/503e526a-6730-55ad-b2c9-ef42bf5e7110', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/503e526a-6730-55ad-b2c9-ef42bf5e7110'}, {'abstract': 'Daniel Kopulos, the owner of Fauna, tends to birds, fish and reptiles. Some are endangered species that he nurtures for conservation, not commercial, reasons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/nyregion/fauna-caters-to-exotic-animals.html', 'snippet': 'Daniel Kopulos, the owner of Fauna, tends to birds, fish and reptiles. Some are endangered species that he nurtures for conservation, not commercial, reasons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EVERY afternoon except Tuesday, when this Upper West Side temple to alternative pets — jungle birds, desert reptiles and all things aquatic — is closed, Bob Myles and his Norfolk terrier, Bruno, pop through the front door and make a beeline for the spectacular glassed-in aviary at the back. There, Bruno plants himself on his haunches, nose to glass, and stares at the parrots in free flight on the other side. Mr. Myles, who lives in the neighborhood, is a regular at the shop, Fauna, but not a customer. “Bruno leads me here, and the people who run the store are very gracious about it,” he said. “They know we’re just here to look. The little green parrots are his favorites.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/nyregion/05JOINT1/05JOINT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/nyregion/05JOINT1/05JOINT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jum