tps://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/movies/shawn-kus-beautiful-boy-review.html', 'snippet': 'A married couple’s bottled-up tensions are blasted open as they cope with their son’s shooting rampage and suicide.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Beautiful Boy” tiptoes into a minefield, where it loses its nerve and remains frozen in its tracks, too timid to find a way back. As you watch this earnest-to-a-fault, sensitively acted film about the parents of a campus killer reacting to their son’s shooting rampage and suicide, you may wonder why. Why make a fictional movie, however tasteful, about the aftermath of a horror apparently based on the 2007 Virginia Tech massacre, whose perpetrator killed 32 people and then himself? Why not a documentary?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/BEAUTIFUL-span/BEAUTIFUL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/BEAUTIFUL-span/BEAUTIFUL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 349}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/BEAUTIFUL-span/BEAUTIFUL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/BEAUTIFUL-span/BEAUTIFUL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Alone to Face the Fallout From a Son’s Horrific Crime', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Beautiful Boy'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Alone to Face the Fallout From a Son’s Horrific Crime', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bello, Maria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheen, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tudyk, Alan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T20:54:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d45733c5-873e-5f87-a7e4-7512abccee13', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d45733c5-873e-5f87-a7e4-7512abccee13'}, {'abstract': 'YouTube has created a section of its video editor to help people find videos online that can be borrowed and re-edited into new content without fear of breaking copyright rules.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/youtube-enables-creative-commons-videos-sharing/', 'snippet': 'YouTube has created a section of its video editor to help people find videos online that can be borrowed and re-edited into new content without fear of breaking copyright rules.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YouTube is home to some of the more creative individuals online — people who create content, often splicing and dicing numerous videos together to create mesmerizing mashups. Now YouTube wants to help these people find videos online that are intended to be borrowed and re-edited without fear of violating copyrights.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'YouTube Enables Creative Commons Videos Sharing', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recordings and Downloads (Video)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Creative Commons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T20:57:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53d77ad2-ee96-526c-b0f8-c611f0d24102', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53d77ad2-ee96-526c-b0f8-c611f0d24102'}, {'abstract': 'A selected guide to pop, rock, hip-hop, folk, metal and other music performances in New York and the area.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/arts/music/pop-and-rock-listings-for-june-3-9.html', 'snippet': 'A selected guide to pop, rock, hip-hop, folk, metal and other music performances in New York and the area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pop', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pop and Rock Listings for June 3-9', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Folk Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Central Park Summerstage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'PNC Bank Arts Center (NJ)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T20:58:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0de3c1f-aba5-5d2d-ab0b-e6a3127d86d3', 'word_count': 1697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0de3c1f-aba5-5d2d-ab0b-e6a3127d86d3'}, {'abstract': 'A woman writes about the issue of marital infidelities and a friends’ foursome.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/fashion/questions-on-social-situations-social-qs.html', 'snippet': 'A woman writes about the issue of marital infidelities and a friends’ foursome.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My husband and I have been good friends with another couple for several years. We’ve vacationed together and have dinner several times a month. Recently, the other husband confided in my husband about his marital infidelities. Now my husband is no longer interested in being friends with him. But his wife doesn’t understand why we’re not socializing with them. I want to remain friends with her. What should I say? Anonymous', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/02/21/fashion/21social-1/21social-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/02/21/fashion/21social-1/21social-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Be a Foul-Weather Friend', 'kicker': 'Social Q’s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Be a Foul-Weather Friend', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T21:06:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c957af65-91db-523f-bf42-f3044fbd54d5', 'word_count': 786, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c957af65-91db-523f-bf42-f3044fbd54d5'}, {'abstract': 'The Arsenale’s onslaught of competing sensibilities and artistic missions can overwhelm.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/venice-biennale-the-enormity-of-the-beast/', 'snippet': 'The Arsenale’s onslaught of competing sensibilities and artistic missions can overwhelm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'And now the fun begins. I spent the second day of press previews for the Venice Biennale in the Arsenale, a narrow gauntlet of art compared to the Giardini, with its discreet national pavilions and semblance of order. Here “ILLUMInations,” as the Biennale exhibition is called — having started in the spacious galleries of the Giardini’s Central Pavilion — becomes a kind of extended scrum, spliced with additional national pavilions. The impossible enormity of the Biennale beast begins to sink in. With all the additional pavilions scattered about town and the independent exhibitions that are out there, too, Venice currently has more contemporary art on offer than any one person can see, even without the usual considerations of time, money and eye-strain.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Venice Biennale: The Enormity of the Beast', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venice Biennale', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marclay, Christian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Song Dong', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turrell, James', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-02T21:07:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'mi