Maria Speck. (Ten Speed Press, $29.99.) Mediterranean whole-grain recipes for barley, farro, kamut, polenta, wheat berries and more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '25 More Cookbooks', 'kicker': 'Web Extra', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '25 More Cookbooks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T00:28:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/aee342e0-1005-5e49-a8b2-dc8ed3a7093e', 'word_count': 802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aee342e0-1005-5e49-a8b2-dc8ed3a7093e'}, {'abstract': 'In the age of Google, it’s not that hard to find a photograph of of The Times’s restaurant critic — but we’re not about to make Sam Sifton’s day job any more difficult by printing one here.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/up-front-sam-sifton.html', 'snippet': 'In the age of Google, it’s not that hard to find a photograph of of The Times’s restaurant critic — but we’re not about to make Sam Sifton’s day job any more difficult by printing one here.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the age of Google, it’s not that hard to find a photograph of Sam Sifton — but we’re not about to make his day job, as the restaurant critic of The New York Times, any more difficult by printing one here. (Besides, he tells us he’s taller now than in any extant picture, with much longer hair.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Sam Sifton', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sifton, Sam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T00:28:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4751180d-fc2c-5aa9-a8d6-3fc2073220ed', 'word_count': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4751180d-fc2c-5aa9-a8d6-3fc2073220ed'}, {'abstract': 'Seven more notable new gardening books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/additional-gardening-books.html', 'snippet': 'Seven more notable new gardening books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Inspired by Seattle Tilth, a community of “learning gardens,” Lisa Taylor’s YOUR FARM IN THE CITY: An Urban Dweller’s Guide to Growing Food and Raising Animals (Black Dog & Leventhal, paper, $18.95) includes helpful plant profiles and useful design ideas. For straightforward and lovely kitchen garden designs, along with helpful tips for companion plantings, consult Ellen Ecker Ogden’s COMPLETE KITCHEN GARDEN (Stewart, Tabori & Chang, paper, $24.95). Graham Kerr, of “Galloping Gourmet” fame, makes the most mouth-watering garden-to-table connection with simple, unpretentious recipes in GROWING AT THE SPEED OF LIFE: A Year in the Life of My First Kitchen Garden (Perigee, $27). And for those who neglected to sow beans in the spring, it’s not too late. With the guidance of the clear, concise instructions in Edward C. Smith’s VEGETABLE GARDENER’S CONTAINER BIBLE: How to Grow a Bounty of Food in Pots, Tubs and Other Containers (Storey, paper, $19.95), you can get a complete kitchen garden going. Just be prepared to do a lot of watering.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Additional Gardening Books', 'kicker': 'Web Extra', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Additional Gardening Books', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T00:28:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/781aaaf3-65e4-5034-9829-417c3834776e', 'word_count': 526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/781aaaf3-65e4-5034-9829-417c3834776e'}, {'abstract': 'More than a dozen new cookbooks, full of fantasy, truth, good meals and bad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-summer-cookbook-roundup.html', 'snippet': 'More than a dozen new cookbooks, full of fantasy, truth, good meals and bad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The lamb shakes rain from his wool, pea tendrils rise from the earth and a new yield of cookbooks arrives on bookstore shelves, ready for the fire. I worked with more than a dozen this spring, cooking like a summertime madman and considering sentences beautiful and lame. I read fantasy and prepared truth, imagined myself cooking in England and France, Mumbai and California, Italy, Spain, North Carolina, Georgia, Vancouver, Seoul. I made beautiful meals and terrible ones, found myself well instructed, poorly instructed, often coddled, sometimes lied to, perpetually amused. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Cooking-t_CA0/bkr-Cooking-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 943, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Cooking-t_CA0/bkr-Cooking-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Cooking-t_CA0/bkr-Cooking-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cookbooks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cooking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T00:28:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e6cba78-014f-5e51-9e88-40df4ff5b13c', 'word_count': 2443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e6cba78-014f-5e51-9e88-40df4ff5b13c'}, {'abstract': 'A bumper crop of new gardening books that make a good case for the simple joy of growing things.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-summer-gardening-roundup.html', 'snippet': 'A bumper crop of new gardening books that make a good case for the simple joy of growing things.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The garden book jury has returned a verdict. You are either growing vegetables or you have become one. Attitude looms as large as artichokes. Unfortunately, the important book on how exactly to overhaul farms too big to fail has yet to be written — mainly because the standard wisdom is that they are, by definition, beyond hope of sustainability. But with over 300 million people in the United States, and with the global population approaching seven billion, most people will continue to depend for their meals on staggeringly large-scale agriculture. What’s to be done?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Gardening-t_CA0/bkr-Gardening-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 945, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Gardening-t_CA0/bkr-Gardening-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Gardening-t_CA0/bkr-Gardening-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gardening Books', 'kicker': 'Summer Reading', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gardening', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Local Food', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vegetables', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bees', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T00:28:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dominique Browning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dominique', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5afeccfd-80eb-5b14-98bd-1cfaa8b64a0f', 'word_count': 2625, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5afeccfd-80eb-5b14-98bd-1cfaa8b64a0f'}, {'abstract': 'In this season’s travel books, the most resonant journeys are recorded by writers who hit the road to escape failed relationships, broken marriages and dead-end careers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-summer-travel-roundup.html', 'snippet': 'In this season’s travel books, the most resonant journeys are recorded by writers who hit the road to escape failed relationships, broken marriages and dead-end careers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Failed relationships, broken marriages and dead-end careers are among the catalysts that lead this season’s travel writers to hit the road. The escapees range from a middle-aged journalist seeking high-altitude transcendence in the Himalayas to a frustrated poetry scholar in search of beauty among the holy spots of Iran. And then there’s the global nomad torn between a longing for domestic tranquility and a masochistic impulse to throw himself into the world’s “messy heart.” This summer’s selection also includes authors driven by simple journalistic curiosity and academic inquiry, but it’s the more tortured souls whose journeys resonate the most. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Travel-t_CA0/bkr-Travel-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 940, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Travel-t_CA0/bkr-Travel-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Travel-t_CA0/bkr-Travel-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Travel Books', 'kicker': 'Summer Reading', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Travel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T00:28:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Hammer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hammer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/52057bc4-4566-5cf6-ba2a-aa2e496f67d4', 'word_count': 2376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52057bc4-4566-5cf6-ba2a-aa2e496f67d4'}, {'abstract': 'Can there really be no possible alternative to California’s plan to close one-fourth of its state parks?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/fall-of-the-wild/', 'snippet': 'Can there really be no possible alternative to California’s plan to close one-fourth of its state parks?', 'lead_paragraph': 'GLEN ELLEN, Calif. — Dead at the age of 40, Jack London left behind more than 50 books produced by his fevered pen, a string of opinionated lovers a