Parenthood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/opinion/03fri1.html', 'snippet': 'Federal officials are right to block the Republican drive against Planned Parenthood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Obama administration has rightly decided to reject a mean-spirited and dangerous Indiana law banning the use of Medicaid funds at Planned Parenthood clinics, which provide vital health services to low-income women. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/opinion/03fri1_art/03fri1_art-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/opinion/03fri1_art/03fri1_art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/03/opinion/03fri1_art/03fri1_art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When States Punish Women', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When States Punish Women: Federal officials are right to block the Republican drive against Planned Parenthood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Planned Parenthood Federation of America', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daniels, Mitchell E Jr', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berwick, Donald M', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indiana', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T01:33:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d8dd913-5a40-5775-8eb8-1b8f17190512', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d8dd913-5a40-5775-8eb8-1b8f17190512'}, {'abstract': 'A Chilean judge has opened an investigation into the death of the country’s revered poet, Pablo Neruda, responding to allegations that he may have been poisoned just days after the 1973 coup, officials said Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/world/americas/03briefs-Chile.html', 'snippet': 'A Chilean judge has opened an investigation into the death of the country’s revered poet, Pablo Neruda, responding to allegations that he may have been poisoned just days after the 1973 coup, officials said Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Chilean judge has opened an investigation into the death of the country’s revered poet, Pablo Neruda, responding to allegations that he may have been poisoned just days after the 1973 coup, officials said Thursday. Mr. Neruda officially died of prostate cancer at 69 on Sept. 23, 1973, 12 days after the coup that toppled the government of his close friend, President Salvador Allende. But several witnesses, including Mr. Neruda’s longtime associate and driver, Manuel Araya, have raised questions about his death. Mr. Araya has said the poet was poisoned by agents of Chile’s dictator, Gen. Augusto Pinochet. The Pablo Neruda Foundation, which administers the poet’s estate, said there was no evidence to support Mr. Araya’s claim. Eduardo Contreras, a lawyer for the Communist Party, which requested the investigation, said that lawyers expect to request the exhumation of Mr. Neruda’s body.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/world/03briefs-neruda/03briefs-neruda-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 405, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chile: Inquiry Ordered in Poet’s Death', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chile: Inquiry Ordered in Poet’s Death', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chile', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Neruda, Pablo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Coups D'Etat and Attempted Coups D'Etat", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T01:36:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexei Barrionuevo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexei', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barrionuevo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/23659db9-c189-59c9-af15-591c5f895111', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23659db9-c189-59c9-af15-591c5f895111'}, {'abstract': 'The justices upheld a ban on corporate donations to candidates. Now Judge Cacheris can.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/opinion/03fri2.html', 'snippet': 'The justices upheld a ban on corporate donations to candidates. Now Judge Cacheris can.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a rare day that a federal judge decides to revisit a decision the week after making it or that the Justice Department apologizes for failing to brief him on the crucial precedent. A re-do is clearly called for.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'About That Precedent', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'About That Precedent: The justices upheld a ban on corporate donations to candidates; now Judge Cacheris can', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizens United v Federal Election Commission (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cacheris, James C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T01:36:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5213c11-e313-59df-a65b-563f6494dc76', 'word_count': 403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5213c11-e313-59df-a65b-563f6494dc76'}, {'abstract': 'The federal government will soon begin a new phase in its effort to clean the Grand Calumet River, a huge undertaking considering that the river is one of the nation’s most polluted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/us/03cncriver.html', 'snippet': 'The federal government will soon begin a new phase in its effort to clean the Grand Calumet River, a huge undertaking considering that the river is one of the nation’s most polluted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thomas Simon remembers the day he found a carp in the Grand Calumet River in 1985, barely alive, bloody and with no fins. “It looked like someone had beaten it up,” said Mr. Simon, a biologist who studied the river for 26 years.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/us/03CNCRIVER2/03CNCRIVER2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/us/03CNCRIVER2/03CNCRIVER2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/03/us/03CNCRIVER2/03CNCRIVER2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Toxic River Improves, but Still Has Far to Go', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Toxic River Improves, but Still Has Far to Go', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T01:41:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kari Lydersen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lydersen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b6f203f-6801-58d6-af0e-6054c5c2262a', 'word_count': 1103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b6f203f-6801-58d6-af0e-6054c5c2262a'}, {'abstract': 'The Libyan woman whose mistreatment by the government of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi made her a symbol of its brutality has been deported against her will to Benghazi.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/world/africa/03briefs-Libya.html', 'snippet': 'The Libyan woman whose mistreatment by the government of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi made her a symbol of its brutality has been deported against her will to Benghazi.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Libyan woman whose mistreatment by the government of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi made her a symbol of its brutality has been deported against her will from her temporary refuge in Qatar to the Libyan rebel stronghold of Benghazi. Eman al-Obeidi gained international attention in March when she told foreign journalists that she had been abducted and gang raped by Qaddafi militia. She was arrested, and later smuggled out of the country by a defecting soldier. She was in Qatar awaiting asylum elsewhere. A spokesman for the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees said she was deported Thursday by force and without explanation. A spokesman for the rebel authority in Benghazi said she was free to leave if she wanted. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Libya: Qatar Deports Dissident Woman', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Libya: Qatar Deports Dissident Woman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deportation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T01:42:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/105a69d5-2c90-545c-b66c-ab66671be577', 'word_count': 119, 'uri': 'nyt://article/105a69d5-2c90-545c-b66c-ab66671be577'}, {'abstract': 'Why using “human capital contracts” to finance higher education isn’t as scary as it sounds.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/02/a-way-to-pay-for-college-with-dividends/', 'snippet': 'Why using “human capital contracts” to finance higher education isn’t as scary as it sounds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you were a student looking for financing to pursue a degree in social science, would you accept an offer of $16,000, in exchange for paying 4.5 percent of your income for 10 years after you graduate?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Way to Pay for College, With Dividends', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T01:43:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fb168e8-942f-525c-8fee-b850e5f64fd1', 'word_count': 1669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fb168e8-942f-525c-8fee-b850e5f64fd1'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the most important antibiotics ever developed should be used to treat sick humans, not to promote growth among farm animals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/opinion/03fri3.html', 'snippet': 'Some of the most important antibiotics ever developed should be used to treat sick humans, not to promote growth among farm animals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The point of factory farming is cheap meat, made possible by confining large numbers of animals in small spaces. Perhaps the greatest hidden cost is its potential effect on human health.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The High Cost of Cheap Meat', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The High Cost of Cheap Meat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Natural Resources Defense Council', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factory Farming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIBIOTICS', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T01:43:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b100246-43aa-518a-9ef3-aa1034dbe753', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b100246-43aa-518a-9ef3-aa1034dbe753'}, {'abstract': 'After asking sacrifices from the citizens of New Jersey, Gov. Chris Christie was using a State Police helicopter for nonstate business.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/opinion/03fri4.html', 'snippet': 'After asking sacrifices from the citizens of New Jersey, Gov. Chris Christie was using a State Police helicopter for nonstate business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey means it when he says that he isn’t running for president. No thinking presidential candidate — especially a Republican who wants to be known for ruthlessly cutting his state budget — would take a taxpayer-financed helicopter to his son’s baseball games. Nor would he use the State Police helicopter to rush to a meeting with Iowa business executives trying to recruit him to run for president.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking New Jersey for a Ride', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking New Jersey for a Ride', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Helicopters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T01:45:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ee6cbc5-b471-5919-a907-a697b69aa3f1', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ee6cbc5-b471-5919-a907-a697b69aa3f1'}, {'abstract': 'Russia’s investigative committee announced that it had enough evidence to prove that Rustam Makhmudov, a fugitive arrested in Chechnya on Tuesday, was hired to kill the journalist Anna Politkovskaya.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/world/europe/03briefs-Russia.html', 'snippet': 'Russia’s investigative committee announced that it had enough evidence to prove that Rustam Makhmudov, a fugitive arrested in Chechnya on Tuesday, was hired to kill the journalist Anna Politkovskaya.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Russia’s investigative committee announced Thursday that it had enough evidence to prove that Rustam Makhmudov, a fugitive arrested in Chechnya on Tuesday, was hired to kill the journalist Anna Politkovskaya. He has been charged with her 2006 murder, and with kidnapping and extortion related to cases from the 1990s, prosecutors said. Murad Musayev, a lawyer who has represented Mr. Makhmudov’s family, said this week that Mr. Makhmudov had nothing to do with the crime and has long sought to turn himself in and cooperate with investigators. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Charges in Reporter’s Killing', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: Charges in Reporter’s Killing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Politkovskaya, Anna', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T01:46:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ellen Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ce30f95-7ced-5a2e-a17d-b56804ba857d', 'word_count': 88, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ce30f95-7ced-5a2e-a17d-b56804ba857d'}, {'abstract': 'Members of the commissioner’s office were said to have spoken with the Yankees and were satisfied that Yuri Sucart, who was implicated by Alex Rodriguez as his steroid provider, was not in violation of restrictions that were imposed two years ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/sports/baseball/yankees-say-rodriguezs-cousin-has-not-violated-restrictions.html', 'snippet': 'Members of the commissioner’s office were said to have spoken with the Yankees and were satisfied that Yuri Sucart, who was implicated by Alex Rodriguez as his steroid provider, was not in violation of restrictions that were imposed two years ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hardly anyone knew who Yuri Sucart was until February 2009, when Alex Rodriguez implicated an unnamed cousin as his steroid provider and that cousin was subsequently identified as Sucart.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yankees Say Rodriguez’s Cousin Has Not Violated Restrictions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yankees Say Rodriguez’s Cousin Has Not Violated Restrictions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodriguez, Alex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sucart, Yuri', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Steroids', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T01:47:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/105e1269-1a07-5db2-a7e1-cf8b5f184413', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/105e1269-1a07-5db2-a7e1-cf8b5f184413'}, {'abstract': 'Removing baskets at three intersections has supporters, but some merchants complain people are tossing soda cans on the sidewalk near them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/nyregion/brooklyn-tries-fighting-trash-by-removing-baskets.html', 'snippet': 'Removing baskets at three intersections has supporters, but some merchants complain people are tossing soda cans on the sidewalk near them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New Yorkers angry about trash on their sidewalk