lue': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T02:26:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bunkley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bunkley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f136ddf8-e9a9-59ba-a463-cf6b66dd0ec6', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f136ddf8-e9a9-59ba-a463-cf6b66dd0ec6'}, {'abstract': 'Civil rights groups filed a class-action suit on Thursday against a new Georgia law that allows the police to inquire about the immigration status of anyone they stop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/us/03brfs-Georgia.html', 'snippet': 'Civil rights groups filed a class-action suit on Thursday against a new Georgia law that allows the police to inquire about the immigration status of anyone they stop.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Several civil rights groups filed a class-action lawsuit on Thursday against a new Georgia law that authorizes the police to inquire about the immigration status of anyone they stop and in practice requires everyone in the state, including United States citizens, to carry documents verifying their status. The federal suit was brought by the American Civil Liberties Union and the Southern Poverty Law Center, among other organizations, against the law, which is scheduled to take effect July 1. One plaintiff, Paul Bridges, a Republican who is mayor of the small farming town of Uvalda, said he feared he could be criminally prosecuted for transporting residents who are illegal immigrants to the doctor or the grocery store. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Georgia: Suit Challenges Immigration Law', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | SOUTH', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Georgia: Suit Challenges Immigration Law', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights and Liberties', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T02:26:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/92e1af48-1cbe-5182-9307-4c960aef4840', 'word_count': 116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92e1af48-1cbe-5182-9307-4c960aef4840'}, {'abstract': 'The first of the Wal-Mart Express stores, scaled down from the chain’s typical superstore format, is to open next week in Gentry, Ark.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/business/03walmart.html', 'snippet': 'The first of the Wal-Mart Express stores, scaled down from the chain’s typical superstore format, is to open next week in Gentry, Ark.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GENTRY, Ark. — The neighborhood grocer could soon face some competition.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wal-Mart to Test Small Store Format', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wal-Mart, Seeing a Niche, Introduces Small-Store Format', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wal-Mart Stores Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T02:31:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name'