432-145f-5416-8f37-f09060017381', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1588432-145f-5416-8f37-f09060017381'}, {'abstract': 'A look back at a pivotal moment in an East Village park that became the epicenter of clashes between the police and residents.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/a-turning-point-for-the-east-village-20-years-later/', 'snippet': 'A look back at a pivotal moment in an East Village park that became the epicenter of clashes between the police and residents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the city of New York built a band shell near the southern end of Tompkins Square Park in 1966, it was quickly embraced by people who began using it for concerts, rallies and poetry readings. Twenty-five years later, after police and protesters battled during a Memorial Day concert in the park, city officials closed it for renovations that lasted more than a year and included the destruction of the band shell. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Turning Point for the East Village, 20 Years Later', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Village (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tompkins Square Park (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T11:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1f1493e-9931-506a-b810-a47d9ac1d18b', 'word_count': 868, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1f1493e-9931-506a-b810-a47d9ac1d18b'}, {'abstract': 'I knew things had to change. I also knew that there was something fundamentally wrong with my business: me.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/reaching-your-limit-as-a-business-owner/', 'snippet': 'I knew things had to change. I also knew that there was something fundamentally wrong with my business: me.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I remember the day I reached my limit with the retail coffee and espresso business that my husband and I started in 2003. When we started our little food service venture, it was supposed to be a fun project that would eventually run itself and act as an additional source of income for our household. But after three years, two locations and countless hours, it became evident that our plan was easier said than done.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reaching Your Limit as a Business Owner', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T11:00:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barbara Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barbara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec86b450-3789-5d1d-ab40-bbd50b4e7014', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec86b450-3789-5d1d-ab40-bbd50b4e7014'}, {'abstract': 'The Tunisian authorities have recovered the bodies of 150 refugees, mostly Africans, who drowned after their boat foundered this week in the Mediterranean Sea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/04/world/africa/04refugees.html', 'snippet': 'The Tunisian authorities have recovered the bodies of 150 refugees, mostly Africans, who drowned after their boat foundered this week in the Mediterranean Sea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — The Tunisian authorities have recovered the bodies of 150 refugees, mostly sub-Saharan Africans, who drowned after their boat foundered this week in the Mediterranean Sea, a United Nations official said Friday, but up to 120 refugees were still missing. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bodies Recovered After Migrants’ Boat Founders', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Africa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tunisia: 150 Bodies Recovered In Shipwreck of Refugees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T11:42:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Sayare and J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sayare', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e20dfc2-b9ae-5871-a3d3-5a54d36fbc2e', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e20dfc2-b9ae-5871-a3d3-5a54d36fbc2e'}, {'abstract': 'Live analysis of the men’s semifinals between Rafael Nadal and Andy Murray', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/live-soon-nadal-vs-murray/', 'snippet': 'Live analysis of the men’s semifinals between Rafael Nadal and Andy Murray', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Rafael Nadal of Spain defeated Andy Murray of Great Britain, 6-4, 7-5, 6-4, to reach the final round of the French Open Friday, giving him a chance to win a sixth crown at Roland Garros.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nadal Beats Murray to Reach Finals', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T11:59:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c0892eb-9cba-58d7-8470-27d850fa127e', 'word_count': 2802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c0892eb-9cba-58d7-8470-27d850fa127e'}, {'abstract': 'Local governments are struggling to maintain community services for the elderly, according to a report released Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/good-news-and-bad-on-community-services/', 'snippet': 'Local governments are struggling to maintain community services for the elderly, according to a report released Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Five years ago, the first edition of “The Maturing of America,” a report undertaken by the National Association of Area Agencies on Aging with groups representing local governments and city planners, found that most communities hadn’t begun to plan for the tidal shifts of the coming years. The proportion of people older than 65 is expected to swell from 13 percent of the nation’s population today to nearly 20 percent by 2030.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Good News, and Bad, About Community Services', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1769edb1-893f-5085-b868-06ce86a00b5c', 'word_count': 541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1769edb1-893f-5085-b868-06ce86a00b5c'}, {'abstract': 'Racing the clock to figure out why a woman is losing control of her muscles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/guillain-barre-syndrome-creeping-paralysis.html', 'snippet': 'Racing the clock to figure out why a woman is losing control of her muscles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SYMPTOMSA 50-year-old woman came to the emergency room at St. Mary’s Hospital in Waterbury, Conn., with severe tooth pain. After several hours she was sent home with a painkiller and an antibiotic to treat a presumed dental abscess. The pills didn’t help. Two days later, the woman awoke in excruciating pain. It felt as if a knife were being plunged into her tooth, her jaw, her face. She called her son, but as soon as she began to tell him how terrible the pain was, he cut her off: “Ma, why are you talking like that?” Her words were slurred, as if she were having a hard time getting them out of her mouth. Hang up, he told her; then he called 911.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/03/22/magazine/diagnosis-well-promo/diagnosis-well-promo-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 190, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/03/22/magazine/diagnosis-well-promo/diagnosis-well-promo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/03/22/magazine/diagnosis-well-promo/diagnosis-well-promo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Woman With Creeping Paralysis', 'kicker': 'Diagnosis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CREEPING PARALYSIS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Sanders, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanders', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11a53059-1e45-5216-ac03-ffb7cc8bd48d', 'word_count': 1418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11a53059-1e45-5216-ac03-ffb7cc8bd48d'}, {'abstract': '“‘Seven Years In Tibet’ flew by; felt like no more than six, tops.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/five-ku-short-poems-on-the-films-of-brad-pitt.html', 'snippet': '“‘Seven Years In Tibet’ flew by; felt like no more than six, tops.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“Thelma and Louise”: Your abs alone deserved aStar on Walk of Fame. Moral of “Se7en”:When a psycho sends FedExNever sign for it.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '51', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Five-Ku: Short Poems on the Films of Brad Pitt', 'kicker': 'Bonus Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'FIVE-KU: Short Poems on the Films of Brad Pitt, Currently Appearing in “Tree of Life”', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pitt, Brad', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67161977-f8ee-50b1-9557-3637fbb343af', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67161977-f8ee-50b1-9557-3637fbb343af'}, {'abstract': 'Geopolitics spill onto the pitch whenever two of the most bitter rivals meet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/in-israel-a-soccer-game-reflects-a-divide.html', 'snippet': 'Geopolitics spill onto the pitch whenever two of the most bitter rivals meet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s 10 minutes before game time, and the police lined up outside the entrances of Doha Stadium aren’t letting anyone in. If Bnei Sakhnin, the only Arab-Israeli team in Israel’s first division, loses today, the club will drop to the second division, an ignominious distinction, the equivalent of going from the major to minor leagues in baseball. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05yourhere1/mag-05Here-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05yourhere1/mag-05Here-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 408}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05yourhere1/mag-05Here-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05yourhere1/mag-05Here-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05yourhere1/mag-05Here-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Israel, a Soccer Game Reflects a Divide', 'kicker': 'You Are Here', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE ONE-GOAL SOLUTION', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Etgar Keret', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Etgar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keret', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8052b807-1997-59ba-8d70-59039e19367a', 'word_count': 821, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8052b807-1997-59ba-8d70-59039e19367a'}, {'abstract': '“Friday Night Lights” is a TV show about teenagers, high school and teamwork. So is the musical dramedy. But only one of these shows became a huge hit. And not the one that deserved to be.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/how-football-players-got-trounced-by-glee.html', 'snippet': '“Friday Night Lights” is a TV show about teenagers, high school and teamwork. So is the musical dramedy. But only one of these shows became a huge hit. And not the one that deserved to be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You can’t blame NBC for thinking that it had a winner on its hands when “Friday Night Lights” had its premiere in 2006. The football drama translated the nostalgia and longing of small-town existence into moments of surprising sweetness and grace, while embracing dramatic improvisation and documentary-style camera work that placed this naturally humble tale at the stylistic forefront of TV. And it did this in the service of its central story, which, along with dissecting the harsh continual sorting of winner from loser in American life, thoughtfully explored 1) the little triumphs and defeats of high school; 2) the perils of striving for an egalitarian marriage; 3) the trials of young love; 4) the charms and flaws of a small town; 4) the charms and flaws of people who live in small towns; and 5) the uncertain futures of young people with big (and often delusional) dreams.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05riff_span/mag-05Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 110, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05riff_span/mag-05Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 110}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05riff_span/mag-05Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 209, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05riff_span/mag-05Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05riff_span/mag-05Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Football Players Got Trounced by ‘Glee’', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘A Violent Burst of Sequins and Jazz Hands’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Havrilesky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Havrilesky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e2a8dda-afe6-53ad-8a04-ab6928e3629e', 'word_count': 1859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e2a8dda-afe6-53ad-8a04-ab6928e3629e'}, {'abstract': 'Let me take off my tinfoil hat for a moment to discuss why otherwise-smart people fall for crackpot theories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/a-theory-of-conspiracy-theories.html', 'snippet': 'Let me take off my tinfoil hat for a moment to discuss why otherwise-smart people fall for crackpot theories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dear Mr. Keller: Last night on the “PBS NewsHour,” they had a story about some Los Angeles Times reporters who uncovered corruption in a nearby, small city in California. The newspaper eventually received a Pulitzer Prize. That is what you can have if you will talk with me. Within one hour, I will convince you and your staff that Lee Oswald did not assassinate President Kennedy. Then, I will give you the evidence for the real killers, and how the cover up could be perpetrated. It is a great story, fully documented and supported with facts, many from the Warren Commission itself!!', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lede/05lede-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 275, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lede/05lede-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 275}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lede/mag-05lede-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 283, 'width': 618, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lede/mag-05lede-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lede/mag-05lede-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Theory of Conspiracy Theories', 'kicker': 'First', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'LET ME TAKE OFF MY TINFOIL HAT FOR A MOMENT', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d347a9eb-2f18-5d9d-a958-3746c5cd50bb', 'word_count': 1185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d347a9eb-2f18-5d9d-a958-3746c5cd50bb'}, {'abstract': 'Frank’s fourth and final wife fights for the last word on their relationship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/barbara-sinatras-way.html', 'snippet': 'Frank’s fourth and final wife fights for the last word on their relationship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In your new book, “Lady Blue Eyes,” you describe one of your earliest visits to Frank Sinatra’s Palm Springs home, when you were still married to his neighbor, Zeppo Marx. You were keeping time for a charades game, and when you called time on Sinatra, he grabbed a clock from your hands and smashed it against a door. You never thought, Wow, getting involved with this man might be dangerous?  I never felt danger around him. He was always very much a gentleman, and he really cared about treating me well. He came from Hoboken, where the streets were very tough. I guess some of that came back when he was drinking.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05talk/mag-05talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 580, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05talk/mag-05talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05talk/mag-05talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Barbara Sinatra’s Way', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'BARBARA SINATRA’S WAY', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sinatra, Frank', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38f5b91d-c2e5-5609-af6a-65e1fded8cd9', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38f5b91d-c2e5-5609-af6a-65e1fded8cd9'}, {'abstract': 'College economics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/the-ethicist-lesson-lent.html', 'snippet': 'College economics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My daughter-in-law’s parents “forgot” to make payments on the $17,000 loan they took out for her college 14 years ago, when she was 19. Now they owe $40,000, and their tax refunds and Social Security are being garnished. Her parents are of limited means. My son is not, but he and my daughter-in-law believe that this is a problem of her parents’ own making (through mismanagement of money, gambling and not working as much as they could) and the debt is their responsibility. What do you think? J.G., BOCA RATON, FLA.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lesson Lent', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'LESSON LENT', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/70b48afa-0a56-525b-82f6-f6a63178a449', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70b48afa-0a56-525b-82f6-f6a63178a449'}, {'abstract': 'Indie rocker Justin Vernon has tasted megastardom. Which was nice. But he’d rather be in Wisconsin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/who-what-and-where-is-bon-iver.html', 'snippet': 'Indie rocker Justin Vernon has tasted megastardom. Which was nice. But he’d rather be in Wisconsin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even by Kanye West standards, the Bowery Ballroom show last November was a spectacle. West had chosen the tiny club for a surprise concert, celebrating the release of “My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy,” and it didn’t take long for the scrum outside to get ugly. Having tickets, which went on sale that day and sold out instantly, didn’t guarantee entry. The line stretched around the corner, and for most people, that’s as far as they got. Even those who made it inside — rappers, models, record-company executives, Diddy, Spike Lee, ordinary fans with the good fortune to have shown up very early — had to endure an hours-long wait.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05Iver/mag-05Iver-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 844, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05Iver/05Iver-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05Iver/05Iver-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who, What and Where is Bon Iver?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "KANYE'S BOY IN EAU CLAIRE", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vernon, Justin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6af3baa9-7318-5fb6-9941-cce084a333e9', 'word_count': 2947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6af3baa9-7318-5fb6-9941-cce084a333e9'}, {'abstract': 'Approaching the singles scene statistically.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/nate-silver-wednesday-night-is-right-for-loving.html', 'snippet': 'Approaching the singles scene statistically.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Love is blind. Of course it is. Still, there’s no reason to believe that there aren’t legitimate, data-driven ways to improve your dating life. So I recently joined up with Christian Rudder of OkCupid, a matchmaking site that takes a playful attitude toward the data it collects (like which camera takes the most successful profile photos). I asked Rudder if there was a way to determine which days of the week — and which times of day — are the best to meet someone at a bar. What he came up with is something called the “sexual availability index.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05-SubversionChart/05-SubversionChart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05-SubversionChart/05-SubversionChart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05-SubversionChart/05-SubversionChart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wednesday Night Is All Right for Loving', 'kicker': 'Acts of Mild Subversion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'WEDNESDAY NIGHT IS ALL RIGHT FOR LOVING', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Single Persons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a8fe5f6-4b7c-59f9-a03f-5a14c8d0b865', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a8fe5f6-4b7c-59f9-a03f-5a14c8d0b865'}, {'abstract': 'Is there anything about you that’s really worth insuring?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/lives-whole-life-premium.html', 'snippet': 'Is there anything about you that’s really worth insuring?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lacy, the auto-insurance agent, transfers me. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '62', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lives/05lives-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 756, 'width': 1021, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lives/mag-05lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Whole-Life Premium', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Whole-Life Premium', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insurance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brendan Tapley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brendan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tapley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc49c028-1a91-5c28-a742-2fe39094fd97', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc49c028-1a91-5c28-a742-2fe39094fd97'}, {'abstract': 'A 100-year old technology that is essential to modern life is about to be snuffed out. Yikes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/magazine/bulb-in-bulb-out.html', 'snippet': 'A 100-year old technology that is essential to modern life is about to be snuffed out. Yikes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past few years, in conditions of strict secrecy, a multinational team of scientists has been making a mighty effort to change the light bulb. The prototype they’ve developed is four inches tall, with a familiar tapered shape, and unlighted, it resembles a neon yellow mushroom. Screw it in and switch it on, though, and it blazes with a voluptuous radiance. It represents what people within the lighting industry often call their holy grail, an invention that reproduces the soft luminance of the incandescent bulb — Thomas Edison’s century-old technology — but conforms to much higher standards of energy efficiency and durability. The prototype is supposed to last for more than 22 years, maybe as long as you own your house, so you won’t need to stock up at the supermarket. And that’s fortunate, because one day very soon, traditional incandescent bulbs won’t be available in stores anymore. They’re about to be effectively outlawed.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lightbulb_span/mag-05lightbulb-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lightbulb_span/mag-05lightbulb-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lightbulb_span/mag-05lightbulb-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 633, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lightbulb_span/mag-05lightbulb-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/magazine/05lightbulb_span/mag-05lightbulb-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bulb In, Bulb Out', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bulb In Bulb Out', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light Bulbs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Compact Fluorescent Light Bulbs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lighting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rice', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rice', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9a01a25-3674-5f6c-a760-2be30141f84a', 'word_count': 2783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9a01a25-3674-5f6c-a760-2be30141f84a'}, {'abstract': 'The Times’s chief film critics discuss the lingering bias against movies that aspire to more than entertainment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/movies/films-in-defense-of-slow-and-boring.html', 'snippet': 'The Times’s chief film critics discuss the lingering bias against movies that aspire to more than entertainment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHAT is boring? This question was inspired by a piece in the May 1 edition of The New York Times Magazine by Dan Kois that offered a cheerful conformist’s take on what in certain circles is sometimes termed slow cinema and that he simply finds boring, the equivalent of eating his “cultural vegetables.” Mr. Kois writes that he knows he’s supposed to embrace celebrated films that he variously describes as “slow-moving, meditative” and “stately, austere” and “deliberately paced.” But he can’t, won’t, doesn’t like or understand them. In this he empathizes with his 6-year-old daughter, Lyra, who, at a friend’s urging, tuned into “Phineas and Ferb,” a TV show that she doesn’t fully get but watches “aspirationally, as a sort of challenge to herself.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/CRITICS-4/CRITICS-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/CRITICS-4/CRITICS-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/CRITICS-4/CRITICS-4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 745, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/CRITICS-4/CRITICS-4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/CRITICS-4/CRITICS-4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Defense of the Slow and the Boring', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Defense Of the Slow And the Boring', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reichardt, Kelly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Godard, Jean-Luc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tarkovsky, Andrei', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T12:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis and A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95633a5d-7d2d-5be3-aa8e-69ffad6f5c81', 'word_count': 1734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95633a5d-7d2d-5be3-aa8e-69ffad6f5c81'}, {'abstract': 'Guy Laliberté, the chief executive of Cirque du Soleil, prepares to open three huge shows before the end of the year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/theater/guy-laliberte-guides-cirque-du-soleil.html', 'snippet': 'Guy Laliberté, the chief executive of Cirque du Soleil, prepares to open three huge shows before the end of the year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTREAL — With a wolfish grin, mangled pinky and a bald head shaped like a