', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Early Celebration Leaves the Heat With a Game 2 Hangover', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bosh, Chris', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nowitzki, Dirk', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spoelstra, Erik', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Mavericks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Terry, Jason', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Heat', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, LeBron', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wade, Dwyane', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:04:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Beck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7df4c528-7b84-59ad-b38e-ea761041f377', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7df4c528-7b84-59ad-b38e-ea761041f377'}, {'abstract': 'The price of an unwinding stimulus.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/what-a-drag/', 'snippet': 'The price of an unwinding stimulus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From Goldman Sachs, an estimate of the federal budget impact on growth. The gray line shows the direct impact, the blue line includes the induced “multiplier” effects. Notice that this shows the rate of change, not the level effects. I explained the difference a while back.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What A Drag', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:05:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f04c649-5999-5230-aaa1-c85bac84184a', 'word_count': 80, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f04c649-5999-5230-aaa1-c85bac84184a'}, {'abstract': 'I can haz hi-res?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/the-american-way/', 'snippet': 'I can haz hi-res?', 'lead_paragraph': 'From First Draft:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The American Way', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:10:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a25bc94e-a71e-57ec-a799-d2ecc2010408', 'word_count': 3, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a25bc94e-a71e-57ec-a799-d2ecc2010408'}, {'abstract': 'Eman al-Obeidy, a Libyan woman who said that she was gang-raped by members of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s security forces in March, was expelled from Qatar on Thursday, and arrived in Libya with bruises and scars.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/libyan-dissident-says-she-was-beaten-in-qatar-during-forced-deportation/', 'snippet': 'Eman al-Obeidy, a Libyan woman who said that she was gang-raped by members of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s security forces in March, was expelled from Qatar on Thursday, and arrived in Libya with bruises and scars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As my colleague David Kirkpatrick reported, on Thursday Qatar forcibly deported a Libyan woman whose mistreatment by the government of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi made her a symbol of its brutality.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Libyan Dissident Says She Was Beaten in Qatar During Forced Deportation', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obeidy, Eman al-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations High Commission for Refugees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Qatar', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:21:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0dea731-2eda-5925-9728-83e6297ff725', 'word_count': 347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0dea731-2eda-5925-9728-83e6297ff725'}, {'abstract': 'About those interest rates.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/liquidity-preference-and-loanable-funds-still-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'About those interest rates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than two years have passed since the big debate over the effect of budget deficits on interest rates in a liquidity trap. The original argument of the anti-stimulus types was that large-scale government borrowing would drive up interest rates even in the face of high unemployment, and that this would hurt the economy. At that time the argument was framed in terms of simple crowding out, not solvency. And I argued that the whole thing represented a failure to understand basic macroeconomics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Liquidity Preference and Loanable Funds, Still (Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:22:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/860e30be-da4e-576a-836f-0bfd41df9a2a', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/860e30be-da4e-576a-836f-0bfd41df9a2a'}, {'abstract': 'For our regular Q&A column, which appears both on this blog and in our print section, we want your questions about travel.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/we-want-your-travel-questions/', 'snippet': 'For our regular Q&A column, which appears both on this blog and in our print section, we want your questions about travel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do you have a question about an upcoming trip? Looking for information on where to go? You’ve come to the right place. For our regular Q&A column, which appears both on this blog and in our print section, we want your questions about travel. Post them below, or e-mail them to travmail@nytimes.com. (A tip: be specific, but not too specific. And ask ahead of time — if you are hoping to travel next week, it’s probably too late.) ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'We Want Your Travel Questions', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:24:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6108bbb-38da-5e18-bb39-679e9514a0b3', 'word_count': 79, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6108bbb-38da-5e18-bb39-679e9514a0b3'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/what-were-reading-196/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:26:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d80a0a97-80c3-53c8-9552-b3a698b3f7f5', 'word_count': 347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d80a0a97-80c3-53c8-9552-b3a698b3f7f5'}, {'abstract': 'The decision in the Massey case looks like one that allows management to escape charges of wrongdoing, but the truth is much more complicated, the Deal Professor says.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/the-logic-in-refusing-to-block-masseys-sale/', 'snippet': 'The decision in the Massey case looks like one that allows management to escape charges of wrongdoing, but the truth is much more complicated, the Deal Professor says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stuart Grant, one of the name partners of the law firm Grant & Eisenhofer, is mad.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Logic in Refusing to Block Massey’s Sale', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alpha Natural Resources Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Massey Energy Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Upper Big Branch Mine', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:30:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/834059ca-0c8e-5f5d-ad5a-907f1500b9f9', 'word_count': 1717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/834059ca-0c8e-5f5d-ad5a-907f1500b9f9'}, {'abstract': 'Josh and Raquel Garcia were disqualified from the New York City high school handball tournament because they had entered a competition that awarded cash prizes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/04/nyregion/garcia-twins-banned-from-high-school-handball-tournament.html', 'snippet': 'Josh and Raquel Garcia were disqualified from the New York City high school handball tournament because they had entered a competition that awarded cash prizes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The finer points of street handball, unlike other youth sports, are not learned from camps and coaches and clinics, but from pickup games on hot concrete courts around the city. There is no professional league to aspire to; college scholarships are rare.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/nyregion/04handball_337/04handball_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/04/nyregion/04handball_337/04handball_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 349}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/nyregion/04handball_337/04handball_337-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/nyregion/04handball_337/04handball_337-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/04/nyregion/04handball_337/04handball_337-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Styles of Handball Clash, Two High-School Stars Are Disqualified', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Styles of Handball Clash, Two High-School Stars Are Disqualified', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Handball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Schools Athletic League', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:30:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00215859-ecab-5d02-95c4-46bf1c8a2157', 'word_count': 1069, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00215859-ecab-5d02-95c4-46bf1c8a2157'}, {'abstract': 'A meticulous, tiptoeingly respectful biography of Robert Redford: actor, director and environmental activist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-robert-redford-by-michael-feeney.html', 'snippet': 'A meticulous, tiptoeingly respectful biography of Robert Redford: actor, director and environmental activist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We all like to count among our friends principled people who are serious about their chosen profession and dedicated to working toward the greater good. But do we want to read about them? That’s the uphill battle fought by Michael Feeney Callan in his meticulous, tiptoeingly respectful “Robert Redford: The Biography,” which covers in detail Redford’s four-decade acting career, his emergence as a director and his dedication to environmental causes. Redford is also, of course, the founder of the Sundance Institute, established in 1981 to help independent filmmakers polish their skills and bring their work to a wider audience; in 1985 he expanded the Sundance empire by taking over the United States Film and Video Festival, thereafter known as the Sundance Film Festival.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Zacharek-1/Zacharek-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 807, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Zacharek-1/Zacharek-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Zacharek-1/Zacharek-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert Redford, Mr. Nice Guy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Way He Is', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Redford, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:33:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Zacharek', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zacharek', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e84b2fe-c41c-52d9-8e4b-9aec12f90169', 'word_count': 1061, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e84b2fe-c41c-52d9-8e4b-9aec12f90169'}, {'abstract': 'The most effective section of Mitt Romney’s kick-off speech.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/how-obama-loses/', 'snippet': 'The most effective section of Mitt Romney’s kick-off speech.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I didn’t care much for Mitt Romney’s speech yesterday announcing his presidential bid. It struck me as vintage Romney, by which I mean it resembled a somewhat tin-eared consultant’s idea of the kind of political speech right-wing primary voters want to hear. (Okay, now the anti-European boilerplate: “The President seems to take his inspiration not from the small towns and villages of New Hampshire but from the capitals of Europe.” Now the hyperbole: “We are only inches away from ceasing to be a free market economy.”\xa0 Now the completely non-specific promises to cut spending: “I will cap federal spending at 20% or less of the GDP and finally, finally balance the budget.” Now the clunky one-liner: “You know, if you want to create jobs, it helps to have had a job.” Now wait for the applause …)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Obama Loses', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:34:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e38d3f1-aa14-5b7b-a5a8-ecd004c8eb05', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e38d3f1-aa14-5b7b-a5a8-ecd004c8eb05'}, {'abstract': 'SmartyPig, an online savings bank, has cut its interest rate for the third time in nearly nine months. It now pays 1.1 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/smartypig-cuts-interest-rates-yet-again/', 'snippet': 'SmartyPig, an online savings bank, has cut its interest rate for the third time in nearly nine months. It now pays 1.1 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s official: After cutting its interest rate for the third time in nearly nine months, SmartyPig’s rates are no longer the most attractive in the land of online savings accounts. The spectacled Pig is merely average.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'SmartyPig Cuts Interest Rates Yet Again', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Savings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ING Groep NV', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:35:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/239bc55b-5d7a-560e-b55e-1da3ba0cd09f', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/239bc55b-5d7a-560e-b55e-1da3ba0cd09f'}, {'abstract': 'Benefits include long-term stability, but risks stem from how much cash companies have on hand to pay dividends and how those dividends are taxed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/04/your-money/stocks-and-bonds/04wealth.html', 'snippet': 'Benefits include long-term stability, but risks stem from how much cash companies have on hand to pay dividends and how those dividends are taxed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE first security I was ever aware of was a dividend-paying stock, the AT&T shares that my grandfather, a retired postman, owned when I was little. Those shares along with some other dividend-paying stocks formed the foundation of his nest egg. By reinvesting his dividends, he was able to amass sizable savings for someone who probably never earned more than $20,000 a year.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/your-money/stocks-and-bonds/04wealth-span/04wealth-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/your-money/stocks-and-bonds/04wealth-span/04wealth-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/04/your-money/stocks-and-bonds/04wealth-span/04wealth-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Assessing the Value of Owning Dividend-Paying Stocks', 'kicker': 'Wealth Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Assessing the Value of Owning Dividend-Paying Stocks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Net Worth Individuals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:44:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'subsection_name': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a0809d4-49f2-58f2-90f2-829bf5b5ff78', 'word_count': 1315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a0809d4-49f2-58f2-90f2-829bf5b5ff78'}, {'abstract': 'Memoirs by Dick Van Dyke and Barbara Eden recall a pioneering era of television comedy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-memoirs-by-dick-van-dyke-and-barbara-eden.html', 'snippet': 'Memoirs by Dick Van Dyke and Barbara Eden recall a pioneering era of television comedy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the snowy cathode days before cable TV, let alone niche channels like Nick at Nite or TV Land, misshapen UHF antennas and God-forsaken rabbit ears pulled in, if you were lucky, three networks and a handful of independent stations. The latter broadcast an era-and-mind-scrambling mixture of reruns — a daily delirium wherein “The Honeymooners” might rub shoulders with “The Avengers,” “The Rifleman” was just a shot away from “That Girl,” and a mad parade of castaways, cowboys, talking \xadhorses, detectives, bewitching witches, doctors, avuncular Martians, fugitives and flying nuns followed “Route 66” straight into “The Twilight Zone.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/SUBB-Hampton/SUBB-Hampton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 769, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/SUBB-Hampton/SUBB-Hampton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/SUBB-Hampton/SUBB-Hampton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Dick Van Dyke and Barbara Eden Wrought', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rob and Laura, Tony and Jeannie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eden, Barbara', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Dyke, Dick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:49:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Hampton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hampton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a31320d-83e0-5871-9644-47f7516d3841', 'word_count': 1265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a31320d-83e0-5871-9644-47f7516d3841'}, {'abstract': 'The former senator is accused of conspiring to cover up an affair by “secretly obtaining,” misreporting and misusing contributions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/04/us/politics/04edwards.html', 'snippet': 'The former senator is accused of conspiring to cover up an affair by “secretly obtaining,” misreporting and misusing contributions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Edwards, the former Democratic senator from North Carolina and nominee for vice president in 2004, was once one of his party’s most promising young stars. But on Friday he found himself in a stunning fall from grace — in a courtroom in North Carolina, being read his rights. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/us/JP-EDWARDS-1/JP-EDWARDS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/04/us/JP-EDWARDS-1/JP-EDWARDS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/us/JP-EDWARDS-1/JP-EDWARDS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/us/JP-EDWARDS-1/JP-EDWARDS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/04/us/JP-EDWARDS-1/JP-EDWARDS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Edwards Charged With Election Finance Fraud', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Edwards Charged With Election Finance Fraud', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunter, Rielle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edwards, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2008', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADULTERY', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:55:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f959d2c-ee6c-5ab5-98ef-d1d96b1c6faa', 'word_count': 1104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f959d2c-ee6c-5ab5-98ef-d1d96b1c6faa'}, {'abstract': 'Cary Grant’s daughter celebrates their relationship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-good-stuff-a-reminiscence-of-my-father-cary-grant-by-jennifer-grant.html', 'snippet': 'Cary Grant’s daughter celebrates their relationship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Jennifer Grant was born, in 1966, a 62-year-old Cary Grant retired from movies to concentrate on fatherhood. Following a divorce from Jennifer’s mother, Dyan Cannon, soon after, he began keeping an actual vault of photographs, audiotapes, Super 8 films and meticulously labeled aides-\xadmémoires of and for his only child. Jennifer Grant’s stream-of-consciousness hagiography offers glimpses of this material, along with a torrent of filial piety. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Calhoun/Calhoun-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 607, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Calhoun/Calhoun-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Calhoun/Calhoun-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Daughter Remembers Cary Grant', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Father’s Love', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Cary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Jennifer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T14:57:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ada Calhoun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ada', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calhoun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/42893a52-887a-5ce8-bd3f-70671007c39d', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42893a52-887a-5ce8-bd3f-70671007c39d'}, {'abstract': 'John Edwards, the former senator from North Carolina and presidential candidate, was indicted Friday morning by a grand jury in Raleigh on charges that he violated campaign finance law during his 2008 presidential campaign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/06/04/us/politics/04edwards-text.html', 'snippet': 'John Edwards, the former senator from North Carolina and presidential candidate, was indicted Friday morning by a grand jury in Raleigh on charges that he violated campaign finance law during his 2008 presidential campaign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Edwards, the former senator from North Carolina and presidential candidate, was indicted Friday morning by a grand jury in Raleigh on charges that he violated campaign finance law during his 2008 presidential campaign.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Indictment Shows Six Counts Against Edwards', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edwards, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2008', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunter, Rielle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T15:04:43+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/51e8d523-8eb9-5602-9263-869ba208b70f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/51e8d523-8eb9-5602-9263-869ba208b70f'}, {'abstract': 'Paramount Pictures as seen from on high when the American new wave came in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-infamous-players-by-peter-bart.html', 'snippet': 'Paramount Pictures as seen from on high when the American new wave came in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1967, when the lumbering old Hollywood studios were about to be shaken up by independent, visionary filmmakers, Peter Bart left his job as a New York Times reporter to become Paramount Pictures’ No. 2 production executive. “Infamous Players,” Bart’s breezy, anecdotal account of his eight years at the studio, casts him as the level-headed Sancho Panza to his pal, the production head Robert Evans, who comes across as a risk-taking, eventually cocaine-fueled Don Quixote. Together they led Paramount out of the dark ages of big-budget disasters like “Darling Lili” into the glorious, cutting-edge era of “The Godfather,” “Rosemary’s Baby” and “Chinatown.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-James-t_CA0/bkr-James-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 797, 'width': 769, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-James-t_CA0/bkr-James-t_CA0-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-James-t_CA0/bkr-James-t_CA0-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paramount Pictures, From the Peak', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hollywood Babylonian', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bart, Peter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Evans, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paramount Pictures', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T15:05:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caryn James', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'James', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/481d18b9-007c-56e0-8432-3ba6d3194a60', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/481d18b9-007c-56e0-8432-3ba6d3194a60'}, {'abstract': 'Everybody loves Ryan, except the voters.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/the-commentariat-candidate/', 'snippet': 'Everybody loves Ryan, except the voters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jonathan Chait thinks Paul Ryan will run for president, and that he might well be the Republican nominee. Interesting point. On the surface, it seems crazy: Ryan’s budget plan is, after all, both spectacularly bad policy and spectacularly bad politics. But Ryan may have a secret weapon: despite everything, much of the punditocracy (myself obviously not included) still has a crush on him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Commentariat Candidate', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T15:11:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68014323-cd8b-5b96-bbf0-1e35e4c82a2a', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68014323-cd8b-5b96-bbf0-1e35e4c82a2a'}, {'abstract': 'The increase, to take effect July 1, is to $25 from $20.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/metropolitan-museum-raising-suggested-admission-fee/', 'snippet': 'The increase, to take effect July 1, is to $25 from $20.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art, facing what it calls a “number of daunting, ongoing budgetary challenges,” has decided to raise its suggested admission fee to $25, from $20, where it has been set for the last five years. The change takes effect July 1.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Metropolitan Museum Raising Suggested Admission Fee', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Finances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campbell, Thomas P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T15:22:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b7be49a-3db6-5d4f-9526-f72e8664e078', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b7be49a-3db6-5d4f-9526-f72e8664e078'}, {'abstract': 'Tod Seelie, a Williamsburg photographer, captures some of the city’s most unusual and arresting underground happenings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/nyregion/a-chronicler-of-the-creative-underground.html', 'snippet': 'Tod Seelie, a Williamsburg photographer, captures some of the city’s most unusual and arresting underground happenings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the windy darkness of a recent spring morning, 30 people of an arty, mostly Brooklynite persuasion gathered after midnight for an illicit get-together in a maintenance shed, high atop the Williamsburg Bridge. Billed as the “Third-Annual NYC Undercover, You-Might-Be-Arrested, Clandestine Errantry Trespassing Adventure Party,” the event attracted members of a distinct, risk-taking subset of the New York art world — heights-loving writers, courageous painters, a devil-may-care guitarist, a guy lugging bongos and the Williamsburg photographer, Tod Seelie — all of whom had been quietly invited to the late-night affair by its pseudonymous organizers, Agent Verde and Agent Rojo.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/nyregion/05PHOTOG1/05PHOTOG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/nyregion/05PHOTOG1/05PHOTOG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/nyregion/05PHOTOG1/05PHOTOG1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/nyregion/05PHOTOG1/05PHOTOG1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/nyregion/05PHOTOG1/05PHOTOG1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Chronicler of the Art-Vandal Underground', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chronicler of the Art-Vandal Underground', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Williamsburg (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T15:22:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Feuer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feuer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe067e75-847c-55c2-8b3d-2c436c4e525