 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:44:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/369fb49c-8dbc-595b-84be-47c970b32fb5', 'word_count': 2287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/369fb49c-8dbc-595b-84be-47c970b32fb5'}, {'abstract': 'Prada said in a filing to the Hong Kong stock exchange that it expected to report at least 150.7 million euros in profit for the six months ending July 31.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/prada-predicts-218-million-in-first-half-profit/', 'snippet': 'Prada said in a filing to the Hong Kong stock exchange that it expected to report at least 150.7 million euros in profit for the six months ending July 31.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prada, the Italian fashion house, said Friday in a filing to the Hong Kong stock exchange that it expected to report at least 150.7 million euros, or $218 million, in profit for the six months ending July 31, and announced ambitious growth plans once its issue is completed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prada Predicts $218 Million in First-Half Profit', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PRADA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:44:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76d9f7e6-5448-5add-a873-79c0a106ee6a', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76d9f7e6-5448-5add-a873-79c0a106ee6a'}, {'abstract': 'Courthouse Confidential: A new bar president; employment changes; and a new practice.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/new-state-bar-chief-focuses-on-veterans-issues/', 'snippet': 'Courthouse Confidential: A new bar president; employment changes; and a new practice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometime in the past year, Vincent E. Doyle III was at a charity dinner with his wife to raise money for Iraq war veterans. The veterans needed money for things like modifying their homes. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New State Bar Chief Focuses on Veterans’ Issues', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legal Profession', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DLA Piper', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York State Bar Assn', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:45:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Eligon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2adf8de9-15b6-525b-aaab-4e9a317bda51', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2adf8de9-15b6-525b-aaab-4e9a317bda51'}, {'abstract': 'Two short books on lobster, the food and the creature.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/considering-the-lobster.html', 'snippet': 'Two short books on lobster, the food and the creature.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s nearly impossible to put a live lobster into a pot of boiling water without asking yourself, “Does it feel pain?” In Richard J. King’s elegant little history of the world’s pre-eminent crustacean, he reveals that anguishing over this “final vestige of what it is like to butcher or kill our own food” isn’t restricted to our PETA-\xadsensitized age. Apparently, it’s been debated in the West since at least the early 19th century.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Considering the Lobster', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Creature Comfort', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobsters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:46:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dawn Drzal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dawn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drzal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/16581584-b6f6-5af3-93f5-0f42bf0c2503', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16581584-b6f6-5af3-93f5-0f42bf0c2503'}, {'abstract': 'Essays on boxing by A. J. Liebling, Richard Wright, Joyce Carol Oates, Gay Talese and others survey a world of extreme risk and unique nobility.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-at-the-fights-american-writers-on-boxing.html', 'snippet': 'Essays on boxing by A. J. Liebling, Richard Wright, Joyce Carol Oates, Gay Talese and others survey a world of extreme risk and unique nobility.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, championship boxing is lucky to win half a column in the sports pages. There was a time, though, when things were otherwise. In one of two introductions to “At the Fights,” George Kimball, who edited this anthology with John Schulian, recalls the 1910 fight between Jack Johnson and Jim Jeffries: “More than 600 writers of varying stripes augmented the ringside crowd of 20,000 spectators. . . . Western Union transmitted more than a million words of copy, a record that would stand until Lindbergh touched down . . . 17 years later.” And as indicated by the editors elsewhere in the book, when Jack Dempsey defended his title against Georges Carpentier, the coverage in The New York Times “consumed not just the sports section on July 3, 1921, but almost the entire paper.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Marino/Marino-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 869, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Marino/Marino-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Marino/Marino-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Of Writers and Boxers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Boxing and the Muse', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boxing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schulian, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:47:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gordon Marino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gordon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a989a6e-c7d2-5cf2-8afa-c755e4a83983', 'word_count': 930, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a989a6e-c7d2-5cf2-8afa-c755e4a83983'}, {'abstract': 'The cyclist Robert Penn sets out to assemble a “talismanic machine.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-its-all-about-the-bike-by-robert-penn.html', 'snippet': 'The cyclist Robert Penn sets out to assemble a “talismanic machine.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Evangelizing cyclists will often pronounce, in a moment of populist zeal, something like: “It doesn’t matter what you ride, as long as you ride.” This is a rather oleaginous half-truth spread across a larger reality. It may not matter what you ride, but it matters deeply what I ride. People pick up on this. A Brooklyn bike shop owner recently described to me the almost apologetic air of patrons bringing him bicycles for repair, as if they feared the dusty specimens they had just freed from storage revealed some philistine streak, as if they knew some better bike would complete them.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Vanderbilt-t_CA0/bkr-Vanderbilt-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 994, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Vanderbilt-t_CA0/bkr-Vanderbilt-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Vanderbilt-t_CA0/bkr-Vanderbilt-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Building the Perfect bike', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gearhead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:48:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Vanderbilt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vanderbilt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6347900-6098-58d6-be88-9275fe9f7ebb', 'word_count': 649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6347900-6098-58d6-be88-9275fe9f7ebb'}, {'abstract': 'This biography of the Yankees’ shortstop has only good things to say about him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-the-captain-the-journey-of-derek-jeter-by-ian-oconnor.html', 'snippet': 'This biography of the Yankees’ shortstop has only good things to say about him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Yankee shortstop Derek Jeter is widely revered as a dignified and respectful clutch hitter, a leader by example and an adroit spouter of clichés who has managed to remain scandal-free and child-friendly even during baseball’s steroids era. Ian O’Connor isn’t about to challenge that view, as he makes clear in the introduction to his new hagiography, “The Captain.” When O’Connor writes that he spent 18 months in search of the “tangible explanation for Jeter’s intangible grace” and “the passion behind his pinstripes,” he is more than foreshadowing that he did not unearth Montgomery Burns-like evil within this “biracial golden child.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Sandomir/Sandomir-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 778, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Sandomir/Sandomir-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Sandomir/Sandomir-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'St. Derek Jeter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'St. Derek', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeter, Derek', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Connor, Ian", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:48:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/24551f6e-a585-58fe-8c3a-17453d6e8d89', 'word_count': 1096, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24551f6e-a585-58fe-8c3a-17453d6e8d89'}, {'abstract': 'Short biographies of Joe DiMaggio and Hank Greenberg, two of the most feared hitters in baseball.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/the-glory-of-joe-dimaggio-and-hank-greenberg.html', 'snippet': 'Short biographies of Joe DiMaggio and Hank Greenberg, two of the most feared hitters in baseball.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yale University Press, the pre-eminent academic publishing house, founded in 1908 by George Parmly Day, decided to roll out two books on baseball in the same month of 2011. Who wants to tell the Mayans their calendar is a year off?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Scheft/Scheft-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 808, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Scheft/Scheft-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Scheft/Scheft-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Glory of Joe DiMaggio and Hank Greenberg', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Glory of Their Times', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kurlansky, Mark', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenberg, Hank', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Charyn, Jerome', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DiMaggio, Joe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:48:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Scheft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scheft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/55ed355d-fbb1-5fcc-bde7-51e7d3e3a77a', 'word_count': 1233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55ed355d-fbb1-5fcc-bde7-51e7d3e3a77a'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of Stan Musial, one of baseball’s great hitters who nonetheless kept a low profile.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-stan-musial-by-george-vecsey.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of Stan Musial, one of baseball’s great hitters who nonetheless kept a low profile.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here are just a few of the interesting things Stan Musial, the great slugger for the St. Louis Cardinals, didn’t do:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/EIG/EIG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 652, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/EIG/EIG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/EIG/EIG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Silent Slugger', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Silent Slugger', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vecsey, George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Musial, Stan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:50:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Eig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8043fa7e-6b67-5803-a8f7-368515129ebb', 'word_count': 1300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8043fa7e-6b67-5803-a8f7-368515129ebb'}, {'abstract': 'A grudge-bearing memoir by Bill White; accounts of Joe DiMaggio’s 56-game hit streak and a 19th-century manager’s back-to-back pennant wins; and a retired ballplayer’s Zen-inspired meditation on the game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/baseball-chronicle.html', 'snippet': 'A grudge-bearing memoir by Bill White; accounts of Joe DiMaggio’s 56-game hit streak and a 19th-century manager’s back-to-back pennant wins; and a retired ballplayer’s Zen-inspired meditation on the game.', 'lead_paragraph': ' UPPITY  My Untold Story About the Games People Play.  By Bill White with Gordon Dillow.  Illustrated. 303 pp. Grand Central. $26.99.  ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Tracy/Tracy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 918, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Tracy/Tracy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Tracy/Tracy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Baseball Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Baseball Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, Shawn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DiMaggio, Joe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:50:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Tracy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tracy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bbfd8cb-a5ad-5982-ae18-8c16dc0a7168', 'word_count': 1035, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bbfd8cb-a5ad-5982-ae18-8c16dc0a7168'}, {'abstract': 'In time for the 100th anniversary of the conquest of the South Pole, a history of Antarctic exploration through the lens of science.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-an-empire-of-ice-by-edward-j-larson.html', 'snippet': 'In time for the 100th anniversary of the conquest of the South Pole, a history of Antarctic exploration through the lens of science.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the 100th anniversary of the conquest of the South Pole coming this December, books about Antarctica will be everywhere this year. One of the first out of the gate is “An Empire of Ice,” which looks at Antarctic exploration through the lens of science. Yes, many of the famous adventure tales are here, including the central one in which Roald Amundsen beats the less-prepared Robert Falcon Scott to the South Pole, and then Scott and his men perish on the way home. But to Edward J. Larson, a professor of history and law at Pepperdine University, the important thing about this story is not who arrived first at the pole, but who did the best field research along the way. And by this measure, Scott was the clear victor. “If the race to the South Pole eventually consumed Scott, it was never at the expense of science,” Larson contends. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Kingson/Kingson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 686, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Kingson/Kingson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Kingson/Kingson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Science and the Race for the South Pole', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Polar Ambitions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Pole', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AMUNDSEN, ROALD', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Robert Falcon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larson, Edward J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shackleton, Ernest', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:50:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer A. Kingson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Kingson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3bf4c86-43d2-503d-bd27-31d254f48d45', 'word_count': 934, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3bf4c86-43d2-503d-bd27-31d254f48d45'}, {'abstract': 'For this traveler, each new place is more mirage than reality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-wanderlust-by-elisabeth-eaves.html', 'snippet': 'For this traveler, each new place is more mirage than reality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travel and sexual conquest have long been cast as male impulses, from Casanova’s far-flung affairs to the promiscuity of the modern adventure hero. “Men know they are sexual exiles,” Camille Paglia has written. “They wander the earth seeking satisfaction, craving and despising, never content. There is nothing in that anguished motion for women to envy.” But then Paglia hadn’t read “Wanderlust.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Slutzky/Slutzky-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 875, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Slutzky/Slutzky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Slutzky/Slutzky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Memoirs of an Insatiable Traveler', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Timbuktu of the Mind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:51:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zoë Slutzky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zoë', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Slutzky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a217c7a6-7e4f-5a61-b3b5-70a322ad3bad', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a217c7a6-7e4f-5a61-b3b5-70a322ad3bad'}, {'abstract': 'How three World War II sightseers survived a crash in remote New Guinea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-lost-in-shangri-la-by-mitchell-zuckoff.html', 'snippet': 'How three World War II sightseers survived a crash in remote New Guinea.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Adventure” these days is merely an experience that allows for a modestly elevated number of variables — renting a car without a GPS unit, for instance, or visiting a friend in East Flatbush. Not that the world is denuded of excitement. It’s just that true adventure requires something that defies an itinerary: failure. Failure is the engine of adventure.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '41', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Washburn/Washburn-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Washburn/Washburn-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u