entury publishing event; and a 21st-century quest. Andrea di Robilant is a distinguished scholar of Venetian history, and in “Irresistible North,” his third book, he strives to bind his tripartite material into a persuasive narrative unity.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Wheeler/Wheeler-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Wheeler/Wheeler-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Wheeler/Wheeler-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Really Discovered America?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Off the Map', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:52:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Wheeler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wheeler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/382f21b5-9d13-5409-b5ea-4a1eec121640', 'word_count': 1223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/382f21b5-9d13-5409-b5ea-4a1eec121640'}, {'abstract': 'A somewhat unlikely adventurer describes his trek down South America’s great mountain range to its icy finish in Patagonia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-andes-by-michael-jacobs.html', 'snippet': 'A somewhat unlikely adventurer describes his trek down South America’s great mountain range to its icy finish in Patagonia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He may call himself an aging backpacker, but Michael Jacobs is hardly a rough-and-ready, revel-in-the-wilderness kind of guy. The first clue comes early in “Andes,” his rambling account of a journey from the northern reaches of South America’s great mountain range to its icy finish in Patagonia. The book is dedicated to his brother — and to “the bus drivers of the Andes.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Becker/Becker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Becker/Becker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Becker/Becker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Becker/Becker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Becker/Becker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Journey Down South America’s Spine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Down the Spine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANDES MOUNTAINS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobs, Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:52:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alida Becker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alida', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Becker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc8e4251-cf99-53ab-8069-9a64754cc331', 'word_count': 1223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc8e4251-cf99-53ab-8069-9a64754cc331'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Theroux’s literary travel volume cites passages from his favorite authors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-the-tao-of-travel-by-paul-theroux.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Theroux’s literary travel volume cites passages from his favorite authors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thoreau said he couldn’t preserve his health and spirits unless he spent four hours a day “sauntering.” Rousseau passed most of his last 15 years in walking. “Everything is finished for me on earth,” he declared, a touch melodramatically. “People can no longer do good or evil to me. . . . Here I am, tranquil at the bottom of the abyss, a poor unfortunate mortal, but unperturbed, like God himself.” Words\xadworth walked about 180,000 miles in his lifetime, in spite of being “not a well-made man,” according to Thomas De Quincey (who added that his friend’s legs were “serviceable”). When a visitor asked Wordsworth’s servant to show him his study, she answered: “Here is his library, but his study is out of doors.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Shukman/Shukman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Shukman/Shukman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Shukman/Shukman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Shukman/Shukman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Shukman/Shukman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paul Theroux’s Travel Miscellany', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Importance of Elsewhere', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Theroux, Paul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:52:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry Shukman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shukman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8cfca93-2446-5736-9c53-0e01816edaef', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8cfca93-2446-5736-9c53-0e01816edaef'}, {'abstract': 'Hours after Bahrain’s security forces again fired tear gas at protesters on Friday, the World Motor Sport Council unanimously approved a decision to reschedule the Bahrain Grand Prix for Oct. 30.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/bahrain-grand-prix-approved-despite-new-clashes/', 'snippet': 'Hours after Bahrain’s security forces again fired tear gas at protesters on Friday, the World Motor Sport Council unanimously approved a decision to reschedule the Bahrain Grand Prix for Oct. 30.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated | 2:49 p.m. Hours after Bahrain’s security forces again fired tear gas at protesters on Friday, the World Motor Sport Council unanimously approved a decision to hold the Bahrain Grand Prix in October. The race was to have been held in March, but was postponed as the country struggled to come to grips with mass demonstrations against the government.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bahrain Grand Prix Approved Despite New Clashes', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khalifa, Hamad Bin Isa al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bahrain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:53:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/535e0653-8d44-5d8b-a1aa-cfa33783c127', 'word_count': 474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/535e0653-8d44-5d8b-a1aa-cfa33783c127'}, {'abstract': 'While softcover and hardcover editions have proved popular with fans, there’s been a growing number of deluxe, Absolute, Martini and Omnibus editions that have been catching fire.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/graphic-books-best-sellers-collected-cool/', 'snippet': 'While softcover and hardcover editions have proved popular with fans, there’s been a growing number of deluxe, Absolute, Martini and Omnibus editions that have been catching fire.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Waiting for trade” is a common refrain from comic book fans. Rather than pick up the monthly issues of a comic book series, many fans haveopted to wait (sometimes patiently) for the collected edition of the title to arrive months later. It’s a risky proposition – if the monthly sales of a book sink, the chances of a collected edition may decrease dramatically. While softcover and hardcover editions have proved popular with fans, there’s been a growing number of deluxe, Absolute, Martini and Omnibus editions that have been catching fire.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Graphic Books Best Sellers: Collected Cool', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:56:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f203937-1ffe-5105-82aa-a610cd11bd95', 'word_count': 333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f203937-1ffe-5105-82aa-a610cd11bd95'}, {'abstract': '“Judy Moody and the Not Bummer Summer” hopes to achieve box-office success with the tween market.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/movies/hollywood-challenge-portrayals-of-tween-girls.html', 'snippet': '“Judy Moody and the Not Bummer Summer” hopes to achieve box-office success with the tween market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHO wants to watch a tween girl on the big screen? ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/MOODY-1/MOODY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/MOODY-1/MOODY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/MOODY-1/MOODY-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/MOODY-1/MOODY-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/MOODY-1/MOODY-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Girls of a Certain Age Challenge Hollywood', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Girls of a Certain Age Challenge Hollywood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:57:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46f53d20-fa46-522f-9acd-0d833e697983', 'word_count': 1277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46f53d20-fa46-522f-9acd-0d833e697983'}, {'abstract': 'UBS said on Friday that it had hired Yusuf Nahmiyas from Deutsche Bank for its technology, media and telecommunications mergers team.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/ubs-hires-tech-and-media-banker-from-deutsche-bank/', 'snippet': 'UBS said on Friday that it had hired Yusuf Nahmiyas from Deutsche Bank for its technology, media and telecommunications mergers team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UBS said on Friday that it had hired Yusuf Nahmiyas as a managing director for its technology, media and telecommunications mergers team. Mr. Nahmiyas was most recently at Deutsche Bank and was previously at JPMorgan Chase. He is the Swiss bank’s latest hire amid a series of high-profile defections.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'UBS Hires Tech and Media Banker From Deutsche Bank', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'UBS AG', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:57:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5874c252-4c82-5a52-a94f-16dd6e03141c', 'word_count': 49, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5874c252-4c82-5a52-a94f-16dd6e03141c'}, {'abstract': 'Here are a couple of exchanges that didn’t make it into the magazine.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/dvd-extras-from-the-barbara-sinatra-interview/', 'snippet': 'Here are a couple of exchanges that didn’t make it into the magazine.', 'lead_paragraph': "Kitty Kelley got scads of attention for her 1991 book, “Nancy Reagan: The Unauthorized Biography,” by alleging that Frank Sinatra and Nancy Reagan carried on an affair for years, even during Reagan’s years in the White House. In Kelley’s book, Old Blue Eyes and Old Adolfo Suits would supposedly disappear “for three-or four-hour ‘private luncheons'” in the family quarters. At the time, some disputed the claim in print.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'DVD Extras From the Barbara Sinatra Interview', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelley, Kitty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reagan, Nancy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scorsese, Martin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sinatra, Frank', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T16:59:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3922b6c0-9e9e-592d-a859-2a862ef4d7d2', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3922b6c0-9e9e-592d-a859-2a862ef4d7d2'}, {'abstract': 'How much violence is necessary to show real horror without being lurid?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/picturing-mob-violence/', 'snippet': 'How much violence is necessary to show real horror without being lurid?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few months ago, Barry Bearak called. Barry lives in Johannesburg, where he and his wife, Celia Dugger, have been our bureau chiefs for southern Africa for the past three and a half years. He called to describe a video he had recently seen of a mob that was beating and killing a man. Barry’s response was immediate: he had seen something terrible, something terribly wrong, and he had to understand why it had happened. Barry won a Pulitzer in 2002 for his coverage of life in war-torn Afghanistan; in 2005, he recreated the day the tsunami wiped out much of Banda Aceh, Indonesia, telling the stories of six survivors. Suffice it to say that he works tirelessly to get the details that convey the truth of the matter, which he does in this most recent piece, a powerful, disturbing story, “Death by a Thousand Blows,” that appears in this Sunday’s magazine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Picturing Mob Violence', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bearak, Barry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:03:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vera Titunik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vera', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Titunik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17085eef-93e4-54e2-aaca-eec3b313eee3', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17085eef-93e4-54e2-aaca-eec3b313eee3'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Sullivan, in his Wealth Matters column, discusses some financial advisers’ renewed interest in dividend-paying stocks.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/renewed-interest-in-dividend-paying-stocks/', 'snippet': 'Paul Sullivan, in his Wealth Matters column, discusses some financial advisers’ renewed interest in dividend-paying stocks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Sullivan writes in his Wealth Matters column this week about a type of stock that had fallen into disfavor over the last couple of decades because it had none of the go-go appeal of a technology start-up — the dividend-paying kind. Paul speaks to several financial advisers who say they are now promoting dividend-paying stocks to people who still have money in cash or low-yielding bonds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Renewed Interest in Dividend-Paying Stocks', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Planners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Paul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:06:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bucks Editors', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bucks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Editors', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f49dd7c-dc69-5e83-9a06-17537aaa5236', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f49dd7c-dc69-5e83-9a06-17537aaa5236'}, {'abstract': 'A look at how far the United States economy has to go before it fully recovers from the Great Recession.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/comparing-recessions-and-recoveries-job-changes/', 'snippet': 'A look at how far the United States economy has to go before it fully recovers from the Great Recession.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Source: Bureau of Labor Statistics. Chart by Amanda Cox. Horizontal axis shows months. Vertical axis shows the ratio of that month’s nonfarm payrolls to the nonfarm payrolls at the start of recession. Note: Because employment is a lagging indicator, the dates for these employment trends are not exactly synchronized with National Bureau of Economic Research’s official business cycle dates.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Comparing Recessions and Recoveries: Job Changes', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:08:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c53ca57a-a16f-5322-97b3-dbdc1aea9535', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c53ca57a-a16f-5322-97b3-dbdc1aea9535'}, {'abstract': 'The brainchild of the artist duo Allora & Calzadilla, it is a pipe organ with an A.T.M. embedded in its belly that is computer-programmed to play a tune when a person puts in their pin number.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/venice-biennale-money-talks-make-that-sings/', 'snippet': 'The brainchild of the artist duo Allora & Calzadilla, it is a pipe organ with an A.T.M. embedded in its belly that is computer-programmed to play a tune when a person puts in their pin number.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s please dispense with the clichés about art and money and instead take a look at getting hold of cash in Venice. There is only one A.T.M. machine in the whole of the Giardini, the gardens at the tip of the city that is home to the national pavilions.\xa0 It’s in a space of its own at the back of the American pavilion. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Venice Biennale: Money Talks, Make That Sings', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:08:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/833ab842-5e91-57f8-8bfd-625e714cd247', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/833ab842-5e91-57f8-8bfd-625e714cd247'}, {'abstract': 'Kors’s resort collection was inspired by his first trip down under, where he was blown away by the urban beach culture.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/just-one-look-michael-kors-resort/', 'snippet': 'Kors’s resort collection was inspired by his first trip down under, where he was blown away by the urban beach culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Kors has been all over the map — in a manner of speaking. His resort collection was inspired by his first trip down under, where he was blown away by the urban beach culture. (“Women wear bathing suits as lingerie,” he exclaimed. “Divine!”) His homage includes a double-faced crepe “rashguard dress” and this three-piece power suit. Wish we worked in that  office.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Michael Kors Resort', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:10:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10a33270-effb-537f-84c6-df0ea3b3d3d8', 'word_count': 67, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10a33270-effb-537f-84c6-df0ea3b3d3d8'}, {'abstract': 'The four-bedroom apartment was sold by the Oxygen channel founder, Geraldine Laybourne, and her husband, Kit.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/big-ticket-sold-for-11750000/', 'snippet': 'The four-bedroom apartment was sold by the Oxygen channel founder, Geraldine Laybourne, and her husband, Kit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A co-op at Harperley Hall, a 1911 Arts and Crafts-style building at 41 Central Park West, was the biggest sale of the week, closing for $11.75 million, according to city property records.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Ticket | Sold for $11,750,000', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CENTRAL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:10:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kershaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2da2171b-885d-5386-a477-df7a2392fca1', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2da2171b-885d-5386-a477-df7a2392fca1'}, {'abstract': 'Marlene Dietrich had a break from her collaborations with Josef von Sternberg to star in Rouben Mamoulian’s “Song of Songs” (1933), new on DVD.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/movies/homevideo/marlene-dietrich-in-song-of-songs-on-dvd.html', 'snippet': 'Marlene Dietrich had a break from her collaborations with Josef von Sternberg to star in Rouben Mamoulian’s “Song of Songs” (1933), new on DVD.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AFTER “Blonde Venus” flopped in 1932, Paramount decided to break up the powerful creative alliance between the director Josef von Sternberg and the actress Marlene Dietrich, whom he had discovered in Germany and brought to Hollywood. While Sternberg, who was Jewish, entertained bizarre fantasies of returning to Germany to work for the state-controlled studio Ufa (he thought the Germans would treat him as the great artist he was), Dietrich was hustled directly into “The Song of Songs,” which has recently surfaced as half of a “Pre-Code Double Feature” available exclusively through the TCM Web site. (The other film is “This Is the Night.”)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/KEHR-1/KEHR-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/KEHR-1/KEHR-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/KEHR-1/KEHR-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 782, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/KEHR-1/KEHR-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/KEHR-1/KEHR-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chopin, Schubert and Dietrich (the Latter Nude)', 'kicker': 'Video', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chopin, Schubert And Dietrich (The Latter Nude)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DVD (Digital Versatile Disc)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dietrich, Marlene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:12:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'DVD', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Kehr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kehr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/889edb4a-8042-5137-ada5-1fa90bc87bba', 'word_count': 1439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/889edb4a-8042-5137-ada5-1fa90bc87bba'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE LAST HERO: A Life of Henry Aaron,  by Howard Bryant (Anchor, $16.95.) Henry Aaron was his era’s greatest hitter; when he retired, in 1976, after a 23-year career, only Ty Cobb had more hits or scored more runs, and no one had more home runs. Bryant’s book is a rigorously researched and illuminating portrait of Aaron — he was the last Hall of Famer to play in the Negro Leagues — and his dogged pursuit of Babe Ruth’s career home run record.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '52', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:13:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/82481674-3539-5404-a0e6-89b9810cddc3', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82481674-3539-5404-a0e6-89b9810cddc3'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/EdChoice-t.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE GREATER JOURNEY: Americans in Paris,  by David McCullough (Simon & Schuster, $37.50.) An exploration of the intellectual legacy that France settled on such 19th-century visitors as Mark Twain, Harriet Beecher Stowe, Samuel Morse and many others.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent books of particular interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:13:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/95d4a996-bd85-5eb7-bb35-9c584d10f14c', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95d4a996-bd85-5eb7-bb35-9c584d10f14c'}, {'abstract': 'Speculative fiction by Lauren Beukes, Genevieve Valentine, Peter S. Beagle and Jo Walton.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-science-fiction-chronicle.html', 'snippet': 'Speculative fiction by Lauren Beukes, Genevieve Valentine, Peter S. Beagle and Jo Walton.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ZOO CITY  By Lauren Beukes.  Angry Robot, paper, $7.99. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Vandermeer-t_CA0/bkr-Vandermeer-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 651, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Vandermeer-t_CA0/bkr-Vandermeer-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/bkr-Vandermeer-t_CA0/bkr-Vandermeer-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Science Fiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Science Fiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science Fiction', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:13:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff VanderMeer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'VanderMeer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/51c65700-8e6f-5ac4-b523-6a5dbfe7dd4b', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51c65700-8e6f-5ac4-b523-6a5dbfe7dd4b'}, {'abstract': 'This darkly comic novel’s child heroine quotes Nietzsche at the dinner table and names her pet rabbit “God.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-when-god-was-a-rabbit-by-sarah-winman.html', 'snippet': 'This darkly comic novel’s child heroine quotes Nietzsche at the dinner table and names her pet rabbit “God.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why do good things happen to bad people? For some of us, this is one of life’s big questions, bigger perhaps than those all-stars: What makes Iago evil? How can a country justify firing both missiles and schoolteachers? The undeserved job promotion, the bequest without cause, the random assigning of accolades or attention: when unmerited rewards are doled out to others, some of us are capable of descending into decades of moping.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Allford/Allford-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 819, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Allford/Allford-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Allford/Allford-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bleak Childhood, Dark Comedy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lord of the Carrots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rabbits', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:13:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry Alford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6755d828-8021-57a4-a942-fde16a06b2e5', 'word_count': 740, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6755d828-8021-57a4-a942-fde16a06b2e5'}, {'abstract': 'In this first novel, a couple shattered by World War II struggle to start anew.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-22-britannia-road-by-amanda-hodgkinson.html', 'snippet': 'In this first novel, a couple shattered by World War II struggle to start anew.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For Silvana, the Polish Holocaust survivor at the center of Amanda Hodgkinson’s accomplished first novel, “22 Britannia Road,” maternal love is a heart-scorching, perilous emotion. During the war, threats to the life of Aurek, her young son, were everywhere: from the “filthy” skies, raked with thunder clouds and German planes, to the sunless, brambly depths of the Polish forests, where she and Aurek, like two figures in a fairy tale, hid out for years. They are rescued from a refugee camp by Janusz, the husband and father from whom they were separated at the beginning of the fighting. Janusz brings them to Ipswich, to the small house and garden that give the novel its title. There Silvana forces herself to believe that a normal life is possible: that she and Janusz will rediscover themselves as husband and wife, that Aurek will have a father — and that the secret still threatening her son’s security will stay buried.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/TOWER/TOWER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 803, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/TOWER/TOWER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/TOWER/TOWER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After the Holocaust', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strangers in the Family', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:13:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Towers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Towers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1af6fdbd-8390-517e-a928-c0e3c39f3296', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1af6fdbd-8390-517e-a928-c0e3c39f3296'}, {'abstract': 'Beach-reading season may be in full swing, but that doesn’t mean there isn’t a whole lot of constitutional law being debated on the best-seller list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Beach-reading season may be in full swing, but that doesn’t mean there isn’t a whole lot of constitutional law being debated on the best-seller list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CON LAW: Beach-reading season may be in full swing, but that doesn’t mean there isn’t a whole lot of constitutional law being debated on the best-seller list. In “Where’s the Birth Certificate?,” at No. 14 in its second week on the hardcover nonfiction list, the veteran conspiracy theorist Jerome Corsi argues that Barack Obama’s supposed foreign birth runs afoul of Article 2, Section 1, which requires that the president be a “natural-born citizen.” (Didn’t Donald Trump ruin that story line a few weeks ago?) Meanwhile, in Steve Berry’s latest pretzel of a thriller, “The Jefferson Key” (No. 5 on the fiction list), the swashbuckling Justice Department operative Cotton Malone comes out of semiretirement to foil a presidential assassination rooted in a secret clause encoded in Article 1, Section 8.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:13:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83bafa1d-50c9-52e9-a7e5-fe2989319cd0', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83bafa1d-50c9-52e9-a7e5-fe2989319cd0'}, {'abstract': 'More picture books reviewed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/childrens-books-bookshelf-growing-up.html', 'snippet': 'More picture books reviewed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LITTLE MIST  By Angela McAllister. Illustrated by Sarah Fox-Davies.  32 pp. Knopf. $16.99. (Picture book; ages 2 to 6)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/05bookshelf/05bookshelf-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/05bookshelf/05bookshelf-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/05bookshelf/05bookshelf-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bookshelf: Growing Up', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bookshelf: Growing Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:13:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d4d94c4-6858-52f5-992a-e7773e09b44c', 'word_count': 563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d4d94c4-6858-52f5-992a-e7773e09b44c'}, {'abstract': 'A lurid murder case from the 1930s sheds light on a time of social change in France.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-violette-noziere-by-sarah-maza.html', 'snippet': 'A lurid murder case from the 1930s sheds light on a time of social change in France.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Aug. 21, 1933, Violette Nozière, the 18-year-old only daughter of an engine driver and a housewife who lived in a claustrophobic two-room apartment in the working-class 12th \xadArrondissement of Paris, gave her parents drinks laced with a lethal dose of barbiturates. Her father died. Her mother, who drank only half her glass, fell into a deeply drugged sleep, but survived. When Violette was arrested — after a brief attempt at flight and a costly shopping spree paid for with her parents’ pilfered savings — she gave a full confession. She also explained, as motive for her crime, that her father had been raping her for the past six years. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/WARNER/WARNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 707, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/WARNER/WARNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/WARNER/WARNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Murder That Transfixed 1930s Paris', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Death in Paris', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Thirties', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:13:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Warner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/79eeb304-095b-5a9d-ab1f-5cf46c75fd66', 'word_count': 1233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79eeb304-095b-5a9d-ab1f-5cf46c75fd66'}, {'abstract': 'On a distant planet, humans introduce the natives to a destructive habit — lying.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-embassytown-by-china-mieville.html', 'snippet': 'On a distant planet, humans introduce the natives to a destructive habit — lying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The innovative and protean British writer China Miéville’s declared ambition to write a novel in every genre offers an insight into his powers of invention. “I’m in this . . . business for the monsters,” he said in an interview a few years ago. “Unfortunately, you can’t really sell books of monsters to publishers. They insist on stories linking them.” To create the necessary narratives, Miéville repurposes genre formulas like a salvage artist, mixing a connoisseur’s respect for recovered materials with heretical joy in putting them to surprising uses.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '43', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Rotella/Rotella-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 825, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Rotella/Rotella-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Rotella/Rotella-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Teaching Aliens How to Lie', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dreaming in Ariekene', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mieville, China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science Fiction', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:14:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carlo Rotella', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carlo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rotella', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a967217c-a00d-5360-a3b7-9d0a969d77b2', 'word_count': 869, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a967217c-a00d-5360-a3b7-9d0a969d77b2'}, {'abstract': 'In Richard North Patterson’s new thriller, Al Qaeda plans to set off a nuclear bomb on Sept. 11, 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-the-devils-light-by-richard-north-patterson.html', 'snippet': 'In Richard North Patterson’s new thriller, Al Qaeda plans to set off a nuclear bomb on Sept. 11, 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing solidifies the reputation of a spy novelist so much as prescience. Graham Greene’s “Quiet American” appeared only a year after the French defeat at Dien Bien Phu, and before American advisers had been sent to Vietnam in large numbers. Four years before the Cuban missile crisis, with Fulgencio Batista still in power, Greene wrote another novel, this one about possible secret military installations in Cuba. As for Ian Fleming, his vision of nonstate terrorist groups seeking nuclear weapons remains the most frightening and relevant aspect of the James Bond series.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Richard North Patterson’s Al Qaeda Thriller', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Alternate Ending', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patterson, Richard North', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:14:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isaac Chotiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isaac', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chotiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d570e79-4360-5ae5-afae-48b2883adc91', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d570e79-4360-5ae5-afae-48b2883adc91'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, a free-spirited sister’s death — perhaps it was murder — forces a highly conventional woman to examine the truth of their relationship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-sister-by-rosamund-lupton.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, a free-spirited sister’s death — perhaps it was murder — forces a highly conventional woman to examine the truth of their relationship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two years ago, a British study suggested that men and women who grew up with sisters had happier lives and rosier outlooks than men and women who didn’t. The presence of even one sister in a household was enough to foster an atmosphere of emotional openness that helped family members communicate and tackle problems. No such benefit accrued to people who had only brothers. The study was small — only 571 subjects took part — but it was heartening to think that sisters, so often portrayed as fractious rivals, envious of one another’s attractions and covetous of one another’s friends and clothes, might actually serve as mortar binding the bricks of a family’s psyche. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Schillinger1/Schillinger1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 344, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Schillinger1/Schillinger1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 344}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Schillinger1/Schillinger1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Schillinger1/Schillinger1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Schillinger1/Schillinger1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Mystery of a Sister’s Death', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Double Helix', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:14:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1db0ae7c-4259-5af2-aae0-ef8c24baf95f', 'word_count': 1198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1db0ae7c-4259-5af2-aae0-ef8c24baf95f'}, {'abstract': 'Lynne Tillman experiments with narrative form in these innovative stories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-someday-this-will-be-funny-by-lynne-tillman.html', 'snippet': 'Lynne Tillman experiments with narrative form in these innovative stories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite the claim of the title, “Someday This Will Be Funny,” you wouldn’t want to reach for Lynne Tillman’s new book just for a good howl. In fact, that “someday” the title promises may never come. Tillman’s stories are too piercing, the obsessions of her characters too connected to their psychic wounds, for them to be considered exactly “funny.” In any case, it isn’t “someday” but rather “meantime” that counts for readers. And in the meantime, Tillman’s fictions tend to be (to steal a line from one of her stories) as “outrageously ineffable, obdurate and evasive” as the forms of desire they describe. Gorgeously at ease and technically virtuosic, the stories are ever on point — on point, that is, if the point of your reading has more to do with psychological nuance and bravura performances of language than with conventional story lines.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/GANDER/GANDER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 924, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/GANDER/GANDER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/GANDER/GANDER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lynne Tillman’s Innovative Stories', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Are You Laughing At?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tillman, Lynne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:14:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Forrest Gander', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Forrest', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gander', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/78ed6477-c9e6-5ccf-8ad5-de4e5040389c', 'word_count': 912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78ed6477-c9e6-5ccf-8ad5-de4e5040389c'}, {'abstract': 'In this debut young adult novel, a girl searches for her brother in a postapocalyptic world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/young-adult-books-blood-red-road-by-moira-young.html', 'snippet': 'In this debut young adult novel, a girl searches for her brother in a postapocalyptic world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How many times can the world come to an end? For Saba, the fatalistic 18-year-old heroine of “Blood Red Road,” quite a few. She lives long after Earth’s last major civilization, the Wreckers, went extinct. Her mother died nine years earlier, giving birth to Saba’s sister, Emmi. The lake beside her family’s shack is drying up, replaced by a wasteland of dust storms and heat.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Teens After Armageddon', 'kicker': 'Young Adult', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hotwinds and Hellwurms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:14:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Bruder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9ff8c0c-ffad-53b6-a29d-599920930499', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9ff8c0c-ffad-53b6-a29d-599920930499'}, {'abstract': 'In this middle grade novel, a boy becomes aware of his father’s strengths, and his own, while spending the summer with relatives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/childrens-books-the-absolute-value-of-mike-by-kathryn-erskine.html', 'snippet': 'In this middle grade novel, a boy becomes aware of his father’s strengths, and his own, while spending the summer with relatives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twice in “The Absolute Value of Mike,” 14-year-old Mike is asked, “What’s your story?” The first time he equivocates, talking instead about the circumstances that surround him. The second time, he evades: “I — I don’t know.” Both are deceptions that come out of a humiliating sadness; because he does know his story. He is the kid whose father is a math and engineering genius, burdened with an un-smart son, one who is bound to disappoint and will probably end up on the street, because he is so much less than his father.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Figuring Out Dad', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Figuring Out Dad', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:14:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By GARY D. SCHMIDT', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/212a907e-9253-5a07-a54e-b8dc23ebeaba', 'word_count': 650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/212a907e-9253-5a07-a54e-b8dc23ebeaba'}, {'abstract': 'Mystery novels by Michael Koryta, Justin Evans, Jason Starr and Sara Gran.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-crime-novels-by-michael-koryta-justin-evans-jason-starr-and-sara-gran.html', 'snippet': 'Mystery novels by Michael Koryta, Justin Evans, Jason Starr and Sara Gran.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s almost summer, so let’s get serious about those vacation reading lists. On second thought, let’s save the 600-page historical sagas and thickly plotted espionage thrillers for another day and kick back with something weird and wonderful — like a supernatural mystery.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '47', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Crime/Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 771, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ghostbusters', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ghostbusters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Starr, Jason', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Evans, Justin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gran, Sara', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koryta, Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:14:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8be46c9f-41ed-5fde-b5f6-f070552442fb', 'word_count': 918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8be46c9f-41ed-5fde-b5f6-f070552442fb'}, {'abstract': 'A Brooklyn family experiences a blackout in this picture book.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/childrens-books-blackout-by-john-rocco.html', 'snippet': 'A Brooklyn family experiences a blackout in this picture book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you believe that books set in your neighborhood will somehow help your child love to read, then you would be lucky these days to be a parent in Brooklyn. Books for children by Brooklyn authors now match the similarly numberless books by Brooklyn-based authors for grown-ups. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Moody-sub/Moody-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 357, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Moody-sub/Moody-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 357}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Moody-sub/Moody-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 609, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Moody-sub/Moody-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/books/review/Moody-sub/Moody-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When New York City Went Dark', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The City Went Dark', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rocco, John (Illustrator)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:14:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Moody', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moody', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4f1a588-b98b-5368-b87f-d524cbc84eb1', 'word_count': 408, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4f1a588-b98b-5368-b87f-d524cbc84eb1'}, {'abstract': 'An illustrated history of American newspaper comics, from the Yellow Kid to Dilbert.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/books/review/book-review-the-comics-by-brian-walker.html', 'snippet': 'An illustrated history of American newspaper comics, from the Yellow Kid to Dilbert.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1906, the poet Ralph Bergengren, in the name of “common sense and Christianity,” denounced the Sunday comics supplement as “pandemonium, . . . the clamor of hooting mobs, the laughter of imbeciles, and the crash of explosives.”', 'print