': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Edmonton Oilers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'U2', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:27:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne C. Gerstner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Gerstner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c972c35-e5b1-5bb3-9a1a-99a775d2e18e', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c972c35-e5b1-5bb3-9a1a-99a775d2e18e'}, {'abstract': 'For reporters living for the second time in eight years under a nightly fusillade of bombs and missiles, it is a case, in many respects, of going back to the future.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/history-repeats-for-a-correspondent-in-tripoli/', 'snippet': 'For reporters living for the second time in eight years under a nightly fusillade of bombs and missiles, it is a case, in many respects, of going back to the future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TRIPOLI, Libya – In March 2003, the Pentagon described its airborne onslaught on Baghdad at the outset of the campaign to topple Saddam Hussein as one intended to provoke “shock and awe.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'History Repeats for a Correspondent in Tripoli', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Seif al-Islam el-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:32:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b6d988d-42fe-58f1-a035-953259133c4a', 'word_count': 2672, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b6d988d-42fe-58f1-a035-953259133c4a'}, {'abstract': 'Tarragona, just 60 miles from Barcelona, is a relatively unexplored, laid-back city full of eye-opening Roman ruins.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/travel/in-tarragona-roman-ruins-an-hour-from-barcelona.html', 'snippet': 'Tarragona, just 60 miles from Barcelona, is a relatively unexplored, laid-back city full of eye-opening Roman ruins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHAT draws travelers to the northeast of Spain? Cutting-edge food, of course. To Barcelona? Modernist architecture, perhaps. Along the coast, the beaches. And to Tarragona, a laid-back city about 60 miles south of Barcelona, the ancient ruins. On our first afternoon there, my husband and I headed from our hotel down the broad promenade of the Rambla Nova, the city’s main artery. We stopped at one of the outdoor cafes along the way, mixing with a crowd that seemed more local than tourist, and ordered gin-and-tonics.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/travel/05overnighter-span/05overnighter-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/travel/05overnighter-span/05overnighter-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/travel/05overnighter-span/05overnighter-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/travel/05overnighter-span/05overnighter-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/travel/05overnighter-span/05overnighter-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Near Barcelona, Gems From Ancient Rome', 'kicker': 'Overnighter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Near Barcelona, Gems From Ancient Rome', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:33:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6dc8063-f279-52e0-9876-91d7ca5c1b4f', 'word_count': 1838, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6dc8063-f279-52e0-9876-91d7ca5c1b4f'}, {'abstract': 'The riot grrrl movement still inspires musicians, years after it ended.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/arts/music/the-riot-grrrl-movement-still-inspires.html', 'snippet': 'The riot grrrl movement still inspires musicians, years after it ended.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A quartet of teenage girls was onstage at the Knitting Factory, the Brooklyn club. Playing guitars and wearing mini-skirts, they did a tuneful pop cover of Ben Lee’s “I Wish I Was Him,” about the 1990s alt-rock heartthrob Evan Dando and given a flip by these long-haired 14- and 15-year-olds who called themselves Toy Sugar. Performing the song in 1994, Kathleen Hanna did that first, when, as the frontwoman for the punk band Bikini Kill and a founder of the feminist riot grrrl movement, she made it her business to overturn gender norms in a way that you could sing, or scream, along to.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/GRRL-1/GRRL-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/GRRL-1/GRRL-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/GRRL-1/GRRL-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/GRRL-1/GRRL-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/GRRL-1/GRRL-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Feminist Riot That Still Inspires', 'kicker': 'Music', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Feminist Riot That Still Inspires', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bikini Kill', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Le Tigre', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T17:33:34+