content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'June 5 — 11', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T18:16:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70c15157-02be-52ad-b2c5-fc9f2afa6403', 'word_count': 1785, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70c15157-02be-52ad-b2c5-fc9f2afa6403'}, {'abstract': 'Calling a voucher system Medicare doesn’t make it so.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/whats-in-a-name-5/', 'snippet': 'Calling a voucher system Medicare doesn’t make it so.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lot, or at least that’s what Republicans think. Greg Sargent reports that they’re demanding that a TV station stop running ads saying that the GOP wants to end Medicare; the claim is that this is a lie, because the new program the GOP wants to impose in place of Medicare is still Medicare.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s In A Name?', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T18:18:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4859b38b-5c42-5bd3-868b-cd3d4e976fbb', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4859b38b-5c42-5bd3-868b-cd3d4e976fbb'}, {'abstract': 'In a competition called “Step It Up,” teams of young dancers and steppers put together campaigns about hot-button issues like domestic violence, gangs and H.I.V. prevention; the winners get their own Web site, a $2,500 donation to the cause of their choice and $2,500 to do with as they wish.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/dance-for-a-cause-and-let-the-votes-fall-where-they-may/', 'snippet': 'In a competition called “Step It Up,” teams of young dancers and steppers put together campaigns about hot-button issues like domestic violence, gangs and H.I.V. prevention; the winners get their own Web site, a $2,500 donation to the cause of the...', 'lead_paragraph': 'And now, a public-service announcement about public-service announcements.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dance for a Cause and Let the Votes Fall Where They May', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Youth and Community Development Dept', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T18:19:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68030a65-803a-52b9-a292-7b96bd1db46b', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68030a65-803a-52b9-a292-7b96bd1db46b'}, {'abstract': 'Many operas have tried, and failed, as sequels to “The Barber of Seville” and “The Marriage of Figaro.” Conducted by Riccardo Muti, “I Due Figaro” (“The Two Figaros”) may have some hopes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/arts/music/another-figaro-tale-or-two-in-salzburg.html', 'snippet': 'Many operas have tried, and failed, as sequels to “The Barber of Seville” and “The Marriage of Figaro.” Conducted by Riccardo Muti, “I Due Figaro” (“The Two Figaros”) may have some hopes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FIRST “The Barber of Seville,” then “The Marriage of Figaro.” Then what? Whether we are speaking of the plays of Pierre-Augustin Caron de Beaumarchais or the operas they inspired, the Figaro triptych many have dreamed of — including Beaumarchais himself — remains an elusive goal. On Friday the Whitsun edition of the Salzburg Festival revives an opera that just might serve as the missing third panel: “I Due Figaro” (“The Two Figaros”), by Saverio Mercadante.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/FIGAROS-Jp-1/FIGAROS-Jp-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 305, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/FIGAROS-Jp-1/FIGAROS-Jp-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 305}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/FIGAROS-Jp-1/FIGAROS-Jp-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 717, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Tale, or Two, Of That Operatic Figaro', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Another Tale, or Two, Of That Operatic Figaro', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orpheus Chamber Orchestra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Muti, Riccardo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T18:20:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Gurewitsch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gurewitsch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a76980e9-80dc-5bcb-85b5-e2af7b0138da', 'word_count': 1623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a76980e9-80dc-5bcb-85b5-e2af7b0138da'}, {'abstract': 'The choreographer Jody Oberfelder reimagines Stravinsky’s “Histoire du Soldat” (“A Soldier’s Tale”), setting it in the present and making its Devil a woman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/arts/music/stravinskys-histoire-du-soldat-updated.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographer Jody Oberfelder reimagines Stravinsky’s “Histoire du Soldat” (“A Soldier’s Tale”), setting it in the present and making its Devil a woman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE setting, dialogue and dance steps of Igor Stravinsky’s “Histoire du Soldat,” a music-theater piece from 1918, are as malleable as the Faust story on which the work is based. What doesn’t change is the music: a tart panoply of jazzy figures, dance forms and marches.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/SOLDIER-1/SOLDIER-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/SOLDIER-1/SOLDIER-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/SOLDIER-1/SOLDIER-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stravinsky’s Devil, Reignited', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stravinsky’s Devil, Reignited', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pace University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stravinsky, Igor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T18:21:27+0000