/article/366f5eae-47f8-5a1a-ac49-f86190e4001b'}, {'abstract': 'An article in The Financial Times offers an account of an odd deal between the French bank and Libya.', 'web_url': 'https://norris.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/did-socgen-bet-against-its-own-share-price/', 'snippet': 'An article in The Financial Times offers an account of an odd deal between the French bank and Libya.', 'lead_paragraph': '4:20 p.m. | Updated to correct reference to hedging strategies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Did SocGen Bet Against Its Own Share Price?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T20:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c278485-12ab-58f5-bd9d-2c5fbd3bfe46', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c278485-12ab-58f5-bd9d-2c5fbd3bfe46'}, {'abstract': 'Women writers are “quite different,” said V.S. Naipaul.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/more-than-a-room-of-ones-own/', 'snippet': 'Women writers are “quite different,” said V.S. Naipaul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Women writers are “quite different,” said V.S.\xa0Naipaul in an interview at the Royal Geographic Society, picked up by The Guardian. “I read a piece of writing, and within a paragraph or two I know whether it is by a woman or not. I think [it is] unequal to me.” (To see if you have the master’s knack for sexing literature, go to what  New York magazine calls “Guess the Sex of the Authors on the V.S. Naipaul Test” on the Guardian’s Web site.) He goes on to explain that, in part, women fall short because they aren’t the boss at home. “She is not a complete master of a house, so that comes over in her writing too.” Maybe Virginia Woolf should have asked for more real estate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Than a Room of One’s Own', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T20:02:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Glaser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glaser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62901d4f-e6b3-5170-8bc2-02c06ef7958f', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62901d4f-e6b3-5170-8bc2-02c06ef7958f'}, {'abstract': 'Uncle Mo has been diagnosed with a liver disease and his return to racing is uncertain.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/liver-disease-keeps-uncle-mo-sidelined/', 'snippet': 'Uncle Mo has been diagnosed with a liver disease and his return to racing is uncertain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Uncle Mo, the 2-year-old champion who was scratched the day before the Kentucky Derby, has been diagnosed with a liver disease called cholangiohepatitis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Liver Disease Keeps Uncle Mo Sidelined', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pletcher, Todd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Repole, Mike', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T20:02:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Drape', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drape', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a290644d-8197-56bf-9e56-f9b2c084a230', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a290644d-8197-56bf-9e56-f9b2c084a230'}, {'abstract': 'With Iraq, Egypt, Syria, the United Arab Emirates and Saudi Arabia all showing at the Venice Biennale, Middle Eastern art was Topic A at parties.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/fashion/middle-eastern-artists-at-the-venice-biennale.html', 'snippet': 'With Iraq, Egypt, Syria, the United Arab Emirates and Saudi Arabia all showing at the Venice Biennale, Middle Eastern art was Topic A at parties.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adel Abidin and Ahmed Alsoudani, the young artists who represent Iraq at the 54th Venice Biennale, were sitting on the terrace of the Bauer Hotel here at dusk on Wednesday, studying their elaborately hand-written invitations to a private dinner given by François Pinault, the French billionaire. How would they cross the water to San Giorgio Maggiore Island?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/fashion/jp-VENICE/jp-VENICE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/fashion/jp-VENICE/jp-VENICE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/05/fashion/jp-VENICE/jp-VENICE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art World’s New Darlings', 'kicker': Non