ame': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asparagus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eggs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T21:32:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ac4a60a-9d0d-5f92-b4c1-488c0472e52a', 'word_count': 191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ac4a60a-9d0d-5f92-b4c1-488c0472e52a'}, {'abstract': 'Recipes celebrating asparagus and eggs that are substantial enough to serve as main courses but still light and spring-like.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/dining/asparagus-and-eggs-take-center-stage-a-good-appetite.html', 'snippet': 'Recipes celebrating asparagus and eggs that are substantial enough to serve as main courses but still light and spring-like.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ASPARAGUS and eggs have an affinity for each other. The voluptuous yolk softens and smoothes the grassy sharpness of the vegetable, while the asparagus brightens up the dull richness of the egg.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/dining/08appe-2/08appe-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/08/dining/08appe-2/08appe-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/dining/08appe-2/Appe-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/dining/08appe-2/Appe-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/08/dining/08appe-2/Appe-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Asparagus and Eggs Take Center Stage', 'kicker': 'A Good Appetite', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Longer Supporting Characters, Two Take Center Stage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asparagus', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eggs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T21:32:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73ff1dcf-0552-562b-bc69-2ef01f2a2575', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73ff1dcf-0552-562b-bc69-2ef01f2a2575'}, {'abstract': 'The lists of the artfully organized, now on view at the Morgan Library.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/checking-it-twice/', 'snippet': 'The lists of the artfully organized, now on view at the Morgan Library.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whether you’re simply organized or mildy compulsive, a list can provide the structure for the everyday. However, when the to-do list is compiled by such artistic luminaries as Pablo Picasso, Alexander Calder and Franz Kline, it transcends its ostensible purpose and provides insights into the life and times of its maker. This is obvious from a new show — “To-dos, Ilustrated Inventories, Collected Thoughts, and Other Artists’ Enumerations,” curated by Liza Kirwin from the Smithsonian’s Archives of American Art — that opened today at the Morgan Library & Museum in Manhattan. For example, Pablo Picasso’s recommendations for inclusion in the 1913 Armory Show, the first international exhibition of modern art in the United States, astutely foresaw the historical importance of Marcel Duchamp, Fernand Leger and Juan Gris. Franz Kline’s shopping list for booze for his 1960 New Year’s party was evidence of his financial success (it was a hefty bill for the time) as well as his social nature. The artist Adolf Konrad illustrated his 1962 packing list: Khaki trench. Check. Six pairs of socks. Check. Sunglasses. Check. Two lemons. Check. Ah, the beauty of the artfully organized.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Checking It Twice', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T21:37:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Pask', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pask', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab29e83a-cbe6-5fa2-b2a7-87dded345515', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab29e83a-cbe6-5fa2-b2a7-87dded345515'}, {'abstract': 'This week: A shocking, videotaped murder, Bill Simmons, Bon Iver and editor credits.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/this-sunday-a-meaningful-death/', 'snippet': 'This week: A shocking, videotaped murder, Bill Simmons, Bon Iver and editor credits.', 'lead_paragraph': "A couple of months ago, when we started putting credits and email addresses for the editors of feature stories on the magazine’s table of contents, Jack Shafer, media critic for Slate, felt inspired to not only mock the plan, but to impugn the editing profession generally. “Try complimenting an editor sometime about a good piece in his publication,” Jack wrote, “and you’re certain to get this eye-rolling response: ‘You shoulda seen it when it came in!'”", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Sunday: A Meaningful Death', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-03T21:41:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1940f4cb-5204-5f5b-9fdf-13447d3249c6', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1940f4cb-5204-5f5b-9fdf-13447d3249c6'}, {'abstract': "June 3 - China's Li Na hopes to be the first Asian to win a Grand Slam tennis tournament.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/sports/100000000848802/timescast--chinese-tennis-sensation.html', 'snippet': "June 3 - China's Li Na hopes to be the first Asian to win a Grand Slam tennis tournament.", 'lead_paragraph': "June 3 - China's Li Na hopes to be the first Asian to win a Grand Slam tennis tournament.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/sports/video-tc-110603-tennis/video-tc-110603-tennis-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/03/sports/video-tc-110603-tennis/video-tc-110603-tennis-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'