s, says an Asian immigrant influx has depressed business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Maria Campanella steers her truck through the streets of Bensonhurst, she blasts the speakers, blares the horn and rings the bells. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/nyregion/METJOURNAL11/METJOURNAL11-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/04/nyregion/METJOURNAL11/METJOURNAL11-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/nyregion/METJOURNAL11/METJOURNAL11-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/nyregion/METJOURNAL11/METJOURNAL11-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/04/nyregion/METJOURNAL11/METJOURNAL11-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Neighborhood Changes; Its Ice Cream Girl Doesn’t', 'kicker': 'Bensonhurst Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Neighborhood Changes; Its Ice Cream Girl Doesn’t', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bensonhurst (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ICE CREAM', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vendors, Street', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-04T01:02:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Robbins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robbins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16baad35-83a0-5b0e-9532-22e4f366ed8a', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16baad35-83a0-5b0e-9532-22e4f366ed8a'}, {'abstract': 'Such teachers have become minor Internet celebrities, lauded by their fans for exposing students’ insolent manners and desultory work habits. But they should not be defended.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/04/opinion/04zimmerman.html', 'snippet': 'Such teachers have become minor Internet celebrities, lauded by their fans for exposing students’ insolent manners and desultory work habits. But they should not be defended.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1927, a schoolteacher in Secaucus, N.J., named Helen Clark lost her teaching license. The reason? Somebody had seen her smoking cigarettes after school hours. In communities across the United States, that was a ground for dismissal. So was card-playing, dancing and failure to attend church. Even after Prohibition ended, teachers could be dismissed for drinking or frequenting a place where liquor was served. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/opinion/04oped-art/04oped-art-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/04/opinion/04oped-art/04oped-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/04/opinion/04oped-art/04oped-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Teachers Talk Out of School', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Teachers Talk Out of School', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-04T01:05:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Zimmerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f16ac219-f74c-5a3f-adfc-381d23d26b6c', 'word_count': 803, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f16ac219-f74c-5a3f-adfc-381d23d26b6c'}, {'abstract': 'The co-owner of the Black Cow Coffee Company has been rising at dawn to open his new store in Pleasantville.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/nyregion/black-cow-coffee-company-in-pleasantville-review.html', 'snippet': 'The co-owner of the Black Cow Coffee Company has been rising at dawn to open his new store in Pleasantville.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a steamy Saturday morning, I had to dodge toddlers and strollers to get in line at the Black Cow Coffee Company in Pleasantville, but the clientele was so cheerful, and Michael Grant, the shop’s co-owner, and his fellow baristas were so adept at fielding orders, that the wait was, well, a pleasant one. ', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Caffeine Jolt, With a Smile', 'kicker': 'Quick Bite | Pleasantville', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Caffeine Jolt, With a Smile', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pleasantville (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coffee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-04T01:08:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alice Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9529d1c9-a2d2-562c-ac02-5083c9ebf719', 'word_count': 309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9529d1c9-a2d2-562c-ac02-5083c9ebf719'}, {'abstract': 'At Myong Gourmet, the strong Asian influence — don’t expect white rice, though — meets flavors from many other locales.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/nyregion/myong-gourmet-dining-review.html', 'snippet': 'At Myong Gourmet, the strong Asian influence — don’t expect white rice, though — meets flavors from many other locales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON my first visit to Myong Gourmet, my guests and I were greeted by a round of amuse bouches: smoked duck with pickled radish and, I believe, crème fraîche. The tastes blended wonderfully, but what was the orientation? French? Asian? New American? Next came a bowl of warm pita wedges matched with three spreads: edamame and tofu, some kind of pickled vegetable, and fruit and tofu. Again, delicious. And again, I felt a little unmoored.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/nyregion/05DINEWE2-1306888470227/05DINEWE2-1306888470227-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 898, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'