'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80ccd1fd-06e5-520a-92ce-ba71027c3fa6', 'word_count': 1160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80ccd1fd-06e5-520a-92ce-ba71027c3fa6'}, {'abstract': 'Even if Greece is pulled from danger, economists say, European leaders will probably confront similar aid operations over the next several years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/business/global/06euro.html', 'snippet': 'Even if Greece is pulled from danger, economists say, European leaders will probably confront similar aid operations over the next several years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VENICE — As European leaders move toward a second bailout for Greece, some economists are warning that a new rescue would merely kick the country’s problems further down the road and might not avert a default that could strain Europe’s monetary union.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prospect of 2nd Greek Bailout Worries Economists', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Greece’s Fiscal Crisis Grows, Economists Are Wary of a Second Bailout', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T18:35:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Alderman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alderman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4a32a3b-6aec-56b0-bb2a-91b4904d7286', 'word_count': 722, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4a32a3b-6aec-56b0-bb2a-91b4904d7286'}, {'abstract': 'The latest installment of this comic-book adventure has brought new life to a sagging film franchise.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/first-place-for-x-men-first-class/', 'snippet': 'The latest installment of this comic-book adventure has brought new life to a sagging film franchise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the year’s best-reviewed wide-release movies, “X-Men: First Class,” took in a solid $56 million at the North American box office over the weekend — easily enough for No. 1 and to help reboot a sagging franchise. Facing audience and critical rebellion over the last two “X-Men” installments, Tom Rothman, co-chairman of 20th Century Fox, took creative risks with “First Class” that appeared to pay off, including casting a cadre of untested young actors and approving a screenplay that includes Russian, French, German and Spanish dialogue — highly unusual for a summer blockbuster. In partial release overseas, the film took in an additional $64 million. “The Hangover Part II,” the raunchy buddy comedy from Warner Brothers, was second with an estimated $32.4 million for a two-week total of $187 million, according to Hollywood.com, which compiles ticketing statistics. “Kung Fu Panda 2” (DreamWorks Animation via Paramount) was third with about $24.3 million, sending its two-week total past $100 million. “Pirates of the Caribbean: On Stranger Tides” showed continued life in fourth place, taking in about $18 million for a three-week total of $190.3 million. The crude comedy “Bridesmaids” (Universal) was fifth with about $12 million for a four-week total of $107.3 million.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'First Place for ‘X-Men: First Class’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rothman, Tom', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hollywood.com', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T18:52:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08ce8f59-ee53-5cd5-ba05-5c67856ce2a0', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08ce8f59-ee53-5cd5-ba05-5c67856ce2a0'}, {'abstract': 'Rafael Nadal reeled in Bjorn Borg and won his sixth French Open title by holding off his customary foil, Roger Federer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/sports/tennis/2011-french-open-nadal-defeats-federer-to-tie-borgs-record.html', 'snippet': 'Rafael Nadal reeled in Bjorn Borg and won his sixth French Open title by holding off his customary foil, Roger Federer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Bjorn Borg could not make it to Roland Garros on Sunday, which turned out to be a missed opportunity. It would have made for just the right photograph: Borg handing over the Coupe des Mousquetaires to his successor in the soft evening light in Paris.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/sports/tennis/nadal7/nadal7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/05/sports/tennis/nadal7/nadal7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ