://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/business/economy/06bond.html', 'snippet': 'The following tax-exempt fixed-income issues are scheduled for pricing this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Treasury’s schedule of financing this week includes Monday’s regular weekly auction of new three- and six-month bills and an auction of four-week bills on Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Treasury Auctions Set for This Week', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treasury Auctions Set for This Week', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T21:21:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1144c66-3c59-5d87-bee1-f5cd8776740e', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1144c66-3c59-5d87-bee1-f5cd8776740e'}, {'abstract': 'Mrs. Sisulu was considered by many to be the mother of South Africa’s liberation struggle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/world/africa/06sisulu.html', 'snippet': 'Mrs. Sisulu was considered by many to be the mother of South Africa’s liberation struggle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JOHANNESBURG — Albertina Sisulu, considered by many to be the mother of South Africa’s liberation struggle, a woman who was hounded and jailed by the apartheid government but who lived to see her children assume leadership roles in a democratic nation, died here on Thursday. She was 92.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/world/06sisulu1/06sisulu1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/world/06sisulu1/06sisulu1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/06/world/06sisulu1/06sisulu1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Albertina Sisulu, Who Helped Lead Apartheid Fight, Dies at 92', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Albertina Sisulu Dies at 92; Helped Fight Apartheid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AFRICAN NATIONAL CONGRESS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Apartheid (Policy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T21:23:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barry Bearak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bearak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/51f078a4-387d-53e8-b3f9-09181ad25d7c', 'word_count': 969, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51f078a4-387d-53e8-b3f9-09181ad25d7c'}, {'abstract': 'Is it our desires or our values that determine who we “really” are?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/in-search-of-the-true-self/', 'snippet': 'Is it our desires or our values that determine who we “really” are?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mark Pierpont used to be an important figure in the evangelical Christian effort to help “cure” gay people of their homosexual desires. He started out just printing up tracts and handing them out in gay bars, but his ministry grew over time, and eventually he was traveling the world and speaking to crowds that sometimes numbered in the thousands. There was just one problem. Mark Pierpont himself was gay. He continued to feel sexual desires toward other men and was constantly engaged in an effort to suppress them. In the documentary film “Protagonist,” Pierpont movingly describes his inner conflict, saying that he sometimes felt an almost physical revulsion at his own desires and would then think: “Good. I hate this.\xa0 I hate sin, just like God hates sin.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Search of the True Self', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T21:35:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Knobe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Knobe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba7e0c73-099c-5864-a96b-30d4882a2de2', 'word_count': 1380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba7e0c73-099c-5864-a96b-30d4882a2de2'}, {'abstract': 'The 54th Venice Biennale, on view until November, includes a diverse array of exhibitions, from 89 countries, scattered across the city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/05/arts/design/20110606-venice-ss.html', 'snippet': 'The 54th Venice Biennale, on view until November, includes a diverse array of exhibitions, from 89 countries, scattered across the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 54th Venice Biennale, on view until November, includes a diverse array of exhibitions, from 89 countries, scattered across the city.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/05/arts/design/20110606-venice-ss-slide-CSTH/20110606-venice-ss-slide-CSTH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At the Bienniale', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venice Biennale', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T21:40:30+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/982375d1-2d82-557b-bcbc-5188db5da889', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/982375d1-2d82-557b-bcbc-5188db5da889'}, {'abstract': 'Pandora’s primary competition is in the analog dashboard of terrestrial radio, and unlike Sirius XM, the company depends primarily on advertising.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/business/06views.html', 'snippet': 'Pandora’s primary competition is in the analog dashboard of terrestrial radio, and unlike Sirius XM, the company depends primarily on advertising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pandora Media is distinctly Web 2.0. Its Music Genome Project tailors Internet radio stations to individual tastes. Pandora is using that digital DNA to capitalize on investor demand for hot technology plays, with an initial public offering that could value the company at $1.4 billion. But investors should be going in with their eyes, and not just their ears, wide open. Pandora’s business model is pretty old school.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/business/views/views-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Pandora’s Valuation, a Few Sour Notes', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Listen Carefully For the Sour Notes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pandora.com', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T21:52:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Goldfarb and Wayne Arnold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldfarb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Wayne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arnold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/966ec55f-9ab0-54d8-8e4b-99a43efe1608', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/966ec55f-9ab0-54d8-8e4b-99a43efe1608'}, {'abstract': 'Liturgy, the Brooklyn black-metal band, played at the Knitting Factory in celebration of the release of its second album, “Aesthethica.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/arts/music/liturgy-at-the-knitting-factory-review.html', 'snippet': 'Liturgy, the Brooklyn black-metal band, played at the Knitting Factory in celebration of the release of its second album, “Aesthethica.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hunter Hunt-Hendrix, the frontman and dogma director of Liturgy, the Brooklyn black-metal band, has a lot to say about the genre. He says it with his boyish mien, reminiscent of Shannon Hoon: soft, long hair; baby face; ever-present faint smile. He says it with his vocals, which are notionally the growls that predominate the music, though he delivers them gently, like a cat lapping up water. Mostly what he’s saying to purists is: Kiss off.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/LITURGY/LITURGY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 852, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/LITURGY/LITURGY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/LITURGY/LITURGY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'If You Celebrate Nihilism, Is It Somethingism?', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'If You Celebrate Nihilism, Is It Somethingism?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Liturgy (Music Group)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/579183a9-36f6-5241-953a-5d3b4b466295', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/579183a9-36f6-5241-953a-5d3b4b466295'}, {'abstract': 'Ten-Hut!', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/05/jug/', 'snippet': 'Ten-Hut!', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — There doesn’t seem to be a special occasion for this puzzle, but it’s a fine tribute nonetheless. Gary Whitehead offers us a shout out to WEST POINT, the military academy that has been churning out Army officers since 1802. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Jug Band Instrument', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3299f194-ae4e-541b-b72e-ce4addedd7e4', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3299f194-ae4e-541b-b72e-ce4addedd7e4'}, {'abstract': '“Phaedra” by Hans Werner Henze was given its American premiere by the Opera Company of Philadelphia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/arts/music/phaedra-by-hans-werner-henze-in-philadelphia-review.html', 'snippet': '“Phaedra” by Hans Werner Henze was given its American premiere by the Opera Company of Philadelphia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHILADELPHIA — Unsettling news from the financially struggling Philadelphia Orchestra, whose recent announcement of shortened seasons, a smaller ensemble and a populist programming agenda sent tremors of shock and dismay throughout the classical-music world, paints a bleak prognosis for progressive artistic initiatives here. But look at what’s going on in the city this month, and a different picture emerges.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/SUB-HEINZ/SUB-HEINZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 595, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/SUB-HEINZ/SUB-HEINZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/SUB-HEINZ/SUB-HEINZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Philadelphia, an Operatic Portrait of a Stepmother for the Ages', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Philadelphia, an Operatic Portrait of a Stepmother for the Ages', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Opera Company of Philadelphia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Henze, Hans Werner', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia Orchestra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:02:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf99826b-e9b9-58fa-bcc7-6bcf8424514e', 'word_count': 767, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf99826b-e9b9-58fa-bcc7-6bcf8424514e'}, {'abstract': 'Kaija Saariaho’s monodrama “Émilie” is the centerpiece of this year’s Spoleto Festival USA.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/arts/music/emilie-by-kaija-saariaho-at-spoleto-festival-usa.html', 'snippet': 'Kaija Saariaho’s monodrama “Émilie” is the centerpiece of this year’s Spoleto Festival USA.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARLESTON, S.C. — At a gathering like the Spoleto Festival USA here, the profusion of events large and small often yields revealing juxtapositions, not necessarily intended. Two unrelated programs last week showed the good and the bad of combining classical music with video projections, a recourse hailed in some quarters as an ideal way to attract new listeners in a visually oriented age if not the very salvation of the field.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/spoleto/spoleto-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 848, 'width': 863, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/spoleto/spoleto-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/spoleto/spoleto-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mistress of Voltaire, Set to Video', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Mistress of Voltaire, Set to Video', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spoleto Festival USA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Futral, Elizabeth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cahill, Sarah', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saariaho, Kaija', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:05:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1a49a38-eefa-51e6-b1bc-c0bf0014c624', 'word_count': 1010, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1a49a38-eefa-51e6-b1bc-c0bf0014c624'}, {'abstract': 'The Creole Choir of Cuba, composed of singers of Haitian background, made its United States debut at Brooklyn Academy of Music as part of the ¡Sí Cuba! Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/arts/music/creole-choir-of-cuba-in-si-cuba-festival-at-bam-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Creole Choir of Cuba, composed of singers of Haitian background, made its United States debut at Brooklyn Academy of Music as part of the ¡Sí Cuba! Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Music follows history’s paths. One leads to the Creole Choir of Cuba, which is known there as Grupo Vocal Desandann, the Choir of Descendants. The choir made its exhilarating United States debut on Saturday night at the Brooklyn Academy of Music as part of the ¡Sí Cuba! Festival. Desandann is a 10-member voice-and-percussion choir from the Cuban city of Camagüey, although only four men and five women performed Saturday because of the seemingly inevitable visa problems. The choir is made up of Cuban descendants of Haitians who were twice displaced: first from Africa to Haiti by the slave trade, then from Haiti to Cuba in waves dating back to Haiti’s 1790 revolution. Their families held on to the Haitian Creole language and to songs remembered through generations.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/CREOLE/CREOLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1012, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuban Visitors Share Sounds and Moves of Their Roots', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cuban Visitors Share Sounds and Moves of Their Roots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:07:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd4100b7-ebbf-545f-8cbb-ea563e375710', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd4100b7-ebbf-545f-8cbb-ea563e375710'}, {'abstract': 'The American Ballet Theater’s production of “Lady of the Camellias” was created in 1978 by John Neumeier, based on the novel by Alexandre Dumas fils.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/arts/dance/american-ballet-theater-lady-of-the-camellias-review.html', 'snippet': 'The American Ballet Theater’s production of “Lady of the Camellias” was created in 1978 by John Neumeier, based on the novel by Alexandre Dumas fils.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At a certain point in “Lady of the Camellias” — and that point is pretty much the beginning — the audience member driven to despair by the inanity before her is reduced to trivial entertainments like counting the number of times Armand Duval, the ballet’s petulant leading man, casts himself to the ground in a fit of something or other. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/camellias/camellias-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 757, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/camellias/camellias-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/camellias/camellias-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dreams of a Dying Courtesan', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dreams of a Dying Courtesan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Neumeier, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:09:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia La Rocco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Rocco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3d82a05-1264-5540-92aa-487eded10e41', 'word_count': 527, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3d82a05-1264-5540-92aa-487eded10e41'}, {'abstract': 'In “A Wild Surge of Guilty Passion” Ron Hansen delves into the real-life case of a wife who coaxed her lover into killing her husband for insurance money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/books/a-wild-surge-of-guilty-passion-by-ron-hansen-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “A Wild Surge of Guilty Passion” Ron Hansen delves into the real-life case of a wife who coaxed her lover into killing her husband for insurance money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before it became one of the most familiar premises for hard-boiled fiction and film noir, the tale of the wife who coaxed her lover into getting rid of her annoying husband and cashing in on his life insurance was an actual news event. As he writes in an essay called “Whence Novels Come” (posted on the Web site of Santa Clara University, where he teaches) Ron Hansen found this out only accidentally. But he has made it the basis for “A Wild Surge of Guilty Passion,” a novel that separates the mawkish tabloid murder case from its later, tougher incarnations.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/jpbooks/jpbooks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/jpbooks/jpbooks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/jpbooks/jpbooks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Affair, a Murder, a Sensation', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Affair, A Murder, A Sensation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hansen, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:14:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/43dde770-1bc4-5b26-b342-1fdf9b6a05c8', 'word_count': 1035, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43dde770-1bc4-5b26-b342-1fdf9b6a05c8'}, {'abstract': 'Counter)induction, the new-music ensemble, had its evening at Bargemusic, the floating chamber-music space, on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/arts/music/counterinduction-plays-wuorinen-at-bargemusic-review.html', 'snippet': 'Counter)induction, the new-music ensemble, had its evening at Bargemusic, the floating chamber-music space, on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Looking at a concert program on Friday and seeing that the opening work was by Charles Wuorinen, I was expecting the thorny, dense style — fascinating but difficult to penetrate—that is his trademark.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/barge/barge-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 960, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/barge/barge-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/06/arts/barge/barge-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Experimental Sounds, Soothed by the Waves', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Experimental Sounds, Soothed by the Waves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'counter)induction', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:18:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/71872222-5b68-58f7-8d69-5a46637c9b3e', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71872222-5b68-58f7-8d69-5a46637c9b3e'}, {'abstract': 'Why I plan to withdraw from my nomination to the Federal Reserve Board.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/opinion/06diamond.html', 'snippet': 'Why I plan to withdraw from my nomination to the Federal Reserve Board.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lexington, Mass.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When a Nobel Prize Isn’t Enough', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When a Nobel Prize Isn’t Enough', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nobel Prizes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shelby, Richard C', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:20:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By PETER A. DIAMOND', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'DIAMOND', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5686b02-50da-5c36-b1cc-790ae6856d2a', 'word_count': 850, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5686b02-50da-5c36-b1cc-790ae6856d2a'}, {'abstract': 'Maybe MTV has figured out a way to keep “Jersey Shore” on the air for eternity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/arts/television/more-jersey-shore.html', 'snippet': 'Maybe MTV has figured out a way to keep “Jersey Shore” on the air for eternity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe MTV has figured out a way to keep “Jersey Shore” on the air for eternity. (Cue the rapture predictions once more.) With production of Season 4 of this hit reality show about to be shown, the network said it had ordered a fifth season, to begin production this month in New Jersey. The cast members (including Snooki, below) evidently won’t have much time for a vacation between their on-camera vacations. On Friday MTV said that Season 4, based in Italy, would have its premiere on Aug. 4. “Jersey Shore” is, by far, the most popular series in MTV’s history.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More ‘Jersey Shore’', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More ‘Jersey Shore’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MTV Networks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:21:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/54f5ca53-4a73-5e51-ba4f-58f5008a4d63', 'word_count': 104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54f5ca53-4a73-5e51-ba4f-58f5008a4d63'}, {'abstract': 'An attack on a police station in the border state of Southern Kordofan raised concerns among Western diplomats about more violence before the south declares its independence on July 9.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/world/africa/06juba.html', 'snippet': 'An attack on a police station in the border state of Southern Kordofan raised concerns among Western diplomats about more violence before the south declares its independence on July 9.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JUBA, Sudan — Fighting broke out in a heavily militarized part of Sudan on Sunday, United Nations and Sudanese officials said, escalating tensions between the northern and southern areas of the country a month before it is to be officially divided.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Broader Conflict Is Feared as Fighting Breaks Out on the Border', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Broader Conflict Is Feared as Fighting Breaks Out on the Border', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Juba (Sudan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:22:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Kron and Jeffrey Gettleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gettleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3783388-3d7a-549a-a354-f40b6661aec2', 'word_count': 415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3783388-3d7a-549a-a354-f40b6661aec2'}, {'abstract': 'The Upright Citizens Brigade Theater will start a weekly hourlong live radio show on SiriusXM Radio.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/arts/upright-citizens-brigade-joins-siriusxm-radio.html', 'snippet': 'The Upright Citizens Brigade Theater will start a weekly hourlong live radio show on SiriusXM Radio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Upright Citizens Brigade Theater will start a weekly hourlong live radio show on SiriusXM Radio, that satellite radio company announced. “Live From UCB Theater” will record a different show each week at its theaters in New York and Los Angeles, showcasing the comedy troupe’s usual mix of improv, sketch comedy and stand-up. The first episode will run at 11 p.m. on Saturday on the radio service’s Raw Dog Comedy Channel. The partnership increases the potential audience for Upright Citizens Brigade performances from 245, the maximum number of seats sold at its two theaters combined, to 20.6 million, the reported number of SiriusXM subscribers. “This is just one step in our secret evil plan to take over the world! Oops,” Amy Poehler, a founding member of the group, said in a statement. The initial shows will include “Totally J/K,” hosted by Joe Mande and Noah Garfinkel; “Waiting for Obama: A Night at the Hall of Presidents”; and “The Roast of Justin Bieber.” “We were always told we had a face for radio,” Ms. Poehler said. “We just never knew it would be satellite.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Upright Citizens Brigade Joins SiriusXM Radio', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Upright Citizens Brigade Joins SiriusXM Radio', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Upright Citizens Brigade', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-05T22:23:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename':