], 'headline': {'main': 'What Limits Should There Be on Teachers Writing Publicly About Students?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T08:39:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d463db49-62f9-54ae-a44d-1e16a9b10702', 'word_count': 364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d463db49-62f9-54ae-a44d-1e16a9b10702'}, {'abstract': 'Washington is rarely short on theatrics, but they were particularly abundant Sunday night as Broadway traveled south for the annual Ford’s Theatre Gala.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/lent-influential-ears-the-arts-salute-education/', 'snippet': 'Washington is rarely short on theatrics, but they were particularly abundant Sunday night as Broadway traveled south for the annual Ford’s Theatre Gala.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Washington is rarely short on theatrics, but they were particularly abundant Sunday night as Broadway traveled south for the annual Ford’s Theater Gala. Bringing together the worlds of art and politics, the invitation-only benefit, “Lincoln’s Legacy and a New Era of Hope,” celebrated President Abraham Lincoln’s hope for a world of tolerance and acceptance — with a call for influential guests to do their part to make it a reality.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lent Influential Ears, the Arts Salute Education', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T08:46:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_na