ds of the work from before 1903, is both familiar and transformative.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/arts/dance/giselle-from-pacific-northwest-ballet-review.html', 'snippet': 'Pacific Northwest Ballet’s “Giselle,” based on three detailed production records of the work from before 1903, is both familiar and transformative.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEATTLE — “Giselle,” the two-act Romantic ballet about (among much else) love beyond death, will be 170 on June 28. Because Peter Boal’s new production for Pacific Northwest Ballet is based on research by the dance historians Doug Fullington and Marian Smith, and in particular on three detailed production records from between 1842 and 1903, I went expecting to see features I had never encountered in the many productions I have watched over the last 36 years. I was not disappointed. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/arts/GISELLE/GISELLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 540, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/arts/GISELLE/GISELLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/07/arts/GISELLE/GISELLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At 170, a Venerable Ballet Returns to Its (Quicker) Roots', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At 170, a Venerable Ballet Returns to Its (Quicker) Roots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pacific Northwest Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T21:35:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/86567df1-9f90-59ec-8637-90a5bf277257', 'word_count': 1106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86567df1-9f90-59ec-8637-90a5bf277257'}, {'abstract': 'Lance Gries, a former member of the Trisha Brown Dance Company, presented a new solo, “Etudes for an Astronaut,” his first show in New York in 10 years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/arts/dance/lance-gries-at-la-mama-moves-dance-festival-review.html', 'snippet': 'Lance Gries, a former member of the Trisha Brown Dance Company, presented a new solo, “Etudes for an Astronaut,” his first show in New York in 10 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'La MaMa Moves! Dance Festival, now in its sixth year, is frustratingly exhaustive, the kind of event that is overrun with too many mixed bills. But on Friday night there was an exception: Lance Gries, a former member of the Trisha Brown Dance Company, presented a new solo, his first show in New York in 10 years. “Etudes for an Astronaut,” described in program notes as “one man’s search for his place in the universe,” was already in motion as the audience entered La MaMa’s intimate club space. In head-to-toe black and flip-flops Mr. Gries repeated a movement sequence that gave his body — through deceptively simple means of sitting, standing, leaning and rolling — a sense of melting and hardening. In “Etudes” he is searching for balance between inner space (as in the mind) and outer space (the world).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/arts/ASTRONAUT/ASTRONAUT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 602, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/arts/ASTRONAUT/ASTRONAUT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/07/arts/ASTRONAUT/ASTRONAUT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Under the Milky Way, in Suspended Reality', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Under the Milky Way, In Suspended Reality', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'La MaMa ETC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gries, Lance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T21:37:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a43b4a4-c49d-50dd-86d6-6333af97d67e', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a43b4a4-c49d-50dd-86d6-6333af97d67e'}, {'abstract': 'The wine panel recently tasted 20 dry reds from Roussillon, a sunny region in France.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/dining/reviews/reds-from-roussillon-into-the-sunlight-wine-review.html', 'snippet': 'The wine panel recently tasted 20 dry reds from Roussillon, a sunny region in France.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT’S not easy being an afterthought, yet that’s exactly what has happened to the tail of the fused French compound region commonly referred to in wine books as Languedoc-Roussillon.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/dining/08wine-span/Wines-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 326, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/08/dining/08wine-span/Wines-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 326}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/dining/08wine-span/Wines-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 557, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/dining/08wine-span/Wines-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/08/dining/08wine-span/Wines-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Roussillon, Into the Sunlight', 'kicker': 'Wines of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Roussillon, Into the Sunlight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T21:38:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'subsection_name': 'Dining & Wine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/674a1afd-726e-5b93-9297-75820518d6cd', 'word_count': 1287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/674a1afd-726e-5b93-9297-75820518d6cd'}, {'abstract': 'So what do we really know about cellphones and health? Here are some answers to common questions about the issue.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/piercing-the-fog-around-cellphones-and-cancer/', 'snippet': 'So what do we really know about cellphones and health? Here are some answers to common questions about the issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The debate about cellphone safety was reignited yet again last week when a panel of the World Heath Organization declared that it was “possible” the phones could cause cancer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Piercing the Fog Around Cellphones and Cancer', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bluetooth Wireless Technology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Headphones and Headsets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radiation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tumors', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Agency for Research on Cancer', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Health Organization', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T21:38:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d23e75f-c614-5a1c-b47e-03446e3fd5d7', 'word_count': 1118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d23e75f-c614-5a1c-b47e-03446e3fd5d7'}, {'abstract': 'How a 17-year-old immigrant came to America and assumed a succession of identities, eventually passing himself off as one Clark Rockefeller.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/books/the-man-in-the-rockefeller-suit-by-mark-seal-review.html', 'snippet': 'How a 17-year-old immigrant came to America and assumed a succession of identities, eventually passing himself off as one Clark Rockefeller.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It sounds like the plot of a movie: “Catch Me if You Can,” the 2002 high-spirited Steven Spielberg thriller about a serial impostor named Frank Abagnale Jr., who managed to pass himself off as an airline pilot, a doctor and a lawyer, while making millions of dollars forging checks. Or maybe something darker, like Alfred Hitchcock’s 1943 classic, “Shadow of a Doubt,” about a man wanted for murder who hides out with his sister’s family in a quiet California community where he charms the unsuspecting townsfolk.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/arts/jp-book/jp-book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/arts/jp-book/jp-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/07/arts/jp-book/jp-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fooling Them All With a Big Name', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fooling Them All With a Big Name', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rockefeller, Clark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T21:43:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/31fbeaf6-dff1-541a-a62e-e054a52883a0', 'word_count': 1027, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31fbeaf6-dff1-541a-a62e-e054a52883a0'}, {'abstract': 'Traditional classroom learning is generally rules first, application later. However, researchers are finding that repeated exposure to patterns seems to deepen understanding.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/health/07learn.html', 'snippet': 'Traditional classroom learning is generally rules first, application later. However, researchers are finding that repeated exposure to patterns seems to deepen understanding.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like any other high school junior, Wynn Haimer has a few holes in his academic game. Graphs and equations, for instance: He gets the idea, fine — one is a linear representation of the other — but making those conversions is often a headache.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/science/07learningspan/07learningspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/07/science/07learningspan/07learningspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/science/07learningspan/07learningspan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'im