e/994c9d15-271f-51a6-87c1-d51837d3df7a'}, {'abstract': 'Game 3 of the National Basketball Association finals topped the ratings on Sunday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/arts/television/abc-is-a-winner-with-the-nba.html', 'snippet': 'Game 3 of the National Basketball Association finals topped the ratings on Sunday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Game 3 of the National Basketball Association finals topped the ratings on Sunday night, lifting ABC far ahead of its network rivals, which broadcast mostly repeats in prime time. Final audience figures were not available on Monday for ABC’s live basketball coverage of the Miami Heat’s victory over the Dallas Mavericks, but Nielsen’s preliminary overnight estimates were higher for Sunday’s broadcast than for either of the first two games in the series, which attracted 15.5 million and 15.2 million viewers last week. CBS ranked second on the night, garnering its best ratings at 7 p.m. for “60 Minutes” (9.4 million). CBS broadcast a repeat of “Undercover Boss” at 9 (4.6 million) between reruns of “CSI: Miami” at 8 (5.7 million) and at 10 (6.1 million). NBC was third with “Dateline” at 7 (3.7 million) followed by “Minute to Win It” at 8 (2.9 million) and a rerun of last week’s installment of “America’s Got Talent” from 9 to 11 (5.2 million). Fox finished fourth with repeats of its animated sitcoms.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ABC Is a Winner With the N.B.A.', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'ABC Is a Winner With the N.B.A.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T22:02:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/38af7123-ad22-570f-b1d9-b7f9d43ce5ad', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38af7123-ad22-570f-b1d9-b7f9d43ce5ad'}, {'abstract': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art is not the only cultural institution around playing with its prices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/arts/dance/new-york-city-ballet-adjusts-its-ticket-pricing.html', 'snippet': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art is not the only cultural institution around playing with its prices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art is not the only cultural institution around playing with its prices (raising its suggested admission fee to $25 from $20 as of July 1). New York City Ballet has also adjusted its prices for subscription tickets in Lincoln Center’s David H. Koch Theater for its 2011-12 season in an effort to increase ticket revenue by 5 to 10 percent, the company’s spokesman, Robert Daniels, said in a telephone interview. City Ballet, whose dancers include, at left, Robert Fairchild and Teresa Reichlen, is not simply raising prices. Instead, it is offering a wider range of prices within each section, with some seats becoming more affordable and others more expensive. For example, seats in the orchestra, which in the 2010-11 season have ranged from $85 to $100, will now be $26 to $119. (Orchestra seats for American Ballet Theater performances at the Metropolitan Opera House run $30 to $102.) Seats in the third ring, formerly $50 or $60, will now range from $26 to $103. “New audiences especially want to come in, want to sample the company at a lower ticket price,” Mr. Daniels said, “so we wanted some of those ticket prices to be available in the orchestra level, where they didn’t used to be.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York City Ballet Adjusts Its Ticket Pricing', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York City Ballet Adjusts Its Ticket Pricing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T22:03:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/da369fe9-1284-5e69-b839-1a01be239ca1', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da369fe9-1284-5e69-b839-1a01be239ca1'}, {'abstract': 'George Balanchine’s “Jewels,” choreographed in 1967, is simple and complex, thought-provoking and crowd-pleasing, deeply traditional and utterly modern.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/arts/dance/balanchines-jewels-at-new-york-city-ballet-review.html', 'snippet': 'George Balanchine’s “Jewels,” choreographed in 1967, is simple and complex, thought-provoking and crowd-pleasing, deeply traditional and utterly modern.', 'lead_paragraph': 'George Balanchine’s “Jewels,” choreographed in 1967, is an anthology of ballet history, a densely packed guide to the evolution of classical dance over the last 150 years. Or it’s simply a marvelously entertaining compilation of three linked yet unrelated ballets — “Emeralds,” “Rubies” and “Diamonds” — to wonderful music by Fauré, Stravinsky and Tchaikovsky.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/arts/JEWELS/JEWELS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height':