ultimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brain Trauma and Art (1 Letter)', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brain Trauma and Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T22:31:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccfab8ed-c856-53cc-ab0d-a8137b6991f0', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccfab8ed-c856-53cc-ab0d-a8137b6991f0'}, {'abstract': 'Syrian activists have found a new source of amateur video to undercut reports on state-controlled television reports blaming them for weeks of violence: recordings made by members of the security forces.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/syrian-soldiers-record-trophy-videos-of-dead-protesters/', 'snippet': 'Syrian activists have found a new source of amateur video to undercut reports on state-controlled television reports blaming them for weeks of violence: recordings made by members of the security forces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While the Syrian government continues to insist that it is facing not an uprising by peaceful protesters but a rebellion by “armed groups,” a different pictures emerges in video clips apparently recorded inside Syria’s besieged cities by members of the Syrian security forces.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Syrian Soldiers Record ‘Trophy Videos’ of Dead Protesters', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T22:36:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e56a2be5-a3dc-54e2-8da3-397a4d33bea2', 'word_count': 651, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e56a2be5-a3dc-54e2-8da3-397a4d33bea2'}, {'abstract': 'A 20th century voice for meshing innovation and environmental progress speaks to a new century.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/a-despairing-optimist-considered-anew/', 'snippet': 'A 20th century voice for meshing innovation and environmental progress speaks to a new century.', 'lead_paragraph': '8:06 p.m. | Updated When I was in college in the 1970’s, I became a devotee of René Dubos — a scientist and essayist who combined extraordinary achievements in microbiology, most notably his methodical discovery of the first practical antibiotic, with a passion for forging a sustainable human relationship with the environment. I had the good fortune of interviewing him in his office at Rockefeller University in the fall of 1981, just a few months (as it turned out) before his death on Feb. 20, 1982 — his 81st birthday.*', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A ‘Despairing Optimist’ Considered Anew', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Microbiology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rockefeller University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T22:38:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ca8f76f-1dcf-5629-a21d-50aa11cabde9', 'word_count': 3225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ca8f76f-1dcf-5629-a21d-50aa11cabde9'}, {'abstract': 'Rising concerns about nuclear power coupled with hugely expanded estimates of gas reserves lead to a revamped energy future.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/are-we-entering-a-golden-age-of-gas/', 'snippet': 'Rising concerns about nuclear power coupled with hugely expanded estimates of gas reserves lead to a revamped energy future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That’s the question posed — and largely answered with a “yes” — in a new report from the International Energy Agency on the place of natural gas in the global energy mix for decades to come. This echoes the assessment of the Department of Energy’s Energy Information Administration now long ago. The news release heading on the new report summarizes the forces in play:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are We Entering a Golden Age of Gas?', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drilling and Boring', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shale', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Energy Information Administration', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Energy Agency', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North America', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T22:39:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0df6ed9f-5d93-58ce-9f2a-a0fd6350544b', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0df6ed9f-5d93-58ce-9f2a-a0fd6350544b'}, {'abstract': 'Rose Apocada, the author of a new book on Fred Hayman, shares some tales about the man behind Giorgio Beverly Hills.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/bookshelf-fred-hayman-and-the-story-of-rodeo-drive/', 'snippet': 'Rose Apocada, the author of a new book on Fred Hayman, shares some tales about the man behind Giorgio Beverly Hills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, Fred Hayman, the “Godfather of Rodeo Drive,” was welcomed into an elite club (Giorgio Armani, Tom Ford, Donatella Versace, etc.), \xa0when he received a Rodeo Drive Walk of Style award at the age of 86. The honor comes at a fortuitous time. Later this month, “Fred Hayman: The Story of Rodeo Drive, Hollywood Glamour and the Showman Who Sold It All” will be available in stores. Its author, Rose Apodaca, a West Coast journalist and former Los Angeles bureau chief for WWD, shares some tales about the man behind Giorgio’s with T’s Rachel Waldman.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bookshelf | Fred Hayman and the Story of Rodeo Drive', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T22:39:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Waldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31cd67f3-92b5-5bde-9e65-fc5e336470a9', 'word_count': 1451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31cd67f3-92b5-5bde-9e65-fc5e336470a9'}, {'abstract': 'Barclays Capital has promoted Greg Dalvito and Richard Hardegree to be the firm’s co-heads of technology mergers and acquisitions.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/barclays-names-co-heads-for-tech-m-a/', 'snippet': 'Barclays Capital has promoted Greg Dalvito and Richard Hardegree to be the firm’s co-heads of technology mergers and acquisitions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Barclays Capital has promoted Greg Dalvito and Richard Hardegree to be the firm’s co-heads of technology mergers and acquisitions. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Barclays Names Co-Heads for Tech M.&A.', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AT&T Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays PLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cisco Systems Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'UBS AG', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Union Square (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-06T22:43:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fd62ad6-8921-540c-a6c1-0bb5fd66d9df', 'word_count': 592, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fd62ad6-8921-540c-a6c1-0bb5fd66d9df'}, {'abstract': 'When Ronald Reagan asked voters “Are you better off than you were four years ago?” dwelling on President Jimmy Carter’s economic and foreign policy failures, voters decided that they weren’t, and Mr. Reagan became the 40th president. Against this background, it’s worth considering the argument that President Barack Obama and the Republican nominee will have about the state of the economy.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/06/the-ten-word-question-that-could-cost-obama-the-election/', 'snippet': 'When Ronald Reagan asked voters “Are you better off than you were four years ago?” dwelling on President Jimmy Carter’s economic and foreign policy failures, voters decided that they weren’t, and Mr. Reagan became the 40th president. Against this ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The year 1980 was one in which economic forecasting models were in agreement that the economy had slumped too significantly to allow President Jimmy Carter to win re-election. Unemployment was at 7.5 percent and inflation was at 9.7 percent. Per-capita GDP and disposable income growth, adjusted for inflation, had been negative over the course of Mr. Carter’s term. The economy was officially in recession for much of the election year. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 10-Word Question That Could Cost Obama the Election', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4