', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c3ef4b7-9481-5e4e-a4e9-2074fc49e3d7', 'word_count': 938, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c3ef4b7-9481-5e4e-a4e9-2074fc49e3d7'}, {'abstract': 'If the House ethics investigation shows that Representative Weiner broke any House rules in his Internet relationships with women, he should resign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/opinion/07tue3.html', 'snippet': 'If the House ethics investigation shows that Representative Weiner broke any House rules in his Internet relationships with women, he should resign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than a week, Representative Anthony Weiner, a New York City Democrat, baldly lied in denying that he had sent a lewd photo of himself to a woman over Twitter, claiming that his account was hacked. When he finally and tearfully confessed the truth on Monday, it turned out to be worse than expected: He admitted to a longstanding pattern of sending inappropriate photos to women.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘I Don’t Know What I Was Thinking’', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘I Don’t Know What I Was Thinking’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Anthony D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T01:18:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'o