ing Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea46e4ba-41bc-583f-a6fa-bb668d229623', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea46e4ba-41bc-583f-a6fa-bb668d229623'}, {'abstract': 'Who are Rafael Nadal, Bjorn Borg, Roger Federer, Li Na and Francesca Schiavone?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/big-victories-at-the-french-open/', 'snippet': 'Who are Rafael Nadal, Bjorn Borg, Roger Federer, Li Na and Francesca Schiavone?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHO are Rafael Nadal, Bjorn Borg and Roger Federer?WHO are Li Na and Francesca Schiavone?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Victories at the French Open', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T08:38:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd0ca0dd-93db-5bd5-854a-ff8f96f83a91', 'word_count': 85, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd0ca0dd-93db-5bd5-854a-ff8f96f83a91'}, {'abstract': 'Erin Trieb spent time with the 10th Mountain Division in Afghanistan but found fierce battles closer to home.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/home-from-afghanistan-to-new-battles/', 'snippet': 'Erin Trieb spent time with the 10th Mountain Division in Afghanistan but found fierce battles closer to home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Afghanistan with the Third Brigade of the 10th Mountain Division, the photojournalist Erin Trieb faced firefights, anxieties about land mines and the strangeness of being the only woman among hundreds of alpha males. Then came the hard part.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Home From Afghanistan to New Battles', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fort Drum (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T09:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f86fa36-2349-5820-94e1-23cbb922bb32', 'word_count': 919, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f86fa36-2349-5820-94e1-23cbb922bb32'}, {'abstract': 'Do you receive a paper or digital report card? Which would you prefer? Do you save it? What kinds of conversations have you had about your report card with your parents?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/do-you-talk-about-report-cards-with-your-parents/', 'snippet': 'Do you receive a paper or digital report card? Which would you prefer? Do you save it? What kinds of conversations have you had about your report card with your parents?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Complaint Box column on City Room this week, a mother laments the fact that report cards now are sent by snail mail or by e-mail, unlike in the past, when students received a paper report card at school and had to bring it home to show their parents. Do you get your report card at school, or do your parents receive it by mail or e-mail? Which method would you prefer? Do you ever save them as keepsakes? Do you review and discuss your grades with your parents? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do You Talk About Report Cards With Your Parents?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T09:26:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1380d46-daa4-56df-9776-e0bc4f1b2b8c', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1380d46-daa4-56df-9776-e0bc4f1b2b8c'}, {'abstract': 'A move to put distance between the two groups could smooth the path for peace negotiations with the Taliban, but the challenges remain considerable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/world/asia/08afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'A move to put distance between the two groups could smooth the path for peace negotiations with the Taliban, but the challenges remain considerable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — A top United Nations official said Tuesday that the Taliban in Afghanistan should be treated separately from Al Qaeda extremists as nations weigh sanctions against the two groups, a step that could help clear a path for negotiations with the Taliban. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Separate Sanctions Urged for Al Qaeda and Taliban', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Separate Sanctions Urged For Al Qaeda and Taliban', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T09:58:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rod Nordland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rod', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nordland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3478d06c-f88f-5751-9516-4b9bac6fc050', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3478d06c-f88f-5751-9516-4b9bac6fc050'}, {'abstract': 'The notion that Europeans pay much higher taxes that Americans does not acknowledge that in return they generally receive health care, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/health-care-costs-and-the-tax-burden/', 'snippet': 'The notion that Europeans pay much higher taxes that Americans does not acknowledge that in return they generally receive health care, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bruce Bartlett held senior policy roles in the Reagan and George H.W. Bush administrations and servedon the staffs of Representatives Jack Kemp and Ron Paul.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Your Taxes Do (and Don’t) Buy for You', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Tax', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T10:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Bartlett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middl