$1.4 billion, with an initial public offering of part of its subsidiary, the glass container maker Verallia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saint-Gobain Seeks Up to $1.4 Billion With Verallia I.P.O.', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'COMPAGNIE DE SAINT-GOBAIN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T10:05:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bf54de5-9024-5328-8ad6-b3fca119ec11', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bf54de5-9024-5328-8ad6-b3fca119ec11'}, {'abstract': 'What does it mean to live in New York, to be challenged in New York, to work like a New Yorker works, and how does one move beyond New York, if ever?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/oliver-broudy-on-moving-beyond-new-york/', 'snippet': 'What does it mean to live in New York, to be challenged in New York, to work like a New Yorker works, and how does one move beyond New York, if ever?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recently, The Paris Review blog ran a Q. and A. with Oliver Broudy about his forthcoming book, “The Saint.” And in that Q. and A., Broudy responded to a question about his relationship with New York, and he answered (for himself) the much larger New York question: What does it mean to live in New York, to be challenged in New York, to work like a New Yorker works, and how does one move beyond New York, if ever? And like all New Yorkers, I am drawn to anything that helps me to understand what it means to live in this city. This paragraph deserved to be saved, reread and questioned by other New Yorkers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oliver Broudy on Moving Beyond New York', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paris Review', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T10:18:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Zilar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zilar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90cab5c6-3768-5d0f-83cf-94cf074af6ae', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90cab5c6-3768-5d0f-83cf-94cf074af6ae'}, {'abstract': 'Security researchers have found thousands of photos from searches within the Google Images site that have been infected with malicious code. Here’s what to do to avoid them.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/qa-poisoned-pictures-on-the-web/', 'snippet': 'Security researchers have found thousands of photos from searches within the Google Images site that have been infected with malicious code. Here’s what to do to avoid them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Poisoned Pictures on the Web', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Malware', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Security and Warning Systems', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T10:57:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a125604e-439f-54a0-bbc3-83cd6b703005', 'word_count': 241, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a125604e-439f-54a0-bbc3-83cd6b703005'}, {'abstract': 'Water quality in Zhejiang Province was returning to normal, officials said, more than two days after a truck collision led to a spill of carbolic acid that tainted drinking water for thousands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/world/asia/08spill.html', 'snippet': 'Water quality in Zhejiang Province was returning to normal, officials said, more than two days after a truck collision led to a spill of carbolic acid that tainted drinking water for thousands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Water quality in the coastal Zhejiang Province was returning to normal, Chinese officials said Tuesday, more than two days after a collision of two heavy trucks spilled 20 tons of carbolic acid into a river that supplies drinking water for hundreds of thousands of people, including one major city, Hangzhou.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Drinking Water in Chinese Province Returning to Normal After Spill', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Crisis Eases Over Acid Spill in River', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACIDS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T10:58:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd64f90c-ba20-5b15-99b9-754549d52a5c', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd64f90c-ba20-5b15-99b9-754549d52a5c'}, {'abstract': 'Whether they are causing problems with customers, stealing, breaking things, suing you, or doing something that gets you in trouble with a regulatory agency, employees can be trouble.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/would-your-business-be-better-without-employees/', 'snippet': 'Whether they are causing problems with customers, stealing, breaking things, suing you, or doing something that gets you in trouble with a regulatory agency, employees can be trouble.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I recently visited a business owner’s facility. As our meeting ended, she turned to me and said, “Wouldn’t it be great if you could run a business without employees?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Would Your Business Be Better Without Employees?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Resources', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T11:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Goltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organizatio