'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/07/multimedia/video-tonys-joshuahenry/video-tonys-joshuahenry-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/multimedia/video-tonys-joshuahenry/video-tonys-joshuahenry-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Joshua Henry Performs', 'kicker': 'TONY AWARDS', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Scottsboro Boys, The (Play)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T14:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Tony Awards', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/4af3a199-5b9f-5bd5-9887-914cb5748a7a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/4af3a199-5b9f-5bd5-9887-914cb5748a7a'}, {'abstract': 'Anthony Weiner’s incomplete contrition.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/how-can-we-forgive-you-if-you-wont-go-away/', 'snippet': 'Anthony Weiner’s incomplete contrition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At a lunchtime conversation yesterday, just before Anthony Weiner used a tearstained press conference to confess to being both a serial Twitterer of tasteless amateur photography and an exceptionally ineffective liar, a colleague of mine made the case for forgiveness in such cases. His point, at least as I understood it, was that most politicians are raging narcissists of one stripe or another, walking a tightrope between flagrant egotism and true pathology. But public figures who pass through the fire of a major scandal-driven humiliation have a rare chance to come to terms with that narcissism’s catastrophic consequences, and as a result many of them probably come out of these kind of self-inflicted disasters as better people — and perhaps, by implication, better public servants — than your typical Joe Q. Politician.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Can We Forgive You If You Won’t Go Away?', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T14:39:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4f74043-dbd3-5543-af2f-de9c05c08c33', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4f74043-dbd3-5543-af2f-de9c05c08c33'}, {'abstract': 'We are planning a two-week stay in Italy in mid-September. We will spend one week in Rome and would like to spend the second week at a resort location within a reasonable driving distance. Someone suggested taking a ferry to Ponza. I would welcome your suggestions. Peter F. Eder, Darien, Conn.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/q-a-a-week-on-ponza-italy/', 'snippet': 'We are planning a two-week stay in Italy in mid-September. We will spend one week in Rome and would like to spend the second week at a resort location within a reasonable driving distance. Someone suggested taking a ferry to Ponza. I would welcome...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q & A: A Week on Ponza, Italy', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T14:40:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Rivas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rivas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf486f9e-8d1d-5e01-a75b-77f22768fe9a', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf486f9e-8d1d-5e01-a75b-77f22768fe9a'}, {'abstract': 'The news as sex ed.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/explaining-anthony-weiner-to-children/', 'snippet': 'The news as sex ed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Link for today:  Protecting Children on the Internet, by Congressman Anthony D. Weiner.Really.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Explaining Anthony Weiner to Children', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pornography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T14:42:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16e92272-a1fe-568f-9233-13c815a79c2a', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16e92272-a1fe-568f-9233-13c815a79c2a'}, {'abstract': 'The British Board of Film Classification will not give even its strictest rating to “The Human Centipede II (Full Sequence),” saying that the film poses a “real risk” to audiences and that no amount of editing would make it acceptable.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/human-centipede-sequel-is-banned-in-britain/', 'snippet': 'The British Board of Film Classification will not give even its strictest rating to “The Human Centipede II (Full Sequence),” saying that the film poses a “real risk” to audiences and that no amount of editing would make it acceptable.', 'lead_paragraph': '12:51 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Human Centipede’ Sequel Is Banned in Britain', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Six, Tom', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Board of Film Classification', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Guardian (British Newspaper)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T14:47:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/168e280c-f823-562a-b77e-9102aba6c46a', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/168e280c-f823-562a-b77e-9102aba6c46a'}, {'abstract': 'Momofuku sweets are now more easily available in Brooklyn, I Trulli has a daytime venue and soon, there will be a bar at Chelsea Market.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/off-the-menu-milk-bar-brooklyn-enoteca-to-go-and-the-tippler/', 'snippet': 'Momofuku sweets are now more easily available in Brooklyn, I Trulli has a daytime venue and soon, there will be a bar at Chelsea Market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Momofuku restaurants have had a commissary in Williamsburg, Brooklyn for some time, with space for offices on the premises. And now they have created a retail outlet in a small ground floor space, where the Momofuku Milk Bar desserts will be sold starting on Wednesday. In addition, some specialties from the commissary, including pork buns and vegetable buns, and occasionally, other items, too, will be available. It’s open 9 a.m to 10 p.m., Sunday to Thursday; to 11 p.m. on Friday and Saturday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Menu: Milk Bar Brooklyn, Enoteca to Go and the Tippler', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T14:49:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9561a4f0-d802-5361-a7cc-80262b380682', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9561a4f0-d802-5361-a7cc-80262b380682'}, {'abstract': 'In a sudden escalation of NATO’s air campaign over Libya, warplanes dropped more than 80 bombs on targets in Tripoli.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/world/africa/08tripoli.html', 'snippet': 'In a sudden escalation of NATO’s air campaign over Libya, warplanes dropped more than 80 bombs on targets in Tripoli.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TRIPOLI, Libya — In a sudden, sharp escalation of NATO’s air campaign over Libya, warplanes dropped more than 80 bombs on targets in Tripoli in an assault that began Tuesday morning and continued into the predawn hours of Wednesday, obliterating large areas of Colonel Muammar el-Qaddafi’s Bab al-Aziziya command compound and what NATO identified as other military targets around the capital. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/world/TRIPOLI-2/TRIPOLI-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 544, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/world/TRIPOLI-2/TRIPOLI-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/08/world/TRIPOLI-2/TRIPOLI-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Qaddafi Compound Is Pounded in Day Raid', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Qaddafi Compound Is Pounded in Day Raid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T15:11:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/112f3fb8-fdc4-59fb-8c48-bece3cecf0c7', 'word_count': 1123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/112f3fb8-fdc4-59fb-8c48-bece3cecf0c7'}, {'abstract': 'The French finance minister appeared in India’s capital as part of a campaign to win support from emerging nations for her candidacy to lead the International Monetary Fund.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/business/global/08imf.html', 'snippet': 'The French finance minister appeared in India’s capital as part of a campaign to win support from emerging nations for her candidacy to lead the International Monetary Fund.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI — French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde promised Tuesday to support emerging markets so wholeheartedly that a little part of her “would turn Indian” if she were elected head of the International Monetary Fund. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/business/global/08imf-span/08imf-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/08/business/global/08imf-span/08imf-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/business/global/08imf-span/08imf-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On Global Charm Offensive, Lagarde Promises Support to Developing Countries', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Global Charm Offensive, Lagarde Promises Support to Developing Countries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lagarde, Christine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T15:12:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Timmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Timmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/446818ff-1df5-578b-8224-7dda00cd1f93', 'word_count': 844, 'uri': 'nyt://article/446818ff-1df5-578b-8224-7dda00cd1f93'}, {'abstract': 'Our sixth poem favorite, chosen from among 650 submissions to our Found Poem Student Challenge. We’ll publish four more.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/found-poem-favorite-heartbreakingly-innocent/', 'snippet': 'Our sixth poem favorite, chosen from among 650 submissions to our Found Poem Student Challenge. We’ll publish four more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s poem, chosen from more than 650 submissions to our Found Poem Student Challenge, comes from Imogen, who revealed neither an age nor a hometown.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Found Poem Favorite | ‘Heartbreakingly Innocent’', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T15:21:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63c422cf-2e82-5dbc-8ed4-04a998f371d4', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63c422cf-2e82-5dbc-8ed4-04a998f371d4'}, {'abstract': 'Are the nutrition decisions we make for our animals the right ones? We asked Dr. Tony Buffington, professor of veterinary nutrition at Ohio State University Veterinary Medical Center, to respond to a reader questions about pet nutrition.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/answers-about-feeding-dogs-and-cats/', 'snippet': 'Are the nutrition decisions we make for our animals the right ones? We asked Dr. Tony Buffington, professor of veterinary nutrition at Ohio State University Veterinary Medical Center, to respond to a reader questions about pet nutrition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dog and cat owners invest time, money and emotion into choosing the best foods for their pets, a phenomenon recently explored in Andrew Martin’s Business Day feature about the booming pet market. But are the nutrition decisions we make for our animals the right ones?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Answers About Feeding Dogs and Cats', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cats', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gluten', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labeling and Labels', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pet Foods', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pets', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterinary Medicine', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T15:37:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb5f56ab-e2bb-5f83-88ce-6d90ae6e4186', 'word_count': 765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb5f56ab-e2bb-5f83-88ce-6d90ae6e4186'}, {'abstract': 'What Sebastião Salgado sees in places untouched by humanity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/12/magazine/alaska-sebastiao-salgado.html', 'snippet': 'What Sebastião Salgado sees in places untouched by humanity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What Sebastião Salgado sees in places untouched by humanity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/Alaska-slide-C7XM/Alaska-slide-C7XM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/Alaska-slide-C7XM/Alaska-slide-C7XM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/Alaska-slide-C7XM/Alaska-slide-C7XM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Another World, Another Planet: Alaska’s North', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALASKA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARCTIC NATIONAL WILDLIFE REFUGE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T15:40:10+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/9e182230-6532-5627-866b-0200e6d498f8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/9e182230-6532-5627-866b-0200e6d498f8'}, {'abstract': 'Newly independent Kosovo cannot field an international team to play in any FIFA-sponsored events, but that doesn’t keep a group of Kosovars from forming a team for the Cosmos Copa NYC.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/team-kosovo-made-in-new-york/', 'snippet': 'Newly independent Kosovo cannot field an international team to play in any FIFA-sponsored events, but that doesn’t keep a group of Kosovars from forming a team for the Cosmos Copa NYC.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every four years soccer fans all over the world re-establish their sense of patriotism and national pride through the World Cup. But not only do many nations not qualify for the global party, some don’t even have a team that can attempt to qualify. Such is the fate of Kosovo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Team Kosovo, Made in New York', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEW YORK COSMOS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T15:58:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Belac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6d55ec6-ab9b-5191-8365-4f0fc00527ba', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6d55ec6-ab9b-5191-8365-4f0fc00527ba'}, {'abstract': 'The final works are complete for the main Belmont Stakes contenders.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/three-final-workouts-all-different-for-the-belmont/', 'snippet': 'The final works are complete for the main Belmont Stakes contenders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The final works are complete for the main Belmont Stakes contenders. The three main protagonists, the Kentucky Derby winner and Preakness runner-up Animal Kingdom, the Preakness winner Shackleford, and the Kentucky Derby runner-up Nehro, all completed different types of works for their final tune-up, but their connections all made the right noises afterward to signal their happiness with their horses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Three Final Workouts, All Different, for the Belmont', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Belmont Stakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4dae1f40-ee06-5d23-a041-fd074ffa48cc', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4dae1f40-ee06-5d23-a041-fd074ffa48cc'}, {'abstract': 'A measure in Albany would require operators of hotels and motels to provide sexual harassment training and create a system for reporting harassment or abuse.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/bill-aims-to-protect-hotel-workers-from-sexual-abuse/', 'snippet': 'A measure in Albany would require operators of hotels and motels to provide sexual harassment training and create a system for reporting harassment or abuse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two Democratic lawmakers have introduced legislation to require hotel and motel owners in New York State to provide sexual harassment training to their employees and to provide a clear system for reporting episodes of sexual abuse.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bill Aims to Protect Hotel Workers From Sexual Abuse', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexual Harassment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strauss-Kahn, Dominique', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:01:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f7bb656-c58b-5cee-8739-b33153cf8c7a', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f7bb656-c58b-5cee-8739-b33153cf8c7a'}, {'abstract': 'When section two of the High Line opens to the public tomorrow, more modest denizens of the neighborhood will have a piece of starchitecture to call their own.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/high-notes-new-art-on-the-high-line/', 'snippet': 'When section two of the High Line opens to the public tomorrow, more modest denizens of the neighborhood will have a piece of starchitecture to call their own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Section 2 of the High Line, which opens to the public tomorrow, passes just beyond Frank Gehry’s IAC building and Jean Nouvel’s 100 11th Avenue Residences one block to the west. With the installation of Sarah Sze’s “Still Life with Landscape (Model for a Habitat),” some of the more modest denizens of the neighborhood will have a piece of starchitecture to call their own. The sculpture, a dynamic steel and wood complex of bird, ladybug and butterfly condos, sits between 20th and 21st Streets, and serves as a sort of gateway onto the High Line microclimate known as the Chelsea Thicket. Among its noteworthy amenities are feeding trays that will be regularly restocked with seeds and nectar, and miniature troughs designed to catch rainwater. But as with any luxury real estate development — even one with a boldfaced name attached to it — its occupancy rate is not a guarantee. “It’s really an experiment,” says Lauren Ross, the curator and director of arts programs at Friends of the High Line, adding that Sze consulted various urban birding groups but received conflicting predictions. “It functions first and foremost as sculpture. We are not sure how wildlife will respond to it.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'High Notes | New Art on the High Line', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:06:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5792177a-a253-5673-8f4c-8c8c6422abe9', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5792177a-a253-5673-8f4c-8c8c6422abe9'}, {'abstract': 'The blog Gay Girl in Damascus reported that its author was detained on Monday in Damascus, in a post said to be written by a family member.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/syrian-american-blogger-detained/', 'snippet': 'The blog Gay Girl in Damascus reported that its author was detained on Monday in Damascus, in a post said to be written by a family member.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:17 p.m. Update: After this post about the author of the blog A Gay Girl in Damascus was published, Andy Carvin, an NPR journalist and expert at debunking Internet rumors, pointed out that none of the reports of the arrest of Amina Abdallah Arraf appeared to have been written by journalists who had previously met or interviewed her. A few hours after Mr. Carvin asked his network of followers on Twitter, “has anyone met Amina (Gay Girl In Damascus) in person?” he observed: “It’s just odd that I can’t find anyone who has actually met her in person.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Report of Disappearance, Questions About Syrian-American Blogger', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:15:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey and Liam Stack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Liam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b44298d-d6d2-5d4b-9d19-3cdc4562c3b8', 'word_count': 2129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b44298d-d6d2-5d4b-9d19-3cdc4562c3b8'}, {'abstract': 'The Federal Reserve and other regulatory agencies decide to extend the public comment period, which was set to expire on Friday, until Aug.  1', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/regulators-push-back-deadline-on-risk-retention-rules/', 'snippet': 'The Federal Reserve and other regulatory agencies decide to extend the public comment period, which was set to expire on Friday, until Aug.  1', 'lead_paragraph': 'Financial regulators have granted the public nearly two additional months to weigh in on controversial new mortgage securities restrictions, a nod to the mounting collection of banks and consumer groups scrambling to challenge the proposed rules. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Regulators Push Back Deadline on Risk-Retention Rules', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgage-Backed Securities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:18:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd46e81b-96e0-5f07-9da7-c38a007fc0d5', 'word_count': 409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd46e81b-96e0-5f07-9da7-c38a007fc0d5'}, {'abstract': 'Spc. Michael Gannon guides us through a mission on Qurghan Tapa Hill by sharing the video he captured on a helmet camera.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/multimedia/100000000851400/combat-in-the-first-person-mine-field.html', 'snippet': 'Spc. Michael Gannon guides us through a mission on Qurghan Tapa Hill by sharing the video he captured on a helmet camera.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Spc. Michael Gannon guides us through a mission on Qurghan Tapa Hill by sharing the video he captured on a helmet camera.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/multimedia/video-1-87-helmet-qthill/video-1-87-helmet-qthill-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/07/multimedia/video-1-87-helmet-qthill/video-1-87-helmet-qthill-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/07/multimedia/video-1-87-helmet-qthill/video-1-87-helmet-qthill-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/07/multimedia/video-1-87-helmet-qthill/video-1-87-helmet-qthill-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Combat in the First Person: Mine Field', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:29:03+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Nancy Donaldson|James Dao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': 'Donaldson', 'lastname': 'James', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/4bd37d4c-e8cb-50f5-adfe-0a440f9d2dfa', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/4bd37d4c-e8cb-50f5-adfe-0a440f9d2dfa'}, {'abstract': 'Mayor Bloomberg and the Council speaker, Christine C. Quinn, tried very hard to steer the subject at a news conference away from Representative Anthony D. Weiner, without much success.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/weiner-was-not-the-topic-really/', 'snippet': 'Mayor Bloomberg and the Council speaker, Christine C. Quinn, tried very hard to steer the subject at a news conference away from Representative Anthony D. Weiner, without much success.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg called a news conference on Tuesday to unveil an effort to improve safety in apartment buildings. But the reporters in attendance seemed more interested in another question: Should Representative Anthony D. Weiner resign?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weiner Was Not the Topic. Really.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quinn, Christine C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Anthony D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:29:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Javier C. Hernández', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Hernández', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/011abf02-a4fa-5294-8903-25762f4f2a9a', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/011abf02-a4fa-5294-8903-25762f4f2a9a'}, {'abstract': 'It’s not just that intensive agriculture causes nitrogen from fertilizer to volatilize into a major greenhouse gas, or that it washes into rivers; such farming is not sustainable over the long haul even on its own terms.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/can-the-yield-gap-be-closed-sustainably/', 'snippet': 'It’s not just that intensive agriculture causes nitrogen from fertilizer to volatilize into a major greenhouse gas, or that it washes into rivers; such farming is not sustainable over the long haul even on its own terms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In my article on Sunday about the future of the food system, I briefly mentioned the potential for improved agricultural output in Africa. The situation there is a prime example of a larger problem that concerns the world’s agronomists: the yield gap.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can the Yield Gap Be Closed — Sustainably?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Borlaug, Norman E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buffett, Warren E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Bill', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Melinda', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alliance for a Green Revolution in Africa (AGRA)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gates, Bill and Melinda, Foundation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Monsanto Co', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rockefeller Foundation', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Bank', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:35:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Gillis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/179bb466-3311-5fe5-9ddb-e1a1755756a5', 'word_count': 1577, 'uri': 'nyt://article/179bb466-3311-5fe5-9ddb-e1a1755756a5'}, {'abstract': 'Sony’s mishaps with users’ security provides ample fodder for Tweeting jokesters.', 'web_url': 'https://pogue.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/were-here-all-week-folks/', 'snippet': 'Sony’s mishaps with users’ security provides ample fodder for Tweeting jokesters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re not on Twitter, you’re missing all kinds of hilarity. I have no big point to make with today’s blog post — I just wanted to share some of the witty responses to a Tweet I sent out last week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'We’re Here All Week, Folks…', 'kicker': 'Pogue&#039;s Posts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:36:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29c4b1e5-4127-5aa9-b9ed-4fae84f25994', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29c4b1e5-4127-5aa9-b9ed-4fae84f25994'}, {'abstract': 'In-car music continues to move to the cloud.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/mog-streaming-music-service-syncs-up-with-car-audio-manufacturers/', 'snippet': 'In-car music continues to move to the cloud.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Later this week at the Telematics Detroit show, companies will highlight their efforts to put Web-served music into automobiles, with the hope of supplanting satellite and traditional radio. To that end, Mog, a subscription-based music service, announced several new partnerships on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mog, Streaming Music Service, Syncs Up With Car Audio Manufacturers', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mog', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pandora.com', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roku', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corporation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Visteon Automotive Systems', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'XM Satellite Radio Holdings Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:38:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By John R. Quain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Quain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50d94a01-1f35-5182-8ac1-1a0465f26ea8', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50d94a01-1f35-5182-8ac1-1a0465f26ea8'}, {'abstract': 'Two companies offer free versions of their identity-theft protection services.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/companies-offer-free-versions-of-identity-theft-services/', 'snippet': 'Two companies offer free versions of their identity-theft protection services.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The recent spate of computer hacking and security breaches has heightened interest in credit and identity-theft monitoring services. Two companies, TrustedID and Debix, have started offering free versions of their surveillance systems.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Companies Offer Free Versions of Identity Theft Services', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Identity Fraud', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:40:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca712db7-80d9-50ef-b8d9-5b4f885bb58a', 'word_count': 715, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca712db7-80d9-50ef-b8d9-5b4f885bb58a'}, {'abstract': 'Germany’s government came under increased attack by critics who accuse it of mismanaging the E. coli crisis that has killed at least 22 people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/world/europe/08ecoli.html', 'snippet': 'Germany’s government came under increased attack by critics who accuse it of mismanaging the E. coli crisis that has killed at least 22 people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — The German government came under increased criticism on Tuesday at home and abroad, accused of mismanaging the crisis surrounding an E. coli outbreak that has killed more than 20 people. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/world/SUB-ECOLI/SUB-ECOLI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/world/SUB-ECOLI/SUB-ECOLI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/08/world/SUB-ECOLI/SUB-ECOLI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Germany Is Criticized Even as E. Coli Outbreak Slows', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Germany Is Criticized Even as E. Coli Outbreak Slows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E Coli (Bacteria)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:41:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbba013d-a692-5f55-943e-bee0b3bed935', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbba013d-a692-5f55-943e-bee0b3bed935'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Pawlenty describes the tax and spending cuts he would favor as president, and decries what he calls President Obama’s “central planning.”', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/pawlenty-promises-high-growth-low-taxes-tight-spending/', 'snippet': 'Tim Pawlenty describes the tax and spending cuts he would favor as president, and decries what he calls President Obama’s “central planning.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tim Pawlenty, the former governor of Minnesota, called on Tuesday for across-the-board federal spending reductions and large tax cuts for businesses and individuals, saying that such policies would drive rapid economic growth, create jobs and reduce the federal debt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pawlenty Promises High Growth, Low Taxes and Tight Spending', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pawlenty, Tim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schultz, Debbie Wasserman', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Chicago', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:41:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9e874d1-9d39-59b0-9eba-927c0e6f6b24', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9e874d1-9d39-59b0-9eba-927c0e6f6b24'}, {'abstract': 'Yao Ming is hoping to make it back to the N.B.A., but a lot questions hang in the air, from his playing abilities to who will sign him.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/yao-still-hopes-for-n-b-a-comeback/', 'snippet': 'Yao Ming is hoping to make it back to the N.B.A., but a lot questions hang in the air, from his playing abilities to who will sign him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The clock is ticking for Yao Ming. His contract with the Houston Rockets expires June 30, and he’s still trying to recover from a serious left foot/ankle injury and surgery in January that cost him all but five games of the 2010-11 season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yao Still Hopes for N.B.A. Comeback', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McHale, Kevin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yao Ming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Houston Rockets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:42:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne C. Gerstner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Gerstner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd274e29-9df5-5b0b-b04f-63109e363c93', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd274e29-9df5-5b0b-b04f-63109e363c93'}, {'abstract': 'The newspaper admitted to intercepting the actress’s cellphone messages and using them to publish articles about her private life, a practice that was apparently pervasive at the paper.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/world/europe/08london.html', 'snippet': 'The newspaper admitted to intercepting the actress’s cellphone messages and using them to publish articles about her private life, a practice that was apparently pervasive at the paper.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The News of the World formally apologized to the actress Sienna Miller on Tuesday for systematically, and illegally, intercepting her cellphone messages in the mid-2000s and using the information to publish articles about her private life. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/world/LONDON/LONDON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/world/LONDON/LONDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/08/world/LONDON/LONDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'British Tabloid Apologizes to Actress for Hacking', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'British Tabloid Apologizes to Actress for Hacking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Sienna', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:44:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Lyall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e297f092-6972-5e71-bd1f-f701acef5278', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e297f092-6972-5e71-bd1f-f701acef5278'}, {'abstract': 'Shareholders will receive a dividend of as much as 2 euros per share as part of an effort to secure support for the merger of the two exchange operators.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/deutsche-borse-sweetens-nyse-bid/', 'snippet': 'Shareholders will receive a dividend of as much as 2 euros per share as part of an effort to secure support for the merger of the two exchange operators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NYSE Euronext and Deutsche Börse announced on Tuesday that they would pay shareholders a special dividend in an effort to secure support for their merger deal. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deutsche Borse Sweetens NYSE Bid', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deutsche Borse AG', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NYSE Euronext', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:48:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d18a94bf-073f-5dfb-b8f5-c110cfc15868', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d18a94bf-073f-5dfb-b8f5-c110cfc15868'}, {'abstract': 'Even better than parenting books is conversation about parenting books. Pull up a chair. I’ll bring the wine.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/introducing-the-motherlode-book-club/', 'snippet': 'Even better than parenting books is conversation about parenting books. Pull up a chair. I’ll bring the wine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My shelves are filled with parenting books. A good percentage of them are there because parenting is now my beat — new ones arrive in the mail every day and they are now piled everywhere. But my collection began long before Motherlode. Even before I was pregnant I figured that if I gathered enough of these volumes I would find the answers. Part of me still believes this, and an archaeologist could easily see what I was looking for over time by excavating down through the piles. “What to Expect When You’re Expecting,” “Your Baby and Child,” “Operating Instructions,” “Siblings Without Rivalry,” “The iConnected Parent,” “Parenting Your Adult Child.”  ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Introducing the Motherlode Book Club', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T16:54:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea1e4d7d-5020-531c-8608-69e07e97b7af', 'word_count': 946, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea1e4d7d-5020-531c-8608-69e07e97