'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Naples (Italy)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T18:32:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Kugel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kugel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dc7071e-4a20-5e03-bedb-fed74b704099', 'word_count': 1702, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dc7071e-4a20-5e03-bedb-fed74b704099'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Thiel, a prominent Silicon Valley investor, says we are not being carried away in a second tech bubble. The doomsayers are simply hungover from the last bubble’s burst, he said.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/thiel-tech-bubble-what-tech-bubble/', 'snippet': 'Peter Thiel, a prominent Silicon Valley investor, says we are not being carried away in a second tech bubble. The doomsayers are simply hungover from the last bubble’s burst, he said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There has been lots of chatter about a technology bubble in Silicon Valley. LinkedIn’s initial public offering, Groupon’s upcoming I.P.O. and bloated start-up investments and valuations are bringing back painful memories of the dot-com bubble’s burst roughly a decade ago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thiel: Tech Bubble? What Tech Bubble?', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thiel, Peter A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T18:34:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c36597a4-e37b-504d-9d06-30a79d36f0c7', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c36597a4-e37b-504d-9d06-30a79d36f0c7'}, {'abstract': 'President Bashar al-Assad’s brother, Maher, has emerged as a lightning rod of dissent over his control of elite units in the army.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/world/middleeast/08syria.html', 'snippet': 'President Bashar al-Assad’s brother, Maher, has emerged as a lightning rod of dissent over his control of elite units in the army.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As reports mount of defections in the Syrian military and the government staggers from the killing of soldiers and police officers in a northern city this week, President Bashar al-Assad may turn increasingly to his brother, Maher, whose elite units in a demoralized army could prove decisive to his government’s survival, activists and analysts say.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/world/JP-SYRIA/JP-SYRIA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/world/JP-SYRIA/JP-SYRIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/08/world/JP-SYRIA/JP-SYRIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Syrian Leader’s Brother Seen as Enforcer of Crackdown', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Assad Brother Plays Big Role In Ruling Syria', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Maher al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T18:35:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Zoepf and Anthony Shadid', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zoepf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shadid', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3df25bfd-ec12-5533-85cc-285679e70aec', 'word_count': 1336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3df25bfd-ec12-5533-85cc-285679e70aec'}, {'abstract': 'T caught up with Brian Donnelly, a k a KAWS, to talk about graffiti, New York and his sculpture at the Standard.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/the-kaws-effect/', 'snippet': 'T caught up with Brian Donnelly, a k a KAWS, to talk about graffiti, New York and his sculpture at the Standard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the back of Brian Donnelly’s studio in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, behind three wheeled, wooden work tables and neatly organized racks containing bottles of brightly colored acrylic paint, past an array of painted canvases in different shapes and sizes, hides a four-foot-tall sculpture of Mickey Mouse. Mickey has seen better days, apparently. He’s gray. And his eyes are x-ed out, as if he just drank from a bottle labeled “Poison.” Donnelly, 36, who is better known by his alias, KAWS, is getting ready to haul a 16-foot-tall version of this mouse, titled “Companion (Passing Through),” from the Aldrich Contemporary Art Museum in Ridgefield, Conn., to the Standard hotel in New York. A graffiti artist, illustrator, toymaker, sculptor and painter, Donnelly first collaborated with the hotel earlier this year, with a series of blue, red and green light bulbs that sold out in a matter of days. For all of his visibility in the worlds of fashion and art, Donnelly remains an unassuming, self-proclaimed “blusher” from New Jersey. T caught up with him on the eve of the unveiling at The Standard to talk about art, life and why his large “companion” looks so darned shy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The KAWS Effect', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T18:38:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a83bfe4-5492-5000-ad0e-9750e58cc504', 'word_count': 1746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a83bfe4-5492-5000-ad0e-9750e58cc504'}, {'abstract': 'In 1976, Muhammad Ali met a professional Japanese professional wrestler who did not float like a butterfly.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/ali-at-budokan/', 'snippet': 'In 1976, Muhammad Ali met a professional Japanese professional wrestler who did not float like a butterfly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My in-house Japanese-culture consultant turned to me Sunday night and said, “Do you remember when Muhammad Ali fought the Japanese wrestler in the 1970s?” I assumed this was a gross misinterpretation of something. Maybe a cartoon she saw once. But no. On June 26, 1976, Ali met Antonio Inoki, a professional wrestler, in the ring in Tokyo (at Budokan!). Inoki, for reasons best known to him, which is not to rule out terror given his prominent chin, spent most of the match on his back, trying to kick Ali. It ended in a draw. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ali at Budokan', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALI, MUHAMMAD', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T18:39:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Dickson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dickson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94d0acc6-581a-5aa6-9b21-44cd5abd1bd1', 'word_count': 95, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94d0acc6-581a-5aa6-9b21-44cd5abd1bd1'}, {'abstract': 'Of philosophy and junk proposals.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/flim-flammed-2/', 'snippet': 'Of philosophy and junk proposals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been getting requests that I respond to my colleague David Brooks’s column today. Actually, I’ll just outsource it. Read Ezra Klein, Jonathan Chait, and Jonathan Cohn. The bottom line is, sorry, the evidence is indeed dispositive: decentralized, “consumer-based” health care is worse, and far more expensive, than universal systems run by the government.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Flim-Flammed', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T18:50:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9abb80fc-f815-58c5-a2d3-83c4fde878e7', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9abb80fc-f815-58c5-a2d3-83c4fde878e7'}, {'abstract': 'The not-for-profit insurer has come under criticism for high rate hikes and high executive compensation.', 'web_url': 'https://prescriptions.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/blue-shield-of-california-vows-to-cap-profits/', 'snippet': 'The not-for-profit insurer has come under criticism for high rate hikes and high executive compensation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blue Shield of California, a not-for-profit health insurer based in San Francisco, is promising to limit its profits and give the bulk of any excess income it makes back to policyholders who are buying coverage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Blue Shield of California Vows to Cap Profits', 'kicker': 'Prescriptions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Blue Shield of California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T18:50:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Reed Abelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Reed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Abelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b01c5d39-be46-5a60-8dbf-e0e53671dc77', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b01c5d39-be46-5a60-8dbf-e0e53671dc77'}, {'abstract': 'Another confidence trick.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/ma-hes-looking-at-me-funny-watch/', 'snippet': 'Another confidence trick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oh, my: apparently the new line on the right is that the economy is weakening because Obama, that nasty man, said bad things about Paul Ryan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ma! He’s Looking At Me Funny! Watch', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T18:53:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67f6cfee-fc09-5bb1-9b57-029dabe042c9', 'word_count': 42, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67f6cfee-fc09-5bb1-9b57-029dabe042c9'}, {'abstract': 'For environmentalists, one dividend of the protracted fight over the pipeline is sowing unease about oil sands among investors.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/the-keystone-xl-as-environmental-cudgel/', 'snippet': 'For environmentalists, one dividend of the protracted fight over the pipeline is sowing unease about oil sands among investors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I noted in Tuesday’s article about Canada’s contingency plans should the Keystone XL pipeline be vetoed, the project has become a surrogate for the American debate on Canada’s oil sands development. Because the pipeline spans the international border, the State Department must give its approval before construction begins.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Keystone XL as Environmental Cudgel', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil Sands', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pipelines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Council on Foreign Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sierra Club', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T18:59:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2b0b90a-2b2b-5918-b86c-ef57aa8ec8dc', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2b0b90a-2b2b-5918-b86c-ef57aa8ec8dc'}, {'abstract': 'The Getty Research Institute in Los Angeles announced on Tuesday that it has acquired a substantial archive of art-related documents complied by the late curator Harald Szeemann, consisting of more than 1,000 boxes of correspondence, research files, drawings, and ephemera, as well as some 28,000 books and 36,000 photographs.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/getty-acquires-vast-archive-of-post-war-art-documents/', 'snippet': 'The Getty Research Institute in Los Angeles announced on Tuesday that it has acquired a substantial archive of art-related documents complied by the late curator Harald Szeemann, consisting of more than 1,000 boxes of correspondence, research file...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harald Szeemann, who died in 2005, was not only one of the most prominent contemporary art curators of the late 20th century; he was also a somewhat obsessive documentarian of the curatorial process and of his relationships with artists, including Joseph Beuys, Bruce Nauman, Richard Serra and Cy Twombly. The Getty Research Institute in Los Angeles announced on Tuesday that it has acquired Mr. Szeemann’s substantial archive, consisting of more than 1,000 boxes of correspondence, research files, drawings, and ephemera, as well as some 28,000 books and 36,000 photographs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getty Acquires Vast Archive of Post-War Art Documents', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Archives and Records', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beuys, Joseph', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeanne-Claude', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nauman, Bruce', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenquist, James', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Serra, Richard', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Twombly, Cy', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warhol, Andy', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Getty Research Institute', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T19:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58fc07c1-6d0f-54e7-9aa8-195556969b68', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58fc07c1-6d0f-54e7-9aa8-195556969b68'}, {'abstract': 'In my life with horses, I’ve had tenuous connections with the Belmont Stakes.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/my-tenuous-connection-to-the-belmont-stakes/', 'snippet': 'In my life with horses, I’ve had tenuous connections with the Belmont Stakes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve always felt that if I had a racehorse and it won the Belmont Stakes, I’d have myself a pretty nice horse. I’m sure others feel the same way. I’d venture that the owners of the 142 winners of the Belmont Stakes have felt the same way too. Funny how horse people think alike.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My (Tenuous) Connection to the Belmont Stakes', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Belmont Stakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T19:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': "By Liz O'Connell", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connell", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d41e0d4c-75ef-5917-b945-9111fd95b4b0', 'word_count': 999, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d41e0d4c-75ef-5917-b945-9111fd95b4b0'}, {'abstract': 'The city unveils a program it says will make it easier for inspectors to gain access to buildings where units have been illegally subdivided and pose a fire risk.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs