rson': [{'firstname': 'Sean', 'middlename': 'Collins', 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b928798f-ad0f-5786-840f-dd288b85f0ea', 'word_count': 657, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b928798f-ad0f-5786-840f-dd288b85f0ea'}, {'abstract': '“The Bachelorette” had the highest ratings in prime time on Monday, extending ABC’s winning streak to 13 weeks as the most-watched network on the night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/arts/television/abc-rules-again-on-monday-night.html', 'snippet': '“The Bachelorette” had the highest ratings in prime time on Monday, extending ABC’s winning streak to 13 weeks as the most-watched network on the night.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Bachelorette” had the highest ratings in prime time on Monday, extending ABC’s winning streak to 13 weeks as the most-watched network on the night. According to Nielsen’s estimates, 8 million viewers tuned in to ABC from 8 to 10 p.m. for Week 3 of this reality dating series, easily outperforming Fox’s second-season premiere of “MasterChef” in the 8 p.m. hour (4.3 million). But the news was not all positive for ABC as its new 10 p.m. series, “Extreme Makeover: Weight Loss Edition” (5.7 million), lagged behind its time-slot competitors, “Law & Order: Los Angeles” on NBC (6.2 million) and a rerun of “Hawaii Five-0” on CBS (6.5 million). For the night CBS ranked second, broadcasting repeats of its sitcom lineup from 8 to 10. NBC was third with reruns of “Minute to Win It” at 8 (3.9 million) and “Law & Order: Criminal Intent” at 9 (4.5 million). Fox finished in fourth place, garnering 3.4 million at 9 for a repeat of “House.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ABC Rules Again on Monday Night', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'ABC Rules Again On Monday Night', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T22:20:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7efd754-0955-566e-8363-d544323fc8b4', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7efd754-0955-566e-8363-d544323fc8b4'}, {'abstract': 'CW, the home of series like “Gossip Girl” and “The Vampire Diaries,” is the second broadcast television network to complete its upfront advertising sales before the beginning of the 2011-12 season.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/cw-becomes-2nd-network-to-finish-upfront-sales/', 'snippet': 'CW, the home of series like “Gossip Girl” and “The Vampire Diaries,” is the second broadcast television network to complete its upfront advertising sales before the beginning of the 2011-12 season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CW, home of series like “Gossip Girl” and “The Vampire Diaries,” has become the second broadcast television network to complete its upfront advertising sales before the beginning of the 2011-12 season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CW Becomes 2nd Network to Finish ‘Upfront’ Sales', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CW Television Network', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T22:25:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80d6344d-024d-5aaf-9ec9-a5f5a55f81c0', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80d6344d-024d-5aaf-9ec9-a5f5a55f81c0'}, {'abstract': 'One new poll finds that most Americans say the war in Afghanistan is not worth fighting, while another shows that Americans are likely to blame the nation’s debt on the wars in Afghanistan and Iraq.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/public-opinion-on-the-wars-new-polls-offer-conflicting-evidence/', 'snippet': 'One new poll finds that most Americans say the war in Afghanistan is not worth fighting, while another shows that Americans are likely to blame the nation’s debt on the wars in Afghanistan and Iraq.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is the trend line of American public opinion about the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan these days? Two new polls offer conflicting evidence.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Public Opinion on the Wars? New Polls Offer Conflicting Evidence', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pew Research Center', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Post', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-07T22:35:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk