gn Shores', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fleeing to Foreign Shores', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T01:26:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Graham Bowley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Graham', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bowley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a651a201-bf86-55da-b524-4d8720b65bbd', 'word_count': 1618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a651a201-bf86-55da-b524-4d8720b65bbd'}, {'abstract': 'The Mets’ home field hosted Ecuador and Greece in an international friendly that may lay the groundwork for an M.L.S. team in Queens.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/sports/soccer/citi-field-welcomes-soccer-for-a-night.html', 'snippet': 'The Mets’ home field hosted Ecuador and Greece in an international friendly that may lay the groundwork for an M.L.S. team in Queens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the first time in weeks, fans occupied nearly every seat at Citi Field on Tuesday night. Only this time vuvuzelas, not “Take Me Out to the Ballgame,” resonated throughout the ballpark, and yellow, not blue, was the dominant color in the stands.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citi Field Welcomes Soccer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Citi Field Welcomes Soccer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ecuador', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citi Field (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T01:29:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jorge Castillo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jorge', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castillo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/150bb289-eadc-5dd6-97af-e849423e680b', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/150bb289-eadc-5dd6-97af-e849423e680b'}, {'abstract': 'Throughout the war, East Tennessee would remain a thorn in the eyes of the Confederate republic, and an enticing and romantic rallying cry for the Union.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/07/the-switzerland-of-america/', 'snippet': 'Throughout the war, East Tennessee would remain a thorn in the eyes of the Confederate republic, and an enticing and romantic rallying cry for the Union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'East Tennessee, wrote a pamphleteer shortly after the end of the Civil War, had long been considered the Switzerland of America. “Its towering mountains locking up deep, rich and verdant valleys and coves, its succession of ridges and valleys, its magnificent forests, its roaring streams, the general fertility of the soil, the glory of the climate, the salubrity of the atmosphere, the sublimity, beauty and picturesqueness of the scenery, the freshness and voluptuous abundance of the country, all conspire to make it one of the most desirable spots in America.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Switzerland of America', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T01:30:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aaron Astor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aaron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Astor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d20c6ba-f70d-55a9-a2e9-4e8e01af4b47', 'word_count': 1918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d20c6ba-f70d-55a9-a2e9-4e8e01af4b47'}, {'abstract': 'Trade restrictions are exactly the wrong response to a sluggish global economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/opinion/08wed2.html', 'snippet': 'Trade restrictions are exactly the wrong response to a sluggish global economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world’s big trading nations have done a fairly good job over the last two years of resisting protectionism even as their economies stalled or shrank. But their patience with open markets seems to be wearing thin. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Keeping Protectionism at Bay', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Keeping Protectionism at Bay: Trade restrictions are exactly the wrong response to a sluggish global economy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Group of Twenty', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Trade Organization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Protectionism (Trade)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T01:31:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9de2086-5a6a-5e72-bcc1-6a1c07b3d337', 'word_count': 328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9de2086-5a6a-5e72-bcc1-6a1c07b3d337'}, {'abstract': 'Alan R. Mulally, the chief executive of Ford, hopes the automaker sells eight million vehicles a year by the middle of the decade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/business/08views.html', 'snippet': 'Alan R. Mulally, the chief executive of Ford, hopes the automaker sells eight million vehicles a year by the middle of the decade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ford Motor’s punchy global ambitions are not as worrying as they look. The Motor City automaker expects to sell eight million vehicles a year by the middle of the decade, according to the plan unveiled on Tuesday by Alan R. Mulally, the chief executive. That is 50 percent higher than Ford’s showing in 2010 and more bullish than the one-third sales pickup he sees for the industry.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ambitious Goals for Ford', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Ambitious Goals For Ford's Chief", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mulally, Alan R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T01:31:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By ANTONY CURRIE and PETER THAL LARSEN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Antony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CURRIE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'Thal', 'lastname': 'LARSEN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/21426312-3a05-5b87-b10c-e5126977ee94', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21426312-3a05-5b87-b10c-e5126977ee94'}, {'abstract': 'The plan sets the amount of electricity to be obtained from renewable sources at 22.5 percent by 2021, down from 30 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/science/earth/08christie.html', 'snippet': 'The plan sets the amount of electricity to be obtained from renewable sources at 22.5 percent by 2021, down from 30 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Chris Christie said Tuesday that he planned to scale back New Jersey’s goals for renewable energy as he looked for an “achievable” approach to generating electricity in the state.', 'print_section': 'A', 'prin