preme Court ruling could change the culture of research universities and of who owns the rights to the original work done by their faculty and staff members.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before the Bayh-Dole Act was passed in 1980, the billions of federal dollars spent on scientific research rarely benefited the public through commercial applications. Fewer than 5 percent of government patents were licensed to industry. To push patents into practical use, the law set up a scheme for awarding the rights to institutions, like universities, that have incentive to bring inventions to market.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fair Rewards of Invention', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fair Rewards of Invention', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roche Holding A.G', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T01:36:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e3e4201-57cf-578d-9270-90c31a170a9c', 'word_count': 349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e3e4201-57cf-578d-9270-90c31a170a9c'}, {'abstract': 'Contrary to conventional wisdom, both landlords and tenants are embracing subleasing as the commercial real estate market begins to tighten.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/realestate/commercial/manhattan-office-sublets-show-benefit-as-markets-tighten.html', 'snippet': 'Contrary to conventional wisdom, both landlords and tenants are embracing subleasing as the commercial real estate market begins to tighten.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Six months ago, when the law firm Venable began a search for a permanent home in New York, it did not end up moving far. Venable, based in Baltimore, had been subletting 52,000 square feet at 1270 Avenue of the Americas. With the agreement about to expire, it decided to stay on and sign a direct lease with its landlord, Tishman Speyer, for the same space. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/business/SUBLEASE/SUBLEASE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Unexpected Trend Toward Office Sublets', 'kicker': 'Square Feet', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Unexpected Trend Toward Office Sublets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Office Buildings and Commercial Properties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T01:41:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'subsection_name': 'Commercial Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Satow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Satow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8e217c5-2e2e-55d3-87d6-5e654f10c2fa', 'word_count': 1178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8e217c5-2e2e-55d3-87d6-5e654f10c2fa'}, {'abstract': 'Investigators scoured 11 locations along Ocean Parkway for the remains of Shannan Gilbert, a prostitute who went missing in May 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/nyregion/state-police-and-fbi-resume-search-for-bodies-on-long-island.html', 'snippet': 'Investigators scoured 11 locations along Ocean Parkway for the remains of Shannan Gilbert, a prostitute who went missing in May 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BABYLON, N.Y. — It was 84 degrees on Tuesday, with an abundance of sun and a healthy breeze — the kind of day that Oliver Kurzyp and Adam Janowski could not let pass without a trip to the beach.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/nyregion/BODIES1/BODIES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/08/nyregion/BODIES1/BODIES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/nyregion/BODIES1/BODIES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/nyregion/BODIES1/BODIES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/08/nyregion/BODIES1/BODIES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Search for Bodies Resumes on Long Island, Aided by Aerial Images', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Search for Bodies Resumes on Long Island, Aided by Aerial Images', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilbert, Shannan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Suffolk County (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ocean Parkway (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Serial Murders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T01:41:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Stelloh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelloh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13327099-d70e-5fac-9c95-3b866f498ca2', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13327099-d70e-5fac-9c95-3b866f498ca2'}, {'abstract': 'If you listen to the songs he played, you get a vivid feel for the sensibility of that era of southern California pop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/opinion/08wed4.html', 'snippet': 'If you listen to the songs he played, you get a vivid feel for the sensibility of that era of southern California pop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At one time, I thought I owned every album Andrew Gold played on between 1974 and 1980, nearly all of them recorded and produced in southern California. I was wrong. I never owned the albums he recorded with Barbi Benton, Hugh Hefner’s girlfriend in the early 1970s, even though she went to high school with me. I wasn’t intentionally collecting Gold, who died last week at age 59. He just happened to back up, produce or appear with nearly everyone I was listening to at the time.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Andrew Gold and the ’70s Sound', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Appreciations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Andrew Gold and the ’70s Sound', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ronstadt, Linda', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T01:44:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verlyn Klinkenborg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klinkenborg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/4082056c-f15f-58c1-93be-27c565134469', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4082056c-f15f-58c1-93be-27c565134469'}, {'abstract': 'The rector, Maksud I. Sadikov, had been leading a government-backed effort to counter violence by reviving the local traditions of Sufi Islam.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/world/europe/08russia.html', 'snippet': 'The rector, Maksud I. Sadikov, had been leading a government-backed effort to counter violence by reviving the local traditions of Sufi Islam.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — Gunmen on Tuesday killed the rector of a Muslim university in southern Russia who had been leading a government-sponsored effort to counter violence in the region by reviving the local traditions of Sufi Islam that he said were less likely to inspire suicide bombers.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/world/RUSSIA-1/RUSSIA-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/world/RUSSIA-1/RUSSIA-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/08/world/RUSSIA-1/RUS