bnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Winning Games for Tablets', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T20:55:19+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Technology', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/09e6f7b9-bb03-53fd-b92e-676e3584e13c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/09e6f7b9-bb03-53fd-b92e-676e3584e13c'}, {'abstract': 'Games on tablets can become more involved and more strategic, and have longer-term ambitions that those designed for cellphones.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/technology/personaltech/09TABLETS.html', 'snippet': 'Games on tablets can become more involved and more strategic, and have longer-term ambitions that those designed for cellphones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tablets are just great for gaming. Everything that modern cellphones bring to games — touch screens, tilt sensitivity and of course constant Internet access — the tablets do better. The screens are finer and larger and the games are more immersive. And you can use a tablet in ways and places that rarely work for even the smallest netbooks.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/technology/personaltech/tablet1/tablet1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 802, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Games on Tablets: Lean Back, Commence Tapping', 'kicker': 'Video Games', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Games on Tablets: Lean Back, Commence Tapping', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T20:59:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Schiesel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schiesel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f219d8d-d159-5403-8c57-9e3b4ac9b78b', 'word_count': 671, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f219d8d-d159-5403-8c57-9e3b4ac9b78b'}, {'abstract': 'Krakoff’s resort collection took its cues — and also perhaps its often vibrant hues — from abstract art.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/just-one-look-reed-krakoff-resort-collection/', 'snippet': 'Krakoff’s resort collection took its cues — and also perhaps its often vibrant hues — from abstract art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reed Krakoff is one to wear his knowledge of art on his sleeve. His resort collection took its cues — and also perhaps its often vibrant hues — from the likes of Mark Rothko, Helen Frankenthaler and Josef Albers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Reed Krakoff Resort Collection', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4893baa3-fa6b-518e-9c79-c0a105b31637', 'word_count': 42, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4893baa3-fa6b-518e-9c79-c0a105b31637'}, {'abstract': 'For “Theater for One” a single actor and a single audience member are sequestered in a plush red booth in Times Square for a one-on-one performance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/theater/reviews/theater-for-one-in-times-square-review.html', 'snippet': 'For “Theater for One” a single actor and a single audience member are sequestered in a plush red booth in Times Square for a one-on-one performance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People with intimacy issues — which is to say most respectably neurotic New Yorkers — may need to steel themselves before a visit to “Theater for One,” in which a single actor and a single audience member are sequestered in a plush red booth in Times Square for a one-on-one performance. The experience will last no longer than a few minutes but is likely to settle somewhere in your soul for a lot longer than most of the splashy jamborees in the neighboring theaters do.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/one/one-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/one/one-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/one/one-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Booth for Two, Dialogue Optional', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Theater for One'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Booth for Two, Dialogue Optional', 'name': None, 'seo': None