logs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/weiners-wife-is-pregnant/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Weiner’s wife, Huma Abedin, is in the early stages of her pregnancy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Their marriage has become the subject of intense speculation and scrutiny amid an embarrassing online sex scandal. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weiner’s Wife Is Pregnant', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Weiner’s Wife Said to Be in Early Stages of Pregnancy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Anthony D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:11:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Barbaro and Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98d52520-8dc5-5b2c-96cb-54f34d4340d5', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98d52520-8dc5-5b2c-96cb-54f34d4340d5'}, {'abstract': 'Nublu is a place where you can find Brazilian hillbilly music and Puerto Rican boogaloo under the same roof as acid jazz.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/arts/music/nublu-an-east-village-club-where-everything-goes.html', 'snippet': 'Nublu is a place where you can find Brazilian hillbilly music and Puerto Rican boogaloo under the same roof as acid jazz.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the outside the club appears to be just another shuttered and somewhat grotty East Village storefront, and the interior, with its scarred wooden floor and graffiti-daubed walls, isn’t any more impressive. Instead, it’s what happens onstage that has made Nublu’s reputation as an incubator of musical talent, with some of the most adventurous and varied offerings in the city.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SUBNUBLU1/SUBNUBLU1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SUBNUBLU1/SUBNUBLU1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 346}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SUBNUBLU1/SUBNUBLU1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SUBNUBLU1/SUBNUBLU1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SUBNUBLU1/SUBNUBLU1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Club Mixes Old, New and Blue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Club Mixes Old, New And Blue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:12:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17e8e84e-6c70-5e46-b78b-68d7f4cce49e', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17e8e84e-6c70-5e46-b78b-68d7f4cce49e'}, {'abstract': 'The latest devices for cars include high-tech items to keep you awake, keep your bicycles on safely and guide you to your destination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/technology/personaltech/09CARS.html', 'snippet': 'The latest devices for cars include high-tech items to keep you awake, keep your bicycles on safely and guide you to your destination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TYING a big red bow around a new sports car is one way to give someone the latest in-car technologies, but for most of us that’s just a marketer’s fantasy. Fortunately, there are plenty of high-tech baubles that can be added to your own car to improve safety, convenience or both. And you don’t have to take out a new loan to get them (or give them).', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/technology/personaltech/cars1/cars1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 361, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/09/technology/personaltech/cars1/cars1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 361}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/technology/personaltech/cars1/cars1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/technology/personaltech/cars1/cars1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/09/technology/personaltech/cars1/cars1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Update Your Car With GPS, Radar and Other Gadgets', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Add-On Gadgets For Safety Or Convenience', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:16:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By John R. Quain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Quain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66cfe21a-e01c-5720-bbbb-ceb33562f051', 'word_count': 893, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66cfe21a-e01c-5720-bbbb-ceb33562f051'}, {'abstract': 'Only Game 4 of the N.B.A. Finals drew more viewers.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/despite-drop-americas-got-talent-still-draws-a-crowd/', 'snippet': 'Only Game 4 of the N.B.A. Finals drew more viewers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Opposite Game 4 of the National Basketball Association Finals on ABC, “America’s Got Talent” saw a drop in its ratings compared to its premiere a week ago the NBC reality show still outperformed all other programming on the night excluding basketball. According to Nielsen’s estimates, 12.7 million viewers tuned in to “America’s Got Talent” at 8 p.m., down from 15.3 million who watched the two-hour premiere last week. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Despite Drop, ‘America’s Got Talent’ Still Draws a Crowd', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen Media Research', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:20:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32555e96-6310-5bf4-9ddd-da80f780b6d5', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32555e96-6310-5bf4-9ddd-da80f780b6d5'}, {'abstract': 'The producers of the Broadway musical “Million Dollar Quartet” announced on Tuesday that the show would transfer Off Broadway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/theater/footnote.html', 'snippet': 'The producers of the Broadway musical “Million Dollar Quartet” announced on Tuesday that the show would transfer Off Broadway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The producers of the Broadway musical “Million Dollar Quartet” announced on Tuesday that the show would transfer Off Broadway to New World Stages after it plays its final performance at the Nederlander Theater on Sunday. No opening date for the Off Broadway production was announced.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnote', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:22:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/654ce9cd-40f6-5254-8f37-892fdd7048b9', 'word_count': 45, 'uri': 'nyt://article/654ce9cd-40f6-5254-8f37-892fdd7048b9'}, {'abstract': 'Yo-Yo Ma’s Silk Road Ensemble opened the Central Park SummerStage season on Tuesday, with the loose theme of stars and other heavenly bodies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/arts/music/yo-yo-mas-silk-road-ensemble-at-summerstage-review.html', 'snippet': 'Yo-Yo Ma’s Silk Road Ensemble opened the Central Park SummerStage season on Tuesday, with the loose theme of stars and other heavenly bodies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As cultures blend, as art forms meld with one another and with science and other disciplines, all is convergence, and the cellist Yo-Yo Ma is somehow at the center.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SILK-1/SILK-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 339, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SILK-1/SILK-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 339}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SILK-1/SILK-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 618, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SILK-1/SILK-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SILK-1/SILK-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Trolling Heaven and Earth for Sounds', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trolling Heaven And Earth For Sounds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:24:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e040c1e3-af08-526e-bf40-72a60cc486d0', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e040c1e3-af08-526e-bf40-72a60cc486d0'}, {'abstract': 'A rule that caps the swipe fees that merchants pay banks will go into effect next month, but will consumers benefit?', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/debit-card-fees-to-fall-but-will-consumers-benefit/', 'snippet': 'A rule that caps the swipe fees that merchants pay banks will go into effect next month, but will consumers benefit?', 'lead_paragraph': 'After weeks of back-and-forth debate and heavy lobbying by banks and retailers, the Senate voted Wednesday against delaying a cap on the swipe fees that stores must pay for the processing of debit card purchases.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debit Card Fees to Fall, but Will Consumers Benefit?', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debit Cards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:24:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c178aef-56e1-55a8-a3eb-b8a018068cc8', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c178aef-56e1-55a8-a3eb-b8a018068cc8'}, {'abstract': 'How resource limits vanish in the face of innovations.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/on-limits-and-leaps/', 'snippet': 'How resource limits vanish in the face of innovations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tom Friedman’s column today focused on Paul Gilding’s new book, “The Great Disruption: Why the Climate Crisis Will Bring on the End of Shopping and the Birth of a New World.” The column and the cited comments from Gilding gave the impression of planetary overload, but a critical clause from Gilding was easy to miss and is worth highlighting here (my italics):', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Limits and Leaps', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History (Academic Subject)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NEW YORK CITY', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:24:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d108e65-9c77-56ac-abbb-092cf4a42a12', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d108e65-9c77-56ac-abbb-092cf4a42a12'}, {'abstract': 'The surprising origins of California’s red script.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/who-made-californias-license-plate/', 'snippet': 'The surprising origins of California’s red script.', 'lead_paragraph': 'California has been having a lot of problems lately, but despite the budget, gas prices and the fear of earthquakes, the Golden State can still conjure romantic notions of endless summer: surfing in the Pacific, camping in Redwood, taking the archetypal road trip down a coastal highway. Driving is a large part of California’s culture, and typographically speaking, the bold red script on the state’s license plate seemingly embraces the happily casual and free-spirited notions commonly associated with the West Coast.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made California’s License Plate?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Registration and License Plates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:25:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22c11333-7493-5b0f-b095-1ec97a295524', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22c11333-7493-5b0f-b095-1ec97a295524'}, {'abstract': "Mike Tanier's mission: to cover six major New York sports events in a span of 24 hours. ", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/multimedia/100000000855618/24-hours-in-new-york-sports.html', 'snippet': "Mike Tanier's mission: to cover six major New York sports events in a span of 24 hours. ", 'lead_paragraph': "Mike Tanier's mission: to cover six major New York sports events in a span of 24 hours. ", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/multimedia/video-tanier/video-tanier-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/08/multimedia/video-tanier/video-tanier-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/multimedia/video-tanier/video-tanier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/08/multimedia/video-tanier/video-tanier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '24 Hours in New York Sports', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:25:38+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Justin Sablich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/717d051b-b971-5259-874e-85a1b3c0bd53', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/717d051b-b971-5259-874e-85a1b3c0bd53'}, {'abstract': 'While the countries agree that they cannot combat the violence by themselves, the path to cooperation is paved with a history of distrust.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/world/americas/09drugs.html', 'snippet': 'While the countries agree that they cannot combat the violence by themselves, the path to cooperation is paved with a history of distrust.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN SALVADOR, El Salvador — The nations of Central America agree on one thing: They cannot by themselves combat the drug and organized crime violence that is making the region among the world’s unsafest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Suspicion and Disunity Impede Central America’s Effort to Fight Drug Violence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Suspicion and Disunity Impede Central America’s Effort to Fight Drug Violence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guatemala', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Central America', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'El Salvador', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colombia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zetas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Crime', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:27:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randal C. Archibold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randal', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Archibold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8184cde6-9f8a-5589-bf20-8ae896d11086', 'word_count': 748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8184cde6-9f8a-5589-bf20-8ae896d11086'}, {'abstract': 'A School of American Ballet workshop performance on Tuesday provided an exuberant display of what its students have learned.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/arts/dance/school-of-american-ballet-workshop-performance-review.html', 'snippet': 'A School of American Ballet workshop performance on Tuesday provided an exuberant display of what its students have learned.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Tuesday night at the Peter Jay Sharp Theater five teenage girls from the School of American Ballet, dancing to Tchaikovsky, each extended a leg toward the audience above hip height. Then, keeping the legs high in that position, they all began to fall forward off balance toward the audience. They didn’t actually fall, of course, but, before they brought the legs down, they had transferred much of their weight in its direction. It was easy for the audience to feel the suspense involved in the moment of extension and then the audacity involved in that transfer of weight: those girls were aiming those legs straight at us. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SAB/SAB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 876, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SAB/SAB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/SAB/SAB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Balanchine Way, Imprinted', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Balanchine Way, Imprinted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'School of American Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:27:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f191c68-0d06-5cb3-8efd-092849e200a2', 'word_count': 923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f191c68-0d06-5cb3-8efd-092849e200a2'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. McCartney, who has previously appeared in concert at Shea Stadium and Citi Field, will play his first Yankee Stadium show on July 15.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/paul-mccartney-to-perform-at-yankee-stadium/', 'snippet': 'Mr. McCartney, who has previously appeared in concert at Shea Stadium and Citi Field, will play his first Yankee Stadium show on July 15.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Sorry, Queens, but you’ve had plenty of opportunities to feature the Cute Beatle, now it’s time to let the Bronx host him for an evening: Paul McCartney will play his first concert ever at Yankee Stadium on July 15, Live Nation Entertainment said on Wednesday. Mr. McCartney, the pop musician whose work in Wings is perennially overshadowed by his time with another popular British rock outfit, has some familiarity with New York ballparks, having served as the inaugural performer at Citi Field as in 2009, and as a surprise guest when Billy Joel closed down Shea Stadium in 2008; Mr. McCartney also played at Shea Stadium in 1965 as part of that beloved band whose name momentarily escapes us. (No, not the Rolling Stones, the other guys.) A statement from Live Nation said Mr. McCartney’s performance at Yankee Stadium “can safely be predicted to feature some nearly three hours of the world’s best loved and most known music, with hits, deep cuts and surprises spanning his unrivaled career,” including the 2008 album “Electric Arguments” that he released under the name the Fireman. With a top ticket price of $279.50 for this show, Mr. McCartney will hopefully throw in “Band on the Run” or “Let It Be,” too. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paul McCartney to Perform at Yankee Stadium', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCartney, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yankee Stadium (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:29:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab6062e6-edee-5e40-8d20-0d60ce400a49', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab6062e6-edee-5e40-8d20-0d60ce400a49'}, {'abstract': 'The swift resolution of the case, where an ethnic Han killed an ethnic Mongolian herder in a hit-and-run accident last month, reflected Chinese leaders’ deep concern about ethnic tensions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/world/asia/09mongolia.html', 'snippet': 'The swift resolution of the case, where an ethnic Han killed an ethnic Mongolian herder in a hit-and-run accident last month, reflected Chinese leaders’ deep concern about ethnic tensions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — An ethnic Han truck driver who killed an ethnic Mongolian herder with his truck last month, sparking Inner Mongolia’s largest protests in 20 years, was sentenced to death on Wednesday by a court in Xilinhot, the area of Inner Mongolia where the incident occurred.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese Truck Driver Sentenced to Death Over Homicide', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Truck Driver Sentenced to Death', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mongolia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hit-and-Run Drivers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:29:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab37b898-b6bf-5524-8b83-8c0d5f9af042', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab37b898-b6bf-5524-8b83-8c0d5f9af042'}, {'abstract': 'The Knicks announced an exclusive affiliation agreement with the Erie Bayhawks of the N.B.A. Development League.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/houston-to-manage-knicks-new-development-league-team/', 'snippet': 'The Knicks announced an exclusive affiliation agreement with the Erie Bayhawks of the N.B.A. Development League.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Knicks announced an affiliation partnership with the Erie (Pa.) Bayhawks of the N.B.A. Development League on Wednesday, with Allan Houston to serve as the team’s general manager as well as continuing his role as assistant general manger of the Knicks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Houston to Manage Knicks’ New Development League Team', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Houston, Allan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:42:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efebcd89-690b-538a-8673-aebd746653fa', 'word_count': 350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efebcd89-690b-538a-8673-aebd746653fa'}, {'abstract': 'New York is due to lose 2 of its 29 seats in 2012, and for reasons of geography and demographics as well as partisan politics, Mr. Weiner’s Ninth District would seem to be a prime candidate.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/weiners-seat-could-be-scrambled-in-redistricting/', 'snippet': 'New York is due to lose 2 of its 29 seats in 2012, and for reasons of geography and demographics as well as partisan politics, Mr. Weiner’s Ninth District would seem to be a prime candidate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is much speculation about whether Representative Anthony D. Weiner will remain in Congress or resign his seat. But there is a politically expedient solution for Democrats if they want to show him the door: Neuter his district in the redistricting process, which will require New York to lose 2 of its 29 seats in the House of Representatives.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weiner’s Seat Could Be Scrambled in Redistricting', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Redistricting and Reapportionment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ackerman, Gary L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clarke, Yvette D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grimm, Michael G', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hayworth, Nan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hochul, Kathleen C', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Peter T', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Carolyn', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rangel, Charles B', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Anthony D', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River Valley (NY)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Staten Island (NYC)', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T21:44:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 