{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T22:17:15+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/dcdfa06f-c84a-50c0-ab7e-66f09d9fa89e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/dcdfa06f-c84a-50c0-ab7e-66f09d9fa89e'}, {'abstract': 'A roundup of new art and design books, about screen printing, graffiti lettering, signage in South African townships and pavement chalk artists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/books/review/book-review-visuals-graphic-design-books.html', 'snippet': 'A roundup of new art and design books, about screen printing, graffiti lettering, signage in South African townships and pavement chalk artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before the personal computer, one way to pick out graphic designers in a crowd was from the pieces of colored adhesive film, like Cello-tak or Rubylith, stuck to their clothes. Most of the designers I knew back then yearned to rid themselves of messy cutting and pasting. So in 1984, when the Macintosh was introduced and became a design tool, they enthusiastically embraced desktop-computer design. Today, though, many designers are proud to engage in dirty handwork. Young and old designers alike are employing revered methods of making art and design by hand, especially in silk-screen print-making. Having clothes stained with ink and paint is now a status symbol.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/08/books/review/08visuals-slide-3443/08visuals-slide-3443-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail',