’s to sell, her luck has been running the other way.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/stolen/stolen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 742, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/stolen/stolen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/stolen/stolen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Treasured Pissarro Print Turns Into Costly Headache', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treasured Pissarro Print Turns Into Costly Headache', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pissarro, Camille', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-08T23:59:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53aa29be-d5e1-52c0-b0e9-7290214755f6', 'word_count': 867, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53aa29be-d5e1-52c0-b0e9-7290214755f6'}, {'abstract': 'The drummer Adam Cruz shows how Latin and jazz are two categories whose distinctions have blurred.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/arts/music/adam-cruz-and-milestone-embody-grammys-latin-jazz-dilemma.html', 'snippet': 'The drummer Adam Cruz shows how Latin and jazz are two categories whose distinctions have blurred.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps you’ve heard that Latin jazz has been in a state of uproar recently. Since the announced elimination in April of its category from the Grammy Awards, some of the genre’s most prominent artists have united in protest, charging the National Academy of Recording Arts and Sciences, which administers the Grammys, with a cultural affront. (Some 30 other categories also got the ax, but the Native American, Cajun and Hawaiian constituencies have been relatively quiet.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/CRUZ/CRUZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/CRUZ/CRUZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/CRUZ/CRUZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 622, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When the Latin and Jazz Converge', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When the Latin and Jazz Converge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T00:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0246e0f9-a559-5d06-b755-476b1bb1fecc', 'word_count': 782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0246e0f9-a559-5d06-b755-476b1bb1fecc'}, {'abstract': 'Rather than run in five or six races and retire to stud, Paddy O’Prado is taking the summer and fall off to nosh some of Kentucky’s finest bluegrass. Then, he will be the subject of an equine form of “The Bachelor.”', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/for-paddy-oprado-a-time-to-heal/', 'snippet': 'Rather than run in five or six races and retire to stud, Paddy O’Prado is taking the summer and fall off to nosh some of Kentucky’s finest bluegrass. Then, he will be the subject of an equine form of “The Bachelor.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Liz O’Connell is a professional horsewoman who just finished foaling season and is looking for new adventures. She is a member of the New York State Task Force on Retired Race Horses and spends too much time rehoming off-the-track thoroughbreds. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Paddy O’Prado, a Time to Heal', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T00:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': "By Liz O'Connell", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connell", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f89e3860-0834-5479-852f-3e52c2afe354', 'word_count': 563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f89e3860-0834-5479-852f-3e52c2afe354'}, {'abstract': 'A response to an editorial about maintaining the momentum to find a cure for AIDS.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/opinion/lweb09aids.html', 'snippet': 'A response to an editorial about maintaining the momentum to find a cure for AIDS.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'AIDS Prevention', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'AIDS Prevention', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T00:01:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1d27c4f-a807-564b-80e3-2877abf529a6', 'word_count': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1d27c4f-a807-564b-80e3-2877abf529a6'}, {'abstract': 'Yanna Avis’s show at the Metropolitan Room is a nod to the glamorous, high-style cabaret shows that flourished in New York in the 1950s and early ’60s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/arts/music/yanna-avis-at-the-metropolitan-room-review.html', 'snippet': 'Yanna Avis’s show at the Metropolitan Room is a nod to the glamorous, high-style cabaret shows that flourished in New York in the 1950s and early ’60s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ah, Paree! As the statuesque French chanteuse Yanna Avis sang Cole Porter’s great ode to the City of Light, “You Don’t Know Paree,” on a recent evening, a composite view of the city as imagined by Balzac, Zola and of course, Porter himself, flickered in my imagination. “Until you’ve lived a lot, and loved a lot, and lost a lot, you don’t know Paree,” go lyrics that Ms. Avis, a soigné international beauty, sang with a wry understanding.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/09/arts/AVIS/AVIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_nam