g “to pursue new opportunities,” the company said Wednesday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'General Counsel of News Corp. Resigns in Wake of Settlement', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'General Counsel of News Corp. Resigns in Wake of Settlement', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News of the World', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News International Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T01:58:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42c7246b-8ef5-533c-ac72-6a2bd58b04a9', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42c7246b-8ef5-533c-ac72-6a2bd58b04a9'}, {'abstract': 'A class-action lawsuit seeks homes for disabled veterans left on Los Angeles streets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/opinion/09thu2.html', 'snippet': 'A class-action lawsuit seeks homes for disabled veterans left on Los Angeles streets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The federal government should not have to be sued into giving veterans with mental illnesses and brain injuries the care they need so they don’t end up living in the street. But it has come to that. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Housing for the Wounded', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Housing for the Wounded: A class-action lawsuit seeks homes for disabled veterans left on Los Angeles streets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Civil Liberties Union', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veterans Affairs Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public and Subsidized Housing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T01:59:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d912f09-0e8a-589b-a19e-46e07253adb5', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d912f09-0e8a-589b-a19e-46e07253adb5'}, {'abstract': 'Joon Pahk goes us one better.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/08/durer/', 'snippet': 'Joon Pahk goes us one better.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THURSDAY’S PUZZLE — Solvers frequently comment on a constructor’s use of the “rare” letters seen in crossword puzzles, particularly J, Q, X and Z. In fact, this usage has even been given a nickname. A puzzle with a plethora of these letters is described as “Scrabbly.” I have to confess that I enjoy a Scrabbly puzzle as much as the next person, although I am in the camp that believes that you shouldn’t endanger a perfectly good theme or sector just to get those letters into the grid.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday: Do as Dürer Did', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T02:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c33ca8bc-0c86-5a48-b6a4-8c4b1c569700', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c33ca8bc-0c86-5a48-b6a4-8c4b1c569700'}, {'abstract': 'The current exhibit at the Hopper home in Nyack provides a prophetic glimpse through the young artist’s eye.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/opinion/09thu4.html', 'snippet': 'The current exhibit at the Hopper home in Nyack provides a prophetic glimpse through the young artist’s eye.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trackers of the artist Edward Hopper tirelessly pursue the true settings of his city tableaux, so tinged with Gotham loneliness. Was the night-shrouded “Drug Store” modeled from the Waverly Place shop in Greenwich Village currently occupied by a corner bookstore? The artist offered them little help. “What I wanted to do was to paint sunlight on the side of a house,” was his typically terse clue about his work. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shadowing the Reclusive Painter of Light', 'kicker': 'Editorial Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shadowing the Reclusive Painter of Light', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nyack (NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hopper House Art Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hopper, Edward', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T02:04:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francis X. Clines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francis', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Clines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae254f8f-e2b1-5bcc-922a-07180697c19d', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae254f8f-e2b1-5bcc-922a-07180697c19d'}, {'abstract': 'Alternate-side street-cleaning rules are suspended on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/nyregion/special-parking-rules-in-new-york-city.html', 'snippet': 'Alternate-side street-cleaning rules are suspended on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In New York City, because of Shavuot, alternate-side street-cleaning regulations will be suspended on Thursday. Other regulations will remain in effect.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Special Parking Rules', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Parking Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parking', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T02:05:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/4821071a-e82e-520b-a584-9656f8653e31', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4821071a-e82e-520b-a584-9656f8653e31'}, {'abstract': 'As the government and private industry rewrite home mortgage rules, it is crucial that they settle on the correct size of the future mortgage market.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/business/09views.html', 'snippet': 'As the government and private industry rewrite home mortgage rules, it is crucial that they settle on the correct size of the future mortgage market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Regulators and financiers could be focused on the wrong mortgage market. They are right that healthy American home lending hinges on the private sector’s ability to finance all or most of it, rather than relying on government-run Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac or their equivalents. But architects of the postcrisis home loan market may be assuming it needs to be larger than it really does. It could be scarcely half as big as the current $10.5 trillion and therefore much easier to finance.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mortgage Market Must Be Balanced', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mortgage Market Must Be Balanced', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fitch Ratings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T02:05:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Agnes T. Crane and Richard Beales', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Agnes', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Crane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beales', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1d13cd1-2d43-50cf-99a8-c3c9305593ad', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1d13cd1-2d43-50cf-99a8-c3c9305593ad'}, {'abstract': 'By reaffirming his ruling on corporate campaign contributions, Judge Cacheris flouts Supreme Court precedent. The Justice Department must appeal his decision.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/opinion/09thu3.html', 'snippet': 'By reaffirming his ruling on corporate campaign contributions, Judge Cacheris flouts Supreme Court precedent. The Justice Department must appeal his decision.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Judge James Cacheris gave the impression last week that he was considering putting right what he got wrong when he ruled in May that corporations are free to make direct donations to federal candidates. That first judgment directly contravened last year’s Citizens United case and the Supreme Court’s earlier decision in Federal Election Commission v. Beaumont, which upholds a ban on corporate donations to candidates. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citizens United’s Outrageous Offspring', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Citizens United’s Outrageous Offspring', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cacheris, James C', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizens United v Federal Election Commission (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T02:09:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcf40671-756b-59fc-a9b2-feda82fb99f3', 'word_count': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcf40671-756b-59fc-a9b2-feda82fb99f3'}, {'abstract': 'Winning lottery numbers for June 8, 2011, for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/nyregion/lottery-numbers-for-new-york-new-jersey-and-connecticut.html', 'snippet': 'Winning lottery numbers for June 8, 2011, for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'June 8, 2011', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lottery Numbers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lottery Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lotteries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T02:16:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1d7045d-34f6-5ac5-b01c-fce4119047a9', 'word_count': 218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1d7045d-34f6-5ac5-b01c-fce4119047a9'}, {'abstract': 'Hamas said Egypt had agreed to allow 550 people a day to leave Gaza through the Rafah border and to lengthen the operating hours of the crossing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/world/africa/09briefs-Border.html', 'snippet': 'Hamas said Egypt had agreed to allow 550 people a day to leave Gaza through the Rafah border and to lengthen the operating hours of the crossing.', 'lead_paragraph': ' After days of acrimony between Hamas and Egypt over limitations on who could pass through the Rafah border crossing between Gaza and Egypt, Hamas said Egypt had agreed to allow 550 people a day to leave Gaza and to lengthen the operating hours of the crossing. The crossing was shut for much of the past four years after Israel, with Egyptian cooperation, closed it to pressure Hamas, which rules Gaza. Hamas said differences remained, chiefly concerning blacklists of Gazans who were forbidden to travel during Hosni Mubarak’s rule. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt and Hamas Reach Accord on Rafah Crossing', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | MIDDLE EAST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt and Hamas Reach Accord on Rafah Crossing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T02:19:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fares Akram', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fares', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Akram', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/381a153f-4e2a-51f9-ab5d-2d2b1fb974c1', 'word_count': 90, 'uri': 'nyt://article/381a153f-4e2a-51f9-ab5d-2d2b1fb974c1'}, {'abstract': 'Four young leaders of Iraq’s struggling government-reform movement were released from jail after being held for 12 days without formal charges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/world/middleeast/09briefs-Iraq.html', 'snippet': 'Four young leaders of Iraq’s struggling government-reform movement were released from jail after being held for 12 days without formal charges.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Four young leaders of Iraq’s struggling government-reform movement were released from jail after being held for 12 days without formal charges. At a news conference on Wednesday, a day after their release, the men said they had been interrogated by officers who asked questions about possible ties between the protest movement and Al Qaeda or the outlawed Baath Party. Ultimately, security officials accused the men of carrying fake identification cards — a charge all of them denied. It is unclear whether officials will attempt to formally prosecute them. Human rights activists have called the detentions an attempt to sap the strength of the youth-led reform movement. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iraq: 4 From Movement Are Released From Jail', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | MIDDLE EAST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iraq: 4 From Movement Are Released From Jail', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T02:21:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By DURAID ADNAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Duraid', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ADNAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/494745e1-f591-52ba-b94f-12f12521792d', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/494745e1-f591-52ba-b94f-12f12521792d'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Defense recently confirmed the deaths of the following Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/us/09list.html', 'snippet': 'The Department of Defense recently confirmed the deaths of the following Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Department of Defense has identified