idelines, specifically noting that any apps that appeared to promote driving under the influence would be rejected.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/apple-will-reject-d-u-i-checkpoint-apps/', 'snippet': 'The company recently updated its app guidelines, specifically noting that any apps that appeared to promote driving under the influence would be rejected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From our colleagues at Bits:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apple Will Reject D.U.I. Checkpoint Apps', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T15:24:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/304670fc-931c-523f-ba47-320ab8148671', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/304670fc-931c-523f-ba47-320ab8148671'}, {'abstract': 'In J. J. Abrams’s “Super 8,” teenagers making a zombie movie in 1979 discover something even stranger than the undead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/movies/j-j-abramss-super-8-zooms-in-on-a-dark-secret-review.html', 'snippet': 'In J. J. Abrams’s “Super 8,” teenagers making a zombie movie in 1979 discover something even stranger than the undead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is something odd about watching a movie called “Super 8” digitally projected onto an Imax screen with 12,000 watts of gut-rumbling Dolby sound. J. J. Abrams, the writer and director and presiding pop-culture guru-geek, is surely aware of the incongruity. You might even say that it’s the subject of the movie, which makes much of the technological gap between the present day and 1979, when this story of kids and monsters takes place.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/SUPER8/SUPER8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/SUPER8/SUPER8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/SUPER8/SUPER8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 616, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/SUPER8/SUPER8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/SUPER8/SUPER8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hey, Guys, Let’s Make a Monster Flick', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Super 8'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hey, Guys, Let’s Make a Monster Flick', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABRAMS, J J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spielberg, Steven', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chandler, Kyle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fanning, Elle', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eldard, Ron', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emmerich, Noah', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T15:25:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/058bca1f-fc8a-5cf0-b5b1-3adebd7764ba', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/058bca1f-fc8a-5cf0-b5b1-3adebd7764ba'}, {'abstract': 'If signed into law, a new bill would allow car owners to rent their vehicles out to other people, with car sharing companies assuming the insurance burden.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/peer-to-peer-car-sharing-gains-traction-in-oregon/', 'snippet': 'If signed into law, a new bill would allow car owners to rent their vehicles out to other people, with car sharing companies assuming the insurance burden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oregon soon could become the second state to allow peer-to-peer car sharing. Legislation establishing standards for personal vehicle sharing programs was approved this week by the House of Representatives and the Senate. HB 3149 paves the way for residents to rent their cars directly to other people through vehicle sharing programs like Getaround or the Google-backed RelayRides. California passed a similar law last year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Peer-to-Peer Car Sharing Gains Traction in Oregon', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Insurance and Liability', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN AUTOMOBILE ASSN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'RelayRides', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ZipCar', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oregon', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T15:26:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Witkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ab1870e-80e9-509b-8521-f0d9b2a64f00', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ab1870e-80e9-509b-8521-f0d9b2a64f00'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/morning-take-out-261/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bank Said No? Hedge Funds Fill a Void in Lending | With traditional lenders still avoiding risky borrowers in the wake of the financial crisis, hedge funds and other opportunistic investors are filling the void. They are going after midsize businesses that cannot easily raise money in the bond markets like their bigger brethren.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T15:27:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6a618f2-4669-5ab3-9b7c-34ccf4d1f086', 'word_count': 1915, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6a618f2-4669-5ab3-9b7c-34ccf4d1f086'}, {'abstract': 'His big economic speech reads as if the Bush years never happened.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/tim-pawlentys-supply-side-time-warp/', 'snippet': 'His big economic speech reads as if the Bush years never happened.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this week, Ramesh Ponnuru had an excellent column on the Republican Party’s struggle to come up with an economic agenda that doesn’t just feel like “half-remembered bits of Reaganism.” Here’s how it ended:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tim Pawlenty’s Supply-Side Time Warp', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T15:35:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fea2bb01-6b07-5ef1-86c1-081cc8f6f1b3', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fea2bb01-6b07-5ef1-86c1-081cc8f6f1b3'}, {'abstract': 'Despite all the advances in depression treatment, suicide remains stubbornly persistent.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/lives-cut-short-by-depression/', 'snippet': 'Despite all the advances in depression treatment, suicide remains stubbornly persistent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is something about a first friend that is irreplaceable. No matter how disparately your lives travel, the first friend you ever had occupies a special place in your heart. I was lucky that Michael was considerate enough to be born four months before me, waiting next door, ready to join me in elaborate childhood games of hide-and-seek, multilevel couch forts and family camping trips in the Catskills.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lives Cut Short by Depression', 'kicker': 'We