harmonic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Dresden Staatskapelle will replace the Berlin Philharmonic as the resident orchestra of the Salzburg Easter Festival starting in 2013, officials have announced. The Berliners wanted to increase their performances at the Easter festival but were rebuffed for lack of money, so they announced a plan to move to Baden-Baden’s festival hall after next year. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Salzburg Easter Festival Finds an Orchestra', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Easter and Holy Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thielemann, Christian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Berlin Philharmonic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dresden Staatskapelle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Salzburg (Austria)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T19:40:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5c81095-7407-58d6-8d70-c194bbe76702', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5c81095-7407-58d6-8d70-c194bbe76702'}, {'abstract': 'The Times’s Danny Hakim is responding to readers’ questions about instances of abuse in homes for the mentally disabled.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/answers-about-abuse-in-state-run-homes/', 'snippet': 'The Times’s Danny Hakim is responding to readers’ questions about instances of abuse in homes for the mentally disabled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Monday, The New York Times published a second article in a series examining the treatment of the developmentally disabled in New York State. In his investigation, Danny Hakim of The Times reviewed thousands of state records and court documents and interviewed current and former employees at nine state-run institutions that house the developmentally disabled. He found that supervisors at these institutions have tolerated physical and psychological abuse, knowingly hired unqualified workers, ignored complaints by whistle-blowers and failed to investigate credibly cases of abuse and neglect.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Answers About Abuse in State-Run Homes', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Group Homes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carey, Michael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office for People With Developmental Disabilities (NYS)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T19:41:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b376954-248f-52d3-93ee-74c9a83a97b7', 'word_count': 1012, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b376954-248f-52d3-93ee-74c9a83a97b7'}, {'abstract': 'The brother of the United States attorney in Manhattan is the co-founder of a company that was recently sold to Amazon.com for more than half a billion dollars.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/the-bhararas-join-an-elite-band-of-brothers/', 'snippet': 'The brother of the United States attorney in Manhattan is the co-founder of a company that was recently sold to Amazon.com for more than half a billion dollars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are a bunch of brothers and sisters who have reached the pinnacles of their respective fields. Standouts include the late Bruce Wasserstein, the star investment banker, and his late sister, Wendy Wasserstein, the renowned playwright. There is Maryanne Trump Barry, a federal judge in New Jersey, and her brother, The Donald. And most famously these days, we have the Emanuels: Rahm, the mayor of Chicago; Ari, the Hollywood super agent; and Zeke, a health care expert. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fabulous Bharara Boys', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bharara, Preet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T19:44:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3168568-d3fc-5f1d-a957-60cae25528bb', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3168568-d3fc-5f1d-a957-60cae25528bb'}, {'abstract': 'A chat with William Gerstenmaier, who is the chief of NASA’s shuttle and space station programs.', 'web_url': 'https://pogue.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/a-look-back-at-the-space-shuttle/', 'snippet': 'A chat with William Gerstenmaier, who is the chief of NASA’s shuttle and space station programs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two weeks ago, “CBS Sunday Morning” aired my story about the end of the Space Shuttle program. It was a story dear to my heart, one I’d hoped to do for years. It took me three trips to Kennedy Space Center to finally film the liftoff (because the final liftoff of the shuttle Endeavour kept being delayed).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Look Back at the Space Shuttle', 'kicker': 'Pogue&#039;s Posts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Space Station', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'SPACE SHUTTLE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Space Stations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T19:48:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/088558a1-8bdf-56c4-8dbd-3cc7f72cbbe8', 'word_count': 2272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/088558a1-8bdf-56c4-8dbd-3cc7f72cbbe8'}, {'abstract': 'Photo: dawn still life with haze-shrouded cruise ship and lifeguard awaiting arrival of bathing hordes.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/coney-island-523-a-m/', 'snippet': 'Photo: dawn still life with haze-shrouded cruise ship and lifeguard awaiting arrival of bathing hordes.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coney Island, 5:23 A.M.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CONEY ISLAND (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T19:54:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f34304e-a405-504d-bb2e-45472806a713', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f34304e-a405-504d-bb2e-45472806a713'}, {'abstract': 'Few major-studio films this summer are about women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/movies/few-summer-movies-aimed-at-women.html', 'snippet': 'Few major-studio films this summer are about women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IF you’re a woman who roared, snorted or sniggered at “Bridesmaids,” if you like watching other women on screen, you should see it again. Because that hit comedy written by Kristen Wiig and Annie Mumolo and directed by Paul Feig, turns out to be one of the few occasions this summer when you can enjoy a movie about and with women released by a major studio. From now through August, American films will again be almost all male, almost all the time (the occasional decorative gal pal notwithstanding) as this year’s boys of summer — the Green Lantern and Captain America, Conan the Barbarian and Conan O’Brien — invade the multiplex, seizing media-entertainment minds and your dollars. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/arts/DARGIS-1/DARGIS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 273, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/12/arts/DARGIS-1/DARGIS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 273}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/arts/DARGIS-1/DARGIS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 467, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/arts/DARGIS-1/DARGIS-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/arts/DARGIS-1/DARGIS-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Living Is Easy; The Women Are Missing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Living Is Easy;The Women Are Missing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T19:56:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38cb26de-0c6a-53dd-bba4-cff4e65d5989', 'word_count': 1013, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38cb26de-0c6a-53dd-bba4-cff4e65d5989'}, {'abstract': 'Senator Rand Paul asks whether the government is adequately screening and then keeping tabs on those who enter the country with student visas or as refugees.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/to-rand-paul-legal-immigration-is-also-a-concern/', 'snippet': 'Senator Rand Paul asks whether the government is adequately screening and then keeping tabs on those who enter the country with student visas or as refugees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s common to hear a senator express concerns about illegal immigration these days, but Senator Rand Paul, Republican of Kentucky, is also concerned about legal immigration.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Rand Paul, Legal Immigration Is Also a Concern', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bersin, Alan D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Rand', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shahzad, Faisal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Customs and Border Protection (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yemen', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T19:58:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emmarie Huetteman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emmarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huetteman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7af0eb3c-b775-56bf-9152-ac460a693fd3', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7af0eb3c-b775-56bf-9152-ac460a693fd3'}, {'abstract': 'Critics who want a preview of “Terra Nova,” Fox’s mix of time travel, dystopia, dinosaurs and family drama that is scheduled for that network’s fall season, are restricted to small screenings of the series’ first hour at NewsCorp headquarters in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/foxs-terra-nova-steeped-in-mystery/', 'snippet': 'Critics who want a preview of “Terra Nova,” Fox’s mix of time travel, dystopia, dinosaurs and family drama that is scheduled for that network’s fall season, are restricted to small screenings of the series’ first hour at NewsCorp headquarters in M...', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a rhythm, slightly irritating to television critics, to the rollout of the new fall shows on the major television networks. After the upfront presentations in May, DVDs are mailed or online screeners are posted with warnings that the shows in question are works in progress, and that credits and effects — or even actors and entire plot lines — may change. Later in the summer the final screeners are provided (meaning that the conscientious or curious critic could end up watching every new pilot twice).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fox’s ‘Terra Nova’ Steeped in Mystery', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T20:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6038b7d-9bf5-56ad-aba5-f96e6f88dfd0', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6038b7d-9bf5-56ad-aba5-f96e6f88dfd0'}, {'abstract': 'Massachusetts regulators call the practice “a dishonest and unethical violation” of the state’s securities act.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/goldman-fined-10-million-over-trading-huddles/', 'snippet': 'Massachusetts regulators call the practice “a dishonest and unethical violation” of the state’s securities act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stock research has long been considered a big money loser on Wall Street. It costs millions to produce and most investors are unwilling to pay hard cash for it. But in 2006 Goldman Sachs came up with a plan to leverage its equity research, funneling short-term stock tips to its biggest trading clients. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goldman Fined $10 Million for Tips Sent to Big Clients', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T20:01:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susanne Craig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Craig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98777a92-63d7-502a-b98f-261293f986cf', 'word_count': 728, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98777a92-63d7-502a-b98f-261293f986cf'}, {'abstract': 'Ford announced on Thursday that it would exclusively offer hybrid and plug-in hybrid versions of its new people mover to American customers.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/ford-c-max-to-spawn-a-prius-fighting-family/', 'snippet': 'Ford announced on Thursday that it would exclusively offer hybrid and plug-in hybrid versions of its new people mover to American customers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At its corporate headquarters in Dearborn, Mich., the Ford Motor Company announced Thursday that it would introduce its C-Max small crossover vehicle in 2012 as the foundation of a dedicated line of parallel hybrid and plug-in hybrid vehicles. The strategy closely resembles that of the Toyota Prius, the hybrid sedan that soon will be offered also as a plug-in hybrid, as well as in a wagon body style and potentially a compact hatchback.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ford C-Max to Spawn a Prius-Fighting Family', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T20:04:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Garrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c06a711-3067-532c-a6a3-3978eb0f1a55', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c06a711-3067-532c-a6a3-3978eb0f1a55'}, {'abstract': 'Just about everyone who knows the couple says their marrying seemed inevitable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/fashion/weddings/sarah-fox-and-calvin-ford-weddings.html', 'snippet': 'Just about everyone who knows the couple says their marrying seemed inevitable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN July 2009, a few weeks after her fiancé called off their engagement, Sarah Fox sent word in an e-mail to her high school sweetheart, Calvin Ford.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/fashion/weddings/VOWS-1/VOWS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/12/fashion/weddings/VOWS-1/VOWS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 349}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype