or Djo Tunda Wa Munga gives “Viva Riva!” a playful sensuality that goes a long way toward disguising formula.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/ROUNDUP-VIVA/ROUNDUP-VIVA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/ROUNDUP-VIVA/ROUNDUP-VIVA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/ROUNDUP-VIVA/ROUNDUP-VIVA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 628, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/ROUNDUP-VIVA/ROUNDUP-VIVA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/ROUNDUP-VIVA/ROUNDUP-VIVA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Viva Riva!’', 'kicker': 'Movie Review ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Viva Riva!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:06:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a77ea3f0-9ec3-5aa8-8e3b-7be0fa99c9be', 'word_count': 374, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a77ea3f0-9ec3-5aa8-8e3b-7be0fa99c9be'}, {'abstract': '“Double Solitaire,” at the Katonah Museum of Art, takes a fascinating look at the married life and work of a Surrealist It couple, Kay Sage and Yves Tanguy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/arts/design/double-solitaire-art-by-kay-sage-and-yves-tanguy-review.html', 'snippet': '“Double Solitaire,” at the Katonah Museum of Art, takes a fascinating look at the married life and work of a Surrealist It couple, Kay Sage and Yves Tanguy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KATONAH, N.Y. — “Double Solitaire: The Surreal Worlds of Kay Sage and Yves Tanguy,” at the Katonah Museum of Art, takes a fascinating look at the married life and work of this Surrealist It couple. Their union was remarkably equitable, even by modern standards, though it may also have been artistically suffocating. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/DOUBLE-span/DOUBLE-1307639918935-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/DOUBLE-span/DOUBLE-1307639918935-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/DOUBLE-span/DOUBLE-1307639918935-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/DOUBLE-span/DOUBLE-1307639918935-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/DOUBLE-span/DOUBLE-1307639918935-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Surrealist Partners in Painting, but Don’t Call Them a Team', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Surrealist Partners in Painting, but Don’t Call Them a Team', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanguy, Yves', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Katonah Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:07:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e539b939-d6ad-50d5-82bf-b07de65d1509', 'word_count': 973, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e539b939-d6ad-50d5-82bf-b07de65d1509'}, {'abstract': 'Hopeless.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/the-white-house-believes-in-the-confidence-fairy/', 'snippet': 'Hopeless.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ryan Avent brings grim news from a White House forum on the economy:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The White House Believes in the Confidence Fairy', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:09:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad5e13fd-c53c-598c-b11b-5682688ebf52', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad5e13fd-c53c-598c-b11b-5682688ebf52'}, {'abstract': 'The ongoing fallout from the decision to go, and then not to go, to the Bahrain Grand Prix is a hot topic in Montreal before the Canadian Grand Prix, but little is being said.', 'web_url': 'https://formulaone.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/lots-of-talk-on-bahrain-but-no-answers/', 'snippet': 'The ongoing fallout from the decision to go, and then not to go, to the Bahrain Grand Prix is a hot topic in Montreal before the Canadian Grand Prix, but little is being said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTREAL — Sometimes you get the feeling that there is a united front in Formula One among team directors, drivers, stakeholders and others when it comes to thorny issues. Today in the heat of the paddock in Montreal, in near tropical weather conditions of 28 degrees Celsius and 57 percent humidity, the hot topic of Formula One’s Bahrain about-face was discussed at length. But nothing much was said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lots of Talk on Bahrain but No Answers', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:13:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e46bf77-a41c-5b86-b1e8-32387344f646', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e46bf77-a41c-5b86-b1e8-32387344f646'}, {'abstract': 'The ongoing fallout from the decision to go, and then not to go, to the Bahrain Grand Prix is a hot topic in Montreal before the Canadian Grand Prix, but little is being said.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/lots-of-talk-on-bahrain-but-no-answers/', 'snippet': 'The ongoing fallout from the decision to go, and then not to go, to the Bahrain Grand Prix is a hot topic in Montreal before the Canadian Grand Prix, but little is being said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTREAL — Sometimes you get the feeling that there is a united front in Formula One among team directors, drivers, stakeholders and others when it comes to thorny issues. Today in the heat of the paddock in Montreal, in near tropical weather conditions of 28 degrees Celsius and 57 percent humidity, the hot topic of Formula One’s Bahrain about-face was discussed at length. But nothing much was said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lots of Talk on Bahrain but No Answers', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:13:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dda62510-92a8-568a-a82e-69db6914b058', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dda62510-92a8-568a-a82e-69db6914b058'}, {'abstract': 'The work of the photographer Elliott Erwitt, who seized and froze decisive moments over several decades, is the subject of an exhibition at the International Center of Photography.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/arts/design/elliott-erwitts-photographs-review.html', 'snippet': 'The work of the photographer Elliott Erwitt, who seized and froze decisive moments over several decades, is the subject of an exhibition at the International Center of Photography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a photograph by Elliott Erwitt, “New York City, 1949,” we behold, from behind, Augustus Saint-Gaudens’s bronze sculpture of a nude Diana. Drawing her bow, she seems to be aiming at the silhouetted figure of a man far away down a long hallway. In this, like so many of Mr. Erwitt’s images, the light humor thinly veils poetic profundity. A modern cipher of a man is about to get his comeuppance from a mythical divinity: Diana, the virginal huntress, the sight of whom makes men fall prey to the hounds of their own lust. As the sex scandals reported almost daily in the news media attest, many powerful men have suffered, metaphorically, the fate of that unlucky voyeur Actaeon. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/ERWITT-span/ERWITT-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/ERWITT-span/ERWITT-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/ERWITT-span/ERWITT-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Captured: A New York Minute, or One in Havana', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Captured: A New York Minute, or One in Havana', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erwitt, Elliott', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Center of Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:14:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, '