ease of the Pentagon Papers? Take a guess in a Twitter message.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What were the 11 words? It was an irresistible mystery.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Guessing Game Over 11 Secret Words', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pentagon Papers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:19:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31ddcd61-b78a-56b3-994a-28800f2ee67b', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31ddcd61-b78a-56b3-994a-28800f2ee67b'}, {'abstract': 'In the ratings battle of reality shows on Wednesday, NBC’s “America’s Got Talent” outperformed Fox’s “So You Think You Can Dance” by a wide margin, lifting the network to No. 1 on the night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/arts/television/reality-vs-reality-nbc-tops-fox.html', 'snippet': 'In the ratings battle of reality shows on Wednesday, NBC’s “America’s Got Talent” outperformed Fox’s “So You Think You Can Dance” by a wide margin, lifting the network to No. 1 on the night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the ratings battle of reality shows on Wednesday, NBC’s “America’s Got Talent” outperformed Fox’s “So You Think You Can Dance” by a wide margin, lifting the network to No. 1 on the night. According to Nielsen’s estimates, 12.3 million viewers tuned in for the two-hour installment of “America’s Got Talent” from 9 to 11 p.m., while “So You Think You Can Dance” drew 7.1 million from 8 to 10. NBC’s “Minute to Win It” garnered a meager 4.7 million at 8, but NBC still averaged more viewers over all for the night than second- place Fox. CBS was third with repeats of “Undercover Boss” (4.4 million), “Criminal Minds” (5.7 million) and “Blue Bloods” (6.2 million). ABC finished fourth, earning 4.4 million for back-to-back reruns of “The Middle” in the 8 p.m. hour, followed by repeats of “Modern Family” at 9 and 9:30, which also drew 4.4 million. At 10, an ABC “20/20” special exploring the allure of reality television lagged well behind in its time slot, attracting just 3.6 million viewers.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reality vs. Reality: NBC Tops Fox', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reality vs. Reality: NBC Tops Fox', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:25:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a35169aa-a174-5a1e-8f71-ae366a9697b6', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a35169aa-a174-5a1e-8f71-ae366a9697b6'}, {'abstract': "June 9, 2011 - At a meeting in Dubai, foreign nations discussed how to fund Libya's opposition.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/world/africa/100000000857486/timescast--financing-libyas-opposition.html', 'snippet': "June 9, 2011 - At a meeting in Dubai, foreign nations discussed how to fund Libya's opposition.", 'lead_paragraph': "June 9, 2011 - At a meeting in Dubai, foreign nations discussed how to fund Libya's opposition.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None,