ddlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00e60b2a-7c37-57a0-aa2c-74e3c5ed676b', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00e60b2a-7c37-57a0-aa2c-74e3c5ed676b'}, {'abstract': 'Marc Jacobs’s resort collection was largely inspired by 60s and 70s French cinema.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/just-one-look-louis-vuitton-resort-collection/', 'snippet': 'Marc Jacobs’s resort collection was largely inspired by 60s and 70s French cinema.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even though it is rather obvious that Marc Jacobs doesn’t design all of the 947 collections he turns out annually, he has always been gracious when it comes to giving credit where credit is due. And credit for this year’s Louis Vuitton Resort collection goes not only to his studio design director, Julie De Libran, but to his longtime friend Sofia Coppola, who continues to play the role of muse at large. The clothes were largely inspired by 1960s and ’70s French cinema, but a few looks, like this kicky, pleated dress with its matching Vespa helmet, would look at home on Coppola herself, as she tools around Paris’s Sixth Arrondissement.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Louis Vuitton Resort Collection', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:30:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8340d42-1c52-5364-abc2-0316f3e9e180', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8340d42-1c52-5364-abc2-0316f3e9e180'}, {'abstract': 'Long Island City has its fourth annual bike parade, the High Line has a food court and other special offerings, and Lincoln Center is selling discounted tickets to its summer festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/arts/weekend-miser-bike-parade-in-queens-high-line-special-offerings.html', 'snippet': 'Long Island City has its fourth annual bike parade, the High Line has a food court and other special offerings, and Lincoln Center is selling discounted tickets to its summer festival.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0The fourth annual Bike Parade takes off from Socrates Sculpture Park in Queens on Saturday, with cycle-centered festivities throughout the day. Children can get ready to roll in Hallets Cove Playground (Vernon Boulevard and 30th Avenue) from 11 a.m. to 1 p.m. at a bike swap organized by Recycle-a-Bicycle. The city Transportation Department will provide helmet fittings and giveaways, and Bike New York will talk safety. At noon the Free Style Arts Association, a group commissioned by communities to build interest in the arts, helps host a bike-decorating workshop that continues until the parade leaves the park at 3. The celebration ends with a reception in Queensbridge Park. (Free; Long Island City; 718-956-1819, Ext. 12, socratessculpturepark.org.) ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/MISER-span/MISER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 342, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/MISER-span/MISER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 342}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/MISER-span/MISER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 597, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name'