candal; the Republican Field of Candidates Gels; Addressing the Economy During the Elections', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Podcasts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:59:11+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/ed02e70f-686c-5943-95af-0ab29b586ece', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/ed02e70f-686c-5943-95af-0ab29b586ece'}, {'abstract': 'NBC achieved notable gains in total volume and in rates for the sale of commercial time ahead of the 2011-12 television season.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/with-nbcs-finish-the-big-five-complete-upfront-sales/', 'snippet': 'NBC achieved notable gains in total volume and in rates for the sale of commercial time ahead of the 2011-12 television season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NBC on Thursday afternoon became the fifth of the five big English-language broadcast television networks to complete the sale of commercial time ahead of the 2011-12 season in what is known as the upfront market.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With NBC’s Finish, the ‘Big Five’ Complete Upfront Sales', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T21:59:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c32356a3-b59d-516c-949f-a91eb67f02fa', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c32356a3-b59d-516c-949f-a91eb67f02fa'}, {'abstract': 'What the departure of Newt Gingrich from the presidential field would mean for the Republican nominating battle.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/questions-amid-the-wreckage-of-gingrichs-campaign/', 'snippet': 'What the departure of Newt Gingrich from the presidential field would mean for the Republican nominating battle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It doesn’t require any deep thinking to determine that when virtually all of your most senior advisers suddenly decide to quit your campaign — as happened to Newt Gingrich on Thursday afternoon — your bid for the presidency isn’t off to the best start. Although Mr. Gingrich is insisting — at least for the time being — that he’ll remain in the race, betting markets now give him just a 1-in-200 chance of winning the Republican nomination.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Questions Amid the Wreckage of Gingrich’s Campaign', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2008', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cain, Herman', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pawlenty, Tim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T22:01:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77caf2db-69f3-5cb7-b3b8-4d2f5bd6d632', 'word_count': 1015, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77caf2db-69f3-5cb7-b3b8-4d2f5bd6d632'}, {'abstract': 'An ambitious art dealer and modern technology are midwives to new casts of an original Statue of Liberty model.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/arts/design/bronze-casts-of-statue-of-liberty-model-made-in-france.html', 'snippet': 'An ambitious art dealer and modern technology are midwives to new casts of an original Statue of Liberty model.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — For the last century or so, people wanting their own Statue of Liberty settled for a knockoff, like the version that looks down from a hotel rooftop in Pristina, Kosovo, or the copy in the bar of a bowling alley in Warrington, England. Now those with a preference for authenticity (and the means to indulge it) can buy the closest thing possible — a bronze cast made from the plaster sculpture that Frédéric-Auguste Bartholdi used to create the famous monument.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/STATUE-span/STATUE-span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/STATUE-span/STATUE-span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600