. and A. With Alex Brown', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Barbaro (Race Horse)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T23:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14a81f88-2221-5262-a025-ab96fc4fc8c5', 'word_count': 1348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14a81f88-2221-5262-a025-ab96fc4fc8c5'}, {'abstract': 'After a period of conservation and study, Bellini’s “St. Francis in the Desert” gets its own special show at the Frick.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/arts/design/bellinis-st-francis-in-the-desert-at-the-frick.html', 'snippet': 'After a period of conservation and study, Bellini’s “St. Francis in the Desert” gets its own special show at the Frick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Frick Collection has long since mastered the art historical meet and greet. Last spring Rembrandt, seen in a late self-portrait, received fans in the Frick’s skylighted Oval Room.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/BELLINI-span/BELLINI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/BELLINI-span/BELLINI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/BELLINI-span/BELLINI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/BELLINI-span/BELLINI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/BELLINI-span/BELLINI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Alone in the Wilderness and the Spotlight', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Alone in the Wilderness and the Spotlight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Frick Collection', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bellini, Giovanni', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T23:01:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65f2b83f-52a1-5a29-b8bc-766957323d3e', 'word_count': 1584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65f2b83f-52a1-5a29-b8bc-766957323d3e'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Trip” Steve Coogan and Rob Brydon motor to fine restaurants in northern England, and along the way they philosophize, joust and parry, and entertain each other, frequently by imitating someone else.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/movies/the-trip-a-michael-winterbottom-comedy-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Trip” Steve Coogan and Rob Brydon motor to fine restaurants in northern England, and along the way they philosophize, joust and parry, and entertain each other, frequently by imitating someone else.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In contrast to Roger Corman’s 1967 freakout, “The Trip,” no hallucinogens are harmed in the Michael Winterbottom comedy of the same title, a British road movie laced with lacerating laughs and starring Steve Coogan and Rob Brydon. Mr. Coogan does, in fact, smoke a joint, lighting up in the same house where Coleridge wrote “Dejection: An Ode” and indulged in opium, the soporific that enslaved him in “humiliation and debasement.” Mr. Coogan has made a career partly by riffing on narcissism and he’s in fine self-loving form, as is Mr. Brydon. For one man, the humiliation of choice here is fame (with a debasement chaser), while for the other it’s his incessant vocal mugging. Both give you a contact high.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/TRIP/TRIP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 825, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/TRIP/TRIP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/TRIP/TRIP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '2 Pairs of Sharp Elbows On White Tablecloths', 'kicker': "Movie Review | ‘The Trip'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2 Pairs of Sharp Elbows On White Tablecloths', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coogan, Steve', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corman, Roger', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winterbottom, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T23:03:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/37d03777-3af1-5a48-920c-a56f1a68aad7', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37d03777-3af1-5a48-920c-a56f1a68aad7'}, {'abstract': 'In a partnership with AT&T, the city plans to expand Internet access. The Wi-Fi began Thursday in Thomas Jefferson Park, Battery Bosque in Battery Park, and Joyce Kilmer Park in the Bronx.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/wi-fi-set-to-expand-to-19-more-city-parks/', 'snippet': 'In a partnership with AT&T, the city plans to expand Internet access. The Wi-Fi began Thursday in Thomas Jefferson Park, Battery Bosque in Battery Park, and Joyce Kilmer Park in the Bronx.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Free Wi-Fi service will be made available in 19 more city parks, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg announced on Thursday in East Harlem, which would more than double the number of parks with Wi-Fi.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wi-Fi Set to Expand to 19 More City Parks', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T23:13:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/061a8c43-1c01-56b2-98a5-600d08d833ad', 'word_count': 557, 'uri': 'nyt://article/061a8c43-1c01-56b2-98a5-600d08d833ad'}, {'abstract': 'Ezra Klein’s views on physician-assisted suicide, and mine.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/the-implications-of-assisted-suicide/', 'snippet': 'Ezra Klein’s views on physician-assisted suicide, and mine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ezra Klein writes that he didn’t find the arguments in my Jack Kevorkian column persuasive, but I think our views aren’t actually all that far apart. Here’s his critique:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Implications of Assisted Suicide', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T23:16:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdddbcb2-6315-5d7c-aa1b-a51d63f6127e', 'word_count': 806, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdddbcb2-6315-5d7c-aa1b-a51d63f6127e'}, {'abstract': '“Goldman Sachs is the scapegoat of our time,” the stock analyst contends in his latest research note.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/09/richard-bove-does-an-about-face-on-goldman/', 'snippet': '“Goldman Sachs is the scapegoat of our time,” the stock analyst contends in his latest research note.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Richard X. Bove, one of the most bearish stock analysts covering Goldman Sachs, has had an about-face on the firm’s role in the credit crisis. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Richard Bove Does an About-Face on Goldman', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ratings and Rating Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bove, Richard X', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T23:17:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susanne Craig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Craig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0fc03dc-21f8-5269-8361-e8d737fe1c7b', 'word_count': 715, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0fc03dc-21f8-5269-8361-e8d737fe1c7b'}, {'abstract': 'The French novels I read in my youth were really English novels by translators, based on original ideas by Camus and Cocteau.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/the-pleasures-and-perils-of-creative-translation.html', 'snippet': 'The French novels I read in my youth were really English novels by translators, based on original ideas by Camus and Cocteau.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first modern French novel I read — the one that introduced terms like “avant-garde” and “surrealism” to my tongue — was “Les Enfants Terribles,” by Jean Cocteau. I thrilled to the extravagant antics of Paul and Elisabeth, brother and sister, soon to be sleeping partners, in “the Room” they share with teenage friends. Life in Cocteau’s Paris is “the Game,” with its own rules or no rules, a state peculiar to the spirit on the threshold between infancy and adolescence. All lovers of this slim novel seek to unlock the secret door leading from their own room to the Room on the Rue Montmartre. When I open it now, it is my cleareyed 20-year-old self in Scotland who begins to read, as much as the weathered, bespectacled “me” of almost four decades on.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Campbell/Campbell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 654, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Campbell/Campbell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Campbell/Campbell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pleasures and Perils of Creative Translation', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Creative Misreading', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Translation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cocteau, Jean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Language', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Camus, Albert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T23:17:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Campbell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Campbell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e7d5b7f-f572-5cc1-be27-8806c1e9ed46', 'word_count': 1367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e7d5b7f-f572-5cc1-be27-8806c1e9ed46'}, {'abstract': 'Lydia Davis is well known for her extremely short, elliptical stories. But in her parallel career, as a translator of French literature, she has tackled wordier writes, including Proust and Flaubert.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/up-front-lydia-davis.html', 'snippet': 'Lydia Davis is well known for her extremely short, elliptical stories. But in her parallel career, as a translator of French literature, she has tackled wordier writes, including Proust and Flaubert.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On our cover this week, Lydia Davis reviews John Ashbery’s new translation of Rimbaud’s “Illuminations.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 688, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Lydia Davis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Lydia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T23:17:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc14aeda-4d67-5120-b389-6a4c48efda1a', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc14aeda-4d67-5120-b389-6a4c48efda1a'}, {'abstract': 'John Ashbery brings a long and deep familiarity with French life, language and culture to this translation of Arthur Rimbaud’s poetry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/book-review-illuminations-by-arthur-rimbaud.html', 'snippet': 'John Ashbery brings a long and deep familiarity with French life, language and culture to this translation of Arthur Rimbaud’s poetry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some associations with the name Rimbaud are very familiar: the highly romantic photograph taken a few months after he first settled in Paris, already at 17 the dedicatedly bohemian artist, with his pale blue eyes, distant gaze, thatch of hair, carelessly rumpled clothes; the startling, much interpreted declaration Je est un autre (“I is someone else”); the fact that he produced a masterly, innovative and influential body of poetry while still in his teens; that he stopped writing around age 21 and never went back to it, engaging thereafter in various sometimes mysterious commercial and mystical enterprises in exotic locations, including a period of gun-\xadrunning in Africa (and, oddly, an attempt to enlist in the United States Navy).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Davis/Davis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 879, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Davis/Davis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Davis/Davis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rimbaud’s Wise Music', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wise Music', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rimbaud, Arthur', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ashbery, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Language', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Translation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-09T23:17:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Davis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/99e324c3-b5c2-524d-a24d-833afee03678', 'word_count': 1990, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99e324c3-b5c2-524d-a24d-833afee03678'}, {'abstract': 'Readers’ questions: The August Zinsser house, and 31 East 17th Street, a building that used to be taller.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/realestate/readers-questions-streetscapes.html', 'snippet': 'Readers’ questions: The August Zinsser house, and 31 East 17th Street, a building that used to be taller.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. In his family history, my father, William H. Zinsser, writes that his cousin August Zinsser moved his mansion, brick by brick, from West 58th Street and 10th Avenue to 14 West 68th Street. Can you find a picture of the old house in its previous location?', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/realestate/street-1/street-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 843, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'T