crooner Vaughn Monroe starred in commercials where both an RCA Victor radio and an unnamed competing brand were dropped from the top of a ladder, and only the RCA Victor radio remained in one piece. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/business/Adco/Adco-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 808, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/business/Adco/Adco-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/10/business/Adco/Adco-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Old-Time Torture Tests Resurface on YouTube, and Tablets Take a Licking', 'kicker': 'Advertising', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Old-Time Torture Tests Resurface on YouTube, and Tablets Take a Licking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recordings and Downloads (Video)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Guarantees and Warranties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T01:51:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Adam Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Adam', 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/527b340b-fe83-52ee-bd7a-f84e91a7411c', 'word_count': 923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/527b340b-fe83-52ee-bd7a-f84e91a7411c'}, {'abstract': '“The Ring of the Nibelung” offers the San Francisco Opera a chance to announce its creative values.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/us/10bcculture.html', 'snippet': '“The Ring of the Nibelung” offers the San Francisco Opera a chance to announce its creative values.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Ring of the Nibelung,” Richard Wagner’s monumental, four-part opera cycle chronicling the battle between gods, heroes and monsters over an all-powerful, magical ring wrought out of a mystical hoard of gold, is opera’s answer to extreme sports. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/us/10BCCULTURE/10BCCULTURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/10/us/10BCCULTURE/10BCCULTURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/us/10BCCULTURE/10BCCULTURE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/us/10BCCULTURE/10BCCULTURE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/10/us/10BCCULTURE/10BCCULTURE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New ‘Ring’ Straddles Two Visions', 'kicker': 'The Bay Citizen', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New ‘Ring’ Straddles Two Visions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Finances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T01:56:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chloe Veltman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chloe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Veltman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbdc8390-3863-5a70-91af-54dec329c2f4', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbdc8390-3863-5a70-91af-54dec329c2f4'}, {'abstract': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art’s decision to raise its suggested admission fee prompts some discussion about whether museums should charge the public at all.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/arts/design/metropolitan-museum-admission-fee-debate.html', 'snippet': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art’s decision to raise its suggested admission fee prompts some discussion about whether museums should charge the public at all.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art announced last week that it planned to raise its admission price to $25 from $20. Though the price is only recommended (you can pay a penny and get in, as the museum hates to see newspapers write, and as newspapers love to keep writing), the announcement has occasioned an outpouring of sentiment on the Web and elsewhere about the cost of museumgoing. Do sizable admission prices, even suggested ones, discourage lower-income visitorship? (Of course.) Should museums that receive taxpayer money charge for admission? (A lot of people say no, even though many museums receive relatively little in the way of public subsidies.) Do museums have a kind of moral obligation, like libraries, to be free? (Museum directors are divided on the subject. Some, like Philippe de Montebello, the former director of the Met, point out that almost all cultural goods come with a price. “Philosophically, what is it about a work of art that makes it mandatory that it should be available for nothing?” he has asked.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/MUSEUM-1/MUSEUM-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/MUSEUM-1/MUSEUM-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/MUSEUM-1/MUSEUM-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/MUSEUM-1/MUSEUM-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/10/arts/MUSEUM-1/MUSEUM-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Cultural Calculation: Museum Fees', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Cultural Calculation: Museum Fees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T02:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9