few decades more than Christopher Hitchens, who in an e-mail gave some helpful hints on how to start a feud — and more important, how to keep it going.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Sunday’s Week in Review, I wrote about the death of the old-school literary feud now that Paul Theroux has shaken hands with V.S. Naipaul, Rick Moody has thrown a friendly pie at Dale Peck and Twitter has supplanted the White Horse Tavern (or “The Dick Cavett Show”) as the best place to throw a bookish punch.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hitchens on the Art of the Feud', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hitchens, Christopher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T12:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b22eb771-1b6f-570b-8a65-860d9898c6a8', 'word_count': 601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b22eb771-1b6f-570b-8a65-860d9898c6a8'}, {'abstract': 'A scene from "Trollhunter," about a group of student filmmakers who find trolls in the Norwegian countryside.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/movies/100000000848994/a-clip-from-trollhunter.html', 'snippet': 'A scene from "Trollhunter," about a group of student filmmakers who find trolls in the Norwegian countryside.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A scene from "Trollhunter," about a group of student filmmakers who find trolls in the Norwegian countryside.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/video-trollhunter-clip/video-trollhunter-clip-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/video-trollhunter-clip/video-trollhunter-clip-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/video-trollhunter-clip/video-trollhunter-clip-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/03/arts/video-trollhunter-clip/video-trollhunter-clip-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': "Clip: 'Trollhunter'", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T12:45:43+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'None None', 'person': [{'firstname': 'None', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'None', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/54ea2728-9db2-52f4-977e-55bdd461b3f3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/54ea2728-9db2-52f4-977e-55bdd461b3f3'}, {'abstract': 'At next week’s design fair, the Dutch design superstar Hella Jongerius will feature limited-edition vases to go with her new monograph.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/design-miamibasel-hella-jongerius-in-print-and-in-porcelain/', 'snippet': 'At next week’s design fair, the Dutch design superstar Hella Jongerius will feature limited-edition vases to go with her new monograph.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You would expect to see the British publisher Phaidon Press at the Frankfurt book fair, not among the galleries at Design Miami/Basel. Nonetheless, the publisher has reserved a booth at the Swiss event next week, where it will display “Colored Vases (Series 3),” a collection of 300 unique vases by the Dutch design superstar Hella Jongerius, to accompany her monograph, “Hella Jongerius: Misfit.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Design Miami/Basel | Hella Jongerius, in Print and in Porcelain', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Serafin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Serafin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef9ae7a0-8e6c-5c97-8d9b-43a15cada596', 'word_count': 561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef9ae7a0-8e6c-5c97-8d9b-43a15cada596'}, {'abstract': 'Fighting to continue performance-enhancing drugs on race day and keep horse racing leaderless with an outmoded business model is a futile self-preservation strategy by the worst elements of the sport.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/the-guardians-of-racings-slow-death/', 'snippet': 'Fighting to continue performance-enhancing drugs on race day and keep horse racing leaderless with an outmoded business model is a futile self-preservation strategy by the worst elements of the sport.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When injections of furosemide and doses of aminocaproic acid to control exercise-induced pulmonary hemorrhage are given to starters in the 143rd Belmont Stakes Saturday, it might be the last time an American Triple Crown race will ever be run on legal performance-enhancing drugs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Guardians of Racing’s Slow Death', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Squires', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Squires', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65475d7c-a305-529a-9896-61f009dcbb03', 'word_count': 1171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65475d7c-a305-529a-9896-61f009dcbb03'}, {'abstract': 'Chaperon background; freedom foreground.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/magazine/the-ethicist-challenge-the-leader.html', 'snippet': 'Chaperon background; freedom foreground.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Until recently, I worked for a small nonprofit that, among other things, runs an international leadership program for high school students. While working for its president, I had access to his contact list, which included many sex-for-hire people. (Mostly transvestites.) What people do on their own time is their business, but I feel that the parents of these students would want to know this about a man who will be escorting their children overseas for a week. I know I would. Do you agree? A. K.,NEW JERSEY', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Challenge the Leader', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CHALLENGE THE LEADER', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostitution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/31bcbe20-2387-50e7-8363-6d3817488f69', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31bcbe20-2387-50e7-8363-6d3817488f69'}, {'abstract': 'Susan Dominus’s article on the Hogan twins, Krista and Tatiana, and their family was sensitive and insightful.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/magazine/a-mind-of-their-own.html', 'snippet': 'Susan Dominus’s article on the Hogan twins, Krista and Tatiana, and their family was sensitive and insightful.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Susan Dominus’s article on the Hogan twins, Krista and Tatiana, and their family was sensitive and insightful. Throughout, Dominus suggests the under-explored relationship among philosophy, spirituality and science. She does so without ever losing sight of these extraordinary little girls in all of their humanity. Most especially, the author is content with leaving questions unanswered. She acknowledges that there is much we don’t know. The girls provide the opportunity to revel in the questions, the mystery, that motivate great scientific discovery and artistic innovation. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mind of Their Own', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A MIND OF THEIR OWN', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/5962140c-93de-57da-988c-fe9da730453b', 'word_count': 818, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5962140c-93de-57da-988c-fe9da730453b'}, {'abstract': 'The glorious ruins of the Erie Canal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/12/magazine/Erie-Canal.html', 'snippet': 'The glorious ruins of the Erie Canal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The glorious ruins of the Erie Canal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/Erie-Canal-slide-JR8H/Erie-Canal-slide-JR8H-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/Erie-Canal-slide-JR8H/Erie-Canal-slide-JR8H-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/Erie-Canal-slide-JR8H/Erie-Canal-slide-JR8H-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Going for Broken', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Erie Canal (NYS)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Little Falls (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Canals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/8d1d3829-4b58-50bd-8f5e-ca846fb0416a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/8d1d3829-4b58-50bd-8f5e-ca846fb0416a'}, {'abstract': 'Part cathedral, part haunted house, caves hold our earliest arts and our deepest fears.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/magazine/entering-darkness.html', 'snippet': 'Part cathedral, part haunted house, caves hold our earliest arts and our deepest fears.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month I went to a particularly cavelike theater (the basement walls, outside the bathrooms, were covered with synthetic stone) to see “Cave of Forgotten Dreams,” Werner Herzog’s 3-D documentary about cave paintings. The film is a rapturous tour of the Chauvet cave in southern France, a dramatic pocket of rock that was discovered, in 1994, to contain the oldest known paintings in the history of the world: portraits, up to 32,000 years old, of bison, mammoths, reindeer, lions, bears and woolly rhinoceroses — the improbable megafauna of southern France during the last ice age. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/12caves/mag-12Caves-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 998, 'width': 966, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/12caves/mag-12Caves-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/12caves/mag-12Caves-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Entering Darkness', 'kicker': 'Descents', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'ENTERING DARKNESS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Caves and Caverns', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/183ce6fc-b6a0-5d8d-ab84-87ff980bbccc', 'word_count': 2290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/183ce6fc-b6a0-5d8d-ab84-87ff980bbccc'}, {'abstract': 'A father and his teenage daughter travel the road that will divide them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/magazine/the-last-father-daughter-road-trip.html', 'snippet': 'A father and his teenage daughter travel the road that will divide them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a long time now, I’ve been looking forward to this year with apprehension: 2011 is when my daughter, Julia, now 18, will undertake that very American rite of passage and “go away to college” — a phrase whose operative word is “away.” We live in Seattle, and in the Pacific Northwest, “collegeland,” as my daughter calls it, is centered in New England and New York, where most of her immediate friends will be going in September.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/mag-12road-t_CA0/mag-12road-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/mag-12road-t_CA0/mag-12road-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/mag-12road-t_CA0/mag-12road-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/mag-12road-t_CA0/mag-12road-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/mag-12road-t_CA0/mag-12road-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Getaway Car', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE GETAWAY CAR', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Road Trips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (State)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Raban', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Raban', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07ab5f8b-30d7-5691-b73a-03ca219c0a07', 'word_count': 5047, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07ab5f8b-30d7-5691-b73a-03ca219c0a07'}, {'abstract': 'At the glitziest mall in America, shoppers do not come out at night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/magazine/sticker-shock-at-vegass-glitziest-mall.html', 'snippet': 'At the glitziest mall in America, shoppers do not come out at night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saturday night, Las Vegas. In this mecca of extreme retail, shops remain open as late as midnight, like fancy bodegas, because even a craving for Balenciaga ballet flats should have the option of satisfying itself on demand. While overall consumer spending still hasn’t recovered from the recession, there are signs that luxury spending may be on the rise. High-end retailers (Tiffany & Company, Hermès, Saks Inc.) have reported first-quarter gains. In America, analysts note, the wealthiest 2 percent of the population has been doing most of the buying. Where does this leave Las Vegas, purveyor of luxury for the masses?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/12lede/mag-12lede-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 835, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/12lede/12lede-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/12lede/12lede-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sticker Shock and Awe', 'kicker': 'First', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'STICKER SHOCK AND AWE', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Vegas (Nev)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Casinos', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Fortini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fortini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50bd9c8a-14d4-555a-9fcd-4b47d51b73ac', 'word_count': 1354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50bd9c8a-14d4-555a-9fcd-4b47d51b73ac'}, {'abstract': 'A brief history of adventure and travel in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/12/magazine/adventure.html', 'snippet': 'A brief history of adventure and travel in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A brief history of adventure and travel in America.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/adventure-slide-FKP0/adventure-slide-FKP0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/adventure-slide-FKP0/adventure-slide-FKP0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/adventure-slide-FKP0/adventure-slide-FKP0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scout, Ski, Fly and Shoot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History (Academic Subject)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Adventure Travel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By CHARLES WILSON', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'WILSON', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ca4017fe-8dd1-575c-81e1-5ad5b01dd911', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ca4017fe-8dd1-575c-81e1-5ad5b01dd911'}, {'abstract': 'Conversations with a stranger on a bus trip across America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/magazine/lives-the-perfumed-cuban.html', 'snippet': 'Conversations with a stranger on a bus trip across America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In three days, I saw, heard and ate things that you see, hear and eat only on a 3,280-mile Greyhound trip across the United States. I also smelled unusual things. Battle Mountain, a town in Nevada that The Washington Post once called “the armpit of America” didn’t actually reek of rancid sweat. It smelled like clean desert air and the sweetness of boredom. If I close my eyes, I can smell the burritos they sell at 4 a.m. in Wendover, Utah, near the base where the pilots who dropped the atomic bombs were trained. They smelled like melted plastic. After this trip across 11 states, I’m also familiar with the odor of the toilets at the station in Des Moines and the candied peanuts they sell outside the Port Authority in Manhattan, my final destination. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '62', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/12lives/mag-12Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 854, 'width': 942, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/12lives/mag-12Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/magazine/12lives/mag-12Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Perfumed Cuban', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Perfumed Cuban', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spanish Language', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrés Felipe Solano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrés', 'middlename': 'Felipe', 'lastname': 'Solano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8209331c-e820-50bf-9bb4-cd269b939fcf', 'word_count': 825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8209331c-e820-50bf-9bb4-cd269b939fcf'}, {'abstract': 'The glorious ruins of the Erie Canal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/magazine/the-glorious-ruins-of-the-erie-canal.html', 'snippet': 'The glorious ruins of the Erie Canal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We paddled canoe-style; I sat in the middle and Marie at the stern. The water was clean and cold and deserted. We circled an island. Dead branches washed up in piles along the banks, making intricate symmetrical reflections in the nearly still water. Nathaniel Hawthorne described a ride on the Erie Canal in 1835 as “so tiresome.” From our small, slow rowboat, I found it mesmerizing.', 'pr