{'main': 'The Decline of Pimco Macro', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T13:51:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8550b38f-d26e-5c23-9fbc-dcd54af26e3a', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8550b38f-d26e-5c23-9fbc-dcd54af26e3a'}, {'abstract': 'Ireland the model, again.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/the-economic-consequences-of-mr-trichet/', 'snippet': 'Ireland the model, again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In response to my latest Iceland-Ireland post, a commenter at The Irish Economy writes,', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Economic Consequences of Mr. Trichet', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T14:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3b15cab-f458-5fe8-847b-020305580355', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3b15cab-f458-5fe8-847b-020305580355'}, {'abstract': 'Despite what you read, they often end up doing O.K.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/struggling-college-graduates/', 'snippet': 'Despite what you read, they often end up doing O.K.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sally Cameron has an Ivy League graduate degree, and yet found herself tending bar. Mel Rodenstein earned a master’s degree in international affairs but was working in a “mindless” clerk’s job, eating rice and beans to save money. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Struggling College Graduates', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T14:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a35181dd-43cb-5557-ae66-5eabdf3c122f', 'word_count': 403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a35181dd-43cb-5557-ae66-5eabdf3c122f'}, {'abstract': 'Social media is transforming war reporting. In this case, a war reporter was able to corral experts for help identifying a spent munition in Libya.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/following-up-part-i-battlefield-refuse-social-media-and-qaddafis-cluster-bombs/', 'snippet': 'Social media is transforming war reporting. In this case, a war reporter was able to corral experts for help identifying a spent munition in Libya.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Researchers who try to document and track the weapons used in conflicts today sometimes find the unusual or unexpected. This has been the case several times during the war in Libya. After the uprising turned violent in February, weapons from Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s arsenals flowed onto the battlefield and formed a grab bag of killing tools — a mix of mostly formerly Eastern bloc weapon systems and munitions spiced with, among other things, American armored vehicles and self-propelled artillery, French and British mortar rounds, NATO-standard rifle and machine-gun cartridges, Belgian rifles, Chinese Type 84 scatterable land mines and Spanish cluster munitions. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Following Up, Part 1: Battlefield Refuse, Social Media and Qaddafi’s Cluster Bombs', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T14:05:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By C.J. Chivers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Chivers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f3c1d30-8f4c-5d39-b7d7-4e851acce701', 'word_count': 2679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f3c1d30-8f4c-5d39-b7d7-4e851acce701'}, {'abstract': 'The ninth in our series of 10 student favorites, this poem was written by a 15-year-old in a literature class studying “Romeo and Juliet.”', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/found-poem-favorite-its-late-at-night/', 'snippet': 'The ninth in our series of 10 student favorites, this poem was written by a 15-year-old in a literature class studying “Romeo and Juliet.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s poem, chosen from more than 650 submissions to our Found Poem Student Challenge, was written by Lakenya, who is 15 years old and from Milwaukee. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Found Poem Favorite | ‘It’s Late at Night’', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '