ESTRA (OP. 31)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'West-Eastern Divan Orchestra', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CLASSICAL RECORDINGS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T14:33:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8356aaea-ab6e-58c6-88ca-fa53ef8ea90e', 'word_count': 295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8356aaea-ab6e-58c6-88ca-fa53ef8ea90e'}, {'abstract': '“The Watsons” was written in 1804 but never finished.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/a-reunion-of-sorts-two-pieces-of-unpublished-austen-novel-are-both-in-new-york/', 'snippet': '“The Watsons” was written in 1804 but never finished.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beginning on Saturday the two manuscript parts of the unfinished and unpublished Jane Austen novel known as “The Watsons” will not be reunited, exactly, but will be closer they’ve been in almost 100 years — just 35 blocks or so from each other. The first part of the manuscript, some 12 pages, was sold during World War I to benefit the Red Cross, and now belongs to the Morgan Library & Museum. The rest, 68 pages which had been in private hands in England until recently, is going on display for a week at Sotheby’s before being shipped back to London, where it will be auctioned off in mid-July.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Reunion of Sorts: Two Pieces of Unpublished Austen Novel Are Both in New York', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Manuscripts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austen, Jane', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN RED CROSS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Library & Museum', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T14:33:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e74e4e1c-2328-5bf8-91c2-5a8216b8e8ce', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e74e4e1c-2328-5bf8-91c2-5a8216b8e8ce'}, {'abstract': 'Gotham Extra: The calls for Mr. Weiner to resign seem to be less about moral outrage and more about political calculation.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/unforgivable-behavior-except-when-its-not/', 'snippet': 'Gotham Extra: The calls for Mr. Weiner to resign seem to be less about moral outrage and more about political calculation.', 'lead_paragraph': '  “Lying publicly about something like this is unforgivable and he should resign.” — Tim Kaine, former chairman, Democratic National Committee, on Representative Anthony D. Weiner.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unforgivable Behavior, Except When It’s Not', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'nam