of agencies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'POUGHKEEPSIE, N.Y. — For Bill O’Brien, summer had meant the bliss of the Hudson River ever since he went out fishing for stripers as a boy. But last year, after he was stopped once too often by law enforcement patrol boats with armed officers, he decided he had had it. He sold his 22-foot jet boat, convinced that a once-restful afternoon on the Hudson was just becoming too stressful to enjoy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/11/nyregion/BOATS2/BOATS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/11/nyregion/BOATS2/BOATS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/11/nyregion/BOATS2/BOATS2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/11/nyregion/BOATS2/BOATS2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/11/nyregion/BOATS2/BOATS2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Security Checks on Boaters Disrupt Idyllic Life on the Hudson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Security Checks on Boaters Disrupt Idyllic Life on the Hudson', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boats and Boating', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Coast Guard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Security and Warning Systems', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Border Patrol (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Naval Militia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Homeland Security Department', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T15:10:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Applebome', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Applebome', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc479e15-d540-5e5c-abae-0a80a1e14e00', 'word_count': 1103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc479e15-d540-5e5c-abae-0a80a1e14e00'}, {'abstract': 'A progressive political activist asks whether the personalization of search-engine results is a blessing or a curse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/book-review-the-filter-bubble-by-eli-pariser.html', 'snippet': 'A progressive political activist asks whether the personalization of search-engine results is a blessing or a curse.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Just Google it!” has become a common cyber-snobbish response to questions that seem too trivial to merit a human conversation. But is it really an answer? Now that more and more Internet sites are tailoring their services to the idiosyncrasies of individual users, queries for “climate change,” “stem cells” and even “pizza” may yield different outcomes for different people. This may be an era when we are increasingly entitled to our own facts — but should we also be entitled to our own search results?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank':