n Abu Ghosh, Ein Karem and Ein Sataf, enclaves near central Jerusalem where Israelis go to sip cappuccinos, shop for art, hike and otherwise forget that they live in one of the holiest cities in the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CASBAH-LIKE alleys lined with stone houses wound upward toward hills cloaked in pine and cypress trees. Hand-painted signs beckoned visitors to the shops and studios of jewelers, potters and other artisans. Just past a popular brunch spot with a packed terrace, Christian pilgrims from Russia, Poland and the United States gathered around a spring where Mary and Elizabeth, the mother of John the Baptist, were said to have met.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/travel/Sub-Next-4/Sub-Next-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/12/travel/Sub-Next-4/Sub-Next-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/travel/Sub-Next-4/Sub-Next-4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/travel/Sub-Next-4/Sub-Next-4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/travel/Sub-Next-4/Sub-Next-4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jerusalem Outings Go Beyond the Biblical', 'kicker': 'Next Stop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jerusalem Outings Go Beyond the Biblical', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jerusalem (Israel)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiking', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:05:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Hammer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hammer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/425beaa5-9b92-519b-b0f6-f45c6015f1a6', 'word_count': 1508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/425beaa5-9b92-519b-b0f6-f45c6015f1a6'}, {'abstract': 'The tree had stood in front of Dave Thom’s house in Inwood since at least 1934.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/storm-claims-a-venerable-catalpa-tree/', 'snippet': 'The tree had stood in front of Dave Thom’s house in Inwood since at least 1934.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thursday night’s storm, in addition to forcing the cancellation of the Black-Eyed Peas concert in Central Park, uprooted and knocked down Dave Thom’s catalpa tree. It had stood in front of Mr. Thom’s house on West 217th Street in Inwood since at least 1934, where it appears, manicured into a delightful umbrella shape, in the city tax photo below. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Storm Claims a Venerable Catalpa Tree', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'INWOOD (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:05:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55c28df0-0b56-5f66-a6bf-bb4130b800fe', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55c28df0-0b56-5f66-a6bf-bb4130b800fe'}, {'abstract': 'IndyCar drivers at Texas Motor Speedway will contest back-to-back 275-kilometer races on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/after-30-year-hiatus-the-return-of-the-indycar-doubleheader/', 'snippet': 'IndyCar drivers at Texas Motor Speedway will contest back-to-back 275-kilometer races on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Saturday night, Texas Motor Speedway will host the Firestone Twin 275s, the first IndyCar doubleheader since June 28, 1981, when Rick Mears swept two 125-mile races at Atlanta. Saturday’s two 114-lap, 275-kilometer (171 miles) races replace the Firestone 550, which traditionally is held at the Fort Worth track at this time of year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After 30-Year Hiatus, the Return of the IndyCar Doubleheader', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Honda Motor Co Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fort Worth (Tex)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:06:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Caldwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caldwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf401e2f-6f48-547d-9ffb-2562945de886', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf401e2f-6f48-547d-9ffb-2562945de886'}, {'abstract': 'A dispute over the planned use of nonunion workers is resolved.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/giant-rat-will-miss-out-on-tonys-red-carpet-after-deal-is-reached-with-stagehands/', 'snippet': 'A dispute over the planned use of nonunion workers is resolved.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Broadway stagehands’ union will not have a picket line by the red carpet at the Tony Awards on Sunday evening, after reaching an agreement on Friday morning with the managers of the awards festivities to allow union workers to stage the carpet, according to representatives of both sides. The union and the Tony ceremony managers had been in a public standoff over the last 36 hours, with the clock ticking to set up the red carpet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Giant Rat Will Miss Tonys’ Red Carpet After Deal Is Reached With Stagehands', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beacon Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper West Side (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:07:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/401f19e8-b602-5898-9cd8-e3e8d4e2f90d', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/401f19e8-b602-5898-9cd8-e3e8d4e2f90d'}, {'abstract': 'As ever, Germany’s tabloids have more intriguing headlines than normal news magazines. Where Der Spiegel’s cover is “The Enemy in Eating,” Bild’s screams “Watch out for Communion!” How could I not read on?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/in-germany-watch-out-for-communion/', 'snippet': 'As ever, Germany’s tabloids have more intriguing headlines than normal news magazines. Where Der Spiegel’s cover is “The Enemy in Eating,” Bild’s screams “Watch out for Communion!” How could I not read on?', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the swine-flu crisis of 2009, I was living in Germany. I remember bicycling to work down Friedrichstrasse, one of Berlin’s main arteries, passing all the newsstands lined up outside convenience stores. The headlines of Bild, Germany’s best-read and most hysterical tabloid, were printed in such large type that I could take in the day’s news as I biked by. Often I would see one rather ugly word: SCHWEINEGRIPPE!!!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Germany, ‘Watch Out for Communion!’', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E Coli (Bacteria)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swine Influenza', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:11:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nolan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nolan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ab0c021-f011-5e55-bcc0-36f4c29e3ecb', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ab0c021-f011-5e55-bcc0-36f4c29e3ecb'}, {'abstract': 'Should there be a cutoff for when a rain-delayed baseball game can start, say by 9 p.m.?', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/call-it-a-night-or-wait-out-the-rain/', 'snippet': 'Should there be a cutoff for when a rain-delayed baseball game can start, say by 9 p.m.?', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Ben Shpigel reported in “Fans at Stadium Who Waited Had Plenty of Time to Dry Off,” the Yankees expected C.C. Sabathia to throw his first pitch at Yankee Stadium on Thursday night two hours before he eventually did, at 10:32 p.m.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Call It a Night, or Wait Out the Rain?', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Red Sox', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yankee Stadium (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:13:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Plutnicki', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Plutnicki', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72e8e2df-d106-55e9-9084-8e89a5936181', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72e8e2df-d106-55e9-9084-8e89a5936181'}, {'abstract': 'An artists’ colony, an Arab-Israeli town, a hike in the forest and some great food.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/12/travel/20110612-next.html', 'snippet': 'An artists’ colony, an Arab-Israeli town, a hike in the forest and some great food.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An artists’ colony, an Arab-Israeli town, a hike in the forest and some great food.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/travel/12next-slide8/12next-slide8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/travel/12next-slide8/12next-slide8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/10/travel/12next-slide8/12next-slide8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Out and About in Jerusalem', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jerusalem (Israel)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:14:47+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/c25cc657-263a-558d-9bce-639622977a56', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/c25cc657-263a-558d-9bce-639622977a56'}, {'abstract': 'Changing the rules on the fly to support tight money.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/monetary-calvinball/', 'snippet': 'Changing the rules on the fly to support tight money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was trying to come up with a way to describe Raghuram Rajan’s latest on why interest rates should go up despite high unemployment and quiescent inflation — but Mike Konczal saved me the trouble.. As Mike says, it’s Calvinball — making up new rules on the fly to justify whatever you, for some reason, want. And today’s column was about that reason.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monetary Calvinball', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:17:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c50d0d8-7a02-5d37-970b-235184dd97b5', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c50d0d8-7a02-5d37-970b-235184dd97b5'}, {'abstract': 'As food shortages loom, the governments of the developed world have acknowledged a need to invest anew in global agricultural development. Yet they have been slow to fulfill billions of dollars in pledges.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/world-food-supply-whats-to-be-done/', 'snippet': 'As food shortages loom, the governments of the developed world have acknowledged a need to invest anew in global agricultural development. Yet they have been slow to fulfill billions of dollars in pledges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a long article on Sunday and a string of posts on the Green blog this week, The Times has given readers a large body of reporting and analysis on the global food situation, including the risk to the food supply from climate change. I would like to end that run of coverage by pointing concerned readers toward the best thinking out there that tries to answer this question:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'World Food Supply: What’s To Be Done?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 1,