n': 'Despite Crackdown, New Video Emerges of Defiant Protesters in Syria', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:40:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c3b52c7-0c0a-5318-89c5-c48a5d831df7', 'word_count': 564, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c3b52c7-0c0a-5318-89c5-c48a5d831df7'}, {'abstract': 'Bernstein’s Brad Hintz, in a report titled “Is The Firm Worth More Dead or Alive,” values the various pieces of Morgan Stanley.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/breaking-up-morgan-stanley/', 'snippet': 'Bernstein’s Brad Hintz, in a report titled “Is The Firm Worth More Dead or Alive,” values the various pieces of Morgan Stanley.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Analyst Brad Hintz has a solution for Morgan Stanley’s flailing stock price: Send the company to the chop shop.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Breaking Up Morgan Stanley', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:42:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susanne Craig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Craig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6032cb2-6e79-5cff-9c12-726857fd3eab', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6032cb2-6e79-5cff-9c12-726857fd3eab'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, a 33-year-old bureaucrat with his own problems sets out to reveal a nation of fake smiles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/book-review-my-american-unhappiness-by-dean-bakopoulos.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, a 33-year-old bureaucrat with his own problems sets out to reveal a nation of fake smiles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to think of a less privileged creation than the second novel. When we admire a writer’s debut, it’s like falling in love across a crowded room; we are seduced by a part that we have mistaken for the whole. And when the follow-up reveals, as it must, a more complex picture than we at first believed we saw, our feelings are mixed. And so Dean Bakopoulos’s second novel, “My American Unhappiness,” is already at a disadvantage; if you liked his first one, you can be certain only that this one won’t be the same.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Lennon-sub/Lennon-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 905, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Lennon-sub/Lennon-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Lennon-sub/Lennon-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Unhappiness Project', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Red, White and Blah', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bakopoulos, Dean', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Happiness', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T16:43:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. Robert Lennon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'Robert', 'lastname': 'Lennon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ed59fc8-398f-51a3-89c4-47f224b9f649', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ed59fc8-398f-51a3-89c4-47f224b9f649'}, {'abstract': 'Katherine M. Porter, a University of Iowa law professor, said that Treasury’s crackdown on big banks for their mishandling of mortgage modifications is an important step but that the program remains fundamentally flawed.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/a-critics-take-on-the-mortgage-modification-program/', 'snippet': 'Katherine M. Porter, a University of Iowa law professor, said that Treasury’s crackdown on big banks for their mishandling of mortgage modifications is an important step but that the program remains fundamentally flawed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Treasury Department announced penalties on Thursday against three of the nation’s largest banks for what it said was poor performance in the government’s main foreclosure prevention program.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Critic’s Take on the Mortgage Modification Program', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 