height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/automobiles/LIST/LIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '16 Automobiles Worth a Spot in a Museum', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '16 Automobiles Worth a Spot In a Museum', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antique and Classic Cars', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Portland Art Museum', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Portland (Ore)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:14:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f298420-1fd4-55ec-b982-e6954c0cad7b', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f298420-1fd4-55ec-b982-e6954c0cad7b'}, {'abstract': 'John Sayles’s novelistic reimagining of America at the turn of the last century nods to both Harriet Beecher Stowe and Thomas Pynchon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/book-review-a-moment-in-the-sun-by-john-sayles.html', 'snippet': 'John Sayles’s novelistic reimagining of America at the turn of the last century nods to both Harriet Beecher Stowe and Thomas Pynchon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Near the end of his vast new historical novel, “A Moment in the Sun,” John Sayles describes a street performer who “built an elaborate house of tiles on his little table, balancing one upon the other till the structure was almost up to his chin.” Spectators bet against “the master architect” and his precarious creation, but he keeps adding tiles and winning wagers. This performer is, of course, a stand-in for Sayles himself, who has also built an elaborate house of tiles and managed, against all odds, to keep it standing. The book opens in 1897, the year of the Klondike gold rush, and closes in 1903, the year after the Philippine-American War ended, and in between it takes the measure of America on the brink of the 20th century.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/LeClair/LeClair-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 962, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/LeClair/LeClair-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/LeClair/LeClair-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Novel as Big as America', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'America Unbound', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sayles, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:14:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom LeClair', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LeClair', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ff08744-dd03-585d-8839-37a46568081c', 'word_count': 1273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ff08744-dd03-585d-8839-37a46568081c'}, {'abstract': 'The annual Drunken Dragon Festival, a no-holds-barred celebration created by the performer Calamity Chang, attracted a cast of hundreds costumed in either 1940s colonial or Chinese attire.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/notes-from-the-underground-macao-madness/', 'snippet': 'The annual Drunken Dragon Festival, a no-holds-barred celebration created by the performer Calamity Chang, attracted a cast of hundreds costumed in either 1940s colonial or Chinese attire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The word on the street is that no one ever leaves Macao Trading Co. sober. The restaurant cum lounge is a haven for all manner of cool kids and gourmet-food chasers, and the management loves to get the customers sauced and smiling. As a no-holds-barred celebration of this mentality, the lounge, in collaboration with a performer who goes by the name Calamity Chang, hosts an annual Drunken Dragon Festival, where for five hours a completely free open bar and passed hors d’oeuvres menu attract a cast of hundreds costumed in either 1940s colonial or Chinese attire. At the most recent incarnation of the event, several burlesque performers took over the main room, which was packed with revelers and covered in a blanket of confetti. And yes, the snake is real. A smaller version of the madness, Drunken Dragon Nights, is held weekly, downstairs at Macao, and features live burlesque performances. Go to\xa0macaonyc.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes From the Underground | Macao Madness', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:15:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By IO Tillett Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Io', 'middlename': 'Tillett', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc5a69c0-972c-5fb7-b67b-f3858232e4ab', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc5a69c0-972c-5fb7-b67b-f3858232e4ab'}, {'abstract': 'A memoir of how Jane Austen’s novels transformed one reader’s life, and a study of why we still read the “Lady novelist.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/book-review-a-jane-austen-education-and-why-jane-austen.html', 'snippet': 'A memoir of how Jane Austen’s novels transformed one reader’s life, and a study of why we still read the “Lady novelist.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1990, William Deresiewicz was on his way to gaining a Ph.D. in English literature at Columbia University. Describing that time in the opening pages of his sharp, endearingly self-effacing new book, “A Jane Austen Education,” Deresiewicz explains that he faced one crucial obstacle. He loathed not just Jane Austen but the entire gang of 19th-century British novelists: Hardy, Dickens, Eliot . . . the lot.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Seymour/Seymour-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 894, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Seymour/Seymour-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Seymour/Seymour-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lessons From Jane Austen', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jane’s Fame', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austen, Jane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deresiewicz, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:15:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miranda Seymour', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miranda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seymour', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/13cbb931-2e7f-5b6c-b638-b86351dbc9d6', 'word_count': 1227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13cbb931-2e7f-5b6c-b638-b86351dbc9d6'}, {'abstract': 'How a long-lost Latin manuscript became a Nazi talisman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/book-review-a-most-dangerous-book-by-christopher-b-krebs.html', 'snippet': 'How a long-lost Latin manuscript became a Nazi talisman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why is there a Germany? Part of the answer goes back to a battle fought in A.D. 9 in the treacherous marshes and dense thickets of Teutoburg Forest, near modern Osnabrück. As described by the Roman historian Tacitus, three Roman legions led by Quinctilius Varus had crossed the Rhine from Gaul, intent on incorporating the vast area known as Germania into the empire. They were ambushed and annihilated by German tribes under the command of a warrior named Arminius. It was one of the worst military disasters the Romans ever suffered. Some years ago, archaeologists discovered the site of the battle; the debris field was a mile wide and 15 miles long. Caesar Augustus, it is said, roamed the hallways of his palace, crying, “Quinctilius Varus, give me back my legions!” Henceforth, the Romans pretty much wrote off the area east of the Rhine and north of the Alps. The line of demarcation survives to this day as a cultural divide — beer versus wine, butter versus oil, Germanic versus Romance. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Sub-Murphy/Sub-Murphy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Sub-Murphy/Sub-Murphy-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/Sub-Murphy/Sub-Murphy-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Idea of Germany, From Tacitus to Hitler', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Birth of a Nation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roman Civilization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:15:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cullen Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cullen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/14bfb07b-4c23-5001-ba55-ba38ede62044', 'word_count': 846, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14bfb07b-4c23-5001-ba55-ba38ede62044'}, {'abstract': 'A young writer overcomes her self-destructive behavior with the help of a gifted therapist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/book-review-your-voice-in-my-head-a-memoir-by-emma-forrest.html', 'snippet': 'A young writer overcomes her self-destructive behavior with the help of a gifted therapist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Emma Forrest’s memoir, “Your Voice in My Head,” is part of a literary tradition that began long before Susanna Kaysen’s girlhood was interrupted or Elizabeth Wurtzel got her first Prozac prescription. Indeed, on her book’s first page Forrest invokes the patron saint of mentally ill beauties with bad taste in men, as she describes how, at 13, she began making regular pilgrimages after school to the Tate in London, to visit Millais’s portrait of the drowned Ophelia. “I believe that she infected me,” she writes. “I was afraid . . . that I saw in her my own destiny.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/GOULD/GOULD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/GOULD/GOULD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/GOULD/GOULD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Memoir of Self-Destruction and Therapy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Unkindest Cut', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:16:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Gould', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gould', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/658e1918-d9c1-589c-ade9-b102f7af8b4a', 'word_count': 958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/658e1918-d9c1-589c-ade9-b102f7af8b4a'}, {'abstract': 'How an American ambassador to the Third Reich, and his daughter, gradually realized what a mess they were in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/book-review-in-the-garden-of-beasts-by-erik-larson.html', 'snippet': 'How an American ambassador to the Third Reich, and his daughter, gradually realized what a mess they were in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'William E. Dodd was an academic historian, living a quiet life in Chicago, when Franklin D. Roosevelt appointed him United States ambassador to Germany. It was 1933, Hitler had recently been appointed chancellor, the world was about to change.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sleeping With the Gestapo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sleeping With the Gestapo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larson, Erik', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:16:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dorothy Gallagher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dorothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gallagher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5804ef2-97e0-5b21-b5d7-71ef2a7dffa0', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5804ef2-97e0-5b21-b5d7-71ef2a7dffa0'}, {'abstract': 'In J. Courtney Sullivan’s novel, three generations of a family’s women take guilt, secrets and old wounds on a beach retreat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/book-review-maine-by-j-courtney-sullivan.html', 'snippet': 'In J. Courtney Sullivan’s novel, three generations of a family’s women take guilt, secrets and old wounds on a beach retreat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ah, family. Isn’t it satisfying to leave your own briefly behind to drop in on another — and see how thoroughly they bungle it all up? This is the pleasure of “Maine,” J. Courtney Sullivan’s second novel, which delves into the secrets and simmering emotions of one dysfunctional family over the course of a single summer month.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/KING/KING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/KING/KING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/books/review/KING/KING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beach Retreat, Baggage Included', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Permanent Waves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Love (Emotion)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, J Courtney', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:17:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily King', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'King', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/206ba3a5-5a44-58f0-b729-500627907c74', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/206ba3a5-5a44-58f0-b729-500627907c74'}, {'abstract': 'Boxes filled with more than 24,000 printed pages of e-mails that Sarah Palin sent as governor of Alaska were released in Juneau, the state capital.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/alaska-releases-palin-e-mails/', 'snippet': 'Boxes filled with more than 24,000 printed pages of e-mails that Sarah Palin sent as governor of Alaska were released in Juneau, the state capital.', 'lead_paragraph': '[live_updates]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Alaska Releases Palin E-Mails', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALASKA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:20:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title':