tographers and video artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ulm, a small German city on the Danube, entered the contemporary photography scene with last year’s opening of the Walther Collection, a small museum founded by Artur Walther, a native of Ulm and former Wall Street banker. Mr. Walther’s collection of about 1,100 works from the late 19th to the early 21st centuries is noteworthy for its Chinese and African photographs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Appropriated Landscapes’ in Ulm', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Epstein, Mitch', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldblatt, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:32:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daphné Anglès', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daphné', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anglès', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74689e05-4998-50e8-99d6-2cd718012d1b', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74689e05-4998-50e8-99d6-2cd718012d1b'}, {'abstract': 'There is something of this melancholy in Dana Spiotta’s account in the magazine of her journey on the Erie Canal.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/on-the-erie-canal/', 'snippet': 'There is something of this melancholy in Dana Spiotta’s account in the magazine of her journey on the Erie Canal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dana Spiotta joins Sebastião Salgado and J. J. Sullivan in this week’s issue.\xa0In her review of Spiotta’s novel “Eat the Document,” about two radicals on the lam, Michiko Kakutani credited her with erecting “an elliptical narrative filled with musical leitmotifs and searing, strobe-lighted images of contemporary life — a narrative that immerses us, headfirst, in the chaos and incongruities of the American scene while goading us into a melancholy contemplation of the country’s penchant for discarding the past.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Erie Canal', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Canals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spiotta, Dana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:32:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Glaser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glaser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1f17b20-ce43-5931-b0dd-e5cff55fa41e', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1f17b20-ce43-5931-b0dd-e5cff55fa41e'}, {'abstract': 'More talk about what you lose and gain by having children.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/a-father-with-regrets/', 'snippet': 'More talk about what you lose and gain by having children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Friday, Steph asked to hear from parents who regretted having children. Most of you assured her that parents rarely regret the children they have, but sprinkled amid the many comments were a few that were starkly sad. Most of those were from mothers, and they were about the added price women seem to pay — in sleep, income, ambition, time, status — when they have children. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Father With Regrets', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:33:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fe82558-abf7-50f4-acad-4630da8ccf87', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fe82558-abf7-50f4-acad-4630da8ccf87'}, {'abstract': 'The Fiat 500 is cute enough, but now that it also comes as a convertible, it’s too precious. The 500C is big on style and fuel economy, but don’t expect much acceleration or rear visibility.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/automobiles/autoreviews/12BLOCK.html', 'snippet': 'The Fiat 500 is cute enough, but now that it also comes as a convertible, it’s too precious. The 500C is big on style and fuel economy, but don’t expect much acceleration or rear visibility.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TESTED 2012 Fiat 500C ', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/automobiles/BLOC/BLOC-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 265, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/12/automobiles/BLOC/BLOC-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 265}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/automobiles/BLOC/BLOC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/automobiles/BLOC/BLOC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/automobiles/BLOC/BLOC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Still Cute, but Now Sunny as Well', 'kicker': 'Around the Block', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Cute, but Now Sunny as Well', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiat SpA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Convertibles (Autos)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Cars (Compact, Subcompact and Microcars)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:37:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'subsection_name': 'New Cars', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Kurczewski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurczewski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1867ab7b-6d10-5793-8525-0f877f17e2df', 'word_count': 730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1867ab7b-6d10-5793-8525-0f877f17e2df'}, {'abstract': 'ELEGANCE seemed to go viral in the photographs of George Hoyningen-Huene.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/far-beyond-the-clothes/', 'snippet': 'ELEGANCE seemed to go viral in the photographs of George Hoyningen-Huene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elegance seemed to go viral in the photographs of George Hoyningen-Huene. A Russian émigré in Paris in the 1920s, and a baron at that, Huene produced an exquisite quality of longing — or was it remoteness? — in the pages of French Vogue, where he was chief photographer in the most glamorous of 20th-century decades. You might say that Huene (pronounced HEW-neh) sealed the deal between modern-thinking couturiers and photographers, seducing us with light and classical forms. Afterward, nothing seemed the same. In her review of a 2001 exhibition that brought together Huene and his great friend Horst, the critic Vicki Goldberg noted “the two H’s influenced almost everyone else.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Far Beyond the Clothes', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:39:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16789978-2abe-59e7-9401-9f2305d9fec0', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16789978-2abe-59e7-9401-9f2305d9fec0'}, {'abstract': 'This week: Dorothy Gallagher on Erik Larson’s new best-seller, “In the Garden of Beasts”; Emily Gould discusses “Your Voice in My Head,” the new memoir by Emma Forrest; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; and Jennifer Schuessler has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2011/06/10/books/review/10bookreview_pod.html', 'snippet': 'This week: Dorothy Gallagher on Erik Larson’s new best-seller, “In the Garden of Beasts”; Emily Gould discusses “Your Voice in My Head,” the new memoir by Emma Forrest; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; and Jennifer Schuessler has best-seller...', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week: Dorothy Gallagher on Erik Larson’s new best-seller, “In the Garden of Beasts”; Emily Gould discusses “Your Voice in My Head,” the new memoir by Emma Forrest; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; and Jennifer Schuessler has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Erik Larson’s new best-seller; Emily Gould on “Your Voice in My Head,” the new memoir by Emma Forrest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Podcasts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:40:31+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/9eba6539-9f4d-5a90-b59e-43019b9ed129', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/9eba6539-9f4d-5a90-b59e-43019b9ed129'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A GREAT UNRECORDED HISTORY: A New Life of E. M. Forster,  by Wendy Moffat (Picador, $20.) Between 1905 and 1924, E. M. Forster (1879-1970) published five novels, including “Howards End” and “A Passage to India.” Moffat’s perceptive biography explores the 46 years of relative silence that followed, drawing a picture of a sensitive, influential novelist whose homosexuality explained much, but certainly not all, of his mystery.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:42:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/aea04588-69c6-56f3-a274-e1ad6738c527', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aea04588-69c6-56f3-a274-e1ad6738c527'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/books/review/EdChoice-t.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SISTER,  by Rosamund Lupton (Crown, $24.) In Lupton’s novel, which is both tear-jerking and spine-tingling, a free-spirited sister’s death — perhaps it was murder — forces a highly conventional woman to examine the truth of their relationship.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:42:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/e81a94d8-98ea-503a-898e-5d582c837f3c', 'word_count': 271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e81a94d8-98ea-503a-898e-5d582c837f3c'}, {'abstract': 'David Eagleman, who hits the hardcover nonfiction list this week with “Incognito,” is the kind of guy who really does make being a neuroscientist look like fun.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'David Eagleman, who hits the hardcover nonfiction list this week with “Incognito,” is the kind of guy who really does make being a neuroscientist look like fun.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TIME TRAVELER: David Eagleman, who hits the hardcover nonfiction list this week at No. 14 with “Incognito: The Secret Lives of the Brain,” is the kind of guy who really does make being a neuroscientist look like fun. His experiments tend to involve things like dropping people off amusement park thrill rides (to measure the way time seems to slow down during near-death experiences) and hanging out in the studio with Brian Eno and the drummer from Coldplay (to see how professional timekeepers stay precisely on the beat). He wears hip ankle boots and designer jeans while dashing between TED talks and his lab at Baylor. In his spare time, Eagleman has even written an acclaimed collection of speculative short stories, “Sum: Forty Tales From the Afterlives,” that will be staged as a full opera in London next year. He’s also getting some traction with his efforts to start a new quasi-religious movement called Possibilianism, which aims to move beyond the dueling certitudes of traditional religion and atheism to explore radical what-ifs about the universe and human consciousness. One story in “Sum,” for example, considers the possibility that life on earth may be just a brief, pleasurable vacation from our true existence as gigantic beings doing the heavy lifting of holding up the cosmos. (“If you are thinking of dying,” Alexander McCall Smith wrote in the Book Review in 2009, “this book may not exactly increase your peace of mind.”)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/19insidelist/19insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/19insidelist/19insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:42:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3164951-133a-5d91-aa8d-0bb94f1db84f', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3164951-133a-5d91-aa8d-0bb94f1db84f'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column deals with the issue of negative mentions on the Web and offers some suggestions on how to combat them.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/cleaning-up-an-online-reputation/', 'snippet': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column deals with the issue of negative mentions on the Web and offers some suggestions on how to combat them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Sullivan, in his Wealth Matters column this week, takes note of the women who received Representative Anthony Weiner’s texts to make the point that their names will be forever linked to his in any online search. Yes, this is a high-profile example, but Paul says that all sorts of people can be negatively branded on the Web, even if they did nothing wrong.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cleaning Up an Online Reputation', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libel and Slander', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Paul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:44:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bucks Editors', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bucks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Editors', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51bd6beb-582d-5e39-b384-b2bf16e86ce0', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51bd6beb-582d-5e39-b384-b2bf16e86ce0'}, {'abstract': 'Michael D. Shear on the next steps for the Newt Gingrich campaign. Also, Jim Rutenberg previews the release of thousands of pages of Sarah Palin’s old e-mails.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/10/video-gingrich-campaign-implodes-palin-e-mails-released/', 'snippet': 'Michael D. Shear on the next steps for the Newt Gingrich campaign. Also, Jim Rutenberg previews the release of thousands of pages of Sarah Palin’s old e-mails.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael D. Shear on the next steps for the Newt Gingrich campaign. Also, Jim Rutenberg previews the release of thousands of pages of Sarah Palin’s old e-mails. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: Gingrich Campaign Implodes; Palin E-Mails Released', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Mail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T17:49:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben W