nly nascently hip.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/realestate/perpendicular-to-brunch-block-by-block.html', 'snippet': 'Grand Street is in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, but it feels like somewhere else — somewhere quieter and only nascently hip.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON a recent Sunday, Williamsburg, Brooklyn, was bustling at midday. You couldn’t move on the main drag of Bedford Avenue, between the people milling, the sidewalk vendors selling, and the groups jockeying for position in the sport of brunch. Day-trippers from Manhattan were headed for Smorgasburg, the food extension of the Brooklyn Flea; a line had formed outside Blue Bottle Coffee over on Berry Street; and the sign-up sheet at Egg, the little breakfast place on North Fifth Street, was oversubscribed. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/realestate/block-1/block-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 510, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/10/realestate/block-1/block-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/10/realestate/block-1/block-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Perpendicular to Brunch', 'kicker': 'Block by Block | Grand Street, Williamsburg', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Perpendicular to Brunch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Williamsburg (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-10T18:03:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christian L. Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christian', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49aed244-8223-5179-9277-f03c3ff5d142', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49aed244-8223-5179-9277-f03c3ff5d142'}, {'abstract': 'New games including Battlefield 3, Fruit Ninja Kinect and Rage were among those previewed at this year’s E3 convention in Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/11/arts/video-games/new-video-games-at-the-e3-convention.html', 'snippet': 'New games including Battlefield 3, Fruit Ninja Kinect and Rage were among those previewed at this year’s E3 convention in Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — For anyone involved with the video game industry, surviving the E3 convention is an annual rite of passage. Many of the tens of thousands of developers, journalists and PR people here find themselves attending up to 16 hours of meetings each day for four or even five days straight, giving the whole thing the feeling of summer camp meets the Bataan Death March. In a good way. After all, we’re talking about video games.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/11/arts/E3-1/E3-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/11/arts/E3-1/E3-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/11/arts/E3-1/E3-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/11/arts/E3-1/E3-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'heigh