nomic growth, government budget surpluses or deficits and outstanding national debt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The First Shall Be Last: Recovering From a Financial Crisis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-11T03:27:10+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/ae071e42-d452-539e-b276-6660d910083e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/ae071e42-d452-539e-b276-6660d910083e'}, {'abstract': 'The sometimes shaky Robert Luongo recorded a shutout and the offense, while still largely anemic, ended a five-period-plus scoring drought.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/11/sports/hockey/luongo-and-canucks-turn-the-tide-in-game-5.html', 'snippet': 'The sometimes shaky Robert Luongo recorded a shutout and the offense, while still largely anemic, ended a five-period-plus scoring drought.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VANCOUVER, British Columbia — After four games and another two periods on Friday night, the hometown Canucks and the Boston Bruins entered the final period here deadlocked, two wins against two wins, 0-0 in Game 5. With two goaltenders near perfect and two offenses far from it, the final 20 minutes promised to shift these Stanley Cup finals in one direction.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/11/sports/NHL/NHL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 824, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/11/sports/NHL/NHL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75,