': 'By William C. Rhoden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Rhoden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a5f0e3c-548a-5c7e-b51c-38d2ba670ce1', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a5f0e3c-548a-5c7e-b51c-38d2ba670ce1'}, {'abstract': 'People have long brought home emotions they can’t express at work. But now home life, with all its emotional currents, is inextricably woven into the workplace.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/jobs/12pre.html', 'snippet': 'People have long brought home emotions they can’t express at work. But now home life, with all its emotional currents, is inextricably woven into the workplace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN I graduated from college in 1977, the world was still neatly divided into two spheres: work and everything else. Work was supposed to be a hyperrational realm of logic, filled with timetables, organizational charts and returns on investment. It was only outside of work that emotions — so dangerously ill-defined and unpredictable — were supposed to emerge.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/business/PRE/PRE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking Your Feelings to Work', 'kicker': 'Preoccupations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking Your Feelings To Work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Work-Life Balance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emotions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-11T15:51:37+0000', 'document_type': 'ar