'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f65d05c-13a3-5f09-b045-f7161fb10a91', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f65d05c-13a3-5f09-b045-f7161fb10a91'}, {'abstract': 'A few big, smart, bipartisan decisions by Congress on taxes and spending (and mortgages) could get the economy to start improving again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/opinion/12friedman.html', 'snippet': 'A few big, smart, bipartisan decisions by Congress on taxes and spending (and mortgages) could get the economy to start improving again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you want to understand why the unemployment rate has been stubbornly lodged around 9 percent, a good place to start is with the eye-popping mortgage statistics released last week by the economic analysis firm CoreLogic: 38 percent of homeowners with second mortgages are underwater. They borrowed against the value of their homes, and they now owe more than their houses are worth. The total number of underwater homeowners in America, with first and second mortgages, is a stunning 22.7 percent. In Nevada alone, 63 percent of all mortgaged properties are worth less than the owners paid; in Arizona 50 percent, Florida 46 percent, Michigan 36 percent and California 31 percent.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Uncertainty Tax', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Uncertainty Tax', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-11T20:08:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas L. Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/022b9811-5dd3-57b1-9ddc-28750b64e195', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/022b9811-5dd3-57b1-9ddc-28750b64e195'}, {'abstract': 'A rare “golf summit” with the House speaker next weekend is serious political business. Usually the president’s game is anything but.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/weekinreview/12golf.html', 'snippet': 'A rare “golf summit” with the House speaker next weekend is serious political business. Usually the president’s game is anything but.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MIAMI — On the first tee, President Lyndon B. Johnson often warned his golfing partners, “One lesson you’d better learn if you want to be in politics is you never get out on a golf course and beat the president.”', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/weekinreview/NATTA2/NATTA2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 692, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/weekinreview/NATTA2/NATTA2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/12/weekinreview/NATTA2/NATTA2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Handicapping the Obama-Boehner ‘Golf Summit’', 'kicker': 'Games', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama’s Different Strokes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-11T20:08:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Don Van Natta Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Don', 'middlename': 'Van', 'lastname': 'Natta', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a62b6caf-ba87-5d93-8f53-1b911aaa49cd', 'word_count': 1056, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a62b6caf-ba87-5d93-8f53-1b911aaa49cd'}, {'abstract': 'As he begins his new job heading Chicago Public Schools, Jean-Claude Brizard, a former principal from Rochester, N.Y., has his work cut out for him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/us/12cncbrizard.html', 'snippet': 'As he begins his new job heading Chicago Public Schools, Jean-Claude Brizard, a former principal from Rochester, N.Y., has his work cut out for him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With three meetings already under his belt by 10 a.m. on the Monday of his second full week on the job, Jean-Claude Brizard, chief executive of Chicago Public Schools, rode in his city-issued Ford Crown Victoria to visit Northside College Prep High School. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '35B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/12/us/12CNCBRIZARD1/12CNCBRIZARD1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Work Cut Out for Him, a Schools Chief Dives In', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Work Cut Out for Him, A Schools Chief Dives In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brizard, Jean-Claude', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Principals (School)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-11T20:09:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Vevea', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vevea', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a95b8ad-89ea-5824-91bc-6eaf31b9dc14', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a95b8ad-89ea-5824-91bc-6eaf31b9dc14'}, {'abstract': 'UBS finds the suburbs too sterile.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/11/agglomeration-wins/', 'snippet': 'UBS finds the suburbs too sterile.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not about the usual: this article in the Times, Regretting Move, Bank May Return to Manhattan is up my old alley — the stuff I got the Swedish thingie for. And it should make Ed Glaeser happy. Together with Jess Gaspar, Ed wrote a paper 15 years ago arguing that contrary to what many people were saying, the Internet and all that wouldn’t undermine the agglomeration forces that support places like Manhattan; if anything, agglomeration would increase. Actually, I wrote about that back in my “looking backward from the year 2096” piece, written about the same time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Agglomeration Wins', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-11T20:10:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12bd17f0-a731-5c85-ab87-342d34c540e5', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12bd17f0-a731-5c85-ab87-342d34c540e5'}, {'abstract': 'Readers react to articles published in The Times this past week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/sports/in-box-still-standing-behind-tressel-at-ohio-state.html', 'snippet': 'Readers react to articles published in The Times this past week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Sports Editor:', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters to the Editor', 'kicker': 'In-Box', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Standing Behind Tressel at Ohio State', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ohio State University', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tressel, Jim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-11T20:10:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0a18a50-91c0-50eb-be4e-b183638b9881', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0a18a50-91c0-50eb-be4e-b183638b9881'}, {'abstract': 'As we keep feeding our livestock antibiotics, we are creating a breeding ground for bacteria that can lead to untreatable ailments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/opinion/12kristof.html', 'snippet': 'As we keep feeding our livestock antibiotics, we are creating a breeding ground for bacteria that can lead to untreatable ailments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The deaths of 31 people in Europe from a little-known strain of E. coli have raised alarms worldwide, but we shouldn’t be surprised. Our food often betrays us. ', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-article