review.html', 'snippet': 'American Ballet Theater, with brilliant help from Bolshoi guests, perform Ratmansky’s “Bright Stream” at the Met Opera House.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To call Alexei Ratmansky, some of whose works have their obvious flaws, the most fascinating choreographer alive is unfair to such illustrious and older choreographers as Paul Taylor, Twyla Tharp, Mark Morris and Matthew Bourne, each of whom has made several more provably enduring masterpieces. Yet it’s tempting to do so. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/arts/ABT/ABT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/arts/ABT/ABT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/13/arts/ABT/ABT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Peasants Having Fun Down on the Old Soviet Farm', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Peasants Having Fun Down on the Old Soviet Farm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bolshoi Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ratmansky, Alexei', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T21:59:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e74da870-4006-5512-a488-c56a7f6d2895', 'word_count': 1085, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e74da870-4006-5512-a488-c56a7f6d2895'}, {'abstract': 'The conservative party of Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan won with a strong showing that critics fear will be used to further consolidate its power.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/world/europe/13turkey.html', 'snippet': 'The conservative party of Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan won with a strong showing that critics fear will be used to further consolidate its power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISTANBUL — The conservative party of Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan won a clear victory in parliamentary elections on Sunday with a strong showing that critics worry might be used to further consolidate its power after nearly a decade of rule and to circumscribe civil liberties and its political opposition. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/world/TURKEY/TURKEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/13/world/TURKEY/TURKEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/world/TURKEY/TURKEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/world/TURKEY/TURKEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/13/world/TURKEY/TURKEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Erdogan’s Party Wins Third Term in Turkish Elections', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Erdogan’s Party Wins Third Term in Turkish Elections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erdogan, Recep Tayyip', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice and Development Party (Turkey)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T21:59:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sebnem Arsu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebnem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b099dcc7-8e59-5cf9-817b-ae4155c4737c', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b099dcc7-8e59-5cf9-817b-ae4155c4737c'}, {'abstract': 'A debut and a graduation gift for Alex Vratsanos.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/earned/', 'snippet': 'A debut and a graduation gift for Alex Vratsanos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — Right. Let’s all get it out of our systems now: Will Shortz likes to play table tennis. This puzzle is about table tennis. Ergo (from the Latin, meaning “I know some Latin and I like to show it off”), this constructor must have been published because he appealed to Mr. Shortz’s love of the pastime. Makes sense, right?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: How Much You Really Earned', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90d07d8c-25b3-5206-979a-96958efc6c24', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90d07d8c-25b3-5206-979a-96958efc6c24'}, {'abstract': 'The singers Joan Morris and Robert White were accompanied by the composer and pianist William Bolcom in a program centered around World War II on Friday at the Metropolitan Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/arts/music/joan-morris-and-robert-white-at-met-museum-review.html', 'snippet': 'The singers Joan Morris and Robert White were accompanied by the composer and pianist William Bolcom in a program centered around World War II on Friday at the Metropolitan Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Someone Talked!” the World War II propaganda poster screams. A terrified young soldier, drowning, points his finger in accusation toward the viewer, with the implication that his friend’s or neighbor’s gossip about his ship’s location was overheard by a German spy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/arts/TALKED/TALKED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 641, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wartime Songs Keep Luster From Long Ago and Far Away', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wartime Songs Keep Luster From Long Ago and Far Away', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morris, Joan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bolcom, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Robert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:01:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a423dce-2b53-5c45-8c6e-156949306481', 'word_count': 477, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a423dce-2b53-5c45-8c6e-156949306481'}, {'abstract': '“Super 8” had a better-than-expected opening weekend gross of $37 million and the No. 1 spot at the North American box office.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/movies/super-8-surprises-viewers-and-the-box-office.html', 'snippet': '“Super 8” had a better-than-expected opening weekend gross of $37 million and the No. 1 spot at the North American box office.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A mix of science fiction suspense, coming-of-age drama and Spielbergian wonder proved to be a winning formula, giving “Super 8”  a better-than-expected opening weekend gross of $37 million and the No. 1 spot at the North American box office. The film, directed by J. J. Abrams, is one of the few summer releases that does not benefit from being a sequel or having a built-in fan base. In an effort to offset this handicap and spread some quick word of mouth Paramount Pictures held early screenings on Thursday and urged customers to tweet about the film. Private screenings were also held on Wednesday night for Twitter users with large numbers of followers. “X-Men: First Class” from 20th Century Fox held steady at second place with $25 million, dropping 55 percent from last weekend’s total, an above-average second-week showing for a summer blockbuster. That franchise reboot has accumulated a North American total of $98.9 million, according to Box Office Mojo, which tracks sales data. “The Hangover Part II” (Warner Brothers) was third with $18.5 million, proving the continued appeal of the misadventures of the Wolf Pack. In fourth place, “Kung Fu Panda 2” (DreamWorks Animation, via Paramount) piled on another $16.6 million. At fifth place with $10.8 million, “Pirates of the Caribbean: On Stranger Tides” (Walt Disney Studios) crossed the $200 million mark in North America. So far at this summer’s box office, ticket sales are up 13.5 percent compared with this stage in the season last year, but are still down 9 percent for the year so far.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Super 8’ Surprises Viewers and the Box Office', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Super 8’ Surprises Viewers and the Box Office', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:03:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bc3d2ab-422b-5159-af39-9a66107eaa19', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bc3d2ab-422b-5159-af39-9a66107eaa19'}, {'abstract': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center and the Paley Center for Media are teaming up to present one of the most complete Judy Garland retrospectives in recent memory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/movies/judy-garlands-career-in-2-retrospectives.html', 'snippet': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center and the Paley Center for Media are teaming up to present one of the most complete Judy Garland retrospectives in recent memory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center and the Paley Center for Media are teaming up to present one of the most complete Judy Garland retrospectives in recent memory. The film society’s All Singin’, All Dancin’, All Judy! will feature 31 titles from Garland’s career, including “A Star Is Born” and a sing-along screening of “The Wizard of Oz” (above). There will also be a presentation of short films and rarities hosted by John Fricke, a Garland historian and author of the forthcoming “Judy: A Legendary Film Career” (Running Press, 2011). The retrospective will run from July 26 to Aug. 9 at the Walter Reade Theater; the Paley Center’s “Judy Garland: The Television Years,” a comprehensive collection of highlights and performances from “The Judy Garland Show, “The Ed Sullivan Show” and “Tonight,” will run from July 20 to Aug. 18.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Judy Garland’s Career in 2 Retrospectives', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Judy Garland’s Career In 2 Retrospectives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garland, Judy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Film Society of Lincoln Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paley Center for Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:06:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/79e4d46a-22de-5995-8d9f-8653df30e2f6', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79e4d46a-22de-5995-8d9f-8653df30e2f6'}, {'abstract': 'In “Tangled Webs” James B. Stewart looks at four celebrated federal perjury cases emblematic of “a surge of concerted deliberate lying” by the affluent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/books/tangled-webs-by-james-b-stewart-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “Tangled Webs” James B. Stewart looks at four celebrated federal perjury cases emblematic of “a surge of concerted deliberate lying” by the affluent.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Tangled Webs” is James B. Stewart’s engrossing re-examination of a quartet of celebrated federal investigations, all of which culminated in convictions for lying: the insider-trading probe that ultimately ensnared the homemaking diva Martha Stewart; the complex inquiry to determine who leaked to reporters the identity of the former C.I.A. covert operative Valerie Plame Wilson, which led to the perjury conviction of the ex-vice-presidential chief of staff  I. Lewis Libby; the long-running San Francisco grand jury probe into steroid use by athletes that implicated the sprinter Marion Jones and (after the book was finished) the home-run king Barry Bonds; and the Securities and Exchange Commission inquiries in which the reigning king of swindlers, Bernard L. Madoff, managed to gull overworked young investigators and keep his Ponzi scheme alive, prior to his ultimate undoing in 2008.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/arts/JPBOOK/JPBOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/arts/JPBOOK/JPBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/13/arts/JPBOOK/JPBOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking for Truth in All That Lying', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looking for Truth In All That Lying', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, James B', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:09:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Turow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Turow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dd6189f-41da-567b-a867-e729f7fbb6c8', 'word_count': 1225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dd6189f-41da-567b-a867-e729f7fbb6c8'}, {'abstract': 'The American Dance Festival paid tribute to Charles L. Reinhart’s 43-year reign and final season as director.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/arts/dance/american-dance-festival-charles-reinhart-tribute-review.html', 'snippet': 'The American Dance Festival paid tribute to Charles L. Reinhart’s 43-year reign and final season as director.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DURHAM, N.C. — “Modern dance is an ongoing revolution,” Charles L. Reinhart said in 1987. At that point Mr. Reinhart had run the American Dance Festival for almost 20 years; on Thursday night the festival celebrated his 43-year reign and final season as director with an opening-night gala at the Durham Performing Arts Center here. Mr. Reinhart was honored with an appropriately eccentric lineup of pieces, punctuated by brief tributes to his influence on the dance world.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/arts/SUBGALA/SUBGALA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 531, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Eccentric Sendoff for a Festival’s Leader', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Eccentric Sendoff For a Festival’s Leader', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reinhart, Charles L', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hubbard Street Dance Chicago', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMERICAN DANCE FESTIVAL', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dendy, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'African American Dance Ensemble', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:10:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/418c084f-9a04-5d1c-a361-647688a0360d', 'word_count': 600, 'uri': 'nyt://article/418c084f-9a04-5d1c-a361-647688a0360d'}, {'abstract': 'The Moss and Joel teams will play for the Venice Cup, and Schwartz and Kasle will try for the Senior Bowl.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/crosswords/bridge/venice-cup-senior-bowl-teams-chosen-bridge.html', 'snippet': 'The Moss and Joel teams will play for the Venice Cup, and Schwartz and Kasle will try for the Senior Bowl.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After play on Friday and Saturday in Detroit, four teams were selected to represent the United States in the Venice Cup and d’Orsi Senior Bowl world team championships in the Netherlands in October.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Senior Bowl Trip on the Line', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Senior Bowl Trip on the Line', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:13:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c782be4-2237-541d-9aa3-c7d750dcd6cc', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c782be4-2237-541d-9aa3-c7d750dcd6cc'}, {'abstract': 'Sarah Palin’s e-mails and Anthony Weiner’s Twitter exchanges are a logical next step in the evolving relationship of politics and media technologies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/business/media/13carr.html', 'snippet': 'Sarah Palin’s e-mails and Anthony Weiner’s Twitter exchanges are a logical next step in the evolving relationship of politics and media technologies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Richard Nixon did some terrible things in order to maintain a grip on the Oval Office, but the dumbest may have been recording what went on there. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/12/business/media/carr-50/carr-50-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/04/12/business/media/carr-50/carr-50-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Same Gaffes, but Now on Twitter', 'kicker': 'The Media Equation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Same Gaffes, But Now On Twitter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Mail', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:22:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7996a22-cd36-5ad9-b363-8f4525c52fe9', 'word_count': 997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7996a22-cd36-5ad9-b363-8f4525c52fe9'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Isherwood and Dave Itzkoff live-blogged the 65th annual Tony Awards.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/tony-awards-2011-live-blog/', 'snippet': 'Charles Isherwood and Dave Itzkoff live-blogged the 65th annual Tony Awards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tonys Night Live', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Book of Mormon’ and ‘War Horse’ Win Big at Tonys', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Broadway League', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:32:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c00e6703-9ed7-5c3f-b378-a831dc987b47', 'word_count': 5230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c00e6703-9ed7-5c3f-b378-a831dc987b47'}, {'abstract': 'A lapse in the last lap by Sebastian Vettel gives Jenson Button the win. Michael Schumacher drives his best race since his return.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/button-slips-by-vettel-in-montreal/', 'snippet': 'A lapse in the last lap by Sebastian Vettel gives Jenson Button the win. Michael Schumacher drives his best race since his return.', 'lead_paragraph': ' MONTREAL – It had to be seen to be believed. Jenson Button slipped past Sebastian Vettel in the last lap of the Canadian Grand Prix on Sunday to take the lead of the race that had been dominated by Vettel until that point.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Button Slips By Vettel in Montreal', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:55:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27c50a2b-6631-511f-b4de-442ce8288da0', 'word_count': 425, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27c50a2b-6631-511f-b4de-442ce8288da0'}, {'abstract': 'A lapse in the last lap by Sebastian Vettel gives Jenson Button the win. Michael Schumacher drives his best race since his return.', 'web_url': 'https://formulaone.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/button-slips-by-vettel-in-montreal/', 'snippet': 'A lapse in the last lap by Sebastian Vettel gives Jenson Button the win. Michael Schumacher drives his best race since his return.', 'lead_paragraph': ' MONTREAL – It had to be seen to be believed. Jenson Button slipped past Sebastian Vettel in the last lap of the Canadian Grand Prix on Sunday to take the lead of the race that had been dominated by Vettel until that point.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Button Slips By Vettel in Montreal', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T22:55:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c10e3da2-f8b8-57a9-86de-52f090cd2a9f', 'word_count': 425, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c10e3da2-f8b8-57a9-86de-52f090cd2a9f'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Birger was a Holocaust survivor and former official of the young state of Israel who turned the book fair into a thriving literary event.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/world/middleeast/13birger.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Birger was a Holocaust survivor and former official of the young state of Israel who turned the book fair into a thriving literary event.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — Zev Birger, an official in the young state of Israel who later revived and then led the Jerusalem International Book Fair, turning it into a major event on the literary calendar, died here on June 6 after being struck by a motorcycle. He was 85.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zev Birger, Jerusalem Book Fair Leader, Dies at 85', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Zev Birger, 85, Revived Jerusalem Book Fair', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jerusalem (Israel)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Fairs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T23:01:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a01cb50-26c7-54bb-ae3d-09e551bb8d32', 'word_count': 524, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a01cb50-26c7-54bb-ae3d-09e551bb8d32'}, {'abstract': 'The shift to a more overtly educational strategy stems from research showing that parents want products “to help them get their kids ready for school,” Nickelodeon’s chief said.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/nickelodeon-as-provider-of-homework/', 'snippet': 'The shift to a more overtly educational strategy stems from research showing that parents want products “to help them get their kids ready for school,” Nickelodeon’s chief said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nickelodeon has often underpinned its preschool television shows with curriculum-based teaching, but like a parent sneaking vegetables into the pasta, it tended to hide the educational values.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nickelodeon as Provider of Homework', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nickelodeon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-12T23:07:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/220035f7-6843-5c1b-83ce-e2a25d955c15', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/220035f7-6843-5c1b-83ce-e2a25d955c15'}, {'abstract': 'Residents are often forced to hand over a tenth of their earnings, just as they were when the Taliban ran things.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/world/asia/13police.html', 'snippet': 'Residents are often forced to hand over a tenth of their earnings, just as they were when the Taliban ran things.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — Ghulam Hazrat should be a poster boy for the peaceful reintegration of insurgents who want to switch sides. Six months ago he was a Taliban commander in the troubled Imam Sahib district of northern Kunduz Province. Now he and 10 of his followers are in the process of becoming police officers, at which point the government will start paying them salaries. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'sourc