are', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T01:06:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/351d57e0-a1c6-5a36-a5db-38b8235af203', 'word_count': 792, 'uri': 'nyt://article/351d57e0-a1c6-5a36-a5db-38b8235af203'}, {'abstract': 'Animal Kingdom’s sixth-place finish in the Belmont Stakes probably points him to the Travers Stakes instead of the Arlington Million.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/sports/with-winners-times-three-triple-crown-settles-little.html', 'snippet': 'Animal Kingdom’s sixth-place finish in the Belmont Stakes probably points him to the Travers Stakes instead of the Arlington Million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now what? That was what horse owners and trainers who survived the Triple Crown were thinking Sunday in the wake of Ruler on Ice’s improbable victory in the 143rd running of the Belmont Stakes. With long-shot winners in each of the three legs of the series, the 3-year-old division is still in search of a champion.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/sports/RACING/RACING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 645, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/13/sports/RACING/RACING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/13/sports/RACING/RACING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Winners Times Three, Triple Crown Settles Little', 'kicker': 'On Racing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Winners Times Three, Triple Crown Settles Little', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Triple Crown (Horse Racing)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Belmont Stakes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kentucky Derby', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Preakness Stakes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T01:08:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Drape', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drape', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a05b079c-1805-5a39-9fcb-88499fe3ee56', 'word_count': 712, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a05b079c-1805-5a39-9fcb-88499fe3ee56'}, {'abstract': 'In advance of the premiere of “The Smurfs,” Sony is promoting a Global Smurfs Day in June, for which people will paint themselves in blue and dress as Smurfs.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/12/sony-invites-people-to-go-smurfing/', 'snippet': 'In advance of the premiere of “The Smurfs,” Sony is promoting a Global Smurfs Day in June, for which people will paint themselves in blue and dress as Smurfs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cinephiles, microphobes and people for the ethical treatment of cartoon characters should cover their eyes now. The world is about to crawl with little blue people, at least if the movie marketers at Sony have their way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sony Invites People to Go Smurfing', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T01:13:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfd34525-8fd8-51d9-a55c-0c884016ec43', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfd34525-8fd8-51d9-a55c-0c884016ec43'}, {'abstract': 'The Anthony Weiner story is really about narcissism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/opinion/13douthat.html', 'snippet': 'The Anthony Weiner story is really about narcissism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In every time and place, people have associated new technologies with moral decline. “Men think that it is essential that the Nation have commerce,” Henry David Thoreau griped in 1854, “and export ice, and talk through a telegraph, and ride thirty miles an hour ... but whether we should live like baboons or like men, is a little uncertain.” Similar anxieties have greeted most subsequent inventions, from the automobile to the iPhone: We’re always teetering on the brink of baboondom, always one technological leap away from forfeiting our humanity.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank'