iness Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes and Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27400955-774b-5da9-b784-e8c398b6facc', 'word_count': 884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27400955-774b-5da9-b784-e8c398b6facc'}, {'abstract': 'A simplistic Supreme Court ruling foils essential lawsuits to hold government contractors accountable for fraud.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/opinion/13mon2.html', 'snippet': 'A simplistic Supreme Court ruling foils essential lawsuits to hold government contractors accountable for fraud.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a bad sign when the Supreme Court relied on the dictionary as the main authority in a recent ruling that made it harder for whistle-blowers to hold government contractors accountable for fraud. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'False Claims About FOIA', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'False Claims About FOIA: A simplistic Supreme Court ruling foils essential lawsuits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whistle-Blowers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Information Act', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T01:29:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0d3362a-4fde-5ac8-b028-670bbf68f081', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0d3362a-4fde-5ac8-b028-670bbf68f081'}, {'abstract': 'About one in four of the nearly 7,400 elected representatives across the country do not possess a four-year college degree, a study found.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/education/13legis.html', 'snippet': 'About one in four of the nearly 7,400 elected representatives across the country do not possess a four-year college degree, a study found.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About one in four of the nearly 7,400 elected representatives across the country do not possess a four-year college degree, according to a report released Sunday evening by The Chronicle of Higher Education in Washington. That compares with 6 percent of members of Congress, and 72 percent of adults nationwide, said the report, which is based primarily on the officials’ self-reported biographical information.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Many State Legislators Lack College Degrees', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Many State Legislators Lack College Degrees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chronicle of Higher Education', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T01:31:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Winnie Hu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, '