esk': 'Theater', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Tony Awards', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/d6601bae-4e4f-576e-81b5-eced9ffecccf', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/d6601bae-4e4f-576e-81b5-eced9ffecccf'}, {'abstract': 'Tom MacMaster, who identified himself as a 40-year-old American graduate student, claimed that he had authored all the posts on the well-known Syrian blog.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/world/middleeast/13blogger.html', 'snippet': 'Tom MacMaster, who identified himself as a 40-year-old American graduate student, claimed that he had authored all the posts on the well-known Syrian blog.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Six days after a post on a well-known blog called “A Gay Girl in Damascus” triggered panic among its readers by suggesting that the author, who claimed to be a Syrian-American lesbian caught up in the protest movement, had been arrested, a new entry appeared on Sunday that described the entire online diary as a work of fiction by an American man.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Gay Girl in Damascus’ Blog a Hoax, American Says', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Gay Girl in Damascus’ Blog a Hoax, American Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arraf, Amina Abdallah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Damascus (Syria)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T04:14:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54c28cfb-9046-5ea3-90b9-dfb5accf1a3a', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54c28cfb-9046-5ea3-90b9-dfb5accf1a3a'}, {'abstract': 'It is a venerable rite that’s also an endangered one: the archetypal American story of a summer job has been difficult for teenagers to fulfill, with just one in four expected to find work in the coming months.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/us/13jobsintro.html', 'snippet': 'It is a venerable rite that’s also an endangered one: the archetypal American story of a summer job has been difficult for teenagers to fulfill, with just one in four expected to find work in the coming months.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Jobs likes to tell how his own career in computers was shaped by a lucky summer job. Fascinated by electronics and already brash at age 13, he looked up Bill Hewlett, of Hewlett Packard, in the Palo Alto telephone book and called him. They talked shop, and the boy ended up putting in screws on an HP assembly line. His first day, he later said, was “bliss.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tales of the Search for a Summer Job', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tales of the Search for a Summer Job', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T04:17:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://art