an the articles or summaries on today’s paper.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | June 13, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T05:48:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tiffany Frasier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tiffany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frasier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1745919b-087f-5629-ae7a-acd75b462692', 'word_count': 20, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1745919b-087f-5629-ae7a-acd75b462692'}, {'abstract': 'This year’s Bonnaroo festival closed with performances by Dr. John and others.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/bonnaroo-the-final-night/', 'snippet': 'This year’s Bonnaroo festival closed with performances by Dr. John and others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANCHESTER, Tenn. — During the lengthy sound-checking before Superjam, one of the last gigs at Bonnaroo this year, a production assistant brought an enormous stack of towels to the stage. They were hardly used. Dr. John set up on Hammond organ along with Dan Auerbach and some of the members of the Black Keys’ new expanded touring band, as well as the drummer Patrick Hallahan of My Morning Jacket, two singers, and, in time, the Preservation Hall Jazz Band, which has strangely enough acted as a kind of binding glue for this festival.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bonnaroo: The Final Night', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bonnaroo Festival (Manchester, Tenn)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dr John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T05:53:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a83ca3ae-722b-576e-b426-cbc35bbababb', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a83ca3ae-722b-576e-b426-cbc35bbababb'}, {'abstract': 'Can you calculate the ratio of LinkedIn’s market cap at the end of its first week as a publicly traded company to its 2010 earnings?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/test-yourself-math-june-13-2011/', 'snippet': 'Can you calculate the ratio of LinkedIn’s market cap at the end of its first week as a publicly traded company to its 2010 earnings?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your math skills with today’s question, created by Bobson Wong at Math for America from the article “Analysts Wary of LinkedIn’s Stock Surge.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Math, June 13, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T08:25:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f278972-2df0-5f95-bc1d-5891fad83065', 'word_count': 51, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f278972-2df0-5f95-bc1d-5891fad83065'}, {'abstract': 'Following his cancer treatment, Jeffrey Henson Scales spent a year photographing the streets of New York.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/coming-right-back/', 'snippet': 'Following his cancer treatment, Jeffrey Henson Scales spent a year photographing the streets of New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeffrey Henson Scales, 57, a photographer and a photo editor at The New York Times, learned in 2008 that he had prostate cancer. Soon after his return from treatment in Toronto, Mr. Scales began taking pictures again — largely in Morningside Park and Times Square — at the urging of his wife, Meg Henson Scales, herself a photographer and writer. Running from Jan. 9 to Dec. 25, 2009, the pictures compose the exhibition “That Year of Living,” presented by the organization Light Work. It is on display until July 10 at the Robert B. Menschel Media Center in Syracuse.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coming Right Back', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T09:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meg Henson Scales', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meg', 'middlename': 'Henson', 'lastname': 'Scales', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb8c65d9-d5f4-5a28-92a1-968a0e6dd119', 'word_count': 799, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb8c65d9-d5f4-5a28-92a1-968a0e6dd119'}, {'abstract': 'The American man behind the Gay Girl in Damascus blogging hoax explained in an interview with The Times that his attempt to use fiction to promote discussion of the Middle East spun out of control.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/gay-girl-in-damascus-blogger-admits-to-writing-fiction-disguised-as-fact/', 'snippet': 'The American man behind the Gay Girl in Damascus blogging hoax explained in an interview with The Times that his attempt to use fiction to promote discussion of the Middle East spun out of control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Six days after a post on the blog A Gay Girl in Damascus triggered panic among its readers by suggesting that the blog’s author, who claimed to be a Syrian-American lesbian caught up in the protest movement, had been detained in the Syrian capital, a new entry appeared on Sunday that described the entire online diary as a work of fiction by an American man.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gay Girl in Damascus Blogger Admits to Writing Fiction Disguised as Fact', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABUNIMAH, ALI', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arraf, Amina Abdallah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T09:12:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/052b1401-596b-5d6b-9262-accd3165237a', 'word_count': 1511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/052b1401-596b-5d6b-9262-accd3165237a'}, {'abstract': 'Horizon Acquisitions bought APR Energy, the temporary power supplier backed by Madeleine Albright and George Soros, in a deal worth $855 million.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/horizon-buys-power-rental-firm-apr-energy/', 'snippet': 'Horizon Acquisitions bought APR Energy, the temporary power supplier backed by Madeleine Albright and George Soros, in a deal worth $855 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Horizon Acquisitions said Monday that it had bought APR Energy, the temporary power supplier backed by Madeleine Albright and George Soros, in a deal worth $855 million.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Horizon Buys Power Rental Firm APR Energy', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APR Energy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Horizon Acquisition Co PLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T09:53:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a18705bd-d685-56e6-ac2d-2f2329164022', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a18705bd-d685-56e6-ac2d-2f2329164022'}, {'abstract': 'In his first campaign address since his top aides quit en masse last week, the Republican presidential hopeful Newt Gingrich delivered a blistering critique of the Obama administration’s Middle East policy.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/gingrich-attacks-obama-in-return-to-campaign-trail/', 'snippet': 'In his first campaign address since his top aides quit en masse last week, the Republican presidential hopeful Newt Gingrich delivered a blistering critique of the Obama administration’s Middle East policy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — In his first campaign address since his top aides quit en masse last week, the Republican presidential hopeful Newt Gingrich delivered a blistering critique of the Obama administration’s Middle East policy on Sunday, hoping to revive his floundering campaign by shifting the focus to the kind of issue-based discussion he has viewed as his strength in this presidential campaign.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gingrich Attacks Obama in Return to Campaign Trail', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T09:57:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Lovett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf5c37e3-7696-5821-ac7f-eae83c70bf77', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf5c37e3-7696-5821-ac7f-eae83c70bf77'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/the-early-word-new-edition/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: New Edition', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cd01f53-f0ef-5eaf-aa1e-37dc816a8406', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cd01f53-f0ef-5eaf-aa1e-37dc816a8406'}, {'abstract': 'Apple’s changes to iTunes allow all iTunes-purchased music, apps and books to be shared across all your devices, no syncing required.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/everything-you-need-to-know-about-the-new-itunes/', 'snippet': 'Apple’s changes to iTunes allow all iTunes-purchased music, apps and books to be shared across all your devices, no syncing required.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, at the opening of its annual developers’ conference, Apple announced iCloud — its new online storage and syncing service for music, photos, files and software. Although not all of its features are available immediately, one part — “iTunes in the Cloud Beta” — is, if you’ve updated to iTunes 10.3.1. Here is a primer about what you need to know, right now, about it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Everything You Need to Know About the New iTunes', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Digital Audio Players', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPod', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iTunes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recordings and Downloads (Audio)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T10:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Grobart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grobart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03a215d2-21a6-5afd-9f8c-f69be59d642d', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03a215d2-21a6-5afd-9f8c-f69be59d642d'}, {'abstract': 'Britain’s success in reducing child poverty offers lessons for the United States in both policy and political will, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/how-to-cut-child-poverty-in-half/', 'snippet': 'Britain’s success in reducing child poverty offers lessons for the United States in both policy and political will, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nancy Folbre is an economics professor at the University of Massachusetts Amherst.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Cut Child Poverty in Half', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldfogel, Jane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T10:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Folbre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Folbre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/524cfb90-8bd4-5373-a26f-a4bc9671c299', 'word_count': 707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/524cfb90-8bd4-5373-a26f-a4bc9671c299'}, {'abstract': 'On Saturday, the London suburb of Highgate, will host Fair in the Square, a way for visitors to see a casual, weekending London of neighbors, friends and families that the city’s touristy center rarely offers.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/at-fair-the-flavors-of-london-suburbia/', 'snippet': 'On Saturday, the London suburb of Highgate, will host Fair in the Square, a way for visitors to see a casual, weekending London of neighbors, friends and families that the city’s touristy center rarely offers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the casual visitor, London’s squashed suburbia, with its tightly packed terraced houses and the occasional pub for visual relief, isn’t the most appealing aspect of the city. But amid London’s sprawl sit villages that were once outside the city. Among them is Highgate, a desirable neighborhood of Georgian town houses, independent cafes, antique shops and celebrity homes, perched atop a hill.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Fair, the Flavors of London Suburbia', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Festivals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T10:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leo Mirani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mirani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/420b96da-e080-55c2-a3d6-19e5e5132285', 'word_count': 327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/420b96da-e080-55c2-a3d6-19e5e5132285'}, {'abstract': 'What are “shadow” Internet and mobile phone systems?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/the-internet-in-a-suitcase/', 'snippet': 'What are “shadow” Internet and mobile phone systems?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHO is leading a global effort to deploy “shadow” Internet and mobile phone systems? WHO are the groups using–and eager to use–such systems?WHO wants to silence the groups?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The ‘Internet in a Suitcase’', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T10:01:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77713501-17be-5844-bd24-cf720c157d0e', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77713501-17be-5844-bd24-cf720c157d0e'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | Radio, Internet, television, friends–where do you find new music?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/where-do-you-find-new-music/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | Radio, Internet, television, friends–where do you find new music?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an  Op-Ed piece about college radio, former D.J. Freddie O’Connell writes about Vanderbilt University’s campus radio station, which, its license having been sold to the local public radio station, has gone off the air. Do the colleges and universities near where you live have radio stations? Do you listen to them? Do you listen to music on the radio in general? Why or why not?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Do You Find New Music?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T10:04:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41724960-8761-5872-8dcb-ae3ace4bcfdb', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41724960-8761-5872-8dcb-ae3ace4bcfdb'}, {'abstract': 'The House and Senate will both be in session this week for the first time in June with a focus on trying to make progress in the negotiations toward a budget agreement.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/congressional-week-ahead-budget-agreement/', 'snippet': 'The House and Senate will both be in session this week for the first time in June with a focus on trying to make progress in the negotiations toward a budget agreement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The House and Senate will both be in session this week for the first time in June with a focus on trying to make progress in the negotiations toward a budget agreement.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Congressional Week Ahead: Budget Agreement', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethanol', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Anthony D', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T10:27:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Hulse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hulse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc276073-1f4c-5442-95ab-9c2301afb613', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc276073-1f4c-5442-95ab-9c2301afb613'}, {'abstract': 'The deal calls for two Chinese companies to take stakes in the sputtering Swedish automaker, as well as make and sell Saab’s cars in China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/business/global/14saab.html', 'snippet': 'The deal calls for two Chinese companies to take stakes in the sputtering Swedish automaker, as well as make and sell Saab’s cars in China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — In the latest twist on its road to recovery, Saab Automobile announced a deal Monday with two Chinese companies that will take stakes in the Swedish automaker and make and sell its cars in the world’s most populous country. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saab Signs Up Another Chinese Investor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Another Chinese Company Buys a Stake in Saab', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Saab Automobile AB', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T10:43:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Saltmarsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltmarsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f5f9ffa-4d31-577c-a5b9-b4febfcc7b5b', 'word_count': 659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f5f9ffa-4d31-577c-a5b9-b4febfcc7b5b'}, {'abstract': 'Plus: What do you think is the secret to customer service? We try to resolve problems the same day.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/this-week-in-small-business-repeal-the-tanning-tax/', 'snippet': 'Plus: What do you think is the secret to customer service? We try to resolve problems the same day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s affecting me, my clients and other small-business owners this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week in Small Business: Repeal the Tanning Tax!', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T11:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gene Marks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aec9718d-0ac6-5b1b-bac0-b1dc377ee006', 'word_count': 1521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aec9718d-0ac6-5b1b-bac0-b1dc377ee006'}, {'abstract': 'Complaint Box: The escalators from the subway to the street are already moving; is it really necessary to climb over people when they are crowded?', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/complaint-box-elevator-express-lanes/', 'snippet': 'Complaint Box: The escalators from the subway to the street are already moving; is it really necessary to climb over people when they are crowded?', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are many platforms in the city’s rail system so deeply underground that climbing the stairs to the surface is daunting for the best of us. (Think Main Street, Flushing; the No. 7 at Grand Central Terminal; the Long Island Rail Road to 34th Street.) I occasionally braved the stairs, but after I hurt my knee I began to take escalators routinely — and quickly became aware of their express lanes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Complaint Box | Going Up Standing Still', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elevators and Escalators', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Flushing (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T11:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By ELISE ANTREASSIAN BAYIZIAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elise', 'middlename': 'Antreassian', 'lastname': 'BAYIZIAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f62eaebc-1684-5ff2-839b-c53cd3925ad2', 'word_count': 548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f62eaebc-1684-5ff2-839b-c53cd3925ad2'}, {'abstract': 'VF Corporation said it would acquire Timberland in a $2 billion deal that would add to the apparel company’s extensive collection of brands.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/vf-corporation-to-buy-timberland/', 'snippet': 'VF Corporation said it would acquire Timberland in a $2 billion deal that would add to the apparel company’s extensive collection of brands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The VF Corporation agreed on Monday to buy the Timberland Company, the boot maker, for about $2 billion in cash, paying a big premium in an effort to bolster its outdoor clothing offerings.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'VF Corporation to Buy Timberland', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Timberland Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'VF CORP', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T11:27:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e175bb07-fdb5-5b9a-bef4-f20f4c6901ff', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e175bb07-fdb5-5b9a-bef4-f20f4c6901ff'}, {'abstract': 'The Maple Group on Monday moved forward in its hostile play for TMX Group with a $3.8 billion bid, besting a $3.6 billion offer by the London Stock Exchange.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/maple-group-makes-hostile-bid-for-tmx-group/', 'snippet': 'The Maple Group on Monday moved forward