, 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quinlan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d7cbd2b-7470-5dda-924e-d163d58b6fe4', 'word_count': 1650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d7cbd2b-7470-5dda-924e-d163d58b6fe4'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Broadway has just completed an excellent season at the box office, and its importance as a money machine for the city seems hard to dispute. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/a-bang-up-night-for-broadway-even-if-the-wrong-play-won/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Broadway has just completed an excellent season at the box office, and its importance as a money machine for the city seems hard to dispute. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'lead_paragraph': ' On behalf of men who wear hats — real hats, that is, not baseball caps, and certainly not caps with visors facing backward — I want to express some dismay that the Tony Award for best play did not go on Sunday night to the only show on Broadway with “hat” on the marquee. You know which play this is, the one with a troublesome word in the title, something about a guy with unresolved oedipal issues.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bang-Up Night for Broadway, Even if the Wrong Play Won', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T13:05:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/338bda7b-c2ad-5386-998c-edb810c0d5ac', 'word_count': 1008, 'uri': 'nyt://article/338bda7b-c2ad-5386-998c-edb810c0d5ac'}, {'abstract': 'A green and pleasant break.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/raising-keynes-2/', 'snippet': 'A green and pleasant break.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m off to England for a couple of weeks; a shortish bike trip, then a conference, then some more touristing. Posting will be rare, columns will be on hold.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Raising Keynes', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T13:19:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a736971-da73-5613-9bd5-9f00a7b7765e', 'word_count': 29, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a736971-da73-5613-9bd5-9f00a7b7765e'}, {'abstract': 'How one veteran feels about being called a “hero.”', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/when-hero-rings-hollow/', 'snippet': 'How one veteran feels about being called a “hero.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' It is not uncommon for politicians, media figures and the general public to claim – without question – that those serving in the armed forces are heroes. Military service is unique, and the challenges faced by service members are unlike those of other professions. Violent death is a real possibility while wearing a service uniform. But does this make everyone who served a hero?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When ‘Hero’ Rings Hollow', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T13:19:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Don Gomez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Don', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gomez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/574fe673-741b-5b20-b3f8-e787dfb3c654', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/574fe673-741b-5b20-b3f8-e787dfb3c654'}, {'abstract': 'Where I’m coming from.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/macro-readings-update/', 'snippet': 'Where I’m coming from.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I mentioned in an earlier post, I often have the sense that many of this readers of this blog are, in effect, entering in the middle of a conversation, not aware of the steps by which I got to the latest argument. This is especially true on issues of macroeconomics. So I thought it might be useful to maintain, for some time, a list of links to earlier posts that lay out some basic ideas I use repeatedly. Here they are as a blog post; in the near future they’ll become a standing feature on the right side of this page.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Macro Readings Update', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T13:29:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b6f8008-54bc-50ac-9fc6-78578cab3db9', 'word_count': 188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b6f8008-54bc-50ac-9fc6-78578cab3db9'}, {'abstract': 'There is often a surprisingly fine line between being kind and being kind of stupid or irresponsible or naïve. Here are some real life examples of issues I’ve wrestled with.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/are-you-a-kind-boss-or-kind-of-a-boss/', 'snippet': 'There is often a surprisingly fine line between being kind and being kind of stupid or irresponsible or naïve. Here are some real life examples of issues I’ve wrestled with.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been writing for this blog for about two years now. It’s been interesting to learn how readers view the boss. Many people who comment are the boss, some want to be the boss, and others just have an opinion about the boss.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are You a Kind Boss? Or Kind of a Boss?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Resources', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T13:30:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'sectio