ig Words', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dictionaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T15:55:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7077972c-8ead-57b2-badf-a7272ecbc864', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7077972c-8ead-57b2-badf-a7272ecbc864'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Harford’s new book, “Adapt: Why Success Always Starts with Failure,” offers a very useful guide for people preparing to live in the world as it really is.', 'web_url': 'https://brooks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/living-with-mistakes/', 'snippet': 'Tim Harford’s new book, “Adapt: Why Success Always Starts with Failure,” offers a very useful guide for people preparing to live in the world as it really is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some of the blogs I follow—Marginal Revolution, Ezra Klein—have given ample attention to Tim Harford’s new book, “Adapt: Why Success Always Starts with Failure.” So I solipsistically assumed that everybody must be aware of it. But then I happened to glance at this book’s Amazon ranking, which as I write is down on the wrong side of 1,500. This is an outrage, people! For the good of the world, a bigger slice of humanity should be aware of its contents.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Living With Mistakes', 'kicker': 'David Brooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harford, Tim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marginal Revolution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T16:01:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/229f3947-2662-5e10-ac54-681dd1cb59a3', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/229f3947-2662-5e10-ac54-681dd1cb59a3'}, {'abstract': 'Paul McCartney adds a second show because of “overwhelming popular demand.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/aging-left-hander-will-play-two-at-yankee-stadium/', 'snippet': 'Paul McCartney adds a second show because of “overwhelming popular demand.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul McCartney has added a second date at Yankee Stadium, “by overwhelming popular demand,” according to an announcement on Monday morning. The second concert will be Saturday, July 16, the day after the first show, and tickets went on sale through Ticketmaster.com at 11:40 a.m., Eastern time. As for the first concert, prices range from $34 to $279.50, plus fees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aging Left-Hander Will Play Two at Yankee Stadium', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCartney, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yankee Stadium (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T16:02:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a728236d-8c94-5c91-a0a4-c958b8d14de1', 'word_count': 60, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a728236d-8c94-5c91-a0a4-c958b8d14de1'}, {'abstract': 'There is a strong correlation between how often people search online for “estate agents” and actual house prices, two Bank of England researchers wrote in a report on Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/using-google-searches-to-track-housing-prices/', 'snippet': 'There is a strong correlation between how often people search online for “estate agents” and actual house prices, two Bank of England researchers wrote in a report on Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Bank of England has started to use Google search data to help it spot trends in Britain’s housing and labor markets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Using Google Searches to Track Housing Prices', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T16:07:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92db83d1-6fc2-505c-9be6-18b2078104d2', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92db83d1-6fc2-505c-9be6-18b2078104d2'}, {'abstract': 'The chemical oxybenzone is a concern for some.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/health/14really.html', 'snippet': 'The chemical oxybenzone is a co