nt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:12:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna M. Foster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25c3c440-19e0-5f96-823d-d77749b49c51', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25c3c440-19e0-5f96-823d-d77749b49c51'}, {'abstract': 'In his first week as anchor of the CBS Evening News, Scott Pelley drew a slightly smaller audience than Harry Smith did as the substitute anchor, and showed no growth from the audience that Katie Couric averaged during her last month on the air.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/a-new-anchor-but-the-same-ratings-on-cbs/', 'snippet': 'In his first week as anchor of the CBS Evening News, Scott Pelley drew a slightly smaller audience than Harry Smith did as the substitute anchor, and showed no growth from the audience that Katie Couric averaged during her last month on the air.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CBS introduced Scott Pelley as the new anchor of its evening news with no fanfare last week, and the result was no new fans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Anchor, but the Same Ratings, on CBS', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pelley, Scott', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:15:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4f00c15-c339-57e7-96b9-d2a56c26aa73', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4f00c15-c339-57e7-96b9-d2a56c26aa73'}, {'abstract': 'At the Met on Saturday, a sampling of Philip Glass’s evolving style.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/arts/music/philip-glass-at-the-metropolitan-museum-review.html', 'snippet': 'At the Met on Saturday, a sampling of Philip Glass’s evolving style.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Followers of the composer Philip Glass could scarcely miss a turn in recent decades from the hypnotic extended works that earned him the Minimalist label toward the traditional forms and trappings of the classical music mainstream. Interviewing Mr. Glass onstage in the Grace Rainey Rogers Auditorium at the Metropolitan Museum of Art on Saturday night, Richard Guérin, the associate director of Mr. Glass’s record label, Orange Mountain Music, noted the development. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/arts/glass-2/glass-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 347, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/14/arts/glass-2/glass-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 347}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/arts/glass-2/glass-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/arts/glass-2/glass-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/14/arts/glass-2/glass-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Glass’s Players Warm Up for a Festival in August', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Glass’s Players Warm Up For a Festival in August', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orange Mountain Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glass, Philip', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:17:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef7df6aa-a25f-5710-8cd9-7f612865df63', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef7df6aa-a25f-5710-8cd9-7f612865df63'}, {'abstract': 'Thanks to bright young fans like Alexa Chung and Keira Knightley, the London-based designer Kiely is enjoying some well-deserved celebrity on her home turf.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/now-arriving-orla-kiely/', 'snippet': 'Thanks to bright young fans like Alexa Chung and Keira Knightley, the London-based designer Kiely is enjoying some well-deserved celebrity on her home turf.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On this side of the Atlantic, Orla Kiely is best known for her instantly recognizable leaf-print bags, which hang on strollers from Brooklyn to Santa Monica. But the Irish-born, London-based designer makes much more than just mummy-friendly diaper bags. She also has home décor and ready-to-wear lines, both of which feature cheerful graphics and mod silhouettes. And while it’s taken a while, London hipsters have finally lit upon her her clothing designs, which make references to the Chelsea girl charm of Mary Quant, Julie Christie and Jean Shrimpton. Thanks to bright young fans like Alexa Chung and Keira Knightley, Kiely is enjoying some well-deserved celebrity on her home turf. It helps, of course, that she has a bevy of cool girls working for her. Venetia Scott, the Marc Jacobs stylist-turned-photographer, shoots her ad campaigns, and Leith Clark, the Canadian editor of the British indie fashion magazine Lula, styles them. Her first American store is set to open in SoHo in September. In anticipation, Kiely’s hosting an event at the Ace Hotel tomorrow night. Chung will D.J. and Mercedes Heinwein’s short film about the fall collection, which was inspired by Tippi Hedren’s wardrobe in “The Birds,” will be screened. Describing her recent success, which she’s hoping to replicate in the United States, Kiely is as well mannered and ladylike as her neat blouses and primly tailored coats. “Oh, gosh, I’ve really just been enjoying it,” she says. “I hope Americans like the clothes, too.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Arriving | Orla Kiely', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:20:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy MacDonell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca3f5ccf-8a24-5974-ab69-6e95355362f5', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca3f5ccf-8a24-5974-ab69-6e95355362f5'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama said in an interview with NBC that Representative Anthony D. Weiner should step down.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/13/obama-suggests-that-weiner-should-step-down/', 'snippet': 'President Obama said in an interview with NBC that Representative Anthony D. Weiner should step down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama said on Monday that if he were in Representative Anthony D. Weiner’s position, “I would resign,” according to NBC, which conducted an extensive interview with him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Suggests That Weiner Step Down', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Anthony D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:20:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Barbaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89e000da-2d9e-5f46-a69c-b6140b777941', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89e000da-2d9e-5f46-a69c-b6140b777941'}, {'abstract': 'The Gotham Dance Festival offered works by Ashleigh Leite, Julian Barnett and Sydney Skybetter at the Joyce on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/arts/dance/gotham-dance-festival-sampler-julian-barnett-ashleigh-leite.html', 'snippet': 'The Gotham Dance Festival offered works by Ashleigh Leite, Julian Barnett and Sydney Skybetter at the Joyce on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Gotham Dance Festival wrapped up over the weekend with its second summer sampler matinee, and it was as mixed up as mixed bills can be.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/arts/festival/festival-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 698, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Afternoon of Aggressive Hair and Forlorn Boots', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Afternoon of Aggressive Hair and Forlorn Boots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:20:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c44ac44-8cf5-521f-bcb0-c14eb4edd9b1', 'word_count': 524, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c44ac44-8cf5-521f-bcb0-c14eb4edd9b1'}, {'abstract': 'As executive producer of “Pretty Woman” (1990), Ms. Ziskin insisted on an ending in which Julia Roberts has changed Richard Gere as fully as he has changed her.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/movies/laura-ziskin-behind-spider-man-films-dies-at-61.html', 'snippet': 'As executive producer of “Pretty Woman” (1990), Ms. Ziskin insisted on an ending in which Julia Roberts has changed Richard Gere as fully as he has changed her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Laura Ziskin, a prominent Hollywood producer who ventured into the largely male world of special-effects movies to become a shaping force behind the blockbuster “Spider-Man” franchise, died on Sunday at her home in Santa Monica, Calif. She was 61.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/movies/14ziskin/14ziskin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/movies/14ziskin/14ziskin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/14/movies/14ziskin/14ziskin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Laura Ziskin, Producer of ‘Spider-Man’ and ‘Pretty Woman,’ Dies at 61', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Laura Ziskin, Producer of ‘Spider-Man’ and ‘Pretty Woman,’ Dies at 61', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ziskin, Laura', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breast Cancer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:28:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aljean Harmetz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aljean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harmetz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2c0cce5-e714-5677-b3e2-80f30b5d8c59', 'word_count': 914, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2c0cce5-e714-5677-b3e2-80f30b5d8c59'}, {'abstract': 'Stanford University plans to announce on Tuesday that it will soon be a major West Coast presence in postwar art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/arts/design/major-art-gift-to-stanford.html', 'snippet': 'Stanford University plans to announce on Tuesday that it will soon be a major West Coast presence in postwar art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an economically rough period when many university art museums seem to be in a position of retreat, Stanford University plans to announce on Tuesday that it will soon be a major West Coast presence in postwar art. The university is accepting a donation of works from one of the most important private collections of Abstract Expressionism. The group of 121 paintings and sculptures, owned by Harry W. and Mary Margaret Anderson, had long been expected to make its way to the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art, which has been the beneficiary of the Andersons for many years. But the couple, known as Hunk and Moo, said in a statement that “we have always been closely associated with colleges and universities, and in making this gift to Stanford we anticipate the students, the public and the entire art community will have the opportunity to fully engage the collection.” The collection, which the Andersons began building in the 1960s, includes one of the last major Jackson Pollock drip paintings still in private hands, “Lucifer,” from 1947, when Pollock was making his transition from a more traditional style to his signature volcanic canvases. Several years ago dealers familiar with the painting said that the Andersons had turned down as much as $100 million for it. The collection also includes significant works by Franz Kline, Robert Motherwell, Mark Rothko, David Smith, Helen Frankenthaler, Sam Francis and Philip Guston. Stanford said that by 2014 it expected to complete construction of a building to house the collection, which includes 86 artists in all, in styles including Abstract Expressionism and reflecting movements like Light and Space and Color Field painting.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Major Art Gift to Stanford', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Major Art Gift to Stanford', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford University', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:29:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e39f77ec-64c7-56b0-9543-87e0954067da', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e39f77ec-64c7-56b0-9543-87e0954067da'}, {'abstract': 'A half century later, the Turnesa name is back in the U.S. Open golf tournament.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/sports/golf/100000000862775/the-turnesa-legacy.html', 'snippet': 'A half century later, the Turnesa name is back in the U.S. Open golf tournament.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A half century later, the Turnesa name is back in the U.S. Open golf tournament.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/sports/video-turnesagolf/video-turnesagolf-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/14/sports/video-turnesagolf/video-turnesagolf-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/sports/video-turnesagolf/video-turnesagolf-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/14/sports/video-turnesagolf/video-turnesagolf-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Turnesa Legacy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kilgannon, Corey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:36:42+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'David Frank', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frank', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/c1181d0e-323c-5944-a532-a9a3e8f9ce19', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/c1181d0e-323c-5944-a532-a9a3e8f9ce19'}, {'abstract': 'Republican lawmakers say that if the State Supreme Court does not rule by Tuesday on collective bargaining limits they passed this spring, they will simply pass the limits again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/us/14brfs-REPUBLICANSM_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Republican lawmakers say that if the State Supreme Court does not rule by Tuesday on collective bargaining limits they passed this spring, they will simply pass the limits again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Republican lawmakers say that if the State Supreme Court does not rule by Tuesday on collective bargaining limits they passed this spring, they will simply pass the limits again. The law, which sharply curtails bargaining rights for public workers, has not taken effect because of legal challenges. A judge ruled last month that Republican legislators violated open-meetings requirements when they passed it, but state officials have appealed that decision. Lawmakers may now attach the limits to the state’s new budget bill, which is to be considered this week, Speaker Jeff Fitzgerald, Republican of Horicon, said Monday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wisconsin: Republicans May Not Wait for Court to Rule on Union Limits', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Midwest ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wisconsin: Republicans May Not Wait For Court to Rule on Union Limits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:42:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Davey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cc6f161-7399-58c4-84dd-2b1003e0b6ea', 'word_count': 96, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cc6f161-7399-58c4-84dd-2b1003e0b6ea'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Christine Gregoire, a two-term Democrat who has steered her state through a period of painful budget cuts, said Monday that she would not seek a third term.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/us/14brfs-GOVERNORWONT_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Christine Gregoire, a two-term Democrat who has steered her state through a period of painful budget cuts, said Monday that she would not seek a third term.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Christine Gregoire, a two-term Democrat who has steered her state through a period of painful budget cuts, said Monday that she would not seek a third term. Her decision was not unexpected, and it clears the way for what some political observers expect to be an extremely close race in 2012. Rob McKenna, a Republican who is the state’s attorney general, announced his long-anticipated candidacy last week. Representative Jay Inslee, a Democrat whose district includes parts of the politically crucial Seattle region, has signaled for months that he would run if Ms. Gregoire did not. The state, like many others, has been forced to make major spending cuts, including to schools and social programs, and the question is whether frustration with the its direction will be enough to overcome Democratic control. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Washington: Governor Won’t Run in ’12', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Northwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Washington: Governor Won’t Run in ’12', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (State)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gregoire, Christine O', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:43:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Yardley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yardley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/5807f3a3-2d91-5de0-aae4-fb84f21b0745', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5807f3a3-2d91-5de0-aae4-fb84f21b0745'}, {'abstract': 'As the Missouri River continued to rise to record levels, two protective levees ruptured Monday, sending floodwaters pouring over fields and toward small communities in Missouri and Iowa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/us/14brfs-2LEVEESAREBR_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'As the Missouri River continued to rise to record levels, two protective levees ruptured Monday, sending floodwaters pouring over fields and toward small communities in Missouri and Iowa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the Missouri River continued to rise to record levels, two protective levees ruptured Monday, sending floodwaters pouring over fields and toward small communities in Missouri and Iowa. The breaches had been expected in both Hamburg, Iowa, and Big Lake, Mo. Despite the construction of secondary levees to hold back the water, evacuations were under way because of concerns that those hastily built barriers might also fail. The dam-controlled river, which has caused flooding from Montana to Missouri because of record rain and snow, is expected to remain at flood levels for the next two months. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Missouri: 2 Levees Are Breached', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Midwest ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Missouri: 2 Levees Are Breached', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Missouri', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Levees and Dams', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:44:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. G. Sulzberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Sulzberger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb67d1ee-217d-529d-92da-a663906b2dd4', 'word_count': 97, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb67d1ee-217d-529d-92da-a663906b2dd4'}, {'abstract': 'Neither the London Stock Exchange nor the Maple Group has made a compelling bid for the bourse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/business/14views.html', 'snippet': 'Neither the London Stock Exchange nor the Maple Group has made a compelling bid for the bourse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pity the TMX Group’s shareholders. The owners of the Toronto stock exchange face an unenviable choice of suitor. Neither the London Stock Exchange nor the Maple Group, which went hostile with its counteroffer of 3.7 billion Canadian dollars ($3.8 billion) on Monday, has made a compelling bid for the bourse.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/business/Views/Views-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '2 Underwhelming Choices for TMX', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2 Underwhelming Choices for TMX', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TMX Group', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PRADA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-13T22:45:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By ANTONY CURRIE and WEI GU', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Antony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CURRIE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Wei', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'GU', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f7968e8-43fe-5891-80cd-951984668dda', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f7968e8-43fe-5891-80cd-951984668dda'}, {'abstract': 'A study remeasuring the skull collection of the 19th-century biologist Samuel Morton appears to debunk a famous claim by Stephen Jay Gould about racism in science.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/science/14skull.html', 'snippet': 'A study remeasuring the skull collection of the 19th-century biologist Samuel Morton appears to debunk a famous claim by Stephen Jay Gould about racism in science.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scientists have often been accused of letting their ideology influence their results, and one of the most famous cases is that of Morton’s skulls — the global collection amassed by the 19th-century physical anthropologist Samuel George Morton.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/science/SKUL/SKUL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/14/science/SKUL/SKUL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/science/SKUL/SKUL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/14/science/SKUL/SKUL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/14/science/SKUL/SKUL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scientists Measure the Accuracy of a Racism Claim', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scientists Measure the Accuracy of a Racism Claim', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'va