Adam Goodheart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodheart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75cfb1f1-2ca0-5f91-9813-61db311f7559', 'word_count': 1489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75cfb1f1-2ca0-5f91-9813-61db311f7559'}, {'abstract': 'Global overfishing is threatening the oceans and the economies that depend on them. We need strong international rules to end all unsustainable fishing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/opinion/14tue4.html', 'snippet': 'Global overfishing is threatening the oceans and the economies that depend on them. We need strong international rules to end all unsustainable fishing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The scale and toll of industrial fishing is far less familiar than even the scale and toll of industrial farming. Groups like Seafood Watch at the Monterey Bay Aquarium put out sustainable seafood lists for diners and shoppers, good reminders that fisheries are in decline. But even they do not convey how bad things have become. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Clear View of the Troubled Oceans', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Clear View of the Troubled Oceans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-14T01:33:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/3eb84359-a39b-5f40-8d9e-efc4586c6e2c', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3eb84359-a39b-