ect the best antonym?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/test-yourself-english-june-14-2011/', 'snippet': 'Can you select the best antonym?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your editing skills with today’s question, created by Danielle Hoagland and Judith McCaffrey at Grammarlogues, from the article “Study Questions Treatment Used in Heart Disease.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | English, June 14, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-14T07:22:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5190edb0-6275-5acf-9633-369c954930ee', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5190edb0-6275-5acf-9633-369c954930ee'}, {'abstract': 'A little rough-and-tumble play can aid in children’s emotional development and in bonding with parents.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/in-praise-of-roughhousing/', 'snippet': 'A little rough-and-tumble play can aid in children’s emotional development and in bonding with parents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Go ahead. Throw your children around. It’s good for them, say Anthony T. DeBenedet and Lawrence J. Cohen, the authors of “The Art of Roughhousing: Good Old-Fashioned Horseplay and Why Every Kid Needs It.” You can trust them, because they are doctors. Well, DeBenedet is an M.D., while Cohen has a Ph.D. And in the days before Father’s Day they chatted with me by e-mail about exactly how “rough” the horseplay should get. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Praise of Roughhousing', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords