rson': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Lubben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/adecc90d-ee3d-5166-8f1d-7b6e983c66a7', 'word_count': 469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/adecc90d-ee3d-5166-8f1d-7b6e983c66a7'}, {'abstract': 'Responding to a judge’s order, state regulators in California undertook a new analysis of how different strategies would succeed in reducing emissions. The bottom line: they think cap-and-trade would work best.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/california-redoes-greenhouse-gas-analysis/', 'snippet': 'Responding to a judge’s order, state regulators in California undertook a new analysis of how different strategies would succeed in reducing emissions. The bottom line: they think cap-and-trade would work best.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing focuses the mind like being told by a thesis adviser (or a judge) that your analysis is inadequate and that your degree (or your carefully constructed policy for reducing greenhouse gas emissions) will be on hold until you provide a better one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California Redoes Its Greenhouse Gas Analysis', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'California Air Resources Board', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'