, 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Godfrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Godfrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a89f3148-59e7-56a7-bdcf-c477020c813f', 'word_count': 823, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a89f3148-59e7-56a7-bdcf-c477020c813f'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s Critics’ Pick, A. O. Scott looks at a slice of surreal life in Luis Buñuel and Salvador Dalí’s 1928 short film “Un Chien Andalou.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/critics-picks-video-un-chien-andalou/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s Critics’ Pick, A. O. Scott looks at a slice of surreal life in Luis Buñuel and Salvador Dalí’s 1928 short film “Un Chien Andalou.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s Critics’ Pick, A. O. Scott looks at a slice of surreal life in Luis Buñuel and Salvador Dalí’s 1928 short film “Un Chien Andalou.” The film, which catalogs a variety of random, sometimes shocking scenes, was part of the Surrealism art movement, one that rebelled against order and coherence. Mr. Scott says that even now, 80 years later, the movie “still seethes with creative, revolting imagery.” Mr. Scott praises Mr. Buñuel’s filmmaking, saying that he was “among the first to discover that the language of cinema could proceed beyond the boundaries of sense and produce powerful and strange effects and emotions.” Have you seen “Un Chien Andalou?” Please share your thoughts in the comments area below. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Critics’ Picks Video: ‘Un Chien Andalou’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dali, Salvador', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-14T14:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6476096-9257-5630-92c7-9ecb4a194b81', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6476096-9257-5630-92c7-9ecb4a194b81'}, {'abstract': 'The morning after the first major Republican debate, Mitt Romney visits Derry.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/the-caucus-click-mitt-romney-in-derry-n-h/', 'snippet': 'The morning after the first major Republican debate, Mitt Romney visits Derry.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Caucus Click: Mitt Romney in Derry, N.H.', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-14T14:04:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45fb76f7-3d8c-5cea-899b-2becca930fff', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45fb76f7-3d8c-5cea-899b-2becca930fff'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the staff at T share what they’re getting for their fathers this year.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/fathers-day-gift-guide-part-2/', 'snippet': 'Some of the staff at T share what they’re getting for their fathers this year.', 'lead_paragraph': ' NYTD.fullScreenSlideShowFactory.createSlideshow("#95", 592, 490, true, chameleonData);', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Father’s Day Gift Guide, Part 2', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-14T14:16:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Madison Shimoda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Madison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shimoda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0e99994-c9c9-5de6-a0ef-9a95f996ab84', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0e99994-c9c9-5de6-a0ef-9a95f996ab84'}, {'abstract': 'RockMelt, a Web browser for the Facebook era, teams up with Facebook to spruce up its offering.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/social-web-browser-rockmelt-teams-up-with-facebook/', 'snippet': 'RockMelt, a Web browser for the Facebook era, teams up with Facebook to spruce up its offering.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the Facebook- and Twitter-obsessed, the RockMelt Web browser, introduced last year by veterans of Netscape, offers plenty of goodies: a button to share any Web page or link; a rail showing what’s happening on their favorite social sites; another rail showing the activities of their friends and making it easy to chat with them; and plenty of other nifty social features.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Social Web Browser RockMelt Teams Up With Facebook', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web Browsers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook.com', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'RockMelt Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-14T14:23:32+0000'