iven the recent decline in gasoline prices, we’re likely to see a negative headline inflation number by June. What will the inflationistas say?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Power of Minus', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-14T16:37:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/763a5bce-eb75-54fd-937c-c88f9c25638f', 'word_count': 71, 'uri': 'nyt://article/763a5bce-eb75-54fd-937c-c88f9c25638f'}, {'abstract': 'Weekenders nostalgic for Montauk can capture its fading fisherman’s-village feeling with a logo tee from Local Knit.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/local-colors/', 'snippet': 'Weekenders nostalgic for Montauk can capture its fading fisherman’s-village feeling with a logo tee from Local Knit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Montauk, N.Y., may be the last hold out against the looming tide of Hamptonization, but its stuck-in-time fisherman’s-village feeling is fading fast. Which might explain the appeal of a new collection of T-shirts by Local Knit, featuring the logos from Montauk institutions like Duryea’s Lobster Deck, the Montauket and Paulie’s Tackle. Montaukers are snapping them up, as are weekenders. (Curbed’s Lockhart Steele is a fan and ordered a Herb’s Market shirt in beach plum with the slogan “You Can’t Beat Herb’s Meat.”) Amagansett and East Hampton — two towns ripe for nostalgia — will get their own logo tees next.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Local Colors', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-14T16:38:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Earle-Levine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Earle-Levine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e665a04e-99ca-5d31-be7d-9572821a693e', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e665a04e-99ca-5d31-be7d-9572821a693e'}, {'abstract': 'After saying he would have made the save on the winning goal in Game 5 — a 1-0 Bruins loss — Canucks goalie Roberto Luongo was pulled after allowing three goals in Game 6.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/sports/2011-stanley-cup-playoffs-canucks-goalie-goes-flat-again.html', 'snippet': 'After saying he would have made the save on the winning goal in Game 5 — a 1-0 Bruins loss — Canucks goalie Roberto Luongo was pulled after allowing three goals in Game 6.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to put Vancouver goalie Roberto Luongo in the same villain category as LeBron James. He did not embarrass franchises when he left — the Islanders and the Panthers did that all on their own, thank you — or predict that multiple championships would flock to him. He has no self-reverential tattoos and his entourage consists of equipment guys who hang up his goalie pads. And it’s hard to gin up much dislike for a hockey player in the United States, where Luongo’s name recognition hovers somewhere around the Dodgers’ backup shortstop.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Canucks Goalie Goes Flat', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Canucks Goalie Goes Flat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nowitzki, Dirk', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Bruins', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luongo, Roberto', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vancouver Canucks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Mavericks', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Golf)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Heat', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sedin, Daniel', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, LeBron', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuban, Mark', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-14T16:47:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f4c7d64-37dc-5120-9e74-2dc891016c1f', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f4c7d64-37dc-5120-9e74-2dc891016c1f'}, {'abstract': 'After wishing for a tweak to Gmail that would repost received messages at later dates, Boomerang, a free Gmail plug-in, offers to do that and a whole lot more.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/dear-gmail-labs-part-ii-the-rise-of-boomerang/', 'snippet': 'After wishing for a tweak to Gmail that would repost received messages at later dates, Boomerang, a free Gmail plug-in, offers to do that and a whole lot more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I just learned (first from a friend, then from the many comments people posted on my previous post) that what I’ve been looking for from Gmail (reposting of received messages at a future date) actually exists in the form of Boomerang—a free Gmail plug-in that does exactly what I described. I just installed it and it works perfectly—messages can reappear in my inbox (marked as unread, starred and/or at the top of my inbox) at a date or time I set. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dear Gmail Labs, Part II – The Rise of Boomerang', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-14T16:49:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Grobart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grobart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3301256-9381-5c44-a9af-08a3c5f61e19', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3301256-9381-5c44-a9af-08a3c5f61e19'}, {'abstract': 'Lawyers for a major figure in the Galleon insider trading case submitted a filing that at times reads more like a Harlequin romance novel than a legal brief.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/14/chiesi-seeks-lighter-prison-term-citing-love-affair-with-boss/', 'snippet': 'Lawyers for a major figure in the Galleon insider trading case submitted a filing that at times reads more like a Harlequin romance novel than a legal brief.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Danielle S. Chiesi, the former beauty queen turned convicted Wall Street trader, deserves a light prison sentence because her crimes were the result of a tortured love affair with her boss, her lawyers said. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline':