ion': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Danny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hakim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64152f01-8a6c-5e89-8ca9-4f0fd3b98491', 'word_count': 773, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64152f01-8a6c-5e89-8ca9-4f0fd3b98491'}, {'abstract': 'In a study of brain volume, intelligence and race, there may have been a prejudice, all right. But, if so, a surprising one.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/opinion/15wed4.html', 'snippet': 'In a study of brain volume, intelligence and race, there may have been a prejudice, all right. But, if so, a surprising one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stephen Jay Gould, a prominent evolutionary biologist, gained broad public attention for exposing how scientists’ biases can skew their research. In one celebrated case, he charged that a famous study of human skulls in the mid-19th century had been manipulated, probably unconsciously, to support racist notions. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bias and the Beholder', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bias and the Beholder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gould, Stephen Jay', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morton, Samuel George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Intelligence (IQ)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skull (Body Part)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T01:16:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_de