: 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T13:55:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37d871c6-633a-5994-898c-3137886d5679', 'word_count': 928, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37d871c6-633a-5994-898c-3137886d5679'}, {'abstract': 'After hosting a royal wedding, the dean of Westminster comes to New York for a little relaxation, and for his first margarita.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/after-royal-wedding-kicking-back-in-new-york/', 'snippet': 'After hosting a royal wedding, the dean of Westminster comes to New York for a little relaxation, and for his first margarita.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Any party planner or diplomatic official would agree that hosting visits from the pope and the president and hosting a royal wedding within the past nine month warrants a vacation. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Royal Wedding, Kicking Back in New York', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hall, John R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Westminster Abbey (London)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T14:06:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Haughney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haughney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa3845c0-3840-5176-991c-72e894232f44', 'word_count': 765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa3845c0-3840-5176-991c-72e894232f44'}, {'abstract': 'It’s been observed that governors often win the White House but senators rarely do (Barack Obama notwithstanding). Is there predictive power in that trend? A close look at the numbers reveals that as they pursue their party’s nomination, candidates who are or were governors tend to overperform their initial poll standing by a much wider margin than sitting or former senators do.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/the-governors-advantage-in-presidential-races-is-bigger-than-you-thought/', 'snippet': 'It’s been observed that governors often win the White House but senators rarely do (Barack Obama notwithstanding). Is there predictive power in that trend? A close look at the numbers reveals that as they pursue their party’s nomination, candidate...', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s reasonably well known that governors have a more successful record than senators when it comes to winning presidential nominations. Throughout American history, about twice as many governors as senators have been chosen to be standard bearers by the major parties, even though at any given moment there are only half as many sitting governors as sitting senators.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Governors’ Advantage in Presidential Races Is Bigger Than You Thought', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T14:14:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91f24042-05e2-5ad0-bbe1-2e9c9dd2a308', 'word_count': 1475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91f24042-05e2-5ad0-bbe1-2e9c9dd2a308'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/what-were-reading-204/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T14:17:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/503c7c74-9366-5a71-9fbc-ef2533c85042', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/503c7c74-9366-5a71-9fbc-ef2533c85042'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Greenstone, an M.I.T. economist, argues for a payroll-tax cut that benefits only those businesses that add employees.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/a-targeted-payroll-tax-cut/', 'snippet': 'Michael Greenstone, an M.I.T. economist, argues for a payroll-tax cut that benefits only those businesses that add employees.', 'lead_paragraph': '11:34 a.m. | Updated with today’s report on inflation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Targeted Payroll-Tax Cut', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Payroll Tax', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T14:20:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc453252-dcb2-5d65-8db0-7c9de5536f96', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc453252-dcb2-5d65-8db0-7c9de5536f96'}, {'abstract': 'Two new books uncover the murders, mysteries, and medicines of a forgotten Paris.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/murder-madness-cherchez-la-femme/', 'snippet': 'Two new books uncover the murders, mysteries, and medicines of a forgotten Paris.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the last hot weeks of the summer of 1933 settled over the working-class edges of Paris, a tall 18-year-old murderess, robed in a dark dress purchased hours before at an opulent department store, surveyed her family’s two-room apartment in the crowded Rue de Madagascar. The poison that she had fed her parents had by that time left her father dead and her mother unconscious. The girl stepped around their bodies as she searched the apartment for money, then opened the gas valve in the kitchen. After waiting for the gas to fill the air, she walked quietly out of the apartment to her neighbor’s door. “Help!” she called. “I think my parents have committed suicide!”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Murder? Madness? Cherchez la Femme', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hustvedt, Asti', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maza, Sarah', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Noziere, Violette', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Medical Muses: Hysteria in Nineteenth-Century Paris (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T14:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Kennedy Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'Kennedy', 'lastname': 'Jones', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72299a25-3eac-557c-a71b-e5c0bd093b52', 'word_count': 1010, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72299a25-3eac-557c-a71b-e5c0bd093b52'}, {'abstract': "The Internet is vast but it's also running out of addresses. Bettina Edelstein speaks with Times contributor Verne Kopytoff about IPv6, a successor to the IPv4 protocol that allows many more addresses to be created — around 340 undecillion. Who doesn't love free? J.D. Biersdorfer and Pedro Rafael Rosado chat about ways to find free books, music and movies on the Web. The news roundup includes a suit against Apple over the name iCloud; Apple changing In-App subscription policy; new console from Nintendo called the Wii U; and a report of defections from Facebook. J.D.'s tech tip, about quick answers from search engines for trigonometric functions, among other things.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2011/06/15/technology/15techtalk.html', 'snippet': "The Internet is vast but it's also running out of addresses. Bettina Edelstein speaks with Times contributor Verne Kopytoff about IPv6, a successor to the IPv4 protocol that allows many more addresses to be created — around 340 undecillion. Who do...", 'lead_paragraph': "The Internet is vast but it's also running out of addresses. Bettina Edelstein speaks with Times contributor Verne Kopytoff about IPv6, a successor to the IPv4 protocol that allows many more addresses to be created — around 340 undecillion. Who doesn't love free? J.D. Biersdorfer and Pedro Rafael Rosado chat about ways to find free books, music and movies on the Web. The news roundup includes a suit against Apple over the name iCloud; Apple changing In-App subscription policy; new console from Nintendo called the Wii U; and a report of defections from Facebook. J.D.'s tech tip, about quick answers from search engines for trigonometric functions, among other things.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Expanding the Internet; finding free music, books and movies; news.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T14:32:56+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/cda42fe7-d259-5a9e-aec7-e7f961a3b5cc', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/cda42fe7-d259-5a9e-aec7-e7f961a3b5cc'}, {'abstract': 'As Congress considers slashing the budget of the Commodity Futures Trading Commission, the agency’s head, Gary Gensler, fights for extra money to deal with a widening mandate.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/c-f-t-c-faces-more-budget-woes/', 'snippet': 'As Congress considers slashing the budget of the Commodity Futures Trading Commission, the agency’s head, Gary Gensler, fights for extra money to deal with a widening mandate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gary Gensler is fighting for funding — again. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'C.F.T.C. Faces More Budget Woes', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gensler, Gary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commodity Futures Trading Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T14:44:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/194fc88c-559e-5058-9c6b-82454af0c988', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/194fc88c-559e-5058-9c6b-82454af0c988'}, {'abstract': 'An author of a new evolutionary theory of why human reasoning developed, responding to readers’ comments, finds his theory to be widely misunderstood.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/researcher-responds-to-arguments-over-his-theory-of-arguing/', 'snippet': 'An author of a new evolutionary theory of why human reasoning developed, responding to readers’ comments, finds his theory to be widely misunderstood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An article posted on Tuesday about an academic theory proposing a new evolutionary explanation for the development of human reasoning has generated a lively debate among readers. One of the theory’s co-authors, Hugo Mercier, a researcher at the University of Pennsylvania, read through the first 200 or so online comments and wrote this brief response from London, where he is attending a conference.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Researcher Responds to Arguments Over His Theory of Arguing', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard University', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T14:49:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Mercier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mercier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6447cff-7a54-51f2-89ba-ffafd66fcdf7', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6447cff-7a54-51f2-89ba-ffafd66fcdf7'}, {'abstract': 'The Consumer Price Index was up 0.2 percent in May, compared with 0.4 percent in April. The monthly rise was the lowest since November, when the index was up 0.1 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/business/economy/16econ.html', 'snippet': 'The Consumer Price Index was up 0.2 percent in May, compared with 0.4 percent in April. The monthly rise was the lowest since November, when the index was up 0.1 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Consumer prices crept up last month at the slowest pace so far this year, tempered by a decline in energy prices, according to government figures released on Wednesday, while a regional report for New York suggested a decline in manufacturing activity and optimism.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'May Consumer Inflation Rose at a Slower Rate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'May Consumer Inflation Rose at a Slower Rate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Price Index', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T14:54:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5a708e7-7270-5150-8759-29ea2adc9050', 'word_count': 727, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5a708e7-7270-5150-8759-29ea2adc9050'}, {'abstract': 'State Representative David Simpson has sponsored what he calls an “antigroping” bill to ban airport screening searches that many feel have gone too far.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/us/16questions.html', 'snippet': 'State Representative David Simpson has sponsored what he calls an “antigroping” bill to ban airport screening searches that many feel have gone too far.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Texas House of Representatives approved a bill last month banning “intrusive” airport screening, but the legislation stalled in the State Senate after Washington threatened to consider halting flights to Texas if screeners were prevented from doing their jobs properly. The bill would have banned any pat-down that “touches the anus, sexual organ, buttocks or breast of another person including through the clothing, or touches the other person in a manner that would be offensive to a reasonable person.” The measure might be reintroduced in the current special session, where its backers say it might have enough votes this time to pass the Senate. Its sponsor, Representative David Simpson, a Republican from Longview, discusses. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/16/us/16questions/16questions-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'su