ewers.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/cable-channel-spots-new-stars/', 'snippet': 'The E! cable channel will air a half-hour show on Wednesday that looks at the cast members of four popular television commercials who are becoming increasingly familiar to viewers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A special on the E! cable channel on Wednesday night is devoted to the actors who are watched by the public a half-minute at a time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cable Channel ‘Spots’ New Stars', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'E! Entertainment Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T15:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d828f237-32b1-52cf-812f-48dc7ec0fa44', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d828f237-32b1-52cf-812f-48dc7ec0fa44'}, {'abstract': 'SoundCloud is gaining traction as a way for musicians to post their songs and for fans to discover them.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/soundcloud-an-audio-sharing-site-hits-5-million-users/', 'snippet': 'SoundCloud is gaining traction as a way for musicians to post their songs and for fans to discover them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the past, musicians who wanted to share their tracks online might have used MySpace Music. But SoundCloud, a popular audio-sharing service, is steadily gaining traction as a way for musicians to post their songs and for fans to discover them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'SoundCloud, an Audio-Sharing Site, Hits 5 Million Users', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SoundCloud', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T15:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c13e48fd-8c74-5dc6-a53f-08db9a0e131d', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c13e48fd-8c74-5dc6-a53f-08db9a0e131d'}, {'abstract': 'Many companies with publicly traded stock are expected to capitalize on the growth of Medicaid plans.', 'web_url': 'https://prescriptions.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/medicaid-managed-care-plans/', 'snippet': 'Many companies with publicly traded stock are expected to capitalize on the growth of Medicaid plans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As more states turn to health plans to deliver Medicaid services, a Commonwealth Fund report published on Wednesday analyzes the cost and quality among plans with publicly traded stock.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Medicaid Managed Care Plans', 'kicker': 'Prescriptions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commonwealth Fund', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T15:23:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Reed Abelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Reed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Abelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba7b9ccc-e60a-5db2-b677-f1a788efba75', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba7b9ccc-e60a-5db2-b677-f1a788efba75'}, {'abstract': 'If a prediction of a protracted downturn in solar activity holds up, there are big implications for climate policy.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/would-solar-lull-snuff-climate-action/', 'snippet': 'If a prediction of a protracted downturn in solar activity holds up, there are big implications for climate policy.', 'lead_paragraph': '[5:14 p.m. | Updated I’ve just posted a detailed new critique of the predicted sunspot lull by one of the country’s leading solar scientists.]According to news reports (and a news release) from a meeting of solar scientists in New Mexico, three separate lines of analysis point to decent prospects for a sustained downturn in solar activity, with important potential implications for Earth’s climate, and for climate policy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Would Solar Lull Snuff Climate Action?', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy and Power', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Solar Energy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN ASTRONOMICAL SOCIETY', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Cruces (NM)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T15:36:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de5666b1-4b2f-5315-88f2-f6f677b73f7b', 'word_count': 645, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de5666b1-4b2f-5315-88f2-f6f677b73f7b'}, {'abstract': 'U.N. officials said that there is a “there is a growing sense of panic” in the volatile Kordofan area, with aid convoys blocked, ethnic clashes and dozens dead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/world/africa/16sudan.html', 'snippet': 'U.N. officials said that there is a “there is a growing sense of panic” in the volatile Kordofan area, with aid convoys blocked, ethnic clashes and dozens dead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NAIROBI, Kenya — United Nations officials warned on Wednesday of “a growing sense of panic” in the volatile Kordofan area of central Sudan, with 60,000 people displaced, aid convoys blocked, ethnic clashes erupting and dozens dead — possibly including several United Nations workers. President Obama urged the Sudanese government to cease “its military actions immediately.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/16/world/SUDAN/SUDAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/16/world/SUDAN/SUDAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'ty