 — his first without his father. That is hard enough when the memories are mostly good ones. But when the memories are muddled, and there is a lingering feeling of what might have been, the day can take on a meaning not found in any Hallmark card. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Missing Dad', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alcohol Abuse', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Father's Day", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T15:43:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39e2c47b-5cc8-59a2-8de4-d59f9fa77071', 'word_count': 1170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39e2c47b-5cc8-59a2-8de4-d59f9fa77071'}, {'abstract': 'We’ve all been there; we’ve all fibbed our way through a “What have you read lately?” conversation at least once in our lifetime, replacing Tina Fey’s “Bossypants” with George Eliot’s “Middlemarch.” Mark O’Connell from The Millions describes this experience in his recent essay, aptly titled, “The Stockholm Syndrome Theory of Long Novels.”', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/the-stockholm-syndrome-theory-of-long-novels/', 'snippet': 'We’ve all been there; we’ve all fibbed our way through a “What have you read lately?” conversation at least once in our lifetime, replacing Tina Fey’s “Bossypants” with George Eliot’s “Middlemarch.” Mark O’Connell from The Millions describes this ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than a month ago, Dan Kois wrote a Riff on force-feeding yourself highbrow cinema. And while this essay is still reverberating loudly online, Kois also wrote a review last week for NPR of “The Pleasures of Reading in an Age of Distraction,” by Alan Jacobs, dealing with the literary equivalent of “cultural vegetables” and the much-bemoaned American affliction: reading with A.D.D.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stockholm Syndrome Theory of Long Novels', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T15:44:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yuri Chong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yuri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e926c887-b339-5646-b4d0-09322860205c', 'word_count': 346, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e926c887-b339-5646-b4d0-09322860205c'}, {'abstract': 'Peñarol of Uruguay and Santos of Brazil open the home-and-away Copa Libertadores finals Wednesday night in Montevideo.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/copa-libertadores-final-penarol-vs-santos/', 'snippet': 'Peñarol of Uruguay and Santos of Brazil open the home-and-away Copa Libertadores finals Wednesday night in Montevideo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been a long, long time since the Estadio Centenario in Montevideo, Uruguay, the site of the first World Cup final in 1930, has hosted a match of such significance (and that would probably be the 1995 Copa América final won by the hosts against Brazil).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Copa Libertadores Finals: Peñarol vs. Santos', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo':