tage print and update it, substituting a sun bleached, watercolor Ikat for the familiar 60’s pattern. There are other innovations in this rich, Euro-looking collection, including skinny mod suits, and hippie sundresses, with a fabulous use of zebra accents on belts, bags and blazers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Emilio Pucci Resort', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T18:45:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ede03b3d-4bd0-5076-904f-0cc89c3b794c', 'word_count': 62, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ede03b3d-4bd0-5076-904f-0cc89c3b794c'}, {'abstract': 'With the cost of fuel on the rise again, some hotels are giving guests a break with gas cards.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/at-some-hotels-helping-you-fill-the-tank/', 'snippet': 'With the cost of fuel on the rise again, some hotels are giving guests a break with gas cards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the cost of gas on the rise again, some hotels this summer are doing their part to help ease the crunch for guests. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Some Hotels, Helping You Fill the Tank', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MasterCard Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boulder (Colo)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T18:47:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shivani Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/718d7e10-75ca-562f-918f-014990c6abb6', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/718d7e10-75ca-562f-918f-014990c6abb6'}, {'abstract': '“America’s Got Talent” draws the night’s biggest audience.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/viewers-shun-reruns-for-nbc-reality/', 'snippet': '“America’s Got Talent” draws the night’s biggest audience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a night of mostly reruns, new episodes of “America’s Got Talent” and “The Voice” lifted NBC to No. 1 in the ratings on Tuesday. According to Nielsen’s estimates “America’s Got Talent” delivered the night’s largest audience with 12.3 million viewers at 8 p.m. although “The Voice,” which drew 12.1 million from 9 to 11, attracted higher ratings in the demographic of viewers 18 to 49. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Viewers Shun Reruns for NBC Reality', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen Media Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T18:47:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccb49338-1aa1-5f63-8d66-23c5adc45909', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccb49338-1aa1-5f63-8d66-23c5adc45909'}, {'abstract': 'Governing Class: Outfitted with sleeping bags and stamina, protesters vow to sleep outside City Hall until Mayor Bloomberg’s budget cuts are reversed.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/in-bloombergville-budget-protesters-sleep-in/', 'snippet': 'Governing Class: Outfitted with sleeping bags and stamina, protesters vow to sleep outside City Hall until Mayor Bloomberg’s budget cuts are reversed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So it may not be quite on the scale of the so-called Walkerville sit-in in Wisconsin, or the protests in Tahrir Square in Cairo. Nor will it remind anyone of the anti-apartheid shantytowns on college campuses in the 1980s, or even, on a much less serious note, the legions of Duke students who regularly camp out in a makeshift tent city, known as Krzyzewskiville, before college basketball games.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In ‘Bloombergville,’ Budget Protesters Sleep In', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T18:47:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b406a31-1efc-5e1e-8002-b1197ce59fc1', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b406a31-1efc-5e1e-8002-b1197ce59fc1'}, {'abstract': 'A sixth book, another job in television and other opportunities are on the way for the fitness guru Jillian Michaels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/fashion/jillian-michaels-is-fit-for-new-challenges-after-the-biggest-loser.html', 'snippet': 'A sixth book, another job in television and other opportunities are on the way for the fitness guru Jillian Michaels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON a Tuesday night in April, Jillian Michaels, the fitness guru, sat down on a table at the Borders store in Columbus Circle and kicked off shiny beige Christian Louboutin pumps, revealing a blue pedicure. She was faced by a snaking line of hundreds, mostly women and teenage girls (but also a few potbellied men) clutching her sixth book, “Unlimited: How to Build an Exceptional Life.” Periodically, a giddy squeal would erupt from the crowd.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/16/fashion/Z-JILLIAN-P1-3/Z-JILLIAN-P1-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/16/fashion/Z-JILLIAN-P1-3/Z-JILLIAN-P1-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/16/fashion/Z-JILLIAN-P1-3/Z-JILLIAN-P1-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/16/fashion/Z-JILLIAN-P1-3/Z-JILLIAN-P1-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/16/fashion/Z-JILLIAN-P1-3/Z-JILLIAN-P1-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fit for Life’s Challenges', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fit for Life’s Challenges', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Michaels, Jillian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T18:47:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Saint Louis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': 'Saint', 'lastname': 'Louis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35cec9c3-c659-59b2-866c-ce8e31ce5c29', 'word_count': 1929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35cec9c3-c659-59b2-866c-ce8e31ce5c29'}, {'abstract': '“My Baby Blue Jays” chronicles a family of birds living on the author’s balcony; and “How Things Work in the Ward” explains the everyday mysteries of acorns, dandelions, rocks and dirt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/books/review/childrens-books-picture-books-about-the-backyard.html', 'snippet': '“My Baby Blue Jays” chronicles a family of birds living on the author’s balcony; and “How Things Work in the Ward” explains the everyday mysteries of acorns, dandelions, rocks and dirt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is sheer speculation to imagine that John Berendt, the best-selling author of “Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil,” has writer’s block (his last book was 2005’s “City of Falling Angels”), but if he does, what greater serendipity than to have a family of birds alight and make their home on his New York City balcony? If nothing else, it’s perfect fodder for Berendt’s first children’s book.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/15/books/review/15childrens-1/15childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 249, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/15/books/review/15childrens-1/15childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 249}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/15/books/review/15childrens-1/15childrens-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/15/books/review/15childrens-1/15childrens-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/15/books/review/15childrens-1/15childrens-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Picture Books About the Backyard', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T18:48:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 