, J Courtney', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T22:35:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susannah Meadows', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susannah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meadows', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ac7a6ad-d2a2-57c7-9502-4f2fe9b58483', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ac7a6ad-d2a2-57c7-9502-4f2fe9b58483'}, {'abstract': 'A closer look at America’s winter tomato capital.', 'web_url': 'https://bittman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/everything-you-ever-wanted-to-know-about-immokalee-fla/', 'snippet': 'A closer look at America’s winter tomato capital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fifty years after Edward R. Murrow concluded his tenure at CBS with “Harvest of Shame,” an arresting expose of migrant field workers in rural Florida, CBS News correspondent Byron Pitts returned to Florida’s tomato, bean and sugarcane fields for an update. In Harvest of Shame: 50 Years Later, Mr. Pitts found that, while wages have improved, the work — when it could be found — is just as backbreaking as it was in 1960. Juan Lopez, a 62-year-old tomato picker in Immokalee, said he gets up before dawn hoping to find work; in 2009 he said he earned “$7,800 for a full year of work.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Everything You Ever Wanted to Know About Immokalee, Fla.', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Farmers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Migrant and Foreign Workers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seminole Indians', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tomatoes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murrow, Edward R', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Coalition of Immokalee Workers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taco Bell Corp', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yum Brands Inc', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T22:38:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Mascia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mascia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5efd24dc-7411-5f25-9c16-372df3fc3f40', 'word_count': 834, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5efd24dc-7411-5f25-9c16-372df3fc3f40'}, {'abstract': 'A couple combined their building with an adjacent one to create a modern spin on the Victorian-era town house.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/06/15/greathomesanddestinations/20110616_sydney.html', 'snippet': 'A couple combined their building with an adjacent one to create a modern spin on the Victorian-era town house.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A couple combined their building with an adjacent one to create a modern spin on the Victorian-era town house.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Sydney, Finding Room to Grow', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sydney (Australia)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {