izations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T22:52:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71ce3f45-c481-592f-bb6e-4242c10d006c', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71ce3f45-c481-592f-bb6e-4242c10d006c'}, {'abstract': 'A new free tool for consumers from Qualys called BrowserCheck makes sure your browser (and its plug-ins) are as up-to-date and protected from malware as possible.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/a-tool-to-help-secure-your-browser/', 'snippet': 'A new free tool for consumers from Qualys called BrowserCheck makes sure your browser (and its plug-ins) are as up-to-date and protected from malware as possible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Web browsers are top targets for hackers. They’re abused in more than 90 percent of attacks meant to hit random Internet users (versus more targeted strikes), according to Kaspersky Labs. And more than half of those attacks exploit flaws in browser software and seek to install dangerous malware on computers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tool to Help Secure Your Browser', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Malware', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hackers (Computers)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web Browsers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', '