'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/16/garden/16garden-span/16garden-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sowing Enchantment', 'kicker': 'In the Garden', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sowing Enchantment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fry, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T23:06:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Wadler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wadler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ddf28b8-9d42-5ad9-a97d-00500d07157a', 'word_count': 1554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ddf28b8-9d42-5ad9-a97d-00500d07157a'}, {'abstract': 'As horrified and shaken as I am by Barry Bearak’s article “Watching the Murder of an Innocent Man,” I am also grateful for it., a reader writes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/a-murder-in-diepsloot.html', 'snippet': 'As horrified and shaken as I am by Barry Bearak’s article “Watching the Murder of an Innocent Man,” I am also grateful for it., a reader writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As horrified and shaken as I am by Barry Bearak’s article “Watching the Murder of an Innocent Man,” I am also grateful for it. We are too often inured to what we do not ourselves confront, and we need to be shaken from our complacency. Some of the comments take the familiar tack of dehumanizing the “other,” of casting the murder of this man as an act committed by “savages.” Well, as others have noted, we all have the potential to be savages, all of us. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Murder in Diepsloot', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A MURDER IN DIEPSLOOT', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T23:10:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac0f1a5f-cbf1-5844-ba5e-44d6d34cc36a', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac0f1a5f-cbf1-5844-ba5e-44d6d34cc36a'}, {'abstract': 'A sunglasses expert gives tips for selecting sunglasses that fit your face properly.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/15/the-hunt-for-the-right-shades/', 'snippet': 'A sunglasses expert gives tips for selecting sunglasses that fit your face properly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I thought about buying a pair of round sunglasses this summer. I pictured myself wearing sphere-shaped tortoiseshell shades with bright red lipstick for the entire summer, until I changed my look again for fall. But something has held me back from running to the nearest optical boutique with debit card in hand. Fear, rooted in vanity, had me questioning whether round frames are the look for me.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hunt for the Right Shades', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sunglasses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T23:19:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simone S. Oliver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simone', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Oliver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8194b86-f93c-50e2-9c3d-73d0aee4e6db', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8194b86-f93c-50e2-9c3d-73d0aee4e6db'}, {'abstract': 'A reader’s response to Bill Keller’s column, “Let Me Take Off My Tinfoil Hat for a Moment.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/first.html', 'snippet': 'A reader’s response to Bill Keller’s column, “Let Me Take Off My Tinfoil Hat for a Moment.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Let Me Take Off My Tinfoil Hat for a Moment,” Bill Keller muses on conspiracy theories and discusses “why otherwise smart people fall for crackpot conspiracy theories.” I read the article to see if Keller would include in his examples how in reporting the events leading to the Iraq war, “otherwise smart people” at The Times fell for the malicious crackpot conspiracy theory of the Bush-Cheney administration that Iraq possessed weapons of mass destruction that threatened the United States. But alas, no. Millions of people here and abroad, though, saw through this particularly wacky notion, the consequences of which, in deaths, displacements and destruction, are far worse than the consequences of some poor guy believing he has the key to the J.F.K. assassination. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'First', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'FIRST', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keller, Bill', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-15T23:25:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1d