, Mason', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T01:12:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7181fd54-8424-563b-92d8-b50329693334', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7181fd54-8424-563b-92d8-b50329693334'}, {'abstract': 'A new center at Ellis Island plans to tell the full story of the teeming masses that came to this country, including many missing tales of American history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/opinion/16thu4.html', 'snippet': 'A new center at Ellis Island plans to tell the full story of the teeming masses that came to this country, including many missing tales of American history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Border-guarding Minutemen and immigrant-pummeling politicians should get a grip on this country’s history at the Ellis Island Immigration Museum in New York Harbor. Exhibits are planned to tell the full story of the teeming masses, including African slaves, displaced Indians, modern globetrotters and Know-Nothing nativists. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Untold Stories of How Everyone Got Here', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Untold Stories of How Everyone Got Here', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ellis Island (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ellis Island Immigration Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T01:12:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/bad0e6f2-0960-5cd1-b5b3-0bd6f7aa1609', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bad0e6f2-0960-5cd1-b5b3-0bd6f7aa1609'}, {'abstract': 'While details remain scarce, there are new signs of progress as owners and players have their “noses to the grindstone” to reach a deal perhaps by July 4.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/sports/football/latest-nfl-labor-talks-evoke-cautious-optimism.html', 'snippet': 'While details remain scarce, there are new signs of progress as owners and players have their “noses to the grindstone” to reach a deal perhaps by July 4.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this month, as the prospect of a shortened N.F.L. season loomed, so did the pessimism about the state of labor negotiations as owners and players awaited a critical court ruling in their fight over how to divide billions of dollars.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Labor Talks Offer Signs of Optimism for the N.F.L.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'N.F.L. Talks Offering Signs Of Optimism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T01:14:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c8e3767-e155-5b10-a29a-62