ument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Viera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/445ea3d6-9795-56fd-b0f6-8407a84be1c3', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/445ea3d6-9795-56fd-b0f6-8407a84be1c3'}, {'abstract': 'Proposed legislation shows small but consequential steps that lawmakers from both parties think they might accomplish.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/us/politics/16immig.html', 'snippet': 'Proposed legislation shows small but consequential steps that lawmakers from both parties think they might accomplish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — For many lawmakers in Congress, the immigration issue has become too hot to touch, especially with presidential campaigns starting up and voters, burdened with persistent unemployment, in a volatile mood. But competing bills introduced this week, one by Republicans and another by Democrats, show small but consequential steps on immigration that leaders on both sides think they might accomplish in coming months, before electoral politics overwhelm the debate.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Separate Bills Focus on Two Pieces of Immigration Puzzle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Separate Bills Focus on Two Pieces of Immigration Puzzle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Lamar S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Migrant and Foreign Workers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lofgren, Zoe', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T02:25:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e24f0b48-2d0f-54bf-a0cf-64bc2be69d61', 'word_count': 784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e24f0b48-2d0f-54bf-a0cf-64bc2be69d61'}, {'abstract': 'Several unions filed a federal lawsuit on Wednesday to block a law that limits collective bargaining rights for public workers in the state.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/us/16brfs-Wisconsin.html', 'snippet': 'Several unions filed a federal lawsuit on Wednesday to block a law that limits collective bargaining rights for public workers in the state.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Several unions filed a federal lawsuit on Wednesday to block a law that limits collective bargaining rights for public workers in the state. A day earlier, the Wisconsin Supreme Court reinstated the law, overruling a lower court’s decision to halt it because it had been passed improperly. The unions, which represent teachers and state and city workers, say provisions of the law violate the Constitution by singling out only some public workers and not others. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wisconsin: Law Curbing Unions Is Back in Court', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | MIDWEST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wisconsin: Law Curbing Unions Is Back in Court', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collective Bargaining', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T02:26:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emma G. Fitzsimmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Fitzsimmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8deabc33-5687-5509-84fe-51f09e86930c', 'word_count': 75, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8deabc33-5687-5509-84fe-51f09e86930c'}, {'abstract': 'The former speaker of the State House was convicted of conspiracy, extortion and fraud for helping award a state contract to a software company in exchange for kickbacks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/us/16brfs-Massachusetts.html', 'snippet': 'The former speaker of the State House was convicted of conspiracy, extortion and fraud for helping award a state contract to a software company in exchange for kickbacks.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The former speaker of the State House was convicted Wednesday of conspiracy, extortion and fraud for helping award a lucrative state contract to a software company in exchange for kickbacks. The former speaker, Salvatore F. DiMasi, 65, of Boston, was convicted on seven of nine counts and faces up to 20 years in prison. Prosecutors said that Mr. DiMasi lobbied Gov. Deval Patrick and other state officials to steer $17.5 million in contracts toward the company, Cognos. Mr. DiMasi received $65,000 over two years and worked with two co-conspirators, the authorities said. Sentencing is scheduled for Aug. 18. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Massachusetts: Ex-Speaker Guilty of Taking Kickbacks', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | NEW ENGLAND', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Massachusetts: Ex-Speaker Guilty of Taking Kickbacks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DiMasi, Salvatore F', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cognos Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T02:28:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Zezima', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zezima', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc3122c8-1e96-5794-a9f4-569789233c6b', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc3122c8-1e96-5794-a9f4-569789233c6b'}, {'abstract': 'A supporter of Pfc. Bradley E. Manning, who is accused of leaking hundreds of thousands of documents to WikiLeaks, was called before a federal grand jury in Alexandria, Va.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/us/politics/16brfs-Washington.html', 'snippet': 'A supporter of Pfc. Bradley E. Manning, who is accused of leaking hundreds of thousands of documents to WikiLeaks, was called before a federal grand jury in Alexandria, Va.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A supporter of Pfc. Bradley E. Manning, who is accused of leaking hundreds of thousands of documents to WikiLeaks, was called before a federal grand jury in Alexandria, Va., on Wednesday, but he said he declined to answer any questions. The supporter, David M. House, a freelance computer scientist, said he invoked his Fifth Amendment right against self-incrimination, because he believes the Justice Department is “creating a climate of fear around WikiLeaks and the Bradley Manning support network.” The grand jury inquiry is separate from the military prosecution of Private Manning and is believed to be exploring whether the WikiLeaks founder, Julian Assange, or others in the group violated the law by acquiring and publishing military and State Department documents. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Supporter of Leak Suspect Is Called Before Grand Jury', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WASHINGTON', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Supporter of Leak Suspect Is Called Before Grand Jury', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manning, Bradley E', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fifth Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classified Information and State Secrets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jury System', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WikiLeaks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T02:34:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Shane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/876b0be3-6767-5a09-bb45-4393354d5c3d', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/876b0be3-6767-5a09-bb45-4393354d5c3d'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Windham was a chronicler of Southern life, from hoop snakes to ghosts, in books, recipes and commentaries on public radio.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/books/kathryn-tucker-windham-southern-storyteller-dies-at-93.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Windham was a chronicler of Southern life, from hoop snakes to ghosts, in books, recipes and commentaries on public radio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kathryn Tucker Windham, who wove a tapestry of Southern folklore, bucolic scenes, reminiscences, cherished recipes and ghost stories through books and commentaries on public radio, died Sunday at her home in Selma, Ala. She was 93. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/16/us/16windham/16windham-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 759, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/16/us/16windham/16windham-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/16/us/16windham/16windham-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kathryn T. Windham, a Storyteller of the South, Dies at 93', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kathryn T. Windham, 93, A Storyteller of the South', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T02:46:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Hevesi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hevesi', 'qualifier': Non