 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T22:08:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d18cf759-9fda-5dc8-9f57-839f8d7eab39', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d18cf759-9fda-5dc8-9f57-839f8d7eab39'}, {'abstract': 'The singer-songwriter Meshell Ndegeocello brought her own identity to a show of Prince covers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/arts/music/meshell-ndegeocello-covering-prince-review.html', 'snippet': 'The singer-songwriter Meshell Ndegeocello brought her own identity to a show of Prince covers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Meshell Ndegeocello outlined a catalog of social and natural disasters at one point in her sold-out show at the Highline Ballroom on Wednesday night. “You turn on the telly and every other story/Is tellin’ you somebody died,” she sang in her deep-shadow vocal register, a notch or two above a murmur. The tune was “Sign o’ the Times,” by Prince, rearranged for upright bass, pinging keyboards and hazy rhythm. The overhaul affected everything but melody and lyrics, amplifying the bitterness in the song. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/arts/17meshell-span/17meshell-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/17/arts/17meshell-span/17meshell-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 356}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/arts/17meshell-span/SUB-MESHELL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/arts/17meshell-span/17meshell-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/17/arts/17meshell-span/17meshell-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Songs Of Prince, Faithfully Reimagined', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Songs Of Prince, Faithfully Reimagined', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ndegeocello, Meshell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gospel Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Highline Ballroom', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T22:08:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/29adca1e-5fae-5dc1-b4dc-fe44626d7bc3', 'word_count': 537, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29adca1e-5fae-5dc1-b4dc-fe44626d7bc3'}, {'abstract': '“Those of us who remember Bill Simmons from his Boston Sports Guy days can’t possibly love him as much now as we did then,” a reader writes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/espns-bill-simmons.html', 'snippet': '“Those of us who remember Bill Simmons from his Boston Sports Guy days can’t possibly love him as much now as we did then,” a reader writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Those of us who remember Bill Simmons from his Boston Sports Guy days can’t possibly love him as much now as we did then. Back then, he really was one of us — just another fan — and his fatalistic tone was perfect for the awful Boston sports scene of the late ’90s and very early 2000s. Jonathan Mahler would have done well to mention that the unprecedented success of Boston sports franchises over the last decade did a lot to boost Simmons’s profile, particularly in the mid-2000s; I wonder whether a Cleveland Sports Guy or a Buffalo Sports Guy would have had the same success. In fact, I wonder whether a Cleveland Sports Guy, just as witty and clever as Simmons, has been writing for a tiny audience for years and is seething about living in a town that has arguably the most depressing sports scene in America.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ESPN’s Bill Simmons', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "ESPN'S BILL SIMMONS", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ESPN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simmons, Bill (1969- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T22:09:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0ff3c1c-039f-5596-a90a-381ccfa59c1c', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0ff3c1c-039f-5596-a90a-381ccfa59c1c'}, {'abstract': 'Susan Rethorst presents two new works from her “208 East Broadway” series, named after the address of the apartment that she will soon be vacating.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/arts/dance/susan-rethorst-retrointrospective-at-danspace-project-review.html', 'snippet': 'Susan Rethorst presents two new works from her “208 East Broadway” series, named after the address of the apartment that she will soon be vacating.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During four weeks this spring at Danspace Project, the choreographer Melinda Ring has redefined the organization’s Platform series by paying homage to a single and singular dance artist: Susan Rethorst. This weekend, “Susan Rethorst: Retro(intro)spective,” programmed by Ms. Ring, concludes the series with two new works — “208 East Broadway Part 3: Over and Out,” which was shown for the first time on Tuesday evening and “208 East Broadway Part 4: Sold,” a performance installation that will be presented on Saturday afternoon.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/arts/RETHORST-1/RETHORST-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 853, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Moving On Simply by Moving Out', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Moving On Simply by Moving Out', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Danspace Project', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rethorst, Susan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T22:09:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a57a98c9-2796-568e-88a6-385926a768cd', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a57a98c9-2796-568e-88a6-385926a768cd'}, {'abstract': 'Toyota expects production at its North American plants to return to pre-quake levels in September.', 'web_url': 'http