emporary Performance at Mannes College the New School for Music.', 'lead_paragraph': '“My only intention is to have no intentions,” Schoenberg wrote in 1909, the year he composed his landmark Three Piano Pieces (Op. 11), “no formal, architectural or other artistic intentions (except perhaps of capturing the mood of a poem), no aesthetic intentions.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/arts/INSTITUTE-1/INSTITUTE-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 939, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Piano Tour of Schoenberg’s Career', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Piano Tour of Schoenberg’s Career', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sherman, Russell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mannes College of Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T22:11:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/03db5eed-472b-5ce5-9d35-f4f2a49d41b4', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03db5eed-472b-5ce5-9d35-f4f2a49d41b4'}, {'abstract': 'The Chelsea Music Festival, in its second year, promised Mahler, but also provided Masur, in the form of Ken-David and Melinda Lee Masur, artistic directors of the series. The emphasis was on youthful players.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/arts/music/chelsea-music-festival-plays-mahler-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Chelsea Music Festival, in its second year, promised Mahler, but also provided Masur, in the form of Ken-David and Melinda Lee Masur, artistic directors of the series. The emphasis was on youthful players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How often do you encounter a program of chamber music named for Mahler? Only one Mahler chamber work has survived in the literature; or rather, part of a work. What we call the Piano Quartet in A minor — written around 1876, when Mahler was 16 — was intended as the first movement of a larger piece, otherwise represented by only 24 bars of a scherzo.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/arts/MUSIC/MUSIC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Mahler’s Chamber, a Radical Departure', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Mahler’s Chamber, a Radical Departure', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mahler, Gustav', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baeck, Leo, Institute', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T22:12:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c623c49-908e-5fbf-8d9b-f98a40c95c63', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c623c49-908e-5fbf-8d9b-f98a40c95c63'}, {'abstract': 'At last, there was a dignified moment in the decline and fall of the New York congressman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/opinion/17fri4.html', 'snippet': 'At last, there was a dignified moment in the decline and fall of the New York congressman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At last, there was a dignified moment in the decline and fall of Anthony Weiner.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Anthony Weiner Resigns', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Anthony Weiner Resigns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Anthony D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T22:12:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b876b922-f612-5fe6-8667-5c023d48c271', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b876b922-f612-5fe6-8667-5c023d48c271'}, {'abstract': 'Norah O’Donnell, the long-time Washington-based correspondent for NBC News, will join CBS News as its new chief White House correspondent.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/norah-odonnell-leaves-nbc-for-cbs/', 'snippet': 'Norah O’Donnell, the long-time Washington-based correspondent for NBC News, will join CBS News as its new chief White House correspondent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CBS announced Thursday that it had hired Norah O’Donnell, the long-time Washington-based correspondent for NBC News, as its new chief White House correspondent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Norah O’Donnell Leaves NBC for CBS', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T22:14:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72f143e9-46cb-53eb-abba-0b6d2d8e8fd5', 'word_count': 113, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72f143e9-46cb-53eb-abba-0b6d2d8e8fd5'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Pawlenty, the former Minnesota governor, took to Twitter on Thursday to poke fun at himself and also to tweak his main rival for the Republican presidential nomination.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/pawlenty-goes-after-romney-on-twitter/', 'snippet': 'Tim Pawlenty, the former Minnesota governor, took to Twitter on Thursday to poke fun at himself and also to tweak his main rival for the Republican presidential nomination.', 'lead_paragraph': '9:39 p.m. | Updated Four days after declining to criticize Mitt Romney, the former governor of Massachusetts, during a Republican presidential debate, Tim Pawlenty acknowledged his flub in a Twitter message on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pawlenty Goes After Romney', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pawlenty, Tim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-16T22:17:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e946b26-7dc9-576c-9b49-77deb604881f', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e946b26-7dc9-576c-9b49-77deb604881f'}, {'abstract': '“We knew from the beginning we wanted to marry each other,” the bridegroom said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/fashion/weddings/andrea-catsimatidis-and-christopher-cox-weddings.html', 'snippet': '“We knew from the beginning we wanted to marry each other,” the bridegroom said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RICHARD NIXON could never have scripted it — that it would take a Democrat to help his avowedly Republican oldest grandson fall head over heels in love.\xa0Yet that is what fate had in store in April 2008 for Christopher Nixon Cox, then 29 and working for Senator John McCain’s presidential campaign.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/fashion/weddings/VOWS-1/VOWS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/fashion/weddings/VOWS-1/VOWS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/fashion/weddings/VOWS-1/VOWS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jum