rl': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/nyregion/same-sex-marriage-opponents-frustrated-in-ny-lobbying.html', 'snippet': 'With the fight for same-sex marriage close, ministers and others against the legislation are denied the kind of access Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg got to the G.O.P. caucus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALBANY — The Rev. Duane R. Motley, a Baptist minister, stood in a marble hallway in the Capitol on Thursday, frustrated. Behind the closed door, all of the Senate Republicans were meeting with one of the state’s most prominent supporters of same-sex marriage, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg. But Mr. Motley, a leading opponent of same-sex marriage, could not get the same audience.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/nyregion/SUB-OPPOSE/SUB-OPPOSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/17/nyregion/SUB-OPPOSE/SUB-OPPOSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/nyregion/SUB-OPPOSE/SUB-OPPOSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/nyregion/SUB-OPPOSE/SUB-OPPOSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/17/nyregion/SUB-OPPOSE/SUB-OPPOSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Same-Sex Marriage Opponents Frustrated in N.Y. Lobbying', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Opponents Frustrated as Other Side Gets Lawmakers’ Ears', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T01:04:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98d26a0a-679c-5e7c-af21-f4d1d1bfd3ad', 'word_count': 766, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98d26a0a-679c-5e7c-af21-f4d1d1bfd3ad'}, {'abstract': 'Why are some Christians “certain” that the rapture will occur, even thought they don’t know it in the scientific sense?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/16/epistemology-and-the-end-of-the-world/', 'snippet': 'Why are some Christians “certain” that the rapture will occur, even thought they don’t know it in the scientific sense?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the coming weeks, The Stone will feature occasional posts by Gary Gutting, a professor of philosophy at the University of Notre Dame, that apply critical thinking to information and events that have appeared in the news.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Epistemology and the End of the World', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T01:07:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Gutting', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gutting', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abd7e6c4-c5bd-5e3f-85ed-231eb668bb59', 'word_count': 839, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abd7e6c4-c5bd-5e3f-85ed-231eb668bb59'}, {'abstract': 'Storms on the sun can have a significant impact on life on Earth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/opinion/17baker.html', 'snippet': 'Storms on the sun can have a significant impact on life on Earth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LATELY, the Sun has been behaving a bit strangely. In 2008 and 2009, it showed the least surface activity in nearly a century. Solar flare activity stopped cold and weeks and months went by without any sunspots, or areas of intense magnetism. Quiet spells are normal for the Sun, but researchers alive today had never seen anything like that two-year hibernation. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/opinion/17oped-img/17oped-img-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 418, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/17/opinion/17oped-img/17oped-img-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 418}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/opinion/17oped-img/17oped-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/17/opinion/17oped-img/17oped-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images