s resignation to an audibly hostile audience on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From Today’s Times:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Heckled Out', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T10:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emmarie Huetteman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emmarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huetteman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2967cb83-25d9-545c-ab72-2e2fe45dcf2c', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2967cb83-25d9-545c-ab72-2e2fe45dcf2c'}, {'abstract': 'A century’s worth of scooter designs will come on view at the Los Angeles museum on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/at-petersen-automotive-museum-a-salute-to-the-scoot/', 'snippet': 'A century’s worth of scooter designs will come on view at the Los Angeles museum on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The image of scooters often includes the cool (think Gregory Peck and Audrey Hepburn in “Roman Holiday”) and the quirky (almost everyone else), and now the entire spectrum is on display in Los Angeles. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Petersen Automotive Museum, a Salute to the Scoot', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Petersen Automotive Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Piaggio SpA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T10:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Kurczewski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurczewski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e2c1e5e-6b21-52ad-a174-5690c188525f', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e2c1e5e-6b21-52ad-a174-5690c188525f'}, {'abstract': 'A conversation with Diane Coyle, a British economist and the author of  “The Economics of Enough.”', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/making-choices-as-if-the-future-matters/', 'snippet': 'A conversation with Diane Coyle, a British economist and the author of  “The Economics of Enough.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Diane Coyle is a British economist who blogs at The Enlightened Economist and is an active Twitter user. Her previous book, “The Soulful Science,” examined the revival of economics as a field relevant to the rest of us, and her latest book is called “The Economics of Enough.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Making Choices ‘as if the Future Matters’', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coyle, Diane', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T10:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9189972f-db7a-5a1d-ba67-27c7de94af29', 'word_count': 1387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9189972f-db7a-5a1d-ba67-27c7de94af29'}, {'abstract': 'The Musée Jaquemart-André in Paris has now brought work of the Caillebotte brothers together in an exhibition, running through July 11, that offers a glimpse into the world of the Parisian haute bourgeoisie during the cusp of the 20th century.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/paris-show-spotlights-caillebotte-brothers/', 'snippet': 'The Musée Jaquemart-André in Paris has now brought work of the Caillebotte brothers together in an exhibition, running through July 11, that offers a glimpse into the world of the Parisian haute bourgeoisie during the cusp of the 20th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gustave and Martial Caillebotte, wealthy men about town, shared lodgings on the Boulevard Haussmann in the fashionable, newly-built Eighth Arrondissement of fin-de-siècle Paris. From there, Martial, an enthusiastic amateur photographer, and his brother, a dilettante painter of exceptional talent, observed and chronicled the daily life of the belle époque.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Show Spotlights Caillebotte Brothers', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T10:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia Barbieri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbieri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7dadcb4-709e-52fb-9c55-cb1bfe7786df', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7dadcb4-709e-52fb-9c55-cb1bfe7786df'}, {'abstract': 'Syrian security forces were coming nearer to makeshift refugee camps lining the border with Turkey.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/18border.html', 'snippet': 'Syrian security forces were coming nearer to makeshift refugee camps lining the border with Turkey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AIN al-BEIDA, Syria — Final exams were canceled in this farming village, but the concrete schoolhouse is full. The students have been replaced by anxious families seeking shelter from a violent crackdown on antigovernment protesters that has spread in recent weeks here to the rural northwestern corner of Syria.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/border/border-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/border/border-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/border/border-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/border/border-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/border/border-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Syrian Town Is Strained by Flood of Refugees', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Syrian Town Is Strained By Flood Of Refugees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T10:03:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liam Stack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41c8821a-69fd-5462-8325-78df2ec45bc6', 'word_count': 930, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41c8821a-69fd-5462-8325-78df2ec45bc6'}, {'abstract': 'The ability of search engines to calculate basic arithmetic right in the search box is well known, but some can handle higher math as well.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/tip-of-the-week-using-search-engines-for-higher-math/', 'snippet': 'The ability of search engines to calculate basic arithmetic right in the search box is well known, but some can handle higher math as well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ability of search engines to calculate basic arithmetic right in the search box is well known, but some can handle higher math as well. Both Bing and Google can perform trigonometric functions like sine, cosine and tanget when the sin, cos and tan operators are typed into the search box. For example, typing “sin 45 degrees” and hitting the Enter key brings a result of 0.707107 — with no digging around for a calculator. Microsoft has a full list of mathematical operations that work with Bing here; a similar list for Google is here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip of the Week: Using Search Engines for Higher Math', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search Engines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T10:05:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d47f0a88-a4cb-5bef-b96f-b81cc1ed3778', 'word_count': 95, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d47f0a88-a4cb-5bef-b96f-b81cc1ed3778'}, {'abstract': 'After Margot Owett received a postcard in the mail that was postmarked 1912, City Room explores if it had really taken 99 years for it to reach its destination.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/a-postcard-from-1912-returns-to-brooklyn/', 'snippet': 'After Margot Owett received a postcard in the mail that was postmarked 1912, City Room explores if it had really taken 99 years for it to reach its destination.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Postcard From 1912 Returns to Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bar Codes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Post Offices and Equipment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Postal Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Postcards', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brookhaven (NY)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cobble Hill (NYC)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T10:51:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elissa Gootman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gootman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9f60a07-9482-572a-9096-7ff2dce46bc7', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9f60a07-9482-572a-9096-7ff2dce46bc7'}, {'abstract': 'Vacation or no vacation, that is the question. I have three different answers, depending on your situation.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/fathers-day-and-the-vacation-dilemma/', 'snippet': 'Vacation or no vacation, that is the question. I have three different answers, depending on your situation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A widely quoted recent study done by American Express says that only 46 percent of business owners are planning to take a vacation this year — down from 67 percent in 2006. Given the economy, gas prices and rising airfares, this probably will surprise few business owners. For entrepreneurs, the decision of whether to take a vacation can be complicated and gut-wrenching.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Father’s Day and the Vacation Dilemma', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Father's Day", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paid Time Off', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T11:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Goltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f03f123f-e63a-5db3-b3c8-349a98e7d945', 'word_count': 912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f03f123f-e63a-5db3-b3c8-349a98e7d945'}, {'abstract': 'Shopping on mobile phones is expected to increase significantly, but continue to remain a relatively small percentage of overall sales, according to a report from Forrester Research.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/mobile-shopping-set-to-spike-says-forrester/', 'snippet': 'Shopping on mobile phones is expected to increase significantly, but continue to remain a relatively small percentage of overall sales, according to a report from Forrester Research.', 'lead_paragraph': '6:40 p.m. | Updated  to reflect that the graphic above refers to billions of dollars, not millions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mobile Shopping Set to Spike, Says Forrester', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T11:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Brustein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brustein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/643f2927-b97c-5019-bb8e-d86dcca4f734', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/643f2927-b97c-5019-bb8e-d86dcca4f734'}, {'abstract': 'In the center of Beirut, one man waits for the arrival of his Arab Spring.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/a-quiet-revolution-in-beirut.html', 'snippet': 'In the center of Beirut, one man waits for the arrival of his Arab Spring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the end of Lebanon’s 15-year civil war, in 1990, Beirut’s downtown was an apocalyptic scene of rubble and weeds, inhabited by squatters in skeletal buildings and bored soldiers moving among sewage-filled craters. The rare splash of color came from the blue advertisements for a billboard company that read: “What do our boards have in common with the C.I.A.? They’re both all over the place.” In the two decades since then, Beirut has become a monument to globalism, home to Louis Vuitton, Chanel and the priciest hotels, the power of capitalism concealing but never really healing the war’s scars. Among the chrome-and-glass buildings downtown stands a grand statue of Riyadh al-Solh, a founder of modern Lebanon, looking like a fat and happy pasha. And beneath that statue, in a patch of bare sand he calls the Square of Change, sits a lonely man named Tony Daoud. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19here/mag-19here-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19here/mag-19here-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19here/mag-19here-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Very Quiet Revolution', 'kicker': 'You Are Here', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE VERY QUIET REVOLUTION', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beirut (Lebanon)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T11:20:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Shadid', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shadid', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7a21caf-38a9-5737-b4ab-01324e888deb', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7a21caf-38a9-5737-b4ab-01324e888deb'}, {'abstract': 'How Amanda Hocking, 26, having reached peak rankings on the Kindle e-book best-seller list, solved the publishing business all by herself.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/amanda-hocking-storyseller.html', 'snippet': 'How Amanda Hocking, 26, having reached peak rankings on the Kindle e-book best-seller list, solved the publishing business all by herself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amanda Hocking, the star of self-publishing, was sitting in the front seat of her Ford Escape earlier this spring when she spotted a messenger delivering flowers to her home in Austin, Minn. She watched her best friend and roommate, Eric Goldman, get the door.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19hocking/19hocking-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 844, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19hocking/19hocking-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19hocking/19hocking-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Storyseller', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'STORYSELLER', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hocking, Amanda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T11:25:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Strawberry Saroyan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Strawberry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saroyan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b15968f-128c-507d-87fe-9823e0a4d005', 'word_count': 2971, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b15968f-128c-507d-87fe-9823e0a4d005'}, {'abstract': 'The impulsive pursuits of Vincent Moon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/vincent-moons-take-away-videos.html', 'snippet': 'The impulsive pursuits of Vincent Moon.', 'lead_paragraph': ' About five years ago, a prodigious young French filmmaker named Vincent Moon reinvented the music video. Filming in an intimate cinéma vérité style in odd locations, mostly around Paris, Moon created dozens and dozens of raw, enigmatic clips that stripped away everything but the performer and the song. He put musicians in awkward situations, and the results were magnetic. He set up the band Phoenix, already fairly famous at that point, as buskers in a sea of tourists outside the Eiffel Tower; the National sat crammed elbow to elbow around a picnic table in the South of France; Yo La Tengo played a Troggs cover amid squealing schoolchildren in Montmartre. But it was the clip of Arcade Fire — 10 of them, strings, horns, xylophone, guitar — packed into an elevator and singing, with the rip of magazine pages for percussion, that came to represent Moon’s innovative new oeuvre. A combination cool-hunter and documentarian, Moon proved it’s possible to reinvent an old, tired format (the music video) using the very thing (the Internet) that supposedly killed it.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19moon/19moon-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19moon/19moon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19moon/19moon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Video Explorer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE VIDEO EXPLORER', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recordings and Downloads (Video)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T11:25:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lizzy Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lizzy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/993a8a0b-f077-579c-98a9-a3bf644934b4', 'word_count': 764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/993a8a0b-f077-579c-98a9-a3bf644934b4'}, {'abstract': 'A homemade campaign to beat drug dealers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/lives-how-my-mom-discovered-crack.html', 'snippet': 'A homemade campaign to beat drug dealers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was 1985. I was 12 and standing next to my mother in a police station in Greenwich Village. She was a pretty red-haired gal in her late 30s, but the three police officers she was talking to weren’t looking at her. They were looking at the bag of crack vials she had in her hands, confused about what they were. I wasn’t confused. We had a lot of crack vials in our apartment at that point. Hundreds of them. My brothers and I played with them in Washington Square Park. We carried them around in our pockets the way other kids carried marbles.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19lives/mag-19Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19lives/19lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19lives/19lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How My Mom Discovered Crack', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Vials of Washington Square', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington Square Park (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moses, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch, Edward I', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T11:25:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Carney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c94edc7a-2d86-5fa4-801b-1f2830d25241', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c94edc7a-2d86-5fa4-801b-1f2830d25241'}, {'abstract': 'A reader puts Andrew Rice’s “Bulb In, Bulb Out” in perspective.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/lights-out.html', 'snippet': 'A reader puts Andrew Rice’s “Bulb In, Bulb Out” in perspective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To put Andrew Rice’s “Bulb In, Bulb Out” in perspective: More than 100 years ago, Robert Louis Stevenson’s “Plea for Gas Lamps” grieved the passing of the evening lamplighter and ranted against the horror of electric light. “The word ‘electricity’ now sounds the note of danger. . . . A new sort of urban star now shines out nightly, horrible, unearthly, obnoxious to the human eye; a lamp for a nightmare! Such a light as this should shine only on murders and public crime or along the corridors of lunatic asylums, a horror to heighten horror. To look at it only once is to fall in love with gas, which gives a warm domestic radiance.” Technologies past are always “warmer” than the harsh, cold, high-tech future, even in the 19th century.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lights Out', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'LIGHTS OUT', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Andrew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T11:25:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/42bf9970-bae0-5398-b731-af9668e762e4', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42bf9970-bae0-5398-b731-af9668e762e4'}, {'abstract': 'Waiting room; renting room.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/doctors-order.html', 'snippet': 'Waiting room; renting room.', 'lead_paragraph': ' I have a chronic medical condition, which is being treated with injections every other week. The drug has a potentially deadly side effect that can occur without warning, after years of symptom-free doses and days after an injection. The treatment for this side effect is epinephrine, which I carry with me as a precaution. My doctor recently announced that after each injection, I must wait 30 minutes before leaving the clinic. There is no reason I would be able to use an Epi-pen after 30 minutes but not before, but he says, “These are new guidelines from the manufacturer, and I won’t allow you to be dosed unless you agree to comply with them, here and now.” Is this a textbook example of coercion? Thinking that term might be hyperbole, I looked up various definitions, many of which apply. P. C., NORWELL, MASS.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Doctor’s Order', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "DOCTOR'S ORDER", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T11:25:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/7661c8aa-a50e-5278-8567-d996212cb322', 'word_count': 904, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7661c8aa-a50e-5278-8567-d996212cb322'}, {'abstract': 'Inside the bald, angry head of Louis C.K.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/inside-the-bald-angry-head-of-louis-ck.html', 'snippet': 'Inside the bald, angry head of Louis C.K.', 'lead_paragraph': ' I think about your HBO show, “Lucky Louie,” which lasted just one season, and I think, I’ve seen your penis.  That’s exactly right. It wasout there. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/mag-19talk-t_CA0/mag-19talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 549, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/mag-19talk-t_CA0/mag-19talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/mag-19talk-t_CA0/mag-19talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Grumpus Maximus', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'GRUMPUS MAXIMUS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FX', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T11:25:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/df6223e6-39fc-5bb5-aa2d-af0368a7b959', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df6223e6-39fc-5bb5-aa2d-af0368a7b959'}, {'abstract': 'Hoarding programs have become the voyeur-horror shows of reality television. Why are we so compelled to explore the landscapes of other people’s trash?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/magazine/exploring-the-landscapes-of-other-peoples-trash.html', 'snippet': 'Hoarding programs have become the voyeur-horror shows of reality television. Why are we so compelled to explore the landscapes of other people’s trash?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1947, Erich Fromm, a humanist, psychoanalyst and philosopher, developed a theory of character that divided people into five “orientations,” mostly determined by their relationship to stuff. He characterized four of these — the receptive, exploitive, hoarding and marketing orientations — as part of the “having” mode, which is focused on consuming, obtaining and possessing. (The fifth orientation was “productive,” which focuses on experience and human connection.) Fromm specifically linked the hoarding orientation to the Protestant work ethic and the American merchant middle class and argued that this orientation is characterized by, among other things, being “constipated and squinty.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '52', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19riff_span/mag-19Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 104, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19riff_span/mag-19Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 104}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19riff_span/mag-19Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 209, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19riff_span/mag-19Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/magazine/19riff_span/mag-19Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Underneath Every Hoarder Is a Normal Person Waiting to Be Dug Out’', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Underneath Every Hoarder Is a Normal Person Waiting to Be Dug Out’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hoarding', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T11:27:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carina Chocano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chocano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/926674ad-ede1-57ac-96c1-b90b971935bd', 'word_count': 2058, 'uri': 'nyt://article/926674ad-ede1-57ac-96c1-b90b971935bd'}, {'abstract': 'Here are four Washington repercussions from Mr. Weiner’s weeks-long Twitter scandal.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/washington-fallout-from-weiners-resignation/', 'snippet': 'Here are four Washington repercussions from Mr. Weiner’s weeks-long Twitter scandal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Twitter scandal that engulfed Representative Anthony D. Weiner played out its final scene on Thursday in New York, amid lurid catcalls, screaming tabloid headlines and expressions of contrition by the fallen lawmaker.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Washington Fallout From Weiner’s Resignation', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Anthony D', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'na