lan. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/05/28/business/business-lieber-headshot/business-lieber-headshot-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/05/28/business/business-lieber-headshot/business-lieber-headshot-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Hunt For a Better 401(k) Plan', 'kicker': 'Your Money', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Hunt For a Better 401(k) Plan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': '401(k), 403(b) and 457 Plans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paychex Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dimensional Fund Advisors', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Retirement', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T15:38:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'subsection_name': "401(k)'s and Similar Plans", 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Lieber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lieber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f241de7-6171-5392-8de4-bc1dd5d96ace', 'word_count': 1485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f241de7-6171-5392-8de4-bc1dd5d96ace'}, {'abstract': 'This clear, accessible account of World War II asks how the Wehrmacht, the best fighting force, wound up losing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-the-storm-of-war-a-new-history-of-the-second-world-war-by-andrew-roberts.html', 'snippet': 'This clear, accessible account of World War II asks how the Wehrmacht, the best fighting force, wound up losing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How did the Wehrmacht, the best fighting force, lose World War II? The reader seeking the answer to this question, posed by Andrew Roberts in his splendid history, will be treated to a brilliantly clear and accessible account of the war in all of its theaters: Asian, African and European. Roberts’s descriptions of soldiers and officers are masterly and humane, and his battlefield set pieces are as gripping as any I have ever read. He has visited many of the battlefields, and has an unusually good eye for detail as well as a painterly skill at physical description. (His nearly perfect sense of terrain and geography is marred only by his regrettable conflation of Russia with the Soviet Union, which leads to confusion about battlefield locations, German war aims and Soviet casualties.) He is just as much at home at sea as on land; from Midway to El Alamein his prose is unerringly precise and stirringly vivid. It is hard to imagine a better-told military history of World War II. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Snyder/Snyder-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Snyder/Snyder-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Snyder/Snyder-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 648, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Snyder/Snyder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Snyder/Snyder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Hitler Could Have Won', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After the Lightning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T15:38:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Snyder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Snyder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3d5e21f-aa83-5af5-8b3c-c11311ec88b9', 'word_count': 1261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3d5e21f-aa83-5af5-8b3c-c11311ec88b9'}, {'abstract': 'An Israeli and a Palestinian have joined forces on wind turbine installations, making it easier to forge business relationships in both Israel and the Palestinian territories.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/partners-in-wind-power-bridge-a-mideast-divide/', 'snippet': 'An Israeli and a Palestinian have joined forces on wind turbine installations, making it easier to forge business relationships in both Israel and the Palestinian territories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I first sought out Muhammad Salem, he was awaiting the birth of his fourth child, a girl, at Al-Makassed hospital in East Jerusalem. The baby’s name would go through several iterations in the course of the night. First it was Luna. Then Natalie. Eventually the family would settle on Dina.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Partners in Wind Power Bridge a Mideast Divide', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wind Power', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Bank', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T15:50:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamir Elterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamir', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/597f91ae-8ce8-5ad3-a897-21a9507778ee', 'word_count': 632, 'uri': 'nyt://article/597f91ae-8ce8-5ad3-a897-21a9507778ee'}, {'abstract': 'Readers share their own tales of their efforts to make their 401(k) plans better.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/how-you-solved-your-401k-problem/', 'snippet': 'Readers share their own tales of their efforts to make their 401(k) plans better.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this weekend’s Your Money column, I tell the tale of Alan Wenker, who spent 10 years hunting for a better 401(k) for his fellow workers and himself. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How You Solved Your 401(k) Problem', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': '401(k), 403(b) and 457 Plans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Retirement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dimensional Fund Advisors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T15:52:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Lieber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lieber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39810543-c4a0-5909-89b6-fa9c873bfe61', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39810543-c4a0-5909-89b6-fa9c873bfe61'}, {'abstract': 'The debate over movies that may be “nutritious” but not always tasty is joined by a writer and The Times’s chief film critics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/movies/critics-discuss-cinema-thats-good-for-you.html', 'snippet': 'The debate over movies that may be “nutritious” but not always tasty is joined by a writer and The Times’s chief film critics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON May 1 The New York Times Magazine published an article, “Reaching for Culture That Remains Stubbornly Above My Grasp,” in which Dan Kois wrote about watching certain critically regarded movies, like Andrei Tarkovsky’s “Solaris,” that he likened to eating his “cultural vegetables.” In the June 5 Arts & Leisure section the chief film critics of The Times, Manohla Dargis and A. O. Scott, defended such films, adding to a vigorous, sometimes fractious discussion about so-called boring and slow films that is still continuing, lighting up the blogosphere and beyond, and inspiring hundreds of readers’ comments. They invited Mr. Kois to join them in the still-simmering conversation.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/arts/CRITICS-1/CRITICS-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/arts/CRITICS-1/CRITICS-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/arts/CRITICS-1/CRITICS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/arts/CRITICS-1/CRITICS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/arts/CRITICS-1/CRITICS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sometimes a Vegetable Is Just a Vegetable', 'kicker': 'Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sometimes A Vegetable Is Just a Vegetable', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T15:55:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott and Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09cf6623-4e40-57f5-ab0e-67715115c1d1', 'word_count': 1780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09cf6623-4e40-57f5-ab0e-67715115c1d1'}, {'abstract': 'This is the first sustained treatment of the Central Park jogger case since the defendants’ convictions were vacated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-the-central-park-five-by-sarah-burns.html', 'snippet': 'This is the first sustained treatment of the Central Park jogger case since the defendants’ convictions were vacated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the wake of the 1989 rape and near-fatal beating of a 28-year-old white woman named Trisha Meili (known to many as the Central Park jogger), and after the arrests, confessions and eventual convictions of one Latino and four African-\xadAmerican teenagers for the crime, the media relentlessly asked: How did this happen? In her slim but ambitious book, “The Central Park Five: A Chronicle of a City Wilding,” Sarah Burns tackles this same question, but with a changed referent. “This,” rather than signifying a horrific gang rape in New York City’s bucolic backyard, here signifies a preventable miscarriage of justice that put five Harlem teenagers behind bars for a crime they didn’t commit. Each of the boys — Antron McCray, Kevin Richardson, Yusef Salaam, Korey Wise and Raymond Santana Jr. — served between 7 and 13 years. Their convictions were vacated in 2002 by the New York State Supreme Court, after a confession and DNA analysis linked a serial rapist, Matias Reyes, to the crime. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Nelson/Nelson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Nelson/Nelson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Nelson/Nelson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Central Park Jogger Case Revisited', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Surviving Justice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CENTRAL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Confessions', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meili, Trisha', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burns, Sarah', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T15:58:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maggie Nelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/551ca00e-9152-5de4-8cbb-b1208a2681a5', 'word_count': 1307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/551ca00e-9152-5de4-8cbb-b1208a2681a5'}, {'abstract': 'Going from long hair to short marks a drastic change in any woman’s life, but for ballet dancers it’s almost a political act.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/arts/dance/ballet-dancers-hair-bun-or-bob.html', 'snippet': 'Going from long hair to short marks a drastic change in any woman’s life, but for ballet dancers it’s almost a political act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EVERYONE has bad hair days, but just consider the plight of the ballerina: her locks are teased, sprayed and shellacked into submission. For dancers split ends or an unruly cowlick are first-world problems. What about receding hairlines caused by scraping the hair, day in and day out, into a bun?', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bob or Bun? A Ballerina’s Tough Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bob or Bun? A Ballerina’s Tough Choice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hair', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T16:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9354ed1-fada-5d0e-b23a-480a90b32b5c', 'word_count': 1166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9354ed1-fada-5d0e-b23a-480a90b32b5c'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition at the Imperial War Museum North in Manchester, England, covers war reporting since 1914.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/reporting-under-fire-a-survey-of-a-century-of-war-correspondents/', 'snippet': 'An exhibition at the Imperial War Museum North in Manchester, England, covers war reporting since 1914.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANCHESTER, England — Within a few hundred yards of each other in Old Trafford lie the Manchester United Football Club and the Imperial War Museum North. One is a monument to the global sport that unites continents and peoples, the other a monument to the study of forces that divide and despoil them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reporting Under Fire: a Survey of a Century of War Correspondents', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T16:06:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Farrell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farrell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a68b476-68ae-58b9-915b-0243c715ca9b', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a68b476-68ae-58b9-915b-0243c715ca9b'}, {'abstract': 'Nasdaq OMX wants to take a minority stake in LCH.Clearnet, the swaps clearinghouse, as it heads into another stand-off with NYSE Euronext.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/nasdaq-in-talks-to-buy-stake-in-lch-clearnet/', 'snippet': 'Nasdaq OMX wants to take a minority stake in LCH.Clearnet, the swaps clearinghouse, as it heads into another stand-off with NYSE Euronext.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nasdaq OMX wants to take a minority stake in LCH.Clearnet, the London-based swaps clearinghouse, as the exchange operator heads into another stand-off with its rival, NYSE Euronext.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nasdaq Said to Seek Stake in London Clearinghouse', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject'