: 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/paradoxical-storytelling-for-children.html', 'snippet': '“The Lying Carpet” and “The Girl Who Circumnavigated Fairyland in a Ship of Her Own Making” celebrate paradox and the transformative power of storytelling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A tiger lies on the parquet floor of a deserted room, telling lies to a newly awakened stone statue: Long ago, the tiger says, he had a family, but he was shot and stuffed with straw, and glass baubles were sewn where his eyes had been. Later, however, he says he was made in a factory out of artificial fibers. Which story is true? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Ingall-t_CA0/bkr-Ingall-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 310, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Ingall-t_CA0/bkr-Ingall-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 310}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Ingall-t_CA0/bkr-Ingall-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 529, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Ingall-t_CA0/bkr-Ingall-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Ingall-t_CA0/bkr-Ingall-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paradoxical Storytelling for Children', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leaps of Faith', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T16:26:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marjorie Ingall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marjorie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ingall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/59862f94-7886-57aa-9fc9-90c09901157c', 'word_count': 790, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59862f94-7886-57aa-9fc9-90c09901157c'}, {'abstract': 'A blossoming 10-year-old seeks a rare seashell in this middle grade novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-junonia-by-kevin-henkes.html', 'snippet': 'A blossoming 10-year-old seeks a rare seashell in this middle grade novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes things don’t happen exactly the way you want them to happen. In this gentle tale of growing up, 9-year-old Alice Rice learns that even though she’s growing older, life is seldom about achieving perfection. She senses this in the very first paragraph of the story, by Kevin Henkes, a Newbery Honor winner and Caldecott medalist, as her family crosses the bridge that connects the Florida mainland to the island they visit every winter. Alice, who ordinarily enjoys being suspended between two worlds, suddenly feels strange. “Her breath caught high in her chest and she became light-headed. It seemed as though there wasn’t enough air in the car.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Beachcomber’s Guide to Growing Up', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sea Changes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Henkes, Kevin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T16:32:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann M. Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc823644-6df7-52d4-8237-8aa156ebc20a', 'word_count': 286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc823644-6df7-52d4-8237-8aa156ebc20a'}, {'abstract': 'Deaths and injuries are reported as government forces attack demonstrators in two large cities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/18syria.html', 'snippet': 'Deaths and injuries are reported as government forces attack demonstrators in two large cities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — Thousands of protesters poured into the streets in the suburbs of Damascus, Syria, and in three of the country’s five largest cities on Friday, in a weekly show of defiance that came days before President Bashar al-Assad was expected to address Syrians for the first time in two months. Activists said at least 19 people were killed and dozens wounded.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/syria/syria-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/syria/syria-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/syria/syria-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbn