and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T16:41:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2ab4641-b4db-533d-aa71-a7905e097d9c', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2ab4641-b4db-533d-aa71-a7905e097d9c'}, {'abstract': 'At No. 1 on the hardcover list this week is “Wonder Woman: Odyssey,” in which the superheroine wakes up to find that someone has altered her past.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/graphic-book-best-sellers-wonder-woman-and-zombies/', 'snippet': 'At No. 1 on the hardcover list this week is “Wonder Woman: Odyssey,” in which the superheroine wakes up to find that someone has altered her past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At No. 1 on the hardcover list this week is “Wonder Woman: Odyssey,” in which the superheroine wakes up to find that someone has altered her past. She sets out – in a new costume – to find out what went wrong. This is the first half of the journey, written by J. Michael Straczynski and illustrated by Don Kramer. About halfway through the story, Mr. Straczynski departed to work on a sequel to “Superman: Earth One,” an interpretation of the Man of Steel’s origin for today’s audience. The first installment of that story hit No. 1 in our hardcover list in November. Phil Hester, who took over the Wonder Woman story, has two installments in “Odyssey” that were helped along by the original writer’s notes. Of course, they’ll likely be more tinkering with the Amazing Amazon’s origin in September when DC Comics restarts the majority of its superhero series with new number ones.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Graphic Book Best Sellers: Wonder Woman and Zombies', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DC Comics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T16:43:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa8fb5c7-8f09-5f70-941b-0fc13e6612be', 'word_count': 446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa8fb5c7-8f09-5f70-941b-0fc13e6612be'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column recounts his experience hearing an unvarnished critique of his financial life.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/an-unvarnished-critique-of-your-finances/', 'snippet': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column recounts his experience hearing an unvarnished critique of his financial life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Sullivan, in his Wealth Matters column this week, writes about the experience of having his financial life examined by eight wealthy men, all members of an investment club called Tiger 21.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Unvarnished Critique of Your Finances', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disability Insurance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Life Insurance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Paul', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T1