ue': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pandora Media Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PRADA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:04:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04e8598e-eea4-551a-8e5c-ce035ce6d114', 'word_count': 261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04e8598e-eea4-551a-8e5c-ce035ce6d114'}, {'abstract': 'David McCullough first appeared on the hardcover nonfiction list in 1977. This week, his “Greater Journey” sits atop the list for the second week in a row.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/06/26/books/review/bestsellers-weekly-graphic.html', 'snippet': 'David McCullough first appeared on the hardcover nonfiction list in 1977. This week, his “Greater Journey” sits atop the list for the second week in a row.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David McCullough first appeared on the hardcover nonfiction list in 1977. This week, his “Greater Journey” sits atop the list for the second week in a row.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Best Sellers Weekly Graphic: American Historian', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:13+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/1944cccc-c16d-517d-bba5-aa5867141939', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/1944cccc-c16d-517d-bba5-aa5867141939'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HALF A LIFE: A Memoir,  by Darin Strauss (Random House, $13.) At 18, Strauss was behind the wheel when his Oldsmobile struck and killed a fellow high school student in suburban Long Island. In his subtle yet wrenching book, Strauss, the author of “Chang and Eng” (2000), explores memory, guilt and coming-of-age after a life-altering event.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/9724a81d-acce-5e5a-8718-245ae5014c4f', 'word_count': 512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9724a81d-acce-5e5a-8718-245ae5014c4f'}, {'abstract': 'Ann Patchett’s heroine, on the trail of a reclusive scientist in the Amazon, faces demons real and imagined.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-state-of-wonder-by-ann-patchett.html', 'snippet': 'Ann Patchett’s heroine, on the trail of a reclusive scientist in the Amazon, faces demons real and imagined.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ann Patchett’s most characteristic subject is the hell turned unlikely paradise, a kind of reverse “Lord of the Flies” in which a group of strangers, shipwrecked into a disaster beyond their imagining, manage nonetheless to create a peaceable habitat where love and decency prevail. Patchett’s best-\xadselling 2001 novel, “Bel Canto,” opened with a botched terrorist operation in a South American country, after which an American opera diva, a Japanese industrialist, a French ambassador, various Russian businessmen and their Marxist-Leninist guerrilla captors turned their long captivity into a peculiar sort of idyll. “State of Wonder,” Patchett’s eighth book and sixth novel, is no less multinational in its cast of characters, or high-stakes in its plot.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/jp-book/jp-book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/jp-book/jp-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/02/arts/jp-book/jp-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ann Patchett’s Amazon Wonder Drug Novel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Serpents in the Garden', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMAZON JUNGLE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patchett, Ann', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fernanda Eberstadt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fernanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eberstadt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d70d616d-d747-5dbb-859b-18a3788df301', 'word_count': 1081, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d70d616d-d747-5dbb-859b-18a3788df301'}, {'abstract': 'A first novel about young love in a Russian émigré community.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-vaclav-lena-by-haley-tanner.html', 'snippet': 'A first novel about young love in a Russian émigré community.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Magic and magical thinking are the means by which the innocent can acquire power. This accounts for the appeal not only of the Harry Potter books but of adult-minded novels like Michael Chabon’s “Amazing Adventures of Kavalier & Clay.” Now Haley Tanner’s first novel, “Vaclav & Lena,” takes the figure of the young magician and mixes it with a second magical device, the mutual love of a young boy and girl.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/19ferriss/19ferriss-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 357, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/19ferriss/19ferriss-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 357}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/19ferriss/19ferriss-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 610, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/19ferriss/19ferriss-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/19ferriss/19ferriss-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Young Love in Little Russia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brighton Beach Magic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brighton Beach (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanner, Haley', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lucy Ferriss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lucy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ferriss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/06b361f8-d7a6-591f-bb4a-65ad05d374b4', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06b361f8-d7a6-591f-bb4a-65ad05d374b4'}, {'abstract': 'Ann Coulter comes roaring back to the list with “Demonic: How the Liberal Mob is Endangering America,” though her brand does seem to have undergone a bit of tweaking.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Ann Coulter comes roaring back to the list with “Demonic: How the Liberal Mob is Endangering America,” though her brand does seem to have undergone a bit of tweaking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WOMAN IN BLACK: When Ann Coulter last appeared on the hardcover nonfiction list in 2009, with “Guilty: Liberal ‘Victims’ and Their Assault on America,” some commentators asked whether the conservative attack dog had lost a bit of her sales bite. “Guilty” spent six weeks on the list — an enviable showing by most standards, but a decline from the mark set by “Godless” (12 weeks), “Treason” (13 weeks) and “Slander” (15 weeks).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/26insidelist/26insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/26insidelist/26insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/343aef70-7fd2-5815-8a77-fd6888430302', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/343aef70-7fd2-5815-8a77-fd6888430302'}, {'abstract': 'Between 1640 and 1760, Mary Beth Norton contends, men were increasingly viewed as public beings and women as private ones.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-separated-by-their-sex-by-mary-beth-norton.html', 'snippet': 'Between 1640 and 1760, Mary Beth Norton contends, men were increasingly viewed as public beings and women as private ones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I\u2007wonder how today’s advice columnists might handle a query John Dunton received in 1691. A London printer and bookseller, Dunton was the great-granddaddy of all agony aunts and uncles. He had offered to answer questions from readers of his periodical, The Athenian Mercury. Someone asked him what a man should do if his mother and his sweetheart were in equal peril at the same time. Which one should he save? Dunton suggested that “we esteem it more generous, were it possible, to die himself than lose either.” Gallant Mr. Dunton appears nearly halfway through Mary Beth Norton’s suggestive if idiosyncratic new book, “Separated by Their Sex.” He is a villain in the story, since Norton assigns him the role of defining women as suited to the soft glories of home life and men as strivers in the public world. His chivalry was part of a larger effort to keep the “fair sex” away from political power. Or so it seems.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Chaplin/Chaplin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 959, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Chaplin/Chaplin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Chaplin/Chaplin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Women Lost the Vote', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Public Men, Private Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Norton, Mary Beth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History (Academic Subject)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce E. Chaplin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Chaplin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/59755469-fc67-55de-8cb5-6d80e759ae1f', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59755469-fc67-55de-8cb5-6d80e759ae1f'}, {'abstract': 'David Mamet comes out swinging against liberalism, offering his views on religion and American culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-the-secret-knowledge-by-david-mamet.html', 'snippet': 'David Mamet comes out swinging against liberalism, offering his views on religion and American culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is an extraordinarily irritating book, written by one of those people who smugly believe that, having lost their faith, they must ipso facto have found their reason. In order to be persuaded by it, you would have to be open to propositions like this:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Hitchens-t_CA0/bkr-Hitchens-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 649, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Hitchens-t_CA0/bkr-Hitchens-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Hitchens-t_CA0/bkr-Hitchens-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'David Mamet’s Right-Wing Conversion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Antagonist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mamet, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Hitchens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hitchens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/24077689-6a42-554c-8d0d-69b1c25dcca6', 'word_count': 1287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24077689-6a42-554c-8d0d-69b1c25dcca6'}, {'abstract': 'Asti Hustvedt examines the dubious research of a 19th-century French doctor who used hypnosis to induce hysteria in female subjects.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-medical-muses-hysteria-in-nineteenth-century-paris-by-asti-hustvedt.html', 'snippet': 'Asti Hustvedt examines the dubious research of a 19th-century French doctor who used hypnosis to induce hysteria in female subjects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Readers who can’t identify Jean-Martin Charcot as the name of the French neurologist whose 19th-century experiments with hypnosis influenced Sigmund Freud’s theory of neurosis may yet recognize the work he conducted at the Saltpêtrière Hospital in Paris. Photographs and illustrations of Charcot’s patients, all women suffering hysteria, remain in currency today, 140 years after they were made, if more as curiosities than as clinically valuable documents. Once seen, these images — of, for example, a woman wearing little more than a tangle of bed sheets, her eyes rolled up into her head in either “ecstasy” or “delirium,” or fixed on the invisible object of her “amorous supplication” — are not easily forgotten, let alone dismissed. Poses classified as “passionate attitudes,” they have the disquieting aspect of pornography masquerading as intellectual inquiry.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/HARRISON/HARRISON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 563, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/HARRISON/HARRISON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/HARRISON/HARRISON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pseudoscience of Hysteria', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Under the Influence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freud, Sigmund', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Harrison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harrison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/293ce1e0-ef1b-5390-9d63-9de0990fcd7b', 'word_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/293ce1e0-ef1b-5390-9d63-9de0990fcd7b'}, {'abstract': 'A sensational Jazz Age crime that also inspired James M. Cain and William Styron is the basis for Ron Hansen’s propulsive novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-a-wild-surge-of-guilty-passion-by-ron-hansen.html', 'snippet': 'A sensational Jazz Age crime that also inspired James M. Cain and William Styron is the basis for Ron Hansen’s propulsive novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even by the standards of a time when some dozen major New York area newspapers competed for scoops and sensations, the 1927 murder trial of Ruth Snyder and her lover, Judd Gray, was a huge story. The pair, who murdered Snyder’s husband, embodied the sort of colorful contradictions that reporters love — and that novelists are hard-pressed to better. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Heighton/Heighton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Heighton/Heighton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/Heighton/Heighton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Story of a Jazz Age Murder', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pick Your Poison', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hansen, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Heighton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heighton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/33f2fd65-c47e-5e35-b234-58d894584942', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33f2fd65-c47e-5e35-b234-58d894584942'}, {'abstract': 'Worried about whether you’re evil? Two new books, complete with diagnostic checklists, can help you decide.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/im-ok-youre-a-psychopath.html', 'snippet': 'Worried about whether you’re evil? Two new books, complete with diagnostic checklists, can help you decide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do psychopaths enjoy reading books about psychopaths? In his engagingly irreverent new best seller, The Psychopath Test: A Journey Through the Madness Industry (Riverhead, $25.95), the journalist Jon Ronson notes that only about one in 100 people are psychopaths (there is a higher proportion in prisons and corporate boardrooms), but he wonders if this population will be overrepresented among readers of his book. After all, people do enjoy learning about themselves, and psychopaths in particular have an enhanced sense of their own importance. And they might like what Ronson has to say. He approvingly quotes experts who argue that psychopaths make “the world go around.” Despite their small numbers, they cause such chaos that they remold society — though not necessarily for the better.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Bloom-t_CA0/bkr-Bloom-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 851, 'width': 824, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Bloom-t_CA0/bkr-Bloom-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Bloom-t_CA0/bkr-Bloom-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I’m O.K., You’re a Psychopath', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I’m O.K., You’re a Psychopath', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ronson, Jon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emotions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Bloom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bloom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd2c6155-cef5-5bb1-9f01-e767470b4f8f', 'word_count': 1273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd2c6155-cef5-5bb1-9f01-e767470b4f8f'}, {'abstract': 'Manning Marable’s biography of Malcolm X draws upon letters, diaries, F.B.I. reports and interviews with contemporaries to trace his career and illuminate his intellectual and spiritual development.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-malcolm-x-by-manning-marable.html', 'snippet': 'Manning Marable’s biography of Malcolm X draws upon letters, diaries, F.B.I. reports and interviews with contemporaries to trace his career and illuminate his intellectual and spiritual development.', 'lead_paragraph': '“His aura was too bright,” the poet Maya Angelou said of her first meeting with Malcolm X. “His masculine force affected me physically. A hot desert storm eddied around him and rushed to me, making my skin contract, and my pores slam shut.” Malcolm X had that same sort of bone-deep, visceral impact on America. He got under everyone’s skin — either in the sense that he seeped into your pores and transformed you the way the great love of your life does, or in the sense that he annoyed or scared the living hell out of you. There is no middle ground with Malcolm. If you hate him or distrust him, you should consider giving him another try: officers assigned to monitor the wiretaps on his phones sometimes ended up being flipped, because close listening led them to believe that his programs and philosophies were sensible and righteous and that law enforcement agencies should not have been working against him at all. And while Malcolm’s ideas changed America, his life journey has captivated us even more. He went from a petty criminal and drug user to a long-term prisoner to an influential minister to a separatist political activist to a humanist to a martyr. Throughout his life he continually grew upward, unafraid to challenge or refute what he believed, giving hope that any of us can rise above even our deepest convictions to become better people.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/TOURE/TOURE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 677, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/TOURE/TOURE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/TOURE/TOURE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Malcolm X: Criminal, Minister, Humanist, Martyr', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Criminal, Minister, Humanist, Martyr', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malcolm X', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marable, Manning', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nation of Islam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By TOURÉ', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Touré', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d84f27d2-f187-5a66-aa1b-a6b5a6b2457a', 'word_count': 1531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d84f27d2-f187-5a66-aa1b-a6b5a6b2457a'}, {'abstract': 'The cultural diaspora of the Nazi years, through the eyes of Thomas Mann’s brother and unlikely sister-in-law.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-house-of-exile-by-evelyn-juers.html', 'snippet': 'The cultural diaspora of the Nazi years, through the eyes of Thomas Mann’s brother and unlikely sister-in-law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The not unimportant but somewhat undervalued writer Heinrich Mann (1871-1950) and his second wife, Nelly Kroeger-Mann, 27 years younger and socially several rungs lower, had suspenseful lives during the great emigration — or cultural diaspora — of the Hitler years, and years of exile thereafter. This could make for a wonderful book if the author exercised sufficient self-control rather than showing off in various ways, and kept the work reasonably concise. Unfortunately, \xadEvelyn Juers’s “House of Exile” is not, or not quite, that book.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/SIMON-1/SIMON-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 777, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/SIMON-1/SIMON-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/SIMON-1/SIMON-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Escaping Hitler, Cracking Up in L.A.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Blue Angels', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mann, Thomas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mann, Heinrich', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Simon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/17bd4ec7-2764-5207-a5aa-f185df503b33', 'word_count': 1719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17bd4ec7-2764-5207-a5aa-f185df503b33'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/EdChoice-t.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ILLUMINATIONS,  by Arthur Rimbaud. Translated by John Ashbery (Norton, $24.95.) Besides his early absorption of Rimbaud’s poetry, Ashbery brings to this resourceful translation a long and deep familiarity with French life, language and culture.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Editors' Choice | Recent books of particular interest", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b5723e0-44be-55a4-b6ae-d825f96da775', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b5723e0-44be-55a4-b6ae-d825f96da775'}, {'abstract': 'In the 1970s, eccentric young scientists challenged convention and re-energized modern physics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-how-the-hippies-saved-physics-by-david-kaiser.html', 'snippet': 'In the 1970s, eccentric young scientists challenged convention and re-energized modern physics.', 'lead_paragraph': '“What the Bleep Do We Know!?,” a spaced-out concoction of quasi physics and neuroscience that appeared several years ago, promised moviegoers that they could hop between parallel universes and leap back and forth in time — if only they cast off their mental filters and experienced reality full blast. Interviews of scientists were crosscut with those of self-proclaimed mystics, and swooping in to explain the physics was Dr. Quantum, a cartoon superhero who joyfully demonstrated concepts like wave-particle duality, extra dimensions and quantum entanglement. Wiggling his eyebrows, the good doctor ominously asked, “Are we far enough down the rabbit hole yet?” All that was missing was Grace Slick wailing in the background with Jorma Kaukonen on \xadguitar.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/JOHNSON/JOHNSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 713, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/JOHNSON/JOHNSON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/JOHNSON/JOHNSON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Physics Owes the Counterculture', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Psychedelic Universe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaiser, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hippies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Seventies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/08ff08f1-d06a-5212-a137-7189e2bc0c3c', 'word_count': 1104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08ff08f1-d06a-5212-a137-7189e2bc0c3c'}, {'abstract': 'A memoir by Robert Jay Lifton, a leading “psychohistorian” who studied how individuals have coped with extreme circumstances: war, torture, genocide.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/book-review-witness-to-an-extreme-century-by-robert-jay-lifton.html', 'snippet': 'A memoir by Robert Jay Lifton, a leading “psychohistorian” who studied how individuals have coped with extreme circumstances: war, torture, genocide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For many years, Robert Jay Lifton has been recognized as a leading “psychohistorian,” or as he prefers to define his vocation, a “historically minded psychiatrist.” Psychohistory is the field of inquiry that explores the psychological motives of individuals and groups of historical actors, as well as the psychological impact of historical events. Lifton is perhaps best known as the author of “Death in Life: Survivors of Hiroshima,” published in 1967, which received a National Book Award. In that book, he described how the hibakusha, those residents of Hiroshima who survived the atomic bomb on Aug. 6, 1945, lived with deep and shameful feelings of being “inwardly poisoned” by their experience, as well as an embittered “sense of special knowledge” that set them apart from those who had not witnessed the horrors of the attack.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/ISSERMAN/ISSERMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/ISSERMAN/ISSERMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/ISSERMAN/ISSERMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life of a Psychohistorian', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dr. Hiroshima', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History (Academic Subject)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lifton, Robert Jay', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maurice Isserman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maurice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isserman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/239843d5-fd2a-516c-ab85-42d6235f3849', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/239843d5-fd2a-516c-ab85-42d6235f3849'}, {'abstract': 'Mystery novels by Peter Lovesey, Marcus Sakey, Elizabeth Brundage and Duane Swierczynski.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/books/review/crime-novels-by-peter-lovesey-marcus-sakey-elizabeth-brundage-and-duane-swierczynski.html', 'snippet': 'Mystery novels by Peter Lovesey, Marcus Sakey, Elizabeth Brundage and Duane Swierczynski.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in the golden age of British detective fiction, the New Zealand author and theater producer Ngaio Marsh set the bar for backstage mysteries with “Enter a Murderer” (1935), distinguished by its realistic theatrical settings and strong dramatic themes. Dame Ngaio still rules, but Peter Lovesey carries on in her honorable tradition with STAGESTRUCK (Soho Crime, $25), a brilliantly conceived and smartly executed mystery set in the hallowed Theater Royal of Bath.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Crime-t_CA0/bkr-Crime-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 584, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Crime-t_CA0/bkr-Crime-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/books/review/bkr-Crime-t_CA0/bkr-Crime-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Final Curtain', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Final Curtain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lovesey, Peter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sakey, Marcus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swierczynski, Duane', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/77d4c6c1-92df-5f0d-975f-1ecd43433f58', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77d4c6c1-92df-5f0d-975f-1ecd43433f58'}, {'abstract': 'A three-bedroom, three-and-a-half-bathroom co-op with 4,235 square feet of space and “quite a unique history,” according to the broker who is handling the $21.4 million listing.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/big-deal-all-units-are-unique-some-uniquely-so/', 'snippet': 'A three-bedroom, three-and-a-half-bathroom co-op with 4,235 square feet of space and “quite a unique history,” according to the broker who is handling the $21.4 million listing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Real estate brokers often describe the properties they market as “unique.” (Roughly 99.9 percent of the time.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Deal | All Units Are Unique, Some Uniquely So', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gramercy Park (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T17:11:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kershaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53b395d3-986c-5e34-a4fb-cb2558ee279f', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53b395d3-986c-5e34-a4fb-cb2558ee279f'}, {'abstract': 'The housing stock in Berkeley Heights runs the gamut from older frame houses, to 1960s-era split-levels, to recently built brick colonials; most have roomy front yards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/realestate/suburbia-but-not-the-cookie-cutter-kind-living-inberkeley-heights-nj.html', 'snippet': 'The housing stock in Berkeley Heights runs the gamut from older frame houses, to 1960s-era split-levels, to recently built brick colonials; most have roomy front yards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ADRIENNE NATRILLO and her husband, Javier Gomez, moved to Berkeley Heights, N.J., from Connecticut four years ago because the town was 15 miles from her office in East Hanover and had houses they thought were much better buys than those in nearby communities.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/realestate/living/living-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/realestate/living/living-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/realestate/living/living-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/realestate/living/living-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/realestate/living/living-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Suburbia, but Not the Cookie-Cutter Kind', 'kicker': 'Living In | Berkeley Heights, N.J.', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Suburbia, but Not the Cookie-Cutter Kind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 