 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/fashion/STUDIED/STUDIED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Botox, Looking Good and Feeling Less', 'kicker': 'Studied', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Botox, Looking Good And Feeling Less', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emotions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Botox (Drug)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Face', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T18:54:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7aba275-7808-52b9-9621-adf2e25e34f5', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7aba275-7808-52b9-9621-adf2e25e34f5'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to the latest travel articles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/travel/letters-crowd-free-colorado.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to the latest travel articles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor: Regarding the Practical Traveler column, “Bargain Summer Trips Free From Crowds” (May 29): Winter Park and the Grand Valley offer fabulous golfing on three stunning mountain courses. Dwight Eisenhower thought this valley, the headwaters of the Colorado River, to be the best fly-fishing in the country. Many agree. The southern entrance to Rocky Mountain National park is a few miles away. If you want an authentic Colorado experience for a family, rent a pontoon boat on Grand Lake then take the kids to the rodeo. You can ride at Devil’s Thumb Ranch or the Y.M.C.A. of the Rockies; all are within a few miles of the resort. There are several top-notch restaurants where you can enjoy creative food in a casual environment. Leave your mink coat at home.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: Crowd-Free Colorado', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'LETTERS; CROWD-FREE COLORADO', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T18:55:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/dac83b27-f578-54c0-b8fa-601c29560086', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dac83b27-f578-54c0-b8fa-601c29560086'}, {'abstract': 'Forty years after the honeymoon, a couple returns. What has changed more, the landscape or them?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/travel/provence-seen-in-youth-and-40-years-later.html', 'snippet': 'Forty years after the honeymoon, a couple returns. What has changed more, the landscape or them?', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE difference, for the traveler, between a first and repeated visit is crucial. To “go back” is not “to go.” Yet old, familiar places retain a kind of magic, and all the more so when the memories are shared. For our 40th wedding anniversary year, my wife, Elena, and I returned to the place where our marriage began: the South of France. We planned to visit old haunts, the area we’d once called home. Then, we had had all the time in the world; now we could spend a week. Too, there’s a difference between 20-somethings living on a shoestring and tourists “of a certain age” who stay in fine hotels.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Provence1_span/19Provence1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Provence1_span/19Provence1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Provence1_span/Provence-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Provence1_span/19Provence1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Provence1_span/19Provence1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Back to Provence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Back to Provence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Provence (France)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T18:55:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Delbanco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Delbanco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79aede98-d1ac-550c-ae96-6037e4f44e0d', 'word_count': 3180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79aede98-d1ac-550c-ae96-6037e4f44e0d'}, {'abstract': 'With nothing on the menu over $20, the high-tech flamboyance at this Chicago restaurant is done in the name of fun.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/travel/restaurant-report-ing-in-chicago.html', 'snippet': 'With nothing on the menu over $20, the high-tech flamboyance at this Chicago restaurant is done in the name of fun.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Chicago’s Fulton Market meatpacking district, the hot restaurant of the moment is Next, where the chef Grant Achatz will change the entire concept behind the menu every three months. Instead of reservations, the owners are selling nonrefundable tickets to dinner, offered, in order, to potential diners who have signed up online. Those who tire of waiting for notification might consider heading just two doors down to iNG, the latest project from Homaro Cantu, Chicago’s other cutting-edge chef.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19-BITES/19-BITES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19-BITES/19-BITES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19-BITES/19-BITES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Restaurant Report: ING, in Chicago', 'kicker': 'Bites', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chicago: iNG', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T18:55:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Cheshes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cheshes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9a2115c-d5a9-545f-8bb8-a73df0a81f5d', 'word_count': 445, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9a2115c-d5a9-545f-8bb8-a73df0a81f5d'}, {'abstract': 'Ernest Hemingway loved Madrid, leaving a distinct, mostly booze-stained trail.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/travel/a-tour-of-hemingways-madrid.html', 'snippet': 'Ernest Hemingway loved Madrid, leaving a distinct, mostly booze-stained trail.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the Legazpi neighborhood of Madrid, a vast complex of early 20th-century buildings of ornate stone and brick sits near the banks of the Manzanares River. For most of the 20th century, the Matadero Madrid, as the compound is known, was the city’s main slaughterhouse; its robust stench lingered far beyond the high stone walls surrounding it and deep into the working-class neighborhood nearby.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Madrid1_span/19Madrid1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Madrid1_span/19Madrid1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Madrid1_span/Madrid-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Madrid1_span/19Madrid1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Madrid1_span/19Madrid1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Blood, Sand, Sherry: Hemingway’s Madrid', 'kicker': 'Footsteps', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Blood, Sand, Sherry: Hemingway’s Madrid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hemingway, Ernest', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madrid (Spain)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T18:55:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Farley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ef1546d-4c93-5e32-8523-4f9904509d0d', 'word_count': 1867, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ef1546d-4c93-5e32-8523-4f9904509d0d'}, {'abstract': 'Several of the most innovative properties in Los Angeles, including homes the architects built for themselves, are open to the public.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/travel/in-los-angeles-seeing-distinctly-california-houses.html', 'snippet': 'Several of the most innovative properties in Los Angeles, including homes the architects built for themselves, are open to the public.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PERHAPS the most clichéd Los Angeles tourist activity is the curbside ogling of celebrity homes. But take away the famous residents, and Los Angeles is still the ultimate showplace of American dream houses. With legions of architects and Hollywood set designers equally adept at building a Southern plantation or a medieval Transylvanian castle, it’s no wonder that some streets are lined with English Tudors and Mexican haciendas that somehow look more authentic than anything you’d find in Chichester or Chihuahua.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Cultured1_span/19Cultured1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Cultured1_span/19Cultured1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Cultured1_span/Journeys-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Cultured1_span/19Cultured1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/travel/19Cultured1_span/19Cultured1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'California Houses as Celebrities in Themselves', 'kicker': 'Cultured Traveler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California Houses As Celebrities In Themselves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T18:55:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Ferr