6+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fa8a32c-6ea3-5f4d-9b94-0824357f665b', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fa8a32c-6ea3-5f4d-9b94-0824357f665b'}, {'abstract': 'The Senate’s vote Thursday to repeal tax breaks on ethanol will have little practical impact on the industry, at least in the short term.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/ethanol-industry-is-unruffled-by-senate-vote-against-tax-breaks/', 'snippet': 'The Senate’s vote Thursday to repeal tax breaks on ethanol will have little practical impact on the industry, at least in the short term.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Senate dealt the ethanol industry a rare defeat on Thursday when it voted 73-27 to end the annual $6 billion tax break given to blenders of ethanol, along with a tariff on foreign ethanol intended to protect the domestic industry from Brazilian sugar ethanol imports. The vote was on an amendment to a bill with a very uncertain future, but it did suggest that Congress will be looking at ethanol when it takes an ax to federal spending to get the budget deficit and national debt under control later this summer. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ethanol Industry Is Unruffled by Senate Vote Against Tax Breaks', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corn', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethanol', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sugar', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ARCHER-DANIELS-MIDLAND CO', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Renewable Fuels Assn', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T20:50:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clifford Krauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clifford', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0ade407-d861-5db6-92db-7e8af6301521', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0ade407-d861-5db6-92db-7e8af6301521'}, {'abstract': 'A new column tracking New York’s Twittersphere.  This week, it was dramatic.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/trending-nyc-the-week-on-twitter/', 'snippet': 'A new column tracking New York’s Twittersphere.  This week, it was dramatic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New Yorkers went to bed on Sunday and woke up on Monday buzzing — and posting — about the Tonys (also  #tonyawards), where a strong slate of nominees, a much-talked about and much-nominated “The Book of Mormon” and a disarming host — that would be Neil Patrick Harris or @ActuallyNPH, as he is known on Twitter — restored the theater awards to a place of unlikely hipness. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '#trendingNYC: The Week on Twitter', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T20:57:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63114353-ae03-567a-b84d-df66ec38670f', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63114353-ae03-567a-b84d-df66ec38670f'}, {'abstract': 'Patterned shorts and a busy tank will give you a head start on one of resort’s more pronounced trends.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/wear-resort-now-full-body-prints/', 'snippet': 'Patterned shorts and a busy tank will give you a head start on one of resort’s more pronounced trends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Come November, when resort hits the stores, you’ll see lots of head-to-toe pattern worn in one of two ways: You can either belt a matching top and bottom, à la Marc Jacobs, to look like a pseudo-jumpsuit, or you can wear your patterns all mixed up, à la Thakoon. Either way, this is one trend that can be activated right now with a pair of printed mini shorts, like the cotton ones from Etoile Isabel Marant ($225; go to net-a-porter.com), and an equally busy tank top from your closet. Just remember to stick to the same palette. Or, you could revive that printed onesie you were hoping never to wear again. Courage, girl.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wear Resort Now | Full Body Prints', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fd34b2d-e7b7-59ff-9d09-d6c5d3fee80e', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fd34b2d-e7b7-59ff-9d09-d6c5d3fee80e'}, {'abstract': 'On Sunday, the “Criminal Intent” branch of the deathless franchise airs “Icarus,” an episode loosely based on Broadway’s most attention-grabbing show.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/turn-on-the-tv-law-order-episode-inspired-by-spider-man-airs-on-sunday/', 'snippet': 'On Sunday, the “Criminal Intent” branch of the deathless franchise airs “Icarus,” an episode loosely based on Broadway’s most attention-grabbing show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been two days without any “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark” headlines but fear not: “Law & Order” is here to fill the gap, ripping the outlines of a script from the much-reported travails of that $70-million superhero musical, which opened on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turn On The TV: ‘Law & Order’ Episode Inspired by ‘Spider-Man’ Airs on Sunday', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nixon, Cynthia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Patti', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roundabout Theater Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'USA Network', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:01:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Heller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da36c241-3fc8-5fde-912e-923f59460d66', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da36c241-3fc8-5fde-912e-923f59460d66'}, {'abstract': 'Seth Kugel reports on his personal challenges for his Mediterranean trip: exercising regularly and learning to like olives.', 'web_url': 'https://frugaltraveler.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/exercise-and-olives-part-2/', 'snippet': 'Seth Kugel reports on his personal challenges for his Mediterranean trip: exercising regularly and learning to like olives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Dalmatian ham with Pag (a salty sheep cheese) that my parents and I ordered recently on the Croatian island of Mljet came with loads of olives. Fourteen, by my count. My mother, who likes olives, took four. My father, whose hatred of olives I’ve inherited, took none. Since I had challenged myself to eat every olive I could this summer in an effort to appreciate what so many of my friends love in them, that left me with 10. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exercise and Olives, Part 2', 'kicker': 'Frugal Traveler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Apulia (Italy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:01:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Kugel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kugel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66d0f8c9-a906-55f8-a4ed-a6669b2633cb', 'word_count': 394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66d0f8c9-a906-55f8-a4ed-a6669b2633cb'}, {'abstract': 'Learning on the job, in unexpected ways.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/becoming-a-father/', 'snippet': 'Learning on the job, in unexpected ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You don’t become a parent until you have a child. No class can really prepare you, and no conversation can truly make you ready. That said, having a child is just the start. What you think you know the moment they are born will change by the hour.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Becoming a Father', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Father's Day", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:03:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f37d26cf-9e77-551c-928c-7e2ea32cd6e3', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f37d26cf-9e77-551c-928c-7e2ea32cd6e3'}, {'abstract': 'Plates and glasses printed with standard or custom phrases add to the dinner conversation without saying a word.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/fashion/weddings/printed-tableware-adds-to-the-dinner-conversation-registry.html', 'snippet': 'Plates and glasses printed with standard or custom phrases add to the dinner conversation without saying a word.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IF it’s sparkling dinner-table conversation the happy couple is after, maybe they should let the dishes and glasses do the talking, too. These conversation pieces lend an edge to a\xa0setting that gold rims and floral motifs could never match. Some dinnerware and glassware come printed with standard sayings; others may be custom-ordered with whatever image, witticism or wise words fit your style. All will elicit a smile (or a smirk). ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/fashion/REGISTRY8/REGISTRY8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 898, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/fashion/REGISTRY8/REGISTRY8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/fashion/REGISTRY8/REGISTRY8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Voice at the Table: Your Plates', 'kicker': 'Registry', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Another Voice At the Table: Your Plates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gifts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tableware', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:05:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marianne Rohrlich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marianne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohrlich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16582273-c060-5afd-a0d6-74b298ac9ac1', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16582273-c060-5afd-a0d6-74b298ac9ac1'}, {'abstract': 'The Oaktree Capital Group, the firm founded by Howard Marks, filed for an initial public offering on Friday, the latest alternative asset manager looking to tap into the stock market.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/oaktree-capital-files-to-go-public/', 'snippet': 'The Oaktree Capital Group, the firm founded by Howard Marks, filed for an initial public offering on Friday, the latest alternative asset manager looking to tap into the stock market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Oaktree Capital Group filed for an initial public offering on Friday, the latest alternative asset manager looking to tap into the stock market.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oaktree Capital Files to Go Public', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oaktree Capital Management LLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pershing Square Capital Management', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:09:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fc38cc9-b9e6-547f-9587-168fd2a60989', 'word_count': 330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fc38cc9-b9e6-547f-9587-168fd2a60989'}, {'abstract': 'The Sony Ericsson Experia Play combines a phone and a PlayStation-style keypad to create a gaming phone.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/sony-playstation-phone-it-lives/', 'snippet': 'The Sony Ericsson Experia Play combines a phone and a PlayStation-style keypad to create a gaming phone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The folks at Sony Ericsson have engaged in the sort of experimentation that got Victor Frankenstein in trouble. They have combined a phone and a PlayStation-style keypad to create a gaming phone. Here you may imagine an evil laugh and crash of thunder if you wish.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sony PlayStation Phone: It Lives!', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'PlayStation (Video Game System)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Ericsson Mobile Communications', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:12:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/427061e2-db3e-5558-ae16-bfa03fe79558', 'word_count': 270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/427061e2-db3e-5558-ae16-bfa03fe79558'}, {'abstract': 'Doug Davis, who entered the game with an 0-5 record and a 5.90 earned run average, held the Yankees to three hits in seven and a third innings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/sports/baseball/yankees-visit-to-wrigley-starts-with-a-loss.html', 'snippet': 'Doug Davis, who entered the game with an 0-5 record and a 5.90 earned run average, held the Yankees to three hits in seven and a third innings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO — It had been eight years since the Yankees visited Wrigley Field, so for their return Friday, the City of Broad Shoulders opened its arms wide for them. The weather obliged, producing an idyllic June afternoon. A block party outside peddled New York-style hotdogs. And the Chicago Cubs cooperated by offering up a starting pitcher, Doug Davis, who had yet to win any of his six starts this season.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/18yankees2/18yankees2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/18yankees2/18yankees2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/18yankees2/18yankees2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/18yankees2/18yankees2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/18yankees2/18yankees2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Open Arms for Yankees, but Bats Can’t Deliver', 'kicker': 'Cubs 3, Yankees 1', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Open Arms for Yankees, But Bats Can’t Deliver', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Cubs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garcia, Freddy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Doug', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:17:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/868897d4-ada9-5e0e-8f59-6eb7bd3b471e', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/868897d4-ada9-5e0e-8f59-6eb7bd3b471e'}, {'abstract': 'As part of its approval process for new owners, Major League Baseball scrutinizes their personal, business and criminal backgrounds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/sports/baseball/company-led-by-potential-astros-buyer-ran-afoul-of-law.html', 'snippet': 'As part of its approval process for new owners, Major League Baseball scrutinizes their personal, business and criminal backgrounds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Major League Baseball investigates Jim Crane’s $680 million deal to buy the Houston Astros, it will be easy for its officials to find the public information about federal crimes committed by two executives of his former company, Eagle Global Logistics, a freight-forwarding company based in Houston.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/18astros1/18astros1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 838, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/18astros1/18astros1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/18astros1/18astros1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Company Led by Potential Astros Buyer Was Fined for Violations', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Potential Astros Buyer Led Company That Was Fined for Federal Crimes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Houston Astros', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crane, Jim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Baseball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:21:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01ee5f9c-64cd-508b-b0fc-073d657eaceb', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01ee5f9c-64cd-508b-b0fc-073d657eaceb'}, {'abstract': 'The former vice chairman of General Motors made numerous media appearances in Manhattan this week to promote a new book.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/bob-lutz-car-guy-on-the-make-in-manhattan/', 'snippet': 'The former vice chairman of General Motors made numerous media appearances in Manhattan this week to promote a new book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert A. Lutz, the former General Motors vice chairman, made whistle stops around New York this week to promote his new book, “Car Guys vs. Bean Counters: The Battle for the Soul of American Business.” He appeared Monday night at the Russian Tea Room in midtown on a program for the Hudson Union Society and spent the rest of the week doing radio and television appearances.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bob Lutz, Car Guy, on the Make in Manhattan', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lutz, Robert A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:22:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Patton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b927a396-5528-5c68-993a-aae937435405', 'word_count': 830, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b927a396-5528-5c68-993a-aae937435405'}, {'abstract': 'Curing small fillets is one of the more effortless ways to enjoy the fat, rich wild salmon that are in the prime of their season right now.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/dining/gravlax-party-food-without-a-party.html', 'snippet': 'Curing small fillets is one of the more effortless ways to enjoy the fat, rich wild salmon that are in the prime of their season right now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GRAVLAX is party food, the kind of thing I used to whip up for large, boisterous cocktail parties in my energetic pre-child days. The recipe started with an entire side of salmon and ended three days later when I carefully carved the fish, one rosy, gossamer sliver at a time.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/dining/22appe-span/APP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/22/dining/22appe-span/APP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/dining/22appe-span/APP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/dining/22appe-span/APP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/22/dining/22appe-span/APP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gravlax, Party Food Without a Party', 'kicker': 'A Good Appetite', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Party Food Without a Party', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salmon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:22:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f9c3562-146a-5735-836f-06e9a7076700', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f9c3562-146a-5735-836f-06e9a7076700'}, {'abstract': 'Medical examiners found that Jerry J. Borg died Dec. 15, 2010, of complications of pulmonary sarcoidosis, an inflammatory disease of the lungs, and that it was caused by inhaling dust thrown up during the collapse of the twin towers.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/another-name-added-to-the-list-of-sept-11-victims/', 'snippet': 'Medical examiners found that Jerry J. Borg died Dec. 15, 2010, of complications of pulmonary sarcoidosis, an inflammatory disease of the lungs, and that it was caused by inhaling dust thrown up during the collapse of the twin towers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jerry J. Borg was a bit-part actor and a small-time inventor, but his day job was as an accountant for New York State. On Sept. 11, 2001, he was working in an office a block or two from the World Trade Center.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Actor, Inventor, Accountant, and Now, a 9/11 Victim', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of the Chief Medical Examiner of the City of New York', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:23:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anemona Hartocollis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anemona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartocollis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65e3fab7-a80c-576e-a438-9f73f20874de', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65e3fab7-a80c-576e-a438-9f73f20874de'}, {'abstract': 'Barely cured, then barely sear it.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/a-good-appetite-gravlax-on-the-grill/', 'snippet': 'Barely cured, then barely sear it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most gravlax is served cured and cold, as with my version with fennel and tarragon. But recently, my friend Jeff Joseph told me about his innovative gravlax recipe, which requires a grill.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Good Appetite: Gravlax on the Grill', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:23:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/271ac3ff-0a77-5563-98ef-6fec9898d798', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/271ac3ff-0a77-5563-98ef-6fec9898d798'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Bittman revisits a soup recipe consisting of only peas, stock and sour cream.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/17/the-minimalist-cold-pea-soup/', 'snippet': 'Mark Bittman revisits a soup recipe consisting of only peas, stock and sour cream.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This Minimalist recipe comes from July 2001, an age of relative innocence (and youth!), or so it seems to me now. The column was on weeknight entertaining, made possible by a menu of make-ahead dishes, including white bean and garlic dip, green salad with fresh mild herbs and hazelnut oil, puréed fresh pea soup, salmon with cherry tomatoes en papillote, and coeur à la crème (that creamy French concoction that’s often chilled in heart-shaped molds and served with fresh fruit). Of these, only the salmon and dessert strike me as dated — the former because salmon no longer seems nearly as good and guilt-free as it did a decade ago, and the latter because these days I rarely indulge in desserts that are 80 percent full-fat dairy and 20 percent sugar, as coeur à la crème is. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Minimalist: Cold Pea Soup', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Peas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:35:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddd73e91-16c3-5498-add9-f3d645cba12c', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddd73e91-16c3-5498-add9-f3d645cba12c'}, {'abstract': 'This week: Jeff Sommer with Steve Lohr on lessons from I.B.M., David Gillen and Geraldine Fabrikant on Graff Diamonds, and Greg Mankiw on the health care consensus. And Sommer discusses financial history that rhymes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2011/06/17/business/economy/17weekendbiz_pod.html', 'snippet': 'This week: Jeff Sommer with Steve Lohr on lessons from I.B.M., David Gillen and Geraldine Fabrikant on Graff Diamonds, and Greg Mankiw on the health care consensus. And Sommer discusses financial history that rhymes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week: Jeff Sommer with Steve Lohr on lessons from I.B.M., David Gillen and Geraldine Fabrikant on Graff Diamonds, and Greg Mankiw on the health care consensus. And Sommer discusses financial history that rhymes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lessons From I.B.M.; Consensus On Health Care; Financial History That Rhymes.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:35:16+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/d7ab1c4a-6424-5ecd-b264-566de00ff94d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/d7ab1c4a-6424-5ecd-b264-566de00ff94d'}, {'abstract': 'The judges of “So You Think You Can Dance” made a surprise move on Thursday’s results episode and opted not to eliminate any of the remaining contestants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/arts/television/a-surprise-on-fox.html', 'snippet': 'The judges of “So You Think You Can Dance” made a surprise move on Thursday’s results episode and opted not to eliminate any of the remaining contestants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The judges of “So You Think You Can Dance” made a surprise move on Thursday’s results episode and opted not to eliminate any of the remaining contestants. But ratings for that Fox reality show were modest, according to Nielsen’s estimates. The network finished in third place, garnering 6.3 million viewers for “So You Think You Can Dance” at 8 p.m. and 3.4 million at 9 for a rerun of “Glee.” CBS led with repeats of “CSI” (7.3 million) and “The Mentalist” (8.1 million) from 9 to 11 after reruns of “The Big Bang Theory” (6.7 million) and “Rules of Engagement” (5.6 million) at 8 and 8:30. ABC was third, averaging 5.8 million for “Wipeout” reruns from 8 to 10, but 3.5 million viewers at 10 for a repeat of “Rookie Blue.” NBC lagged in fourth, drawing 2.3 million in the 10 p.m. hour for “Love Bites.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Surprise on Fox', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Surprise on Fox', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:41:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/86088691-6817-5707-90e1-9093f5389ac6', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86088691-6817-5707-90e1-9093f5389ac6'}, {'abstract': 'Egyptian prosecutors are seeking the extradition of Hussein K. Salem to be tried on corruption charges related to his past dealings with the former president, Hosni Mubarak.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/world/middleeast/18egypt.html', 'snippet': 'Egyptian prosecutors are seeking the extradition of Hussein K. Salem to be tried on corruption charges related to his past dealings with the former president, Hosni Mubarak.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MADRID — Spanish judges set bail of more than $38 million on Friday for a billionaire and friend of former President Hosni Mubarak of Egypt.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bail Set for Billionaire Accused of Corruption With Mubarak', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bail Set for Billionaire Accused of Corruption With Mubarak', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salem, Hussein K', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Extradition', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-17T21:42:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raphael Minder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raphael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Minder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/812f54b8-9cfc-558d-ad66-671f98d957a4', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/812f54b8-9cfc-558d-ad66-671f98d957a4'}, {'abstract': 'A slide show of photographs of cultural events from this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/19/arts/design/20110619-WIC-ss.html', 'snippet': 'A slide