e Open this week — and one of the youngest in Open history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/sports/golf/us-open-a-normal-teenager-whos-playing-in-the-open.html', 'snippet': 'At 16, Beau Hossler is the youngest player at the Open this week — and one of the youngest in Open history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BETHESDA, Md. — A quick glance at Beau Hossler staring down the fairway for his second shot Friday at the United States Open revealed hints that he was unlike the typical player here.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/YKID/YKID-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 352, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/YKID/YKID-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 352}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/YKID/YKID-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/YKID/YKID-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/YKID/YKID-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Normal Teenager, Who’s Playing in the Open', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Normal Teenager, Who’s Playing in the Open', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hossler, Beau', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Golf)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T00:05:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Macur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macur', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9013dfdf-a1b4-5f99-bd09-8f9dafe4568a', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9013dfdf-a1b4-5f99-bd09-8f9dafe4568a'}, {'abstract': 'A Senate committee plans to investigate whether the Bush administration sought to have federal officials collect personal information on a critic of the Iraq war in order to discredit him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/us/politics/18brfs-SENATEINQUIR_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'A Senate committee plans to investigate whether the Bush administration sought to have federal officials collect personal information on a critic of the Iraq war in order to discredit him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Senator Dianne Feinstein, a California Democrat and chairwoman of the Senate Intelligence Committee, said Friday that the panel would investigate accusations that the White House of President George W. Bush sought to have federal intelligence officials collect damaging personal information on a prominent critic of the Iraq war in order to discredit him. In a brief statement, Mrs. Feinstein said, “Depending on what we find, we may take further action.” She was responding to an article in The New York Times on Thursday in which a former senior C.I.A. official said the Bush White House sought to direct intelligence officials to collect sensitive information on the critic, Juan Cole, a University of Michigan professor and Middle East expert. The Central Intelligence Agency has denied the accusations and said agency officials never provided any derogatory information to the White House about Mr. Cole. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Senate Inquiry Promised in Case of War Critic', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WASHINGTON', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Senate Inquiry Promised in Case of War Critic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate Committee on Intelligence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cole, Juan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T00:05:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Risen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Risen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/86c2e0a3-3921-596e-a3bb-e46b3693d5be', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86c2e0a3-3921-596e-a3bb-e46b3693d5be'}, {'abstract': 'Terrace on the Park, the catering hall in Flushing Meadows-Corona Park, has three cavernous ballrooms, a versatile kitchen and breathtaking views.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/nyregion/64-worlds-fair-leftover-in-demand-for-proms-and-weddings.html', 'snippet': 'Terrace on the Park, the catering hall in Flushing Meadows-Corona Park, has three cavernous ballrooms, a versatile kitchen and breathtaking views.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This being June, weddings and proms are colliding at Terrace on the Park banquet hall in Flushing Meadows-Corona Park, and doing so with a particularly Queensian flair.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/nyregion/METJOURNAL2/METJOURNAL2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/18/nyregion/METJOURNAL2/METJOURNAL2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/nyregion/METJOURNAL2/METJOURNAL2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Towers With Babel’s Diversity, but a Much Happier History', 'kicker': 'Flushing Meadows Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Towers With Babel’s Diversity, But a Much Happier History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "World's Fair", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T00:08:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Berger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78f0dd67-47fd-5e2a-8267-a8b1cbba6092', 'word_count': 1052, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78f0dd67-47fd-5e2a-8267-a8b1cbba6092'}, {'abstract': 'Technical problems will keep a Turkish humanitarian aid ship that was the target of a fatal raid by the Israeli military last year from joining a flotilla set to leave for Gaza next week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/world/europe/18briefs-Turkey.html', 'snippet': 'Technical problems will keep a Turkish humanitarian aid ship that was the target of a fatal raid by the Israeli military last year from joining a flotilla set to leave for Gaza next week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Technical problems will keep a Turkish humanitarian aid ship that was the target of a fatal raid by the Israeli military last year from joining a flotilla that is set to leave for Gaza next week, the pro-Palestinian charity that owns the boat announced on Friday. The charity, the Humanitarian Relief Foundation, said the decision was not the result of political pressure from the government. Nine people were killed in the raid on the ship, the Mavi Marmara, straining ties between Turkey and Israel. The new flotilla is expected to challenge Israel’s blockade of the Gaza Strip this month. Israeli military officials warned Thursday that they would use force if necessary to prevent boats from breaking the blockade. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkey: Aid Ship Won’t Join Flotilla', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turkey: Aid Ship Won’t Join Flotilla', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanitarian Aid', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T00:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sebnem Arsu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebnem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4aba0dad-c398-5916-a0e3-9759b34b785a', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4aba0dad-c398-5916-a0e3-9759b34b785a'}, {'abstract': 'A scheduled property trial could determine whether the team will continue to belong to Frank McCourt or will be sold as a community asset.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/sports/baseball/one-day-trial-will-decide-if-dodgers-are-sold.html', 'snippet': 'A scheduled property trial could determine whether the team will continue to belong to Frank McCourt or will be sold as a community asset.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Dodgers’ ownership situation, and perhaps the team’s future, gained some clarity Friday in a divorce settlement reached by Frank and Jamie McCourt. The judge overseeing the divorce scheduled a one-day trial on Aug. 4 to determine if the team will remain solely Frank McCourt’s or will be sold as a community property asset.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/DODGERS1/DODGERS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 646, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/DODGERS1/DODGERS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/18/sports/DODGERS1/DODGERS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Short Trial May Decide if Dodgers Are Sold', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Short Trial Could Decide If Dodgers Will Be Sold', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Divorce, Separations and Annulments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Dodgers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCourt, Frank H Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCourt, Jamie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Selig, Bud', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T00:09:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db127ee8-f70c-5e4d-9357-dfacf53d0bf7', 'word_count': 656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db127ee8-f70c-5e4d-9357-dfacf53d0bf7'}, {'abstract': 'China is bulking up its maritime surveillance operations, according to a report on Friday in China Daily, the official English-language newspaper.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/world/asia/18briefs-China.html', 'snippet': 'China is bulking up its maritime surveillance operations, according to a report on Friday in China Daily, the official English-language newspaper.', 'lead_paragraph': ' China is bulking up its maritime surveillance operations, according to a report on Friday in China Daily, the official English-language newspaper. The force will grow to 15,000 people from 9,000 by 2020, the report said. By 2015, the aviation branch of the force, which is officially civilian, will have 16 aircraft, and by 2020, its sea patrol fleet will have 520 vessels. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Maritime Force Is Beefed Up', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | ASIA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Maritime Force Is Beefed Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T00:14:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d23d03a-e144-57c1-98d7-4215f6c3c580', 'word_count': 62, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d23d03a-e144-57c1-98d7-4215f6c3c580'}, {'abstract': 'Police officials say they recovered a nearly 800-year-old relic of St. Anthony — the patron saint of lost objects — from the home of Maria Solis, who they say stole it from a church on Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/us/18brfs-California.html', 'snippet': 'Police officials say they recovered a nearly 800-year-old relic of St. Anthony — the patron saint of lost objects — from the home of Maria Solis, who they say stole it from a church on Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The parishioners of St. Anthony’s Catholic Church in Long Beach may well think their prayers have been answered. Police officials say they recovered a nearly 800-year-old relic of St. Anthony — the patron saint of lost objects — from the home of Maria Solis, who they say stole it from the church on Monday. Rico Fernandez, a spokesman for the Long Beach Police Department, said officers used surveillance video from a nearby business to identify Ms. Solis, 41, who was arrested on burglary charges. Mr. Fernandez said that the relic appeared to be undamaged and was promptly returned to the church on Thursday, but that officers were still questioning Ms. Solis. The relic had been on rare display for Mass last Sunday, and church officials realized early Monday that it had disappeared from its glass case. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California: Woman Arrested in Relic’s Disappearance', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WEST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California: Woman Arrested in Relic’s Disappearance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T00:16:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Medina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Medina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/eed22225-4dd8-5271-9db4-698657e9ae0a', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eed22225-4dd8-5271-9db4-698657e9ae0a'}, {'abstract': 'A walking tour of a bygone musical world where you didn’t need a degree from Juilliard, though you might have, later in life, needed a hearing aid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/nyregion/quirky-tour-of-a-new-york-punk-scene-of-a-bygone-era.html', 'snippet': 'A walking tour of a bygone musical world where you didn’t need a degree from Juilliard, though you might have, later in life, needed a hearing aid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Enough already with the jazz. It’s all over the city this month, what with the Blue Note Jazz Festival, the Undead Jazzfest and the Vision Festival: flitty, formless music that makes me want to bang my head against some bathroom porcelain. I had to go see Jake Szufnarowski and John Joseph to restore my heavy-punk-metal-thrashing-garage-acid-hard-rock soul.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/nyregion/19jpCRITIC2/19jpCRITIC2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 659, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/nyregion/19jpCRITIC2/19jpCRITIC2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/nyregion/19jpCRITIC2/19jpCRITIC2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Walk This Way: The Punk Tour', 'kicker': 'City Critic', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Walk This Way: The Punk Tour', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Walking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lower East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T00:17:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc19bc85-af1a-5af8-af14-aa11feb44d36', 'word_count': 973, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc19bc85-af1a-5af8-af14-aa11feb44d36'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Haast relied on daily injections of snake venom to protect him from the 173 bites he sustained while handling some three million snakes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/us/18haast.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Haast relied on daily injections of snake venom to protect him from the 173 bites he sustained while handling some three million snakes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bill Haast figured he had handled more than three million poisonous snakes over the years, and he had the hands to prove it.', '