lead_paragraph': 'IN March 1969, Charles M. Schulz created six sequential “Peanuts” cartoon strips following the pensive beagle Snoopy on a journey to the moon and back. They not only reflected America’s anticipation of Apollo 11 — the first manned lunar landing, which would take place in July of that year — but also drew on the relationship between NASA and the “Peanuts” gang. That connection is the subject of “To the Moon: Snoopy Soars With NASA,” now on view at the Stamford Museum and Nature Center.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/nyregion/19MUSECT1/19MUSECT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 124, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/19/nyregion/19MUSECT1/19MUSECT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 124}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/nyregion/19MUSECT1/19MUSECT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 212, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/nyregion/19MUSECT1/19MUSECT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/nyregion/19MUSECT1/19MUSECT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Snoopy Landed on the Moon', 'kicker': 'Arts | Connecticut', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Snoopy Landed on the Moon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stamford Museum and Nature Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons