'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Laugh Lines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T14:44:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9dbe88f-fbfc-583e-a1ac-742957d23827', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9dbe88f-fbfc-583e-a1ac-742957d23827'}, {'abstract': 'Ten years after it became standard gear in the United States Army, the beret has been replaced by the patrol cap as the default head gear for soldiers wearing combat uniforms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/weekinreview/19grist.html', 'snippet': 'Ten years after it became standard gear in the United States Army, the beret has been replaced by the patrol cap as the default head gear for soldiers wearing combat uniforms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The black beret is out. Ten years after it became standard gear in the United States Army, it has been replaced by the patrol cap as the default head gear for soldiers wearing combat uniforms, CNN reports. Soldiers had complained that the wool beret was hot, hard to adjust and took two hands to put on. Soldiers who were already wearing berets, and who will continue to wear them — like Special Forces, airborne troops and Army Rangers — were not happy either. They thought that having every soldier wear a beret diminished the work they had done to earn theirs. The decision by Gen. Martin Dempsey, the Army chief of staff, reverses a policy laid down by a predecessor, Gen. Eric Shinseki. The patrol cap looks like a baseball cap, but with a flat, not rounded, top. Soldiers wearing their dress uniforms will still wear berets, but by reducing the number they will need from two to one, the Army will save $6.5 million.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/weekinreview/GRIST/GRIST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/19/weekinreview/GRIST/GRIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/19/weekinreview/GRIST/GRIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Out of Fashion', 'kicker': 'Grist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out of Fashion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T14:48:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4587378c-3f85-5ec7-b7f0-f1493f68b29f', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4587378c-3f85-5ec7-b7f0-f1493f68b29f'}, {'abstract': 'The annual cost, in millions of dollars, of insuring $10 million in Greek sovereign debt for five years as of Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/weekinreview/19primenumber.html', 'snippet': 'The annual cost, in millions of dollars, of insuring $10 million in Greek sovereign debt for five years as of Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The annual cost, in millions of dollars, of insuring $10 million in Greek sovereign debt for five years as of Thursday. Greece’s financial and political crisis, compounded by new fears about the pace of the economic recovery in the United States, sent financial markets reeling last week. The worry is that in the worst case, a Greek debt default would lead to damaging losses for European banks and replay the global panic of 2008. Markets calmed a bit after Chancellor Angela Merkel of Germany retreated from her demand that private financial institutions be pressured to help rescue the Greek economy. But the potential for trouble in Europe remained.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prime Number: 2', 'kicker': 'Prime Number', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T14:50:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15f18bef-d34f-5b4b-baf0-2ff5add479d1', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15f18bef-d34f-5b4b-baf0-2ff5add479d1'}, {'abstract': 'Some school districts are lowering the homework load, to try to reduce the pressure on students. But the competition for college admission and the stress of testing remain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/weekinreview/19fight.html', 'snippet': 'Some school districts are lowering the homework load, to try to reduce the pressure on students. But the competition for college admission and the stress of testing remain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some school districts are lowering the homework load, to try to lower the pressure on students. But the competition for college admission, not to mention the stress of testing, remains.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fighting Words', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fighting Words', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T14:53:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b6f6a30-af6b-5755-880a-a28cf1a2e5f5', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b6f6a30-af6b-5755-880a-a28cf1a2e5f5'}, {'abstract': 'Penny Toler scored the first points in the history of the W.N.B.A. for the Los Angeles Sparks, the team for which she has been the longtime general manager.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/18/30-seconds-with-penny-toler-a-w-n-b-a-pioneer/', 'snippet': 'Penny Toler scored the first points in the history of the W.N.B.A. for the Los Angeles Sparks, the team for which she has been the longtime general manager.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On June 21, 1997, the Liberty and the Los Angeles Sparks took the court at the Great Western Forum in Inglewood , Calif., for the first game in the W.N.B.A. There were 14,284 fans in the stands to see the Liberty win, 67-57. Penny Toler, who had played professionally overseas for eight years, was the Sparks’ starting point guard, and she scored the first points in the league’s history. The W.N.B.A., in its 15th season, is recognizing the anniversary of the game Tuesday night with a matchup between the Liberty and the Sparks at Staples Center in Los Angeles. Toler, 45, will be there for the anniversary. She is in her 12th season as the Sparks’ general manager and has built two championship teams.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '30 Seconds With Penny Toler, a W.N.B.A Pioneer', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Sparks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Women's National Basketball Assn", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-18T14:53:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None,