jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Women Love One Man, Worthy of Neither', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Women Love One Man, Worthy of Neither', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Lee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T21:54:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Langer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Langer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cf6009b-398d-5344-b23a-269bd4de8f25', 'word_count': 992, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cf6009b-398d-5344-b23a-269bd4de8f25'}, {'abstract': 'The Royal Ballet in East London shed pretenses and decamped to the O2 arena to perform “Romeo and Juliet,” enjoyed by the masses along with beer, nachos and hot dogs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/arts/dance/royal-ballet-courts-the-masses-in-a-london-arena.html', 'snippet': 'The Royal Ballet in East London shed pretenses and decamped to the O2 arena to perform “Romeo and Juliet,” enjoyed by the masses along with beer, nachos and hot dogs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The idea was to bring the ballet to the masses, so it was perhaps inevitable that the masses would bring nachos, beer and hot dogs to the ballet. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBROMEO/SUBROMEO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 799, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBROMEO/SUBROMEO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBROMEO/SUBROMEO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beer. Nachos. Arena. Ballet?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beer. Nachos. Arena. Ballet?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Ballet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T21:57:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Lyall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d25f82f6-78f1-5fbf-b584-f91dc1923926', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d25f82f6-78f1-5fbf-b584-f91dc1923926'}, {'abstract': 'Lynn Lempel strong-arms us into doing her puzzle.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/crime/', 'snippet': 'Lynn Lempel strong-arms us into doing her puzzle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Administrivial Upcoming Tournament Alert!: If you plan to be in the New York City area in August, sign yourself up for the most fun you can have with a sharpened #2 pencil, Lollapuzzoola 4. Hosted by Brian Cimmet and Patrick Blindauer (who is not at all bitter that Brian beat him out as ACPT Division E Winner in 2009, I don’t care what Brian says), Lollapuzzoola is tons of fun and gaggles of crossword challenges in a single day for the low, low cost of $25.00. Kick in $5.00 more and they will convince you that you will be getting pizza at the end. Clicky here to read more about it and sign up.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Employee-Abetted Crime', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67c388e3-9eae-553b-b711-bf8f0090e15d', 'word_count': 425, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67c388e3-9eae-553b-b711-bf8f0090e15d'}, {'abstract': 'Julie Taylor, who was fired from “Spider-Man” in March, speaks out against theatrical focus groups and instant Web and twitter critics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/theater/julie-taymor-discusses-spider-man-and-twitter-critics.html', 'snippet': 'Julie Taylor, who was fired from “Spider-Man” in March, speaks out against theatrical focus groups and instant Web and twitter critics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Breaking her silence about “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark,” the Broadway musical from which she was fired in March, Julie Taymor tacitly criticized her former producers on Saturday afternoon for relying on audience focus groups and said that the rise of Twitter and blogs for instant theater criticism was damaging to shows.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/TAYMOR/TAYMOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taymor Tries to Reclaim a Reputation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taymor Tries To Reclaim A Reputation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taymor, Julie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:02:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25491640-5c94-5ccf-a87c-fe76ce13a23f', 'word_count': 1111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25491640-5c94-5ccf-a87c-fe76ce13a23f'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Bonner was a fierce protector of her dissident husband and a force in her own right on behalf of human rights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/world/europe/20bonner.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Bonner was a fierce protector of her dissident husband and a force in her own right on behalf of human rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elena G. Bonner, the Soviet dissident and human-rights campaigner who endured banishment and exile along with her husband, the dissident nuclear physicist Andrei D. Sakharov, died Saturday in Boston. She was 88.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/obituaries/20bonner-span/20bonner-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/20/obituaries/20bonner-span/20bonner-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/obituaries/20bonner-span/20bonner-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/obituaries/20bonner-span/20bonner-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/obituaries/20bonner-span/20bonner-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Elena Bonner, Widow of Sakharov, Dies at 88', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Elena Bonner, Human-Rights Activist And Widow of Sakharov, Dies at 88', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bonner, Elena', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sakharov, Andrei D', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:04:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alessandra Stanley and Michael Schwirtz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alessandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stanley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwirtz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/b91912a2-753b-5617-9dc8-77eaf9b73d1a', 'word_count': 1240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b91912a2-753b-5617-9dc8-77eaf9b73d1a'}, {'abstract': 'Even as Al Jazeera English continues its battle for broader cable distribution in the United States, it is reaping a growing audience in Los Angeles on a broadcast channel.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/al-jazeera-at-home-on-a-former-pbs-outlet/', 'snippet': 'Even as Al Jazeera English continues its battle for broader cable distribution in the United States, it is reaping a growing audience in Los Angeles on a broadcast channel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even as Al Jazeera English continues its battle for broader cable distribution in the United States, it is reaping a growing audience in Los Angeles on a broadcast channel.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Al Jazeera at Home on a Former PBS Outlet', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL JAZEERA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'KCET-TV', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:06:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a06a0ddf-6ed6-5dbc-a2ad-96abf7db9aff', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a06a0ddf-6ed6-5dbc-a2ad-96abf7db9aff'}, {'abstract': 'Katy Perry’s kewpie-doll smiles, on display at the Nassau Coliseum on Friday night, are camouflage to keep parents from worrying.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/arts/music/katy-perrys-sweetened-raunch-at-nassau-coliseum-review.html', 'snippet': 'Katy Perry’s kewpie-doll smiles, on display at the Nassau Coliseum on Friday night, are camouflage to keep parents from worrying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNIONDALE, N.Y. — Katy Perry shrewdly polled her sold-out audience — at least 85 percent female, young and eager to scream — at Nassau Coliseum on Friday night. She asked who had just graduated from college, high school and junior high, and junior high won, decisively. That may be why Ms. Perry made her concert all bright colors, candy-shaped scenery and wide-eyed, kewpie-doll smiles. It was camouflage to keep parents from worrying. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/PERRY/PERRY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 665, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/PERRY/PERRY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/PERRY/PERRY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Giggles and Cotton Candy Camouflaging the Raunch', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Giggles and Cotton Candy Camouflaging the Raunch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Katy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:06:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/66f16111-7ce7-5f26-9f79-98618b7dd0d6', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66f16111-7ce7-5f26-9f79-98618b7dd0d6'}, {'abstract': 'In the season finale of “The Killing” on AMC, the murder of Rosie Larsen is resolved, but with a touch of uncertainty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/arts/television/the-killing-on-amc-solves-murder-in-season-finale.html', 'snippet': 'In the season finale of “The Killing” on AMC, the murder of Rosie Larsen is resolved, but with a touch of uncertainty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early on in “The Killing,” the American adaptation of a Danish thriller that has had a talked-about run on AMC, an unusually creepy funeral director guides a pair of bereaved parents through the retail dimensions of his mortuary. Mitch and Stan Larsen have lost their 17-year-old daughter, Rosie, to a deranged act of violence and stare achingly into the bottom of an expensive coffin they cannot afford. Trailing the couple as though they were shopping for hatchbacks, the undertaker offers that the object of their attention is “a beautiful choice for a young girl.” He goes on to instruct the Larsens on how their child should look for her interment, discreetly relishing the thought of her corpse in a pretty dress.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/KILLINGS/KILLINGS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Obsessive Killer Is Revealed in a Stylish Whodunit', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Murder and Melodrama: An Obsessive Killer Is Revealed in a Stylish Whodunit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:08:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df606d8b-48d6-5fdb-9459-65f5db6fca53', 'word_count': 741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df606d8b-48d6-5fdb-9459-65f5db6fca53'}, {'abstract': 'The Ojai North! festival in California concluded with a program including George Crumb’s song cycle and a performance by an East Bay Afghan ensemble.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/arts/music/dawn-upshaw-in-winds-of-destiny-at-ojai-north.html', 'snippet': 'The Ojai North! festival in California concluded with a program including George Crumb’s song cycle and a performance by an East Bay Afghan ensemble.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERKELEY, Calif. — Though the experienced arts administrator Thomas W. Morris is the official artistic director of the Ojai Music Festival, much of the artistic planning for this innovative annual four-day event is turned over each June to a rotating music director, who organizes the programs. This summer that artist was the soprano Dawn Upshaw, and not surprisingly she devised diverse and adventurous offerings. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBNORTH/SUBNORTH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 779, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Afghanistan Veteran, Afghan Sounds', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Afghanistan Veteran, Afghan Sounds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:09:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/59eb3bd3-2623-55a7-be51-163dfdf37725', 'word_count': 645, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59eb3bd3-2623-55a7-be51-163dfdf37725'}, {'abstract': 'In addition to John Adams’s “Death of Klinghoffer,” the Opera Theater of St. Louis offered works by Mozart, Debussy and Donizetti.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/arts/music/opera-theater-of-st-louis-review.html', 'snippet': 'In addition to John Adams’s “Death of Klinghoffer,” the Opera Theater of St. Louis offered works by Mozart, Debussy and Donizetti.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. LOUIS — The Opera Theater of St. Louis is a small, hardy institution not averse to risk. Its choice of John Adams’s “Death of Klinghoffer,” which opened triumphantly on Wednesday, was only the latest proof. Founded in 1976 and modeled after the Santa Fe Opera, another seasonal company, the theater earned its renown by championing opera in English, including standard works sung in translation, and by offering premieres and unusual, important revivals among its steady supply of user-friendly chestnuts.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/PELLEAS1/PELLEAS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 623, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Orphan, a Libertine and the Walking Dead', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Orphan, a Libertine And the Walking Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Opera Theater of St Louis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:11:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d983908-e71b-578b-9ea4-b2e0876162a7', 'word_count': 913, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d983908-e71b-578b-9ea4-b2e0876162a7'}, {'abstract': '“The High Life,” a drama by the documentarian Zhao Dayong, is the story of a scam artist that turns into a political metaphor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/movies/zhao-dayongs-high-life-starring-shen-shaoqiu-review.html', 'snippet': '“The High Life,” a drama by the documentarian Zhao Dayong, is the story of a scam artist that turns into a political metaphor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a pair of critically lauded independent documentaries, “Street Life” and “Ghost Town,” Zhao Dayong turns to fiction in his new feature, “The High Life.” His subjects are the same — the rootlessness and avarice that afflict contemporary China, and the agonies of self-expression in a country that limits speech and effaces identity. His storytelling skills, however, are not at the same level in this awkward mash-up of what feel like two completely different movies.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBHIGH/SUBHIGH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 334, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBHIGH/SUBHIGH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 334}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBHIGH/SUBHIGH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 986, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBHIGH/SUBHIGH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBHIGH/SUBHIGH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Captive to Politics and Radical Poetry', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The High LIfe'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Captive to Politics and Radical Poetry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:16:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/baec4bc3-5819-5892-ab13-7a78eca7174d', 'word_count': 384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/baec4bc3-5819-5892-ab13-7a78eca7174d'}, {'abstract': 'A pivotal deal in the semifinal of the trials to choose d’Orsi Senior Bowl teams.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/crosswords/bridge/a-dorsi-bowl-bid-for-gaylor-kasle-bridge.html', 'snippet': 'A pivotal deal in the semifinal of the trials to choose d’Orsi Senior Bowl teams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 60-board match that chose the U.S.A.-2 d’Orsi Senior Bowl Cup world championship team this month in Detroit was won by Gaylor Kasle of Boca Raton, Fla.; Larry Kozlove of Louisville, Ky.; Peter Boyd of Darnestown, Md.; Steve Robinson of Arlington, Va.; Neil Chambers of Schenectady, N.Y.; and John Schermer of Seattle.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Great Time for a Perfect Read', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Great Time for a Perfect Read', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:22:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3017925c-12b3-51f3-9d48-0b0a48a5bfb3', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3017925c-12b3-51f3-9d48-0b0a48a5bfb3'}, {'abstract': 'Amy Winehouse canceled part of her European tour after strange behavior during a performance on Saturday night in Belgrade, Serbia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/arts/music/a-booed-winehouse-cancels-shows.html', 'snippet': 'Amy Winehouse canceled part of her European tour after strange behavior during a performance on Saturday night in Belgrade, Serbia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Only weeks since checking out of an alcohol rehabilitation program, the British pop star Amy Winehouse, right, canceled part of her European tour after strange behavior during a performance on Saturday night in Belgrade, Serbia. During the concert, which one Serbian newspaper called “the worst in the history of Belgrade,” Ms. Winehouse was booed as she mumbled and stumbled her way through her performance, The Associated Press reported. A spokesman for Ms. Winehouse said that she would return to London after agreeing with management that “she cannot perform to the best of her ability.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Booed Winehouse Cancels Shows', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Booed Winehouse Cancels Shows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winehouse, Amy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:25:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/612fb2ef-7b71-5bcc-846f-4d5d8ab40bd7', 'word_count': 97, 'uri': 'nyt://article/612fb2ef-7b71-5bcc-846f-4d5d8ab40bd7'}, {'abstract': 'The world premiere of a play by David Rabe is among the New Group’s productions for the 2011-12 season, the theater company announced.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/theater/new-group-lineup-features-rabe-play.html', 'snippet': 'The world premiere of a play by David Rabe is among the New Group’s productions for the 2011-12 season, the theater company announced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world premiere of a play by David Rabe is among the New Group’s productions for the 2011-12 season, the theater company announced. “An Early History of Fire,” a coming-of-age story set in the 1960s and directed by Jo Bonney, heralds Mr. Rabe’s return to original theater production after an eight-year absence. The Off Broadway New Group company previously collaborated with Mr. Rabe on a revival of his 1984 play, “Hurlyburly.” Other coming productions include “Russian Transport” by Erika Sheffer and “Burning” by Thomas Bradshaw (“The Bereaved”). Both plays will be directed by the company’s artistic director, Scott Elliott (“Marie and Bruce”).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Group Lineup Features Rabe Play', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Group Lineup Features Rabe Play', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:26:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ab82490-b20e-5353-aeae-d4e7c7f96b44', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ab82490-b20e-5353-aeae-d4e7c7f96b44'}, {'abstract': 'The London production of “Love Never Dies,” the sequel to “Phantom of the Opera,” will close on Aug. 27 after running for a little more than a year, BBC News reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/theater/love-does-in-fact-die.html', 'snippet': 'The London production of “Love Never Dies,” the sequel to “Phantom of the Opera,” will close on Aug. 27 after running for a little more than a year, BBC News reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The London production of “Love Never Dies,” the sequel to “Phantom of the Opera,” will close on Aug. 27 after running for a little more than a year, BBC News reported. Andrew Lloyd Webber’s troubled musical received poor opening reviews, underwent numerous rewrites and was ultimately met with a tepid reception at the box office. Although it was nominated this year for seven Olivier awards, including best new musical, it won none. The Sydney Morning Herald recently reported that Mr. Lloyd Webber would prefer to bring the better received Australian version to Broadway in the future, instead of the West End version.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Love Does in Fact Die', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love Does in Fact Die', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lloyd Webber, Andrew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:27:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa6ae629-7e78-5e54-a153-da0c78173a4a', 'word_count': 107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa6ae629-7e78-5e54-a153-da0c78173a4a'}, {'abstract': 'There is no “crime gene,” but researchers at the National Institute of Justice conference will consider looking for inherited traits that are linked to aggression and antisocial behaviors, which may lead to violent crime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/arts/genetics-and-crime-at-institute-of-justice-conference.html', 'snippet': 'There is no “crime gene,” but researchers at the National Institute of Justice conference will consider looking for inherited traits that are linked to aggression and antisocial behaviors, which may lead to violent crime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was less than 20 years ago that the National Institutes of Health abruptly withdrew funds for a conference on genetics and crime after outraged complaints that the idea smacked of eugenics. The president of the Association of Black Psychologists at the time declared that such research was in itself\xa0 “a blatant form of stereotyping and racism.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Genetic Basis for Crime: A New Look', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Genetic Basis For Crime: A New Look', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Institute of Justice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forensic Science', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:33:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5e5267b-76b2-58dc-97fc-7f03dc992906', 'word_count': 1376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5e5267b-76b2-58dc-97fc-7f03dc992906'}, {'abstract': 'The Daily Hookup, an online deal site aimed at affluent gay men, hopes to carve out a profitable niche in the hotly contested social buying space.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/technology/20hookup.html', 'snippet': 'The Daily Hookup, an online deal site aimed at affluent gay men, hopes to carve out a profitable niche in the hotly contested social buying space.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like millions of people, Mario Correa wakes up every morning to an in-box full of offers and deals. But most of the time, he just isn’t interested. As he puts it, he doesn’t “want to learn to knit, go on a Segway ride, get my varicose veins removed or take a mommy-and-me yoga class.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/business/hookup/hookup-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/20/business/hookup/hookup-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/business/hookup/hookup-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/business/hookup/hookup-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/business/hookup/hookup-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Daily Deal Site Aimed Squarely at Gay Men', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Daily Deal Site Aimed Squarely at Gay Men', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coupons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:33:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hannah Seligson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hannah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seligson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b06f6d2-29e0-5657-8135-fb5b7a166f50', 'word_count': 836, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b06f6d2-29e0-5657-8135-fb5b7a166f50'}, {'abstract': 'The young Bolshoi Ballet star Ivan Vasiliev charmed his way across the Metropolitan Opera House stage as Franz in an American Ballet Theater production of “Coppélia.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/arts/dance/ivan-vasiliev-in-ballet-theaters-coppelia-review.html', 'snippet': 'The young Bolshoi Ballet star Ivan Vasiliev charmed his way across the Metropolitan Opera House stage as Franz in an American Ballet Theater production of “Coppélia.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Bolshoi Ballet star Ivan Vasiliev, still in his early 20s, is just what you want a Russian male dancer to be: powerful, ardent, engaging and a bit on the wild side. Even without matinee-idol looks, he has loads of physical charm. His smile is happy, his dark brown hair is none too tidy, there’s a mischievous glint in his eyes, and he’s virile without having completely shaken off his boyishness. Vast though the stage of the Metropolitan Opera House is, he makes it look much too small for comfort when he tears around it. His jumps are out of all proportion to his medium height.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/COPPELIA/COPPELIA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1012, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/COPPELIA/COPPELIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/COPPELIA/COPPELIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Wild Man-Child by Way of the Bolshoi Tears Up the Met Stage', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Wild Man-Child by Way of the Bolshoi Tears Up the Met Stage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vasiliev, Ivan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bolshoi Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T22:43:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/56368e8b-ce13-5872-af3b-2d9301927fe1', 'word_count': 1019, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56368e8b-ce13-5872-af3b-2d9301927fe1'}, {'abstract': 'Rigor and humor, two qualities you don’t see much of in Milan, were the marquee features in two assured presentations: Bottega Veneta and Prada.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/in-milan-rigor-at-bottega-veneta-wit-at-prada/', 'snippet': 'Rigor and humor, two qualities you don’t see much of in Milan, were the marquee features in two assured presentations: Bottega Veneta and Prada.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rigor and humor, two qualities you don’t see much of in Milan, were the marquee features in two assured presentations bracketing a day that didn’t lack for stimulus, what with 11 scheduled fashion shows by major labels and dozens of presentations in showrooms across the city. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Milan: Rigor at Bottega Veneta; Wit at Prada', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T23:11:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Guy Trebay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Guy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Trebay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9c1e34a-42dc-5a92-8b4b-9ed83f3cb124', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9c1e34a-42dc-5a92-8b4b-9ed83f3cb124'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the Milan Fashion Week photo diary of Sonny Vandevelde.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/photos-of-the-moment-calvin-klein-3/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the Milan Fashion Week photo diary of Sonny Vandevelde.', 'lead_paragraph': ' NYTD.fullScreenSlideShowFactory.createSlideshow("#311", 592, 490, true, chameleonData);', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photos of the Moment | Calvin Klein', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-19T23:12:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sonny Vandevelde', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sonny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vandevelde', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a11f1f12-b6c3-5af2-96c7-0b3c2f329dc4', 'word_count': 35, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a11f1f12-b6c3-5af2-96c7-0b3c2f329dc4'}, {'abstract': 'In Xul, Mexico, the arrival of Italian settlers has set off whispers that they are awaiting the end of the world; the truth is more mundane.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/world/americas/20xul.html', 'snippet': 'In Xul, Mexico, the arrival of Italian settlers has set off whispers that they are awaiting the end of the world; the truth is more mundane.', 'lead_paragraph': 'XUL, Mexico — In Mayan, the name of this speck of a village means “the end.” A long time ago, it was just the end of the road. Those who tell tall tales, though, say the strangers who settled here four years ago have come to await the end of the world. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/world/XUL-1/XUL-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/20/world/XUL-1/XUL-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/world/XUL-1/XUL-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/world/XUL-1/XUL-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/world/XUL-1/XUL-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight