/2011/06/20/sports/soccer/for-bob-bradley-and-his-two-stars-a-win-win-situation.html', 'snippet': 'Bob Bradley took a chance by allowing Landon Donovan and Clint Dempsey to leave the U.S. team, but they paid him back with a win.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Washington', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/sports/YVECSEY/YVECSEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/20/sports/YVECSEY/YVECSEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/sports/YVECSEY/YVECSEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/sports/YVECSEY/YVECSEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/sports/YVECSEY/YVECSEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Coach and His Stars, a Win-Win Situation', 'kicker': 'Sports of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Coach and His Stars, a Win-Win Situation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dempsey, Clint', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradley, Bob', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Donovan, Landon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold Cup (Soccer)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T01:57:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Vecsey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vecsey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1beb6d3c-856b-5745-8edc-f8331b446c65', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1beb6d3c-856b-5745-8edc-f8331b446c65'}, {'abstract': 'Joely Richardson and Cotter Smith play a combustible couple in “Side Effects” by Michael Weller at the Lucille Lortel Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/theater/reviews/joely-richardson-in-michael-wellers-side-effects-review.html', 'snippet': 'Joely Richardson and Cotter Smith play a combustible couple in “Side Effects” by Michael Weller at the Lucille Lortel Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now that Joely Richardson has returned to the New York theater, I have just one question: Can we keep her? Seen Off Broadway a decade ago as the older woman in Macaulay Culkin’s life in “Madame Melville,” Ms. Richardson has since become best known for her work on television (“Nip/Tuck,” “The Tudors”).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBSIDE/SUBSIDE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBSIDE/SUBSIDE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/arts/SUBSIDE/SUBSIDE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Golden Couple Melts Down, Puddles Ensue', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Side Effects'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Golden Couple Melts Down, Puddles Ensue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weller, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Cotter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MCC Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Richardson, Joely', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T02:01:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7014a190-2c61-5718-a676-767fa1aea6c9', 'word_count': 1076, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7014a190-2c61-5718-a676-767fa1aea6c9'}, {'abstract': 'Assessing HBO’s adaptation of George R.R. Martin’s fantasy saga.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/notes-on-game-of-thrones/', 'snippet': 'Assessing HBO’s adaptation of George R.R. Martin’s fantasy saga.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By now, the idea that television serials like “The Sopranos” or “The Wire” are the great popular novels of our time feels like a cliché rather than an insight. But HBO’s adaptation of George R. R. Martin’s fantasy saga is one of the few high-profile attempts to take the cliche and try to literalize it, by faithfully translating a big fat teeming novel into a 10-episode television season. With exceptions like “True Blood,” which is based on Charlaine Harris’s vampire novels (though how closely I’m not sure, as a non-fan of the show and books\xa0 alike), few of the major cable dramas have been adaptations of existing stories. From Al Swearengen to Walter White to Stringer Bell, the great characters of television’s dramatic golden age have sprung from the imaginations of TV writers, rather than the printed page.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes on ‘Game of Thrones’', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T02:03:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d593f97-4fc4-5fb5-90d5-5d93878d1d8e', 'word_count': 1234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d593f97-4fc4-5fb5-90d5-5d93878d1d8e'}, {'abstract': 'Lottery numbers for June 19, 2011, for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/nyregion/lottery-numbers-for-new-york-new-jersey-and-connecticut.html', 'snippet': 'Lottery numbers for June 19, 2011, for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'June 19, 2011', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lottery Numbers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lottery Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lotteries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T02:06:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://ar