d name, military researchers are now at work shrinking these devices to the size of insects and birds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aerial drones are transforming the way America fights and thinks about its wars. While the Cessna-sized Predator drones have become a brand name, military researchers are now at work shrinking these devices to the size of insects and birds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Blimps to Bugs: The Miniaturization of Drone Technnology', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drones (Pilotless Planes)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T03:14:44+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/596c5f38-cbd6-5253-a6f2-dfb25f48680f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/596c5f38-cbd6-5253-a6f2-dfb25f48680f'}, {'abstract': 'Drones are playing an increasingly important role in the American military. Only 10 years ago, the Pentagon had about 50 drones; now there are 7,000 drones in its inventory, ranging in size from large blimps to tiny Hummingbirds. Here are 10 drones currently on the battlefield or on the drawing board.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/06/19/world/drone-graphic.html', 'snippet': 'Drones are playing an increasingly important role in the American military. Only 10 years ago, the Pentagon had about 50 drones; now there are 7,000 drones in its inventory, ranging in size from large blimps to tiny Hummingbirds. Here are 10 drone...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Drones are playing an increasingly important role in the American military. Only 10 years ago, the Pentagon had about 50 drones; now there are 7,000 drones in its inventory, ranging in size from large blimps to tiny Hummingbirds. Here are 10 drones currently on the battlefield or on the drawing board.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Changing Shapes of Air Power', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drones (Pilotless Planes)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T03:16:06+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/54b8c7f1-f709-51e1-a170-a28221b2d676', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/54b8c7f1-f709-51e1-a170-a28221b2d676'}, {'abstract': 'As technology once found only in  “Get Smart” and James Bond movies are now being developed for espionage and the battlefield, The New York Times asked three spy novelists to speculate on the future of spy drones and military gadgetry.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/19/imagining-the-future-of-military-gadgetry/', 'snippet': 'As technology once found only in  “Get Smart” and James Bond movies are now being developed for espionage and the battlefield, The New York Times asked three spy novelists to speculate on the future of spy drones and military gadgetry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Micro air vehicles may seem like the stuff of science fiction, but, as our colleagues Elisabeth Bumiller and Thom Shanker report, they are a very real part of a drone explosion that is transforming the way America fights its wars. So as these “spy flies” — once found only in “Get Smart” and James Bond movies — are now being developed for espionage and the battlefield, The New York Times asked three spy novelists to speculate on the future of spy drones and military gadgetry. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Imagining the Future of Military Gadgetry', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drones (Pilotless Planes)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Finder, Joseph', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McEuen, Paul', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'KGB', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T03:17:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By At War', 'person': [{'firstname': 'At', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'War', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8ec2c24-b4a3-5826-b579-19b022a8c3b6', 'word_count': 1334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8ec2c24-b4a3-5826-b579-19b022a8c3b6'}, {'abstract': 'The top ranked “K Computer” is three times faster than a Chinese rival that previously held the top spot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/technology/20computer.html', 'snippet': 'The top ranked “K Computer” is three times faster than a Chinese rival that previously held the top spot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — I