, 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/multimedia/video-caucus-062011-bai/video-caucus-062011-bai-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/20/multimedia/video-caucus-062011-bai/video-caucus-062011-bai-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/20/multimedia/video-caucus-062011-bai/video-caucus-062011-bai-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Profiling Jon Huntsman', 'kicker': 'POLITICS', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bai, Matt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntsman, Jon M Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T08:30:21+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/7948a0d8-57d8-5217-b68f-249c8682a35e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/7948a0d8-57d8-5217-b68f-249c8682a35e'}, {'abstract': 'Someone has to win the nomination. It might as well be Jon Huntsman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/magazine/jon-huntsman-steps-into-the-republican-vacuum.html', 'snippet': 'Someone has to win the nomination. It might as well be Jon Huntsman.', 'lead_paragraph': '‘I’m not sure there’s any way to be prepared for something like this,” Jon Huntsman told me last month. He was reflecting on his debut a few days earlier as a prospective presidential candidate: a gorgeous Thursday evening in New Hampshire when his chartered plane touched down at a little airport and a waiting Suburban whisked him off to Jesse’s, a hillside steakhouse a few miles from the Dartmouth campus. As the recently departed ambassador to China and a former governor of Utah, Huntsman has some experience with polite, state-run media and small cliques of capital reporters. When he stepped out of the car, he had in mind that sort of familiar scene.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/mag-26Huntsman-t_CA1/mag-26Huntsman-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/mag-26Huntsman-t_CA1/mag-26Huntsman-t_CA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/mag-26Huntsman-t_CA1/mag-26Huntsman-t_CA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Huntsman Steps Into the Republican Vacuum', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Is It Always Like This?’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCain, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntsman, Jon M Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weaver, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T08:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Bai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3df80339-ebc4-5fe5-8542-1aa2822731de', 'word_count': 6243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3df80339-ebc4-5fe5-8542-1aa2822731de'}, {'abstract': 'What role do graduation ceremonies play in the lives of the graduations, their loved ones, and the school communities they are leaving?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/70483/', 'snippet': 'What role do graduation ceremonies play in the lives of the graduations, their loved ones, and the school communities they are leaving?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Preschoolers, kindergartners, eighth graders, those who have finished courses of study in high schools, colleges, universities, military academies, trade schools, myriad professional training programs–they all don the graduation garb, walk across countless stages, hear words of encouragement and say “goodbye,” especially around this time of year. What do you remember most about graduation or other rite of passage ceremonies you’ve participated in or attended as a guest? What is the reason for such ceremonies?  ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Makes a Graduation Ceremony Memorable?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T08:34:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c25a01b-3f4e-55f8-bf69-02228a2d9bec', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c25a01b-3f4e-55f8-bf69-02228a2d9bec'}, {'abstract': 'Andrew Burton was inspired by the tenacity of John Moore, who risked his life for a photo of an explosion in 2007.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/devastation-pictured-in-the-details/', 'snippet': 'Andrew Burton was inspired by the tenacity of John Moore, who risked his life for a photo of an explosion in 2007.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andrew Burton, 24, grew up in Australia and Oregon. He graduated from Syracuse University in 2010. He has interned with The Syracuse Post-Standard, Bloomberg News, The Oregonian and Getty Images, and now works as a freelance photographer and multimedia journalist for various organizations, including The New York Times. He covered the aftermath of the earthquake and tsunami in Japan. This conversation with Kerri MacDonald, for the Turning Point series, is condensed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Devastation Pictured in the Details', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bhutto, Benazir', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T09:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kerri MacDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kerri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7bb19b6-f38e-5207-8fcc-3c70a2443ccf', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7bb19b6-f38e-5207-8fcc-3c70a2443ccf'}, {'abstract': 'Health care is cheaper if you don’t provide it.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/not-wrong-again/', 'snippet': 'Health care is cheaper if you don’t provide it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve written recently about what should be a well-known fact: Medicare spending has risen less than premiums on private health insurance. This shouldn’t be a controversial proposition; it comes straight from the Center for Medicare and Medicaid Services health expenditure data. You can argue about why it’s happening and whether it’s sustainable, but the fact shouldn’t be up for dispute.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not Wrong, Again', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T09:11:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aaa1b36f-bfce-53ad-abda-af85a2de01cb', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aaa1b36f-bfce-53ad-abda-af85a2de01cb'}, {'abstract': 'Not a flirtation, a torrid affair.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/our-lost-decade-relationship/', 'snippet': 'Not a flirtation, a torrid affair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Clive Crook argues that we’re flirting with the possibility of a lost decade. I disagree — the flirting took place three years ago. The lost decade question now isn’t whether our flirtation with a lost decade will turn into something serious; it’s whether the torrid affair we’re now having with the potential for a lost decade can somehow be broken up.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Our Lost Decade Relationship', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T09:29:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b47091f-3a32-5959-9769-5d2e98aa0f02', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b47091f-3a32-5959-9769-5d2e98aa0f02'}, {'abstract': 'Has there ever been a time  when the league’s best speed rusher and bull rusher were the same guy?', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/top-10-defensive-ends-in-n-f-l/', 'snippet': 'Has there ever been a time  when the league’s best speed rusher and bull rusher were the same guy?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Friendly Disclaimer: The following list is influenced largely -– but not strictly -– by film study of all 32 teams from the 2010 season. Stats weren’t acknowledged, but players with poor numbers generally don’t make top 10 lists anyway. Vague enough for you? Criteria for top-10 lists tend to be. That’s why most of you will have no trouble finding some disagreement with what you’re about to read.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Top 10 Defensive Ends in N.F.L.', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T10:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd3d09f0-7ee1-5790-952e-e6388104c97b', 'word_count': 453, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd3d09f0-7ee1-5790-952e-e6388104c97b'}, {'abstract': 'With so much evidence that investing in early childhood education pays huge dividends, an economist asks, why is business reluctant to support it more forcefully?', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/will-business-buy-in-to-early-childhood-education/', 'snippet': 'With so much evidence that investing in early childhood education pays huge dividends, an economist asks, why is business reluctant to support it more forcefully?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nancy Folbre is an economics professor at the University of Massachusetts Amherst.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Business Buy In to Early Childhood Education?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (Pre-School)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Head Start Program', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T10:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Folbre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Folbre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f21876d8-1a00-5b02-897e-5c67c49eed24', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f21876d8-1a00-5b02-897e-5c67c49eed24'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/the-early-word-alliances/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Alliances', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_he