major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bono', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carney, Reeve', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edge, The', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parker, Trey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stone, Matt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T15:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b545ad9d-a179-5230-8766-e486448cf15c', 'word_count': 439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b545ad9d-a179-5230-8766-e486448cf15c'}, {'abstract': 'Off the Rails: How a banal bit of decommissioned street signage morphed into a sought-after collector’s item is a tale of design trends and changing tastes.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/walk-dont-walk-open-your-wallet/', 'snippet': 'Off the Rails: How a banal bit of decommissioned street signage morphed into a sought-after collector’s item is a tale of design trends and changing tastes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Consider the “Don’t Walk” sign, that lost classic of the New York streetscape. It is a yin-yang traffic signal, a functional poem — “Don’t Walk/Walk” — inside an iconic yellow box. It is to Martin Scorsese’s midcentury Manhattan what the gas lamp is to Edith Wharton’s gilded age.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Store Sells Old New York ‘Don’t Walk’ Signs. Only £975.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Signals and Signs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T15:45:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/265f5715-13c9-5ee5-97a3-b6de25a78e90', 'word_count': 778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/265f5715-13c9-5ee5-97a3-b6de25a78e90'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists in Madagascar wonder if the combined pressures of deforestation, competition with exotic species, and overfishing have wiped out all but a trace of the island’s endemic cichlid fish.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/is-it-too-late-for-the-damba/', 'snippet': 'Scientists in Madagascar wonder if the combined pressures of deforestation, competition with exotic species, and overfishing have wiped out all but a trace of the island’s endemic cichlid fish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tuesday, June 14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is It Too Late for the Damba?', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Caves and Caverns', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN MUSEUM OF NATURAL HISTORY', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madagascar', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T15:55:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By John S. Sparks and Christopher B. Braun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Sparks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3194c653-05ab-5d06-a25b-f2b04edac312', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3194c653-05ab-5d06-a25b-f2b04edac312'}, {'abstract': 'The classic Brooklyn Italian restaurant has been modernized.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/the-lights-are-on-again-at-montes-in-gowanus/', 'snippet': 'The classic Brooklyn Italian restaurant has been modernized.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been 105 years since Angelo and Angelina Monte opened the Brooklyn landmark once called Monte’s Venetian Room, known for its murals of Venice, and its Rat Pack clientele. Now, after having been closed for more than two years, new owners, Tina Esposito and her husband, Dominick Castelvetre, have refurbished the restaurant, but without the murals. “They were gone when we took over,” Ms. Esposito said. But except for a new marble top, they’ve left the bar room alone, just cleaning and brightening it.The menu is less focused on traditional “parmigiana” dishes, offering more authentically regional Italian fare. The chef, Salvatore Zapparata, is a native of Sicily.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lights Are On Again at Monte’s in Gowanus', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T16:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organizatio