s-from-the-annie-open-casting-call/', 'snippet': 'Hundreds of girls (and their parents, guardians and vocal coaches) were in line to audition at an open casting call for the coming Broadway revival of “Annie.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Residents of several Upper East Side blocks awoke one recent Sunday morning not to the peals of church bells or birdsong, but to Katie Schults, of Glen Ridge, N.J., belting out “Tomorrow,” from the musical “Annie,” as if her 14-year-old life depended on it. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Little Girls, Everywhere: Portraits From the ‘Annie’ Open Casting Call', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Annie (Play)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T19:08:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c1093ec-2608-578c-84e8-565509a7c21d', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c1093ec-2608-578c-84e8-565509a7c21d'}, {'abstract': 'A new analysis appears to refute critics who have charged the F.D.A. is less efficient than the European Medicines Agency in approving new cancer drugs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/health/research/21perceptions.html', 'snippet': 'A new analysis appears to refute critics who have charged the F.D.A. is less efficient than the European Medicines Agency in approving new cancer drugs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New cancer drugs are approved in just six months on average in the United States, half the time it takes for the same drugs to be approved in Europe, a new report finds.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Perceptions: Cancer Drugs Get on a Faster Track in U.S.', 'kicker': 'Vital Signs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Perceptions: Cancer Drugs on a Faster Track in U.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T19:28:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roni Caryn Rabin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roni', 'middlename': 'Caryn', 'lastname': 'Rabin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0280dddd-049c-5e93-89db-a80254aad65a', 'word_count': 216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0280dddd-049c-5e93-89db-a80254aad65a'}, {'abstract': 'Asia now accounts for 64 percent of the world’s asbestos use and 13 percent of asbestos-related deaths, but it takes 30 to 50 years after exposure for many related illnesses to develop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/health/21global.html', 'snippet': 'Asia now accounts for 64 percent of the world’s asbestos use and 13 percent of asbestos-related deaths, but it takes 30 to 50 years after exposure for many related illnesses to develop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Deaths from asbestos-related diseases will surge in Asia over the next 20 years, a recent study has warned. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/science/GLOB/GLOB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/21/science/GLOB/GLOB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/science/GLOB/GLOB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/science/GLOB/GLOB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/21/science/GLOB/GLOB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Asia’s Heavy Use of Asbestos Is Expected to Cause Rise in Deaths in Coming Decades', 'kicker': 'Global Update', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'ENVIRONMENTAL HEALTH: Asia’s Heavy Use of Asbestos Is Expected To Cause Rise in Deaths in Coming Decades', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asbestos', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Far East, South and Southeast Asia and Pacific Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T19:30:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66c982aa-7941-5a40-a47b-219ccd867906', 'word_count': 240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66c982aa-7941-5a40-a47b-219ccd867906'}, {'abstract': 'Harold Prince, who has won 21 Tony Awards for producing and directing some of the most well-known musicals of the 20th centurey, is planning a return to Broadway in the fall of 2012 with a show that celebrates the material from his career.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/broadways-prince-revisits-material-that-earned-him-the-title/', 'snippet': 'Harold Prince, who has won 21 Tony Awards for producing and directing some of the most well-known musicals of the 20th centurey, is planning a return to Broadway in the fall of 2012 with a show that celebrates the material from his career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harold Prince, who has won more Tony Awards than anyone in history — 21 — for producing and directing some of the greatest musicals of the 20th century, is planning a return to Broadway in the fall of 2012 with a new show that celebrates and re-examines some of the material from his theater career. Dancap Productions announced on Monday that the new musical, “Prince of Broadway,” is in development for a production to begin in Toronto in the summer of 2012 to be followed by a Broadway run.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Broadway’s Prince Revisits Material That Earned Him the Title', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prince, Harold', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stroman, Susan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T19:31:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/316ec786-80de-5e56-978c-c3ee4e430c57', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/316ec786-80de-5e56-978c-c3ee4e430c57'}, {'abstract': 'Frank Bender, a forensic sculptor, is trying to help investigators identify a woman whose decomposed remains were found by a deer hunter in 2001.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/science/21bender.html', 'snippet': 'Frank Bender, a forensic sculptor, is trying to help investigators identify a woman whose decomposed remains were found by a deer hunter in 2001.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHILADELPHIA — For decades, Frank Bender has stared at human skulls, handled them, even boiled scraps of flesh off them. He has done this to shape clay busts in the hope of giving names to rotting corpses and skeletons found in woods or alleys or abandoned houses.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/science/BEND/BEND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/21/science/BEND/BEND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/science/BEND/BEND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/science/BEND/BEND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/21/science/BEND/BEND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Recomposing Life’s Details From Scraps', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Recomposing Life’s Details From Scraps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forensic Science', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T19:35:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Stout', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stout', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f08f8319-bd4f-56d6-9c38-d25ef644908c', 'word_count': 1339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f08f8319-bd4f-56d6-9c38-d25ef644908c'}, {'abstract': 'It’s time to shake off the cobwebs and take our first crack at the 2011 fantasy football season (let’s just move forward as if there will be an N.F.L. season). It’s still early, and later this summer we’ll be proving our complete and detailed rankings for each position, but for now we’ll get the blood pumping with our top 10 quarterbacks for 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/fantasy-first-take-quarterbacks/', 'snippet': 'It’s time to shake off the cobwebs and take our first crack at the 2011 fantasy football season (let’s just move forward as if there will be an N.F.L. season). It’s still early, and later this summer we’ll be proving our complete and detailed rank...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The brothers Sablich are here to help with fantasy football analysis for the 2011 season. Follow them on Twitter (@5thDownFantasy) for more news and analysis. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantasy First Take: Quarterbacks', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T19:35:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Sablich and Jason Sablich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': No