e the Lead', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walcott, Dennis M', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Public Advocate's Office", 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T21:06:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fernanda Santos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fernanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58a17698-aa34-5641-9e43-e3b564f34e26', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58a17698-aa34-5641-9e43-e3b564f34e26'}, {'abstract': 'On the day after Rory McIlroy’s victory at the United States Open, his personality and game were examined on both sides of the Atlantic.', 'web_url': 'https://onpar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/the-day-after-dissecting-mcilroy/', 'snippet': 'On the day after Rory McIlroy’s victory at the United States Open, his personality and game were examined on both sides of the Atlantic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Glory, glory, The Belfast Telegraph writes about Rory McIlroy’s record-breaking victory at the United States Open on Sunday, adding that his “genius” at Congressional Country Club in Bethesda, Md., “has us all gasping.” Of course, the local coverage notes some ambivalence: his eight-stroke victory proves nice guys finish first, but will the news media and the golf world try to make him out to be some kind of saint?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Day After: Dissecting McIlroy', 'kicker': 'On Par', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McIlroy, Rory', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T21:07:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/201b773a-a07a-5d89-a603-fc036c4363f7', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/201b773a-a07a-5d89-a603-fc036c4363f7'}, {'abstract': 'Even though Republican senators have stymied the White House from naming a director, the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau will formally open its doors on July 21.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/director-or-no-wall-streets-newest-cop-is-ready-for-duty/', 'snippet': 'Even though Republican senators have stymied the White House from naming a director, the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau will formally open its doors on July 21.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Houghton for The New York Times Richard Cordray runs the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau’s enforcement division.', 's