ed to protect a pro-Qaddafi journalist was killed over the weekend, possibly by rebel snipers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/world/africa/21libya.html', 'snippet': 'A guard assigned to protect a pro-Qaddafi journalist was killed over the weekend, possibly by rebel snipers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TRIPOLI, Libya — Reports that a guard at the hotel housing foreign journalists here had been fatally shot sent a tremor of anxiety through the Qaddafi government’s media operation on Monday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/world/africa/LIBYA/LIBYA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/21/world/africa/LIBYA/LIBYA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/world/africa/LIBYA/LIBYA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/world/africa/LIBYA/LIBYA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/21/world/africa/LIBYA/LIBYA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Libyan Media Minders Nervous After Guard Death', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Guard’s Shooting Unnerves Libyan Minders as Violence Grows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T21:44:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b44a5a8-47dc-54be-8e27-c2511ff8d177', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b44a5a8-47dc-54be-8e27-c2511ff8d177'}, {'abstract': 'In “101 Ways to Leave a Game Show,” beginning on Tuesday on ABC, contestants try to avoid being ejected over cliffs, out of cannons or off of trucks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/arts/television/101-ways-to-leave-a-game-show-on-abc-on-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'In “101 Ways to Leave a Game Show,” beginning on Tuesday on ABC, contestants try to avoid being ejected over cliffs, out of cannons or off of trucks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SIMI VALLEY, Calif. — On the rim of a rock quarry in the blistering June heat, three people are huddled in spacesuits with metallic jet packs on their backs, worrying aloud about which jet pack is about to be turned on. Five camera operators are sprawled around them, capturing every groan and sigh and exhortation to God. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/GAME/GAME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/GAME/GAME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/GAME/GAME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 610, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/GAME/GAME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/GAME/GAME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hoping to Survive This Round. Literally.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hoping to Survive This Round. Literally.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T21:47:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7e5c5a7-8691-518b-857e-c69738c8872d', 'word_count': 1004, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7e5c5a7-8691-518b-857e-c69738c8872d'}, {'abstract': 'Forty years after the New York Times published the leaked Pentagon Papers, the National Archives has released them in their entirety.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/us/2011_PENTAGON_PAPERS.html', 'snippet': 'Forty years after the New York Times published the leaked Pentagon Papers, the National Archives has released them in their entirety.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Forty years after the New York Times published the leaked Pentagon Papers, the National Archives has released them in their entirety.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Complete Pentagon Papers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pentagon Papers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Archives and Records Administration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T21:52:26+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/2e8475ef-d13a-5a9f-9a81-70c8a53fbdf2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/2e8475ef-d13a-5a9f-9a81-70c8a53fbdf2'}, {'abstract': 'Aimless youth and aimless old age meet in Amy Herzog’s funny, moving new play “4000 Miles.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/theater/reviews/4000-miles-with-mary-louise-wilson-review.html', 'snippet': 'Aimless youth and aimless old age meet in Amy Herzog’s funny, moving new play “4000 Miles.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Death steals quietly into a Greenwich Village apartment, like morning sunlight gradually suffusing a darkened room, in “4000 Miles,” a funny, moving new play by Amy Herzog that opened on Monday night at the Duke on 42nd Street. Beneath the plain-wrap surface of this drama about a grandmother and grandson sharing a few weeks of casual cohabitation lies a quiet meditation on mortality. But it’s hardly a downer: Ms. Herzog’s altogether wonderful drama also illuminates how companionship can make life meaningful, moment by moment, in death’s discomforting shadow.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/MILES/MILES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/MILES/MILES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/MILES/MILES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 612, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/MILES/MILES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/MILES/MILES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Young and Old, With No Particular Place to Go', 'kicker': "Theater Review | '4000 Miles'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Young and Old, With No Particular Place to Go', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Herzog, Amy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Mary Louise', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aukin, Daniel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T21:54:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ff18a70-9089-56da-90c2-c4d3d20e91ec', 'word_count': 958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ff18a70-9089-56da-90c2-c4d3d20e91ec'}, {'abstract': 'Laurel Halo, the headliner at Shea Stadium in Bushwick, Brooklyn, knows how to deploy wobble, drone and nervous energy in refreshing fashion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/arts/music/laurel-halo-and-autre-ne-veut-at-shea-stadium-review.html', 'snippet': 'Laurel Halo, the headliner at Shea Stadium in Bushwick, Brooklyn, knows how to deploy wobble, drone and nervous energy in refreshing fashion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday evening Shea Stadium, a performance space in Bushwick, Brooklyn, had the feel of a dorm party in its dwindling hours. Young people sat on lumpy couches, barely talking to one another, lost in their own reveries. Onstage a series of performers came and went, though at any given time there were as many people smoking and chatting on the balcony as were loosely gathered near the stage to watch.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/HALO/HALO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/HALO/HALO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/HALO/HALO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 607, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/HALO/HALO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/HALO/HALO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Keyboard Speaks Louder Than a Microphone', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Keyboard Speaks Louder Than a Microphone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T21:56:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/01fcd89f-611f-50c3-8ceb-ef27c50e4182', 'word_count': 478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01fcd89f-611f-50c3-8ceb-ef27c50e4182'}, {'abstract': 'In the musical “Next Thing You Know,” with music by Joshua Salzman and book and lyrics by Ryan Cunningham, young artists try to come to grips with life in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/theater/reviews/next-thing-you-know-at-cap21-black-box-theater.html', 'snippet': 'In the musical “Next Thing You Know,” with music by Joshua Salzman and book and lyrics by Ryan Cunningham, young artists try to come to grips with life in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A coming-of-age tale about struggling artists in the big city, “Next Thing You Know” is a sort of “Rent” for the Organization Kid generation.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/next/next-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/next/next-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 367}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/next/next-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 623, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Artists, Growing Up Is Still Hard to Do', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Next Thing You Know' ", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Artists, Growing Up Is Still Hard to Do', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T21:56:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd077ba4-14f7-58fb-8abf-5be7f5c44f29', 'word_count': 419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd077ba4-14f7-58fb-8abf-5be7f5c44f29'}, {'abstract': 'This year’s Boston Early Music Festival was mostly about discovery, with the operatic centerpiece being Steffani’s “Niobe, Regina di Tebe.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/arts/music/boston-early-music-festival-and-exhibition.html', 'snippet': 'This year’s Boston Early Music Festival was mostly about discovery, with the operatic centerpiece being Steffani’s “Niobe, Regina di Tebe.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — You might expect an early-music festival to celebrate the settled canon, with programs devoted to, say, Josquin and Dufay Masses, Bach’s “Brandenburg” Concertos, Mozart’s Requiem and other repertory high-water marks, in interpretations that reflect the latest notions of period style. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/early-1/early-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/early-1/early-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/early-1/early-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 606, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/early-1/early-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/early-1/early-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Festival Brings the Obscure to the Fore, With a Global Touch', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Festival Brings the Obscure to the Fore, With a Global Touch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T21:59:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2679d896-e06e-52e7-a052-c787853de759', 'word_count': 1254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2679d896-e06e-52e7-a052-c787853de759'}, {'abstract': 'A member of the golf club in Rory McIlroy’s hometown of Holywood won 25,000 pounds on Sunday after he bet 50 pounds with 500-1 odds that McIlroy would win a major before turning 25.', 'web_url': 'https://onpar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/for-one-fan-of-mcilroy-a-major-payoff/', 'snippet': 'A member of the golf club in Rory McIlroy’s hometown of Holywood won 25,000 pounds on Sunday after he bet 50 pounds with 500-1 odds that McIlroy would win a major before turning 25.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This weekend 22-year-old Rory McIlroy cruised to a United States Open title, becoming the youngest Open champion since Bobby Jones in 1923. The incredible performance -– he finished 16-under par, eight shots ahead of the runner-up, Jason Day -– included 12 records. And it also sparked comparisons to Tiger Woods as the sport seeks a young superstar for a future that may not feature the dominant Woods the world grew accustomed to.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For One Fan of McIlroy, a Major Payoff', 'kicker': 'On Par', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Golf)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T22:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By JORGE CASTILLO', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jorge', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CASTILLO', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e685b188-bb7b-5027-9e00-c18071b13051', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e685b188-bb7b-5027-9e00-c18071b13051'}, {'abstract': 'There seem to be as many cream-filled series on cable as there are cupcake flavors at Crumbs. On Tuesday “Staten Island Cakes” and a documentary, “Kings of Pastry,” are being added to the mix.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/arts/television/staten-island-cakes-and-pastry-kings.html', 'snippet': 'There seem to be as many cream-filled series on cable as there are cupcake flavors at Crumbs. On Tuesday “Staten Island Cakes” and a documentary, “Kings of Pastry,” are being added to the mix.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By any logical standard, say the collapse of Holland’s tulip bubble in 1637 or Mrs. Fields filing for bankruptcy in 2008, cupcake mania should surely by now be waning.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/SUBPASTRY/SUBPASTRY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 642, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/SUBPASTRY/SUBPASTRY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/21/arts/SUBPASTRY/SUBPASTRY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Viewers Still Drool for Frosting Fantasies', 'kicker': 'The TV Watch', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Viewers Still Drool For Frosting Fantasies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food Network', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TLC (TV Channel)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-20T22:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alessandra Stanley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alessandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stanley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fec897e-a877-55de-8d33-9ec68b49e012', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fec897e-a877-55de-8d33-9ec68b49e012'}, {'abstract': 'Alex Bogomolov, 28, of Miami, defeated Donald Young, 7-5, 4-6, 6-3, 6-1, to reach the second round at Wimbledon.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/thaw-in-a-young-players-dispute-with-the-u-s-t-a/', 'snippet': 'Alex Bogomolov, 28, of Miami, defeated Donald Young, 7-5, 4-6, 6-3, 6-1, to reach the second round at Wimbledon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England -– Two unseeded Americans who traveled thousands of miles from home faced