ree decades was a powerful but polarizing figure in regional and international soccer, resigned his positions as the vice president of FIFA and as the president of Concacaf on Monday. In response, FIFA closed an ethics inquiry that had accused him of bribery and said “the presumption of innocence is maintained.” Read the article here, and come back to comment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Warner Resigns, Cutting Short Ethics Inquiry', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warner, Jack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T01:32:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c443df9-61c8-5bc4-8a4b-4347d0be3336', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c443df9-61c8-5bc4-8a4b-4347d0be3336'}, {'abstract': 'Two brothers who pioneered a needle exchange program in Iran continued their mission to improve the health of others, even in prison.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/20/in-iran-a-brotherhood-of-doctors-and-patients/', 'snippet': 'Two brothers who pioneered a needle exchange program in Iran continued their mission to improve the health of others, even in prison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few doctors anywhere in the world have done their country a greater service than the Iranian brothers Arash and Kamiar Alaei.\xa0 Kamiar, who is 37, is currently living in Albany, N.Y., where he is working on a doctorate in public health.\xa0\xa0 Arash, who is 42, is a resident of Tehran’s notorious Evin prison — where until recently, Kamiar lived as well.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Iran, a Brotherhood of Doctors and Patients', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hypodermic Needles and Syringes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AHMADINEJAD, MAHMOUD', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T01:35:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e5ba0f4-9138-5ef0-a1d0-b6d7ef638ab6', 'word_count': 1816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e5ba0f4-9138-5ef0-a1d0-b6d7ef638ab6'}, {'abstract': 'Friends of Hudson River Park is changing its mission in pursuit of the financial and critical success of a similar group, Friends of the High Line.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/nyregion/for-park-advocacy-group-grass-roots-legacy-yields-to-fund-raising-needs.html', 'snippet': 'Friends of Hudson River Park is changing its mission in pursuit of the financial and critical success of a similar group, Friends of the High Line.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The nature lovers and community activists who founded Friends of Hudson River Park in 1999 never shied from a fight; years earlier, they even helped kill Westway, the superhighway planned to run down the West Side of Manhattan.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Moving Beyond Its Feisty Roots, Hudson River Park Group Focuses on Fund-Raising', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Moving Beyond Its Feisty Roots, Hudson River Park Group Focuses on Fund-Raising', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Line (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hudson River Park Trust', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T01:39:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04546e06-c90f-5c0b-9e0a-1317da7b011c', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04546e06-c90f-5c0b-9e0a-1317da7b011c'}, {'abstract': 'Since the turnover of the air war to NATO in April, American warplanes have struck at Libyan air defenses about 60 times, and drones have fired missiles at Libyan forces about 30 times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/world/africa/21powers.html', 'snippet': 'Since the turnover of the air war to NATO in April, American warplanes have struck at Libyan air defenses about 60 times, and drones have fired missiles at Libyan forces about 30 times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Since the United States handed control of the air war in Libya to NATO in early April, American warplanes have struck at Libyan air defenses about 60 times, and remotely operated drones have fired missiles at Libyan forces about 30 times, according to military officials.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scores of U.S. Strikes in Libya Followed Handoff to NATO', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scores of U.S. Strikes in Libya Followed Handoff to NATO', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Powers Act (1973)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T01:40:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage and Thom Shanker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Thom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shanker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06322747-3909-5997-8a50-b3930d1ade6f', 'word_count': 660, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06322747-3909-5997-8a50-b3930d1ade6f'}, {'abstract': 'Discouraging reports about aid in Afghanistan should drive us to consider the deeper forces underlying societal instability. Hint: It’s not always about the material stuff.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/opinion/21brooks.html', 'snippet': 'Discouraging reports about aid in Afghanistan should drive us to consider the deeper forces underlying societal instability. Hint: It’s not always about the material stuff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So far, few politicians have embraced my plan for a Marshall Plan Tax. The idea is that every time a think-tanker, op-ed writer or retired senator calls for a new Marshall Plan or a moonshot-type initiative to solve a social problem, they would have to pay a tax of $50. Within a few months, we’d have enough money to pay for an actual new Marshall Plan. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Smart Power Setback', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Smart Power Setback', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Aid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T01:41:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ebd7226-4390-5483-9af0-6c24ccb4694f', 'word_count': 819, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ebd7226-4390-5483-9af0-6c24ccb4694f'}, {'abstract': 'Why banks shouldn’t win the fight over capital requirements.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/opinion/21nocera.html', 'snippet': 'Why banks shouldn’t win the fight over capital requirements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Capital matters. Let me put that another way. The current fight over additional capital requirements for the banking industry, eye-glazing though it is, also happens to be the most important reform moment since the financial crisis broke out three years ago. More important than the wrangling over Dodd-Frank. More important than the ongoing effort to regulate derivatives. More important even than the jousting over the new Consumer Financial Protection Bureau.', 'print_section': 'OpEd', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, '