 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/world/asia/21policy.html', 'snippet': 'Administration officials said that President Obama would most likely pull out the 30,000 surge troops by the end of 2012. What is still not known is how soon and how fast.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama plans to announce his decision on the scale and pace of troop withdrawals from Afghanistan in a speech from the White House at 8 p.m. Eastern time Wednesday evening.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama to Announce Plans for Afghan Surge Pullout', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama to Announce Plans For Afghan Surge Pullout', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T02:22:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler and Helene Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90efac10-dadf-5f8a-80d0-07c91bd0ba86', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90efac10-dadf-5f8a-80d0-07c91bd0ba86'}, {'abstract': 'The right-hander retired 24 of the last 26 hitters he faced in holding Cincinnati to four hits over eight innings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/sports/baseball/yankees-rookie-pitcher-ivan-nova-dismantles-powerful-reds-lineup.html', 'snippet': 'The right-hander retired 24 of the last 26 hitters he faced in holding Cincinnati to four hits over eight innings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CINCINNATI — The Cincinnati Reds are the highest-scoring team in the National League, and they play in a ballpark that yields home runs by the bushel. One of their left-handed sluggers, Joey Votto, is the reigning most valuable player. Another, Jay Bruce, is among the league leaders in homers.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/sports/YANKEES/YANKEES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/21/sports/YANKEES/YANKEES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/sports/YANKEES/YANKEES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/sports/YANKEES/YANKEES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/21/sports/YANKEES/YANKEES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Potential on Display, Nova Dismantles Powerful Reds', 'kicker': 'Yankees 5, Reds 3', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Potential on Display, Nova Dismantles Powerful Reds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rivera, Mariano', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nova, Ivan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cincinnati Reds', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T02:31:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea7e0054-e587-5f6f-aa7c-ce654d170400', 'word_count': 873, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea7e0054-e587-5f6f-aa7c-ce654d170400'}, {'abstract': 'Pervasive social media services, cheap cellphone cameras, free photo and video Web hosts have made privacy all but a thing of the past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/us/21anonymity.html', 'snippet': 'Pervasive social media services, cheap cellphone cameras, free photo and video Web hosts have made privacy all but a thing of the past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not too long ago, theorists fretted that the Internet was a place where anonymity thrived. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/us/ANONYMITY_span/ANONYMITY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/21/us/ANONYMITY_span/ANONYMITY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/us/ANONYMITY_span/ANONYMITY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/21/us/ANONYMITY_span/ANONYMITY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/21/us/ANONYMITY_span/ANONYMITY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Upending Anonymity, These Days the Web Unmasks Everyone', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Upending Anonymity, These Days the Web Unmasks Everyone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T02:32:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da06356a-dce1-572d-8eb6-33233fb9d1e1', 'word_count': 1094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da06356a-dce1-572d-8eb6-33233fb9d1e1'}, {'abstract': 'The big trading nations need to fulfill their promises to help the world’s poorest nations rise out of destitution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/opinion/21tue2.html', 'snippet': 'The big trading nations need to fulfill their promises to help the world’s poorest nations rise out of destitution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ten years ago in Doha, Qatar, the world’s leading trading nations began the so-called development round of trade negotiations, billed as an effort to open markets in rich countries to the exports of the world’s poorest nations to help them rise out of destitution.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Doing Right by the Poorest Countries', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Doing Right by the Poorest Countries: The big trading nations need to fulfill their promises', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World and Developing Countries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T02:37:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/130c2b21-755c-5388-8a18-1937833738b0', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/130c2b21-755c-5388-8a18-1937833738b0'}, {'abstract': 'Congress needs to be candid about how loophole-ridden laws have created a huge market for guns, and it should re-enact the assault weapons ban and impose other needed controls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/opinion/21tue3.html', 'snippet': 'Congress needs to be candid about how loophole-ridden laws have created a huge market for guns, and it should re-enact the assault weapons ban and impose other needed controls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Congressional Republicans are really intent on getting to the bottom of an ill-conceived sting operation along the border by the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives, they should call President Felipe Calderón of Mexico as an expert witness.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hypocrisy, Locked and Loaded', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hypocrisy, Locked and Loaded', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco and Firearms', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Cartels', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T02:40:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4cd61a0-308e-5bc7-9656-3371c2ef5a91', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4cd61a0-308e-5bc7-9656-3371c2ef5a91'}, {'abstract': 'The Soviet dissident and widow of Andrei Sakharov told the truth, even when it didn’t advance her cause.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/opinion/21tue4.html', 'snippet': 'The Soviet dissident and widow of Andrei Sakharov told the truth, even when it didn’t advance her cause.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Jan. 22, 1980, shortly after I arrived in Moscow as a reporter, but before I met him, Andrei Sakharov was arrested and flown to internal exile in Gorky, a city closed to foreigners (now called Nizhny Novgorod). The operation was a stunning show of the Soviet state’s paranoia about a mild man who did nothing more than publicly criticize its human-rights violations. All the traffic around Sakharov’s apartment on Chkalova Street was halted and phones in the neighborhood turned off as if armed dissidents were out there ready to defend him.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Elena Georgievna Bonner, a True Human Rights Activist for 40 Years', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Appreciations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Elena Georgievna Bonner, a True Human Rights Activist for 40 Years', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sakharov, Andrei D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bonner, Elena G', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T02:43:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Serge Schmemann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Serge', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmemann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1be85f46-fb35-507a-8ab0-23e21b0a5a87', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1be85f46-fb35-507a-8ab0-23e21b0a5a87'}, {'abstract': 'A class-action lawsuit was filed against the State Department in federal court in Washington by immigrants from more than 20 countries who say officials mishandled the annual lottery of permanent resident visas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/us/politics/21brfs-Washington.html', 'snippet': 'A class-action lawsuit was filed against the State Department in federal court in Washington by immigrants from more than 20 countries who say officials mishandled the annual lottery of permanent resident visas.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A class-action lawsuit was filed against the State Department in federal court in Washington by immigrants from more than 20 countries who say officials mishandled the annual lottery of permanent resident visas. After notifying more than 20,000 foreigners this year that they had won the permanent visas, known as green cards, the State Department canceled them, saying a computer glitch had tainted the process. The immigrants, who are all living legally in the United States, had waited years for the green cards and were bitterly disappointed to have them granted and then taken away. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Immigrants Sue Over Loss of Green Cards', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WASHINGTON', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Immigrants Sue Over Loss of Green Cards', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Visas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T02:57:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9b5e162-e1f5-5417-9acf-9a09885c8832', 'word_count': 95, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9b5e162-e1f5-5417-9acf-9a09885c8832'}, {'abstract': 'Demanding political reform and autonomy, tens of thousands of rebel fighters in the Nuba Mountains have refused the Sudanese government’s threat to disarm.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/world/africa/21sudan.html', 'snippet': 'Demanding political reform and autonomy, tens of thousands of rebel fighters in the Nuba Mountains have refused the Sudanese government’s threat to disarm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NAIROBI, Kenya — The Sudanese Army and its allied militias have gone on an unsparing rampage to crush rebel fighters in the Nuba Mountains of central Sudan, bombing thatch-roofed villages, executing elders, burning churches and pitching another region of the country into crisis, according to United Nations officials and villagers who have escaped.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None,