nt row at Prada. Hole in one!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scorecard | Prada Wins!', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T18:38:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison S. Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ca61652-01c0-5338-a11c-0a9c9cd79805', 'word_count': 1389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ca61652-01c0-5338-a11c-0a9c9cd79805'}, {'abstract': 'In today’s Personal Best column, Gina Kolata explores the history of the 10 percent rule — the notion that runners who increase their weekly mileage by just 10 percent will avoid injury.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/debating-the-10-percent-rule-for-runners/', 'snippet': 'In today’s Personal Best column, Gina Kolata explores the history of the 10 percent rule — the notion that runners who increase their weekly mileage by just 10 percent will avoid injury.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In today’s Personal Best column, Gina Kolata explores the 10 percent rule — the notion that runners who increase their weekly mileage by just 10 percent will avoid injury. She writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debating the 10 Percent Rule for Runners', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Running', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sports Injuries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T18:38:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ta