kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T19:10:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Fox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ef87612-cbd9-57c1-abe7-9d5c80c599c6', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ef87612-cbd9-57c1-abe7-9d5c80c599c6'}, {'abstract': 'Does the rise of anti-war sentiment on the right tell us anything about how the next G.O.P. president might govern?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/the-politics-of-foreign-policy/', 'snippet': 'Does the rise of anti-war sentiment on the right tell us anything about how the next G.O.P. president might govern?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Responding to my column this week, Jon Chait suggests that the rise of right-wing anti-interventionism tells us very little about how the next Republican president might actually conduct foreign policy, because it’s shaped more by anti-Obama partisanship than conservative principle:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Politics of Foreign Policy', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T19:12:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27f0ffae-816b-52f5-9f1c-e4645ebd608c', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27f0ffae-816b-52f5-9f1c-e4645ebd608c'}, {'abstract': 'Amanda Lepore and Ladyfag and other nightlife denizens strike a pose in Jeremy Kost’s new monograph, “It’s Always Darkest Before Dawn.”', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/jeremy-kost-is-ready-for-his-close-up/', 'snippet': 'Amanda Lepore and Ladyfag and other nightlife denizens strike a pose in Jeremy Kost’s new monograph, “It’s Always Darkest Before Dawn.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before Jeremy Kost became a Polaroid-snapping phenom, he was working a day job as a manager for an Internet company in Washington. “I started taking pictures at the big parties I was throwing with Ed Bailey and John Guggenmos, but I eventually felt I had outgrown that city,” says Kost. He had already flirted with New York nightlife during business trips, and in 2006 he quit his Web job, moved to New York and began taking party photos full time. The result has been an oeuvre of Warholian portraits showing off his favorite nocturnal denizens. His first hardbound monograph, “It’s Always Darkest Before Dawn” (PowerHouse, $50), contains his typical club pictures, but blown up and and methodically assembled to show graphic detail. The book features a selection of cropped photographs and Polaroid collages (the result of a collaboration with Sam Shahid, a former creative director of Calvin Klein).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jeremy Kost Is Ready for His Close-Up', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T19:13:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yuri Chong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yuri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e83a90fd-c933-5de0-bbad-324e9325b687', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e83a90fd-c933-5de0-bbad-324e9325b687'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs of a theme park in Indiana that tries to tell history in a different way: not as fact but as experience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/22/arts/design/22prairie.html', 'snippet': 'Photographs of a theme park in Indiana that tries to tell history in a different way: not as fact but as experience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs of a theme park in Indiana that tries to tell history in a different way: not as fact but as experience.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/design/22prairie-slide-H89R/22prairie-slide-H89R-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/design/22prairie-slide-H89R/22prairie-slide-H89R-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/design/22prairie-slide-H89R/22prairie-slide-H89R-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Conner Prairie Interactive History Park', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T19:15:40+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ee199e13-d5ff-5887-b656-f56d4b2d07f1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ee199e13-d5ff-5887-b656-f56d4b2d07f1'}, {'abstract': 