T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:15:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Pask', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pask', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df7ac412-9ca9-5bf0-bcd4-19449c05a32c', 'word_count': 75, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df7ac412-9ca9-5bf0-bcd4-19449c05a32c'}, {'abstract': 'Biegert & Funk, a German design company, has conceived an analog table clock that declares the time of day typographically, using a matrix of letters instead of numbers.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/a-bold-statement-on-time/', 'snippet': 'Biegert & Funk, a German design company, has conceived an analog table clock that declares the time of day typographically, using a matrix of letters instead of numbers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Look Ma, no hands!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bold Statement on Time', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Watches and Clocks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:15:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Schmidt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a92df08b-6e4c-5d43-9fa9-022232b41793', 'word_count': 339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a92df08b-6e4c-5d43-9fa9-022232b41793'}, {'abstract': 'Erik Friedlander, showing off his pizzicato technique, covered all the songs of his new CD, “Bonebridge,” at Joe’s Pub.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/arts/music/erik-friedlander-plays-bonebridge-at-joes-pub-review.html', 'snippet': 'Erik Friedlander, showing off his pizzicato technique, covered all the songs of his new CD, “Bonebridge,” at Joe’s Pub.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a cellist Erik Friedlander has confronted his share of conventions: surrounding technique, repertory and proper usage. He mastered those a while ago, and departed from them. In his work with avant-garde composer-improvisers (John Zorn) and the indie-rock intelligentsia (John Darnielle) he often engages his instrument with an ear tilted toward possibilities. The same is even truer in his own work, especially within the last five years.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/3rd-suB-ERIK/3rd-suB-ERIK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/3rd-suB-ERIK/3rd-suB-ERIK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/3rd-suB-ERIK/3rd-suB-ERIK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/3rd-suB-ERIK/3rd-suB-ERIK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/3rd-suB-ERIK/3rd-suB-ERIK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking the Cello a Ways Down a Country Road', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking the Cello a Ways Down a Country Road', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Joe's Pub", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friedlander, Erik', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:15:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c99a8036-40aa-53cd-a804-a305354378e2', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c99a8036-40aa-53cd-a804-a305354378e2'}, {'abstract': 'There is an exactitude and fullness about “Electric Midwife,” Beth Gill’s new work at the Chocolate Factory in Long Island City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/arts/dance/beth-gills-electric-midwife-at-chocolate-factory-dance-review.html', 'snippet': 'There is an exactitude and fullness about “Electric Midwife,” Beth Gill’s new work at the Chocolate Factory in Long Island City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The titles of dances often seem to have nothing to do with their content. But the name of Beth Gill’s new work, which began a two-week run at the Chocolate Factory over the weekend, is “Electric Midwife,” and despite its non sequitur oddness, the title has a curious resonance after you’ve watched Ms. Gill’s highly formal, minimalist female sextet play itself out over 45 minutes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/MIDWIFE/MIDWIFE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType':