ut controversial graffiti exhibition originated by the Los Angeles Museum of Contemporary Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brooklyn Museum has canceled plans to show “Art in the Streets,” the popular but controversial graffiti exhibition originated by the Los Angeles Museum of Contemporary Art. In both a terse press release and an e-mail that, according to L.A. Weekly was sent to an artist in the show, the Brooklyn Museum’s director, Arnold Lehman, blamed tight finances for the cancellation. In the email quoted by L.A. Weekly, Mr. Lehman said: “With no major funding in place, we cannot move ahead.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brooklyn Museum Cancels Graffiti Show', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graffiti', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Museum of Contemporary Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:29:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b941bd4-1e0c-5633-80c1-c46f4d3d9dc4', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b941bd4-1e0c-5633-80c1-c46f4d3d9dc4'}, {'abstract': 'Designers are suiting up in an anticipatory nod to fall.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/wear-resort-now-suiting/', 'snippet': 'Designers are suiting up in an anticipatory nod to fall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From Stella McCartney’s lemon-drop blazer and candy-striped trousers to Jason Wu’s daytime-meets-nighttime navy suit with black piping, it’s evident from the resort shows that designers are suiting up in an anticipatory nod to fall. Translation? The cropped suit, nipped and trimmed from the leg to the sleeve, never looked so cool. Wear a facsimile now, before the weather turns brutal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wear Resort Now | Suiting', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:30:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Waldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0818710b-d22a-591c-9830-129c010cd217', 'word_count': 63, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0818710b-d22a-591c-9830-129c010cd217'}, {'abstract': 'At the adventurous Brick Theater, the latest theme involves comic books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/theater/reviews/comic-book-theater-festival-at-the-brick.html', 'snippet': 'At the adventurous Brick Theater, the latest theme involves comic books.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Our Greatest Year,” Robert Attenweiler’s tender and knowing inquiry into just why sports fans are so messed up, takes its time listening to what its characters say (or don’t say) and why. As such, it is an oasis of psychological complexity among the bells and whistles of the Comic Book Theater Festival, which is currently stuffing the Brick Theater with no end of chintzy superhero outfits.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, '