lead_paragraph': 'Most people who have read about the great Victorian art critic John Ruskin remember two things about him: that he was sued by Whistler and had to pay a farthing in damages, and that he was unable to consummate his marriage to Effie Gray because, knowing the female body only from paintings and classical statuary, he was appalled by the sight of her pubic hair. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 712, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Victorian Goddesses, a Real Wife and a Sour Marriage', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Victorian Goddesses, a Real Wife and a Sour Marriage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruskin, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:35:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a44dcf87-911f-5612-9eb9-f45a650ff414', 'word_count': 856, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a44dcf87-911f-5612-9eb9-f45a650ff414'}, {'abstract': 'While Syria’s state media lavished attention on rallies in support of the president on Tuesday, activists posted video online showing what they described as evidence that a brutal crackdown on dissent continues.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/violence-continues-in-syria-activists-say/', 'snippet': 'While Syria’s state media lavished attention on rallies in support of the president on Tuesday, activists posted video online showing what they described as evidence that a brutal crackdown on dissent continues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While Syria’s state media lavished attention on rallies in support of President Bashar al-Assad on Tuesday, opposition activists posted video online showing what they described as evidence that security forces are continuing to use violence to suppress dissent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Violence Continues in Syria, Activists Say', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:38:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c888b266-7a4d-5899-a882-909d76012eea', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c888b266-7a4d-5899-a882-909d76012eea'}, {'abstract': 'Remember how after 9/11, Osama bin Laden’s attacks on us could be linked to almost anything, from shopping habits to the rediscovery of Western values to carbon-pricing schemes?  Something similar appears to be happening with Bin Laden’s death. Jihadism sure isn’t what it used to be.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/the-end-of-afpak/', 'snippet': 'Remember how after 9/11, Osama bin Laden’s attacks on us could be linked to almost anything, from shopping habits to the rediscovery of Western values to carbon-pricing schemes?  Something similar appears to be happening with Bin Laden’s death. Ji...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Remember how after 9/11, Osama bin Laden’s attacks on us could be linked to almost anything, from shopping habits to the rediscovery of Western values to carbon-pricing schemes? \xa0Something similar appears to be happening with Bin Laden’s death. Jihadism sure isn’t what it used to be. After 10 years, it seems, the time has come to go home. Troops are and will be coming back to the United States from Iraq and Afghanistan. The military budget will be cut. The outgoing secretary of defense feels able to openly mock NATO because, presumably, he thinks he can afford to — because it doesn’t matter all that much. The global war on terror is being downgraded from Armageddon to something more out of Leviticus: a tricked-out police action, just as John Kerry, in this magazine, always said it should be. On Sunday, Helene Cooper and Mark Landler reported that “American success in the counterterrorism campaign would seem to bolster arguments for a swift withdrawal from Afghanistan.” By Tuesday morning, they were reporting that President Obama would announce his withdrawal plans on Wednesday. Meanwhile, a Harris poll revealed that 51 percent of Americans and 54 percent of Britons believed “the death of Bin Laden meant it was time to bring troops from their countries home.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The End of AfPak', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Landler, Mark', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mullen, Michael G', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Riedel, Bruce', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zawahri, Ayman Al-', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Inter-Services Intelligence', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:44:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Malcomson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malcomson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d709966-67d2-5cf8-8d7a-7ddb55a06e0f', 'word_count': 1394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d709966-67d2-5cf8-8d7a-7ddb55a06e0f'}, {'abstract': 'Early attempts to sell art online met with failure, but lately a company named Artnet has been finding success with online art auctions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/arts/design/artnet-has-online-art-sales-success.html', 'snippet': 'Early attempts to sell art online met with failure, but lately a company named Artnet has been finding success with online art auctions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roland Sledge, a 65-year-old lawyer for an oil and gas company in Houston, is no art world habitué. He began collecting prints and works on paper a little more than a decade ago, focusing on Abstract Expressionism, and has done some business with small New York galleries, “though I mostly just stumbled into things that I liked,” he said in a broad Texas accent.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/artnet-1/artnet-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/artnet-1/artnet-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 367}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/artnet-1/artnet-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/artnet-1/artnet-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/artnet-1/artnet-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Resurgence in Art Buying Over the Web', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Resurgence In Art Buying Over the Web', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:49:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/049a5203-8765-5315-aadb-978bca19b5c6', 'word_count': 1292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/049a5203-8765-5315-aadb-978bca19b5c6'}, {'abstract': 'The F.B.I. raided a Web hosting company, causing several Web sites, including those run by the Curbed Network, to go offline.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/f-b-i-seizes-web-servers-knocking-sites-offline/', 'snippet': 'The F.B.I. raided a Web hosting company, causing several Web sites, including those run by the Curbed Network, to go offline.', 'lead_paragraph': '6:16 p.m. | Updated  Adding that the F.B.I. did not comment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'F.B.I. Seizes Web Servers, Knocking Sites Offline', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Curbed.com', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:54:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verne G. Kopytoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verne', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Kopytoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/933f621d-f927-5431-997b-a8b5058a6f7f', 'word_count': 651, 'uri': 'nyt://article/933f621d-f927-5431-997b-a8b5058a6f7f'}, {'abstract': 'City Council members, union officials and liberal organizers, vowed on Tuesday to conduct mass demonstrations and work stoppages if Mayor Bloomberg’s budget cuts go through.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/at-protest-over-planned-cuts-promises-of-more-protests-to-come/', 'snippet': 'City Council members, union officials and liberal organizers, vowed on Tuesday to conduct mass demonstrations and work stoppages if Mayor Bloomberg’s budget cuts go through.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg’s plan to close 20 fire companies and lay off thousands of teachers has already prompted a steady drumbeat of protests across the city. But that may just be the beginning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Protest Over Planned Cuts, Promises of More Protests to Come', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:55:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Javier C. Hernández', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Hernández', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3c896bd-0385-534f-9163-fc3314fadcc0', 'word_count': 240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3c896bd-0385-534f-9163-fc3314fadcc0'}, {'abstract': 'New York City Opera let some details about its next season dribble out on Tuesday during a meeting with its unions, which confronted the company’s leader, George Steel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/arts/music/unions-and-city-opera-meet-about-next-season.html', 'snippet': 'New York City Opera let some details about its next season dribble out on Tuesday during a meeting with its unions, which confronted the company’s leader, George Steel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The troubled New York City Opera let some details about its next season dribble out on Tuesday during a meeting with its unions, which confronted the company’s leader, George Steel, with symbolic votes of no confidence in his leadership.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/sob-opera/sob-opera-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 551, 'width': 1005, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/sob-opera/sob-opera-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/sob-opera/sob-opera-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unions and Ailing City Opera Meet About Next Season', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unions and Ailing City Opera Meet About Next Season', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN GUILD OF MUSICAL ARTISTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:58:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b73ae6fe-d777-504c-b128-60a07c441c12', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b73ae6fe-d777-504c-b128-60a07c441c12'}, {'abstract': 'Israel’s octogenarian president, Shimon Peres, opened the Israeli Presidential Conference with Shakira by his side.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/world/middleeast/22shakira.html', 'snippet': 'Israel’s octogenarian president, Shimon Peres, opened the Israeli Presidential Conference with Shakira by his side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — When Israel’s octogenarian president, Shimon Peres, opened his Israeli Presidential Conference here on Tuesday, he chose an unlikely partner to stand by his side: Shakira, the Colombian pop star who is partly of Lebanese descent, performed last month in Beirut and was once (wrongly) accused in an Internet rumor of saying she would rather have pigs listen to her music than Israelis. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/world/22shakira_span/22shakira_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/22/world/22shakira_span/22shakira_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/world/22shakira_span/22shakira_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/world/22shakira_span/22shakira_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/22/world/22shakira_span/22shakira_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shakira Makes a Star Turn at an Israeli Conference', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shakira Makes a Star Turn at an Israeli Conference', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': "United Nations Children's Fund", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakira', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peres, Shimon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T21:59:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a887958-f0a7-55c0-bced-315003e6c1a5', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a887958-f0a7-55c0-bced-315003e6c1a5'}, {'abstract': 'While the main fair, Art Basel, was met with mixed reviews, there was consensus that Design Miami/Basel could stand on its own.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/design-miamibasel-wrap-up/', 'snippet': 'While the main fair, Art Basel, was met with mixed reviews, there was consensus that Design Miami/Basel could stand on its own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week professionals, collectors and amateurs of all things art flocked to Basel for the 42nd edition of Art Basel, the “world’s top contemporary art fair.” While the main fair was received with mixed reviews (too crowded, confusing layout, not enough outstanding pieces), an entirely different tune was sung over at Hall 5 of Basel’s venerable Messegelände, where only a day earlier Design Miami opened the doors to its annual sister fair, Design Miami/Basel, a satellite fair specializing in collectible design. There was a noticeable excitement in the air as this was the first Design Miami/Basel under the new directorship of Marianne Goebl. Her debut did not disappoint: a record 43 exhibitors showing everything from 20th-century classics to contemporary designers, intelligent design collaborations with corporate sponsors (take note, Art Basel V.I.P. lounge), a great bookstore (courtesy of doyoureadme?! from Berlin) and a well-curated series of talks (full disclosure: I moderated one of them). Design Miami/Basel may keep flourishing thanks to the cross-pollinating effect from the neighboring art fair(s), but judging by the increasingly educated and design-passionate public, it seems that the fair could very well stand on its own if it had to. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Design Miami/Basel Wrap-Up', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T22:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felix Burrichter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burrichter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7731fad9-9cee-538e-815a-418a5492ceb7', 'word_count': 1303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7731fad9-9cee-538e-815a-418a5492ceb7'}, {'abstract': 'Angela Bassett will appear in Katori Hall’s drama “The Mountaintop,” about Dr. Martin Luther King on the night before his assassination.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/angela-bassett-will-star-opposite-samuel-l-jackson-in-the-mountaintop/', 'snippet': 'Angela Bassett will appear in Katori Hall’s drama “The Mountaintop,” about Dr. Martin Luther King on the night before his assassination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Angela Bassett’s only experience working with Samuel L. Jackson was in the early 1980s when she was an understudy, and he a star, in a Negro Ensemble Company production of “Colored People’s Time,” a play of vignettes set between the Civil War and the Montgomery bus boycott. This fall the two actors will reunite on Broadway — as co-stars — for another play inspired by history, Katori Hall’s drama “The Mountaintop,” about Dr. Martin Luther King on the night before his assassination. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Angela Bassett Will Star Opposite Samuel L. Jackson in ‘The Mountaintop’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bassett, Angela', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hall, Katori', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Samuel L', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T22:01:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88826231-15f0-56b6-8eb4-3dc5bfdceab0', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88826231-15f0-56b6-8eb4-3dc5bfdceab0'}, {'abstract': 'A 14-year-old boy, a trained boxer, was charged with manslaughter after attacking a 13-year-old classmate of his sister in the rural town of Florensac, in what was described as a crime of revenge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/world/europe/22france.html', 'snippet': 'A 14-year-old boy, a trained boxer, was charged with manslaughter after attacking a 13-year-old classmate of his sister in the rural town of Florensac, in what was described as a crime of revenge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — A 14-year-old boy fatally beat a 13-year-old girl at her school in rural southern France this week, in what appears to be an unusual revenge crime that has shocked the local community and drawn wide attention.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beating Death of Schoolgirl Shocks French', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beating Death of Schoolgirl Shocks French', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-21T22:06:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maïa de la Baume', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maïa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de la Baume', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd218bca-28d6-595c-9dce-18801aa88f5f', 'word_count': 328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd218bca-28d6-595c-9dce-18801aa88f5f'}, {'abstract': 'Part 2 of an interview with David Wasserman, the House editor of The Cook Political Report.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/21/lets-keep-talking-about-redistricting/', 'snippet': 'Part 2 of an interview with David Wasserman, the House editor of The Cook Political Report.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is Part 2 of an interview with David Wasserman, the House editor of The Cook Political Report. Mr. Wasserman is Cook’s resident redistricting expert and the author of “Better Know a District,” The Cook Political Report’s 2012 redistricting outlook. (Readers might also w