nd Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T04:54:32+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/7ecc5ce9-26a2-5551-9534-745187e47c2b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/7ecc5ce9-26a2-5551-9534-745187e47c2b'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on June 22, 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/pageoneplus/corrections.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on June 22, 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRONT PAGE', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T04:56:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/57486c47-9434-5e8b-a08e-9ab88c7ebe1e', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57486c47-9434-5e8b-a08e-9ab88c7ebe1e'}, {'abstract': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/news-quiz-june-22-2011/', 'snippet': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See what you know about the news below. To prepare, you might scan the articles or summaries on today’s paper.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | June 22, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T05:09:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tiffany Frasier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tiffany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frasier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a6496f6-406d-5a7e-8280-13bf7cff4d6d', 'word_count': 20, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a6496f6-406d-5a7e-8280-13bf7cff4d6d'}, {'abstract': 'Christie’s chairman, Edward Dolman, left, has been named executive director of the Qatar Museums Authority.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/arts/design/chairman-of-christies-gets-qatar-post.html', 'snippet': 'Christie’s chairman, Edward Dolman, left, has been named executive director of the Qatar Museums Authority.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Christie’s chairman, Edward Dolman, left, has been named executive director of the Qatar Museums Authority to establish a network of museums in Doha, the capital, and to acquire art as well as to preserve the collections of the state. Mr. Dolman will divide his time among Doha, New York and London and will oversee the authority’s various initiatives to promote culture worldwide. In 2008 it opened the Museum of Islamic Art, a zigguratlike structure of white stone designed by I.M. Pei. Last year it opened the Mathaf: Arab Museum of Modern Art, which houses a collection of more than 6,000 works, spanning the 1840s to the present. Its latest project, scheduled for completion in 2014, is the reopening of the Qatar National Museum in a new building designed by the French architect Jean Nouvel. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chairman of Christie’s Gets Qatar Post', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chairman of Christie’s Gets Qatar Post', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dolman, Edward', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Christie's", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Qatar', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T06:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca554be0-62a0-5ff1-86e1-c683d7df7754', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca554be0-62a0-5ff1-86e1-c683d7df7754'}, {'abstract': 'Survival of the wrongest.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/the-triumph-of-bad-ideas/', 'snippet': 'Survival of the wrongest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bill Gross of Pimco calls for more fiscal stimulus and denounces the “anti-Keynesian” consensus. Now he tells us.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Triumph of Bad Ideas', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T06:26:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f78c0216-d2e4-5bbf-a7ad-400081f206ac', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f78c0216-d2e4-5bbf-a7ad-400081f206ac'}, {'abstract': 'Does Major League Baseball need to find a better solution to make plays at first base safer?', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/was-albert-pujolss-injury-avoidable/', 'snippet': 'Does Major League Baseball need to find a better solution to make plays at first base safer?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rule 6.05 (k) A batter is out when, in running the last half of the distance from home base to first base, while the ball is being fielded to first base, he runs outside (to the right of) the three-foot line, or inside (to the left of) the foul line, and in the umpire’s judgment in so doing interferes with the fielder taking the throw at first base, in which case the ball is dead; except that he may run outside (to the right of) the three-foot line or inside (to the left of) the foul line to avoid a fielder attempting to field a batted ball.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Was Albert Pujols’s Injury Avoidable?', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pujols, Albert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T06:31:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Bretherton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bretherton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2b5d7dc-31e9-56d5-a95e-691e099a8444', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2b5d7dc-31e9-56d5-a95e-691e099a8444'}, {'abstract': 'YES, I’M AN ANTI-CAPITALIST.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/why-your-comment-was-deleted/', 'snippet': 'YES, I’M AN ANTI-CAPITALIST.', 'lead_paragraph': '1. Gee whillikers, you used an obscenity! It’s clear that many people don’t even realize that they’re using banned words. But that’s by far the biggest reason for rejection.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Your Comment Was Deleted', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T07:12:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a529b50-766a-5755-bbdd-870eb080a9c2', 'word_count': 107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a529b50-766a-5755-bbdd-870eb080a9c2'}, {'abstract': 'How many posters of the trees in Central Park must two mapmakers sell to break even?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/test-yourself-math-june-22-2011/', 'snippet': 'How many posters of the trees in Central Park must two mapmakers sell to break even?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your math skills with today’s question, created by Bobson Wong at Math for America from the article “A Labor of Wonder: Mapping 19,993 Trees in Central Park.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Math, June 22, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T07:34:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e94f0e2-3adf-5907-9df7-b03808c1d67f', 'word_count': 55, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e94f0e2-3adf-5907-9df7-b03808c1d67f'}, {'abstract': 'No miracle.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/reagan-and-revenues/', 'snippet': 'No miracle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The wholesale voodization of the GOP is a sight to behold; apparently the nonsense about the Reagan tax cuts having led to a vast rise in revenue is now something one must claim to believe. Anyway, a quick note about Federal revenue history.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reagan and Revenues', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T07:42:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50bfb642-7939-5521-a411-d5beb2b8a606', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50bfb642-7939-5521-a411-d5beb2b8a606'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Armstrong expanded the Whitney Museum’s collection and acquired works that are now cornerstones of its collection.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/arts/design/thomas-n-armstrong-iii-whitney-museum-chief-dies-at-78.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Armstrong expanded the Whitney Museum’s collection and acquired works that are now cornerstones of its collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thomas N. Armstrong III, who greatly expanded the Whitney Museum of American Art’s holdings when he was its director in the 1970s and ’80s but whose ambitious plans for a museum addition aroused a firestorm of opposition that led to his dismissal, died on Monday in Manhattan. He was 78.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/22/arts/ARMSTRONG-obit/ARMSTRONG-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 808, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 