S. outdoor track and field championships begin, a look at the forces that keep athletes competing in an event that hasn’t produced Olympic gold for the country since 1956.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EUGENE, Ore. — Lance Deal, the last American to win an Olympic medal in the hammer throw, swept the hammer cage he built free of rain the other day at Hayward Field.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/23hammer-pix/23hammer-pix-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/23hammer-pix/23hammer-pix-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/23hammer-pix/23hammer-pix-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/23hammer-pix/23hammer-pix-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/23hammer-pix/23hammer-pix-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hammer Throw Becomes a Mystery', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Transcendent Thud', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McMahon, Kevin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Track and Field', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deal, Lance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campbell, Amber', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T16:03:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isolde Raftery', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isolde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Raftery', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9994cd6d-510c-5a6e-897f-85699611751a', 'word_count': 1019, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9994cd6d-510c-5a6e-897f-85699611751a'}, {'abstract': 'Another day of runway looks, another Scorecard at your service.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/scorecard-versace-wins-4/', 'snippet': 'Another day of runway looks, another Scorecard at your service.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ah, “heritage” — that lovely buzzword Scorecard knows has been strangely absent from the Milan shows. At Gucci, Frida Giannini cast a long look back to the house’s 1960s heyday, but as even a critic born after 1980 pointed out, if fashion goes in 20-year cycles it was Donatella Versace’s translation of the oversize curlicues and gilded delight of the “Rock the Baroque” Gianni years for the not-so-tanned leading men seated in the front row that got the fash pack’s pulses racing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scorecard | Versace Wins!', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T16:04:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison S. Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/185218e1-6cf3-5e65-a835-5ab3fbad37d6', 'word_count': 1583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/185218e1-6cf3-5e65-a835-5ab3fbad37d6'}, {'abstract': 'The latest line of G.O.P. assault on the incumbent president is to assert that however bad things were when he took office, “he made it worse.”', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/republican-senators-roll-out-an-attack-line/', 'snippet': 'The latest line of G.O.P. assault on the incumbent president is to assert that however bad things were when he took office, “he made it worse.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ready for the next Republican campaign slogan thrashing President Obama? Here it is: “He made it worse.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Republican Senators Roll Out an Attack Line', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALEXANDER, LAMAR', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConnell, Mitch', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T16:08:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d58efa4-ee51-556d-9d55-9dfd12b44108', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d58efa4-ee51-556d-9d55-9dfd12b44108'}, {'abstract': 'Beyond controversies and cost concerns, high-speed trains are reducing hurdles posed by China’s size, bringing benefits in tow and posing challenges for the country’s competitors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/business/global/23rail.html', 'snippet': 'Beyond controversies and cost concerns, high-speed trains are reducing hurdles posed by China’s size, bringing benefits in tow and posing challenges for the country’s competitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHANGSHA, China — Even as China prepares to open bullet train service from Beijing to Shanghai by July 1, this nation’s steadily expanding high-speed rail network is being pilloried on a scale rare among Chinese citizens and news media. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/business/Rail/Rail-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 304, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/23/business/Rail/Rail-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 304}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/business/Rail/Rail-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 518, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/business/Rail/Rail-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/23/business/Rail/Rail-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'High-Speed Rail Poised to Alter China', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'High-Speed Rail Poised to Alter China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High-Speed Rail Projects', 'rank': 2,