 the company’s path to profitability.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Wants Hulu?', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hulu.com', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T18:06:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adrienne Carter, Evelyn M. Rusli and Mac William Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adrienne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c48ffb7-b1a3-55b6-a1ee-7c90cfb9252b', 'word_count': 38, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c48ffb7-b1a3-55b6-a1ee-7c90cfb9252b'}, {'abstract': 'Bethanie Mattek-Sands of the United States wore a jacket with tennis ball halves attached to the sleeves to her first-round match.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/mattek-sands-makes-an-entrance-then-exits/', 'snippet': 'Bethanie Mattek-Sands of the United States wore a jacket with tennis ball halves attached to the sleeves to her first-round match.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — All eyes were glued to Bethanie Mattek-Sands like the tennis balls to her sleeves as she made her way from the locker room to Court 14 Wednesday for her first-round match against Misaki Doi of Japan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mattek-Sands Makes an Entrance, Then an Exit', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mattek-Sands, Bethanie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T18:08:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd91286b-bf78-5c3d-9a11-c781ec82aa34', 'word_count': 562, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd91286b-bf78-5c3d-9a11-c781ec82aa34'}, {'abstract': 'The needle the former Utah governor needs to thread.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/the-huntsman-candidacy/', 'snippet': 'The needle the former Utah governor needs to thread.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can a moderate Republican win the G.O.P. nomination? That’s the question everyone’s asking with regard to Jon Huntsman, who formally announced his candidacy yesterday, and the answer is a resounding yes. From George H.W. Bush and Bob Dole through George W. Bush and John McCain, the modern-day Republican Party has a long tradition of picking nominees whose records and positions place them to the left of True Conservatism, at least as defined by movement institutions, talk radio and the like. Even in the age of the Tea Party, there’s reason to imagine this pattern persisting: Many Republican primary voters hold heterodox positions (for instance, on tax increases on the rich) relative to the official conservative line, several of the crucial early primaries allow independents to cross over and vote, and in races against incumbent presidents there’s a high premium on electability. It’s certainly possible for a Republican to be too liberal for the primary electorate, and often a moderate candidate has to flip-flop on a key issue (or six) to appease the party’s interest groups. But the historical record suggests that there’s more room between Rush Limbaugh and the center than many commentators (and politicians) seem to think.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Huntsman Candidacy', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T18:13:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5318d578-934f-5aee-ad89-132d81c71cbc', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5318d578-934f-5aee-ad89-132d81c71cbc'}, {'abstract': 'Marco Mattiacci says the average Ferrari buyer in the United States is 47 years old, while in China, the brand’s second-largest market, that buyer is 32.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/ferrari-north-america-chief-makes-a-play-for-younger-buyers/', 'snippet': 'Marco Mattiacci says the average Ferrari buyer in the United States is 47 years old, while in China, the brand’s second-largest market, that buyer is 32.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dressed in a Mediterranean-blue summer suit, Marco Mattiacci, the president of Ferrari North America, contrasted nicely with the yellow 458 Italia on the floor of the company’s showroom in Manhattan, as well as with the chic, champagne-sipping crowd surrounding it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ferrari North America Chief Makes a Play for Younger Buyers', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ferrari SpA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Park Avenue (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T18:14:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c8f4355-e534-5cff-bfe1-96fcea06e82a', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c8f4355-e534-5cff-bfe1-96fcea06e82a'}, {'abstract': 'Italy’s foreign minister, Franco Frattini, said Wednesday that his government would support a suspension of NATO-led attacks in Libya in order to open an aid corridor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/world/africa/23libya.html', 'snippet': 'Italy’s foreign minister, Franco Frattini, said Wednesday that his government would support a suspension of NATO-led attacks in Libya in order to open an aid corridor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROME — Italy’s foreign minister, Franco Frattini, said Wednesday that his government would support a suspension of NATO-led attacks in Libya in order to open an aid corridor to bring supplies to the country’s population, which he said was facing “a dramatic humanitarian situation.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Italy Supports Pause in NATO Attacks in Libya', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Italy Supports Pause in NATO Attacks in Libya', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'n