thered jeans impeccably fit. There will be stories of incredible parties with the well-known, casual barbs about the well-to-do. You could get used to this, you think. ', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/fashion/Z-CRITIC-1/Z-CRITIC-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/23/fashion/Z-CRITIC-1/Z-CRITIC-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/fashion/Z-CRITIC-1/Z-CRITIC-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/fashion/Z-CRITIC-1/Z-CRITIC-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/23/fashion/Z-CRITIC-1/Z-CRITIC-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Giving Eccentricities Free Rein', 'kicker': 'Critical Shopper', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Giving Eccentricities Free Rein', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jeans (Apparel)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'NoLIta (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T21:19:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab71a280-2133-5633-83c8-b0494218ccb6', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab71a280-2133-5633-83c8-b0494218ccb6'}, {'abstract': 'In Jason Grote’s “Civilization (All You Can Eat)” at Here, a group of interconnected characters tackle some big questions of modern culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/theater/reviews/civilization-all-you-can-eat-at-here-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Jason Grote’s “Civilization (All You Can Eat)” at Here, a group of interconnected characters tackle some big questions of modern culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We are all creatures rooting in the gutter of appetite, but most of us are also looking at the stars. Or so it goes in Jason Grote’s “Civilization (All You Can Eat),” a play whose conventional heart is not particularly well camouflaged by its postmodern bells and whistles.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/CIVIL/CIVIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Porker Has a Beef With the World', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Civilization (All You Can Eat)", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'This Porker Has a Beef With the World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Clubbed Thumb', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grote, Jason', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Safer, Dan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T21:26:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Saltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c9dc23b-6f11-535c-bad2-68e0b26d8802', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c9dc23b-6f11-535c-bad2-68e0b26d8802'}, {'abstract': 'The chef and restaurateur of Le Pigeon in Portland, Ore., on winning this year’s James Beard Foundation’s Rising Star Chef of the Year award.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/asked-answered-gabriel-rucker/', 'snippet': 'The chef and restaurateur of Le Pigeon in Portland, Ore., on winning this year’s James Beard Foundation’s Rising Star Chef of the Year award.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s early in the evening on a Tuesday, and Gabriel Rucker is in a winning mood. The chef and restaurateur, who first gained national attention in 2007 for his restaurant Le Pigeon in Portland, Ore., is this year’s recipient of the James Beard Foundation’s Rising Star Chef of the Year award, an honor reserved for chefs under 30. His work at Le Pigeon draws from classic technique, the ample larder of Oregon’s Willamette Valley and the character of Portland itself — equal parts divine and dingy, local and wild. Rucker’s latest venture, Little Bird, opened in late 2010 and is more of a classic bistro in style and setting, with a fascinating cocktail list, cheap beer selections and strokes of clever embedded throughout the menu; think enormous marrow bones with balsamic vinegar onions presented à la Fred Flinstone, or a sorbet and ice cream course served in pastel miniature egg cartons.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Asked & Answered | Gabriel Rucker', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T21:32:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jordan Michelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jordan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Michelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccca791c-7254-59ef-ab54-60791deea435', 'word_count': 1377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccca791c-7254-59ef-ab54-60791deea435'}, {'abstract': 'The blog publisher Curbed Network and other companies worked to restore their operations after an F.B.I. raid on a data center affected their Web sites.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/sites-rebuild-after-f-b-i-raid-on-data-center/', 'snippet': 'The blog publisher Curbed Network and other companies worked to restore their operations after an F.B.I. raid on a data center affected their Web sites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Curbed Network, a New York blog publisher, and a few other companies were working to restore their operations to normal on Wednesday after an F.B.I. raid on a data center the day before took them offline or slowed their Web sites’ performance. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sites Rebuild After F.B.I. Raid on Data Center', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search and Seizure', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Curbed.com', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T21:36:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verne G. Kopytoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verne', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Kopytoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45604142-c89c-551d-8cd0-58b8e708a343', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45604142-c89c-551d-8cd0-58b8e708a343'}, {'abstract': 'On “Suits,” relentless sunshine, improbable ascents and, yes, expensive suits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/arts/television/suits-on-usa-review.html', 'snippet': 'On “Suits,” relentless sunshine, improbable ascents and, yes, expensive suits.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Suits,” a new legal series beginning on Thursday on USA, erupts from a Mount Vesuvius of absurdities, and yet like so much else in the USA stable, it comes in relatively good faith — not merely with a snappy sense of its own fun, but also with a chipper belief in the viability of renewal. The channel seems to have a mandate to uplift the national mood, to appeal to the fantasy of second chances and sexy opportunities. On a USA series there are no dank unemployment offices, no cups of weak coffee, only high floors, soaring views and crisp French roasts.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/SUBSUITS/SUBSUITS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/SUBSUITS/SUBSUITS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/SUBSUITS/SUBSUITS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/SUBSUITS/SUBSUITS-thumbStandard.jpg