arge Australian man wearing a dog suit.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Wilfred,” beginning Thursday night on FX, is a semi-dark comedy in which Frodo Baggins frequently shares a bong with a large Australian man wearing a dog suit. Some shows aspire to cult status; this one goes straight there, practically bypassing the need to be broadcast at all.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/WILFRED/WILFRED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 879, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/WILFRED/WILFRED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/WILFRED/WILFRED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Men Hang Out on a Couch. But One Appears to Be a Dog.', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Men Hang Out on a Couch. But One Appears to Be a Dog.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wood, Elijah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FX', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T22:14:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f462f0db-b8b6-512a-a110-c18ac4ec3431', 'word_count': 585, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f462f0db-b8b6-512a-a110-c18ac4ec3431'}, {'abstract': '“Planet Pit,” the sixth album by the Cuban-American rapper Pitbull, completes his long transformation from crunk-era curio to dance-rap star.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/arts/music/pitbull-the-rapper-releases-planet-pit.html', 'snippet': '“Planet Pit,” the sixth album by the Cuban-American rapper Pitbull, completes his long transformation from crunk-era curio to dance-rap star.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Making one of the year’s most important rap albums just doesn’t mean what it used to. For one thing, it can have very little to do with actual rapping. Just ask the Cuban-American Miami rapper Pitbull, now more important than ever, and more popular, thanks to his irrepressible new album, “Planet Pit.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/PITBULLALT/PITBULLALT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/PITBULLALT/PITBULLALT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/PITBULLALT/PITBULLALT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rootless Rapper Finds His Rhythm', 'kicker': "Critic's Notebook", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rootless Rapper Finds His Rhythm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T22:16:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e36525a-d22d-5f96-97a0-64423ea337a5', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e36525a-d22d-5f96-97a0-64423ea337a5'}, {'abstract': 'Sade Adu and her band, on their first American tour in a decade, try turning the Nassau Coliseum into a club.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/arts/music/sade-at-the-nassau-coliseum-review.html', 'snippet': 'Sade Adu and her band, on their first American tour in a decade, try turning the Nassau Coliseum into a club.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNIONDALE, N.Y. — Sade Adu emerged from below the Nassau Coliseum stage here on Tuesday night immaculate: her all-black outfit tight and modest, her heels perilously high, her hair pulled back hard. Ms. Adu is 52, and in almost three decades of leading her band, Sade, she has barely aged and has never once appeared hurried. Stasis is her art.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/SUBSADE/SUBSADE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/SUBSADE/SUBSADE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/arts/SUBSADE/SUBSADE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Singer in No Rush, Deploying Her Big Gestures Carefully', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Singer in No Rush, Deploying Her Big Gestures Carefully', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sade', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T22:19:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/26e6c020-f13a-5e7e-9fa6-728c2125aa6a', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26e6c020-f13a-5e7e-9fa6-728c2125aa6a'}, {'abstract': 'NBC’s reality competitions “The Voice” and “America’s Got Talent” dominated the ratings on Tuesday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/arts/television/reality-contests-win.html', 'snippet': 'NBC’s reality competitions “The Voice” and “America’s Got Talent” dominated the ratings on Tuesday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NBC’s reality competitions “The Voice” and “America’s Got Talent” dominated the ratings on Tuesday night, while two new shows on ABC got off to modest starts. According to Nielsen’s estimates, the most watched show was “America’s Got Talent,” which attracted 12 million viewers at 8, but “The Voice,” from 9 to 11, led the night in the demographic of viewers 18 to 49 while earning nearly as many total viewers (11.8 million). ABC drew 5.5 million at 9 for the premiere of its new series “101 Ways to Leave a Game Show.” The debut of its drama “Combat Hospital” drew 5.3 million viewers. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reality Contests Win', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reality Contests Win', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-22T22:19:59+0000', 'docume