01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sean Collins Walsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sean', 'middlename': 'Collins', 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04ee2afb-c61a-5052-87b3-c6a4c6336b26', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04ee2afb-c61a-5052-87b3-c6a4c6336b26'}, {'abstract': 'An American diplomat’s mission to Italy reveals confusions over the Union’s war aims.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/22/mr-marshs-italian-job/', 'snippet': 'An American diplomat’s mission to Italy reveals confusions over the Union’s war aims.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In June 1861, George Perkins Marsh arrived in Turin to begin his appointment as envoy extraordinary to Italy from the United States of America. It was a fitting title. These were extraordinary times: that spring witnessed both the disintegration of the United States and the unification of Italy. The Lincoln administration, eager to buttress the Union’s European position against burgeoning Confederate diplomacy, immediately recognized the new Italian state. Whether Washington could build an actual alliance with Italy that could serve its war aims was a question Marsh had been sent to answer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mr. Marsh’s Italian Job', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garibaldi, Giuseppe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Seward, William Henry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T00:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Albin J. Kowalewski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Albin', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Kowalewski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d9e343a-dad5-58f1-9453-eaa112458105', 'word_count': 1535, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d9e343a-dad5-58f1-9453-eaa112458105'}, {'abstract': 'Andy Murray, 24, is the latest in a line of British players to try to break a jolly bad string; it has been 75 years since Fred Perry gave Wimbledon its last homegrown men’s singles champion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/sports/tennis/wimbledon-andy-murray-sparks-little-hope-among-britons.html', 'snippet': 'Andy Murray, 24, is the latest in a line of British players to try to break a jolly bad string; it has been 75 years since Fred Perry gave Wimbledon its last homegrown men’s singles champion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — Between bites of a ham and cheese sandwich, Jill Smith, an Englishwoman who attended Wimbledon on Wednesday with her grown daughter, assessed the title chances of Britain’s top player, Andy Murray. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/Y-JP-MURRAY/Y-JP-MURRAY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 348, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/Y-JP-MURRAY/Y-JP-MURRAY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 348}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/Y-JP-MURRAY/Y-JP-MURRAY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'lega