o unshackle their apartments from rent regulation has been to renovate units as soon as the tenants moved out, allowing them to significantly raise rents, to free-market rates. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/nyregion/RENT/RENT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/23/nyregion/RENT/RENT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/nyregion/RENT/RENT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/nyregion/RENT/RENT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/23/nyregion/RENT/RENT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Deal Will Make It Harder to Use Renovation to Free Apartments From Rent Regulation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deal Will Make It Harder to Use Renovation to Free Apartments From Rent Regulation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Landlords', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rent Control and Stabilization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T02:19:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db348b52-c2a0-57ef-9e9e-ed0bddfd9f6a', 'word_count': 596, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db348b52-c2a0-57ef-9e9e-ed0bddfd9f6a'}, {'abstract': 'In many states, courts have stepped in to preserve vital programs for the vulnerable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/opinion/23thu2.html', 'snippet': 'In many states, courts have stepped in to preserve vital programs for the vulnerable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The last three years have been the hardest for state budgets since the depression, and virtually every state has cut services. Most often, it is the poor, the young, and the elderly who are most hurt, as 34 states cut K-12 education, 31 health care, and 29 elderly and disabled services.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Budget Cutters v. the Law', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Budget Cutters v. the Law: In many states, courts have stepped in to preserve vital programs for the vulnerable', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Carolina', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T02:22:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2276d577-0d75-572e-938a-77f8e669b467', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2276d577-0d75-572e-938a-77f8e669b467'}, {'abstract': 'As the legislative session drew to a close, lawmakers considered a bill to encourage more efficient power plants and help utility customers make improvements to their homes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/nyregion/albany-weighs-bill-for-power-plants-and-home-loans.html', 'snippet': 'As the legislative session drew to a close, lawmakers considered a bill to encourage more efficient power plants and help utility customers make improvements to their homes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Utility customers would be able to pay off the cost of making their homes more energy efficient through charges on their monthly electric bills and the process of getting permits for new power plants would be streamlined under a bill that state lawmakers in Albany were expected to pass on Wednesday night. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Albany Mulls Altering Way State Permits Power Plants', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Albany Mulls Altering Way State Permits Power Plants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy Efficiency', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T02:25:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6de6057-c949-5fdc-b420-1e2d3096cde5', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6de6057-c949-5fdc-b420-1e2d3096cde5'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo’s agreement with the biggest public employee union saved money and jobs. Other unions should begin negotiating for similar deals quickly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/opinion/23thu3.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo’s agreement with the biggest public employee union saved money and jobs. Other unions should begin negotiating for similar deals quickly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo of New York and leaders of the state’s biggest public employee union announced a responsible agreement on Wednesday that would create significant budget savings while helping to reduce potential layoffs. The five-year deal with the Civil Service Employees Association freezes wages for three years and raises employee contributions to health benefits. Other unions in the state should quickly adopt similar packages.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Reasonable Public Union Deal', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Reasonable Public Union Deal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T02:25:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_mater