r that he had epilepsy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One day during medical school, my classmates and I learned that one of the most well-liked doctors-in-training in the hospital had had a seizure while leading morning work rounds. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Epilepsy From the Patient’s Point of View', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Epilepsy Specialist With a Patientu2019s Point of View', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Epilepsy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T11:05:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pauline W. Chen, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pauline', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e8a3c20-c1be-580a-bf26-65ca938bbea5', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e8a3c20-c1be-580a-bf26-65ca938bbea5'}, {'abstract': 'Some advice from a “retention” counselor who, not too long ago, was a student himself: wake up at the same time every day, be wise how you work and play, and visit the career center early.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/now-that-youve-gotten-in/', 'snippet': 'Some advice from a “retention” counselor who, not too long ago, was a student himself: wake up at the same time every day, be wise how you work and play, and visit the career center early.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lionel Anderson is an academic adviser at The Fox School, the undergraduate and graduate business school at Temple University, where he leads an initiative to raise graduation rates. A decade ago, he received a New York Times College Scholarship, which he used to attend the University of Pennsylvania and earn bachelor’s and master’s degrees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now That You’ve Gotten In', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T11:26:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lionel Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lionel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bdaad99-06ee-5e43-b497-20652df68e07', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bdaad99-06ee-5e43-b497-20652df68e07'}, {'abstract': 'Even as many senior Afghan officials echoed President Hamid Karzai, closer to the ground, local leaders and ordinary people expressed fears of civil war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/world/asia/24afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'Even as many senior Afghan officials echoed President Hamid Karzai, closer to the ground, local leaders and ordinary people expressed fears of civil war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — Many in Kabul are welcoming the announcement of the United States troop withdrawal, but in southern Afghanistan, in the pomegranate orchards and deserts that are the war’s front lines, the mood is far more sober: Local officials and tribal elders are questioning the ability of Afghan troops to defend them and say the Taliban are far from defeated.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/world/24afghanistan-1308873210029/24afghanistan-1308873210029-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'w