tors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T14:26:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cca44a0-9093-5b62-8a97-5d4be108babb', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cca44a0-9093-5b62-8a97-5d4be108babb'}, {'abstract': 'The Los Angeles artist Lisa Anne Auerbach likes to make statements, in the form of knitted sweater-and-skirt works that feature clever slogans on hot topics.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/seeing-things-studio-visit-lisa-anne-auerbach/', 'snippet': 'The Los Angeles artist Lisa Anne Auerbach likes to make statements, in the form of knitted sweater-and-skirt works that feature clever slogans on hot topics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The artist Lisa Anne Auerbach likes to make statements, which are most often knitted. She is best known for her sweater-and-skirt works that feature clever, assertive slogans offering commentary and critique on issues that are important to her, whether political or personal. After receiving an M.F.A. in photography from the Art Center College of Design in Pasadena, Calif., she no longer had access to the school’s darkroom and taught herself to knit instead. “Knitting was a way I could continue to make art,” she says. “It’s portable and I can do it anywhere. Now that I’m teaching photography at Pomona College, I knit on the train during my commute, and it’s a great way to pass the time in faculty meetings.” Auerbach, who lives and works in a space in downtown Los Angeles (which she shares with several other artists), still knits the old-fashioned way, with needles, but her studio also houses two computer-controlled knitting machines.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Things | Studio Visit: Lisa Anne Auerbach', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T14:30:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ec13a72-ecec-549a-8a38-86bba27ed853', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ec13a72-ecec-549a-8a38-86bba27ed853'}, {'abstract': 'C.J. Chivers examines Internet claims on the use of cluster munitions in Libya.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/following-up-part-iii-down-the-rabbit-hole-qaddafis-cluster-munitions-and-the-age-of-internet-claims/', 'snippet': 'C.J. Chivers examines Internet claims on the use of cluster munitions in Libya.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This month At War has revisited in detail the attacks this spring by Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s forces on Misurata, Libya with cluster munitions. The blog has retraced the steps that led to the identification of the Spanish-made MAT-120 munitions, and documented the sale and transfer of these munitions from Spain to Libya from 2006 through early 2008, addressing, in the process, the Qaddafi government’s claims of not possessing any of these rounds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Following Up, Part III.  Down the Rabbit Hole:  Qaddafi’s Cluster Munitions and the Age of Internet Claims', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cluster Munitions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Human Rights Watch', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Navy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Misurata (Libya)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T14:32:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By C.J. Chivers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Chivers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f41a738-d1cd-5622-972d-5ede91765549', 'word_count': 3825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f41a738-d1cd-5622-972d-5ede91765549'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney made it his mission to oust Whitey Bulger’s brother, William Bulger, as president of the University of Massachusetts.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/romney-waged-political-battle-against-bulgers-brother/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney made it his mission to oust Whitey Bulger’s brother, William Bulger, as president of the University of Massachusetts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The capture of the Boston crime boss Whitey Bulger by the F.B.I. must have been received with great interest in the presidential campaign headquarters of Mitt Romney, the former governor of Massachusetts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Waged Battle to Oust Whitey Bulger’s Brother', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bulger, James J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bulger, William M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T14:33:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87d0c62e-06c7-5578-a46a-e6bbd4292fb3', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87d0c62e-06c7-5578-a46a-e6bbd4292fb3'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s pairing: the poem “Our Lady of Perpetual Help,” and an article from 2009, “A Home to Prayers of Healing and Hope.”', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/poetry-pairing-june-23-2011/', 'snippet': 'This week’s pairing: the poem “Our Lady of Perpetual Help,” and an article from 2009, “A Home to Prayers of Healing and Hope.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In our weekly “Poetry Pairing” series, we collaborate with the Poetry Foundation to feature a work from its American Life in Poetry project alongside content from The Times that somehow echoes, extends or challenges the poem’s themes. Each poem is introduced briefly by Ted Kooser, a former United States poet laureate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry Pairing | June 23, 20