maginable — he refused to say anything more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — After 80 days held without charge, China’s most voluble government critic, the artist Ai Weiwei, emerged from detention late on Wednesday night, thanked reporters for their concern and then did something almost unimaginable — he refused to say anything more. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/world/24china/24china-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/24/world/24china/24china-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/world/24china/24china-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/world/24china/24china-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/24/world/24china/24china-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Free, a Chinese Dissident Muzzles Himself', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Now Free, a Chinese Dissident Muzzles Himself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ai Weiwei', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T18:11:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99630e56-fd9c-548e-ad27-6bd8b5c180e3', 'word_count': 964, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99630e56-fd9c-548e-ad27-6bd8b5c180e3'}, {'abstract': 'The plastic sheaths from the dry cleaner are not your friends. Barbara Harman’s search for the perfect textiles to protect her clothing and linens resulted in the creation of her own business, the Butler’s Closet.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/what-you-store-your-clothes-in-plastic/', 'snippet': 'The plastic sheaths from the dry cleaner are not your friends. Barbara Harman’s search for the perfect textiles to protect her clothing and linens resulted in the creation of her own business, the Butler’s Closet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dry cleaning bags are not your friends. They may appear to be helpful, caring sheaths of plastic, but in the end, they do not act like friends would. In fact, they will yellow your designer clothing and linen tablecloths and, eventually, trap moisture, which leads to mold. Would a friend lead to mold? And those garment bags — the really thick ones from nice stores? They are just as bad, perhaps worse for appearing to be extra protective. Barbara Harman knows this, and it is part of the reason she founded the Butler’s Closet, a new Web-based business that specializes in covers for clothing, furniture, linens and other precious things. The covers, made from fabric that is free of chemicals, bleaches, sizing and dyes, meet the same textile conservation standards used by museum curators. They retail from $16 to $135.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What? You Store Your Clothes in Plastic?', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T18:16:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Mulcahy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mulcahy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49757cfa-74f3-53ce-bae3-b3adeadc6afc', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49757cfa-74f3-53ce-bae3-b3adeadc6afc'}, {'abstract': 'Noreen Connolly, one of two “Win a Trip with Nicholas Kristof” winners for 2011, writes about the difficulty of getting accurate information when dealing with language and cultural barriers.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/a-journalism-teachers-quest-for-accuracy-in-southern-niger/', 'snippet': 'Noreen Connolly, one of two “Win a Trip with Nicholas Kristof” winners for 2011, writes about the difficulty of getting accurate information when dealing with language and cultural barriers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I teach my journalism students to ask good questions. Stay away from ones that elicit yes or no answers. Go for the follow-up. Ask for clarification. Listen. Here in southern Niger, I’m learning that asking good questions is no guarantee of getting accurate information, and that not even patience and skepticism can get to the truth sometimes. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Journalism Teacher’s Quest for Accuracy in Southern Niger', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T18:18:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noreen Connolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noreen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Connolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/607e2df5-c739-5ecc-8281-b9adbdacf437', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/607e2df5-c739-5ecc-8281-b9adbdacf437'}, {'abstract': 'Saumya Dave, one of two “Win a Trip with Nicholas Kristof” winners for 2011, compares her life to that of a woman approximately her age in rural Niger.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/twenty-somethings-compare-notes/', 'snippet': 'Saumya Dave, one of two “Win a Trip with Nicholas Kristof” winners for 2011, compares her life to that of a woman approximately her age in rural Niger.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saumya Dave, a student, and Noreen Connolly, a teacher, are the 2011 “Win A Trip” winners and are currently traveling with Nick through parts of North and West Africa. Saumya’s third post is from Niger. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two 20-Somethings Compare Their Days', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T18:18:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By SAUMYA DAVE', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Saumya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DAVE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f582734-aafa-5d06-bf97-69bcc2907783', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f582734-aafa-5d06-bf97-69bcc2907783'}, {'abstract': 'Employers may see an opportunity to shed their insurance burden once 2014 arrives, but the system that’s coming is so new and different — and complicated — that it seems foolish to presume this will happen right away.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/debating-whether-businesses-will-continue-to-offer-health-insurance/', 'snippet': 'Employers may see an opportunity to shed their insurance burden once 2014 arrives, but the system that’s coming is so new and different — and complicated — that it seems foolish to presume this will happen right away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two weeks ago, the international consulting firm McKinsey & Company threw itself into the rancorous partisan debate over the 2010 health care overhaul by publishing research that appeared to predict that many employers would dump their health insurance plans when the new law fully took effect in 2014. The new law offers subsidies to help low-income people purchase health insurance from state-run exchanges, and it also penalizes companies with more than 50 employees when they fail to offer those employees affordable coverage. As McKinsey notes, paying the penalty will cost less than providing the insurance, so companies could profit by socializing that cost.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debating Whether Businesses Will Continue to Offer Health Insurance', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T18:19:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robb Mandelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mandelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ff98407-9c3a-565a-bca6-f687884d87e8', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ff98407-9c3a-565a-bca6-f687884d87e8'}, {'abstract': 'The day in sports included motocross racing in Austria, golf in Germany and tennis at Wimbledon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/23/sports/20110623-photoreplay.html', 'snippet': 'The day in sports included motocross racing in Austria, golf in Germany and tennis at Wimbledon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The day in sports included motocross racing in Austria, golf in Germany and tennis at Wimbledon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/20110623-photoreplay-slide-2TZP/20110623-photoreplay-slide-2TZP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/20110623-photoreplay-slide-2TZP/20110623-photoreplay-slide-2TZP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/23/sports/20110623-photoreplay-slide-2TZP/20110623-photoreplay-slide-2TZP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Photo Replay: June 23', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soderling, Robin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hewitt, Lleyton', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Montgomerie, Colin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Motocross', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T18:24:25+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/8d05542d-88b8-5c03-a3d2-631288a8c39d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/8d05542d-88b8-5c03-a3d2-631288a8c39d'}, {'abstract': 'The Boston gangster had a soft spot for a Manhattan landmark that could aptly describe the attributes of the investigators who hunted him.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/whitey-bulgers-new-york/', 'snippet': 'The Boston gangster had a soft spot for a Manhattan landmark that could aptly describe the attributes of the investigators who hunted him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No doubt about it, James J. Bulger, known as Whitey, was as Boston as cream pie and the Tea Party, Version 1.0. But even he had his New York moment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Whitey Bulger’s New York', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bulger, James J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Public Library', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T18:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62262b54-77dc-5507-a2f5-b5f5efa6fa1f', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62262b54-77dc-5507-a2f5-b5f5efa6fa1f'}, {'abstract': "Despite deals with Chinese partners, the automaker's owner said that it was unable to pay employees their June salaries, and prospects for obtaining short-term funding were uncertain.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/business/global/24saab.html', 'snippet': "Despite deals with Chinese partners, the automaker's owner said that it was unable to pay employees their June salaries, and prospects for obtaining short-term funding were uncertain.", 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Saab on Thursday found itself facing a possible collapse after the automaker’s Dutch owner said that it was unable to pay employees their salaries for June and warned that the prospects of obtaining short-term financing were uncertain.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saab Workers Miss a Payday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saab Unable To Pay Wages As Financing Looks Hazy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Saab Automobile AB', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T18:31:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26a5ab2b-459c-5f8a-a7ac-fcfd2bf4a12f', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26a5ab2b-459c-5f8a-a7ac-fcfd2bf4a12f'}, {'abstract': 'The proposed medication, from Bristol-Myers Squibb and Pfizer, prevents more strokes with less bleeding risk than existing treatment. It needs F.D.A. approval.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/health/24drugs.html', 'snippet': 'The proposed medication, from Bristol-Myers Squibb and Pfizer, prevents more strokes with less bleeding risk than existing treatment. It needs F.D.A. approval.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A proposed new anticlotting drug for people with irregular heartbeats has been shown to prevent more strokes with less bleeding risk than the existing treatment, the drug makers Bristol-Myers Squibb and Pfizer said late Wednesday. The announcement sent their stocks soaring on Thursday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Anticlotting Drug Shows Promise to Displace a Longtime Standard', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Anticlotting Drug Shows Promise To Displace a Longtime Standard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bristol Myers Squibb Company', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stroke', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pfizer Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T18:40:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Duff Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Duff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd02fcf0-3ad2-5b44-b546-397f73dcc23f', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd02fcf0-3ad2-5b44-b546-397f73dcc23f'}, {'abstract': 'Lleyton Hewitt is one of tennis’s most dogged defenders, but this was ridiculous. On Thursday, Hewitt was battling Father Time, Robin Soderling, a quarterfinalist here last year and the No. 5 seed, and injury.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/career-near-its-end-hewitt-briefly-turns-back-the-clock/', 'snippet': 'Lleyton Hewitt is one of tennis’s most dogged defenders, but this was ridiculous. On Thursday, Hewitt was battling Father Time, Robin Soderling, a quarterfinalist here last year and the No. 5 seed, and injury.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lleyton Hewitt is one of tennis’s most dogged defenders, but this was ridiculous. On Thursday, Hewitt was battling Father Time, Robin Soderling, a quarterfinalist here last year and the No. 5 seed, and injury. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Career Near Its End, Hewitt, Briefly, Turns Back the Clock', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hewitt, Lleyton', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T18:47:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1da3975-7b0d-5c30-bb63-789ce7619df5', 'word_count': 370, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1da3975-7b0d-5c30-bb63-789ce7619df5'}, {'abstract': 'In South Boston, Whitey Bulger — either a modern-day Robin Hood or a ruthless killer, depending on whom you ask — seemed to be everywhere on Thursday morning.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/us/24southie.html', 'snippet': 'In South Boston, Whitey Bulger — either a modern-day Robin Hood or a ruthless killer, depending on whom you ask — seemed to be everywhere on Thursday morning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — In South Boston, the once-evasive Whitey Bulger, the mob boss considered a modern-day Robin Hood or a ruthless killer, depending on whom you ask, was seemingly everywhere on a dreary Thursday morning. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In South Boston, Mixed Memories of Whitey Bulger', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In South Boston, Mixed Memories of Whitey Bulger', 'name': None, 'seo': No