om/2011/06/24/arts/design/the-morgan-library-creates-a-drawing-institute.html', 'snippet': 'The Morgan Library & Museum makes a greater investment in drawing, and a Renoir at the Frick gets a closer look.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To propel itself into the 21st century, the Morgan Library & Museum began to present and collect Modern and contemporary art in the last few years. Now it is also beginning to concentrate more heavily on what it has always been known for: drawings.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/VOGEL/VOGEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 647, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/VOGEL/VOGEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/VOGEL/VOGEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Morgan Creates a Drawing Institute', 'kicker': 'Inside Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Morgan Creates A Drawing Institute', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Library & Museum', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Frick Collection', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bard College', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Neue Galerie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T20:17:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8196462c-1246-5794-90fb-83abb4c2dfec', 'word_count': 1117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8196462c-1246-5794-90fb-83abb4c2dfec'}, {'abstract': 'Contributors to the International Tribune Magazine’s summer issue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/opinion/global/24iht-june24-ihtmag-contributors.html', 'snippet': 'Contributors to the International Tribune Magazine’s summer issue.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/opinion/24ihtcoverimg/24ihtcoverimg-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/24/opinion/24ihtcoverimg/24ihtcoverimg-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/opinion/24ihtcoverimg/24ihtcoverimg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/opinion/24ihtcoverimg/24ihtcoverimg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/24/opinion/24ihtcoverimg/24ihtcoverimg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Contributors', 'kicker': 'I.H.T. Global Agenda | Shifting Power', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Contributors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T20:17:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Global Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/860fe768-fe69-5621-91b1-d5e2ca0c3f8c', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/860fe768-fe69-5621-91b1-d5e2ca0c3f8c'}, {'abstract': 'Fortunato’s jewelry collection for resort was inspired by her travel to Uruguay, Guatemala and Texas.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/dont-forget-resort-jewels-lizzie-fortunato/', 'snippet': 'Fortunato’s jewelry collection for resort was inspired by her travel to Uruguay, Guatemala and Texas.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I am a magpie collecting things everywhere I go,” says Lizzie Fortunato, the designer behind her namesake Lizzie Fortunato Jewels line. Her finds have included antique whalebone poker chips in Montevideo, Uruguay, textiles from a Guatemalan village and vintage arrows at a flea market in rural Texas. This inspiration from travel is delightfully apparent in her resort collection, “Paradise Is an Island.” The assortment is a tropical concoction of polychromatic glass beads, winsome brass arrowheads and carnelian tusks, all strung together on gold chains and rope — the perfect colorful addition to an urbanite’s summer wardrobe. Despite each piece having names of exotic vacation hot spots, like “Easter Island Necklace,” the root of the label could not be more down-to-earth. For Fortunato, jewelry has always been a natural outlet. “By the time I was in college, I was fashioning statement necklaces out of industrial bolts and chains, glass beads that a friend toted back from South Africa and family heirloom pieces — literally anything I could get my hands on,” she explains. The results are a fusion of high and low, with thoughtful handiwork and smart combinations of pearls, colorful rope and shapely gold.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Forget Resort Jewels | Lizzie Fortunato', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T20:18:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Madison Shimoda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Madison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shimoda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c129d342-0e18-53e5-a47a-53a5daa6635e', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c129d342-0e18-53e5-a47a-53a5daa6635e'}, {'abstract': 'Williams says its $39-a-share cash bid is simpler and more attractive than Energy Transfer Equity’s all-stock offer.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/williams-makes-4-9-billion-rival-bid-for-southern-union/', 'snippet': 'Williams says its $39-a-share cash bid is simpler and more attractive than Energy Transfer Equity’s all-stock offer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eric Hylden/Grand Forks Herald, via Associated Press Rail cars arrive in Milton, N.D., in 2008 loaded with pipe.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Williams Bids $4.9 Billion in Fight for Southern Union', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pipelines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Energy Transfer Equity LP', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Southern Union Company', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Williams Cos Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T20:27:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 