r': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:31:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sonny Vandevelde', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sonny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vandevelde', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25d40707-ee7a-5832-862e-c51b6e5c5a52', 'word_count': 35, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25d40707-ee7a-5832-862e-c51b6e5c5a52'}, {'abstract': '“Conan O’Brien Can’t Stop” tracks Mr. O’Brien, the talk-show host, on his “Legally Prohibited From Being Funny on Television Tour” of 32 cities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/movies/conan-obrien-cant-stop-review.html', 'snippet': '“Conan O’Brien Can’t Stop” tracks Mr. O’Brien, the talk-show host, on his “Legally Prohibited From Being Funny on Television Tour” of 32 cities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A thin-skinned, hyperkinetic entertainer under stress: that would describe the brash red-headed star, now 48, of Rodman Flender’s tour documentary, “Conan O’Brien Can’t Stop.” As revealed in the film, this high-strung showoff who often behaves like a fourth grader on the verge of a tantrum is a classic example of talent fueled by anger.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/CONAN-span/CONAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/CONAN-span/CONAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/CONAN-span/CONAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 602, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/CONAN-span/CONAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/CONAN-span/CONAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One Ticked-Off Comic, Venting to the Faithful', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Conan O’Brien Can’t Stop'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Ticked-Off Comic, Venting to the Faithful', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Brien, Conan", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flender, Rodman', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:33:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a5dd41c-d913-500a-a96e-c0f93018739f', 'word_count': 765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a5dd41c-d913-500a-a96e-c0f93018739f'}, {'abstract': 'The classical guitarist Milos Karadaglic performed at Le Poisson Rouge on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/arts/music/milos-karadaglic-at-le-poisson-rouge.html', 'snippet': 'The classical guitarist Milos Karadaglic performed at Le Poisson Rouge on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Milos Karadaglic is a 28-year-old guitarist from Montenegro who studied at the Royal Academy of Music in London and is getting an enormous push from Deutsche Grammophon, which released his first CD, “Mediterráneo,” this week. The disc is likable but a bit odd. Its mostly Spanish program includes technique-testing barnstormers, like Albéniz’s “Asturias” and Tárrega’s “Recuerdos de la Alhambra,” alongside simple pieces (Tárrega’s “Lágrima” and “Adelita,” and the anonymous Romance that Narciso Yepes transformed into a theme for “Jeux Inderdits”) that guitar students typically master in their first year on the instrument. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Easy Road', 'kicker': "Music Review | 'Milos Karadaglic'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Easy Road', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Le Poisson Rouge', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:33:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/54c3d039-ae91-5ab7-8efc-9e24d55c87b1', 'word_count': 339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54c3d039-ae91-5ab7-8efc-9e24d55c87b1'}, {'abstract': 'The New Jersey governor shared his excitement about a bill, seemingly set to become law, that will increase the shares paid by employees to the state pension systems.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/christie-calls-to-crow-about-changes-to-pension-system/', 'snippet': 'The New Jersey governor shared his excitement about a bill, seemingly set to become law, that will increase the shares paid by employees to the state pension systems.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It’s an extraordinary day for New Jersey,” Chris Christie boomed proudly when we talked Thursday afternoon. It’s a pretty extraordinary day for New Jersey’s Republican governor, too.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Christie Crows About Changes to Pension System', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:34:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Bai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf6a992a-92c7-54a9-847c-2ee7102676aa', 'word_count': 815, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf6a992a-92c7-54a9-847c-2ee7102676aa'}, {'abstract': 'Janet Evanovich sold an eye-popping 218,000 copies of her new novel, “Smokin’ Seventeen,” in the first day.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/books/sales-are-smokin-for-evanovich-book.html', 'snippet': 'Janet Evanovich sold an eye-popping 218,000 copies of her new novel, “Smokin’ Seventeen,” in the first day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Janet Evanovich sold an eye-popping 218,000 copies of her new novel, “Smokin’ Seventeen,” in the first day it went on sale this week, her publisher, Random House, said on Thursday. About 100,000 hardcovers, 100,000 e-books and 18,000 audio books were sold on Tuesday, the largest first-day sale for Random House this year. Commercial fiction is a strong seller in e-book format, particularly with well-known authors like Ms. Evanovich. She jumped to Bantam Books, part of Random House, last July after contract negotiations fell through with her former publisher, St. Martin’s Press.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sales Are Smokin’ for Evanovich Book', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sales Are Smokin’ For Evanovich Book', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Random House', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:41:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e2ff119-5603-552c-94c7-2e876ea3a3e4', 'word_count': 92, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e2ff119-5603-552c-94c7-2e876ea3a3e4'}, {'abstract': 'A second consecutive night of “The Voice” and “America’s Got Talent” made NBC No. 1 in the ratings on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/arts/television/reality-shows-lift-nbc-in-ratings.html', 'snippet': 'A second consecutive night of “The Voice” and “America’s Got Talent” made NBC No. 1 in the ratings on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A second consecutive night of “The Voice” and “America’s Got Talent” made NBC No. 1 in the ratings on Wednesday night. While prime-time schedules on most of the networks were interrupted from 8 to 8:15 p.m. in the Eastern time zone for President Obama’s address on Afghanistan, Nielsen’s estimates show NBC’s two reality shows far ahead of their competitors. NBC’s elimination episode of “The Voice” attracted approximately 10 million viewers after the speech, while “America’s Got Talent” garnered close to 13 million during the 9 p.m. hour. CBS and Fox tied for second place, with Fox averaging 5.7 million viewers for “So You Think You Can Dance” opposite reruns on CBS. Sitcom repeats couldn’t lift ABC out of fourth place, nor could a 10 p.m. “Primetime Nightline” special on the Casey Anthony murder case (4.8 million).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reality Shows Lift NBC in Ratings', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reality Shows Lift NBC in Ratings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:42:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d560279-3296-5ae4-97db-fea5e893719d', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d560279-3296-5ae4-97db-fea5e893719d'}, {'abstract': 'Rick Batchelder believes he and his wife, Renee, are the only people in the country who live on a tugboat. They live on board about six months a year, on and off, in stints that can last several months.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/living-on-a-tugboat-and-part-of-a-dwindling-breed/', 'snippet': 'Rick Batchelder believes he and his wife, Renee, are the only people in the country who live on a tugboat. They live on board about six months a year, on and off, in stints that can last several months.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Living on a Tugboat, and Part of a Dwindling Breed', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tugboats and Towboats', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chelsea Piers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Block Island (RI)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seventy-Ninth Street Boat Basin (Manhattan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:43:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. J. Hughes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Hughes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9dad44b2-610f-55e5-81d2-efe5572a76e1', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9dad44b2-610f-55e5-81d2-efe5572a76e1'}, {'abstract': 'Savion Glover’s latest show invokes great names like Gregory Hines and Jimmy Slyde.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/arts/dance/savion-glover-at-the-joyce-theater-review.html', 'snippet': 'Savion Glover’s latest show invokes great names like Gregory Hines and Jimmy Slyde.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first time I met Savion Glover was during a rehearsal for an event that Bill Cosby was hosting at Lincoln Center in 2000. The scene onstage was making him tense — meaning that Mr. Glover had no desire to perform a mugging number in the manner of a “Cosby Show” episode — so eventually, without a word, we left. (I interviewed him in his car as we headed downtown.) Mr. Glover’s struggle was obvious: how to present tap, the art form, in a serious way? ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Few Friendly Ghosts Help Out a Master of Tap', 'kicker': "Dance Review | 'Savion Glover'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Few Friendly Ghosts Help Out a Master of Tap', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glover, Savion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Joyce Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:44:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfd881b6-0f4d-52a7-914a-5e1f187aad40', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfd881b6-0f4d-52a7-914a-5e1f187aad40'}, {'abstract': 'The former lieutenant governor of New York as well as other former government officials will examine the hidden debts of states.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/business/economy/24pension.html', 'snippet': 'The former lieutenant governor of New York as well as other former government officials will examine the hidden debts of states.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Americans who have wondered whether Illinois, California and other troubled states are slouching toward the fate of Greece may get their answer in the coming months.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Panel to Scrutinize Causes Behind Weak State Budgets', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Panel to Scrutinize Causes Behind Weak State Budgets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Volcker, Paul A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ravitch, Richard', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:44:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Williams Walsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Williams', 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0c52ceb-ea9f-5dde-aaf4-328303983d04', 'word_count': 836, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0c52ceb-ea9f-5dde-aaf4-328303983d04'}, {'abstract': 'The idea of two cities 375 miles apart dividing the energies of a modern state rankles some Germans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/world/europe/24berlin.html', 'snippet': 'The idea of two cities 375 miles apart dividing the energies of a modern state rankles some Germans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — Just 20 years ago, German lawmakers hunkered down for a passionate 10-hour debate to make a decision that seemed as momentous as it was a no-brainer: Should the capital of the newly reunified country remain where it was — in Bonn on the Rhine — or move back to its historic, eastern location on the Spree, amid the monuments and mixed memories of Berlin?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/world/24berlin1/24berlin1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/world/24berlin1/24berlin1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/24/world/24berlin1/24berlin1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Germany’s Capitals, Cold War Memories and Imperial Ghosts', 'kicker': 'Memo From Berlin', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Germany’s Capitals, Cold War Memories And Imperial Ghosts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bonn (Germany)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:48:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48bc0604-987a-536f-be5f-a1c4a010a008', 'word_count': 1083, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48bc0604-987a-536f-be5f-a1c4a010a008'}, {'abstract': 'In “Turtle,” the loggerhead, a threatened species, embarks on a quarter-century life voyage, a trip that covers thousands of miles and is fraught with life-and-death dangers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/movies/turtle-the-incredible-journey-a-loggerhead-epic-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “Turtle,” the loggerhead, a threatened species, embarks on a quarter-century life voyage, a trip that covers thousands of miles and is fraught with life-and-death dangers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The trailer for “Turtle: The Incredible Journey” promises more excitement than you might suspect could be packed into a story about loggerhead turtles, a plucky seafarer with high mileage. Since it played at the 2009 Toronto International Film Festival, where it was in the documentary lineup, the movie has picked up plenty of goodwill and awards. Last year its director of photography, Rory McGuinness, won a wildlife/nature award from the Australian Cinematographers Society. Given the quality of their work, the special-effects wizards who whipped up some visual wow for the movie, including computer-generated imagery, deserve a share of the applause too.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/TURTLE-span/TURTLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/TURTLE-span/TURTLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/TURTLE-span/TURTLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/TURTLE-span/TURTLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/TURTLE-span/TURTLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Call of the Wild, Heeded With Tenacity', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Turtle'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Call of the Wild, Heeded With Tenacity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Turtles and Tortoises', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T21:49:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', '