print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Blackboard Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Providence Equity Partners Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T22:03:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c65b310f-8f6e-5a0d-b93f-66d701c27ff3', 'word_count': 204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c65b310f-8f6e-5a0d-b93f-66d701c27ff3'}, {'abstract': 'The arches were designed not just as passageways, but as gracious places to sit in the shade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/realestate/the-fate-of-prospect-parks-five-arches.html', 'snippet': 'The arches were designed not just as passageways, but as gracious places to sit in the shade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOT enough for you? Head for Prospect Park in Brooklyn. Not the rich, intoxicating greenery of its 585 acres, but the shady coolness of any of Olmsted & Vaux’s five unique arches designed not as passageways, but as rooms. Taken together they show the sensitive, humanistic possibilities of the city — which its citizens have betrayed. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/realestate/cover-streets/cover-streets-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 883, 'width': 791, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/realestate/cover-streets/cover-streets-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/realestate/cover-streets/cover-streets-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fate of Prospect Park’s Five Arches', 'kicker': 'Streetscapes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Park’s All but Fallen Arches', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vaux, Calvert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Prospect Park (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Olmsted, Frederick Law', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T22:06:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab8465d2-ed45-58c0-ad34-54f19cdf5f82', 'word_count': 877, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab8465d2-ed45-58c0-ad34-54f19cdf5f82'}, {'abstract': 'During the renovation of the Carnegie Mansion, scheduled to be completed in 2013, Cooper-Hewitt’s exhibitions, events and education programs will be staged at various off-site locations.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/cooper-hewitts-exhibition-galleries-to-close-for-two-year-renovation/', 'snippet': 'During the renovation of the Carnegie Mansion, scheduled to be completed in 2013, Cooper-Hewitt’s exhibitions, events and education programs will be staged at various off-site locations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The exhibition galleries at the Smithsonian’s Cooper-Hewitt, National Design Museum will close on July 4 for a two-year renovation and expansion. The project, which starts this fall, is part of a $64 million capital campaign that includes a 60 percent increase in exhibition space, improvements to the National Design Library and an increased endowment. During the renovation of the Carnegie Mansion, scheduled to be completed in 2013, Cooper-Hewitt’s exhibitions, events and education programs will be staged at various off-site locations. The show “Design with the Other 90%: Cities,” for example, opens Oct. 15 at the United Nations and runs through Jan. 9, 2012. Admission to the museum will be free from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. on July 4 to mark the closing of the final exhibition, “Set in Style: The Jewelry of Van Cleef & Arpels.” The museum’s terrace, garden and shop will remain open this summer without an admission fee. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cooper-Hewitt’s Exhibition Galleries to Close for Two-Year Renovation', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cooper-Hewitt National Design Museum', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T22:06:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b4616da-9b8d-5bcd-9a8e-44c6bffaa227', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b4616da-9b8d-5bcd-9a8e-44c6bffaa227'}, {'abstract': 'Ryan Trecartin’s “Any Ever” is a game-changing exhibition of videos and installation art that teeters giddily at the intersection of art, reality television and social networking.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/arts/design/ryan-trecartins-any-ever-at-moma-ps1-review.html', 'snippet': 'Ryan Trecartin’s “Any Ever” is a game-changing exhibition of videos and installation art that teeters giddily at the intersection of art, reality television and social networking.', 'lead_paragraph': "“Am I overexisting or am I over existing? That's my inside joke.” These words — evoking a doubt-free Hamlet unfazed about whether or not to be — bounce forth from the tumult of video, furniture, music, extreme makeup and insistent jabberwocky that form “Any Ever,” Ryan Trecartin’s game-changing exhibition at MoMA PS1 in Long Island City, Queens.", 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/SUB-JP-TRECARTIN/SUB-JP-TRECARTIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/SUB-JP-TRECARTIN/SUB-JP-TRECARTIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/SUB-JP-TRECARTIN/SUB-JP-TRECARTIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/SUB-JP-TRECARTIN/SUB-JP-TRECARTIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/SUB-JP-TRECARTIN/SUB-JP-TRECARTIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Like Living, Only More So', 'kicker': "Art Review | 'Ryan Trecartin'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Like Living, Only More So', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trecartin, Ryan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T22:06:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa89fcf6-539c-5de5-aebd-322cde26d559', 'word_count': 1279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa89fcf6-539c-5de5-aebd-322cde26d559'}, {'abstract': 'Ruth Pennebaker and Robert Leleux stroll New York City, savoring some flavors of Texas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/travel/texans-thrive-in-new-york-city.html', 'snippet': 'Ruth Pennebaker and Robert Leleux stroll New York City, savoring some flavors of Texas.', 'lead_paragraph': '“TEXANS make the best New Yorkers,” Robert Leleux says loudly. “It’s because we’re bred for size. New Yorkers appreciate that — our extravagance. We wouldn’t play so well in Indiana.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/TOWNER-span/TOWNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/TOWNER-span/TOWNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/TOWNER-span/TOWNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur