ks', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Ali', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ali', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d8f615a-da1a-5051-82fb-9fb98a2a0f8e', 'word_count': 2015, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d8f615a-da1a-5051-82fb-9fb98a2a0f8e'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Philharmonic presents a colorful production of Janacek’s “Cunning Little Vixen” at Avery Fisher Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/arts/music/janaceks-cunning-little-vixen-by-new-york-philharmonic-review.html', 'snippet': 'The New York Philharmonic presents a colorful production of Janacek’s “Cunning Little Vixen” at Avery Fisher Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Philharmonic might want to keep some of the tall sunflowers that loom from the back of the stage at Avery Fisher Hall in its enchanting production of Janacek’s anthropomorphic opera, “The Cunning Little Vixen,” which opened on Wednesday night. The hall is rather drab, and the fairy-tale flowers would dress up the place. Also, on nights when Philharmonic concerts are less than inspired, a remnant of Doug Fitch’s lovely Janacek production would remind listeners of one of Alan Gilbert’s boldest artistic ideas. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/CUNNING-span/CUNNING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/CUNNING-span/CUNNING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/CUNNING-span/CUNNING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/CUNNING-span/CUNNING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/CUNNING-span/CUNNING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Impish Creature That Won’t Be Fenced In', 'kicker': 'Music Review | The Cunning Little Vixen', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Impish Creature That Won’t Be Fenced In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Choral Artists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Janacek, Leos', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T22:21:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/07f9f1e0-d12a-5196-865a-f91e76f464f4', 'word_count': 965, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07f9f1e0-d12a-5196-865a-f91e76f464f4'}, {'abstract': 'Monica Ali’s new novel asks: What if Princess Diana had faked her own death and eventually gone to live under an assumed name in America?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-untold-story-by-monica-ali.html', 'snippet': 'Monica Ali’s new novel asks: What if Princess Diana had faked her own death and eventually gone to live under an assumed name in America?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like many women of my generation, I count the 1981 wedding of Diana and Charles as one of my most vivid early memories. I was 5 when it occurred, and I rose at dawn to watch it on a black-and-white television with my family. I was so captivated by the grandeur — the billowing dress with its 25-foot train, the horse-drawn carriage, the kiss on the balcony of Buckingham Palace — that I asked my parents what steps I myself might take in order to marry a prince. It wouldn’t be possible, they informed me, because we weren’t Episcopalians. It seems, in retrospect, strangely evasive of my mother and father to imply that religion was all that stood between me and the British throne, but maybe I was lucky to have my dreams quashed so early. Apparently, the experience of watching that royal wedding at a formative age is now routinely blamed for turning countless little girls into adult Bridezillas.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Sittenfeld/Sittenfeld-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 846, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Sittenfeld/Sittenfeld-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Sittenfeld/Sittenfeld-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Alternative Life for Princess Diana', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chasing Diana', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ali, Monica', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T22:21:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Curtis Sittenfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Curtis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sittenfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cd18c3e-8a64-5be5-af52-082d323043ff', 'word_count': 1213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cd18c3e-8a64-5be5-af52-082d323043ff'}, {'abstract': 'Homeowners at the Four Seasons at Great Notch in Woodland Park say a revaluation of property unfairly redistributes a larger part of the tax burden to them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/realestate/age-restricted-community-protests-tax-increase-in-the-regionnew-jersey.html', 'snippet': 'Homeowners at the Four Seasons at Great Notch in Woodland Park say a revaluation of property unfairly redistributes a larger part of the tax burden to them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WOODLAND PARK', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/realestate/NJZO/NJZO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 732, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/realestate/NJZO/NJZO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/realestate/NJZO/NJZO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Revaluation Rattles Age-Restricted Community', 'kicker': 'In the Region | New Jersey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Revaluation Rattles Community', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hous