lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3813b91-055c-5d9b-b08f-217bf759affb', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3813b91-055c-5d9b-b08f-217bf759affb'}, {'abstract': 'By changing their color from black to white, it can seem as if more police cars are on the streets. Also: A move to make traffic-ticket data more transparent.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/over-time-traffic-cars-are-looking-like-police-cars/', 'snippet': 'By changing their color from black to white, it can seem as if more police cars are on the streets. Also: A move to make traffic-ticket data more transparent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cars driven by the Police Department’s army of civilian traffic enforcement agents have long stood out from the standard patrol cars driven by officers. The traffic agents’ cars were black with white lettering while the patrol cars are all white and affixed with royal-blue decals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Over Time, Traffic Cars Are Looking Like Police Cars', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Alternatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T22:30:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/047622e7-a311-501d-b664-ae08785919b1', 'word_count': 726, 'uri': 'nyt://article/047622e7-a311-501d-b664-ae08785919b1'}, {'abstract': 'The Paris men’s shows could always surprise with a great venue, or staging, or casting that conveyed the spirit of the designs. But Thursday’s shows put out so little energy that they made a case for seeing the clothes in the showroom. Or catching them later online.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/23/paris-mens-dude-surfers/', 'snippet': 'The Paris men’s shows could always surprise with a great venue, or staging, or casting that conveyed the spirit of the designs. But Thursday’s shows put out so little energy that they made a case for seeing the clothes in the showroom. Or catching...', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the exception of Karl Lagerfeld, who treats the Grand Palais like a Hollywood back lot, designers don’t have the will, or the budgets, for big entertainment anymore. The Paris men’s shows could always surprise with a great venue, or staging, or casting that conveyed the spirit of the designs. But Thursday’s shows put out so little energy that they made a case for seeing the clothes in the showroom. Or catching them later online.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Men’s: Dude Surfers', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paris Fashion Week', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T22:32:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt:/