inal': 'By Bloomberg News', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bloomberg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'News', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1785f1ec-7dc1-57cc-80a7-c4aee4c9ca2d', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1785f1ec-7dc1-57cc-80a7-c4aee4c9ca2d'}, {'abstract': 'Among last year’s 30 first-rounders, Taylor Hall of Edmonton at No. 1, Tyler Seguin of Boston (No. 2), Jeff Skinner of Carolina (No. 7), Alexander Burmistrov of Atlanta (No. 8) and Cam Fowler of Anaheim (No. 12) became N.H.L. regulars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/sports/hockey/nhl-teams-look-for-quick-help-in-2011-draft.html', 'snippet': 'Among last year’s 30 first-rounders, Taylor Hall of Edmonton at No. 1, Tyler Seguin of Boston (No. 2), Jeff Skinner of Carolina (No. 7), Alexander Burmistrov of Atlanta (No. 8) and Cam Fowler of Anaheim (No. 12) became N.H.L. regulars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is like the other professional drafts, with teams choosing amateur players who break into wide smiles when their names are called, even if — rather than being from U.C.L.A. or Tennessee — they come from the Peterborough Petes, MODO Hockey Ornskoldsvik or the University of North Dakota. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Drafting Is an Art, Not a Science, but Some Teams Could Get Quick Help', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drafting Is an Art, Not a Science, but Some Teams Could Get Quick Help', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Draft and Recruitment (Sports)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-23T23:24:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66e99f72-4fef-573a-82ca-c40a4a2145b8', 'word_count': 1012, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66e99f72-4fef-573a-82ca-c40a4a2145b8'}, {'abstract': 'Despite Ford’s higher sales this past year, dissatisfaction with new touch screens and high-mileage engines drove down the company’s quality rankings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/business/24ford.html', 'snippet': 'Despite Ford’s higher sales this past year, dissatisfaction with new touch screens and high-mileage engines drove down the company’s quality rankings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — As sales of Ford Motor vehicles took off in the past year, executives pointed to high-tech options as a major selling point. But now the Detroit automaker is learning that advanced technology like touch screens and high-mileage engines can also cause headaches — and a consumer reaction.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/business/Ford/Ford-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 846, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/business/Ford/Ford-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/24/business/Ford/Ford-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ford’s High-Tech Control System Hurts Ranking for Quality', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'prin