istory, June 24, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T07:51:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fa8e90a-6a90-519b-9b6a-a3421e011e4e', 'word_count': 38, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fa8e90a-6a90-519b-9b6a-a3421e011e4e'}, {'abstract': 'How, according to the American ambassador, might the first lady’s trip affect relations between the United States and South Africa?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/the-first-lady-visits-south-africa/', 'snippet': 'How, according to the American ambassador, might the first lady’s trip affect relations between the United States and South Africa?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHO introduced the first lady Michelle Obama at a church in South Africa? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The First Lady Visits South Africa', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T08:59:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5d5b744-60da-5d72-a367-c7943e121528', 'word_count': 88, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5d5b744-60da-5d72-a367-c7943e121528'}, {'abstract': 'When he looked at the flood-damaged Kodachrome slides his father shot years ago, Todd Heisler learned lesson about photography — and about family.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/a-fathers-voice-through-kodachrome/', 'snippet': 'When he looked at the flood-damaged Kodachrome slides his father shot years ago, Todd Heisler learned lesson about photography — and about family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The small, rectangular aluminum cases stored in my mother’s basement had been beckoning me for years. I knew their contents: flood-damaged Kodachrome slides taken by my father, Gordon Heisler. But what I didn’t know, or wasn’t prepared to know until now, 10 years after his death, is that I would find a posthumous lesson in photography and family history.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Father’s Voice, Through Kodachrome', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grandparents', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heisler, Todd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T09:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Todd Heisler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Todd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heisler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f20584f-964e-58ee-9abd-6f3e06540f25', 'word_count': 762, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f20584f-964e-58ee-9abd-6f3e06540f25'}, {'abstract': 'Do you think J. K. Rowling’s Pottermore might convert more readers to e-books and find new young fans for the “Harry Potter” series?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/do-you-read-e-books/', 'snippet': 'Do you think J. K. Rowling’s Pottermore might convert more readers to e-books and find new young fans for the “Harry Potter” series?', 'lead_paragraph': 'J. K. Rowling, the author of the “Harry Potter” series, started a new interactive Web site that will also sell e-books. Do you read e-books? If so, which books have you read digitally, and on what devices? How have you found the e-reading experience? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do You Read E-Books?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T09:35:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/997fd498-bad1-5697-b381-ebb4a0d6e2dc', 'word_count': 330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/997fd498-bad1-5697-b381-ebb4a0d6e2dc'}, {'abstract': 'A 3.3-ton device that bedeviled the troubled Monju prototype fast-breeder reactor for nearly a year was removed on Friday morning.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/world/asia/25japan.html', 'snippet': 'A 3.3-ton device that bedeviled the troubled Monju prototype fast-breeder reactor for nearly a year was removed on Friday morning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — A 3.3-ton device that bedeviled the troubled Monju prototype fast-breeder reactor for nearly a year was removed on Friday morning, Japan’s Atomic Energy Agency said.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Workers Remove Device From Damaged Japanese Reactor', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan: Obstacle Is Removed at Fast-Breeder Reactor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T09:43:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec1bd43c-90d2-58e6-a24b-182e67869a56', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec1bd43c-90d2-58e6-a24b-182e67869a56'}, {'abstract': 'How to manually fire up Windows 7’s System Maintenance troubleshooter.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/tip-of-the-week-cleaning-out-your-windows-pc/', 'snippet': 'How to manually fire up Windows 7’s System Maintenance troubleshooter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Windows 7 has a number of tools like Disk Cleanup to help keep your system running smoothly, and it also includes a System Maintenance troubleshooter. Although the program usually runs itself once a week, you can manually fire it up by going to the Start menu, clicking on Control Panel and typing the word “troubleshooter” into the Search box at the top of the window. Click Troubleshooting and in the System and Security area, click on “Run maintenance tasks.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip of the Week: Cleaning Out Your Windows PC', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T09:43:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/839e8431-535f-5dd8-be4d-64629b9332c0', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/839e8431-535f-5dd8-be4d-64629b9332c0'}, {'abstract': 'Two congressional Republicans walked out of budget negotiations Thursday, putting the future of the talks in limbo.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/the-early-word-no-deal/', 'snippet': 'Two congressional Republicans walked out of budget negotiations Thursday, putting the future of the talks in limbo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From Today’s Times:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: No Deal', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T10:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emmarie Huetteman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emmarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huetteman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85b46bfa-0805-5882-9cc0-62339218d907', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85b46bfa-0805-5882-9cc0-62339218d907'}, {'abstract': 'The M-Class-based S.U.V. is being touted by the Cincinnati-based company as a direct competitor to the coming Tesla Model S.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/driving-the-amp-electric-mercedes-ml-ev/', 'snippet': 'The M-Class-based S.U.V. is being touted by the Cincinnati-based company as a direct competitor to the coming Tesla Model S.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before the showers arrived on Thursday, New York City was the proverbial steam bath, with temperatures in the 70s, the fog ceiling settling at Midtown’s 20th floor and relative humidity at 100 percent. It was a day to crank the A/C in my 2009 Mercedes-Benz M-Class S.U.V. and hope the battery did not die.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Driving the Amp Electric Mercedes ML EV', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mercedes-Benz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tesla Motors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cincinnati (Ohio)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T10:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0cc60b5-98c8-5598-84d3-61b9320a4a60', 'word_count': 743, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0cc60b5-98c8-5598-84d3-61b9320a4a60'}, {'abstract': 'From June 30 to July 5, the annual fair Masterpiece London will bring together 150 top international dealers in the fine and decorative arts.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/london-fair-offers-masterpieces-of-all-sorts/', 'snippet': 'From June 30 to July 5, the annual fair Masterpiece London will bring together 150 top international dealers in the fine and decorative arts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From June 30 to July 5, the annual fair Masterpiece London will bring together 150 top international dealers in the fine and decorative arts. On display at the South Grounds of the Royal Hospital Chelsea (44-20-7499-7470; www.masterpiecefair.com) will be everything from Bugattis and bronze sculptures to Picassos and pink diamonds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Fair Offers Masterpieces of All Sorts', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T10:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5dadb41-db72-512c-b3c8-758a68c65b85', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5dadb41-db72-512c-b3c8-758a68c65b85'}, {'abstract': 'For those contending that the economic recovery has merely hit some bumps on the way to renewed growth, the auto industry may offer evidence.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/cars-as-a-harbinger-of-an-upturn/', 'snippet': 'For those contending that the economic recovery has merely hit some bumps on the way to renewed growth, the auto industry may offer evidence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For those who believe that the economic recovery has merely hit some bumps, and that growth will accelerate during the second half of the year, the automobile industry may offer the most persuasive evidence.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cars as a Harbinger of an Upturn', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernanke, Ben S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T10:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/193134af-76c1-5fac-8531-eb4b09c7aba2', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/193134af-76c1-5fac-8531-eb4b09c7aba2'}, {'abstract': 'The Steelers’ Troy Polamalu tops the list. He’s a future first ballot Hall of Famer.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/top-10-safeties-in-n-f-l/', 'snippet': 'The Steelers’ Troy Polamalu tops the list. He’s a future first ballot Hall of Famer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Friendly Disclaimer: The following list is influenced largely -– but not strictly -– by film study of all 32 teams from the 2010 season. Stats weren’t acknowledged, but players with poor numbers generally don’t make top 10 lists anyway. Vague enough for you? Criteria for top 10 lists tend to be. That’s why most of you will have no trouble finding some disagreement with what you’re about to read. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Top 10 Safeties in N.F.L.', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T10:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41fe59ff-08fd-516d-9a11-8e5d945ed9d6', 'word_count': 403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41fe59ff-08fd-516d-9a11-8e5d945ed9d6'}, {'abstract': 'EQT, the Swedish private equity firm, agreed to sell the alarm system company Securitas Direct to Bain Capital and Hellman & Friedman for up to or $3.4 billion.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/eqt-to-sell-securitas-direct-for-3-4-billion/', 'snippet': 'EQT, the Swedish private equity firm, agreed to sell the alarm system company Securitas Direct to Bain Capital and Hellman & Friedman for up to or $3.4 billion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EQT, the Swedish private equity firm, said late Thursday that it had agreed to sell the alarm system company Securitas Direct to Bain Capital and Hellman & Friedman for up to 21.9 billion Swedish kronor, or $3.4 billion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'EQT to Sell Securitas Direct for $3.4 Billion', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bain Capital', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HELLMAN & FRIEDMAN', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T11:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3295e0b1-f7fd-57ee-9b93-4a4831598d16', 'word_count': 393, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3295e0b1-f7fd-57ee-9b93-4a4831598d16'}, {'abstract': 'For 10 sweat-inducing minutes on Thursday, our reporter chased tennis balls and made baseline-to-baseline throws as part of the mock tryout to be a ball boy at the U.S. Open.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/want-to-be-a-ball-boy-at-the-u-s-open-get-in-line/', 'snippet': 'For 10 sweat-inducing minutes on Thursday, our reporter chased tennis balls and made baseline-to-baseline throws as part of the mock tryout to be a ball boy at the U.S. Open.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Just stay low,” I mumbled to myself. An old baseball coach once told me you look faster that way, and looking fast seemed like half the battle in the competition to be a ball boy at the United States Open. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Want to Be a Ball Boy at the U.S. Open? Get in Line', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Billie Jean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T11:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5573aa04-6c0f-5f47-83aa-90d6f7661c5d', 'word_count': 476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5573aa04-6c0f-5f47-83aa-90d6f7661c5d'}, {'abstract': 'Audrey MacLean says it’s important to get girls interested in computing by first grade — because by fifth grade, it’s game over.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/why-women-have-an-advantage-in-technology/', 'snippet': 'Audrey MacLean says it’s important to get girls interested in computing by first grade — because by fifth grade, it’s game over.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Audrey MacLean describes herself as an accidental technologist, an accidental entrepreneur, and an accidental investor. But at the age of eight, she made at least one plan: She would earn a college scholarship. Ms. MacLean, 59, executed that plan\xa0 and went on to help start Network Equipment Technologies, which went public in 1987. Later, she was a founder and chief executive of Adaptive. She has given seed financing to numerous successful start-ups including Pure Software (founded by Netflix’s Reed Hastings), AdForce and Selectica.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Women Have an Advantage in Technology', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T11:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adriana Gardella', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adriana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gardella', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d37903f7-d2ad-5f5e-b2eb-645e04f40072', 'word_count': 918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d37903f7-d2ad-5f5e-b2eb-645e04f40072'}, {'abstract': 'The collapse of debt limit talks suggests that direct negotiations between President Obama and House Speaker John A. Boehner are likely to come sooner rather than later.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/on-debt-talks-now-its-obama-and-boehner/', 'snippet': 'The collapse of debt limit talks suggests that direct negotiations between President Obama and House Speaker John A. Boehner are likely to come sooner rather than later.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The day after President Obama reached a deal on tax cuts with Republicans last December, reporters asked him about the battle that was looming over raising the nation’s debt ceiling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Debt Talks, Now It’s Obama and Boehner', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Joseph R Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cantor, Eric', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carney, Jay', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T11:16:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55ff271c-1eff-559c-99f1-076666959f8f', 'word_count': 871, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55ff271c-1eff-559c-99f1-076666959f8f'}, {'abstract': 'The resolution to curb funding in Libya reflects growing hostility toward the Obama administration among House Democrats who oppose the war, as well as many Republicans.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/house-takes-up-a-rebuke-to-obamas-libya-policy/', 'snippet': 'The resolution to curb funding in Libya reflects growing hostility toward the Obama administration among House Democrats who oppose the war, as well as many Republicans.', 'lead_paragraph': '2:32 p.m. | Updated  The House of Representatives dealt a symbolic blow to President Obama on Friday by resoundingly failing to approve a bill authorizing the contentious military operations in Libya. But the House stopped short of a total desertion of a war-time president by also rejecting a measure to limit financing to support the military operations there. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'House Deals Obama Symbolic Blow With Libya Votes', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Powers Act (1973)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerry, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCain, John', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T11:32:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01c32c49-bd9c-5194-9d43-e197f22758ac', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01c32c49-bd9c-5194-9d43-e197f22758ac'}, {'abstract': 'Tourism plays into a decision to place a permanent moratorium on commercial fishing of sharks.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/honduras-creates-a-shark-sanctuary/', 'snippet': 'Tourism plays into a decision to place a permanent moratorium on commercial fishing of sharks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a sign that the global movement to protect sharks is picking up steam, Honduras is declaring its waters to be a permanent sanctuary for the fish.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Honduras Creates a Shark Sanctuary', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sharks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snorkeling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lobo Sosa, Porfirio', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinera, Sebastian', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chile', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Honduras', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T12:02:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d199c55-97cb-5d67-8e11-b99ad1ad1a21', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d199c55-97cb-5d67-8e11-b99ad1ad1a21'}, {'abstract': 'The Google Street View car was just asking for trouble — and I was happy to oblige.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/magazine/lives-tailing-the-google-street-car.html', 'snippet': 'The Google Street View car was just asking for trouble — and I was happy to oblige.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the corner of State and Camp, I came upon a Google Street View car at a stop sign. The driver was a young, husky guy with mirrored shades, a faint goatee and a phone pressed to the side of his head. The car was painted in bright colors with the Google logo, and jutting upward from the roof was a small tower on which sat a bright red ball that contained a camera. It looked like a giant penis with an eye. I was on my Vespa, and I pulled over. The car crossed State Street and headed up Camp, toward Audubon Park. California plates. I took out my iPhone, turned on the camera and pursued.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26lives/26lives-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 882, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stalking the Stalkers', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stalking the Stalkers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Orleans (La)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T12:08:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Beller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0070e7b1-f1ab-51a3-8bdb-b6912b9ced02', 'word_count': 782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0070e7b1-f1ab-51a3-8bdb-b6912b9ced02'}, {'abstract': 'A kidney to give.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/magazine/the-ethicist-life-preserver.html', 'snippet': 'A kidney to give.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A few months ago, I signed up to be a living kidney donor to help someone in need who was not related to me. Recently I was told that I was a match for a local 16-year-old. But if I were to enroll in the national kidney registry, my donation could facilitate a donor chain, potentially benefiting 5 or 10 patients. Should I help one person now or several people in the future? It’s hard to say no to a child, yet does the good of the many outweigh the good of the one in this case? BALA AMBATI, SALT LAKE CITY', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life Preserver', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life Preserver', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Kidney Foundation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T12:09:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c3c69cd-7ac6-5e32-9006-101258efe149', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c3c69cd-7ac6-5e32-9006-101258efe149'}, {'abstract': 'Judge Judy gets paid too much to put up with your sass.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/magazine/judge-judy-has-her-day-in-court.html', 'snippet': 'Judge Judy gets paid too much to put up with your sass.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You have five grown children. I shudder to think what would have happened had any arrived home with a tattoo.  Twenty-five-odd years ago, my son Adam got off the bus from college, and he had an earring in his ear. At that time it just wasn’t a thing that young people did, at least in my family. I said you can come home for break, but the earring is going to stay in your pocket. He thought I was kidding, so I left him at the station.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26talk/26talk-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 688, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26talk/mag-26Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26talk/mag-26Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Order! Order in Judge Judy’s Court!', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Order! Order In Her Court!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T12:10:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/24da0097-1feb-56d6-a20c-24597905cd88', 'word_count': 703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24da0097-1feb-56d6-a20c-24597905cd88'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Draghi, 63, will succeed Jean-Claude Trichet of France as Europe’s most powerful central banker, European leaders said Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/business/global/25iht-draghi25.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Draghi, 63, will succeed Jean-Claude Trichet of France as Europe’s most powerful central banker, European leaders said Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRUSSELS — European leaders appointed Mario Draghi on Friday as the next president of the European Central Bank after resolving a dispute between France and Italy over representation on the bank’s executive board. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/Draghi/Draghi-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/Draghi/Draghi-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/25/business/Draghi/Draghi-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mario Draghi to Head Europe’s Central Bank', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mario Draghi of Italy Will Become Next Head of European Central Bank', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Draghi, Mario', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T12:12:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Castle and Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1b2cf39-aad6-57d9-add0-468cb72d677c', 'word_count': 877, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1b2cf39-aad6-57d9-add0-468cb72d677c'}, {'abstract': 'Brit Marling might have been just another starlet. Then she decided to write the roles she wanted to play.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/magazine/how-to-succeed-in-hollywood-despite-being-beautiful.html', 'snippet': 'Brit Marling might have been just another starlet. Then she decided to write the roles she wanted to play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After she graduated at the top of her class from Georgetown University and turned down a job at Goldman Sachs, Brit Marling moved to Los Angeles with her two best male friends to make movies. Because she is beautiful — she hazily evokes a “Manhattan”-era Meryl Streep crossed with a California surfer girl — she found she could easily land auditions for small roles in horror movies and action films. Those didn’t interest her. So she decided to create some roles for herself.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26marling/mag-26Marling-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26marling/26marling-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26marling/26marling-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Succeed in Hollywood Despite Being Really Beautiful', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Succeed in Hollywood Despite Being Really Beautiful', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marling, Brit', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T12:16:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emma Rosenblum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenblum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df80a2f6-1eba-5076-adb1-b8300cb8062c', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df80a2f6-1eba-5076-adb1-b8300cb8062c'}, {'abstract': 'A U.N. court sentenced Rwanda’s former minister for family and women’s affairs and her son to life prison terms for their roles in the 1994 genocide.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/world/africa/25rwanda.html', 'snippet': 'A U.N. court sentenced Rwanda’s former minister for family and women’s affairs and her son to life prison terms for their roles in the 1994 genocide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — A United Nations tribunal on Friday handed a former government minister and her son life imprisonment for their roles in Rwanda’s 1994 genocide, finding them guilty of genocide, war crimes and crimes against humanity, including multiple rapes. The former minister, who had been in charge of family and women’s affairs, is the first woman to be convicted of genocide by an international tribunal.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Life Sentences in Rwanda Genocide Case', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Official Gets Life Sentence For Genocide In Rwanda', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nyiramasuhuko, Pauline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rwanda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T12:17:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marlise Simons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marlise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bdc297e-c519-5eb6-a3f5-2f0717526e17', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bdc297e-c519-5eb6-a3f5-2f0717526e17'}, {'abstract': 'Deciphering peculiar names in baby trends.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/magazine/the-state-of-babies-names-hello-jayden-goodbye-hannah.html', 'snippet': 'Deciphering peculiar names in baby trends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Social Security Administration released its annual database of baby names earlier this year, there were plenty of grabby little details. Isabella and Jacob remained the most popular names for the second straight year — Jacob has been the No. 1 boy’s name since 1999 — while Iker, Crew and Zuri each cracked the top 1,000 for the first time. But a closer look at the database reveals several deeper trends. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26topdown1/26topdown1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 942, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26topdown1/26topdown1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26topdown1/26topdown1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Say Goodnight, Grace (and Julia and Emma, too)', 'kicker': 'Top Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Say Goodnight, Grace (and Julia and Emma, too)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Social Security Administration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Personal', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T12:21:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eaf5d11b-5ccc-5430-ace0-88770e3f4aa4', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eaf5d11b-5ccc-5430-ace0-88770e3f4aa4'}, {'abstract': 'The danger of overprotecting your kids from technology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/magazine/the-wrong-response-to-online-predators.html', 'snippet': 'The danger of overprotecting your kids from technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 1940s and ’50s, kids fell in love with a new technology, and adults freaked out. A psychiatric researcher warned that this latest pastime was “a distinct influencing factor in the case of every single delinquent or disturbed child” he had studied. In 1948, Time magazine said that it would “not only inspire evil but suggest a form for the evil to take.” As David Hajdu relates in his insightful history of the era, “The Ten-Cent Plague,” states and cities introduced more than a hundred laws to ban or limit its sale. Congress held televised hearings to denounce it.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26lede/mag-26lede-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 991, 'width': 666, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'ima