rganization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4b85705-291a-54af-b9c6-b838f69569a0', 'word_count': 763, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4b85705-291a-54af-b9c6-b838f69569a0'}, {'abstract': "On the 90th anniversary of the Communist Party of China, Times reporters visit Nanjiecun, China, perhaps the country's last Maoist collective.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/magazine/100000000876668/the-reddest-village-in-china.html', 'snippet': "On the 90th anniversary of the Communist Party of China, Times reporters visit Nanjiecun, China, perhaps the country's last Maoist collective.", 'lead_paragraph': "On the 90th anniversary of the Communist Party of China, Times reporters visit Nanjiecun, China, perhaps the country's last Maoist collective.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/video-china-maoists/video-china-maoists-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/video-china-maoists/video-china-maoists-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/video-china-maoists/video-china-maoists-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/video-china-maoists/video-china-maoists-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Reddest Village in China', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mao Zedong', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T12:41:40+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Jonah M. Kessel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f43f6b55-3425-561e-8295-9ff5d1f70734', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f43f6b55-3425-561e-8295-9ff5d1f70734'}, {'abstract': 'Mingling with the party’s loyal servants in the reddest town in China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/magazine/in-china-a-place-where-maoism-still-reigns.html', 'snippet': 'Mingling with the party’s loyal servants in the reddest town in China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wang Hongbin, a 60-year-old man with deep creases lining his face, took a break from his duties as village chief this month to show me around Nanjiecun, in the wheat-heavy center of the country. It was unlike any place I’d seen in my years in China. No commercial advertisements cluttered the streets, just billboards screaming proletariat platitudes and memorializing Communist icons. “Long Live the Invincible Mao Zedong Thought,” read the inscription on an archway near the main government building. In the town’s three convenience stores, female clerks wore olive uniforms meant to evoke the People’s Liberation Army. And in the village square, surrounding the gigantic statue of Mao, stood four 30-foot portraits — one of Marx, one of Lenin, one of Engels and one of Stalin. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26here_span/mag-26here-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26here_span/mag-26here-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, '