'lead_paragraph': 'EVERYONE wants to avoid back trouble, but surprisingly few of us manage to escape it. Up to 80 percent of Americans experience back pain at some point in their lives, and each year 15 percent of all adults are treated for such problems as herniated discs, spinal stenosis or lumbar pain.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/Consumer/Consumer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/Consumer/Consumer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/25/business/Consumer/Consumer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sit Up Straight. Your Back Thanks You.', 'kicker': 'The Health Consumer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sit Up Straight. Your Back Thanks You.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Back (Human Body Part)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physical Therapy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T13:50:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lesley Alderman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lesley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alderman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd5fce0f-b71d-5140-8acb-fc74eafd5662', 'word_count': 1198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd5fce0f-b71d-5140-8acb-fc74eafd5662'}, {'abstract': 'After John Carson was hit by a sport utility vehicle while riding his bike, doctors offered little hope for recovery from his paralysis. This weekend, he is competing in an Ironman triathlon to raise money and awareness for spinal cord injury.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/the-last-ironman/', 'snippet': 'After John Carson was hit by a sport utility vehicle while riding his bike, doctors offered little hope for recovery from his paralysis. This weekend, he is competing in an Ironman triathlon to raise money and awareness for spinal cord injury.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Finishing an Ironman triathlon, which consists of a a 2.4-mile swim, a 112-mile bike ride and a marathon, is a remarkable feat for any athlete. But 30-year-old John Carson, who will retire from the sport after this weekend’s Ironman in Coeur d’Alene, Idaho, is no ordinary athlete.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Last Ironman', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marathon Running', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paralysis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Running', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sports Injuries', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Triathlon', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T13:57:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a4059b7-0bc1-50b7-bc22-3171d0f73eb0', 'word_count': 977, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a4059b7-0bc1-50b7-bc22-3171d0f73eb0'}, {'abstract': 'This fall there will be a strikingly broad range of celebrity children’s books written by a strikingly broad range of celebrities. Here’s a preview.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/eager-for-celebrity-sightings-try-the-childrens-books-section/', 'snippet': 'This fall there will be a strikingly broad range of celebrity children’s books written by a strikingly broad range of celebrities. Here’s a preview.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blogger, chef, governor — celebrities can become anything! And while writing or collaborating on grown-up books is a perennially popular option (see Nicole Richie, Lauren Conrad, even Albert Brooks), writing children’s books involves a lot fewer words, and illustrators do at least half the work.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eager for Celebrity Sightings? Try the Children’s Books Section', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Celebrities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T14:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65685a5d-76a1-5aaf-9e1a-3effa2dad703', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65685a5d-76a1-5aaf-9e1a-3effa2dad703'}, {'abstract': 'A new food blog focuses on New York’s literal and figurative tastemakers.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/a-blog-is-born-the-food-life/', 'snippet': 'A new food blog focuses on New York’s literal and figurative tastemakers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fashion industry isn’t necessarily known for its kinship with food. Restaurants, sure, but food — homegrown, cooked or baked — not really. Lauren Levinger and Michael King are trying to change all that with their blog, The Food Life, which will premiere on June 29. With contributors ranging from artists and designers to restaurateurs (including the model and “It” girl Britt Maren and her boyfriend Derek Orell, the chef at the Fat Radish), the site will offer input from New York’s literal and figurative tastemakers. “We want to provide readers with food stories,” Levinger says. “Other blogs have recipes and restaurant reviews, but we wanted to go further.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Blog Is Born | The Food Life', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T14:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily Miesmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miesmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a88c33a-62f4-5c49-9b30-e331f1e8283a', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a88c33a-62f4-5c49-9b30-e331f1e8283a'}, {'abstract': 'Guest blogger Rebecca Hamilton introduces a letter written by a Sudan analyst who recently escaped the Nuba Mountains area in Sudan.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/crisis-in-sudan-allegations-of-ethnic-cleansing-in-the-nuba-mountains/', 'snippet': 'Guest blogger Rebecca Hamilton introduces a letter written by a Sudan analyst who recently escaped the Nuba Mountains area in Sudan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rebecca Hamilton is the author of “Fighting for Darfur.” Her Sudan reporting is supported by the Pulitzer Center.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Crisis in Sudan: Allegations of Ethnic Cleansing in the Nuba Mountains', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T14:02:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By rebecca hamilton', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bb44960-ab8d-5912-8e45-58c0b97db1c7', 'word_count': 1618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bb44960-ab8d-5912-8e45-58c0b97db1c7'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/what-were-reading-211/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A collection of links by the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T14:07:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/33c18ce1-435b-5ce4-80fe-702e102faa8c', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33c18ce1-435b-5ce4-80fe-702e102faa8c'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Webber paid Fernando Alonso a large compliment during the otherwise staid FIA press conference.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/qualifying-and-a-nice-moment-at-the-fia-press-conference/', 'snippet': 'Mark Webber paid Fernando Alonso a large compliment during the otherwise staid FIA press conference.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VALENCIA, Spain – It was business as usual on the track in Valencia on Friday as the cars went out for the two practice sessions. Mark Webber was the fastest driver of the first session, and as I write these words as he is speeding around slightly behind his teammate at Red Bull, Sebastian Vettel, in the second practice session. The Ferrari drivers are third and fourth at the moment, and it’s a little like where we left off in Canada.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Practice, and a Nice Moment at the FIA Press Conference', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T14:10:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4e299f9-6abb-5b7e-9783-7f7af4f3b129', 'word_count': 577, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4e299f9-6abb-5b7e-9783-7f7af4f3b129'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Webber paid Fernando Alonso a large compliment during the otherwise staid FIA press conference.', 'web_url': 'https://formulaone.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/qualifying-and-a-nice-moment-at-the-fia-press-conference/', 'snippet': 'Mark Webber paid Fernando Alonso a large compliment during the otherwise staid FIA press conference.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VALENCIA, Spain – It was business as usual on the track in Valencia on Friday as the cars went out for the two practice sessions. Mark Webber was the fastest driver of the first session, and as I write these words as he is speeding around slightly behind his teammate at Red Bull, Sebastian Vettel, in the second practice session. The Ferrari drivers are third and fourth at the moment, and it’s a little like where we left off in Canada.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Practice, and a Nice Moment at the FIA Press Conference', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T14:10:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecb4414f-56b9-5457-8f36-d99832d77a08', 'word_count': 577, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecb4414f-56b9-5457-8f36-d99832d77a08'}, {'abstract': 'An independent production company has secured Brian Wilson’s life rights for a film about that troubled pop music genius and creative force behind the Beach Boys.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/wouldnt-it-be-nice-brian-wilson-biopic-in-the-works/', 'snippet': 'An independent production company has secured Brian Wilson’s life rights for a film about that troubled pop music genius and creative force behind the Beach Boys.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a project that will hopefully be completed in less time than it took for Brian Wilson to finish “Smile,” an independent production company is preparing a biographical film about Mr. Wilson, the troubled pop music genius and creative force behind the Beach Boys. River Road Entertainment, which has previously produced features like “The Tree of Life” and “Fair Game,” said that it is developing a film about Mr. Wilson, who with his brothers, Dennis and Carl, and Mike Love and Al Jardine formed the Beach Boys in 1961. That band’s groovy hits include “Surfin’ U.S.A.” and “Good Vibrations,” as well as the album-length mini-masterpiece “Pet Sounds.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wouldn’t It Be Nice: Brian Wilson Biopic in the Works', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Brian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beach Boys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T14:11:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7fd021d-3b2c-5eb9-8de3-05e43f5cf1e8', 'word_count': 204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7fd021d-3b2c-5eb9-8de3-05e43f5cf1e8'}, {'abstract': 'Kevin O’Hare, the Royal Ballet’s next artistic director, has a big challenge ahead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/arts/dance/kevin-ohare-royal-ballets-next-artistic-director.html', 'snippet': 'Kevin O’Hare, the Royal Ballet’s next artistic director, has a big challenge ahead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AT a recent New York City Ballet performance I encountered two senior figures of the Royal Ballet’s management, Jeanetta Laurence, its associate director, and Kevin O’Hare, its administrative director. Since I have known both slightly for years, I greeted them, welcoming them to “my” city. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/arts/OHARE-1/OHARE-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 728, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Royal Ballet’s New Chief Must Find His Own R