k': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'California Air Resources Board', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Navy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Santa Barbara (Calif)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T14:53:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leora Broydo Vestel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leora', 'middlename': 'Broydo', 'lastname': 'Vestel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c5412f9-3464-5fda-952c-5776711cd959', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c5412f9-3464-5fda-952c-5776711cd959'}, {'abstract': 'Goalkeeper Karina LeBlanc will be one of Canada’s key players when it plays the opening match against Germany on Sunday in the 2011 Women’s World Cup.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/leblanc-backstops-canada-at-womens-world-cup/', 'snippet': 'Goalkeeper Karina LeBlanc will be one of Canada’s key players when it plays the opening match against Germany on Sunday in the 2011 Women’s World Cup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is not hyperbole to say that Karina LeBlanc has seen all the changes in women’s soccer, specifically in Canada’s team, right in front of her.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'LeBlanc Backstops Canada at Women’s World Cup', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morace, Carolina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T14:58:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0400c9e5-66ad-58f7-8613-5be9c1838b51', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0400c9e5-66ad-58f7-8613-5be9c1838b51'}, {'abstract': 'South of Amalfi lies Cilento, where a dollar goes further and the traffic and tourists are few and far between.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/euro-zone-the-anti-amalfi/', 'snippet': 'South of Amalfi lies Cilento, where a dollar goes further and the traffic and tourists are few and far between.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each summer, the treacherous coastal road in Italy linking picturesque Sorrento with postcardy Positano is jammed with cars and buses bringing tourists through the fragile hill towns of the Amalfi Coast. Long waits for a table and abominable prices on everything from dinky hotel rooms to mediocre pasta are customary. But just south of Amalfi is Cilento National Park, where a dollar goes further and the traffic and tourists are few and far between. The area encompasses dozens of coastal and mountain towns worth stopping for a stroll or an aperitivo. And throughout the summer you’ll find a smattering of cultural events: the London Festival Opera will perform Puccini’s “La Bohème,” “Tosca” and “Madame Butterfly” in Santa Maria di Castellabate, and the nearby temples of Paestum will play host to performances of Medea and the Decameron.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Euro Zone | The Anti-Amalfi', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rocky Casale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rocky', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Casale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d20c9a71-c3b8-58ae-8164-7b17126de264', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d20c9a71-c3b8-58ae-8164-7b17126de264'}, {'abstract': 'During his weekly radio appearance, the mayor said the slow economy would mean steep reductions in governmental services in New York City and beyond for several years to come.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/economic-doldrums-look-persistent-bloomberg-says/', 'snippet': 'During his weekly radio appearance, the mayor said the slow economy would mean steep reductions in governmental services in New York City and beyond for several years to come.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg on Friday said the national economy showed “no signs of really improving,” and he criticized federal officials for failing to come together to address it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Economic Doldrums Look Persistent, Bloomberg Says', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:11:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Javier C. Hernández', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Hernández', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09f85765-a1fa-5bc4-be54-bae7661e3d63', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09f85765-a1fa-5bc4-be54-bae7661e3d63'}, {'abstract': 'A sober, factual account of Americans, naturalized and native-born, who have joined the global jihad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-jihad-joe-by-j-m-berger.html', 'snippet': 'A sober, factual account of Americans, naturalized and native-born, who have joined the global jihad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mention Fort Hood or Times Square, and there’s a fair chance Americans will think not of soldiers in Texas or neon in Manhattan but of terrorism. Since 2009, a flurry of homegrown plots has alarmed counterterrorism officials and prompted a reassessment of the widely accepted notion that American Muslims are especially resistant to the siren call of Al Qaeda.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/SHANE/SHANE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/SHANE/SHANE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/SHANE/SHANE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The American Jihadis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Homeland Opportunity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:20:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Shane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/78e22d90-9fea-5b25-861b-d51191684f5c', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78e22d90-9fea-5b25-861b-d51191684f5c'}, {'abstract': 'With his team playing its best baseball in years, Nationals Manager Jim Riggleman quit.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/jim-riggleman-quits-on-the-nationals/', 'snippet': 'With his team playing its best baseball in years, Nationals Manager Jim Riggleman quit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The other night in Milwaukee, I talked to Prince Fielder. He’s not signed for next season. This is really by choice; Fielder and the Brewers have not agreed on a contract extension. But, still, he’s unsigned. So what’s his approach?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jim Riggleman Quits on the Nationals', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Riggleman, Jim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Nationals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:24:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e83eb7c4-49a1-5e65-86d2-83defd1116b0', 'word_count': 459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e83eb7c4-49a1-5e65-86d2-83defd1116b0'}, {'abstract': 'In a marked change in tone, Democratic lawmakers and gay rights advocates suggested that the Republicans in the State Senate were deliberately delaying a vote.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/frustration-rising-in-albany-with-no-vote-on-marriage/', 'snippet': 'In a marked change in tone, Democratic lawmakers and gay rights advocates suggested that the Republicans in the State Senate were deliberately delaying a vote.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 1:06 p.m. | Frustrations flared in the Capitol on Friday as Democratic lawmakers and gay rights advocates suggested that the Republicans in the State Senate were deliberately delaying a vote on whether to legalize same-sex marriage in New York.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Frustration Rising in Albany With No Vote on Marriage', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:26:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Barbaro, Thomas Kaplan and Nicholas Confessore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Confessore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9be8e8c6-be87-530d-8379-25b5ce7980de', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9be8e8c6-be87-530d-8379-25b5ce7980de'}, {'abstract': 'From graphic photos of face cancer in Thailand to skulls on cigarette packs in Romania, cigarette packs around the world vividly warn of the perils of smoking.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/pictures-to-warn-the-world-about-smoking/', 'snippet': 'From graphic photos of face cancer in Thailand to skulls on cigarette packs in Romania, cigarette packs around the world vividly warn of the perils of smoking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, American health officials released nine graphic warning labels for cigarette packs. The move puts the United States in the company of nearly 40 other countries that have already adopted pictorial warnings on cigarettes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures to Warn the World About Smoking', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labeling and Labels', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoking and Tobacco', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:28:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc1864eb-1c6f-5ac2-9821-2488de6782d3', 'word_count': 95, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc1864eb-1c6f-5ac2-9821-2488de6782d3'}, {'abstract': 'Two political action committees formed to raise and spend unlimited amounts independently of candidates reported raising more than $4.6 million since the beginning of the year.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/super-pacs-report-strong-early-fund-raising-numbers/', 'snippet': 'Two political action committees formed to raise and spend unlimited amounts independently of candidates reported raising more than $4.6 million since the beginning of the year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two political action committees formed to raise and spend unlimited amounts independent of candidates reported raising more than $4.6 million since the beginning of the year, according to filings with the Federal Election Commission.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Super PACs Report Strong Early Fund-Raising Numbers', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corwin, Jane L', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eychaner, Fred', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soros, George', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sussman, S Donald', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Crossroads', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citizens United', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Election Commission', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:28:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Derek Willis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Derek', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Willis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79c521d0-2dd1-53be-b7fb-e7de03d6aaf9', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79c521d0-2dd1-53be-b7fb-e7de03d6aaf9'}, {'abstract': 'Harold Grinspoon, a western Massachusetts real estate mogul, took a cue from Dolly Parton and started the PJ Library, which sends books with Jewish content to interested families.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/us/25beliefs.html', 'snippet': 'Harold Grinspoon, a western Massachusetts real estate mogul, took a cue from Dolly Parton and started the PJ Library, which sends books with Jewish content to interested families.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONGMEADOW, Mass. — In 2004, Harold Grinspoon, a local real estate mogul, heard a story on National Public Radio about the Imagination Library, founded by the singer Dolly Parton to give free books to children in her native rural Tennessee (and which now gives books around the country, in Canada and in England). Mr. Grinspoon, who has long given millions of dollars to Jewish summer camps and to schools and synagogues here in western Massachusetts, had an idea: do what Dolly does, with a Jewish twist.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/us/BELIEFS-1/BELIEFS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/25/us/BELIEFS-1/BELIEFS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/us/BELIEFS-1/BELIEFS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/us/BELIEFS-1/BELIEFS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/25/us/BELIEFS-1/BELIEFS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Aiming to Spread Judaism One Book at a Time', 'kicker': 'Beliefs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aiming to Spread Judaism One Book at a Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:31:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Oppenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oppenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d119746-375a-5244-a5f2-14df47086942', 'word_count': 1017, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d119746-375a-5244-a5f2-14df47086942'}, {'abstract': 'Alexandra Robbins argues that many of the traits attributed to “losers” in high school contribute to success later in life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-the-geeks-shall-inherit-the-earth-by-alexandra-robbins.html', 'snippet': 'Alexandra Robbins argues that many of the traits attributed to “losers” in high school contribute to success later in life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the modern adult, nothing signifies self-confidence, street credibility and authenticity like one simple confession: “I was such a nerd in high school.” (Emphasize “such” with a dramatic sigh. Then roll your eyes.) It’s pretty easy now to gaze upon the past with rose-colored — and thick-framed — glasses. It’s popular to have been unpopular. A new world order has unfolded, with nerds ascendant. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/BRUDER/BRUDER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 906, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/BRUDER/BRUDER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/BRUDER/BRUDER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Nerds Succeed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Most Likely to Succeed, Someday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robbins, Alexandra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:31:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Bruder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/294ab44c-342f-5c96-9960-75ce7328bfe5', 'word_count': 1152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/294ab44c-342f-5c96-9960-75ce7328bfe5'}, {'abstract': 'Is he a clever moderate, or a lousy negotiator?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/the-presidents-debt-ceiling-strategy/', 'snippet': 'Is he a clever moderate, or a lousy negotiator?', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s been a lot of discussion on liberal blogs (see Matt Yglesias, Jonathan Chait, Kevin Drum, Greg Sargent, and Chait again) about whether President Obama’s decision to go along with the Republican insistence on tying a deficit reduction package to the debt ceiling vote constitutes a blunder or a deliberate strategy — whether he’s a “bad negotiator” who’s let the Republicans hold him (and the American economy) hostage to their demands for spending cuts, or a “clever moderate” who’s using the debt ceiling to force his own base to live with painful deficit choices.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The President’s Debt-Ceiling Strategy', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:33:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72cf746c-feec-5932-96dc-6bab35e875e7', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72cf746c-feec-5932-96dc-6bab35e875e7'}, {'abstract': 'What a quaint, imminently obsolete, inherently limited almanac can teach us about handling the crush of information on the Internet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/magazine/an-accidental-experimental-masterpiece.html', 'snippet': 'What a quaint, imminently obsolete, inherently limited almanac can teach us about handling the crush of information on the Internet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was walking, recently, with my physical body through a physical bookstore when — somewhere between the giant display of e-readers, the Blu-ray wing, the Wi-Fi cafe and the looming tower of interactive language software — I stumbled across an actual, physical book that stopped me cold.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26riff_span/26riff_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 98, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26riff_span/26riff_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 98}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26riff_span/26riff_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 209, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26riff_span/26riff_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/magazine/26riff_span/26riff_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘An Accidental, Experimental Masterpiece’', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘An Accidental, Experimental Masterpiece’', 'name': None, 'seo':