y and New Media at George Mason University has unveiled PressForward, a new open access platform for online scholarship.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/a-digital-helping-hand-for-scholarly-work/', 'snippet': 'The Roy Rosenzweig Center for History and New Media at George Mason University has unveiled PressForward, a new open access platform for online scholarship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Digitally savvy academics have been struggling to reconcile new media with traditional scholarly publishing, which is painfully slow and generally locked behind paywalls or unavailable online. One answer has been offered by the Roy Rosenzweig Center for History and New Media at George Mason University, which on Wednesday unveiled PressForward, a new open access platform for online scholarship.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Digital Helping Hand for Scholarly Work', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scholarships and Fellowships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'George Mason University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:44:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b204d0da-1778-58cb-aa1d-280ef609b6f2', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b204d0da-1778-58cb-aa1d-280ef609b6f2'}, {'abstract': 'Broadway stars like Sutton Foster, Patti LuPone and Victoria Clark depend on their dressers to get them through more than costume changes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/theater/sutton-foster-and-patti-lupone-and-their-dressers.html', 'snippet': 'Broadway stars like Sutton Foster, Patti LuPone and Victoria Clark depend on their dressers to get them through more than costume changes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Sutton Foster won the Tony Award for best actress this month for her performance in “Anything Goes,” she began her acceptance speech by thanking the usual suspects: co-stars, teachers, director, boyfriend. It wasn’t until she thanked her dresser, though, that Ms. Foster’s composure broke, and the waterworks started.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/25dresser/25dresser-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/25dresser/25dresser-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/25dresser/25dresser-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 746, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/25dresser/25dresser-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/25dresser/25dresser-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s All About Holding Hands and Stitching Hems', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s All About Holding Hands and Stitching Hems', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Victoria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foster, Sutton', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Costumes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LuPone, Patti', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:50:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b6ef440-1ca0-586c-8e6b-4a4a0664a40d', 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b6ef440-1ca0-586c-8e6b-4a4a0664a40d'}, {'abstract': 'The Go Daddy Group is near a deal to sell itself to a group led by Kohlberg Kravis Roberts and Silver Lake for $2 billion to $2.5 billion.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/go-daddy-nears-sale-to-k-k-r-and-silver-lake/', 'snippet': 'The Go Daddy Group is near a deal to sell itself to a group led by Kohlberg Kravis Roberts and Silver Lake for $2 billion to $2.5 billion.', 'lead_paragraph': '6:26 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Go Daddy Expected to Be Sold to Buyout Firms', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Go Daddy Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kohlberg Kravis Roberts & Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Silver Lake Partners', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:52:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38d0dfff-3dbd-55ea-91f3-9f8d393eb4c1', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38d0dfff-3dbd-55ea-91f3-9f8d393eb4c1'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Lipsyte looks back at his writing career, and considers the evolving cultural significance of sports in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-an-accidental-sportswriter-by-robert-lipsyte.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Lipsyte looks back at his writing career, and considers the evolving cultural significance of sports in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 19-year-old aspiring novelist and native New Yorker just out of Columbia University, Robert Lipsyte had never bought a copy of The New York Times until he decided to look in the classifieds for a summer job before graduate school. He found one at the paper itself, as a copy boy in the sports department in 1957, though he had little interest or experience in sports. In junior high, he was a self-\xaddescribed nerd and “became a particular target of the bullies because I compulsively talked back and was too fat to run away afterward.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Jennings/Jennings-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Jennings/Jennings-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Jennings/Jennings-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 707, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Jennings/Jennings-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Jennings/Jennings-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert Lipsyte: Skeptic in the Press Box', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Skeptic in the Press Box', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lipsyte, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T15:54:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Jennings', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jennings', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dbcf7f2-93e7-550e-a7b0-59e2ee55f4ec', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dbcf7f2-93e7-550e-a7b0-59e2ee55f4ec'}, {'abstract': 'The wild-card candidates, including Sarah Palin and Jon M. Huntsman Jr., have an outside chance of winning the nomination if things break the right way.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/handicapping-the-republican-field-part-ii-the-wild-cards/', 'snippet': 'The wild-card candidates, including Sarah Palin and Jon M. Huntsman Jr., have an outside chance of winning the nomination if things break the right way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I mentioned last week that I’d thought we’d seen the emergence of a relatively distinct “top tier” of Republican candidates, consisting of Mitt Romney, Tim Pawlenty, Rick Perry and Michele Bachmann. Having taken a deeper look, I am more confident in that claim — I’d posit that there is a better than an 80 percent chance that the nominee will be one of those four people.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Handicapping the Republican Field: Part II, the Wild Cards', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:02:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e9864e2-e334-5883-84f3-8d405e69d9f3', 'word_count': 2183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e9864e2-e334-5883-84f3-8d405e69d9f3'}, {'abstract': 'A guide to the complex landscape of Pakistan from a veteran foreign correspondent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-pakistan-by-anatol-lieven.html', 'snippet': 'A guide to the complex landscape of Pakistan from a veteran foreign correspondent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the regime of Gen. Pervez Musharraf, it became the policy of the United States to help spread Sufi Islam in Pakistan. A Sufi council was formed and a few seminars and some musical concerts were held; a Sufi University is still being worked on. Meanwhile, Sufi shrines have been attacked all across Pakistan. Many Pakistanis now believe Sufis to be the allies of American intelligence operatives, a belief only strengthened by the recent Raymond Davis affair, in which a Central Intelligence Agency contractor was detained in the killing of two Pakistanis. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/HANIF/HANIF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/HANIF/HANIF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/HANIF/HANIF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/HANIF/HANIF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/HANIF/HANIF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Primer on Pakistan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Survival State', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lieven, Anatol', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:04:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohammed Hanif', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohammed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hanif', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bde79317-4670-5528-9c7e-a7f3ff2bfd88', 'word_count': 814, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bde79317-4670-5528-9c7e-a7f3ff2bfd88'}, {'abstract': 'The private-equity-owned company agrees to acquire National Geographic’s digital and print school publishing unit.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/cengage-in-national-geographic-deal/', 'snippet': 'The private-equity-owned company agrees to acquire National Geographic’s digital and print school publishing unit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cengage Learning, a private-equity-backed education company, agreed on Friday to acquire National Geographic’s digital and print school publishing unit. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cengage in National Geographic Deal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Geographic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:05:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf2c8808-a054-5173-91bb-3354d3b0ab99', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf2c8808-a054-5173-91bb-3354d3b0ab99'}, {'abstract': 'Have you ever felt a doctor was steering an elderly family member toward a test or procedure that just didn’t seem necessary?', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/the-story-of-the-mole/', 'snippet': 'Have you ever felt a doctor was steering an elderly family member toward a test or procedure that just didn’t seem necessary?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A very nice dermatologist called me about the small growth on my father’s ear, which he had biopsied. He left a phone message asking me to call him at home (over a holiday weekend! I liked this guy already). His message said that “more removal needs to be done.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Story of the Mole', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:06:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/728c4827-e930-5fc8-bd56-928ef4a38e93', 'word_count': 329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/728c4827-e930-5fc8-bd56-928ef4a38e93'}, {'abstract': 'Jorge G. Castañeda assesses the contradictions that shape and afflict Mexico.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-manana-forever-by-jorge-g-castaneda.html', 'snippet': 'Jorge G. Castañeda assesses the contradictions that shape and afflict Mexico.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jorge G. Castañeda was long involved in efforts to end the 70-year dictatorial reign of the Institutional Revolutionary Party (PRI) over Mexico. In 1988, he supported the presidential ambitions of Cuauhtémoc Cárdenas, who probably won the election that was officially called for the PRI candidate Carlos Salinas de Gortari. Cárdenas decided to contest local races across Mexico, and in 1989 Castañeda traveled to the town of Tepoztlán as an election observer. As he recounts in “Mañana Forever?” it became obvious that the PRI was tampering with the ballots in this election as well, and Castañeda urged the local cardenistas to put up a fight.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Campaigning for Change in Mexico', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Campaigning for Change', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castaneda, Jorge G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:09:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Starr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Starr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/352ffee7-6e4d-5462-981a-343be8c7ddc3', 'word_count': 912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/352ffee7-6e4d-5462-981a-343be8c7ddc3'}, {'abstract': 'Citing the uncertainty of the N.F.L. lockout, the Jets said they would hold their training camp this year in Florham Park, N.J., and not at SUNY Cortland, where it has been held the past two years.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/jets-will-hold-training-camp-in-new-jersey/', 'snippet': 'Citing the uncertainty of the N.F.L. lockout, the Jets said they would hold their training camp this year in Florham Park, N.J., and not at SUNY Cortland, where it has been held the past two years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Citing the uncertainty of the N.F.L. lockout, the Jets said they would hold their training camp this year in Florham Park, N.J., and not at SUNY Cortland, where it has been held the past two years. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jets Will Hold Training Camp in New Jersey', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:10:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/649a71d4-bcd5-590f-b40f-ca439c7f4532', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/649a71d4-bcd5-590f-b40f-ca439c7f4532'}, {'abstract': 'The Book Review inaugurates a new column, named in homage to the critic Hugh Kenner, that will examine the intersections of technology and literature.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/up-front-introducing-the-mechanic-muse.html', 'snippet': 'The Book Review inaugurates a new column, named in homage to the critic Hugh Kenner, that will examine the intersections of technology and literature.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The literary critic Hugh Kenner, who died in 2003, is best known for his intimate studies of Ezra Pound, T. S. Eliot, James Joyce and their contemporaries. Unlike many advocates of these authors, Kenner was also a technology enthusiast; he celebrated the Wright brothers’ flight as the first great act of American modernism and even attempted to translate a passage of Samuel Beckett into the Pascal programming language.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 999, 'width': 641, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Introducing The Mechanic Muse', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kenner, Hugh', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:12:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f21b911-73ad-5f3b-9084-bdaa22302956', 'word_count': 284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f21b911-73ad-5f3b-9084-bdaa22302956'}, {'abstract': 'To uncover the true nature of literature, a scholar says, don’t read the books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/the-mechanic-muse-what-is-distant-reading.html', 'snippet': 'To uncover the true nature of literature, a scholar says, don’t read the books.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Ars longa,” the ancient saying goes, “vita brevis.” Art is long, life short, and the problem is intensifying. As the literary ars lurches exponentially more longa — accommodating the printing press, “Gravity’s Rainbow,” Google Books — our collective TBR pile towers ever more vertiginously overhead. Which raises a question: What are we mortal beings supposed to do with all these books?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/bkr-Schulz-t_CA0/bkr-Schulz-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 296, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/bkr-Schulz-t_CA0/bkr-Schulz-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 296}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/bkr-Schulz-t_CA0/bkr-Schulz-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 504, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/bkr-Schulz-t_CA0/bkr-Schulz-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/bkr-Schulz-t_CA0/bkr-Schulz-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Is Distant Reading?', 'kicker': 'The Mechanic Muse', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Distant Reading', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:12:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Schulz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/631e21e3-92ad-54c0-b68e-e3772b09ca8e', 'word_count': 1185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/631e21e3-92ad-54c0-b68e-e3772b09ca8e'}, {'abstract': 'Filmed for the Communist Party’s 90th anniversary in China, “Beginning of the Great Revival” has battles but little blood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/movies/beginning-of-the-great-revival-review.html', 'snippet': 'Filmed for the Communist Party’s 90th anniversary in China, “Beginning of the Great Revival” has battles but little blood.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Beginning of the Great Revival” (called “The Founding of the Party” in China) — a companion piece to “The Founding of a Republic” from 2009 — is a lavish, sprawling mosaic, spanning Chinese history from 1911 to 1921 and concluding with the establishment of the Chinese Communist Party. (Filmed for the party’s 90th anniversary, “Revival” had a colossal release at home; the State Film Commission sought at least 30 million viewers.) Its principal focus is the May Fourth Movement, which sprang from failed government efforts to wrest Shandong Province from foreign control at the Paris Peace Conference of 1919, resulting in student riots in Beijing.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/BEGINNING-span/ROUNDUP---BEGINNING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/BEGINNING-span/ROUNDUP---BEGINNING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 