', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:13:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/391ca628-0b4d-5e08-9389-ec789b9a61a8', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/391ca628-0b4d-5e08-9389-ec789b9a61a8'}, {'abstract': 'As journalism in the Internet age starts to seem more and more like a remix of “Blow-Up,” reporters have discovered video shot during a riot in Vancouver last week that shows exactly what happened just before a news photographer snapped a beguiling image of a man kissing a woman.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/overlooked-vancouver-video-shows-kissing-couple-was-knocked-down-by-riot-police/', 'snippet': 'As journalism in the Internet age starts to seem more and more like a remix of “Blow-Up,” reporters have discovered video shot during a riot in Vancouver last week that shows exactly what happened just before a news photographer snapped a beguilin...', 'lead_paragraph': 'As journalism in the Internet age starts to seem more and more like a remix of the 1966 film “Blow-Up,” reporters have discovered video shot during a riot in Vancouver, British Columbia, last week that shows exactly what happened just before a news photographer snapped a beguiling image of a man kissing a woman in the middle of a street filled with riot police officers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Overlooked Vancouver Video Shows ‘Kissing Couple’ Was Knocked Down by Riot Police', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:17:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/976011c4-fce9-5f4c-8cb0-8ca422cec1dd', 'word_count': 413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/976011c4-fce9-5f4c-8cb0-8ca422cec1dd'}, {'abstract': 'In this “documentary novel,” a Romanian pharmacist collaborates with the Nazis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-the-druggist-of-auschwitz-by-dieter-schlesak.html', 'snippet': 'In this “documentary novel,” a Romanian pharmacist collaborates with the Nazis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If memory serves, the great Italian poet and novelist Cesare Pavese once made the Archimedean claim that, given a place to stand and a pivot, he could write about anything. The mediocre German-Romanian poet and novelist Dieter Schlesak (may Pavese’s ghost forgive me for mentioning him in the same paragraph) makes the same point ex negativo. To wit, with nowhere to stand and no pivot, he can make nothing happen. “The Druggist of Auschwitz,” his so-called documentary novel, is neither document nor novel. It is that depressing thing, a very bad book on a very terrible subject.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Hofmann/Hofmann-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 927, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Hofmann/Hofmann-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Hofmann/Hofmann-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Death Camp Pharmacist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prescription for Evil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Auschwitz (Poland)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concentration Camps', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:18:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Hofmann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hofmann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/89a42a12-d06d-5a72-9f10-667e948fca3e', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89a42a12-d06d-5a72-9f10-667e948fca3e'}, {'abstract': 'Even when the reasons for its initial adoption are utilitarian, a pen name can assume a life of its own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/essay-the-rise-and-fall-of-pseudonyms.html', 'snippet': 'Even when the reasons for its initial adoption are utilitarian, a pen name can assume a life of its own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the venerable tradition of the pseudonym is discussed, it is often in reductive terms. The other day, someone said to me, “There are three reasons why authors use pen names, right?” and went on to cite them: Women writing as men. Writers with dirty secrets to hide. Highbrow writers slumming it in trashy genres. It’s true that each of those motives is historically common, but there are many others.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Ciurara/Ciurara-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 847, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Ciurara/Ciurara-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Ciurara/Ciurara-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rise and Fall of Pseudonyms', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Secret Sharers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pessoa, Fernando', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Literary Fraud', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:20:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carmela Ciuraru', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carmela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ciuraru', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5f92c33-e51a-5fb6-8c40-67ff96572265', 'word_count': 1097, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5f92c33-e51a-5fb6-8c40-67ff96572265'}, {'abstract': 'Tom Clancy is back at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list with his latest thriller, “Against All Enemies.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Tom Clancy is back at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list with his latest thriller, “Against All Enemies.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOT WAR: Tom Clancy is back at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list with his latest thriller, written with Peter Telep. “Against All Enemies” — no relation to the counterterrorism expert Richard Clarke’s 2004 nonfiction best seller of the same name — recounts the exploits of a former Navy Seals member and C.I.A. paramilitary officer named Maxwell Moore, who survives an attack on an American ship off the coast of Pakistan only to find himself doing battle with the Taliban on the United States/Mexican border.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/03insidelist/03insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/books/review/03insidelist/03insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:20:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65f2a214-449d-574f-9383-112cc236c445', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65f2a214-449d-574f-9383-112cc236c445'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/EdChoice-t.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TEN THOUSAND SAINTS,  by Eleanor Henderson (Ecco/HarperCollins, $26.99.) Henderson’s fierce, elegiac novel follows a group of friends, lovers, parents and children through the straight-edge music scene and the early days of the AIDS epidemic.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent books of particular interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:20:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/e27f2029-514b-5e1a-bb69-8791e8a3ec7b', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e27f2029-514b-5e1a-bb69-8791e8a3ec7b'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YOUR FACE TOMORROW: Volume Three — Poison, Shadow and Farewell,  by Javier Marías. Translated by Margaret Jull Costa (New Directions, $16.95.) In the final volume of Marías’s disquieting novel, his narrator — hired by MI6 in London as an “expert translator or interpreter of people” — peers into the territory of torture as he returns home to Madrid to both spy on and protect his own family.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:20:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/0012930b-160d-5f0a-bd85-bb2db1873058', 'word_count': 513, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0012930b-160d-5f0a-bd85-bb2db1873058'}, {'abstract': 'In the fourth of Ursula Hegi’s novels to be set in the same German village, a schoolteacher heroine, seduced by Nazi propaganda, struggles to follow her moral compass.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-children-and-fire-by-ursula-hegi.html', 'snippet': 'In the fourth of Ursula Hegi’s novels to be set in the same German village, a schoolteacher heroine, seduced by Nazi propaganda, struggles to follow her moral compass.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All novelists are godlike. Sovereign creators of worlds they populate with beings wrought from something less than dust and rib, they set events in motion and determine their consequences. The situation is less ideal than it sounds: omnipotence can be a dreary limitation. That’s why the best novelists are also childlike. Bent over impalpable dollhouses, moving their lips while they rearrange the furniture and figures, they give themselves over to such deep play that their stories read less like a premeditated imposition than obedience to the whispered suggestions of the universe.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Cohen/Cohen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 670, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Cohen/Cohen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Cohen/Cohen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Testing the Conscience of a Village Under the Nazis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'And We Did Not Speak Out', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hegi, Ursula', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:21:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leah Hager Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leah', 'middlename': 'Hager', 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/795666d4-84e6-58f3-befb-b655dd817fb3', 'word_count': 1127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/795666d4-84e6-58f3-befb-b655dd817fb3'}, {'abstract': 'Always a Paris highlight, Dries Van Noten delivered a beautiful, carefully considered show.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/paris-report-dries-van-noten/', 'snippet': 'Always a Paris highlight, Dries Van Noten delivered a beautiful, carefully considered show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Always a Paris highlight, Dries Van Noten delivered a beautiful, carefully considered show. Cabana stripes turned on their side in airy nylon trenches, blousons, pants and wide-legged shorts worn over loose leggings gave an elegance to the athletic inspiration we’ve been seeing this show season. But it was the beautiful proportions of the elongated, trim double-breasted jacket with the loose shorts and leggings that took the prize.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Top 5 | Dries Van Noten', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:21:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Pask', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pask', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/968ba39d-1902-5665-ac60-6369b95bff8e', 'word_count': 72, 'uri': 'nyt://article/968ba39d-1902-5665-ac60-6369b95bff8e'}, {'abstract': 'An account of the writing, reception and modern reputation of Harriet Beecher Stowe’s novel “Uncle Tom’s Cabin,” which taught whites to see slaves as human.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-mightier-than-the-sword-by-david-s-reynolds.html', 'snippet': 'An account of the writing, reception and modern reputation of Harriet Beecher Stowe’s novel “Uncle Tom’s Cabin,” which taught whites to see slaves as human.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No less an authority than Leo Tolstoy included “Uncle Tom’s Cabin” on his short list of “examples of the highest art flowing from love of God and man.” Fifty years later, James Baldwin called the same book a “very bad novel” full of “excessive and spurious emotion.” What goes on here? The question belongs in the present tense because it is by no means settled.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/DelBlanco/DelBlanco-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 752, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/DelBlanco/DelBlanco-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/DelBlanco/DelBlanco-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Impact of ‘Uncle Tom’s Cabin’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "'This Curse of Slavery'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reynolds, David S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stowe, Harriet Beecher', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:21:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Delbanco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Delbanco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e5e21c8-db49-5367-8a0a-584a2e6b58ec', 'word_count': 1230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e5e21c8-db49-5367-8a0a-584a2e6b58ec'}, {'abstract': 'Dorothy Wickenden tells the story of her grandmother’s stint as a teacher on the frontier.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-nothing-daunted-by-dorothy-wickenden.html', 'snippet': 'Dorothy Wickenden tells the story of her grandmother’s stint as a teacher on the frontier.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the spring of 1916, two society girls from Auburn, N.Y. — Smith graduates who, having already done a European tour, were iffy about charity work but picky about husbands — found by chance the answer to the question of what to do with themselves. A lady who’d come for tea mentioned a friend who had a brother, a Princeton man, who was looking for two college-educated women to teach at a schoolhouse he’d built with his neighbors in the Elkhead Mountains of Colorado. Instantly, both knew they wanted to go. One of them, Dorothy Woodruff, was the grandmother of Dorothy Wickenden. And in “Nothing Daunted,” Wickenden has painstakingly recreated the story of how that earlier Dorothy and her friend Rosamond Underwood embarked on a brief but life-changing adventure, teaching the children of struggling homesteaders. Mining a trove of letters as well as oral histories and period documents, including an autobiography published by their employer, Farrington (Ferry) Carpenter, Wickenden lets their tale of personal transformation open out to reveal the larger changes in the rough-and-tumble society of the West — “a back story,” as she aptly puts it, “to America’s leap into the 20th century.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/RUSSO/RUSSO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/RUSSO/RUSSO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/RUSSO/RUSSO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/RUSSO/RUSSO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/RUSSO/RUSSO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Frontier Schoolhouse Story', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reading, Riding and Arithmetic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:22:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8e2ac4d-b62c-5b44-b69a-0ec7f3a39f5e', 'word_count': 1150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8e2ac4d-b62c-5b44-b69a-0ec7f3a39f5e'}, {'abstract': 'Melena Ryzik goes to Brooklyn Bowl to catch Deer Tick, an alt-countryesque indie rock band, perform as Deervana, a Nirvana tribute band.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/arts/music/100000000880012/deer-tick-as-deervana.html', 'snippet': 'Melena Ryzik goes to Brooklyn Bowl to catch Deer Tick, an alt-countryesque indie rock band, perform as Deervana, a Nirvana tribute band.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Melena Ryzik goes to Brooklyn Bowl to catch Deer Tick, an alt-countryesque indie rock band, perform as Deervana, a Nirvana tribute band.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/video-deertick/video-deertick-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/video-deertick/video-deertick-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/video-deertick/video-deertick-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/24/arts/video-deertick/video-deertick-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Deer Tick as Deervana', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:22:44+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Gabe Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gabe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/dd434865-4f0d-5596-b208-050e0f85e97e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/dd434865-4f0d-5596-b208-050e0f85e97e'}, {'abstract': 'A combination of stunning architecture and wonderful food and wine, less than two hours from the capital.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/travel/from-lisbon-a-visit-to-evora-overnighter.html', 'snippet': 'A combination of stunning architecture and wonderful food and wine, less than two hours from the capital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANY day trips and overnight stays — especially to outlying towns and cities in southern Europe where weekend hours for shops and tourist sites can seem almost whimsical — often do little more than whet one’s appetite to return for a proper visit. But, at less than two hours by car from Lisbon, an overnight visit to the storied Portuguese city of Évora and the surrounding area packs a lot into relatively little time.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/travel/Evora-1/Evora-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/travel/Evora-1/Evora-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/travel/Evora-1/Evora-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/travel/Evora-1/Evora-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/travel/Evora-1/Evora-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Lisbon, Visiting the Storied City of Évora', 'kicker': 'Overnighter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Lisbon, Visiting the Storied City of Évora', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Evora (Portugal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lisbon (Portugal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:22:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Ferren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ferren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49da6532-8cef-5e24-b96f-66ea8f379d42', 'word_count': 1449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49da6532-8cef-5e24-b96f-66ea8f379d42'}, {'abstract': 'An overnight visit to a storied Portuguese city packs a lot into relatively little time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/26/travel/20110626-evora.html', 'snippet': 'An overnight visit to a storied Portuguese city packs a lot into relatively little time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An overnight visit to a storied Portuguese city packs a lot into relatively little time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/travel/06262011EVORA-slide-UCCW/06262011EVORA-slide-UCCW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/travel/06262011EVORA-slide-UCCW/06262011EVORA-slide-UCCW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/travel/06262011EVORA-slide-UCCW/06262011EVORA-slide-UCCW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploring Évora', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Portugal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:23:02+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ed001ea5-fe47-5935-89e6-6f0baa7c7183', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ed001ea5-fe47-5935-89e6-6f0baa7c7183'}, {'abstract': 'Two books look at the rise and acrimonious demise of Hüsker Dü, a powerful force in the indie rock scene of the 1980s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-books-about-husker-du-by-bob-mould-and-andrew-earles.html', 'snippet': 'Two books look at the rise and acrimonious demise of Hüsker Dü, a powerful force in the indie rock scene of the 1980s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was early 1983, probably, after the “Everything Falls Apart” EP presaged Hüsker Dü’s departure from hard-core punk and before the “Metal Circus” EP made it official. Just a gig at a crummy club near CBGB, and late — after 1. There weren’t a dozen onlookers, but Hüsker Dü’s two early records were knockouts, and that Minneapolis trio never came east, so there we were. From our booth in back the music sounded terrific: headlong and enormous, the guitar unfashionably full, expressive and unending, with two raving vocalists alternating leads on songs whose words were hard to understand and whose tunes weren’t. Another half-dozen curious fans drifted in. And then, halfway through, the guitarist passed into some other dimension. When he stepped yowling off the low stage, most of us gravitated closer, glancing around and shaking our heads.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/bkr-Christgau-t_CA0/bkr-Christgau-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/bkr-Christgau-t_CA0/bkr-Christgau-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/bkr-Christgau-t_CA0/bkr-Christgau-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hüsker Dü’s Propulsive Liberation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Propulsive Liberation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mould, Bob', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Azerrad, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:23:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Christgau', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Christgau', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a51f343f-44d6-539f-b14c-d81cf88dc32f', 'word_count': 1531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a51f343f-44d6-539f-b14c-d81cf88dc32f'}, {'abstract': 'There’s a lot to be said, especially by Steven Tyler, of his rise to fame with Aerosmith.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-does-the-noise-in-my-head-bother-you-by-steven-tyler.html', 'snippet': 'There’s a lot to be said, especially by Steven Tyler, of his rise to fame with Aerosmith.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Calling Steven Tyler an incoherent loudmouth is like describing James Brown as a guy who moved around a lot. Tyler has devoted his improbably long musical career to testing the limits of incoherence, loudness and his mouth. From his 1970s glory days as the jive-talking lead singer of Aerosmith to his unlikely TV success as a judge on “American Idol,” he’s always delighted in babble for its own sake. In his words, he’s been “61 Highwayed and I did it my wayed; Little-Willie-Johned and been-here-and-goned; million-dollar riffed and Jimmy Cliffed; cotton-picked and Stevie Nick’d.” What he lacks in lucidity, which is just about every\xadthing, he makes up for with garrulous charm. As Laurence Sterne says of Yorick in “Tristram Shandy,” he has big sails but not an ounce of ballast.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/SUBB-Sheffield/SUBB-Sheffield-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 615, 'width': 461, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/SUBB-Sheffield/SUBB-Sheffield-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/SUBB-Sheffield/SUBB-Sheffield-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Steven Tyler’s Warp Speed Memoir', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dude’s a Little Hazy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tyler, Steven (1948- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Aerosmith', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dalton, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:23:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Sheffield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sheffield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f59224a7-93b7-54b7-9df9-3c04ab35c997', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f59224a7-93b7-54b7-9df9-3c04ab35c997'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Parks details his suffering from chronic pain and explains how he found relief from an unexpected source.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-teach-us-to-sit-still-by-tim-parks.html', 'snippet': 'Tim Parks details his suffering from chronic pain and explains how he found relief from an unexpected source.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The appeal of conversion stories often depends on descriptions of the darkness before enlightenment: we enjoy learning in detail about the presalvation misery, debauchery or sinfulness. The more detail, the better. The English novelist Tim Parks understands that principle. In his urbane, droll, weird yet far from charmless account of the pain and misery suffered by his body in general, and by his bladder, prostate, penis and related bits in particular, the conversion is from a cerebral, anxious, hunched-over and compulsively verbal kvetch (not his term, but the literal “squeeze” makes the Yiddish word seem appropriate) to something resembling the opposite. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Pinsky/Pinsky-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 864, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Pinsky/Pinsky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Pinsky/Pinsky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Curing the Pelvic Headache', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pelvic Headaches', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meditation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostate Gland', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parks, Tim', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:25:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Pinsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pinsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/240dceab-defd-54e3-82cb-b9f404e642f6', 'word_count': 1225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/240dceab-defd-54e3-82cb-b9f404e642f6'}, {'abstract': 'The narrator of this novel emends Joseph Conrad’s “Nostromo.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-the-secret-history-of-costaguana-by-juan-gabriel-vasquez.html', 'snippet': 'The narrator of this novel emends Joseph Conrad’s “Nostromo.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The best lines in “The Secret History of Costaguana” were written by Joseph Conrad. Harsh, maybe, but such is the treatment that its Colombian author, Juan Gabriel Vásquez (“The Informers”), invites — with crafty masochism — in this postmodern literary revenge story.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Wimmer/Wimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 295, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Wimmer/Wimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 295}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Wimmer/Wimmer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 503, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Wimmer/Wimmer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Wimmer/Wimmer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking Literary Revenge on Joseph Conrad', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Twice Told Tale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vasquez, Juan Gabriel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Conrad, Joseph', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:25:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natasha Wimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natasha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b0e5d53-b3ff-5822-a00b-ea241bfd8a63', 'word_count': 658, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b0e5d53-b3ff-5822-a00b-ea241bfd8a63'}, {'abstract': 'Why “common sense” is a thoroughly unreliable guide to the social world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-everything-is-obvious-once-you-know-the-answer-by-duncan-j-watts.html', 'snippet': 'Why “common sense” is a thoroughly unreliable guide to the social world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The popularity of the Mona Lisa is an illusion. As Duncan J. Watts explains: “We claim to be saying that the Mona Lisa is the most famous painting in the world because it has attributes X, Y and Z. But really what we’re saying is that the Mona Lisa is famous because it’s more like the Mona Lisa than anything else.” In other words, we are trapped inside a hall of mirrors of our own devising. We think the Mona Lisa is famous because of its traits, but we think those traits are significant only because they belong to the Mona Lisa, which we know to be famous. Ditto Shakespeare? Yes. When an incredulous English professor asked him whether he believed “Shakespeare might just be a fluke of history,” Watts indicated that he meant exactly that.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Christakis/Christakis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 924, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Christakis/Christakis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Christakis/Christakis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Trouble With Common Sense', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jumping to Conclusions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sociology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:25:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas A. Christakis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Christakis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f5af7e3-cc14-5b6d-b506-aad8a4f13057', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f5af7e3-cc14-5b6d-b506-aad8a4f13057'}, {'abstract': 'The characters in Anuradha Roy’s first novel wrestle with love and real estate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-an-atlas-of-impossible-longing-by-anuradha-roy.html', 'snippet': 'The characters in Anuradha Roy’s first novel wrestle with love and real estate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anuradha Roy’s first novel opens with the image of a house, “a Roman-\xadlooking affair with tapering pillars” that seems to float down a river in flood. First its veranda goes under and then its windows, until the rooms look drowned and the building itself appears to leave its moorings. The imagination of disaster is never far from these pages, and though this house will finally stay on its foundation, it does get swamped by a monsoon-swelled river, and with consequences that are bitter indeed. That waterfront property is only one of the three houses in which Roy’s narrative is played out — each of them bedraggled, each living on after its time, and none of them a proper home.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Gorra/Gorra-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 801, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Gorra/Gorra-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Gorra/Gorra-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Love and Real Estate in South Asia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A House for Mr. Babu', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:26:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gorra', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorra', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e37e1b69-c23c-562f-9f06-4984818a1186', 'word_count': 905, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e37e1b69-c23c-562f-9f06-4984818a1186'}, {'abstract': 'The “Great Gatsby” baby grows up to be the worldly heroine of Tom Carson’s picaresque novel, and a perfect mirror of “the American Century.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-daisy-buchanans-daughter-by-tom-carson.html', 'snippet': 'The “Great Gatsby” baby grows up to be the worldly heroine of Tom Carson’s picaresque novel, and a perfect mirror of “the American Century.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When readers met Pamela Buchanan in the pages of “The Great Gatsby,” she was about 2 years old. As “Daisy Buchanan’s Daughter” opens, she is in her 80s, waiting for a birthday call from President George W. Bush, during which she intends to blow her brains out to protest the Iraq war. While waiting, she blogs. Through a suicide note that eventually becomes an autobiography almost perfectly contemporary with “the American Century,” we learn that Pam has been many things: rich mother’s regret, successful author, war correspondent, diplomat’s wife. She was on the beach at Normandy, saw Dachau liberated and knew the Kennedys. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Koelb/Koelb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 784, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Koelb/Koelb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Koelb/Koelb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Great Gatsby’: The Next Generation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘I’m Glad It’s a Girl’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fitzgerald, F Scott', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carson, Tom', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:26:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tadzio Koelb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tadzio', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koelb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0df2e139-2a3d-5057-b39d-b6eeb47d7eb6', 'word_count': 942, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0df2e139-2a3d-5057-b39d-b6eeb47d7eb6'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist finds that poverty, trauma and corruption persist in Cambodia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/books/review/book-review-cambodias-curse-by-joel-brinkley.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist finds that poverty, trauma and corruption persist in Cambodia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the preface to “Cambodia’s Curse: The Modern History of a Troubled Land,” Joel Brink\xadley recalls his first encounter with Cambodia. Brinkley was reporting for The Louisville Courier-Journal from a refugee camp near the Thai border in 1979, in the aftermath of Pol Pot’s reign. “As they tell of years of horror and misery,” Brinkley wrote, “their faces are expressionless and dull . . . as if they’re talking about a dull day of work. Their tales end with a nodding acknowledgment of the death of their nation and culture.” Brinkley, who later worked for The New York Times, finds little has changed in the 32 years since then. As the title suggests, his book is an unabashed plea to refocus international aid and diplomacy on a suffering people. It is also an attempt to hold some of those responsible for that suffering accountable — but not all.Cambodia lost a quarter of its population under the Khmer Rouge. For many, survival meant 14-hour days of backbreaking work, often on little more than a cupful of rice or a smattering of gruel. You could be killed on the least suspicion you sympathized with the Vietnamese. The effects of this period have proven hard to shake.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/books/review/Whitney/Whitney-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cambodia After Year Zero', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Year Zero', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption in Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Khmer Rouge', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cambodia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hun Sen', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T16:26:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joel Whitney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Whitney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/de764dfe-e160-5a2d-a973-e1d2d83b3433', 'word_count': 922, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de764dfe-e160-5a2d-a973-e1d2d83b3433'}, {'abstract': 'The dominant talking points in the days following the Dallas Mavericks’ victory in the N.B.A. finals last week were predictably hero- and player-centric. Had the M.V.P. Dirk Nowitzki forever shed his “soft” label? Had LeBron James choked? Was it all his fault, Miami’s defeat? Less was said about the coaching.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/does-coaching-win-championships/', 'snippet': 'The dominant talking points in the days following the Dallas Mavericks’ victory in the N.B.A. finals last week were predictably hero- and player-centric. Had the M.V.P. Dirk Nowitzki forever shed his “soft” label? Had LeBron James choked? Was it a...', 'lead_paragraph': 