vely, Tom Clancy’s novels have spent more than seven years on the hardcover fiction best-seller list, and all but one — “The Hunt for Red October” — reached No. 1.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Best Sellers Weekly Graphic: Spymaster', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T17:07:25+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/0b529d1c-fe4c-5889-8033-63e3808af93b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/0b529d1c-fe4c-5889-8033-63e3808af93b'}, {'abstract': 'A number of people, offended by the exposure of innocent Internet users’ personal information, are working to stop LulzSec.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/lulzsec-hackers-make-enemies-online/', 'snippet': 'A number of people, offended by the exposure of innocent Internet users’ personal information, are working to stop LulzSec.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lulz Security, the hacking group that has been on a spree lately, might think its antics are hilarious. But some others in the hacker world are sick of its games. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'LulzSec Hackers Make Enemies Online', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Cyberwarfare', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hackers (Computers)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lulz Security', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T17:10:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Riva Richmond and Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Riva', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richmond', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2f7b270-ee07-5754-998a-9bdfd53876dc', 'word_count': 641, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2f7b270-ee07-5754-998a-9bdfd53876dc'}, {'abstract': 'Wheels recently asked readers to send in photos of their wedding-day getaway vehicles. Not surprisingly, there were some interesting submissions.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/the-wheels-wedding-album/', 'snippet': 'Wheels recently asked readers to send in photos of their wedding-day getaway vehicles. Not surprisingly, there were some interesting submissions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wheels recently used the occasion of a Ford scion’s wedding, from which groom and bride fled to the reception in a 1965 Mustang convertible, to invite Wheels readers to submit photos of their wedding-day getaway vehicles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Wheels Wedding Album', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T17:10:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79664b1f-7e5b-5d6f-8319-0476f0e5f3e4', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79664b1f-7e5b-5d6f-8319-0476f0e5f3e4'}, {'abstract': 'China isn’t the only country where parents have a distinct preference for baby boys rather than girls — so is the United States, according to Gallup poll data from the last 70 years.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/preferring-boys-to-girls/', 'snippet': 'China isn’t the only country where parents have a distinct preference for baby boys rather than girls — so is the United States, according to Gallup poll data from the last 70 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'China isn’t the only country where parents have a distinct preference for baby boys rather than baby girls. Americans prefers XY to XX in their offspring, too — and have wanted boys rather than girls for the last 70 years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Preferring Boys to Girls', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gallup Poll', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T17:11:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/82069c4d-2ce3-5095-86d1-6e78ba9614cc', 'word_count': 316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82069c4d-2ce3-5095-86d1-6e78ba9614cc'}, {'abstract': 'A day after vowing to drop an appeal with the Supreme Court, Tyler and Cameron Winklevoss said they would pursue claims that Facebook deceived them during settlement talks.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/winklevosses-pursue-new-legal-avenue-against-facebook/', 'snippet': 'A day after vowing to drop an appeal with the Supreme Court, Tyler and Cameron Winklevoss said they would pursue claims that Facebook deceived them during settlement talks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A day after Tyler and Cameron Winklevoss said they would drop plans to appeal a case against against Facebook to the United States Supreme Court, the twins, along with their partner Divya Narendra, told a federal court in Massachusetts that they would pursue separate claims that Facebook suppressed evidence when the parties were in settlement talks in 2008. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Winklevosses Pursue New Legal Avenue Against Facebook', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winklevoss, Cameron', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winklevoss, Tyler', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook.com', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T17:16:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miguel Helft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miguel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Helft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52013309-3ba2-598f-9770-35f7f4e6484c', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52013309-3ba2-598f-9770-35f7f4e6484c'}, {'abstract': 'The greatest opportunity of Ryan Harrison’s professional tennis career was slipping through his fingers, as evidenced by the racket skidding onto the court.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/harrison-returns-with-his-temper-not-his-game/', 'snippet': 'The greatest opportunity of Ryan Harrison’s professional tennis career was slipping through his fingers, as evidenced by the racket skidding onto the court.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — The greatest opportunity of Ryan Harrison’s professional tennis career was slipping through his fingers, as evidenced by the racket skidding onto the court.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Harrison Returns With His Temper, Not His Game', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T17:20:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4c7c069-adb9-5b32-9767-408ba383f9e1', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4c7c069-adb9-5b32-9767-408ba383f9e1'}, {'abstract': 'The new video by the synth pop group, which makes is U.S. premiere on The Moment today, epitomizes the cool aesthetic of the underground minimal electronics movement.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/videophiles-xeno-oaklanders-the-staircase/', 'snippet': 'The new video by the synth pop group, which makes is U.S. premiere on The Moment today, epitomizes the cool aesthetic of the underground minimal electronics movement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just when you thought winter was finally over, a cold front suddenly hits. The new video for “The Staircase,” by the synth pop group Xeno & Oaklander, which makes is United States premiere on The Moment today, epitomizes the cool aesthetic of the underground minimal electronics movement. The video has the quality of a Xerox, reproduced over and over, blown out and dark, and induces a sense of something lost. Sean McBride and Miss Liz Wendelbo, the duo behind Xeno & Oaklander, along with other members of the Wierd Records label, have been largely credited with the revival of minimal wave here in New York. Taryn Waldman of Boxmotion, who directed the “The Staircase,” and Patrick Stroub, its cinematographer, make references to Roman Polanski’s film “Repulsion,” as a slip of a woman, Xeno (Wendelbo), climbs the stairs of a claustrophobic 19th-century Brooklyn tenement building, seemingly unaware that the object of her affection and of her voyeurism, Oaklander (McBride), is right next to her. (Xeno’s stark, wintry eyes come courtesy of the master retoucher Pascal Dangin, known in fashion circles for his work for Annie Leibovitz, Patrick Demarchelier, Steven Meisel, Steven Klein, Craig McDean and others.) Alienation, disconnection, damage? Oh, city life!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Videophiles | Xeno & Oaklander’s ‘The Staircase’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T17:21:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joy Dietrich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dietrich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/754415cd-1305-502c-b43b-4110a7621a43', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/754415cd-1305-502c-b43b-4110a7621a43'}, {'abstract': 'Residents of Audubon Park in Washington Heights hope that Columbia University’s northward expansion will increase demand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/06/26/realestate/20110626_Living_Audubon.html', 'snippet': 'Residents of Audubon Park in Washington Heights hope that Columbia University’s northward expansion will increase demand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Residents of Audubon Park in Washington Heights hope that Columbia University’s northward expansion will increase demand.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Living In | Audubon Park', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'WASHINGTON HEIGHTS (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T17:21:46+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Real Estate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/49912a8c-463e-51ce-a28c-2dd009137d91', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/49912a8c-463e-51ce-a28c-2dd009137d91'}, {'abstract': 'The proliferation of new series on television, particularly noticeable in the summer, means a flood of advertising and marketing campaigns to promote all those shows.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/big-c-or-small-business-tv-promotions-get-creative/', 'snippet': 'The proliferation of new series on television, particularly noticeable in the summer, means a flood of advertising and marketing campaigns to promote all those shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The proliferation of new series on television is particularly noticeable in the summer, when cable channels pour on the premieres. That means a flood of advertising and marketing campaigns to promote all those shows.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Big C’ or Small Business, TV Promotions Get Creative', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T17:27:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e9f2150-a89d-5ab7-a674-54aeb66ea1bf', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e9f2150-a89d-5ab7-a674-54aeb66ea1bf'}, {'abstract': 'Residents of Audubon Park in Washington Heights hope that Columbia University’s northward expansion will increase demand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/realestate/naturalist-perched-here-living-inaudubon-park.html', 'snippet': 'Residents of Audubon Park in Washington Heights hope that Columbia University’s northward expansion will increase demand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE can imagine how John James Audubon, the renowned naturalist and illustrator of “Birds of America,” might have reacted to the idea: his own name, being used to promote the very development that would transform the rambling woodlands of his beloved Washington Heights estate into a densely populated urban district. But that is precisely what happened, according to Matthew Spady, a longtime resident and magpie collector of historical facts about the area. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', '