ery case, in which no one at the company was charged despite its admission to making illegal payments to veterinarians in Mexico.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/business/25stewart.html', 'snippet': 'In his debut column for The Times, James B. Stewart writes about the Tyson Foods bribery case, in which no one at the company was charged despite its admission to making illegal payments to veterinarians in Mexico.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In late June 2004, a plant manager for one of Tyson Foods’ poultry processing plants in Mexico sent a memo to company headquarters in Springdale, Ark.: two women who “most definitely do not work for Tyson Foods in Mexico” each were paid 30,700 pesos, or about $2,700, a month and had been for years. Tyson is one of the world’s largest producers of poultry, pork and beef products, a ubiquitous presence in American supermarkets that has been trying to increase foreign sales. The memo set off an ethics scandal that reached into Tyson’s executive suite and raises questions about who, if anyone, is being held accountable for high-level corporate crime.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bribery, but Nobody Was Charged', 'kicker': 'Common Sense', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bribery, But Nobody Was Charged', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tyson Foods Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T19:43:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By James B. Stewart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c3a0da7-cc05-5042-8b98-84bbcb2ff4b9', 'word_count': 1469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c3a0da7-cc05-5042-8b98-84bbcb2ff4b9'}, {'abstract': 'The members of Deer Tick, the Rhode Island indie folk band, channeled their inner grunge for a short-lived side project called Deervana, in which they played Nirvana songs and gave fans a realistic dose of 1990s nostalgia.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/deer-tick-channels-the-bands-inner-grunge-to-play-nirvana/', 'snippet': 'The members of Deer Tick, the Rhode Island indie folk band, channeled their inner grunge for a short-lived side project called Deervana, in which they played Nirvana songs and gave fans a realistic dose of 1990s nostalgia.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Here we are now, entertain us.” It’s been more than two decades since a trio of musicians from the Pacific Northwest announced itself, demanded something from its audience and channeled their disaffection into blockbuster record sales, changing the course of rock history. To celebrate the 20th anniversary of Nirvana’s seminal album “Nevermind,” in September, Universal Music will release a deluxe five-disc edition of the album, with “unreleased recordings, rarities, b-sides, BBC radio appearances, alternative mixes, rare live recordings and an unreleased concert,” according to a press release the label sent out this week. (It goes on sale Sept. 20 in North America and a day earlier overseas.) The collection rides a wave of 1990s nostalgia, from\xa0 baby doll dresses and Doc Martens to riot grrrl.\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: Deer Tick Channels the Band’s Inner Grunge to Play Nirvana', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cobain, Kurt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCauley, John J. Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Bowl', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deer Tick (Music Group)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nirvana', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T19:45:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a38d6982-348b-5a3e-948a-c2b55d3807ae', 'word_count': 468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a38d6982-348b-5a3e-948a-c2b55d3807ae'}, {'abstract': 'The House dealt a symbolic blow to President Obama by rejecting a bill to authorize the operations in Libya, but also turned back a measure that would have cut financing for the effort.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/us/politics/25powers.html', 'snippet': 'The House dealt a symbolic blow to President Obama by rejecting a bill to authorize the operations in Libya, but also turned back a measure that would have cut financing for the effort.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The House dealt a symbolic blow to President Obama on Friday by resoundingly rejecting a bill to authorize United States military operations in Libya. But the chamber also defeated a measure that would have limited financing to support those efforts.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/world/africa/25powers/25powers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/25/world/africa/25powers/25powers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type':