: 'nyt://video/a0b0ef0c-fa81-5be3-8d49-f873cb215855', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/a0b0ef0c-fa81-5be3-8d49-f873cb215855'}, {'abstract': '“Expedition Impossible,” a new reality show on ABC from Mark Burnett, got off to a solid if somewhat subdued start on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/arts/television/expedition-leads-abc-to-second-place.html', 'snippet': '“Expedition Impossible,” a new reality show on ABC from Mark Burnett, got off to a solid if somewhat subdued start on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Expedition Impossible,” a new reality show on ABC from Mark Burnett involving teams of contestants traveling through Morocco and competing for money, got off to a solid if somewhat subdued start on Thursday, attracting seven million viewers at 9 p.m., according to Nielsen’s estimates. The premiere’s audience was down slightly from ABC’s show at 8, “Wipeout” (7.4 million), but higher than the network’s “Rookie Blue” at 10 (5.9 million). For the night, ABC finished runner-up to CBS and its lineup of repeats, including “CSI” (7.7 million) and “The Mentalist” (8 million). Fox was third with an elimination episode of “So You Think You Can Dance” at 8 (6.3 million) and a rerun of “Glee” at 9 (3.2 million). NBC and repeats of its comedy block lagged behind in fourth. In the 10 p.m. hour, NBC’s “Love Bites” attracted just under two million viewers.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Expedition’ Leads ABC to Second Place', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Expedition’ Leads ABC To Second Place', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T22:54:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b0e7f04-81f2-5a86-a740-5ece6d4aba7f', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b0e7f04-81f2-5a86-a740-5ece6d4aba7f'}, {'abstract': 'Rakim, backed by the Roots, revisited his album “Paid In Full.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/arts/music/rakim-and-the-roots-at-the-blue-note.html', 'snippet': 'Rakim, backed by the Roots, revisited his album “Paid In Full.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Very early on Thursday morning at the Blue Note, Rakim told a story about going to the house of the producer Marley Marl in the mid-1980s to record a song. Once there, he took the microphone and promptly sat on the couch. Marley Marl, he remembered, became agitated and suggested that he stand up to give the track more energy. Rakim demurred. That low-energy hum? “That’s how I wanna sound.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/Rakim/Rakim-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/Rakim/Rakim-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 346}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/Rakim/Rakim-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/Rakim/Rakim-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/Rakim/Rakim-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Rapper’s Still Stirring Things Up by Being Laid-Back', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Rapper’s Still Stirring Things Up by Being Laid-Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rakim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roots, The', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T22:57:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb25ef59-dc13-5707-8674-cd10756331dd', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb25ef59-dc13-5707-8674-cd10756331dd'}, {'abstract': 'We like Derek Jeter, we really do.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/this-sunday-more-memories-please-derek-jeter/', 'snippet': 'We like Derek Jeter, we really do.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just as no music will ever sound as meaningful to you as your favorite bands did when you were a teenager, no athletes measure up to the idols of your childhood. For Derek Jeter, however, I claim an exception.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Sunday: More Memories, Please, Derek Jeter', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Series', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeter, Derek', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T22:59:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4362dd3e-b721-5cb5-a176-e8857397bd84', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4362dd3e-b721-5cb5-a176-e8857397bd84'}, {'abstract': '“You will see this movie!” seems to be the marketing strategy behind “Beyond the Great Revival.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/movies/chinese-get-viewers-to-propaganda-film-beyond-the-great-revival.html', 'snippet': '“You will see this movie!” seems to be the marketing strategy behind “Beyond the Great Revival.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — This month China’s great masses are being mobilized by their leaders for an unusual purpose. Employees at state-owned companies and at all levels of government are joining students from grade school to universities as they leave their homes, head out into the heat and do their duty: ensure the financial success of the government’s latest propaganda film, “Beginning of the Great Revival.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/CHINA/CHINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/CHINA/CHINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/CHINA/CHINA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/CHINA/CHINA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/25/arts/CHINA/CHINA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'People, You Will See This Film. Right Now.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'People, You Will See This Film. Right Now.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Propaganda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-24T23:04:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Xiyun Yang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Xiyun', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea282203-a2c4-5dd4-b4c2-61f155d56ea6', 'word_count': 928, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea282203-a2c4-5dd4-b4c2-61f155d56ea6'}, {'abstract': 'When The Times posted online a mystery Nazi photo album, it took just hours for readers to identify the photographer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/arts/design/mystery-of-a-nazi-photographer-solved-by-online-readers.html', 'snippet': 'When The Times posted online a mystery Nazi photo album, it took just hours for readers to identify the photographer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It appeared to be something of a mystery: a little photo album that had fallen into the hands of a New York garment industry executive with debts to pay off. In one snapshot was a bus with German lettering on the side. In another, a kitten on a