arge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/opinion/26oped-art/26oped-art-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/opinion/26oped-art/26oped-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/opinion/26oped-art/26oped-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sympathy for the Devils', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sympathy for the Devils', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Serial Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T01:14:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Lindsay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindsay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9686fa7f-d070-5c8a-aa93-8c34eae36bb7', 'word_count': 939, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9686fa7f-d070-5c8a-aa93-8c34eae36bb7'}, {'abstract': 'The Transportation Safety Board of Canada said in a report that three drunken passengers may have caused the crash of a chartered float plane that killed them as well as the pilot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/world/americas/25webbrfs-Canada.html', 'snippet': 'The Transportation Safety Board of Canada said in a report that three drunken passengers may have caused the crash of a chartered float plane that killed them as well as the pilot.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Transportation Safety Board of Canada said in a report that three drunken and argumentative passengers may have caused the crash of a chartered float plane that killed them as well as the pilot. The passengers boarded the brief flight between two islands off the coast of British Columbia in May 2010 after being refused water taxi service because they were carrying a case of beer and several bottles of liquor. In a report issued this week, the board said that it found evidence that one of the passengers, who had been quarreling among themselves, shoved the pilot’s seat from behind, forcing him against the aircraft’s controls and causing a fatal dive into the water. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Canada: Drunken Passengers May Have Caused Plane Crash', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | THE AMERICAS', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Canada: Drunken Passengers May Have Caused Plane Crash', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'British Columbia (Canada)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airplane Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T01:15:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a869993a-a2e3-5e54-a093-09e3251dad68', 'word_count': 116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a869993a-a2e3-5e54-a093-09e3251dad68'}, {'abstract': 'At Patty’s Berries and Bunches, you can buy containers of prepicked berries, but visitors prefer to pick their own, at $5 a container.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/nyregion/pick-your-own-at-pattys-berries-and-bunches-in-mattituck-ny.html', 'snippet': 'At Patty’s Berries and Bunches, you can buy containers of prepicked berries, but visitors prefer to pick their own, at $5 a container.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even as Patricia DiVello, 52, watched several children gathering strawberries on a recent afternoon at Patty’s Berries and Bunches, her pick-your-own farm in Mattituck, she was already looking forward to the next crop. ', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Berries, Ripe for the Picking', 'kicker': 'Quick Bite | Mattituck', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Berries, Ripe for the Picking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Farmers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fruit', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T01:16:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan M. Novick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c619e05b-8d9e-5780-9c8a-510fb3447aca', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c619e05b-8d9e-5780-9c8a-510fb3447aca'}, {'abstract': 'The early hours of the first round of the N.H.L. entry draft proceeded without any major surprises — at least until the Rangers picked center J. T. Miller at No. 15', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/sports/hockey/teams-keep-focus-on-offense-at-top-of-the-nhl-draft.html', 'snippet': 'The early hours of the first round of the N.H.L. entry draft proceeded without any major surprises — at least until the Rangers picked center J. T. Miller at No. 15', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. PAUL — The early hours of the first round of the N.H.L. entry draft proceeded without any major surprises — at least until the Rangers picked at No. 15. The stunned look on the face of center J. T. Miller, a member of the United States under-18 team, showed just how unexpected this was.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/sports/yHOCKEY/yHOCKEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/sports/yHOCKEY/yHOCKEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/25/sports/yHOCKEY/yHOCKEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Move by Rangers Surprises Even the Player They Choose With the 15th Pick', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Move by Rangers Surprises Even the Player They Choose With the 15th Pick', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Draft and Recruitment (Sports)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, J T (1993- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T01:26:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Borzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da28f5fa-0013-56ef-a216-c03442f7b6b4', 'word_count': 818, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da28f5fa-0013-56ef-a216-c03442f7b6b4'}, {'abstract': 'The governing African National Congress said Friday that it now favored “drastically” reducing the scope of a government secrecy bill to apply only the country’s security services.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/world/africa/25webbrfs-Southafrica.html', 'snippet': 'The governing African National Congress said Friday that it now favored “drastically” reducing the scope of a government secrecy bill to apply only the country’s security services.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The governing African National Congress, which has been pushing a bill that would have enshrouded much routine government activity in secrecy and punished whistleblowers and journalists with up to 25 years in prison, said Friday that it now favored “drastically” reducing the scope of the bill to apply only the country’s security services. It also said the harsh minimum prisons sentences should be removed from the legislation. In a statement, the party said the bill must ensure that the law would not be abused to hide corruption, the very outcome the bill’s critics had feared. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Africa: Governing Party Seeks to Tone Down Secrecy Bill', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | AFRICA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Africa: Governing Party Seeks to Tone Down Secrecy Bill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classified Information and State Secrets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AFRICAN NATIONAL CONGRESS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T01:28:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Celia W. Dugger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Celia', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dugger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b574ff21-fbaf-533d-93e8-7d27c912ccb0', 'word_count': 96, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b574ff21-fbaf-533d-93e8-7d27c912ccb0'}, {'abstract': 'Born just miles apart into slavery, Frederick Douglass and Harriet Tubman led lives that were both parallel and, in other ways, radically different.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/24/parallel-lives-from-the-eastern-shore/', 'snippet': 'Born just miles apart into slavery, Frederick Douglass and Harriet Tubman led lives that were both parallel and, in other ways, radically different.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harriet Ross Tubman and Frederick Douglass, two of the best-known African Americans of the Civil War era, were both born into slavery on Maryland’s Eastern Shore, at roughly the same time. Perhaps because of its border-state decision to stay with the Union, Maryland and its slave-owning past are often forgotten today. And yet as the lives and scars of these two attested, slavery in rural Maryland could be as oppressive and isolated as the cotton fields of the Deep South. Swaths of white family-owned farmland were worked by thousands of enslaved people, who also worked on the water to bring back the rich catch of the Chesapeake Bay. And in no part of the state was slavery as brutal as the Eastern Shore — conditions exposed by the oddly parallel lives of these two famous Marylanders. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parallel Lives from the Eastern Shore', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Underground Railroad (19th Century)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Douglass, Frederick', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tubman, Harriet', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maryland', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T01:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jamie Stiehm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jamie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stiehm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c581645-0b6a-5856-aea9-1a83adb4f4fc', 'word_count': 1216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c581645-0b6a-5856-aea9-1a83adb4f4fc'}, {'abstract': 'James Baldwin’s words about how expensive it is to be poor are quite relevant today. If only they could resonate with Washington politicians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/opinion/25blow.html', 'snippet': 'James Baldwin’s words about how expensive it is to be poor are quite relevant today. If only they could resonate with Washington politicians.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Anyone who has ever struggled with poverty knows how extremely expensive it is to be poor.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Them That’s Not Shall Lose', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Them That’s Not Shall Lose', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T01:32:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles M. Blow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/87ca9280-7ead-5034-abda-011b356add13', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87ca9280-7ead-5034-abda-011b356add13'}, {'abstract': 'President Hugo Chávez, recovering in Cuba after emergency surgery there this month, broke an unusual stretch of silence with four brief Twitter messages on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/world/americas/25webbrfs-Venezuela.html', 'snippet': 'President Hugo Chávez, recovering in Cuba after emergency surgery there this month, broke an unusual stretch of silence with four brief Twitter messages on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' President Hugo Chávez, recovering in Cuba after emergency surgery there this month, broke an unusual stretch of silence with four brief Twitter messages on Friday.\xa0 The messages, laden with references to military glory, Venezuela’s national anthem, the weather and an 1821 battle against Spain, were the first public communications from Mr. Chávez since June 12, when he spoke by telephone to a journalist two days after his surgery in Havana for a pelvic abscess. Mr. Chávez made no reference to his health or location, which have stirred fierce debate in Venezuela over the scant disclosure of such information. Still, he singled out Gen. Euclides Campos Aponte, commander of Venezuela’s army, for praise. “Good morning followers!” he wrote in one message. “Today is the day of my Army and the sun is shining! A gigantic hug to my soldiers and my beloved people!” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Venezuela: Chávez Breaks Silence; Says Little', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | THE AMERICAS', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Venezuela: Chávez Breaks Silence; Says Little', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chavez, Hugo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Venezuela', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T01:34:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Romero', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romero', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5b7dfad-2088-5d00-b850-209a98f3c2ce', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5b7dfad-2088-5d00-b850-209a98f3c2ce'}, {'abstract': 'Speaker John A. Boehner has found that he must seek common ground not only with Democrats, but with fellow Republicans as well.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/25/us/politics/25fiscal.html', 'snippet': 'Speaker John A. Boehner has found that he must seek common ground not only with Democrats, but with fellow Republicans as well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The morning after Republicans claimed the House on Nov. 2, John A. Boehner, the soon-to-be speaker, was asked how he was ever going to get a multitrillion-dollar increase in the federal debt ceiling through his new Tea Party-infused majority. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/us/FISCAL/FISCAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/25/us/FISCAL/FISCAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'ima