ll dead. But their heirs are beginning to speak for them through a federal copyright law that practically invites descendants to sue for ownership interests in characters whose current value could never have been imagined at the moment of creation.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26observer-1308944525217/26observer-1308944525217-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26observer-1308944525217/26observer-1308944525217-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26observer-1308944525217/26observer-1308944525217-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 921, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26observer-1308944525217/26observer-1308944525217-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26observer-1308944525217/26observer-1308944525217-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Marvel Superheroes and the Fathers of Invention', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Sunday Observer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Marvel Superheroes and The Fathers of Invention', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marvel Entertainment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirby, Jack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Stan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T22:55:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brent Staples', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brent', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Staples', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce59f149-8e1d-54af-98c4-c6c29b0de6e5', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce59f149-8e1d-54af-98c4-c6c29b0de6e5'}, {'abstract': 'Obama’s decision to release 30 million barrels of oil from the reserve is justified for now, given the turmoil in the Middle East and the weak economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/opinion/sunday/26sun3.html', 'snippet': 'Obama’s decision to release 30 million barrels of oil from the reserve is justified for now, given the turmoil in the Middle East and the weak economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We have never been enthusiastic about quick fixes. But the Obama administration’s decision to release 30 million barrels of oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve — the nation’s emergency stockpiles — makes sense. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tapping the Oil Reserve', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tapping the Oil Reserve', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strategic Petroleum Reserve (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T22:55:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/a24391cb-2b56-554f-bee8-537fffba1c8b', 'word_count': 448, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a24391cb-2b56-554f-bee8-537fffba1c8b'}, {'abstract': 'Giving multinationals a big tax break on foreign profits won’t create more jobs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/opinion/sunday/26sun2.html', 'snippet': 'Giving multinationals a big tax break on foreign profits won’t create more jobs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Big businesses are telling Washington that they are willing to do their bit for the economy — if the price is right. Multinational companies say they could repatriate hundreds of billions in foreign profits and pump them into domestic investment and hiring, but only if Congress and the White House agree to cut the tax rate on those profits to 5.25 percent from 35 percent. They call their plan “the next stimulus.” Sounds more like extortion.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Whose Stimulus?', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Whose Stimulus? Giving multinationals a big tax break on foreign profits won’t create more jobs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T22:56:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/488f68c1-8158-5cb4-ba63-56e26195c5e6', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/488f68c1-8158-5cb4-ba63-56e26195c5e6'}, {'abstract': 'Testing your knowledge of recent events.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/opinion/sunday/26quiz.html', 'snippet': 'Testing your knowledge of recent events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NPR’s “Wait Wait... Don’t Tell Me!” is heard weekends on public radio stations across the country. (npr.org/programs/wait-wait-dont-tell-me/)', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26QUIZ/26QUIZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 348, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26QUIZ/26QUIZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 348}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26QUIZ/26QUIZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26QUIZ/26QUIZ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26QUIZ/26QUIZ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wait Wait ... Don’t Tell Me!', 'kicker': 'Quiz', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wait Wait ... Don’t Tell Me!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-25T22:57:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f3c5dd5-0124-5c6f-ac75-ab284ba15f10', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f3c5dd5-0124-5c6f-ac75-ab284ba15f10'}, {'abstract': 'The field fixes its errors with more difficulty than it would have you believe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/opinion/sunday/26ideas.html', 'snippet': 'The field fixes its errors with more difficulty than it would have you believe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE of the great strengths of science is that it can fix its own mistakes. “There are many hypotheses in science which are wrong,” the astrophysicist Carl Sagan once said. “That’s perfectly all right: it’s the aperture to finding out what’s right. Science is a self-correcting process.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26deDiscovery/26deDiscovery-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 362, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26deDiscovery/26deDiscovery-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 362}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/26/sunday-review/26deDiscovery/26deDiscovery-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legac