.html', 'snippet': 'The Cuban-born pianist David Virelles helped make a set by the Mark Turner Quartet erupt at the Village Vanguard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A quartet under Mark Turner’s name started an early set on Tuesday night at the Village Vanguard with some standards: Thelonious Monk’s “Crepuscule With Nellie” and Bud Powell’s “Dance of the Infidels.” Not long after the beginning, the music started to change from the inside out: known structures, unknown contents. And after that it became even less traceable. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sometimes Even the Lulls Are Full of Action', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sometimes Even the Lulls Are Full of Action', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turner, Mark', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T16:17:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/15b86d98-558f-5b9c-a3fa-9700711db328', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15b86d98-558f-5b9c-a3fa-9700711db328'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the Paris Fashion Week photo diary of Sonny Vandevelde.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/photos-of-the-moment-raf-simons-2/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the Paris Fashion Week photo diary of Sonny Vandevelde.', 'lead_paragraph': ' NYTD.fullScreenSlideShowFactory.createSlideshow("#824", 592, 440, true, chameleonData);', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photos of the Moment | Raf Simons', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T16:37:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sonny Vandevelde', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sonny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vandevelde', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e563cd82-e3eb-55b2-87fe-988aab0eaff7', 'word_count': 35, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e563cd82-e3eb-55b2-87fe-988aab0eaff7'}, {'abstract': 'We’re not all extremists.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/not-a-does-not-imply-b/', 'snippet': 'We’re not all extremists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just a thought suggested by some economic discussions I’ve followed, including the comments on this blog: many people seem to have a hard time accepting that there are intermediate positions, in particular that you can question free-market, hard-money dogma without being a wild man. To say that some inflation can sometimes be a good thing does not mean advocating hyperinflation; to call for deficit spending in slumps does not mean saying that debt and deficits never matter; and so on.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not-A Does Not Imply B', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T16:40:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fb7c9fd-36a9-5214-94a5-2ddf4cdb8d23', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fb7c9fd-36a9-5