, 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/656a503e-bf1f-5a10-8996-ea7f550aba0f', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/656a503e-bf1f-5a10-8996-ea7f550aba0f'}, {'abstract': 'Kim Jones, in his first men’s collection for Louis Vuitton, drew upon his African upbringing for this travel-minded collection.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/paris-report-louis-vuitton/', 'snippet': 'Kim Jones, in his first men’s collection for Louis Vuitton, drew upon his African upbringing for this travel-minded collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kim Jones, in his first men’s collection for Louis Vuitton, drew upon his African upbringing for this travel-minded collection. The classic Damier checked pattern was given a jolt of color inspired by the Masai tribe in bright red and blue shirts, knits and scarves. A waterproof bonded silk drawstring jacket was paired with cashmere jersey shorts and sweatshirt for a medal winning look worthy of this house of luxury goods.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Top 5 | Louis Vuitton', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T18:40:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Pask', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pask', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bbd3621-6ff6-5fb1-9911-6d83a07c0603', 'word_count': 75, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bbd3621-6ff6-5fb1-9911-6d83a07c0603'}, {'abstract': 'Seven patients remained hospitalized as European Union officials discussed action to restrict sales of bean sprouts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/world/europe/27ecoli.html', 'snippet': 'Seven patients remained hospitalized as European Union officials discussed action to restrict sales of bean sprouts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRUSSELS — The French authorities said Sunday that the latest outbreak of a deadly strain of E. coli in Europe appeared to be isolated, even as European Union officials discussed wider action to restrict sales of bean sprouts, the suspected cause of the outbreak. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'French E. Coli Episode Seems Isolated, Officials Say', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'French E. Coli Outbreak Seems to Be Isolated, Officials Say', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Contamination and Poisoning', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E Coli (Bacteria)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T19:14:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Kanter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kanter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fd2e20f-115c-51b2-8056-9a62a66259c5', 'word_count': 863, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fd2e20f-115c-51b2-8056-9a62a66259c5'}, {'abstract': 'A Harlem intersection where he rescued a white colleague was named for Samuel Eliot Battle, who became an officer June 28, 1911.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/nyregion/recalling-samuel-battle-who-became-first-black-on-nypd.html', 'snippet': 'A Harlem intersection where he rescued a white colleague was named for Samuel Eliot Battle, who became an officer June 28, 1911.', 'lead_paragraph': 'His parents were among the last generation born into Southern slavery, and his own birth in 1883 was notable for another benchmark: At 16 pounds, he was the biggest baby ever recorded in North Carolina.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/nyregion/27cop/27cop-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 733, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/nyregion/27cop/27cop-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/27/nyregion/27cop/27cop-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Recalling First Black Appointed to New York Police Dept.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '100 Years Later, Recalling a Police Pioneer in the City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T19:22:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13fabe8a-323d-5dcc-bdd9-962642770bac', 'word_count': 1089, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13fabe8a-323d-5dcc-bdd9-962642770bac'}, {'abstract': '“My Generation,” which was nominated for a daytime Emmy, is bringing attention to the age group that watches more TV than any other.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/arts/television/aarp-makes-television-shows-for-viewers-over-50.html', 'snippet': '“My Generation,” which was nominated for a daytime Emmy, is bringing attention to the age group that watches more TV than any other.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Sunday, the TV show “My Generation,” which runs weekly on PBS, lost the daytime Emmy for outstanding lifestyle programming. Its competition was fierce: “The Nate Berkus Show,” from Oprah’s home design guy; the Style Network’s “How Do I Look?” and “The Martha Stewart Show,” which took home the trophy. But for “My Generation,” a show created by AARP, and aimed at viewers 50 and older, it was truly an honor just to be nominated. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/AARP1/AARP1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/AARP1/AARP1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/AARP1/AARP1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Demographic Gray (or Graying) Lures TV Industry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Demographic Gray (or Graying) Lures TV Industry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AARP', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T19:32:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b8d19ad-52b5-50ed-8c23-de1e0eb4743e', 'word_count': 975, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b8d19ad-52b5-50ed-8c23-de1e0eb4743e'}, {'abstract': '“Cars 2″ has continued Pixar’s run of commercial — if not critical — success.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/amid-criticism-a-fast-start-for-cars-2/', 'snippet': '“Cars 2″ has continued Pixar’s run of commercial — if not critical — success.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pixar’s “Cars 2” overcame surprisingly negative critical reviews to overperform at the weekend box office, selling an estimated $68 million at North American theaters. That No. 1 finish extends Pixar’s perfect box-office track record — all 12 of its feature films have opened in first place. How sales hold up, however, will be watched carefully. “Cars 2,” directed by John Lasseter, chief creative officer at Pixar, represents an end to the studio’s perfect critical track record; only about 34 percent of reviews were positive, according to RottenTomatoes.com, by far the worst for any Pixar title.The R-rated comedy “Bad Teacher” (Sony Pictures Entertainment) was a stronger-than-expected second with about $31 million in ticket sales — a result that cements mega-raunchy films as one of Hollywood’s best performers of the moment. “Green Lantern” (Warner Brothers) fell 65 percent in its second weekend to third place, taking in about $18.4 million for a new total of $89 million, according to Hollywood.com, which compiles ticketing statistics. “Super 8” (Paramount Pictures) continued to show strength in its third weekend, selling about $12 million for a new total of $95 million. “Mr. Popper’s Penguins” (20th Century Fox) was fifth with about $10.3 million for a two-week total of $39.4 million. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Amid Criticism, a Fast Start for ‘Cars 2’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T19:51:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8746208-2125-5694-82b5-3c3a7c7c46f0', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8746208-2125-5694-82b5-3c3a7c7c46f0'}, {'abstract': 'Los Angeles Galaxy forward/midfielder Mike Magee found himself in an unfamiliar spot during Saturday’s match at San Jose — playing goalkeeper for more than half the game.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/and-in-other-soccer-action-the-galaxys-accidental-goalkeeper/', 'snippet': 'Los Angeles Galaxy forward/midfielder Mike Magee found himself in an unfamiliar spot during Saturday’s match at San Jose — playing goalkeeper for more than half the game.', 'lead_paragraph': '… And in other soccer action on Saturday … no one will ever mistake Mike Magee for one of the great goalkeepers of Europe, the United States or anyplace else guys wear grotesquely padded gloves, stand between two posts and try to stop powerful shots launched at their heads.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '… And in Other Soccer Action: The Galaxy’s Accidental Goalkeeper', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARENA, BRUCE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Galaxy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Soccer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Jose (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T20:01:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bd99292-eecf-5041-91bb-2a256ba6bda9', 'word_count': 982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bd99292-eecf-5041-91bb-2a256ba6bda9'}, {'abstract': 'When you are shopping for a camera memory card, faster isn’t always better because you  can end up overpaying for performance you can’t use. Here are some shopping tips.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/how-fast-should-your-cameras-memory-card-be/', 'snippet': 'When you are shopping for a camera memory card, faster isn’t always better because you  can end up overpaying for performance you can’t use. Here are some shopping tips.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you are shopping for a camera memory card, faster is better, right?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Fast Should Your Camera’s Memory Card Be?', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cameras', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T20:18:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f4dd8e4-9706-575d-b66b-e481d1b21902', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f4dd8e4-9706-575d-b66b-e481d1b21902'}, {'abstract': 'The Obama administration is deploying a team to pose as patients, call doctors’ offices and see how difficult it is to get care.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/health/policy/27docs.html', 'snippet': 'The Obama administration is deploying a team to pose as patients, call doctors’ offices and see how difficult it is to get care.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Alarmed by a shortage of primary care doctors, Obama administration officials are recruiting a team of “mystery shoppers” to pose as patients, call doctors’ offices and request appointments to see how difficult it is for people to get care when they need it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/us/JP-DOCS-1/JP-DOCS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 784, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/us/JP-DOCS-1/JP-DOCS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/27/us/JP-DOCS-1/JP-DOCS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Plans Stealth Survey on Access to Doctors', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stealth Survey To Test Access To Physicians', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T20:20:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Money & Policy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Pear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c48fae3-7734-5a2c-804e-b6ec4c86159f', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c48fae3-7734-5a2c-804e-b6ec4c86159f'}, {'abstract': 'Three well-known hedge fund managers, including Elliott Management’s Paul E. Singer, helped finance the campaign to legalize gay marriage in New York. But their support is not as surprising as it initially seems.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/the-financiers-who-helped-back-n-y-gay-marriage-campaign/', 'snippet': 'Three well-known hedge fund managers, including Elliott Management’s Paul E. Singer, helped finance the campaign to legalize gay marriage in New York. But their support is not as surprising as it initially seems.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Much of the attention around the legalization of same-sex marriage in New York has revolved around the back-room politicking involved: the deals, the cajoling, the organizing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Financiers Who Helped Back N.Y. Gay Marriage Campaign', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Asness, Clifford Scott', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Loeb, Daniel S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Singer, Paul E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Elliott Management Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T20:27:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b494234-b376-5faa-a32a-04ff4f6ae9de', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b494234-b376-5faa-a32a-04ff4f6ae9de'}, {'abstract': 'Daisy Goodwin’s novel is about a Gilded Age Newport belle who heads for England to marry her way into a title.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/books/the-american-heiress-by-daisy-goodwin-review.html', 'snippet': 'Daisy Goodwin’s novel is about a Gilded Age Newport belle who heads for England to marry her way into a title.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What should Daisy Goodwin have called her novel about a Gilded Age Newport belle who heads for England to marry her way into a title? It was published in Britain as “My Last Duchess,” since it makes abundant reference to the chilling Robert Browning poem of that name. Now, for American readers, it has been given a forgettably bland name: “The American Heiress.” A hybrid of the two, “The American Duchess,” would have better described what Ms. Goodwin’s sly, glittery period piece is about.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/SUBBOOK/SUBBOO