ne': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0972e92-31d8-53ed-964f-5bb0d5bc52de', 'word_count': 312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0972e92-31d8-53ed-964f-5bb0d5bc52de'}, {'abstract': 'At Carolines on Saturday night, Tracy Morgan mostly avoided the controversy about his antigay remarks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/arts/tracy-morgan-at-carolines-comedy-club.html', 'snippet': 'At Carolines on Saturday night, Tracy Morgan mostly avoided the controversy about his antigay remarks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About halfway through a raucous standup set on Saturday night in which he dispensed much unsolicited romantic advice and hit on several women seated near the stage, Tracy Morgan told the crowd at Carolines, the Times Square comedy club, he knew what it wanted to hear.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/JPTRACYMORGAN/JPTRACYMORGAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 331, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/JPTRACYMORGAN/JPTRACYMORGAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 331}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/JPTRACYMORGAN/JPTRACYMORGAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/JPTRACYMORGAN/JPTRACYMORGAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/JPTRACYMORGAN/JPTRACYMORGAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Comedian, Chastened, Gets Back to Laughs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Comedian, Chastened, Gets Back To Laughs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morgan, Tracy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T21:59:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86075c74-23cd-5fef-bd09-07a717513173', 'word_count': 1027, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86075c74-23cd-5fef-bd09-07a717513173'}, {'abstract': 'Time Magazine contributor Joel Stein tackles a debut by Joseph Samulak. That’s two reasons to party right there.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/stein/', 'snippet': 'Time Magazine contributor Joel Stein tackles a debut by Joseph Samulak. That’s two reasons to party right there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Administrivial Disappearing Comments Update: My apologies for the disappearing comments. I’ve found them, and wouldn’t you know, they’re always in the last place you look. A new system had been put in place and it was sending some of the comments on walkabout. After an epic battle, in which I wielded both power tools and my magic bullet-proof bracelets, that system has been shut down, so we should be back to normal. Thank you for your patience.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Celebrity Guest Solver Joel Stein', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T22:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a515e80c-fe24-568b-80a5-dfbeda0ae2ce', 'word_count': 1102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a515e80c-fe24-568b-80a5-dfbeda0ae2ce'}, {'abstract': 'The Bel Canto at Caramoor opera series opened with Gilbert and Sullivan’s “H.M.S. Pinafore,” stretching their mission to include this energetic comedy packed with popular choruses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/arts/music/hms-pinafore-at-caramoor-international-music-festival.html', 'snippet': 'The Bel Canto at Caramoor opera series opened with Gilbert and Sullivan’s “H.M.S. Pinafore,” stretching their mission to include this energetic comedy packed with popular choruses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KATONAH, N.Y. — What’s wrong with this picture? Since 1997 the Caramoor International Music Festival has presented an opera series devoted to the bel canto repertory, with an emphasis on Rossini, Donizetti, Bellini and Verdi. Neither its name (Bel Canto at Caramoor) nor its mission has changed, but when the series and the festival opened on Saturday evening, the work at hand was Gilbert and Sullivan’s “H.M.S. Pinafore or The Lass That Loved a Sailor.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/SUBPINAFORE/SUBPINAFORE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/SUBPINAFORE/SUBPINAFORE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/arts/SUBPINAFORE/SUBPINAFORE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 542, 'width': 373, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sailing a Saucy Ship Into Bel Canto Seas', 'kicker': "Music Review | 'H.M.S. Pinafore'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sailing a Saucy Ship Into Bel Canto Seas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilbert, William S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Arthur S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Caramoor International Music Festival', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T22:09:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0e9db1d-0f4c-5820-9de3-aa92db7f9a1b', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0e9db1d-0f4c-5820-9de3-aa92db7f9a1b'}, {'abstract': 'In which I solve the G.O.P.’s gay-marriage problem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/magazine/solving-the-gops-gay-marriage-problem.html', 'snippet': 'In which I solve the G.O.P.’s gay-marriage problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Supporters of same-sex marriage say gay people are entitled to be fully equal citizens, including in their yearning to make families. Opponents say the state has no business bestowing the blessings of marriage on those who do not fit the traditional template.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/magazine/mag-03ledepass-t_CA0/mag-03ledepass-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 828, 'width': 684, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/03/magazine/mag-03ledepass-t_CA0/mag-03ledepass-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/03/magazine/mag-03ledepass-t_CA0/mag-03ledepass-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Decent Proposal', 'kicker': 'First', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Decent Proposal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stolberg, Sheryl Gay', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T22:09:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47752494-0fc4-5c98-b445-1f1024afad67', 'word_count': 1174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47752494-0fc4-5c98-b445-1f1024afad67'}, {'abstract': 'Another day of runway looks, another Scorecard at your service.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/scorecard-raf-simons-wins-2/', 'snippet': 'Another day of runway looks, another Scorecard at your service.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a truism that in Paris suits are not for Suits, which Scorecard knows was exemplified Saturday by Kris Van Assche’s whiteout of superskinny-lapel jackets and dropped-crotch high-waters accessorized with wide-brimmed toppers on the Dior Homme runway. Too bad, then, that Raf Simons had to make it all look literally old hat with his witty display of that creative industry favorite — plaid — in a former bank headquarters. It might just be hip to be square.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scorecard | Raf Simons Wins!', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T22:10:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison S. Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0880e97c-c8af-578b-b822-2aba652ed870', 'word_count': 1016, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0880e97c-c8af-578b-b822-2aba652ed870'}, {'abstract': 'In Ontario, the Stratford Shakespeare Festival’s new revival of “Jesus Christ Superstar” has received rave reviews from local theater critics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/theater/raves-for-superstar.html', 'snippet': 'In Ontario, the Stratford Shakespeare Festival’s new revival of “Jesus Christ Superstar” has received rave reviews from local theater critics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the musicals “Sister Act” and “The Book of Mormon” now singing to the heavens on Broadway, and a revival of “Godspell” set to join them this fall, is there room in the congregation for a fourth musical with strong spiritual underpinnings? In Ontario, the Stratford Shakespeare Festival’s new revival of “Jesus Christ Superstar,” the 1971 rock opera by Andrew Lloyd Webber and Tim Rice about the last week of Jesus’ life, has received rave reviews from local theater critics, prompting New York producers and other critics to book flights to Toronto for a look. Mr. Lloyd Webber, who holds the rights to mount a Broadway revival, saw the production this month and called it “fabulous,” adding that it was “very probably the best acted performance of the show I have ever seen.” Mr. Rice has also seen it, adding to the buzz that the production might have a life beyond Stratford. The director, Des McAnuff, who is also artistic director of the festival, said in a telephone interview that “we’re definitely taking calls from New York and elsewhere about having a future elsewhere, but it’s very much an open question.” Asked about a transfer to Broadway, Mr. McAnuff said, “We would of course love to move this and love an opportunity to take our Stratford company members with us if we go.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Raves for ‘Superstar’', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Raves for ‘Superstar’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T22:11:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/add57213-e514-5396-8f24-6a18a1e82efe', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/add57213-e514-5396-8f24-6a18a1e82efe'}, {'abstract': 'The president of the cable channel AMC said he didn’t regret the cliffhanger ending of “The Killing” a week ago but admitted there was a lesson to be learned.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/arts/television/amc-chief-responds-to-killing-complaints.html', 'snippet': 'The president of the cable channel AMC said he didn’t regret the cliffhanger ending of “The Killing” a week ago but admitted there was a lesson to be learned.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The president of the cable channel AMC said he didn’t regret the cliffhanger ending of “The Killing” a week ago but admitted there was a lesson to be learned. “We underestimated the passion” viewers have for closure within a season, Charlie Collier  told Entertainment Weekly on Friday. “If I could do anything differently, it would be to manage expectations.” Many fans of “The Killing” (with Mireille Enos, above), which is about an investigation into the death of a teenage girl in Seattle, were left fuming when the first season ended on June 19 without an explanation for the crime. The anger was noticeable on social media Web sites, prompting Mr. Collier’s acknowledgment. “The audience has an important voice, we heard them and don’t take them for granted,” he said. Mr. Collier said he stood by the show’s executive producer, Veena Sud, who told the public radio show “The Business,” last week that the investigation would be resolved in Season 2. Ms. Sud recounted a conversation on one Web site that had a viewer declaring, “I’ll never ever come back for season two.” She said, “On the same thread three more people popped up and said: ‘I haven’t watched this show yet, but now I will because you guys are so emotional about it, it’s gotta be something to watch.’ So that’s a great thing.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'AMC Chief Responds to ‘Killing’ Complaints', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'AMC Chief Responds To ‘Killing’ Complaints', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T22:13:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c049cad9-9661-5e86-8548-10fb899f8b6f', 'word_count': 227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c049cad9-9661-5e86-8548-10fb899f8b6f'}, {'abstract': 'A new play by Catherine Trieschmann will headline the 2011-12 Women’s Project lineup, the theater company announced.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/theater/womens-project-announces-lineup.html', 'snippet': 'A new play by Catherine Trieschmann will headline the 2011-12 Women’s Project lineup, the theater company announced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new play by Catherine Trieschmann will headline the 2011-12 Women’s Project lineup, the theater company announced. “How the World Began” focuses on a teacher in rural Kansas whose comments about the origins of the universe have unforeseen consequences. Directed by Daniella Topol (“Dead City”), the play is the follow-up to the highly praised “Crooked,” Ms. Trieschmann’s previous collaboration with Women’s Project. The season will begin in October with Rebecca Taichman’s production of “Milk Like Sugar” by Kirsten Greenidge (“Bossa Nova”), followed by “How the World Began,” and “We Play for the Gods,” which features contributions from 16 artists, connecting theater history with the current vision of the Women’s Project Lab.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Women’s Project Announces Lineup', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Women’s Project Announces Lineup', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-26T22:14:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6ce6ec4-5735-5d27-abe6-e871f4d19448', 'word_count': 117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6ce6ec4-5735-5d27-abe6-e871f4d19448'}, {'abstract': 'On Tuesday, at the annual general meeting of the Tokyo Electric Power Company, a bloc of owners will try once again to shift the company away from atomic generation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/business/global/27tepco.html', 'snippet': 'On Tuesday, at the annual general meeting of the Tokyo Electric Power Company, a bloc of owners will try once again to shift the company away from atomic generation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Masafumi Asada bought shares of the Tokyo Electric Power Company almost a decade ago with a single purpose: To vote against the use of nuclear power.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/business/TEPCO1/TEPCO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/27/business/TEPCO1/TEPCO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/business/TEPCO1/TEPCO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height'