efense of Marriage Act, which denies federal recognition of same-sex marriages and allows any state to refuse to recognize another state’s unions.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gay Marriage: A Milestone', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Milestone for Gay Marriage: New York expands rights even with divided government', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T02:59:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c970555-df6c-5b72-95c1-3f92dea1e57d', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c970555-df6c-5b72-95c1-3f92dea1e57d'}, {'abstract': 'The second Monday of Wimbledon is a special day because all 32 remaining singles players are in action for fourth-round matches.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/26/a-monday-full-of-action-and-answers-at-wimbledon/', 'snippet': 'The second Monday of Wimbledon is a special day because all 32 remaining singles players are in action for fourth-round matches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The second Monday of Wimbledon is a special day because all 32 remaining singles players are in action. Christopher Clarey laid out many of the story lines for Week 2 at the All England Club. Who is your sentimental favorite — Roger Federer or Andy Murray? Which upstart do you want to see more off — Bernard Tomic or Sabine Lisicki? Which female star is closer to championship form — Maria Sharapova or Serena Williams? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Monday Full of Action and Answers at Wimbledon', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T03:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbd960c1-ba06-5eac-9d2a-f26b778c1e4f', 'word_count': 224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbd960c1-ba06-5eac-9d2a-f26b778c1e4f'}, {'abstract': 'The rest of the nation waits for the president to “evolve.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/opinion/27mon2.html', 'snippet': 'The rest of the nation waits for the president to “evolve.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thursday night, when same-sex marriage in New York State was teetering on a razor’s edge, President Obama had a perfect opportunity to show the results of his supposed evolution on gay marriage. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gay Marriage: Where’s Mr. Obama?', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Milestone for Gay Marriage: The rest of the nation waits for Mr. Obama to ‘evolve’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T03:01:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ae06ba0-b7e8-51a4-8205-31756578e7fb', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ae06ba0-b7e8-51a4-8205-31756578e7fb'}, {'abstract': 'Bahrain’s promise for a national dialogue after a brutal crackdown is hard to take seriously when many of the people who should be at the negotiating table are still in jail.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/opinion/27mon3.html', 'snippet': 'Bahrain’s promise for a national dialogue after a brutal crackdown is hard to take seriously when many of the people who should be at the negotiating table are still in jail.', 'lead_paragraph': 'King Hamad bin Isa al-Khalifa of Bahrain has promised to launch a national dialogue aimed at reconciling his country after a brutal crackdown on antigovernment protesters. It is hard to take this seriously — or see any real chance of success — when many of the people who should be at the negotiating table are still in jail.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where’s the Reconciliation?', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where’s the Reconciliation?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bahrain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': '