logy', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a81bc86e-9b32-5671-9dd4-23b0faa99515', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a81bc86e-9b32-5671-9dd4-23b0faa99515'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court agreed with a federal court’s decision to throw out California’s ban on the sale or rental of violent video games to minors, saying the law violated minors’ rights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/us/28scotus.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court agreed with a federal court’s decision to throw out California’s ban on the sale or rental of violent video games to minors, saying the law violated minors’ rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Supreme Court on Monday struck down on First Amendment grounds a California law that banned the sale of violent video games to children. The 7-to-2 decision was the latest in a series of rulings protecting free speech, joining ones on funeral protests, videos showing cruelty to animals and political speech by corporations.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justices Reject Ban on Violent Video Games for Children', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Minors Can Buy Violent Games, Justices Decide', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Violence (In Media and Entertainment)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T14:40:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52c2da04-3bbd-54fb-9e93-e97f8e8b7f81', 'word_count': 1094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52c2da04-3bbd-54fb-9e93-e97f8e8b7f81'}, {'abstract': 'Philip Roth, the prolific and ornery novelist, said he no longer has “the same interest in fiction that I once did.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/philip-roth-no-longer-reading-fiction/', 'snippet': 'Philip Roth, the prolific and ornery novelist, said he no longer has “the same interest in fiction that I once did.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whether you’re interested in Philip Roth’s critical exegesis of “A Visit From the Goon Squad,” his contrarian take on “Freedom” or just curious to know what he thinks of “The Girl With the Dragon Tattoo,” his answer is likely to be the same: He probably hasn’t read them because he is no longer reading fiction at all. In an interview with The Financial Times, Mr. Roth, the ornery author more than two dozen novels (the FT credits him with 53 books) including “Portnoy’s Complaint,” “The Human Stain” and “Nemesis,” said that other people’s literary inventions are no longer part of his diet. “I’ve stopped reading fiction,” he said. “I don’t read it at all. I read other things: history, biography. I don’t have the same interest in fiction that I once did.” Asked why he came to this position, Mr. Roth said: “I don’t know. I wised up.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Philip Roth No Longer Reading Fiction', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Man Booker International Prize', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Man Booker Prize', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roth, Philip', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Times', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T14:43:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13a5fb2f-d317-5706-8495-ac6a7987d83e', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13a5fb2f-d317-5706-8495-ac6a7987d83e'}, {'abstract': 'The GSV Capital Corporation, a publicly traded investment firm, has bought 225,000 shares in Facebook at a $70 billion valuation.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/gsv-capital-joins-facebook-party/', 'snippet': 'The GSV Capital Corporation, a publicly traded investment firm, has bought 225,000 shares in Facebook at a $70 billion valuation.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The GSV Capital Corporation, a publicly traded investment firm, has officially punched its ticket to Facebook’s I.P.O.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'GSV Capital Joins Facebook Party', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nasdaq Composite Index', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook.com', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T14:44:01+00