 {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76b7cefe-603e-5eee-b968-bd83cc221cd5', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76b7cefe-603e-5eee-b968-bd83cc221cd5'}, {'abstract': 'The work in question, by a 17th-century Dutch painter, once belonged to an art dealer who was killed at Auschwitz; the painting was likely seized by the Nazis.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/museum-of-fine-arts-reaches-restitution-deal-to-keep-painting/', 'snippet': 'The work in question, by a 17th-century Dutch painter, once belonged to an art dealer who was killed at Auschwitz; the painting was likely seized by the Nazis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Museum of Fine Arts in Boston said it would pay an unspecified amount to the heir of an art dealer who was killed at Auschwitz and will keep a 17th-century Dutch painting in its collection that was once owned by the dealer and likely seized by the Nazis. The Boston Globe reported that after a lengthy investigation into the work, an oil portrait by the Dutch painter Eglon van der Neer, the museum determined that it had belonged to Walter Westfeld, who had run a German gallery shut down by the Nazis in 1936 and who was sent to Auschwitz in 1943. The museum, which purchased the painting in 1941, posted an image of it online in 2000 amid concerns about its provenance; in 2004 it was contacted by Fred Westfield, a nephew of the art dealer. Mr. Westfield, a retired economics professor in Tennessee, learned about the painting from a Google search. The Globe said that the museum had in recent years returned other works stolen during World War II. It also fought a legal battle to keep the Oskar Kokoschka painting “Two Nudes (Lovers)”; a federal appeals court ruled last year that it was the rightful owner of this work.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Museum of Fine Arts Reaches Restitution Deal to Keep Painting', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T16:45:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3abdc6d9-8138-58f6-a24c-75b17c1a96d6', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3abdc6d9-8138-58f6-a24c-75b17c1a96d6'}, {'abstract': 'People still think that income doesn’t need to be greater than expenses. Can you guess why?', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/the-new-american-money-math/', 'snippet': 'People still think that income doesn’t need to be greater than expenses. Can you guess why?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carl Richards is a certified financial planner in Park City, Utah. His sketches are archived here on the Bucks blog and on his personal Web site, BehaviorGap.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The New American Money Math', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T16:47:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Richards', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richards', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f003a01-7b52-5149-93e1-55528b1210d8', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f003a01-7b52-5149-93e1-55528b1210d8'}, {'abstract': "Set designer Kevin O'Callaghan puts the icing on the cake for the cover of the June 26th issue of the Sunday Magazine.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/magazine/100000000883297/creating-a-cake-for-jeter.html', 'snippet': "Set designer Kevin O'Callaghan puts the icing on the cake for the cover of the June 26th issue of the Sunday Magazine.", 'lead_paragraph': "Set designer Kevin O'Callaghan puts the icing on the cake for the cover of the June 26th issue of the Sunday Magazine.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/magazine/video-creatingacakeforjeter/video-creatingacakeforjeter-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/27/magazine/video-creatingacakeforjeter/video-creatingacakeforjeter-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/27/magazine/video-creatingacakeforjeter/video-creatingacakeforjeter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/27/magazine/video-creatingacakeforjeter/video-creatingacakeforjeter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Creating a Cake For Jeter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T16:49:39+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Arem Duplessis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Duplessis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/e49a9e9c-d132-5c7f-82a6-460a6c79b11d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/e49a9e9c-d132-5c7f-82a6-460a6c79b11d'}, {'abstract': 'I’m not doing it maliciously or even consciously, but here are some of the things that drive my optimistic performance projections.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/why-i-tend-to-project-a-little-high/', 'snippet': 'I’m not doing it maliciously or even consciously, but here are some of the things that drive my optimistic performance projections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have a confession to make: I am a chronic over-projector. This fact has even been reported several times by The New York Times. In May 2008 I was quoted predicting that our revenue would be “an estimated $8.5 million this year.” We actually finished the year at $6.6 million. Even as recently as June 2010, I thought we’d hit sales of “$16 million in 2010.” We finished the year at $13.5 million.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why I Tend to Project a Little High', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TerraCycle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T17:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Szaky', 'person': [{'firstname'