 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wolves', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T20:41:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/498001e9-96f5-53f2-91ef-37de95758d87', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/498001e9-96f5-53f2-91ef-37de95758d87'}, {'abstract': 'Maria Sharapova’s big serve and fearless return put enormous pressure on her opponent.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/sharapovas-ruthless-style-helps-her-advance/', 'snippet': 'Maria Sharapova’s big serve and fearless return put enormous pressure on her opponent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maria Sharapova dominates matches with her big first serve and her fearless return. She excels in the first two shots of each point, putting enormous pressure on her opponent with her unbridled aggression. On Monday she gave Shuai Peng a lesson in power tennis on grass, winning, 6-4, 6-2, to advance to the Wimbledon quarterfinals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharapova’s Ruthless Style Helps Her Advance', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharapova, Maria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T20:44:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e438f856-5484-59f2-80f4-f05f3c46b8a2', 'word_count': 437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e438f856-5484-59f2-80f4-f05f3c46b8a2'}, {'abstract': 'As is the case with most rock criticism, the text usually reveals more about the author than the subject. So let’s play the Bon Iver Rorschach Test!', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/the-bon-iver-review-quiz/', 'snippet': 'As is the case with most rock criticism, the text usually reveals more about the author than the subject. So let’s play the Bon Iver Rorschach Test!', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Jon Caramanica made his first trip to Eau Claire, Wisc., to interview Justin Vernon for a profile in the magazine, the second Bon Iver record wasn’t even mastered. So it’s a relief that the reviews have proved to be mostly positive. (You don’t want to put all your energy into the Peter Frampton profile to coincide with the release of “I’m in You.” Not that the first Bon Iver record, “For Emma, Forever Ago,” is exactly analogous to “Frampton Comes Alive!” But you get the picture.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Bon Iver Review Quiz', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vernon, Justin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bon Iver', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T20:48:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Ferguson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ferguson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c93c913d-a3d1-5d7c-8f93-ac910aab0d66', 'word_count': 436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c93c913d-a3d1-5d7c-8f93-ac910aab0d66'}, {'abstract': 'It’s no surprise that trenches are a mainstay of the fall collections, but seeing them at the resort shows reminds us that they’re a fashion staple.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/wear-resort-now-trench-coats/', 'snippet': 'It’s no surprise that trenches are a mainstay of the fall collections, but seeing them at the resort shows reminds us that they’re a fashion staple.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s no surprise that trenches are a mainstay of the fall collections, but seeing them at the resort shows reminds us that they’re a fashion staple. This resort season, Bibhu Mohapatra and Mulberry showed classic khaki, while Lanvin and Nina Ricci favored glamour over utility, casting their versions of the iconic topper in neutral silks. At the prefall collections designers like Marni, Céline and Emilio Pucci reinvented the staple with patent leather, shorter hems and tweed. These not-so-basic basics are worth the investment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wear Resort Now | Trench Coats', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-27T20:52:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily Miesmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miesmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/704bf780-7dfb-522c-a236-3afa985ed957', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/704bf780-7dfb-522c-a236-3afa985ed957'}, {'abstract': 'The end of a deadly disease, dispelling myths about math and the truth about sunscreen', 'web_url': 'ht