bject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Perjury', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T00:40:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Eligon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de70c7ff-d678-50c0-bcb3-c796bc5d15ca', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de70c7ff-d678-50c0-bcb3-c796bc5d15ca'}, {'abstract': 'In indoor football leagues across the country, where small crowds and tiny paychecks are the norm, players continue to chase a modest dream in which Arena Football represents the major leagues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/sports/football/staying-in-the-game-on-footballs-fringe.html', 'snippet': 'In indoor football leagues across the country, where small crowds and tiny paychecks are the norm, players continue to chase a modest dream in which Arena Football represents the major leagues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BETHLEHEM, Pa. — The Lehigh Valley Steelhawks play football in a nearly empty college basketball arena. As players slam opponents onto a playing surface with all the protection of a patio carpet and crash into barely padded plywood walls, the few hundred fans blow plastic trumpets, ring cowbells and thump on seatbacks. The team’s general manager is also its announcer. End-of-quarter entertainment is a family of four playing musical chairs at midfield to a Beastie Boys song.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/28/sports/INDOOR5/INDOOR5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 331, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'le