 its fourth month in the NATO-led military operations in Libya, and questions persist about whether the president has overstepped his authority.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The overwhelming vote in the House on Friday against approving American participation in the NATO action in Libya raised a question in Washington: Why did President Obama not press harder for a vote authorizing the operation months ago when the intervention was more popular?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Timing Is Questioned in Seeking House Vote on U.S. Role in Libya', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vote in Congress Reveals Dissent On Role in Libya', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Powers Act (1973)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T01:22:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage and David E. Sanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Sanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fb4ca34-e51b-5989-84cd-b22f7e47cbd5', 'word_count': 833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fb4ca34-e51b-5989-84cd-b22f7e47cbd5'}, {'abstract': 'All 32 players left in the men’s and women’s singles draws were in action on Day 7, including six who have a combined 51 major titles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/sports/tennis/wimbledon-2011-so-much-ground-to-cover-on-day-7.html', 'snippet': 'All 32 players left in the men’s and women’s singles draws were in action on Day 7, including six who have a combined 51 major titles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — Petra Cetkovska took a bathroom break after she dropped a first-set tie breaker to Sabine Lisicki on Monday, sending some fans watching their fourth-round match into a tizzy. A few had waited in line in dehydrating heat for their unreserved seats for the first fourth-round match of the day on Court 12. Did they really want to sit idly watching the grass grow, even for a few minutes, when so much was happening at Wimbledon? ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Day of Drama, 16 Matches Produce 16 Quarterfinalists', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Now Playing: Everyone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Venus', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Serena', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wozniacki, Caroline', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T01:26:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5895260-cbf6-5c76-a14f-c111803289dc', 'word_count': 1060, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5895260-cbf6-5c76-a14f-c111803289dc'}, {'abstract': 'In addition to forbidding federal recognition of same-sex marriage, DOMA says that no state can be forced to acknowledge a same-sex couple’s marriage from another state.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/us/politics/28doma.html', 'snippet': 'In addition to forbidding federal recognition of same-sex marriage, DOMA says that no state can be forced to acknowledge a same-sex couple’s marriage from another state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York’s new law allowing same-sex marriage could bring renewed focus to an embattled federal law that says other states do not have to recognize such unions.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/28/us/SUB-DOMA/SUB-DOMA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/28/us/SUB-DOMA/SUB-DOMA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/28/us/SUB-DOMA/SUB-DOMA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After New York, New Look at Defense of Marriage Act', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After New York, New Look at Defense of Marriage Act', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense of Marriage Act (1996)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T01:28:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6aa3e7e3-2499-5f01-9813-9fd71e11bf12', 'word_count': 917, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6aa3e7e3-2499-5f01-9813-9fd71e11bf12'}, {'abstract': 'How the Civil War altered life for free and enslaved blacks in Georgia’s most important city.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/27/change-comes-to-savannah/', 'snippet': 'How the Civil War altered life for free and enslaved blacks in Georgia’s most important city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though the river port of Savannah, Ga., lay hundreds of miles from the burgeoning hostilities in Virginia, by the summer of 1861 it was already registering the effects of the Civil War. Like the rest of the Confederacy, Savannah quickly mobilized for war and, as the conflict wore on, came to reflect the wrenching changes that the Confederacy’s bid for independence impressed upon all of Southern society — and nowhere more so than in its population of slaves and free blacks. Indeed, by starting a war to preserve slavery and to celebrate the ties that bound together all whites, the Confederates inadvertently put forces in motion that would begin to undermine that very project. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Change Comes to Savannah', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Savannah (Ga)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T01:29:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacqueline Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacqueline', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jones', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bf0ed8e-5289-52c8-9d18-0eee2faee27f', 'word_count': 1317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bf0ed8e-5289-52c8-9d18-0eee2faee27f'}, {'abstract': 'The lawyers for Kenneth Moreno and Franklin Mata, acquitted of felony rape but convicted of misdemeanor charges, will reportedly request time to examine material from a recent documentary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/nyregion/sentencing-delay-likely-in-east-village-police-rape-case.html', 'snippet': 'The lawyers for Kenneth Moreno and Franklin Mata, acquitted of felony rape but convicted of misdemeanor charges, will reportedly request time to examine material from a recent documentary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The sentencing of two former New York police officers convicted in May of misdemeanor official misconduct but acquitted of felony rape is likely to be postponed on Tuesday so defense lawyers can file motions and review evidence. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sentencing Delay Likely in Police Rape Case', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lawyers Seek To Postpone Sentencing Of Ex-Officers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mata, Franklin L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moreno, Kenneth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T01:29:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Eligon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7214d29c-f1ae-57c3-96b4-3b31962674f6', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7214d29c-f1ae-57c3-96b4-3b31962674f6'}, {'abstract': 'The G-20 summit meeting on soaring food prices failed to do what is needed, including putting an end to government supports for biofuel production.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/opinion/28tue2.html', 'snippet': 'The G-20 summit meeting on soaring food prices failed to do what is needed, including putting an end to government supports for biofuel production.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The price of agricultural commodities has surged by more than a third over the past year — cereal prices by 70 percent — surpassing even the levels that sparked widespread food riots in 2008. According to the World Bank, the rise in prices pushed 44 million more people into hunger in the second half of 2010. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lots of Talk, Too Little Action', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lots of Talk, Too Little Action: The G-20 summit meeting on soaring food prices failed to do what is needed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biofuels', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethanol', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Group of Twenty', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shortages', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T01:35:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2918ef5c-4e32-5d46-9126-7b292177e473', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2918ef5c-4e32-5d46-9126-7b292177e473'}, {'abstract': 'An Iranian colonel was quoted on state television as saying that missiles were “ready to hit the predetermined targets.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/world/middleeast/28iran.html', 'snippet': 'An Iranian colonel was quoted on state television as saying that missiles were “ready to hit the predetermined targets.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Iran unveiled underground silos on Monday that would make its missiles less vulnerable to attack, marking the country’s latest show of force in the long standoff with the West over its nuclear program.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran Unveils Missile Silos as It Begins War Games', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran Unveils Missile Silos As It Begins War Games', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Missiles and Missile Defense Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T01:38:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By William J. Broad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Broad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9734b0a0-74ca-58bb-88f3-02fe39ec2422', 'word_count': 518, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9734b0a0-74ca-58bb-88f3-02fe39ec2422'}, {'abstract': 'Tanzania’s change of plans for a road across Serengeti is too ambiguous. The government should declare its unequivocal commitment to protect the park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/opinion/28tue3.html', 'snippet': 'Tanzania’s change of plans for a road across Serengeti is too ambiguous. The government should declare its unequivocal commitment to protect the park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It would be nice to be able to praise the Tanzanian government and President Jakaya Kikwete for dropping plans to build a road across the northern section of Serengeti National Park. The road, about 32 miles long, would have cut across one of the planet’s major migratory corridors — used by great herds of wildebeest and other animals — and one of the last of its kind on the planet. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Gravel Road Too Far', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Gravel Road Too Far', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tanzania', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Serengeti National Park (Tanzania)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T01:41:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e37c2a01-8bb5-531f-b0fb-f977fded4ac5', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e37c2a01-8bb5-531f-b0fb-f977fded4ac5'}, {'abstract': 'Representative Lynn Woolsey, a liberal Democrat from Northern California, and Representative Jay Inslee, a Seattle-area Democrat have plans to leave Congress.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/us/politics/28brfs-TWOVETERANDE_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Representative Lynn Woolsey, a liberal Democrat from Northern California, and Representative Jay Inslee, a Seattle-area Democrat have plans to leave Congress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Representative Lynn Woolsey, a liberal Democrat from Northern California, said Monday that she would leave Congress at the end of her 10th term next year, one of two veteran Democrats to reveal a change in political plans this week. Ms. Woolsey, 73, has been a leader of the Congressional Progressive Caucus and an antiwar advocate. Representative Jay Inslee, a Seattle-area Democrat, on Monday formally began his bid to be governor of Washington after seven House terms.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Veteran Democrats Leaving', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | WASHINGTON', 'con