tion_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Liss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23d74717-fbdc-599a-83f7-22bc4ac152b1', 'word_count': 1102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23d74717-fbdc-599a-83f7-22bc4ac152b1'}, {'abstract': 'A documentary of Conan’s life post-NBC, “Conan O’Brien Can’t Stop,” reveals that a payout of $45 million doesn’t make the pain of losing “The Tonight Show” go away.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/conan-the-talk-show-host/', 'snippet': 'A documentary of Conan’s life post-NBC, “Conan O’Brien Can’t Stop,” reveals that a payout of $45 million doesn’t make the pain of losing “The Tonight Show” go away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Conan O’Brien’s star was in the ascendant, he appeared on the cover of The Times Magazine. For all the deserved celebration of his talents, Lynn Hirschberg’s in-depth profile contained an intimation of what was to come: “While O’Brien was concentrating\xa0on his final show and his plans for ‘The Tonight Show,’\xa0Jay Leno was becoming more and more unhappy about the idea of retiring from late-night television.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Conan! The Talk-Show Host', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Brien, Conan", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T10:34:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Glaser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glaser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b05bb42-3b92-5aeb-b47b-d0d847ec5fec', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b05bb42-3b92-5aeb-b47b-d0d847ec5fec'}, {'abstract': 'Life is like a tin of sardines.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/but-my-brother-esau-is-an-hairy-man/', 'snippet': 'Life is like a tin of sardines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We went to evensong at Norwich Cathedral, which was beautiful. Also included was a sermon by the bishop, which did not actually sound very much like this:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'But My Brother Esau Is An Hairy Man', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T10:56:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2d0815d-7053-5e89-aeaf-0c020a4f2051', 'word_count': 27, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2d0815d-7053-5e89-aeaf-0c020a4f2051'}, {'abstract': 'Partying like it’s 1934.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/the-old-superstition/', 'snippet': 'Partying like it’s 1934.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I picked up a copy of Lionel Robbins’s 1934 book The Great Depression in a used book shop in Norwich. It’s quite revealing: judicious in tone, full of tables and facts, clearly meant to be seen as the work of a wise observer – indeed, a Very Serious Person.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Old Superstition', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, '