o the rise of Hitler.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On June 28, 1919, Germany signed the Treaty of Versailles, officially ending its involvement in World War I. The treaty subjected Germany to a number of harsh penalties and restrictions that many historians believe contributed to the rise of Adolf Hitler.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'June 28, 1919 | Germany Signs Treaty of Versailles', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T15:50:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6dc1d60-b8a2-587a-93f0-6acc28f21d07', 'word_count': 513, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6dc1d60-b8a2-587a-93f0-6acc28f21d07'}, {'abstract': 'Are parents in other countries “torn.”', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/balancing-life-and-work-around-the-world/', 'snippet': 'Are parents in other countries “torn.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Motherlode Book Club conversation about “Torn,” Lisa Tomanelli, a reader from New York, raises a question that is central to the dilemma of work/life balance.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Balancing Life and Work Around the World', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Work-Life Balance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T15:52:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role'