und strokes, heat, humidity and rain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A view of the day in sports, with goals and ground strokes, heat, humidity and rain.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/28/sports/20110628-photoreplay-slide-SHSX/20110628-photoreplay-slide-SHSX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 662, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/28/sports/20110628-photoreplay-slide-SHSX/20110628-photoreplay-slide-SHSX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/28/sports/20110628-photoreplay-slide-SHSX/20110628-photoreplay-slide-SHSX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Photo Replay: June 28', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Women's World Cup 2011 (Soccer)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Mavericks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nowitzki, Dirk', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup (Soccer)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T17:24:18+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/af4d07f6-523e-5ed4-a1c3-0315b1780422', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/af4d07f6-523e-5ed4-a1c3-0315b1780422'}, {'abstract': 'Ai Weiwei earned his freedom in part by confessing a failure to pay a “huge amount” of taxes and for willfully destroying financial documents, according to the state media.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/world/asia/29artist.html', 'snippet': 'Ai Weiwei earned his freedom in part by confessing a failure to pay a “huge amount” of taxes and for willfully destroying financial documents, according to the state media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Ai Weiwei, the artist and government critic who was released last week after nearly three months in police custody, is facing almost $2 million in fines and unpaid taxes, his mother and an associate said Tuesday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/world/ARTIST/ARTIST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/world/ARTIST/ARTIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/29/world/ARTIST/ARTIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China Said to Seek Back Taxes From Artist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China Said to Seek Back Taxes From Artist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ai Weiwei', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Evasion', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T17:24:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb00e0b7-86ea-5067-b446-8f165f3f55ff', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb00e0b7-86ea-5067-b446-8f165f3f55ff'}, {'abstract': 'While industry and rider associations advocate compromise with lawmakers, some motorcyclists feel their interests are not being represented.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/on-noise-standards-motorcyclists-split/', 'snippet': 'While industry and rider associations advocate compromise with lawmakers, some motorcyclists feel their interests are not being represented.', 'lead_paragraph': '3:12 p.m. | Updated Citizen groups have long agitated for ordinances and laws to curb the noise generated by motorcycles. With the help of some unlikely bedfellows, including the Motorcycle Industry Council and the American Motorcyclist Association, the groups are gaining ground.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Noise Standards, Motorcyclists Split', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Motorcycles, Motor Bikes and Motorscooters', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Noise', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harley-Davidson Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Motorcycle Industry Council', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maine', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T17:26:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0e4bd7e-3c96-54e1-8b35-f90472117e48', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0e4bd7e-3c96-54e1-8b35-f90472117e48'}, {'abstract': 'The Food and Drug Administration is holding a two-day hearing during which Genentech is appealing the agency’s recommendation to withdraw the use of Avastin for breast cancer.', 'web_url': 'https://prescriptions.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/cancer-survivors-appeal-to-f-d-a-over-avastin/', 'snippet': 'The Food and Drug Administration is holding a two-day hearing during which Genentech is appealing the agency’s recommendation to withdraw the use of Avastin for breast cancer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About a dozen women with breast cancer, some tearful, beseeched the Food and Drug Administration on Tuesday to retain the approval of the drug Avastin as a treatment for the disease.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cancer Survivors Appeal to F.D.A. Over Avastin', 'kicker': 'Prescriptions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Avastin (Drug)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breast Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Genentech Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T17:27:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Pollack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pollack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d533c28-089d-5839-94ea-641074bd4537', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d533c28-089d-5839-94ea-641074bd4537'}, {'abstract': 'Caetano Veloso will help to pick and present the films for the 38th Telluride Film Festival, which will be held over the Labor Day weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/telluride-film-festival-taps-a-singing-movie-lover/', 'snippet': 'Caetano Veloso will help to pick and present the films for the 38th Telluride Film Festival, which will be held over the Labor Day weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brazilian singer-songwriter Caetano Veloso has agreed to step into an unfamiliar role, that of “guest director” helping to pick and present the films for the 38th Telluride Film Festival, which will be held over the Labor Day weekend. A confessed cinephile, Mr. Veloso has composed soundtracks for movies, performed in his friend Pedro Almodóvar’s “Talk to Her,” written essays about his love for Italian neo-realist films and even recorded a CD tribute to Federico Fellini and Giulietta Masina.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Telluride Film Festival Taps a Singing Movie Lover', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Veloso, Caetano', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Telluride (Colo)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T17:30:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93148fea-1a1d-56bb-905a-d9d0d1244613', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93148fea-1a1d-56bb-905a-d9d0d1244613'}, {'abstract': 'What’s most interesting about the Supreme Court’s ruling on California’s ban on the sale of violent video games to children is the different thinking among the justices about the impact of technology on children, and the implications for other important clashes over teenagers’ free-speech rights that are percolating in the lower courts.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/technology-free-speech-and-children/', 'snippet': 'What’s most interesting about the Supreme Court’s ruling on California’s ban on the sale of violent video games to children is the different thinking among the justices about the impact of technology on children, and the implications for other imp...', 'lead_paragraph': 'When my column about technology and children was published on Sunday, the Supreme Court had not yet ruled on California’s ban on the sale of violent video games to children under the age of 18. Yesterday as expected, the Supreme Court struck down the ban. It was a 7 to 2 decision, but that suggests greater agreement than there really was, as Adam Liptak maps out. What’s most interesting about the fissures on the court is the different thinking among the justices about the impact of technology on children, and the implications for other important clashes over teenagers’ free-speech rights that are percolating in the lower courts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Technology, Free Speech and Children', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Violence (In Media and Entertainment)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T17:35:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Bazelon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bazelon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1665a2b-5601-55d8-87c3-09855d72e961', 'word_count': 690, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1665a2b-5601-55d8-87c3-09855d72e961'}, {'abstract': 'We’re collaborating with SnagLearning to bring you film festivals of classroom-worthy documentaries. Today’s theme is history. Tell us what you think!', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/film-festival-documentaries-about-history/', 'snippet': 'We’re collaborating with SnagLearning to bring you film festivals of classroom-worthy documentaries. Today’s theme is history. Tell us what you think!', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our summer experiments continue! Last week: Historic Headlines. This week: A collaboration with SnagLearning to bring you “film festivals” of classroom-worthy documentaries, each day on a different theme. Monday’s collection was about technology, and today we’re featuring five films about history. The whole collection is available here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Film Festival | Documentaries About History', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T17:45:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten and Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75d231ca-4783-5fe7-8a39-b41bcebe3d96', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75d231ca-4783-5fe7-8a39-b41bcebe3d96'}, {'abstract': 'Lady Gaga’s online empire expands to Tumblr.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/the-gaga-effect-spreads-to-tumblr/', 'snippet': 'Lady Gaga’s online empire expands to Tumblr.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is there any technology platform that Lady Gaga, the eccentric and entertaining pop songstress, can’t dominate? She’s already appeared in a commercial for Google Chrome, started her own game on FarmVille, crashed Amazon’s servers and partnered with companies like Gilt and Polaroid to release special-edition products. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gaga Effect Spreads to Tumblr', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lady Gaga', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tumblr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T17:46:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85c5a079-0888-5faa-b076-7b8b3348a963', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85c5a079-0888-5faa-b076-7b8b3348a963'}, {'abstract': 'The government moved to drop a racketeering case against James Bulger, saying it wanted to focus on the 19 counts of murder he is charged with.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/us/29bulger.html', 'snippet': 'The government moved to drop a racketeering case against James Bulger, saying it wanted to focus on the 19 counts of murder he is charged with.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — The federal government moved on Tuesday to drop a racketeering case against the longtime fugitive James (Whitey) Bulger, saying it wanted to focus on a separate case that charges him with 19 murders, among other crimes. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/28/us/BULGER/BULGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/28/us/BULGER/BULGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Some Charges May Be Dropped in Bulger Case as Prosecutors Focus on Killings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Some Charges May Be Dropped in Bulger Case as Prosecutors Focus on Killings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bulger, James J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Racketeering and Racketeers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T17:47:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Goodnough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodnough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdc323ba-76b9-5fd3-8849-e3d112b5cf7a', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdc323ba-76b9-5fd3-8849-e3d112b5cf7a'}, {'abstract': 'ABC’s reality competition “The Bachelorette” posted its best ratings in three weeks on Monday, delivering the night’s largest audience with 8 million viewers.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/bachelorette-seeking-love-wins-eyes/', 'snippet': 'ABC’s reality competition “The Bachelorette” posted its best ratings in three weeks on Monday, delivering the night’s largest audience with 8 million viewers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ABC’s reality competition “The Bachelorette” posted its best ratings in three weeks on Monday, delivering the night’s largest audience with eight million viewers from 8 to 10 p.m., according to Nielsen’s estimates. The solid ratings helped ABC extended its winning streak to 16 weeks as the top-rated network on Monday night. NBC ranked second as “Law & Order: LA” finished in a tie with ABC’s “Extreme Makeover: Weight Loss Edition” at 10 p.m., each attracting 6.4 million viewers. Earlier, NBC’s rerun of “America’s Got Talent,” at 8 p.m., garnered 5.8 million viewers and “Law & Order: Criminal Intent” drew 4.8 million at 9, less than the five million viewers who watched a new episode of Fox’s “MasterChef” in the same time slot. For the night, however, Fox lagged behind CBS, which placed third with reruns of its sitcom line-up followed by a “Hawaii Five-0” repeat at 10 p.m. (5.8 million). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Bachelorette,’ Seeking Love, Wins Eyes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ratings and Rating Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'na