'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wall Street (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T18:21:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli, Andrew Ross Sorkin and Mac William Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e05bddaf-ca1b-5d81-91b4-354ec0437b2d', 'word_count': 38, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e05bddaf-ca1b-5d81-91b4-354ec0437b2d'}, {'abstract': 'A pristine example of the rare microcar resides in rural Tennessee.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/despite-star-turn-in-cars-2-a-zundapp-janus-stays-humble/', 'snippet': 'A pristine example of the rare microcar resides in rural Tennessee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For enthusiasts of obscure midcentury car design, the star of the movie “Cars 2″ may be the villainous Professor Z, based on the Zündapp Janus of 1957 and 1958. The stubby vehicle, built by a traditional motorcycle maker, appears to have two front ends; it’s the automotive equivalent of Dr. Dolittle’s Pushmi-Pullyu.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Despite Star Turn in ‘Cars 2,’ a Zündapp Janus Stays Humble', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antique and Classic Cars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T18:29:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Patton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48089e3b-65c6-5352-8303-0edac9b61c60', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48089e3b-65c6-5352-8303-0edac9b61c60'}, {'abstract': 'Designed by the Campana brothers, the New Hotel opens this week in the Greek capital, where good news is in short supply.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/a-tree-grows-in-athens/', 'snippet': 'Designed by the Campana brothers, the New Hotel opens this week in the Greek capital, where good news is in short supply.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Athens is in trouble. The boutiques on the posh shopping streets of Kolonaki are empty. Constitution Square is a landfill of protest placards. And the normally cheery summer afternoon ritual — an iced Nescafé paired with half a pack of cigarettes — has become an exercise in existential brooding. “It makes me so sad,” Stefania Flenga, an executive for the boutique hotel chain Yes! Hotels, said while surveying the slow simmer of civil unrest on view in front of the Greek parliament. She was no doubt thinking about how all these disturbances are bad for the tourism industry, which accounts for almost 18 percent of the Greek economy. “They’re shooting themselves in the foot,” she said of the picketers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tree Grows in Athens', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T18:34:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/298e07c4-c420-5541-bc84-a4cca1b2635c', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/298e07c4-c420-5541-bc84-a4cca1b2635c'}, {'abstract': 'Sudan’s government and southern-allied opposition forces in northern Sudan signed a framework agreement on Tuesday that called for the two sides to govern contested border states together.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/world/africa/29sudan.html', 'snippet': 'Sudan’s government and southern-allied opposition forces in northern Sudan signed a framework agreement on Tuesday that called for the two sides to govern contested border states together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JUBA, Sudan — Sudan’s government and southern-allied opposition forces in northern Sudan signed a framework agreement on Tuesday that called for the two sides to govern contested border states together and for opposition fighters to be absorbed into Sudan’s national army.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sudan Signs Pact With Opposition Forces', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sudan Signs An Accord With Rebels', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sudan People's Liberation Army", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Treaties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War and Guerrilla Warfare', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sudan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T18:36:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Gettleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gettleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfe1e03a-f5fc-5689-af12-b1ac865d80c6', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfe1e03a-f5fc-5689-af12-b1ac865d80c6'}, {'abstract': 'The legal adviser to the State Department said the administration might have been better served by consulting with Congress on Libya.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/us/politics/29powers.html', 'snippet': 'The legal adviser to the State Department said the administration might have been better served by consulting with Congress on Libya.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — A resolution authorizing American intervention in Libya was approved on Tuesday by the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, hours after members skeptically grilled the administration’s legal adviser over his assertion that airstrikes and other military measures did not amount to hostilities. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/world/POWERS/POWERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 718, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Adviser Defends Libya Policy to Senate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Senate Panel Votes in Favor Of U.S. Measures in Libya', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Powers Act (1973)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koh, Harold Hongju', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate Committee on Foreign Relations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T18:37:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4150ca48-2317-57a0-9feb-ecfd051463b7', 'word_count': 800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4150ca48-2317-57a0-9feb-ecfd051463b7'}, {'abstract': 'Opening day at a pool in the Bronx will feature an appearance by Lee Mazzilli, billed as a former Yankee. A Yankee he was, but some New Yorkers will always picture him in a different uniform.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/a-yankee-for-a-moment-but-a-met-forever/', 'snippet': 'Opening day at a pool in the Bronx will feature an appearance by Lee Mazzilli, billed as a former Yankee. A Yankee he was, but some New Yorkers will always picture him in a different uniform.', 'lead_paragraph': 'City Room was thrilled to receive a news release from the Department of Parks and Recreation on Tuesday afternoon announcing that all 54 outdoor city pools will open to the public on Wednesday. Our excitement, however, turned to something else entirely when we read that “former NY Yankee Lee Mazzilli” would be on hand for opening day at a pool in the Bronx.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Yankee for a Moment, but a Met Forever', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mazzilli, Lee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T18:52:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3b0b1d0-7c87-51b8-8393-46e058290450', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3b0b1d0-7c87-51b8-8393-46e058290450'}, {'abstract': 'The comedy “Chinglish,” which chronicles an American businessman’s language difficulties in China, recently opened at the Goodman Theater in Chicago.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/by-way-of-chicago-david-henry-hwang-comedy-chinglish-will-come-to-broadway/', 'snippet': 'The comedy “Chinglish,” which chronicles an American businessman’s language difficulties in China, recently opened at the Goodman Theater in Chicago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you had the number three in your office pool of how many new Broadway plays would be announced on Tuesday, prepare to collect your winnings. Adding to today’s embarrassment of Rialto riches, the producers Jeffrey Richards and Jerry Frankel said that they will bring a production of David Henry Hwang’s comedy “Chinglish” to Broadway in the fall, after its run at the Goodman Theater in Chicago ends in July.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'By Way of Chicago, David Henry Hwang Comedy ‘Chinglish’ Will Come to Broadway', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hwang, David Henry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T19:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename':