': '2011-06-28T19:23:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb2c39cf-d6d3-5105-b3cb-451f229ffa9a', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb2c39cf-d6d3-5105-b3cb-451f229ffa9a'}, {'abstract': 'Google quietly unveiled a new Web page, called What Do You Love, that pulls together search results with all the tools and services the company offers. In creating the template, however, Google’s engineers did not realize how funny the results can be.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/the-unintended-humor-of-googles-new-search-page/', 'snippet': 'Google quietly unveiled a new Web page, called What Do You Love, that pulls together search results with all the tools and services the company offers. In creating the template, however, Google’s engineers did not realize how funny the results can...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, Google quietly began a Web page called What Do You Love, or WDYL for short. (You have to type in www before wdyl.com to get the page.) It’s an aggregation of all the Google tools and services, centered around a search you enter on a familiar-looking search-box page. If you wanted to see all the things Google can do with, say, the Yankees, you could enter that word into the box and get a page that looks like this:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Unintended Humor of Google’s New Search Page', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T19:25:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'bylin