imedia', 'news_desk': 'Food', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/75aba142-4c95-5995-a4f2-67817614c6f8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/75aba142-4c95-5995-a4f2-67817614c6f8'}, {'abstract': 'Fall shopping is upon us, and like a well-organized student, preparation is the key to a successful season. Our tip sheet from the shopping experts should help you get a jump on fall.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/ready-set-go-fall-shopping/', 'snippet': 'Fall shopping is upon us, and like a well-organized student, preparation is the key to a successful season. Our tip sheet from the shopping experts should help you get a jump on fall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Believe it or not, cashmere sweaters and coats are about to fill the racks at Bergdorf’s and Barneys. Fall shopping is upon us, and like a well-organized student, preparation is the key to a successful season. Even if you’re part of the über-prepared Moda Operandi posse and have already pre-ordered your must-haves from the runway, we bet you still have some loose ends. Our tip sheet from the shopping experts should help you get a jump on fall.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ready, Set, Go | Fall Shopping', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T20:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/916d497e-054c-59b4-be70-1841e00274a9', 'word_count': 777, 'uri': 'nyt://article/916d497e-054c-59b4-be70-1841e00274a9'}, {'abstract': 'No matter who wins the Wimbledon title, the streak of consecutive majors in which each women’s finalist is at least 25 will be snapped.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/youth-is-served-in-womens-semifinals/', 'snippet': 'No matter who wins the Wimbledon title, the streak of consecutive majors in which each women’s finalist is at least 25 will be snapped.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — So much for the graying of the Grand Slam women’s single champion. No matter who wins the Wimbledon title, the streak of consecutive majors in which each women’s finalist is at least 25 will be snapped.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Youth Is Served in Women’s Semifinals', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T20:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/541d4a0f-1108-568b-9e10-0e8e01e4f2c7', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/541d4a0f-1108-568b-9e10-0e8e01e4f2c7'}, {'abstract': 'A few political scientists, budget wonks and constitutional scholars argue that he could.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/could-obama-just-ignore-the-debt-ceiling/', 'snippet': 'A few political scientists, budget wonks and constitutional scholars argue that he could.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the ongoing debate over raising the debt ceiling, one option has not had much prominence: whether the Obama administration could ignore it altogether, and just spend the money it owes anyway. Would that be legal?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Could Obama Just Ignore the Debt Ceiling?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T20:03:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25ea3fbf-b41a-50fb-89bd-87a67be77e81', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25ea3fbf-b41a-50fb-89bd-87a67be77e81'}, {'abstract': 'The Smithsonian Institution has indefinitely postponed its plans to mount an exhibition of Chinese artifacts salvaged from a shipwreck because of opposition from archeologists.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/shipwreck-show-postponed/', 'snippet': 'The Smithsonian Institution has indefinitely postponed its plans to mount an exhibition of Chinese artifacts salvaged from a shipwreck because of opposition from archeologists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Smithsonian Institution has indefinitely postponed its plans to mount an exhibition of Chinese artifacts salvaged from a shipwreck because of opposition from archeologists who say the objects were collected by a commercial treasure hunter in a manner that violated professional standards. The exhibition, “Shipwrecked: Tang Treasures and Monsoon Winds,” was tentatively scheduled for next spring at the Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery, the Smithsonian’s Asian art museums. A museum spokeswoman, Deborah Galyan, said that the earliest the museum might mount a “Shipwrecked” exhibit now would be 2013, a delay that she said would give the museums’ director, Julian Raby, who also curated the exhibition, time to address some of the archeologists’ concerns. The exhibition was conceived by the government of Singapore, which bought the artifacts for $32 million from the company that salvaged them from a wreck off the coast of Indonesia. It is currently on view at the ArtScience museum at the Marina Bay Sands resort and casino in Singapore. The salvage company has said it mined the artifacts quickly to avoid looting.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shipwreck Show Postponed', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T20:04:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/daef3c0d-fc95-5c8c-8993-52a3193cc5a5', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/daef3c0d-fc95-5c8c-8993-52a3193cc5a5'}, {'abstract': 'Maria Sharapova is engaged to Sasha Vujacic, an N.B.A. guard who understands the sporting life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/sports/tennis/wimbledon-2011-sharapova-and-vujacic-athletes-who-are-a-couple.html', 'snippet': 'Maria Sharapova is engaged to Sasha Vujacic, an N.B.A. guard who understands the sporting life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — Watching Maria Sharapova’s power and precision, the Centre Court crowd at Wimbledon fell into an awed silence Tuesday, one that was broken before or after some points by the clapping of one person. Without a word, Sasha Vujacic was communicating everything his fiancée, Sharapova, needed to hear during her 6-1, 6-1 quarterfinal victory against Dominika Cibulkova. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/sports/tennis/29couple-pic/29couple-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 678, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/sports/tennis/29couple-pic/29couple-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/29/sports/tennis/29couple-pic/29couple-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fan, a Fiancé, a Fellow Athlete', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Fan, a Fiancé, a Fellow Athlete', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharapova, Maria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Nets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vujacic, Sasha', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T20:09:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e73a554-eec4-5147-9103-e172d75eb548', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e73a554-eec4-5147-9103-e172d75eb548'}, {'abstract': "The police clash with demonstrators in Athens; a media frenzy surrounds a flotilla headed to Gaza; and President Obama's relationship with Wall Street campaign donors.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/multimedia/100000000885504/timescast--june-28-2011.html', 'snippet': "The police clash with demonstrators in Athens; a media frenzy surrounds a flotilla headed to Gaza; and President Obama's relationship with Wall Street campaign donors.", 'lead_paragraph': "The police clash with demonstrators in Athens; a media frenzy surrounds a flotilla headed to Gaza; and President Obama's relationship with Wall Street campaign donors.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/28/multimedia/video-tc-110628/video-tc-110628-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/28/multimedia/video-tc-110628/video-tc-110628-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'TimesCast | June 28, 2011', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Finances', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T20:09:56+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'ContinuousNews', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Zena Barakat|Ben Solomon|Ted Trautman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zena', 'middlename': 'Barakat', 'lastname': 'Ben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f7d8ffb1-3e7c-543d-adc1-babedbc6229a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f7d8ffb1-3e7c-543d-adc1-babedbc6229a'}, {'abstract': 'Yahoo is creating a separate company to provide support and services for users of Hadoop, software it helped develop that manages large sets of data.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/yahoo-creates-company-to-profit-from-hadoop-software/', 'snippet': 'Yahoo is creating a separate company to provide support and services for users of Hadoop, software it helped develop that manages large sets of data.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recent successes at Yahoo are few and far between as it tries to overhaul a slumping business. One notable exception – at least for Silicon Valley’s engineers – is the company’s role in pioneering Hadoop, free software for managing supersized amounts of information.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yahoo Creates Company to Profit From Hadoop Software', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cloud Computing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yahoo! Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T20:12:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verne G. Kopytoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verne', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Kopytoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1bdeb09-b9bc-5b50-9deb-03d6e3e589b1', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1bdeb09-b9bc-5b50-9deb-03d6e3e589b1'}, {'abstract': 'Is she a serious person? Let’s hope not.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/the-mystery-of-lagarde/', 'snippet': 'Is she a serious person? Let’s hope not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OK, so it’s Christine Lagarde for the IMF. I wish her luck. And I wish we had any idea how well she’ll do the job.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Mystery of Lagarde', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T20:14:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba6ae28e-9d16-57ec-90a3-4b54adb832c4', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba6ae28e-9d16-57ec-90a3-4b54adb832c4'}, {'abstract': 'The Mavericks claim their statistical analysis of Rudy Fernandez was positive, while others using the same numbers disagree.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/same-numbers-two-views-of-fernandez/', 'snippet': 'The Mavericks claim their statistical analysis of Rudy Fernandez was positive, while others using the same numbers disagree.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On draft night, the Dallas Mavericks traded away a late first-round pick for the opportunity to add veteran Rudy Fernandez to their championship core. For a team on Dallas’s timeline, the immediacy of such a move makes sense; there’s an allure in a proven N.B.A. rotation player over an untested prospect, even if some of the prospects available (Texas’ Jordan Hamilton, who was once viewed as a talent worthy of the late lottery, chief among them) have intriguing long-term potential.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Same Numbers, Two Views of Fernandez', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Statistics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Mavericks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T20:21:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Mahoney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahoney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/709938d7-c443-5dd0-b917-2ce3567db353', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/709938d7-c443-5dd0-b917-2ce3567db353'}, {'abstract': 'If Bob Bradley is dismissed as coach of the U.S. national team, who should be his replacement. Here are some suggestions from the Goal blog.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/in-bob-we-trust/', 'snippet': 'If Bob Bradley is dismissed as coach of the U.S. national team, who should be his replacement. Here are some suggestions from the Goal blog.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The tubes and wires that are the lifeblood of the Goal blog’s link to its readers have been overheating since Saturday’s Concacaf Gold Cup final, in which Mexico toyed with the United States in its 4-2 victory. Everyone is a critic and no person has come in for more criticism than the coach of the American team, Bob Bradley.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Bob We Trust?', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradley, Bob', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gulati, Sunil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United State Soccer Federation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pasadena (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T20:22:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell and Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/142b5200-5637-58f2-b79b-15186848e140', 'word_count': 1311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/142b5200-5637-58f2-b79b-15186848e140'}, {'abstract': 'According to AFP, in the latest fighting around the southwestern mountains, the rebels on Tuesday captured a network of bunkers in the desert about 15 miles from the hilltown of Zintan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/28/world/africa/20110629-LIBYA.html', 'snippet': 'According to AFP, in the latest fighting around the southwestern mountains, the rebels on Tuesday captured a network of bunkers in the desert about 15 miles from the hilltown of Zintan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'According to AFP, in the latest fighting around the southwestern mountains, the rebels on Tuesday captured a network of bunkers in the desert about 15 miles from the hilltown of Zintan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/28/world/africa/20110629-LIBYA-slide-659T/20110629-LIBYA-slide-659T-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/28/world/africa/20110629-LIBYA-slide-659T/20110629-LIBYA-slide