urt-ruling-on-video-games-means.html', 'snippet': 'The court found that video games have the same First Amendment rights as other media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is now the law of the United States that video games are art. It is now the law of the United States that video games are a creative, intellectual, emotional form of expression and engagement, as fundamentally human as any other.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/JPDECISION1/JPDECISION1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/JPDECISION1/JPDECISION1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/JPDECISION1/JPDECISION1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/JPDECISION1/JPDECISION1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/JPDECISION1/JPDECISION1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Supreme Court Has Ruled; Now Games Have a Duty', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Court Has Ruled; Now Games Have a Duty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Violence (In Media and Entertainment)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T22:04:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Video Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Schiesel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schiesel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b21ccfda-7805-5392-83ed-d11a1f5b66d3', 'word_count': 1031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b21ccfda-7805-5392-83ed-d11a1f5b66d3'}, {'abstract': 'The Moment goes behind the scenes with the London-based, German-born designer and the making of his film for his resort collection.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/being-markus-lupfer/', 'snippet': 'The Moment goes behind the scenes with the London-based, German-born designer and the making of his film for his resort collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With resort still going strong, designers are using art and film to show their collections. Last night at the Mondrian SoHo penthouse, the London-based, German-born designer Markus Lupfer premiered his collection with a mock sci-fi documentary. The director Tabitha Denholm was on hand to provide live commentary along with the actress Margo Stilley (who portrayed Dr. Lupfer). Cory Kennedy spun tracks with the city skyline providing the perfect backdrop. Tennessee Thomas of the Like slipped in and played “It’s My Party and I’ll Cry if I Want To” on the turntables. And all talk centered on the collection featuring bright colors and a clever fit. Here’s our exclusive look behind the scenes of the film, above, directed by Sean Frank. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Being Markus Lupfer', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T22:11:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d2aed84-86c2-5016-9022-f8985ed68c51', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d2aed84-86c2-5016-9022-f8985ed68c51'}, {'abstract': 'Rita Jammet, formerly of the restaurant La Caravelle, is out to prove that her Champagne goes well with more things than people think.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/dining/a-play-date-for-champagne-and-burgers.html', 'snippet': 'Rita Jammet, formerly of the restaurant La Caravelle, is out to prove that her Champagne goes well with more things than people think.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN André and Rita Jammet closed La Caravelle seven years ago last month, devotees of classic French food in New York were bereft. So were devotees of the Jammets, who ran the dining room with requisite Gallic elegance limned with an almost familial warmth. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/dining/FEED-2/FEED-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/29/dining/FEED-2/FEED-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/dining/FEED-2/FEED-2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/dining/FEED-2/FEED-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/29/dining/FEED-2/FEED-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Play Date With Champagne and Burgers', 'kicker': 'Feed Me', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Play Date With Champagne and Burgers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hamburgers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Champagne (Wine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T22:11:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Witchel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witchel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ea99417-ea71-5d0f-9e76-9428693c128d', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ea99417-ea71-5d0f-9e76-9428693c128d'}, {'abstract': 'This summer there won’t be any New York Philharmonic concerts in the city parks, cutting off a free gift to the city when it’s important to make the orchestra seem essential.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/arts/music/new-york-philharmonic-summer-parks-tour-was-beloved-ritual.html', 'snippet': 'This summer there won’t be any New York Philharmonic concerts in the city parks, cutting off a free gift to the city when it’s important to make the orchestra seem essential.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all the milestones in Alan Gilbert’s career, including his first program as music director of the New York Philharmonic in September 2009, no performance made him more visibly excited than the concert he conducted with the Philharmonic in Central Park on a balmy summer night in 2008. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/JPPHIL/JPPHIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/JPPHIL/JPPHIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/JPPHIL/JPPHIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No More Silent Summers', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No More Silent Summers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CENTRAL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilbert, Alan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mehta, Zarin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T22:12:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b3c8a63-f1fa-598c-9574-20a6a7fb3edc', 'word_count': 1235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b3c8a63-f1fa-598c-9574-20a6a7fb3edc'}, {'abstract': 'Brought together primarily by what they oppose, Tea Party groups have had difficulty agreeing on what they stand for.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/us/politics/29teaparty.html', 'snippet': 'Brought together primarily by what they oppose, Tea Party groups have had difficulty agreeing on what they stand for.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HARRISBURG, Pa. — When Tea Party groups celebrated their victories here in November — they had helped take the governor’s office and the legislature — it seemed that one of their priorities, school choice legislation, would have an easy time passing. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/us/29teaparty_337_inline/29teaparty_337_inline-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/us/29teaparty_337_inline/29teaparty_337_inline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/29/us/29teaparty_337_inline/29teaparty_337_inline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tea Party Finds Power Leads to Policy Splits', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tea Party Finds Power Leads to Policy Splits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FreedomWorks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indiana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T22:16:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Zernike', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zernike', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecae42b2-0e7a-5d3f-843a-763540937836', 'word_count': 1156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecae42b2-0e7a-5d3f-843a-763540937836'}, {'abstract': '“Necessary Roughness,” a new series on USA, centers on a tough Long Island female psychotherapist who works with pro football players.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/arts/television/necessary-roughness-with-callie-thorne-on-usa-review.html', 'snippet': '“Necessary Roughness,” a new series on USA, centers on a tough Long Island female psychotherapist who works with pro football players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is so much sports on television — not just the Super Bowl and ESPN but also the MLB Network, the Tennis Channel, the Golf Channel, NBA TV, the Big Ten Network and all those cable shows hooked on boxing, wrestling, cycling and fishing. What is remarkable is that so little of it has bled into dramas or comedies.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/NECESSARY/NECESSARY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 866, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/NECESSARY/NECESSARY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/NECESSARY/NECESSARY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'She Goes From Kitchen to the Locker Room', 'kicker': 'Television Review | ‘Necessary Roughness’', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'She Goes From Kitchen To the Locker Room', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'USA Network', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-28T22:16:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alessandra Stanley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alessandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stanley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd5d2468-edd3-579d-bcf0-355327c497a8', 'word_count': 886, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd5d2468-edd3-579d-bcf0-355327c497a8'}, {'abstract': 'Cristi Puiu stars in and directs “Aurora,” an enigmatic feature about human relations in present-day Romania.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/movies/cristi-puiu-stars-in-aurora-from-romania-review.html', 'snippet': 'Cristi Puiu stars in and directs “Aurora,” an enigmatic feature about human relations in present-day Romania.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the central character in “Aurora” begins eyeballing another person — turning his shiver-inducing gaze on a complaining co-worker, on a salesgirl who anxiously laughs out of turn and even on a child who innocently returns his look — he seems like a man possessed. His eyes lock and the whites catch the light, shining without revelation. You may think you’re in Charles Manson-ville. (It’s only Romania.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/sub-AURORA/sub-AURORA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/29/arts/sub-AURORA/sub-AURORA-ar