ar, Ken', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flake, Jeff', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T01:51:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/acaaa027-7ebc-554c-b311-67def2885651', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acaaa027-7ebc-554c-b311-67def2885651'}, {'abstract': 'Virtually everything the nine Supreme Court justices do and say — whether in robes, suits or leisure wear — has potential bearing on the reputation of the court.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/opinion/29shesol.html', 'snippet': 'Virtually everything the nine Supreme Court justices do and say — whether in robes, suits or leisure wear — has potential bearing on the reputation of the court.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Washington', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should Justices Keep Their Opinions to Themselves?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Should Justices Keep Their Opinions to Themselves?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALITO, SAMUEL A JR', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scalia, Antonin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breyer, Stephen G', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Clarence', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conflicts of Interest', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ginsburg, Ruth Bader', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Judges', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T01:56:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Shesol', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shesol', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7297dc6-2ee2-5462-9ba9-18e1a8783fe5', 'word_count': 1649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7297dc6-2ee2-5462-9ba9-18e1a8783fe5'}, {'abstract': 'A classic American political debate — state versus federal law — is surfacing in the banking sector, as regulators work to put in place the financial reforms passed a year ago in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/business/economy/29bank.html', 'snippet': 'A classic American political debate — state versus federal law — is surfacing in the banking sector, as regulators work to put in place the financial reforms passed a year ago in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the most classic American political debates — state versus federal law — is surfacing again in the banking sector, as regulators work to put in place the financial reforms passed a year ago in Washington.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dodd-Frank Rekindles Old Debate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dodd-Frank Rekindles Old Debate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of the Comptroller of the Currency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major