ject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collective Bargaining', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Players Assn', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T01:58:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Beck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/058bd3a1-48c3-5784-a499-8405d26edd82', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/058bd3a1-48c3-5784-a499-8405d26edd82'}, {'abstract': 'A punny Wednesday with Tony Orbach.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/28/there/', 'snippet': 'A punny Wednesday with Tony Orbach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEDNESDAY’S PUZZLE — It’s midweek, and to help us over the hump we have a very punny puzzle today from Tony Orbach. Not only that, but it has almost a meditative quality, since all of the puns use the “om” sound. I hope you all did the puzzle in lotus pose, as I did. Don’t worry too much if the theme entries were not soundalikes; they’re puns, and it’s O.K. for them to be a bit loose. Besides, too much complaining will harsh my mellow, man.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wednesday: You There?', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T02:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0442234-48ba-5961-aee2-ce15bd666d1c', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0442234-48ba-5961-aee2-ce15bd666d1c'}, {'abstract': 'The prosecutor of the International Criminal Court in The Hague said enforcing the newly issued arrest warrants for Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi, one of his sons and his intelligence chief was mainly the responsibility of Libya.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/world/europe/29briefs-HagueBrf.html', 'snippet': 'The prosecutor of the International Criminal Court in The Hague said enforcing the newly issued arrest warrants for Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi, one of his sons and his intelligence chief was mainly the responsibility of Libya.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The prosecutor of the International Criminal Court in The Hague said Tuesday that enforcing the newly issued arrest warrants for Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi, one of his sons and his intelligence chief was mainly the responsibility of Libya because the war crimes investigation that led to the warrants had been ordered by a resolution of the United Nations Security Council. The prosecutor, Luis Moreno-Ocampo, said at a news conference that two Libyan rebel leaders were in The Hague for talks with his office about their plans, and that arrests could be just months away. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hague: Onus Put on Libyans', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Hague: Onus Put on Libyans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Criminal Court', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T02:05:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marlise Simons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marlise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b06f7684-0fbb-5358-a1a8-526cd0e569be', 'word_count': 95, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b06f7684-0fbb-5358-a1a8-526cd0e569be'}, {'abstract': 'Ralph Jennings, the manager of Fordham station WFUV-FM, transformed it into one of the leading public radio outlets in the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/nyregion/manager-of-wfuv-at-fordam-ralph-jennings-retires-after-26-years.html', 'snippet': 'Ralph Jennings, the manager of Fordham station WFUV-FM, transformed it into one of the leading public radio outlets in the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Drawers pulled out. Empty boxes, with the flaps winged open. Ralph Jennings was packing up his things after 26 years at WFUV-FM, where he had transformed a chaotic college radio station at Fordham University with a tiny audience into one of the leading public radio outlets in the country and a rare cultural force.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/nyregion/ABOUT/ABOUT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}},