06-29T02:15:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd2b413e-4d4a-53f2-99cf-c108f6b69c0d', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd2b413e-4d4a-53f2-99cf-c108f6b69c0d'}, {'abstract': 'Without agreement on an austerity plan, Greece will get no money. The government will default, resulting in a run on banks and an even deeper recession.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/business/29views.html', 'snippet': 'Without agreement on an austerity plan, Greece will get no money. The government will default, resulting in a run on banks and an even deeper recession.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Greek Parliament is prepared to vote on austerity measures on Wednesday, and its people have been gathering in demonstrations and strikes to protest the cuts. Here is an open letter explaining why members of Parliament would be crazy to vote no:', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/business/VIEWS/VIEWS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Making the Argument for Greece’s Austerity Plan', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Making the Case For Cuts in Greece', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T02:15:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Dixon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dixon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddf41ceb-7555-5326-863b-59f664b28a4a', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddf41ceb-7555-5326-863b-59f664b28a4a'}, {'abstract': 'Dennis M. Walcott, who became New York schools chancellor in April, has spoken at 14 commencement ceremonies this season, far more than his predecessors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/nyregion/dennis-walcott-new-chancellor-makes-commencement-rounds.html', 'snippet': 'Dennis M. Walcott, who became New York schools chancellor in April, has spoken at 14 commencement ceremonies this season, far more than his predecessors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dennis M. Walcott  spoke about his own experience in Queens public schools. He described skydiving on his 50th birthday. And he told graduates all over New York City, those finishing fifth, eighth or 12th grade or getting a G.E.D., that he hoped one of them would take his job someday. Mr. Walcott, who became schools chancellor in April, has spoken at 14 commencement ceremonies this month (a 15th was planned for Belmont Preparatory High School in the Bronx on Wednesday), far more than his predecessors (Joel I. Klein addressed four in each of the past two years). On Tuesday, Mr. Walcott spoke at the commencement of his alma mater, Francis Lewis High School. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/29/nyregion/GRAD2/GRAD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/29/nyregion/