l-looks-fresh-in-victory-over-fish/', 'snippet': 'Rafael Nadal of Spain made relatively easy work of Mardy Fish in the quarterfinals of Wimbledon Wednesday, despite dropping only the second set of the tournament.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England – Rafael Nadal of Spain, showing no sign of an injury he feared might force his withdrawal, overpowered Mardy Fish Wednesday, racing to a 6-3, 6-3, 5-7, 6-4 victory and a place in the Wimbledon semifinals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nadal Looks Fresh in Victory Over Fish', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fish, Mardy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T18:48:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11de04cc-a9b7-5f57-96aa-3ba3358d993d', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11de04cc-a9b7-5f57-96aa-3ba3358d993d'}, {'abstract': 'One of 88 pianos placed around the city by the Sing for Hope nonprofit arts group has vanished from Williamsbridge Oval in the Bronx.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/street-piano-vanishes-and-with-it-free-fun-in-the-bronx/', 'snippet': 'One of 88 pianos placed around the city by the Sing for Hope nonprofit arts group has vanished from Williamsbridge Oval in the Bronx.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This would make for an unusual police blotter entry: Be on the lookout for a missing piano.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Street Piano Vanishes (and With It, Free Fun in the Bronx)', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norwood (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T18:54:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Barron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f136fb9d-fcfc-5cea-8539-e4b07129ff0a', 'word_count': 585, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f136fb9d-fcfc-5cea-8539-e4b07129ff0a'}, {'abstract': 'The federal agency advisers find that the drug is neither effective nor safe for treatment of metastatic breast cancer.', 'web_url': 'https://prescriptions.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/f-d-a-panel-still-sees-no-benefit-of-avastin-for-breast-cancer/', 'snippet': 'The federal agency advisers find that the drug is neither effective nor safe for treatment of metastatic breast cancer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'F.D.A. Panel Rejects Use of Avastin for Breast Cancer', 'kicker': 'Prescriptions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Avastin (Drug)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breast Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Genentech Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T18:56:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Pollack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pollack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cb05383-f923-5ef5-a100-ecd79b59006a', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cb05383-f923-5ef5-a100-ecd79b59006a'}, {'abstract': 'A slim but informative guidebook maps Andy Warhol’s life — shows and triumphs, antics and fallings-out — against the grid of Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/andy-walking/', 'snippet': 'A slim but informative guidebook maps Andy Warhol’s life — shows and triumphs, antics and fallings-out — against the grid of Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Had one of Andy Warhol’s more entrepreneurial visions come to fruition, the storefront at the northeast corner of 74th and Madison, today a vintage watch dealer, would be the site of the world’s first Andy-Mat, an international chain of fast-food restaurants for people who prefer to eat alone. “I want to start a chain of restaurants for other people like me called Andy-Mats,” Warhol said. “The restaurant for the lonely person. You get your food and then take your tray into a booth and watch television.” According to architectural drawings, customers would have placed their orders with the kitchen directly, through a system of pneumatic tubes. The food, prepared off-site and frozen, would have been delivered to the franchise and then reheated. The project fell through in 1977, but not before $40,000 in rent had been paid, $1 million in capital had been secured, and Maxine de la McKendry, a society hostess and onetime food columnist for Vogue, had prepared the menu: milk over ice, anyone?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Andy Walking', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Aguirre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Aguirre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24395869-279c-5908-8790-bd69db67d40d', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24395869-279c-5908-8790-bd69db67d40d'}, {'abstract': 'The Russian-born pianist Kirill Gerstein joined the conductor Bramwell Tovey for a New York Philharmonic Summertime Classics concert.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/arts/music/kirill-gerstein-and-new-york-philharmonic-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Russian-born pianist Kirill Gerstein joined the conductor Bramwell Tovey for a New York Philharmonic Summertime Classics concert.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The modest idea of the New York Philharmonic’s Summertime Classics concerts, which began in 2004, is to provide a few postseason programs of lighter fare, novelties and a staple or two at reduced prices. The British conductor Bramwell Tovey, a lively musician who has charmed Avery Fisher Hall audiences with his avuncular commentaries on music, concert protocols or whatever else strikes him, remains the host of choice for these events. While they are perfectly pleasant, you do not attend to hear extraordinary performances at these concerts.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/JPPHIL/JPPHIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/JPPHIL/JPPHIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/JPPHIL/JPPHIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/JPPHIL/JPPHIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/JPPHIL/JPPHIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fresh Take Adds a Jolt to a Standard', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Fresh Take Adds a Jolt To a Standard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tovey, Bramwell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tchaikovsky, Peter Ilyich', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gerstein, Kirill', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:08:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e5a1306-bfdf-5ebb-b379-6ac5e7916160', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e5a1306-bfdf-5ebb-b379-6ac5e7916160'}, {'abstract': 'We’re collaborating with SnagLearning to bring you film festivals of classroom-worthy documentaries. Today’s theme is science. Tell us what you think!', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/film-festival-documentaries-about-science/', 'snippet': 'We’re collaborating with SnagLearning to bring you film festivals of classroom-worthy documentaries. Today’s theme is science. Tell us what you think!', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our summer experiments continue! Last week: Historic Headlines. This week: A collaboration with SnagLearning to bring you “film festivals” of classroom-worthy documentaries, each day on a different theme. Monday’s collection was about technology, Tuesday’s was about history and today’s theme is science. The whole collection is available here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Film Festival | Documentaries About Science', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:12:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten and Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8924880-35ab-567f-b80b-2cbca20fddc1', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8924880-35ab-567f-b80b-2cbca20fddc1'}, {'abstract': 'Featuring a splintered Porsche Cayenne Turbo and a tower built from Beetles, a new exhibition suggests cars, like cellphones, are “extensions of man.”', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/at-german-museum-cars-as-a-medium-of-mobility/', 'snippet': 'Featuring a splintered Porsche Cayenne Turbo and a tower built from Beetles, a new exhibition suggests cars, like cellphones, are “extensions of man.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“Car Culture: Media of Mobility,” a new exhibition at the Zentrum für Kunst und Medientechnologie, or the Center of Art and Media, combines the legacy of two local discoveries: the car and the radio wave. The show runs through Jan. 8.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At German Museum, Cars as a ‘Medium of Mobility’', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chamberlain, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hadid, Zaha', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McLuhan, Marshall', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wurm, Erwin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mercedes-Benz', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:19:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Patton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fa6981a-37c1-506b-bc55-c4ca7e3b7a4c', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fa6981a-37c1-506b-bc55-c4ca7e3b7a4c'}, {'abstract': 'In return for small losses now, some funds offer to protect portfolios against events that are extremely unlikely or even unthinkable.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/a-new-investment-strategy-preparing-for-end-times/', 'snippet': 'In return for small losses now, some funds offer to protect portfolios against events that are extremely unlikely or even unthinkable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Investment professionals have a new pitch: The sky could soon be falling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Investment Strategy: Preparing for End Times', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asset Allocation (Personal Finances)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:20:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/504fea34-1daa-52c9-9842-84e3c4a7f06b', 'word_count': 1149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/504fea34-1daa-52c9-9842-84e3c4a7f06b'}, {'abstract': 'A revamped version of George Gershwin’s “Porgy and Bess,” starring Audra McDonald, Norm Lewis and David Alan Grier, will go to Broadway soon after its previously-announced run at the American Repertory Theater in Cambridge, Mass.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/from-catfish-row-to-times-square-porgy-and-bess-returning-to-broadway/', 'snippet': 'A revamped version of George Gershwin’s “Porgy and Bess,” starring Audra McDonald, Norm Lewis and David Alan Grier, will go to Broadway soon after its previously-announced run at the American Repertory Theater in Cambridge, Mass.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A revamped version of George Gershwin’s “Porgy and Bess,” starring Audra McDonald, Norm Lewis and David Alan Grier, will go to Broadway soon after it completes its run, previously announced, at the American Repertory Theater in Cambridge, Mass.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Catfish Row to Times Square: ‘Porgy and Bess’ Returning to Broadway', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:24:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff9f1cc3-138d-5ce3-96b6-fb90421a1398', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff9f1cc3-138d-5ce3-96b6-fb90421a1398'}, {'abstract': 'Taking another page from Groupon, the daily deal site LivingSocial, which has been growing rapidly, is moving toward the public markets.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/livingsocial-meets-with-bankers-to-discuss-i-p-o/', 'snippet': 'Taking another page from Groupon, the daily deal site LivingSocial, which has been growing rapidly, is moving toward the public markets.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:41 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'LivingSocial Is Said to Talk I.P.O. With Bankers', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:30:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3eb3384-8fdf-5f06-9580-fe75fb92a552', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3eb3384-8fdf-5f06-9580-fe75fb92a552'}, {'abstract': 'According to Nielsen’s estimates, a two-hour episode of this singing competition delivered the night’s largest audience, 12.5 million viewers.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/the-voice-lifts-nbc-again/', 'snippet': 'According to Nielsen’s estimates, a two-hour episode of this singing competition delivered the night’s largest audience, 12.5 million viewers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The final round of NBC’s singing competition “The Voice” garnered its best ratings in four weeks on Tuesday as the four remaining contestants battled it out to be the last singer standing. According to Nielsen’s estimates, the two-hour episode delivered the night’s largest audience, 12.5 million viewers, building on NBC’s ratings at 8 p.m. for “America’s Got Talent” (12 million). No other network came close on the night. CBS placed second with reruns of “NCIS” (8.2 million) and “NCIS: Los Angeles” (8 million) at 8 and 9. ABC was third, drawing its best ratings at 8 for “Wipeout” (5.5 million) and solid results at 9 for “101 Ways to Leave a Game Show” (5.3 million). At 10, ABC’s “Combat Hospital” (4.6 million) lagged just behind CBS’s repeat of “The Good Wife” (4.8 million). Fox finished in fourth as “MasterChef” attracted 4.2 million viewers at 8 while back-to-back reruns of “Raising Hope” averaged 2.2 million viewers at 9 p.m.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Voice’ Lifts NBC Again', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:30:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f2cbd5e-f3a3-5d78-8ee5-2c649ea5956c', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f2cbd5e-f3a3-5d78-8ee5-2c649ea5956c'}, {'abstract': 'The Rhode Island Senate approved a bill allowing not marriage but civil unions for gay couples.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/us/30unions.html', 'snippet': 'The Rhode Island Senate approved a bill allowing not marriage but civil unions for gay couples.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PROVIDENCE, R.I. — Less than a week after same-sex marriage was legalized in New York, the Rhode Island State Senate on Wednesday evening approved a bill allowing not marriage, but civil unions for gay couples, despite fierce opposition from gay rights advocates who called the legislation discriminatory. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/us/UNIONS-1/UNIONS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/30/us/UNIONS-1/UNIONS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/us/UNIONS-1/UNIONS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 650, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rhode Island Lawmakers Approve Civil Unions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rhode Island Senate Approves Civil Unions After Marriage Measure Falters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhode Island', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:37:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Goodnough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodnough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bb2770b-6b0c-5d2a-a56b-dd25f10b02f3', 'word_count': 800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bb2770b-6b0c-5d2a-a56b-dd25f10b02f3'}, {'abstract': 'A standoff at Arrow Wine and Liquor in Flatlands, Brooklyn, ended Wednesday afternoon with the arrest of two suspects, the police said.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/gunmen-may-have-taken-wine-store-owner-hostage-police-say/', 'snippet': 'A standoff at Arrow Wine and Liquor in Flatlands, Brooklyn, ended Wednesday afternoon with the arrest of two suspects, the police said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 6:23 p.m. | A hostage taking that grew out of a foiled armed robbery attempt at a Brooklyn wine shop ended without incident on Wednesday afternoon, the police said, as two suspects surrendered to the authorities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hostage Taking at Brooklyn Wine Store Ends Without Incident', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hostages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:40:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'origin