s North American investment banking operations, will assume a more global role, according to an internal memo.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/jpmorgan-gives-global-role-to-top-north-american-banker/', 'snippet': 'Jeff Urwin, a Bear Stearns veteran and a co-head of JPMorgan Chase’s North American investment banking operations, will assume a more global role, according to an internal memo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JPMorgan Chase on Wednesday promoted Jeff Urwin, one of its co-heads of North American investment banking, to a global role, according to an internal memorandum obtained by DealBook.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'JPMorgan Gives Top North American Banker a Global Role', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:42:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b95aba2-2f84-50ad-a476-88f4d2b4fa43', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b95aba2-2f84-50ad-a476-88f4d2b4fa43'}, {'abstract': 'The unbearable trivialness of being a hack.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/for-what-doth-it-profit-a-woman-if-she-dine-with-tom-friedman/', 'snippet': 'The unbearable trivialness of being a hack.', 'lead_paragraph': 'And lose her soul?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For What Doth It Profit A Woman If She Dine With Tom Friedman', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:47:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8375f4ed-8dd2-57be-b048-b1504ade5e09', 'word_count': 103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8375f4ed-8dd2-57be-b048-b1504ade5e09'}, {'abstract': 'The new cap of 21 to 24 cents for a debit card transaction is higher than the Federal Reserve’s initial figure, but well below the 44 cents that banks now charge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/business/30debit.html', 'snippet': 'The new cap of 21 to 24 cents for a debit card transaction is higher than the Federal Reserve’s initial figure, but well below the 44 cents that banks now charge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Fees paid by retailers to banks for debit card purchases, a $20 billion annual expense that has been the subject of a furious political battle over the last year, will be cut in half after the Federal Reserve voted Wednesday to cap the charges.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/business/Debit/Debit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 611, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fed Halves Debit Card Bank Fees', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fed Halves Debit Card Bank Fees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debit Cards', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T19:48:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wyatt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wyatt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfe2a5f0-74ca-58b8-af70-af1aa8563ecb', 'word_count': 801, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfe2a5f0-74ca-58b8-af70-af1aa8563ecb'}, {'abstract': '“W.E.,” which stars Abbie Cornish as a young woman who finds inspiration in the romance between Britain’s King Edward VIII and and the American divorcee Wallis Simpson, will be released on Dec. 9, 2011 in New York and Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/weinsteins-place-madonnas-new-film-in-oscar-fray/', 'snippet': '“W.E.,” which stars Abbie Cornish as a young woman who finds inspiration in the romance between Britain’s King Edward VIII and and the American divorcee Wallis Simpson, will be released on Dec. 9, 2011 in New York and Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Weinstein Company, which recently acquired the Madonna-directed film “W.E.,” announced Wednesday that it plans to release the film smack in the middle of Oscar season. “W.E.,” which stars Abbie Cornish as a modern-day young woman who finds inspiration in the romance between Britain’s King Edward VIII and the American divorcée Wallis Simpson, will be released on Dec. 9 in New York and Los Angeles. Besides Ms. Cornish, the cast includes Andrea Riseborough (“Never Let Me Go”), James D’Arcy (“Master and Commander: The Far Side of the World”) and Oscar Isaac (“Drive”). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'m