eorg Baselitz and other contemporary German artists soared at an auction by Sotheby’s in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Prices for contemporary German artists — Sigmar Polke, Georg Baselitz and Blinky Palermo, among others — soared to new levels at Sotheby’s here on Wednesday night in an auction that featured 34 works from the collection of Count Christian Duerckheim, a German industrialist who became obsessed with what he calls “the art of his time.” Beginning in the early 1970s Count Duerckheim got to know Mr. Polke and Mr. Baselitz along with Gerhard Richter and lesser-known artists including Jörg Immendorff, Konrad Lueg and Eugen Schönebeck. He often bought their work soon after it was made.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/sub-sothebys-1/sub-sothebys-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/sub-sothebys-1/sub-sothebys-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/sub-sothebys-1/sub-sothebys-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Strong Sales in London for Some German Artists', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strong Sales in London For Some German Artists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palermo, Blinky', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baselitz, Georg', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Polke, Sigmar', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sotheby's", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T21:58:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c6f13eb-53e5-599e-8530-d18a9f4cc54b', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c6f13eb-53e5-599e-8530-d18a9f4cc54b'}, {'abstract': 'Harnesses are for sale at Topshop, were shown in the spring 2010 Comme des Garçons collection, and have been spotted on celebrities including Ciara and Rihanna.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/fashion/the-harness-as-a-fashion-statement.html', 'snippet': 'Harnesses are for sale at Topshop, were shown in the spring 2010 Comme des Garçons collection, and have been spotted on celebrities including Ciara and Rihanna.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DURING Lady Gaga’s performance of “Judas” on the season finale of “Saturday Night Live,” her backup dancers were clad in gold harnesses, custom-made by the Brooklyn designer Zana Bayne. They were worn either over a bikini top or bare skin, artfully framing the chest.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/fashion/Z-NOTICED-3/Z-NOTICED-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 860, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/fashion/Z-NOTICED-3/Z-NOTICED-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/30/fashion/Z-NOTICED-3/Z-NOTICED-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Harness Has Them Champing at the Bit', 'kicker': 'Noticed', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Harness Has Them Champing at the Bit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T21:58:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marisa Meltzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meltzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ef91eb5-279e-5cab-9626-b02a8fde0549', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ef91eb5-279e-5cab-9626-b02a8fde0549'}, {'abstract': 'General Electric extended its global Olympic sponsorship through 2020, adding an estimated $200 million sweetener to the $2 billion it paid for NBC to carry the 2008, ’10 and ’12 Games.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/sports/general-electric-extends-olympic-games-sponsorship.html', 'snippet': 'General Electric extended its global Olympic sponsorship through 2020, adding an estimated $200 million sweetener to the $2 billion it paid for NBC to carry the 2008, ’10 and ’12 Games.', 'lead_paragraph': 'General Electric extended its global Olympic sponsorship on Wednesday through 2020, matching the decision three weeks ago by NBC Universal to acquire the rights to the four Olympics from 2014 to 2020 for $4.38 billion. G.E., which sold majority control of NBC Universal to Comcast, has sponsored the Olympics since 2008, adding an estimated $200 million sweetener to the $2 billion it paid for NBC to carry the 2008, ’10 and ’12 Games.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'G.E. Extends Olympic Sponsorship', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'G.E. Extends Olympic Sponsorship', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Electric Company', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T21:59:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdc7b7e3-1129-5601-8e23-1e9f747ae8ac', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdc7b7e3-1129-5601-8e23-1e9f747ae8ac'}, {'abstract': 'The performance artist talks about her first solo museum show at the Whitney Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/visiting-artists-xavier-cha/', 'snippet': 'The performance artist talks about her first solo museum show at the Whitney Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The performance artist Xavier Cha thrives on the unknown. Her process is largely improvisational and embraces the random variables many artists work hard to avoid. For “Holiday Cruise!,” her solo show at Taxter and Spengemann Gallery back in 2006, Cha enlisted about a hundred performers to interact with her as she played three different characters. One of the characters was dressed in a bodysuit completely covered with cornrows (and elicited numerous requests to dance). Another character simply lay in and among oversized fruits and vegetables in a massive Horn of Plenty. The third character, Polyhedra, was still as a statue.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Visiting Artists | Xavier Cha', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T22:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Johnny Misheff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Johnny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Misheff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39503d29-b2e3-56f6-8452-b1b07f8cf325', 'word_count': 437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39503d29-b2e3-56f6-8452-b1b07f8cf325'}, {'abstract': 'The Wall Street plans to lay off 230 employees in New York. The job cuts would begin at the end of September.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/goldman-prepares-to-cut-jobs/', 'snippet': 'The Wall Street plans to lay off 230 employees in New York. The job cuts would begin at the end of September.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ax is about to fall at Goldman Sachs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goldman Sachs Prepares to Cut Jobs', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T22:01:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susanne Craig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Craig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74de2c80-859a-5bba-a438-b59262c8eeb3', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74de2c80-859a-5bba-a438-b59262c8eeb3'}, {'abstract': 'A team of Americans won a competition at the fifth European Open Championships in Poznan, Poland.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nyt