orks, and its implications. Pedro Rafael Rosado takes a look at the tech term "addressable TV." And J.D. Biersdorfer checks out the Yobot at the Yotel — a giant robot that handles luggage at a new Manhattan hotel. The news roundup includes the Supreme Court\'s ruling on violent video games; a class-action suit against Dropbox; e-reader ownership booming; new Windows malware that burrows deeply. J.D.\'s tech tip: how to save emailed photos in the Mac Mail program directly into iPhoto.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2011/06/29/technology/personaltech/30techtalk_pod.html', 'snippet': 'Game-style voice and motion controls will let Xbox Kinect users interact with ads. Betting Edelstein chats with Times media reporter Tanzina Vega about how this new mode of advertising works, and its implications. Pedro Rafael Rosado takes a look ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Game-style voice and motion controls will let Xbox Kinect users interact with ads. Betting Edelstein chats with Times media reporter Tanzina Vega about how this new mode of advertising works, and its implications. Pedro Rafael Rosado takes a look at the tech term "addressable TV." And J.D. Biersdorfer checks out the Yobot at the Yotel — a giant robot that handles luggage at a new Manhattan hotel. The news roundup includes the Supreme Court\'s ruling on violent video games; a class-action suit against Dropbox; e-reader ownership booming; new Windows malware that burrows deeply. J.D.\'s tech tip: how to save emailed photos in the Mac Mail program directly into iPhoto.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tanzina Vega on interactive motion-controlled ads; the term "addressable TV;" the Yotel robot; news.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Podcasts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-29T22:31:51+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/7e308aad-6cf8-5b5d-b3fd-48327621c378', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/7e308aad-6cf8-5b5d-b3fd-48327621c378'}, {'abstract': 'In this newly translated novel, the Portugese writer António Lobo Antunes recalls the waning days of his country’s colonial efforts in Angola.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/books/lobo-antuness-land-at-the-end-of-the-world-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this newly translated novel, the Portugese writer António Lobo Antunes recalls the waning days of his country’s colonial efforts in Angola.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Combat experiences are like Tolstoy’s unhappy families: no two are alike, which may be why they often make for great novels, as Tolstoy also knew. The cause need not even be noble, since a hopeless situation and senseless violence can actually fortify a work of fiction. Certainly that is the case with António Lobo Antunes’s “Land at the End of the World,” set in Angola in the early 1970s, as Portugal’s ludicrous effort to preserve its African empire was meandering to an inglorious end.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/book/book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 876, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/30/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On a Fool’s Mission in a Dying Colonial World', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content