value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yemen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stadiums and Arenas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T00:22:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Kasinof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kasinof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cc69d5f-5f0b-5efd-aa53-0fa80e84f3b9', 'word_count': 500, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cc69d5f-5f0b-5efd-aa53-0fa80e84f3b9'}, {'abstract': 'At least 30 Tibetans have been detained in 15 incidents of protest this month in a restive part of Sichuan Province, in western China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/world/asia/30briefs-Tibet.html', 'snippet': 'At least 30 Tibetans have been detained in 15 incidents of protest this month in a restive part of Sichuan Province, in western China.', 'lead_paragraph': ' At least 30 Tibetans have been detained in 15 incidents of protest this month in a restive part of Sichuan Province, in western China, according to a report this week by International Campaign for Tibet, an advocacy group based outside China. Those detained are both monks and laypeople. The protests took place in an area that Tibetans call Kardze, known to the Chinese as Ganzi. The local police headquarters had no comment. The area has been unsettled since April 2008, when at least eight Tibetans were killed by Chinese security forces firing into a crowd of protestors, Tibetan exile groups said at the time. Those protests took place as part of a large uprising that began in Lhasa, Tibet, and unfolded across the Tibetan plateau. The report this week also said two monks from Kardze held a protest in the Barkhor market in Lhasa on June 22, possibly the first there since March 2008. Chinese officials have barred foreigners from going to central Tibet from late June to late July. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Tibetans Are Arrested After New Protests in Sichuan', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Tibetans Are Arrested After New Protests in Sichuan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tibet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T00:25:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b074120-35d6-5fce-bc86-08a2fd947d9f', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b074120-35d6-5fce-bc86-08a2fd947d9f'}, {'abstract': 'The bipartisan bill would allow presidents to fill cabinet agency public affairs jobs and other positions without putting appointees through a cumbersome, partisan ordeal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/us/politics/30confirm.html', 'snippet': 'The bipartisan bill would allow presidents to fill cabinet agency public affairs jobs and other positions without putting appointees through a cumbersome, partisan ordeal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Senate took the rare step of relinquishing power on Wednesday, easily passing a measure that would exempt about 170 executive branch appointees from confirmation in an effort to streamline a process that has increasingly tied up the Senate and become punishing for those tapped for administration jobs.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Senate Votes to Streamline the Confirmation Process', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Senate Votes to Streamline the Confirmation Process', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T00:26:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Hulse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hulse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d89aa225-39b0-5765-a01b-9da3becb0cec', 'word_count': 764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d89aa225-39b0-5765-a01b-9da3becb0cec'}, {'abstract': 'Senegal has deployed troops around ministry buildings and an important route in the capital, Dakar, a spokesman said Wednesday, in a bid to forestall further rioting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/world/africa/30briefs-Senegal.html', 'snippet': 'Senegal has deployed troops around ministry buildings and an important route in the capital, Dakar, a spokesman said Wednesday, in a bid to forestall further rioting.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Senegal has deployed troops around ministry buildings and an important route in the capital, Dakar, a spokesman said Wednesday, in a bid to forestall further rioting after several days of antigovernment violence. Offices of the state power company Senelec were burned in riots on Monday night by protesters angry over power cuts lasting hours. “The situation is back to normal now,” said Serigne Mbacké Ndiaye, a spokesman for President Abdoulaye Wade, adding that Mr. Wade, above, had decided to forgo a trip to the African Union summit meeting in Equatorial Guinea in order to watch over the situation in Senegal. Last week, the capital was rocked by riots over Mr. Wade’s plan, subsequently withdrawn, to change electoral rules in what demonstrators said was an effort to prolong his rule. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/world/30briefs-senegal/30briefs-senegal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 644, 'width': 391, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/world/30briefs-senegal/30briefs-senegal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/30/world/30briefs-senegal/30briefs-senegal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Senegal: Soldiers Sent Into Streets of Capital After Fiery Riots', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Africa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Senegal: Soldiers Sent Into Streets Of Capital After Fiery Riots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Senegal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T00:28:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nossiter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nossiter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a55cca51-6573-5eca-9f07-d14bcfa3d199', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a55cca51-6573-5eca-9f07-d14bcfa3d199'}, {'abstract': 'The Knicks extended a qualifying offer to forward Derrick Brown, making him a restricted free agent.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/29/knicks-make-offer-to-brown/', 'snippet': 'The Knicks extended a qualifying offer to forward Derrick Brown, making him a restricted free agent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Knicks extended a qualifying offer to forward Derrick Brown on Wednesday, making him a restricted free agent. The designation means that the Knicks will have the right to match any offer Brown receives, assuming the rules do not change substantially in the N.B.A.’s next labor deal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Knicks Make Offer to Brown', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Free Agents (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Derrick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "D'Antoni, Dan", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weber, Phil', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Herb', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T00:33:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'n