ne': {'original': 'By Benjamin Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6db44d31-62c9-5e64-a543-057f8f185d97', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6db44d31-62c9-5e64-a543-057f8f185d97'}, {'abstract': 'In a series of 6-0 votes, an advisory committee to the F.D.A. endorsed the agency’s proposal to revoke the approval of the drug for use against advanced breast cancer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/business/30drug.html', 'snippet': 'In a series of 6-0 votes, an advisory committee to the F.D.A. endorsed the agency’s proposal to revoke the approval of the drug for use against advanced breast cancer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A resounding vote by a federal committee of cancer experts on Wednesday makes it more likely the drug Avastin will no longer be readily available as a treatment for breast cancer, dealing a blow to women who think the drug is helping them. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/business/Drug/Drug-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Panel Advises F.D.A. to Narrow Its Approval for Avastin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Panel Advises F.D.A. to Narrow Its Approval for Avastin', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Avastin (Drug)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breast Cancer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Genentech Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamburg, Margaret A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clinical Trials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T00:42:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Pollack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pollack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35f7cc10-5b9f-5c6c-9393-55bf6c153e61', 'word_count': 1066, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35f7cc10-5b9f-5c6c-9393-55bf6c153e61'}, {'abstract': 'At 24, Norma M. Inoa got her first-ever job in the mailroom at Fedcap; two promotions later, she is an accounting clerk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/nyregion/after-derailed-dream-working-her-way-up-at-fedcap.html', 'snippet': 'At 24, Norma M. Inoa got her first-ever job in the mailroom at Fedcap; two promotions later, she is an accounting clerk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Epilepsy and pregnancy eclipsed Norma M. Inoa’s dream of becoming a New York police detective; until 2010 she was living on Social Security disability payments. Last summer, she completed a training program run by Fedcap, an agency that develops employment opportunities for the disadvantaged and the disabled. In September, at 24, she got her first-ever job in the Fedcap mailroom; two promotions later, she is an accounting clerk. Now 25, she lives on the Grand Concourse, in the Bronx, with her boyfriend, who is a deliveryman for LaserShip, and her 5-year-old son.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/nyregion/ENTRY/ENTRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/30/nyregion/ENTRY/ENTRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/nyregion/ENTRY/ENTRY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/nyregion/ENTRY/ENTRY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/30/nyregion/ENTRY/ENTRY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After a Derailed Dream, Working Her Way Up', 'kicker': 'Entry Level', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After a Derailed Dream, Working Her Way Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T00:43:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Finn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9e946d4-d809-503f-849f-5e7cd77a0953', 'word_count': 629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9e946d4-d809-503f-849f-5e7cd77a0953'}, {'abstract': 'The Scottish National Party, whose avowed goal is securing Scottish independence, now controls Parliament, although a referendum could create a dilemma for the party’s leader.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/world/europe/30edinburgh.html', 'snippet': 'The Scottish National Party, whose avowed goal is securing Scottish independence, now controls Parliament, although a referendum could create a dilemma for the party’s leader.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EDINBURGH — Scotland’s grievances against England, its more powerful neighbor to the south, are deep and long, stretching back hundreds of years. But despite the country’s fierce pride in the things that make it different — culture, history, haggis — Scottish independence has always seemed like a quixotic dream, the fantasy of a vocal minority with more passion than power. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/world/30edinburgh/30edinburgh-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/30/world/30edinburgh/30edinburgh-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/world/30edinburgh/30edinburgh-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 718, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/world/30edinburgh/30edinburgh-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/30/world/30edinburgh/30edinburgh-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Notion of Independence Makes Halting Progress', 'kicker': 'Memo from Edinburgh', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Notion of Independence Makes Halting Progress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salmond, Alex', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}