ument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a9907ad-9807-589d-a274-dae010690a54', 'word_count': 1385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a9907ad-9807-589d-a274-dae010690a54'}, {'abstract': 'Morgan Stanley’s chief executive, James P. Gorman, who is likely to take over as chairman next year, may wait awhile to fill the president’s chair he would vacate.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/morgan-stanleys-next-no-2-the-guessing-game/', 'snippet': 'Morgan Stanley’s chief executive, James P. Gorman, who is likely to take over as chairman next year, may wait awhile to fill the president’s chair he would vacate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wall Street has a new guessing game: whom will James P. Gorman choose to be his No. 2?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morgan Stanley’s Next No. 2, the Guessing Game', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dimon, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fleming, Gregory J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gorman, James P', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mack, John J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moynihan, Brian T', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Porat, Ruth M', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Stanley', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Smith Barney', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T12:27:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susanne Craig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Craig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66fb15f7-16a1-59db-a2a5-85b58ec92883', 'word_count': 810, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66fb15f7-16a1-59db-a2a5-85b58ec92883'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: The city has a budget in time for the start of the 2012 fiscal year, but it is hard to see how it qualifies as a grand political victory for Mayor Bloomberg. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/athens-on-the-hudson/', 'snippet': 'The Day: The city has a budget in time for the start of the 2012 fiscal year, but it is hard to see how it qualifies as a grand political victory for Mayor Bloomberg. Also, what City Room is reading in other papers and blogs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Baghdad on the Hudson, O. Henry called New York. This week, Athens on the Hudson was more like it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Athens on the Hudson', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T12:33:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2981ec5c-0ecf-5f1c-8980-edf95063001f', 'word_count': 965, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2981ec5c-0ecf-5f1c-8980-edf95063001f'}, {'abstract': 'A new federal Web site lets families compare college costs, Federal Reserve caps debit-card swipe fees, owning a home still retains its allure and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/thursday-reading-compare-college-costs-on-a-new-website/', 'snippet': 'A new federal Web site lets families compare college costs, Federal Reserve caps debit-card swipe fees, owning a home still retains its allure and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Reading: Compare College Costs on a New Web Site', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T12:45:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e012e4d8-73a6-5aaa-aa52-6588db4f78ac', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e012e4d8-73a6-5aaa-aa52-6588db4f78ac'}, {'abstract': 'My hair extensions attracted a bizarre amount of attention, so much that I decided it deserved its own diary.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/vain-glorious-going-to-great-lengths/', 'snippet': 'My hair extensions attracted a bizarre amount of attention, so much that I decided it deserved its own diary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve been noticing more and more women sporting longer, impossibly perfect locks, it’s not your imagination. Hair extensions are practically de rigueur. The newest are Platinum Seamless Hair Extensions, which are tiny, sticky strips applied to the hair in under two hours (not the seven or eight that other methods require), and are said to be less damaging to hair and more natural looking. Stylists say these last up to three months, which is just as well. Platinum can cost $2,500 — no small chunk of change. At Ricardo Rojas, one of the few salons offering Platinum, that fee includes color match, installation, cut and styling. I tried them out last week and Rojas, whose clients include Eva Longoria and Bar Rafaeli, told it to me straight: “You don’t need these.” But now that I have them, I know why women become addicted. My hair attracted a bizarre amount of attention, so much that I decided it deserved its own diary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vain Glorious | Going to Great Lengths', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T13:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Earle-Levine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Earle-Levine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30142ac6-9a5f-51a6-9159-d807757efa52', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30142ac6-9a5f-51a6-9159-d807757efa52'}, {'abstract': 'Now, Rupert Murdoch is left to haggle with BSkyB shareholders, many of whom expect a higher offer than the 700 pence a share he bid last summer.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/path-cleared-for-news-corp-s-bskyb-bid/', 'snippet': 'Now, Rupert Murdoch is left to haggle with BSkyB shareholders, many of whom expect a higher offer than the 700 pence a share he bid last summer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'British regulators confirmed on Thursday that they were ready to pave the way for News Corporation’s takeover of the pay television company BSkyB.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Path Cleared for News Corp.’s BSkyB Bid', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Sky Broadcasting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NEWS CORP', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T13:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37fc4217-70fc-5429-8ad6-36b4d0a98987', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37fc4217-70fc-5429-8ad6-36b4d0a98987'}, {'abstract': 'The United Nations tribunal issued indictments for the assassination of former Prime Minister Rafik Hariri, which unleashed years of discord.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/world/middleeast/01lebanon.html', 'snippet': 'The United Nations tribunal issued indictments for the assassination of former Prime Minister Rafik Hariri, which unleashed years of discord.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — A United Nations-backed tribunal investigating the assassination of a former Lebanese prime minister delivered indictments to prosecutors here on Thursday, naming four men, including two suspected members of Hezbollah, in a six-year-old case that remade the country’s politics and unleashed years of discord.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/world/middleeast/01Lebanon1/01Lebanon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/01/world/middleeast/01Lebanon1/01Lebanon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/world/middleeast/01Lebanon1/01Lebanon1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/world/middleeast/01Lebanon1/01Lebanon1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/01/world/middleeast/01Lebanon1/01Lebanon1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tribunal Names 4 in ’05 Killing of Lebanese Leader', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '4 Indicted In Killing Of a Leader Of Lebanon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lebanon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hariri, Rafik', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T13:17:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nada Bakri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nada', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/608a9c9e-195b-5e06-ba72-839015588549', 'word_count': 967, 'uri': 'nyt://article/608a9c9e-195b-5e06-ba72-839015588549'}, {'abstract': 'In Dupont Circle, a new museum sheds light on laogai, China’s labor camop system.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/in-washington-a-museum-for-chinas-labor-camps/', 'snippet': 'In Dupont Circle, a new museum sheds light on laogai, China’s labor camop system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dupont Circle is one of Washington’s premier destinations for foodies and revelers. Now activists have a place at the table as a new museum sheds light on China’s labor camp system.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Washington, a Museum for China’s Labor Camps', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wu, Harry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T13:20:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aaf937af-e088-5f01-a561-b2cb1bb1bad9', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aaf937af-e088-5f01-a561-b2cb1bb1bad9'}, {'abstract': 'With “The Tree of Life,” moviegoers are lining up at the box office — and then a vocal minority is heading for the exits.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/walking-in-and-walking-right-back-out-of-the-tree-of-life/', 'snippet': 'With “The Tree of Life,” moviegoers are lining up at the box office — and then a vocal minority is heading for the exits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You can use all kinds of numbers as barometers of a film’s life: box office receipts, total theaters, days in release. In the case of “The Tree of Life,” Terrence Malick’s epic vision of modern life in America, take those numbers (some 200 theaters, about $6 million domestically since May) and add walkouts — hundreds, going by Tweets, blog posts and other online reports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Walking in and Walking Right Back Out of ‘The Tree of Life’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T13:21:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb32bfb5-a61f-584e-950d-7b26d92053be', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb32bfb5-a61f-584e-950d-7b26d92053be'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney visits a manufacturing plant that the economic stimulus plan did not save.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/romney-video-attacks-obama-on-stimulus-economy/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney visits a manufacturing plant that the economic stimulus plan did not save.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney, the former governor of Massachusetts, continues his assault on President Obama‘s economic record on Thursday by visiting a shuttered metalworks plant in Allentown, Pa.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Video Attacks Obama on Stimulus and Economy', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic National Committee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T13:24:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aedf80bb-f649-549a-88a9-736e8b355ffc', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aedf80bb-f649-549a-88a9-736e8b355ffc'}, {'abstract': 'Live analysis of the women’s semifinals at Wimbledon.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/live-soon-sharapova-vs-lisicki/', 'snippet': 'Live analysis of the women’s semifinals at Wimbledon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maria Sharapova advanced to the Wimbledon final with a straight-sets victory over Sabine Lisicki. Lisicki’s formidable serve deserted her on Thursday, as she made only 53 percent of her first serves and had no aces. Sharapova leaped all over Lisicki’s second serve, hitting winners or forcing errors to break Lisicki six times. Although Sharapova also had problems serving, committing 13 double-faults, she held serve all but three times to win comfortably.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharapova Is Back in Final After Beating Lisicki', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T13:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd408f25-68b3-564f-831b-6bda330d3ce3', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd408f25-68b3-564f-831b-6bda330d3ce3'}, {'abstract': 'Developments in Greece helped stocks in the United States, closing a volatile quarter marked by debt concerns in the euro zone and by a struggling economic recovery at home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/business/01markets.html', 'snippet': 'Developments in Greece helped stocks in the United States, closing a volatile quarter marked by debt concerns in the euro zone and by a struggling economic recovery at home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stock prices rose sharply on Thursday as easing concerns over European debt troubles and economic growth in the United States helped the Dow Jones industrial average end the second quarter with a modest gain. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dow Gains 153 Points to End the Quarter Higher', 'kicker': 'Stocks & Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dow Gains 153 Points to End the Quarter Higher', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nasdaq Composite Index', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'maj