stname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sciolino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c858bf0f-9a2a-5cec-bf64-b644f57dfdd0', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c858bf0f-9a2a-5cec-bf64-b644f57dfdd0'}, {'abstract': 'After much hand-wringing by teams and circuit owners, the FIA mandated a turbochaged 1.6-liter 6-cylinder engine for the 2014 Formula One season and beyond.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/fia-scraps-plan-for-4-cylinder-engines-in-formula-one/', 'snippet': 'After much hand-wringing by teams and circuit owners, the FIA mandated a turbochaged 1.6-liter 6-cylinder engine for the 2014 Formula One season and beyond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bowing to pressure from team owners, race-circuit managers and the series supremo Bernie Ecclestone, the governing body of Formula One announced Wednesday that it would shelve a plan to require 4-cylinder engines on all racecars in 2013.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'FIA Scraps Plan for 4-Cylinder Engines in Formula One', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Engines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Formula One', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T17:29:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd90c595-07ba-5245-9c85-02a3fa5109ae', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd90c595-07ba-5245-9c85-02a3fa5109ae'}, {'abstract': 'After the producers of the Off Broadway musical “The Magdalene” met with representatives of the New York office of the Anti-Defamation League, they said they would consider making changes to the show’s script.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/magdalene-producers-respond-to-accusations-of-anti-semitism/', 'snippet': 'After the producers of the Off Broadway musical “The Magdalene” met with representatives of the New York office of the Anti-Defamation League, they said they would consider making changes to the show’s script.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The producers of the new Off Broadway musical “The Magdalene” met with representatives of the New York office of the Anti-Defamation League on Wednesday after receiving an e-mail the day before saying that the production contains anti-Semitic elements. The show, inspired by the Gnostic Gospels, offers an alternate version of the traditional story of Mary Magdalene. In the musical the title character, played by Lindsie Van Winkle, is not a prostitute and has a romantic relationship with Jesus, or Yeshua (Shad Olsen), causing infighting among his circle of disciples and further invoking the ire of the Roman government. The story ends as it usually does, with the Jesus character crucified.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Magdalene’ Producers Respond to Accusations of Anti-Semitism', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maltby, Richard Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ANTI-DEFAMATION LEAGUE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T17:30:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven McElroy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McElroy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2308c0c-9e32-57bb-a5dc-4e435bbc6f19', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2308c0c-9e32-57bb-a5dc-4e435bbc6f19'}, {'abstract': 'The day in sports, from women’s tennis to women’s discus, and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/06/30/sports/20110630-photoreplay.html', 'snippet': 'The day in sports, from women’s tennis to women’s discus, and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The day in sports, from women’s tennis to women’s discus, and more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/sports/20110630-photoreplay-slide-YC0H/20110630-photoreplay-slide-YC0H-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/30/sports/20110630-photoreplay-slide-YC0H/20110630-photoreplay-slide-YC0H-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/30/sports/20110630-photoreplay-slide-YC0H/20110630-photoreplay-slide-YC0H-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Photo Replay: June 30', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T17:43:08+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/10678d38-6152-5931-9a59-cd6e5bf36712', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/10678d38-6152-5931-9a59-cd6e5bf36712'}, {'abstract': 'New York City Opera musicians protest to keep the financially troubled orchestra from moving from its Lincoln Center home.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/city-opera-players-provide-their-own-accompaniment-at-labor-protest/', 'snippet': 'New York City Opera musicians protest to keep the financially troubled orchestra from moving from its Lincoln Center home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A brass quintet from New York City Opera’s orchestra played tunes from “Carmen,” “Aida” and “La Bohème” on the sidewalk in front of Lincoln Center Plaza on Thursday. A sanitation truck driver honked in solidarity. Protesters held signs denouncing the company’s leader and demanding that he not move City Opera from the cultural complex.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Opera Players Provide Their Own Accompaniment at Labor Protest', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rudel, Julius', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steel, George', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN GUILD OF MUSICAL ARTISTS', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Koch, David H, Theater', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Opera', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T17:50:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c831357-44b7-5130-95b9-7a6305ac6399', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c831357-44b7-5130-95b9-7a6305ac6399'}, {'abstract': 'Chris Anderson, who runs the high-profile TED conferences, recently came up with 10 principles for easing the problems of e-mail. Here are five more.', 'web_url': 'https://pogue.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/we-have-to-fix-e-mail/', 'snippet': 'Chris Anderson, who runs the high-profile TED conferences, recently came up with 10 principles for easing the problems of e-mail. Here are five more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ll admit it: I have an e-mail problem.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'We Have to Fix E-Mail', 'kicker': 'Pogue&#039;s Posts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Mail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T17:54:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca698111-14d9-511d-abd4-d26e01809f4a', 'word_count': 897, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca698111-14d9-511d-abd4-d26e01809f4a'}, {'abstract': 'Jo Wilfried Tsonga’s victory over Roger Federer was a huge growth step, a moment where he realized his potential on the world’s biggest stage.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/analysis-with-victory-over-federer-tsonga-arrives/', 'snippet': 'Jo Wilfried Tsonga’s victory over Roger Federer was a huge growth step, a moment where he realized his potential on the world’s biggest stage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This was a tale of two matches — the best of times in the first two sets for Roger Federer, and then the best of times for Jo Wilfried Tsonga in the last three sets. The worst of times came for Federer in the fourth and fifth sets, when Tsonga took over the match and made Federer look over the hill. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Analysis: With Victory Over Federer, Tsonga Arrives', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T18:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43574895-6a3d-522b-8aaa-53668b3e8c18', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43574895-6a3d-522b-8aaa-53668b3e8c18'}, {'abstract': 'The digital mailman brought questions from an aunt, an art lover and a devoted granddaughter, all looking for places to eat well in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/dining/dining-well-with-children-on-peking-duck-hey-mr-critic.html', 'snippet': 'The digital mailman brought questions from an aunt, an art lover and a devoted granddaughter, all looking for places to eat well in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The digital mailman brought questions from an aunt, an art lover and a devoted granddaughter, all looking for places to eat well in Manhattan, according to their individual desires and needs. I tried to give them good counsel. You may send your own inquiries about dining in New York to dinejournal@nytimes.com.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/CRITIC-3/CRITIC-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 724, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/CRITIC-3/CRITIC-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/CRITIC-3/CRITIC-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Crunchy, Sweet and Used to Quack?', 'kicker': 'Hey, Mr. Critic', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s Crunchy, Sweet and Used to Quack?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T18:05:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/302c0d8b-9ff8-5103-9654-aa193283dddf', 'word_count': 1007, 'uri': 'nyt://article/302c0d8b-9ff8-5103-9654-aa193283dddf'}, {'abstract': 'The National Pinball Museum in Washington delivers a top-notch overview of the game’s history and ample opportunity to experience the anxious thrills of yore.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/arts/design/national-pinball-museum-in-washington-review.html', 'snippet': 'The National Pinball Museum in Washington delivers a top-notch overview of the game’s history and ample opportunity to experience the anxious thrills of yore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — After Monday it may no longer be possible to slip between the two enormous flippers that frame a doorway in a shopping mall in Georgetown and bounce around in a strange world of sleekly raked playing boards, gleaming silver balls, rubber-wrapped pins, jittery bumpers and raucous bells. It looks as if the National Pinball Museum, which opened here only in December, is about to close, almost before an out-of-town critic could offer it the ringing approval it deserves. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/PINBALL-span/PINBALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/PINBALL-span/PINBALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/PINBALL-span/PINBALL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/PINBALL-span/PINBALL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/PINBALL-span/PINBALL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Old Bing, Bing, Bing, at Full Tilt', 'kicker': 'Museum Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Old Bing, Bing, Bing, at Full Tilt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, '