cations', 'value': 'ARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greenland', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T18:47:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sean Collins Walsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sean', 'middlename': 'Collins', 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d2a9561-f2c7-5109-9e08-b6e215daf30d', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d2a9561-f2c7-5109-9e08-b6e215daf30d'}, {'abstract': 'The federal health insurance program said it would not change coverage, despite the likelihood that the F.D.A. will rescind approval of using Avastin to treat advanced breast cancer.', 'web_url': 'https://prescriptions.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/medicare-will-pay-for-avastin-in-treating-breast-cancer/', 'snippet': 'The federal health insurance program said it would not change coverage, despite the likelihood that the F.D.A. will rescind approval of using Avastin to treat advanced breast cancer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated: Medicare will continue to pay for the drug Avastin as a treatment for breast cancer even if the Food and Drug Administration revokes the drug’s approval as a treatment for that disease, a Medicare spokesman said on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Medicare Will Pay for Avastin in Treating Breast Cancer', 'kicker': 'Prescriptions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Avastin (Drug)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breast Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T18:57:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Pollack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pollack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1566096-f440-57ef-94f1-42f28dfbd116', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1566096-f440-57ef-94f1-42f28dfbd116'}, {'abstract': 'This Jersey Shore town may have embraced a more stylish persona, but you’ll still find all the classics: horse-drawn carriages, mini-golf and endless days at the beach.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/03/travel/36-hours-in-cape-may-nj.html', 'snippet': 'This Jersey Shore town may have embraced a more stylish persona, but you’ll still find all the classics: horse-drawn carriages, mini-golf and endless days at the beach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AT the southernmost tip of New Jersey, where the Garden State Parkway’s exit numbers wind down to zero, Cape May, one of the oldest seaside resorts in the nation, has welcomed vacationers for more than 200 years. The images of the Jersey Shore conjured up in the reality TV show bear little resemblance to this family-oriented destination, with its charming Victorian homes and historic lighthouse. While Cape May’s vacation identity is still very much tied to house tours, horse-drawn carriages and dinner theaters, in recent years it has embraced a more stylish persona. Several of its once-faded grande dame hotels and low-slung motels have been updated and transformed into chic escapes, catering to well-to-do families from Philadelphia, northern New Jersey, New York City and beyond. But traditionalists need not fear — Cape May retains its laid-back charm with mini-golf courses, 25-cent Skee-Ball games and families pedaling around on surreys and bicycles built for two.', 'print_sec