major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T20:58:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5af37023-1a92-5792-88d9-da9d68ef84b3', 'word_count': 202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5af37023-1a92-5792-88d9-da9d68ef84b3'}, {'abstract': 'Carte Blanche, the Norwegian national company of contemporary dance, made its American debut in two works by Sharon Eyal at Jacob’s Pillow Dance Festival in Beckett, Mass.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/arts/dance/carte-blanche-dance-troupe-at-jacobs-pillow-review.html', 'snippet': 'Carte Blanche, the Norwegian national company of contemporary dance, made its American debut in two works by Sharon Eyal at Jacob’s Pillow Dance Festival in Beckett, Mass.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BECKET, Mass. — Almost immediately after Sharon Eyal’s “Love” (2003) began on Wednesday night at the Jacob’s Pillow Dance Festival here, during the American debut of Carte Blanche, the Norwegian national company of contemporary dance, two audience members hustled out of the theater. Had they reached a verdict that quickly? It’s possible. After all, they had just seen pretty much the same dance before intermission.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/BLANCHE-span/BLANCHE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/BLANCHE-span/BLANCHE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/BLANCHE-span/BLANCHE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 546, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/BLANCHE-span/BLANCHE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/BLANCHE-span/BLANCHE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Menacing Working Girls Stuck in Punishing Grind', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Menacing Working Girls Stuck in Punishing Grind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Jacob's Pillow Dance Festival", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BATSHEVA DANCE COMPANY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T20:59:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia La Rocco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Rocco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5d5eedb-6925-5a45-8c0d-67c38398bfb7', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5d5eedb-6925-5a45-8c0d-67c38398bfb7'}, {'abstract': '“Urban Tao Evolution,” by Overground Physical Theater Company, features a group of city dwellers and one large heap of stones.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/arts/dance/urban-tao-evolution-at-dance-theater-workshop-review.html', 'snippet': '“Urban Tao Evolution,” by Overground Physical Theater Company, features a group of city dwellers and one large heap of stones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By the time the performers of Overground Physical Theater Company gathered onstage for their final bow, it had dawned on me that summer — hands down, the bleakest time for dance in New York — is officially here. In “Urban Tao Evolution,” a convoluted and rambling new work performed at Dance Theater Workshop on Wednesday night, the message had something to do with choosing spirituality over technology. What it really looked like was a New Age musical produced in a high school and performed, to a large extent, by the teachers.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/TAO/TAO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 857, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/TAO/TAO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/TAO/TAO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'City Life Isn’t Always Uplifting', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'City Life Isn’t Always Uplifting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dance Theater Workshop', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T21:01:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f08a2757-f9c5-5181-adf8-f8962bc100f1', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f08a2757-f9c5-5181-adf8-f8962bc100f1'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel about a very famous golfer whose extracurricular kinks become a public embarrassment, the authors Michael Bamberger and Alan Shipnuck know their man and know their game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/07/01/books/michael-bamberger-and-alan-shipnucks-swinger-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel about a very famous golfer whose extracurricular kinks become a public embarrassment, the authors Michael Bamberger and Alan Shipnuck know their man and know their game.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Swinger” is a novel about a very famous golfer who has an amazing career record, a slew of endorsement deals, a gorgeous wife and a squeaky-clean reputation — until his extracurricular kinks become a huge public embarrassment and spoil everything. The authors, Michael Bamberger (“This Golfing Life”) and Alan Shipnuck (“Bud, Sweat and Tees”), call this golfer Herbert X. Tremont, known as Tree, and his wife, Belinda. But they don’t insult the reader’s intelligence by claiming that these characters bear no resemblance to persons living or dead.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/BOOK/BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/BOOK/BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 767}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 801, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/01/arts/BOOK/BOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1602, 'sub