 than a commercial banker, he could be retiring with praise, rather than facing possible legal liability for following the time-dishonored practice of “extend and pretend,” in which bad loans are treated as if they were just fine.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/norris-75/norris-75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/04/19/business/norris-75/norris-75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Playing Make-Believe With Greece', 'kicker': 'High & Low Finance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Playing Make-Believe With Greece', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Euro (Currency)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trichet, Jean-Claude', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sullivan & Cromwell', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kazakhstan', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T22:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96222b8f-cd9e-5d6c-a2a2-6312d11dfec0', 'word_count': 1366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96222b8f-cd9e-5d6c-a2a2-6312d11dfec0'}, {'abstract': 'Certainly all city dwellers are used to noise, but there are those who hear a symphony in the cacophony and those who seek out New York’s many pockets of peace and quiet. Here, two views.  Which do you prefer?', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/06/30/the-sounds-of-the-city-do-you-prefer-cacophony-or-quiet/', 'snippet': 'Certainly all city dwellers are used to noise, but there are those who hear a symphony in the cacophony and those who seek out New York’s many pockets of peace and quiet. Here, two views.  Which do you prefer?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Forty thousand fireworks. Now that’s bound to make some noise. There are New Yorkers who will love every decibel on the Fourth of July, when Macy’s puts on its “biggest fireworks display in America.” Other New Yorkers — well, they’d much rather see the rockets’ red glare than hear the bursting in air. Certainly all city dwellers are used to noise, but there are those who hear a symphony in the cacophony and those who seek out New York’s many pockets of peace and quiet. Here, two views. Which do you prefer, the cacophony of New York or the quiet?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sounds of the City — Do You Prefer Cacophony or Quiet?', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Noise', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-06-30T22:02:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Ti